DB.CALL = DB.CALL || {}; Object.assign(DB.CALL, {"rhodiola": "Rhodiola is an energy and stress supplement, not FDA approved. It may interact with antidepressants and some other medicines, so supplement use belongs in the history.", "salidroside": "Salidroside is a marker compound in rhodiola supplements, not FDA approved. Concerns are the same as for rhodiola, including possible antidepressant interactions.", "rosavin": "Rosavins are label markers that show a supplement contains Rhodiola rosea. They are not FDA approved, and cautions are the same as for rhodiola.", "ashwagandha": "Ashwagandha is a stress and sleep supplement, not FDA approved. It can add to sedation, has rarely caused liver injury, may raise thyroid levels and is generally avoided in pregnancy.", "withanolides": "Withanolides are the marker compounds in ashwagandha supplements, not FDA approved. Their risks follow ashwagandha: sedation, rare liver injury and thyroid changes.", "panax-ginseng": "Asian ginseng is an energy supplement and energy drink ingredient, not FDA approved. It can lower blood sugar, cause insomnia, change warfarin response and interact with some antidepressants.", "ginsenosides": "Ginsenosides are the marker compounds of ginseng supplements, not FDA approved. Their concerns follow ginseng: low blood sugar, insomnia and warfarin changes.", "american-ginseng": "American ginseng is a supplement, not FDA approved. It can lower blood sugar and weaken warfarin.", "eleuthero": "Eleuthero (Siberian ginseng) is an energy supplement, not a true ginseng and not FDA approved. It has been linked to an unusual digoxin level in a case report.", "schisandra": "Schisandra is an adaptogen berry supplement, not FDA approved. It can raise transplant drug levels such as tacrolimus.", "holy-basil": "Holy basil (tulsi) is a stress tea and supplement, not FDA approved. It may lower blood sugar with diabetes medicines.", "maca": "Maca is a Peruvian food root sold as a supplement, not FDA approved. Sexual enhancement blends that list herbs like maca have been found to hide prescription drugs.", "astragalus": "Astragalus is an immune supplement, not FDA approved. It may theoretically work against transplant and other immune-suppressing medicines.", "bacopa": "Bacopa (brahmi) is a memory supplement, not FDA approved. Upset stomach is common, and slow pulse is a theoretical concern with heart drugs.", "gotu-kola": "Gotu kola is a memory and circulation supplement, not FDA approved and with no caffeine. It has rarely been linked to liver injury.", "shatavari": "Shatavari is an Ayurvedic women's health and lactation supplement, not FDA approved. Evidence is weak, and asparagus allergy is a concern.", "kava": "Kava is a sedating Pacific Island root drink and supplement, not FDA approved. It adds to alcohol and sedative effects and is linked to serious liver injury.", "kavalactones": "Kavalactones are the active calming compounds in kava, not FDA approved. They add to alcohol and sedatives, and kava products are linked to liver injury.", "valerian": "Valerian is a sleep supplement and tea, not FDA approved. It adds to alcohol and sedative effects, and rare liver injury is reported.", "valerenic-acid": "Valerenic acid is the main calming compound in valerian, not FDA approved. It adds to the effects of alcohol and other sedatives.", "chamomile": "Chamomile is a calming herbal tea and supplement, not FDA approved. Allergy in ragweed-sensitive patients and rare warfarin interactions are the main safety points.", "passionflower": "Passionflower is a calming herb, often in sleep blends, not FDA approved. It can add to the drowsiness from sedatives, pain medicines and alcohol.", "lemon-balm": "Lemon balm is a mild calming herb in teas and sleep blends, not FDA approved. It can add to drowsiness from sedatives, and laboratory data raise a theoretical thyroid caution.", "lavender-oil": "Lavender oil is sold as an oral anxiety supplement and as an aromatherapy oil, not FDA approved. Burping is common, and children who swallow essential oils can become drowsy or aspirate.", "hops": "Hops are a sleep herb, usually combined with valerian, not FDA approved. They add to sedation from other depressants and contain a potent plant estrogen.", "california-poppy": "California poppy is a calming herb, not FDA approved, that contains no morphine. It may still add to drowsiness from opioids, sedatives and alcohol.", "skullcap": "Skullcap is a calming herb, not FDA approved, linked to liver injury in case reports. Jaundice or right upper belly pain in a user points toward possible herbal liver injury.", "magnolia-bark": "Magnolia bark is a GABA-active calming supplement, not FDA approved. It can add to sedation from benzodiazepines, sleep medicines and alcohol.", "saffron": "Saffron is a spice also sold as a mood supplement, not FDA approved. Large amounts stimulate the uterus and can cause bleeding.", "st-johns-wort": "St. John's wort is a mood supplement, not FDA approved, that lowers levels of many drugs including transplant, HIV and birth control medicines. With SSRIs, triptans or tramadol, it can cause serotonin syndrome.", "hyperforin": "Hyperforin is the St. John's wort compound behind its mood effects and most of its drug interactions. It lowers levels of many drugs and can contribute to serotonin syndrome with SSRIs.", "hypericin": "Hypericin is the red St. John's wort pigment that causes sun sensitivity. It adds to photosensitivity from other drugs, while the herb's drug interactions come mostly from hyperforin.", "ginkgo": "Ginkgo is a memory supplement, not FDA approved, that may raise bleeding risk with blood thinners. A large study found it did not prevent dementia.", "ginkgolides": "Ginkgolides are ginkgo compounds that block platelet-activating factor. They may add to bleeding risk with blood thinners and antiplatelet drugs.", "huperzine-a": "Huperzine A is a memory supplement, not FDA approved in the US, that acts like donepezil-type dementia drugs. It can cause nausea, sweating and a slow heart rate, especially combined with those drugs.", "vinpocetine": "Vinpocetine is a synthetic memory supplement ingredient that FDA warns may cause miscarriage and fetal harm. It can also lower blood pressure and add to bleeding risk.", "mucuna": "Mucuna is a seed supplement that naturally contains L-dopa, not FDA approved. It can cause hallucinations and abnormal movements, and a dangerous blood pressure rise with MAOI antidepressants.", "kanna": "Kanna is a South African mood plant, not FDA approved, whose alkaloids block serotonin reuptake. Combining it with antidepressants raises concern for serotonin syndrome.", "damiana": "Damiana is a Mexican libido herb, not FDA approved, with very little evidence. The bigger risk is hidden drugs in libido products and synthetic cannabinoids in smoking blends.", "blue-lotus": "Blue lotus is a psychoactive water lily sold as a tea, smoke or vape, not FDA approved. It can cause drowsiness and confusion, especially with alcohol or concentrated resins.", "hawthorn": "Hawthorn is an herbal heart tonic supplement, not FDA approved, often used by heart failure patients. It may add to the effects of digoxin, nitrates and blood pressure drugs, so ask about it with every cardiac history.", "aged-garlic-extract": "Aged garlic extract is an odorless garlic supplement, not FDA approved. Garlic products can add to bleeding risk with blood thinners, so ask about them in any bleeding or pre-surgery patient.", "guggul": "Guggul is an Ayurvedic tree resin supplement for cholesterol, not FDA approved, and a careful trial showed no LDL benefit. It commonly causes rash and may lower levels of some heart drugs.", "horse-chestnut": "Horse chestnut seed extract is a leg-vein supplement, not FDA approved. Raw horse chestnuts (conkers) are poisonous, so a vomiting, unsteady child who ate them needs supportive care, poison center input and transport.", "butchers-broom": "Butcher's broom is a venous and hemorrhoid supplement, not FDA approved. It acts on alpha receptors in animal studies, so note it in patients with high blood pressure or on alpha blockers.", "dong-quai": "Dong quai is a Chinese women's health root, not FDA approved. It has been linked to raised INR with warfarin and sun-sensitive skin, so ask about it in any bleeding patient on a blood thinner.", "bergamot-polyphenols": "Citrus bergamot is a natural cholesterol supplement, not FDA approved, with only small trials behind it. Some patients use it instead of a statin, so cholesterol supplements belong in the medication history.", "arjuna": "Arjuna is an Ayurvedic heart-tonic bark, not FDA approved, with only small trials behind it. Chest pain in a patient using it is treated as chest pain, and the supplement goes into the medication history.", "hibiscus": "Hibiscus is a sour red tea and supplement, not FDA approved, that may modestly lower blood pressure. In a dizzy patient on blood pressure medicines, herbal teas are part of the history.", "olive-leaf": "Olive leaf extract is a blood pressure and immune supplement, not FDA approved. It may add to blood pressure and diabetes drugs, so ask about it in a dizzy or shaky patient.", "milk-thistle": "Milk thistle is a popular liver supplement, not FDA approved, with no proven benefit in human liver disease. After a wild mushroom ingestion it is not protection: supportive care, poison center contact and transport.", "silymarin": "Silymarin is the active complex in milk thistle, not FDA approved. Oral pills do not treat mushroom poisoning; delayed vomiting and diarrhea after wild mushrooms needs supportive care, poison center contact and transport for everyone who ate them.", "artichoke-leaf": "Artichoke leaf extract is a bile-stimulating digestive supplement, not FDA approved. It may provoke pain in patients with gallstones, so digestive supplements belong in the abdominal pain history.", "dandelion": "Dandelion is a weed sold as a natural water pill, not FDA approved. It may add to diuretics and lithium, so ask about herbal teas in a dehydrated patient or one on lithium.", "peppermint-oil": "Peppermint oil capsules are an IBS supplement, not FDA approved, that can cause heartburn. A toddler who swallowed concentrated essential oil and is drowsy or coughing needs airway support, poison center contact and transport.", "slippery-elm": "Slippery elm is a soothing bark used for sore throats and stomach upset, not FDA approved. Its gel can slow absorption of other medicines swallowed at the same time.", "marshmallow-root": "Marshmallow root is a soothing cough and throat herb, not FDA approved. Its gel may slow absorption of other oral medicines, and a long-lasting cough with fever still needs evaluation.", "aloe-vera": "Aloe gel on the skin is common, but oral aloe latex is a strong laxative removed from OTC laxatives. Diarrhea, weakness and palpitations in a patient on digoxin who drinks aloe products can mean low potassium.", "cascara-sagrada": "Cascara sagrada is an herbal stimulant laxative in many cleanse teas, not FDA approved. Heavy use can drop potassium, so weakness and palpitations in a laxative user call for cardiac monitoring and transport.", "berberine": "Berberine is a popular blood sugar supplement, not FDA approved, that can add to diabetes drugs and raise levels of other medicines. In a hypoglycemic patient, ask about blood sugar supplements.", "goldenseal": "Goldenseal is a yellow root herb sold for colds, not FDA approved, that blocks liver enzymes clearing many medicines. Unexpected drug side effects in a goldenseal user can be an interaction.", "fenugreek": "Fenugreek is a spice seed sold for blood sugar and breast milk supply, not FDA approved. It can drop blood sugar with diabetes medicines and cause a maple syrup body odor.", "triphala": "Triphala is an Ayurvedic three-fruit powder used as a mild laxative, not FDA approved. Some Ayurvedic products have contained lead or other metals.", "dgl-licorice": "DGL is licorice with most of its blood-pressure-raising glycyrrhizin removed, chewed for heartburn, not FDA approved. Whole licorice is different and can cause high blood pressure and low potassium.", "black-seed": "Black seed oil is a Nigella sativa seed supplement, not FDA approved. It may add to blood sugar and blood pressure medicines and can cause skin rashes when applied.", "boswellia": "Boswellia is an Indian frankincense resin sold for joint pain, not FDA approved. It usually causes only mild stomach upset but is not a substitute for prescribed treatment.", "devils-claw": "Devil's claw is a southern African root sold for back and joint pain, not FDA approved. It often upsets the stomach and may interact with warfarin.", "willow-bark": "White willow bark is a natural salicylate source related to aspirin, not FDA approved. People with aspirin allergy, children with viral illness and blood thinner users are at particular risk.", "cats-claw": "Cat's claw is an Amazon vine bark sold for arthritis and immune support, not FDA approved. It may interact with transplant and HIV medicines and lower blood pressure.", "arnica": "Arnica is a bruise-cream flower and homeopathic product, not FDA approved, and the herb, oils and tinctures are poisonous to swallow. Vomiting or palpitations after swallowing arnica is a poisoning call for the poison center.", "feverfew": "Feverfew is a daisy-like herb sold to prevent migraines, not FDA approved. It may add to bleeding with blood thinners and is considered likely unsafe in pregnancy.", "butterbur": "Butterbur is a migraine and hay fever herb, not FDA approved, whose raw plant contains liver-toxic pyrrolizidine alkaloids. Jaundice or a swollen belly in a butterbur user can reflect liver injury.", "black-cohosh": "Black cohosh is a menopause root supplement, not FDA approved, with mixed evidence. Jaundice in a black cohosh user can reflect rare supplement-related liver injury.", "chasteberry": "Chasteberry is a Mediterranean berry sold for PMS and cycle problems, not FDA approved. It acts on dopamine and may interact with antipsychotics, hormones and fertility treatment.", "red-clover": "Red clover is a plant estrogen supplement sold for hot flashes, not FDA approved, with mixed evidence. Its estrogen-like effects matter for hormone-sensitive cancers and hormone treatments.", "evening-primrose-oil": "Evening primrose oil is a GLA-rich seed oil sold for eczema and breast pain, not FDA approved, with little proven benefit. It may add to bleeding risk with blood thinners.", "saw-palmetto": "Saw palmetto is a palm berry extract sold for prostate symptoms, not FDA approved, and large trials found it no better than placebo. Bleeding has been reported, especially around surgery.", "pygeum": "Pygeum is an African tree bark extract sold for prostate symptoms, not FDA approved, with modest evidence from older studies. Urinary symptoms still need medical evaluation.", "stinging-nettle": "Stinging nettle is a stinging weed used as an allergy tea and prostate supplement, not FDA approved. Its leaf contains vitamin K, which can lower INR on warfarin.", "tribulus": "Tribulus terrestris is a spiky weed sold as a testosterone booster, not FDA approved, and it does not raise testosterone in human trials. Multi-ingredient boosters can hide drugs, and rare kidney and liver injury has been reported.", "tongkat-ali": "Tongkat ali is a Southeast Asian root sold as a men's testosterone and libido supplement, not FDA approved. Sexual enhancement blends can hide drugs that dangerously lower blood pressure with nitrates.", "horny-goat-weed": "Horny goat weed is a traditional Chinese herb sold in libido supplements, not FDA approved and weakly supported by evidence. Blends can hide drugs that interact dangerously with nitrates.", "yohimbe": "Yohimbe is a tree bark supplement whose alkaloid yohimbine acts like a stimulant by releasing norepinephrine, not FDA approved. Anxiety, tremor, a racing heart and high blood pressure after a pre-workout or male enhancement product point toward it.", "wild-yam": "Wild yam is a root sold in menopause creams as natural progesterone, but the body cannot turn its diosgenin into hormones. Some creams have real progesterone added, so the exact product matters.", "echinacea": "Echinacea is a daisy-family flower sold for colds, not FDA approved, with mixed evidence. Allergy is the main risk, especially with ragweed allergy or asthma.", "elderberry": "Elderberry is a cooked berry syrup or gummy sold for colds and flu, not FDA approved, with limited evidence. Raw berries, leaves and stems can cause vomiting and diarrhea.", "andrographis": "Andrographis is a bitter herb in cold and flu products, not FDA approved, with limited evidence. It is linked to serious allergic reactions and is avoided in pregnancy.", "pelargonium": "Pelargonium is a South African geranium root sold as a bronchitis and cold syrup, not FDA approved in the US. Evidence is weak, and rare allergic and liver reactions are reported.", "oregano-oil": "Oil of oregano is a concentrated essential oil sold as a natural germ fighter, not FDA approved and not proven in human infections. Undiluted oil irritates skin and mouth, and swallowed essential oils are a choking and aspiration risk in children.", "eucalyptus-oil": "Eucalyptus oil is a leaf oil used in chest rubs and inhalers; swallowed, it is a poison, especially for children. Drowsiness, vomiting, coughing or seizures after ingestion call for airway support, poison control and transport.", "tea-tree-oil": "Tea tree oil is an Australian leaf oil used on skin for acne and fungus, not FDA approved and poisonous if swallowed. A sleepy, unsteady child near an essential oil bottle suggests ingestion.", "propolis": "Propolis is a bee-made resin sold in throat sprays, lozenges and ointments, not FDA approved, with limited evidence. Allergic rashes and mouth swelling are its main risks.", "manuka-honey": "Manuka honey is a food with strong lab antibacterial activity from methylglyoxal, and sterile medical-grade versions are FDA-cleared wound dressings. No honey of any kind is given to infants under 12 months.", "neem": "Neem is an Indian tree used in soaps, toothpastes and pesticides, not FDA approved. Neem oil swallowed by infants and young children can cause vomiting, seizures, coma and a Reye-like liver illness.", "bitter-melon": "Bitter melon is a bitter gourd sold to lower blood sugar, not FDA approved and inconsistently supported by trials. It can cause low blood sugar with diabetes medicines and is avoided in pregnancy and G6PD deficiency.", "gymnema": "Gymnema is a leaf supplement that briefly blocks sweet taste and is sold for blood sugar, not FDA approved. It can add to diabetes medicines and cause low blood sugar.", "cinnamon-cassia": "Cinnamon supplements are sold for blood sugar, not FDA approved and with mixed evidence. The common cassia type contains coumarin, which can harm the liver at high intakes in sensitive people.", "garcinia-cambogia": "Garcinia cambogia is a fruit rind weight-loss pill, not FDA approved and not proven to work. It is linked to liver injury and, with some antidepressants, to serotonin toxicity.", "green-coffee-bean": "Green coffee bean extract is an unroasted coffee supplement sold for weight loss, not FDA approved, with weak evidence. It still contains caffeine, so palpitations and insomnia can occur.", "white-kidney-bean": "White kidney bean extract is a carb blocker supplement that inhibits starch digestion, not FDA approved and with limited evidence. Gas and bloating are common, and it may add to diabetes medicines.", "banaba": "Banaba is a leaf supplement marketed for blood sugar, not FDA approved. It can add to diabetes medicines and contribute to low blood sugar.", "glucomannan": "Glucomannan is a konjac root fiber that swells into a gel, not an FDA-approved drug. Dry tablets and konjac jellies can cause choking or blockage, and it may lower blood sugar.", "bitter-orange": "Bitter orange is a stimulant ingredient in fat burners, not FDA approved. With caffeine it can raise heart rate and blood pressure and has been linked to strokes, heart attacks and arrhythmias.", "raspberry-ketone": "Raspberry ketone is a lab-made raspberry aroma chemical sold for weight loss, not FDA approved and without good human evidence. Many products also contain stimulants.", "forskolin": "Forskolin is a coleus root supplement sold for weight loss, not FDA approved. It can lower blood pressure, speed the heart and may add to blood thinners.", "hoodia": "Hoodia is a desert succulent sold as a diet pill, not FDA approved and not shown to curb appetite in a controlled human trial. Palpitations or high blood pressure in a user call for a check of every ingredient, since blends may hide stimulants.", "cbg": "CBG is a non-intoxicating hemp cannabinoid sold in oils and gummies, not FDA approved and barely studied in people. A sleepy or impaired user may be reacting to THC that is in the product but not on the label.", "cbn": "CBN is a hemp cannabinoid formed from aged THC and sold in sleep gummies, not FDA approved and weakly studied. A very sleepy patient, especially a child, may have taken a blend that also contains THC or melatonin.", "delta-8-thc": "Delta-8 THC is a lab-made, THC-like product sold as hemp, not FDA approved, that causes a marijuana-like high. Sleepiness or breathing trouble after edibles, especially in children, is the main danger.", "thcv": "THCV is a minor cannabis chemical sold for appetite control, not FDA approved, with only small studies behind it. Many THCV products also contain THC and can cause intoxication.", "cannabidiol": "Cannabidiol is a non-intoxicating hemp chemical; purified CBD is FDA approved as Epidiolex for specific seizure disorders, while hemp CBD products are unapproved. Key risks are liver enzyme elevation, sleepiness and drug interactions.", "hemp-seed-oil": "Hemp seed oil is a food oil pressed from hemp seeds, rich in omega fats and not a CBD or THC product. It does not cause a high, though mislabeled products cause confusion.", "beta-caryophyllene": "Beta-caryophyllene is a spicy food terpene that activates the CB2 cannabinoid receptor, not FDA approved and not intoxicating. Human evidence of benefit is minimal.", "lions-mane": "Lion's mane is an edible mushroom supplement marketed for memory and focus, not FDA approved, with mostly laboratory evidence. Rare allergic reactions are the main reported concern.", "hericenones": "Hericenones and erinacines are lion's mane compounds that boosted nerve growth factor in laboratory studies, not FDA approved. Human benefit is unproven and product content is rarely measured.", "reishi": "Reishi is a bitter traditional Chinese mushroom sold for immunity, not FDA approved, with no proven benefit in systematic reviews. It may add to blood thinners and rarely injures the liver.", "ganoderic-acids": "Ganoderic acids are the bitter triterpenes of reishi, not FDA approved, with enzyme effects shown mainly in laboratory studies. Reishi products are rarely linked to liver injury.", "turkey-tail": "Turkey tail is a bracket fungus supplement sold for immune and cancer support, not FDA approved; its standardized extract PSK is a cancer adjunct in Japan. Supplement use during cancer care is shared with the oncology team.", "psk-krestin": "PSK is a turkey tail extract approved in Japan as a cancer add-on, not FDA approved in the US. Its use during cancer treatment is shared with the oncology team.", "shiitake": "Shiitake is a common cooking mushroom whose supplements are unapproved and unproven for immunity or cholesterol. Raw or undercooked shiitake can cause a distinctive whip-like rash.", "lentinan": "Lentinan is a shiitake beta-glucan used as an IV cancer add-on in Japan and sold elsewhere as an unapproved supplement. Oral and IV forms are not equivalent.", "maitake": "Maitake is an edible mushroom supplement sold for immunity and blood sugar, not FDA approved. It may add to diabetes medicines, and a case report described a higher INR with warfarin.", "chaga": "Chaga is a birch fungus tea and supplement, not FDA approved, with very high oxalate. Months of daily use have caused oxalate kidney damage in case reports, especially in people with kidney risk factors.", "cordyceps": "Cordyceps is an insect-parasitic fungus sold for energy and endurance, not FDA approved, with mixed evidence. It may add to blood thinners and diabetes drugs, and some traditional products have been contaminated.", "cordycepin": "Cordycepin is an adenosine look-alike from Cordyceps militaris that blocks RNA chain growth in the laboratory, not FDA approved. The body destroys it within minutes, and supplement use during cancer care is shared with the oncology team.", "agaricus-blazei": "Agaricus blazei is a Brazilian almond mushroom sold for immune and cancer support, not FDA approved. Rare severe liver injury has been reported, and it may lower blood sugar.", "tremella": "Tremella is a white jelly fungus used in Chinese desserts and skin care, not FDA approved and with unproven health claims. Spoiled or long-soaked fungus can carry a deadly bacterial toxin.", "mushroom-beta-glucans": "Beta-glucans are cell wall sugars from mushrooms and yeast sold as immune supplements, not FDA approved, with mixed evidence. Oat and barley beta-glucans are a different fiber with a heart health claim.", "red-yeast-rice": "Red yeast rice is a fermented rice supplement that can contain a natural form of lovastatin, not FDA approved. Muscle pain or dark urine can mean statin-type muscle injury, and some products have been contaminated with kidney toxins.", "monacolin-k": "Monacolin K is the natural lovastatin in red yeast rice supplements. It acts like a prescription statin, so muscle pain, dark urine and drug interactions matter.", "citrinin": "Citrinin is a kidney-toxic mold poison that can contaminate red yeast rice and stored grain. Nobody uses it on purpose, and unexplained kidney problems in a red yeast rice user are worth reporting.", "brewers-yeast": "Brewer's yeast is an inactive yeast supplement sold for B vitamins and chromium, not FDA approved. It may add to diabetes medicines and has MAOI and gout cautions.", "nutritional-yeast": "Nutritional yeast is a deactivated yeast seasoning popular in vegan diets. It supplies vitamin B12 only when fortified, which matters when a vegan has numbness or anemia.", "kombucha": "Kombucha is a fermented tea drink with small amounts of alcohol and caffeine; health claims are unproven. Rare home-brew cases have caused serious acidosis or liver injury, and the alcohol can matter with metronidazole or disulfiram.", "koji-kojic-acid": "Kojic acid is a mold-derived skin-lightening ingredient in creams and soaps, not FDA approved. It commonly irritates skin or causes allergic rash, and imported lightening creams can hide mercury.", "creatine": "Creatine is a well-studied sports supplement that refills muscle energy during short hard efforts, not FDA approved. It raises the creatinine blood test, so creatine use is part of reading kidney labs in athletes.", "caffeine": "Caffeine pills, energy shots and pre-workout powders can cause vomiting, tremor, a racing heart and seizures. Concentrated powder or liquid is the highest risk, and there is no specific antidote.", "beta-alanine": "Beta-alanine is a pre-workout amino acid that often causes harmless skin tingling. Racing heart or chest pain after a pre-workout points to the caffeine or other stimulants in the same product.", "hmb": "HMB is a muscle supplement made from the amino acid leucine and is generally low risk. Symptoms after a gym product usually point to other ingredients, such as stimulants.", "acetyl-l-carnitine": "Acetyl-L-carnitine is a carnitine supplement sold for memory and nerve health and is usually low risk. In taxane chemotherapy it was linked to more nerve damage, and it may affect warfarin.", "l-arginine": "L-arginine is a nitric oxide booster sold for blood flow and workouts. Combined with erectile dysfunction medicines, nitrates or blood pressure pills it can add to low blood pressure.", "bcaa": "BCAAs are muscle amino acids sold as gym drinks and are generally low risk. They can blunt levodopa in Parkinson disease and are avoided in maple syrup urine disease.", "taurine": "Taurine is an energy drink ingredient but not a stimulant. A racing heart after energy drinks points to caffeine.", "d-ribose": "D-ribose is a sugar sold as an energy supplement and is usually low risk. It can lower blood sugar, especially with diabetes medicines.", "whey-protein": "Whey is the milk protein in most protein powders. It can cause allergic reactions in people with milk allergy, and protein shakes can blunt levodopa.", "ecdysterone": "Ecdysterone and turkesterone are plant steroids sold as natural muscle builders, with little human evidence. Signs like jaundice or severe acne raise concern for hidden steroids in gym supplements.", "sodium-bicarbonate-sport": "Athletes sometimes swallow large amounts of baking soda before races. Vomiting, tingling, muscle spasms or confusion afterward can mean metabolic alkalosis, and sudden severe belly pain can mean stomach rupture.", "l-theanine": "L-theanine is a tea amino acid sold for calm focus and is low risk. A racing heart after a focus product points to the caffeine paired with it.", "five-htp": "5-HTP is a serotonin booster sold for mood and sleep. With antidepressants, tramadol or triptans it can contribute to serotonin syndrome: agitation, sweating, tremor, muscle jerks and fever.", "l-tyrosine": "L-tyrosine is an amino acid used to make dopamine and thyroid hormone and is sold for focus. Thyroid and focus blends may hide stimulants or thyroid hormone.", "l-tryptophan": "L-tryptophan is a serotonin and melatonin building block sold for sleep. With antidepressants it can contribute to serotonin syndrome, and it adds to drowsiness from sedatives.", "gaba-supplement": "GABA supplements are sold for calm and sleep and are low risk, with little proof they reach the brain. A GABA-type product causing dependence or withdrawal may actually be phenibut.", "sam-e": "SAMe is a mood and joint supplement that can act on serotonin. With antidepressants it may contribute to serotonin syndrome, and it can trigger mania in bipolar disorder.", "phosphatidylserine": "Phosphatidylserine is a memory supplement made from soy or sunflower and is generally low risk. Memory blends that contain it may include herbs like ginkgo with their own bleeding risks.", "citicoline": "Citicoline is a brain supplement that supplies choline and is generally low risk. Large studies found no benefit after stroke or brain injury, and focus products may contain caffeine.", "alpha-gpc": "Alpha-GPC is a choline supplement in nootropics and pre-workouts. Caffeine in the same products often causes acute symptoms, and one study linked alpha-GPC to higher stroke risk.", "magnesium-l-threonate": "Magnesium L-threonate is a magnesium supplement marketed for memory, not FDA approved. In a patient with kidney failure, weakness, slow pulse or low blood pressure can point to magnesium buildup.", "uridine": "Uridine monophosphate is a nucleotide supplement sold for memory, not FDA approved. Its main clinical relevance is that uridine can counteract fluorouracil chemotherapy.", "piracetam": "Piracetam is an unapproved 'smart drug' sold online, sometimes hidden in brain supplements. It can add to bleeding risk with blood thinners and builds up in kidney disease.", "fish-oil": "Fish oil is a very common omega-3 supplement, not FDA approved as a supplement. It can add to bleeding risk with blood thinners, and higher intakes are linked to atrial fibrillation.", "krill-oil": "Krill oil is an omega-3 supplement from Antarctic krill, not FDA approved. It shares fish oil's small bleeding risk and raises concern in shellfish allergy.", "algal-oil": "Algal oil is a fish-free omega-3 supplement made from algae, not FDA approved. It is generally well tolerated and shares the usual omega-3 cautions with blood thinners.", "flaxseed": "Flaxseed is a fiber and plant omega-3 food and supplement, not FDA approved. The main issues are GI upset, obstruction with too little fluid, and slowed absorption of oral medicines.", "nattokinase": "Nattokinase is a 'natural clot buster' enzyme supplement, not FDA approved. It can add to bleeding risk, especially with aspirin or blood thinners.", "plant-sterols": "Plant sterols and stanols are cholesterol-lowering food ingredients and supplements, not FDA-approved drugs. They modestly lower LDL and are of low toxicity, except in the rare disorder sitosterolemia.", "policosanol": "Policosanol is a sugar cane wax cholesterol supplement, not FDA approved. Independent trials did not confirm a cholesterol benefit, and it may add to bleeding risk with antiplatelets.", "mct-oil": "MCT oil is a fast-absorbed fat used in medical nutrition and keto diets, not FDA approved as a drug. It commonly causes GI upset and is avoided in MCAD deficiency.", "exogenous-ketones": "Exogenous ketones are drinks and powders that raise blood ketones for a few hours, not FDA approved. In a patient with diabetes, they can muddy the picture of ketone-related illness, and salts add sodium or potassium.", "psyllium": "Psyllium is an OTC fiber laxative. Taken with too little liquid or by someone who has trouble swallowing, it can swell and block the throat, so choking after a fiber drink is treated as an airway emergency.", "collagen-peptides": "Collagen peptides are protein supplements from animal skin, bone or fish, not FDA approved. They are low in toxicity but matter for fish allergy, and blends with biotin can affect lab tests.", "colostrum": "Bovine colostrum is a cow's first-milk supplement sold for immunity and gut health, not FDA approved. It is a dairy product, so milk allergy is the main acute concern.", "digestive-enzymes": "OTC digestive enzymes help break down specific foods such as milk sugar or bean sugars, not FDA approved as supplements. Allergic reactions to pork, papaya or pineapple sources are the main acute concern.", "apple-cider-vinegar": "Apple cider vinegar is a popular home remedy and supplement, not FDA approved. Undiluted vinegar or tablets can injure the throat, and it slows stomach emptying.", "d-mannose": "D-mannose is a sugar supplement used to try to prevent repeat UTIs, not FDA approved. It does not treat an active infection, and a recent large trial found no benefit.", "glutathione": "Glutathione is the body's own antioxidant, sold as pills and given as IV drips in wellness and skin-lightening clinics, none FDA approved. Fever, chills, rash or low blood pressure after a clinic infusion can be a reaction or infection from a compounded product.", "pqq": "PQQ is a bacterial cofactor sold as an energy and brain supplement, not FDA approved. Human evidence is limited, and stacks often add CoQ10, which can affect warfarin.", "astaxanthin": "Astaxanthin is a pink-red algae pigment sold as an antioxidant supplement, not FDA approved. It has few known safety problems, but it can tint stool, so real bleeding still has to be assessed.", "spermidine": "Spermidine is a natural polyamine sold as an anti-aging supplement, not FDA approved. The evidence is mostly in animals, and wheat germ-based products may contain gluten.", "msm": "MSM is a sulfur compound sold for joint pain, not FDA approved, with weak evidence and few known risks. It is often mixed with glucosamine and chondroitin, which have their own blood thinner concerns.", "dim": "DIM is a broccoli-derived hormone-balance supplement, not FDA approved. It changes estrogen metabolism and can lower levels of some medicines, including tamoxifen's active forms in a small study.", "calcium-d-glucarate": "Calcium D-glucarate is a detox and hormone-balance supplement, not FDA approved, with mostly animal evidence. It is often sold in stacks with DIM, which have more important interactions.", "chlorella": "Chlorella is a green algae supplement, not FDA approved, sold for nutrition and detox. Its vitamin K can blunt warfarin, and poorly processed products have caused sun-triggered rashes.", "spirulina": "Spirulina is a blue-green algae protein powder, not FDA approved. Contaminated products can carry liver toxins, it contains phenylalanine, and it is not a reliable B12 source.", "sea-moss": "Sea moss is a red seaweed gel sold as a superfood, not FDA approved and almost untested in people. Its variable iodine can push thyroid function up or down.", "kelp-iodine": "Kelp and iodine supplements are not FDA approved and can push the thyroid either way, depending on the person. Palpitations, tremor or weight loss in a kelp user can reflect iodine-triggered hyperthyroidism.", "bee-pollen": "Bee pollen is a bee product sold for energy and allergies, not FDA approved. It can trigger anaphylaxis, and some weight-loss capsules labeled bee pollen contained hidden drugs.", "royal-jelly": "Royal jelly is a bee secretion sold as a tonic, not FDA approved. In people with asthma or allergies it has caused severe asthma attacks, anaphylaxis and deaths.", "glucosamine": "Glucosamine is a joint supplement, not FDA approved, with weak evidence for arthritis pain. In warfarin users it has been linked to a rising INR and bleeding.", "chondroitin": "Chondroitin is a cartilage-derived joint supplement, not FDA approved, with weak evidence. With glucosamine it has been linked to a rising INR and bleeding in warfarin users.", "oral-hyaluronic-acid": "Oral hyaluronic acid is a beauty and joint supplement, not FDA approved, with small studies and few known risks. It is a different product from hyaluronic acid knee injections and skin fillers.", "boron": "Boron is a trace mineral sold for bones and hormones, not FDA approved, with limited evidence. Swallowed boric acid is a poison that causes vomiting, diarrhea and a red peeling rash.", "shilajit": "Shilajit is an Ayurvedic mountain resin sold for energy and testosterone, not FDA approved. Contaminated products can contain lead, arsenic or mercury.", "deer-antler-velvet": "Deer antler velvet is an animal supplement sold for muscle and recovery with IGF-1 claims, not FDA approved. The claims are unproven, and IGF-1 is banned in sport.", "lithium-orotate": "Lithium orotate is an online mood supplement that is still lithium, not FDA approved. Tremor, vomiting and confusion, especially after starting an NSAID or water pill, can mean lithium toxicity.", "colloidal-silver": "Colloidal silver is silver water sold as a cure-all, not FDA approved and without proven benefit. Long-term use permanently turns the skin blue-gray, which can look like cyanosis.", "nad-iv": "NAD+ is an energy coenzyme sold as IV drips and pills for anti-aging, not FDA approved and unproven in people. Chest tightness and nausea during infusions are common, but chest symptoms still need full assessment."});
DB.DEEP = DB.DEEP || {}; Object.assign(DB.DEEP, {"rhodiola": {"origin": "Rhodiola rosea grows on cold, rocky slopes across the Arctic, Scandinavia, the Alps and the mountains of Siberia and Central Asia. Its thick rootstock smells faintly of roses when cut, which gave rise to the names roseroot and golden root, and Linnaeus used the name Rhodiola rosea in the eighteenth century. Folk use in Scandinavia, Russia and among Siberian peoples centered on endurance, cold tolerance and fatigue. The plant entered modern pharmacology through Soviet research in the mid twentieth century, when scientists studying so-called adaptogens, substances claimed to raise nonspecific resistance to stress, screened many plants for effects on work capacity and fatigue. Much of that early literature was published in Russian and is hard to evaluate by current standards. In Western markets rhodiola became a popular supplement for burnout, focus and exercise performance in the 2000s. The European Medicines Agency herbal committee recognized it only as a traditional herbal medicine for temporary relief of stress symptoms such as fatigue, a category based on long-standing use rather than proof from trials. In the United States it is a dietary supplement without any FDA-recognized indication.", "timeline": [["1700s", "Linnaeus uses the name Rhodiola rosea for roseroot."], ["Mid 1900s", "Soviet researchers study rhodiola among plants described as adaptogens."], ["2000s", "Rhodiola extracts become popular Western supplements for fatigue and stress."], ["2010s", "The EMA herbal committee publishes a monograph recognizing traditional use for temporary relief of stress symptoms."], ["2010s to 2020s", "Systematic reviews describe small trials with mixed results and frequent quality concerns."]], "cell": "The rhizome contains several groups of active candidates. Salidroside is a glycoside of tyrosol and is found in many Rhodiola species, while rosavin, rosarin and rosin are cinnamyl alcohol glycosides that are characteristic of Rhodiola rosea and serve as its fingerprint. Laboratory and animal studies report modulation of the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis, with blunting of stress-induced cortisol changes; effects on stress-activated proteins such as heat shock proteins and the kinase JNK; inhibition of monoamine oxidase A and B in vitro; and antioxidant and mitochondrial effects that have been linked to fatigue resistance in animals. Extracts also inhibit some cytochrome P450 enzymes and P-glycoprotein in laboratory systems. None of these actions has been tied firmly to a clinical benefit in humans, and many effects were seen at concentrations that may not be reached after oral use.", "body": [["Brain", "Proposed effects on monoamines and stress signaling; activation, insomnia or irritability reported in some users."], ["Adrenal axis", "Animal and small human studies suggest blunting of stress-related cortisol changes; clinical meaning unclear."], ["Skeletal muscle", "Studied for endurance and recovery with inconsistent results."], ["Liver", "Site of conjugation of salidroside and its aglycone tyrosol; no strong liver injury signal is established."], ["Mouth and salivary glands", "Dry mouth is among the more commonly reported side effects."]], "journey": "Human pharmacokinetic data are limited. After oral intake, glycosides such as salidroside and rosavin are partly absorbed intact and partly hydrolyzed by gut bacteria to their aglycones, tyrosol and cinnamyl alcohol. Salidroside appears in plasma relatively quickly in limited studies, and tyrosol is conjugated in the liver and excreted in urine. Rosavin bioavailability appears low. Because products differ in plant part, species and extraction method, the amount of each compound reaching the bloodstream is highly variable, which makes it hard to compare trials or predict interactions.", "sidefx": "Short trials generally report few adverse effects. The ones described most often are dizziness, dry mouth, insomnia, irritability and nausea. Because of its proposed monoamine effects, rhodiola is used with caution in bipolar disorder, where activation or mood elevation is a theoretical concern, and alongside antidepressants or MAOIs. Allergic reactions are possible but uncommon. Long-term safety has not been well studied, and safety in pregnancy, breastfeeding and children has not been established.", "fun": "When freshly cut, the yellowish root of Rhodiola rosea gives off a scent like roses, which is why a cold-climate succulent with no relation to roses ended up named after them.", "src": ["NIH NCCIH, Rhodiola fact sheet", "EMA HMPC, Community herbal monograph on Rhodiola rosea L., rhizoma et radix", "Panossian, Wikman and Sarris 2010, Phytomedicine, review of Rhodiola rosea", "StatPearls, Herbal supplements overview"]}, "salidroside": {"origin": "Salidroside was identified as a constituent of Rhodiola species during the twentieth-century chemical study of the genus, much of it carried out by Soviet and later Russian and Chinese researchers interested in plants labeled adaptogens. Chemically it is a simple molecule: tyrosol, a phenylethanol also found in olive oil, attached to a glucose sugar. Because it was among the first rhodiola constituents to show activity in animal fatigue models, it became a favored candidate for the active ingredient. Over time, analysts realized that salidroside is present in many Rhodiola species and even in some unrelated plants, while rosavins are specific to Rhodiola rosea, so modern quality testing usually measures both. Wild Rhodiola has been heavily harvested in parts of Asia, and that pressure encouraged production of salidroside by chemical synthesis, plant cell culture and engineered microbes. Salidroside has been studied extensively in cells and animals for brain, heart and metabolic protection. Human data remain thin, and the compound has no FDA-recognized use.", "timeline": [["Mid 1900s", "Chemical studies of Rhodiola species identify salidroside among their glycosides."], ["Late 1900s", "Salidroside becomes a common standardization marker for rhodiola extracts."], ["2000s", "Analysts emphasize pairing salidroside with rosavins to confirm Rhodiola rosea identity."], ["2010s", "Biotechnological production of salidroside in engineered microbes is reported."]], "cell": "In laboratory and animal models salidroside reduces markers of oxidative stress, partly by influencing the Nrf2 antioxidant response, and it activates AMP-activated protein kinase, a cellular energy sensor that also responds to exercise and to metformin. It reduces activity of inflammatory pathways such as NF-kappa-B in models, protects neurons and heart muscle cells in injury models, and has been studied in hypoxia models because Rhodiola grows at altitude. Many effects appear at concentrations that may exceed those reached with oral supplements, and isolated salidroside may not reproduce the effects of a whole extract. No specific receptor has been identified.", "body": [["Brain", "Neuroprotective effects in animal models; clinical meaning unknown."], ["Heart", "Protection in animal ischemia models; no proven human effect."], ["Liver", "Conjugation of tyrosol after hydrolysis."], ["Kidneys", "Excretion of tyrosol conjugates in urine."], ["Gut", "Bacterial and enzymatic hydrolysis releases tyrosol."]], "journey": "Salidroside is water soluble and is partly absorbed intact after oral intake, with peak levels reported relatively quickly in limited studies. It is also hydrolyzed by intestinal enzymes and gut bacteria to tyrosol, which is absorbed and then conjugated in the liver with glucuronic acid or sulfate. Some tyrosol may be converted to hydroxytyrosol. The conjugates are excreted mainly in urine. Half-life appears short, but human data are limited and differ between studies and products.", "sidefx": "Isolated salidroside has not been studied enough in humans to define a side effect profile. Reports from whole rhodiola products include dry mouth, dizziness, insomnia and irritability. The same theoretical cautions as rhodiola apply to bipolar disorder and antidepressant use. Safety in pregnancy, breastfeeding and children has not been established.", "fun": "Salidroside can now be made by engineered yeast and bacteria, an approach partly motivated by overharvesting of wild Rhodiola plants on high mountain slopes.", "src": ["NIH NCCIH, Rhodiola fact sheet", "PubChem, Salidroside compound record", "Panossian, Wikman and Sarris 2010, Phytomedicine, review of Rhodiola rosea"]}, "rosavin": {"origin": "Rosavins were characterized during Soviet-era chemical research on Rhodiola rosea, when investigators sought the compounds behind the plant's reputation for fighting fatigue. Three closely related glycosides, rosavin, rosarin and rosin, were identified; all are built from cinnamyl alcohol attached to sugars, and the name rosavin echoes the species name rosea. What made them important was not proven potency but specificity. Salidroside, the other headline compound, occurs in many Rhodiola species, while rosavins are characteristic of Rhodiola rosea. That made rosavins useful to authenticate products, and many commercial extracts came to be standardized to both rosavins and salidroside in a fixed ratio said to resemble the natural root. As demand grew and wild Rhodiola rosea became scarcer, cheaper species and adulterated products appeared, and analytical studies in the 2010s found that a meaningful share of products labeled as Rhodiola rosea lacked rosavins or contained them at inconsistent levels. Rosavins therefore sit at the center of a supplement quality story more than a pharmacology one, and they have no FDA-recognized use.", "timeline": [["Mid 1900s", "Soviet researchers characterize rosavin, rosarin and rosin in Rhodiola rosea."], ["Late 1900s", "Extracts standardized to rosavins and salidroside become commercial."], ["2010s", "Market surveys report adulteration and inconsistent rosavin content in rhodiola products."], ["2010s", "EMA herbal committee documents Rhodiola rosea traditional use."]], "cell": "The cellular pharmacology of rosavins is poorly defined. In animal studies, isolated rosavin often shows little activity on its own, and some work suggests rosavins add to effects only in combination with salidroside or other root constituents. Proposed actions borrowed from whole-extract studies include modulation of stress signaling and antioxidant effects. The likely metabolic fate involves cleavage of the sugar groups to release cinnamyl alcohol, which can be oxidized to cinnamaldehyde and cinnamic acid. No receptor target is known, and laboratory effects of whole extracts on CYP enzymes cannot be assigned to rosavins specifically.", "body": [["Gut", "Limited absorption; hydrolysis of sugar groups is proposed."], ["Liver", "Proposed oxidation of cinnamyl alcohol to cinnamic acid."], ["Brain", "Effects attributed to whole rhodiola extracts; rosavins alone not shown to act."], ["Kidneys", "Likely route of excretion for metabolites (limited data)."]], "journey": "Absorption of intact rosavins after oral use appears low in animal studies. The glycoside bonds may be cleaved by gut bacteria and intestinal enzymes, releasing cinnamyl alcohol, which is then oxidized in the liver and conjugated, with metabolites excreted mainly in urine. Human pharmacokinetic studies are lacking, so onset, peak and half-life are not established.", "sidefx": "No side effects specific to rosavins are documented. Reports from Rhodiola rosea products include dry mouth, dizziness, insomnia and irritability, and the theoretical cautions for bipolar disorder and antidepressant or MAOI use apply. Safety in pregnancy, breastfeeding and children is not established.", "fun": "Rosavins are more useful to a quality-control laboratory than to pharmacologists: they are one of the main chemical fingerprints used to catch rhodiola supplements made from the wrong species.", "src": ["NIH NCCIH, Rhodiola fact sheet", "EMA HMPC, Assessment report on Rhodiola rosea L., rhizoma et radix", "Panossian, Wikman and Sarris 2010, Phytomedicine, review of Rhodiola rosea", "Booker and colleagues 2016, Phytomedicine, quality of commercial Rhodiola products"]}, "ashwagandha": {"origin": "Withania somnifera is a small woody shrub in the nightshade family that grows in dry regions of India, the Middle East and Africa. In Ayurveda it is classed as a rasayana, a rejuvenating tonic, and it appears in classical Sanskrit medical texts. Its common name combines the Sanskrit words for horse and smell, which is usually explained as a reference to the odor of the fresh root and to the strength it was believed to give; the species name somnifera means sleep-inducing. Western chemists became interested in the plant in the twentieth century, and in the 1960s Israeli researchers described the structure of withaferin A, the first well-characterized withanolide. For decades ashwagandha was a niche Ayurvedic product, but from the 2010s it surged into mainstream wellness markets, promoted on social media for stress, sleep, testosterone and athletic performance, with branded standardized root extracts funding many small trials. As use grew, so did safety reports: case series from Iceland, the United States and elsewhere described cholestatic liver injury, LiverTox added the herb, and thyroid effects were reported. Some European regulators, notably in Denmark, moved to restrict it. In the United States it remains a dietary supplement without an FDA-recognized indication.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Ashwagandha is described as a rejuvenating tonic in classical Ayurvedic texts."], ["1960s", "Israeli chemists describe the structure of withaferin A, a key withanolide."], ["2010s", "Ashwagandha becomes a mainstream wellness supplement in Western markets."], ["Late 2010s to 2020s", "Case series of ashwagandha-associated liver injury are published and LiverTox covers the herb."], ["2020s", "Danish authorities move to prohibit ashwagandha in food supplements; other countries review safety."]], "cell": "The main bioactive group is the withanolides, steroidal lactones built on an ergostane skeleton. Withaferin A, more abundant in the leaves, is highly reactive and in laboratory studies inhibits NF-kappa-B signaling, binds the intermediate filament protein vimentin and affects heat shock protein 90, which explains much of the anti-inflammatory and anticancer cell research. Withanolide A and withanosides, more prominent in root extracts, have been studied for nerve cell growth in animal models. Root extracts show GABA-mimetic activity in animal studies, consistent with sedation and with the species name, and small human trials report lower morning cortisol, suggesting effects on the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis. Trials and case reports describe increases in thyroid hormone levels, possibly from stimulation of the thyroid axis, and some trials report higher testosterone in men. Liver injury appears idiosyncratic, with a cholestatic or mixed pattern; the mechanism is unknown and has not been linked to a specific constituent.", "body": [["Brain", "GABA-related calming and sedation; small trials suggest modest effects on stress and sleep."], ["Adrenal axis", "Lower cortisol reported in small trials."], ["Thyroid", "Thyroid hormone increases and rare thyrotoxicosis reported."], ["Liver", "Rare idiosyncratic cholestatic or mixed liver injury with jaundice and itching."], ["Reproductive system", "Small trials report changes in testosterone and sperm measures; traditionally avoided in pregnancy because of reported miscarriage risk."]], "journey": "Human pharmacokinetic data are limited. Withanolides are moderately lipophilic and are absorbed from the gut to varying degrees; withanolide A appears in plasma in small amounts after oral extracts, while glycosylated withanosides are poorly absorbed intact. Withaferin A showed low and variable oral bioavailability in animal studies. Metabolism occurs in the liver, but the specific enzymes and the excretion route in humans are not well characterized. The large differences between root extracts, leaf extracts and blends mean that exposure varies widely between products.", "sidefx": "The most common complaints are drowsiness, stomach upset, nausea and loose stools. The most serious reported adverse effect is liver injury: LiverTox and published case series describe jaundice, itching and fatigue developing within weeks to a few months of starting a product, usually with a cholestatic or mixed pattern, and generally resolving after the product is stopped, though prolonged courses occur. Thyroid hormone elevations and rare thyrotoxicosis have been reported. Additive sedation with alcohol, benzodiazepines and other depressants is a practical risk, especially in older adults. It is traditionally regarded as unsafe in pregnancy.", "fun": "The plant's orange-red berries sit inside a papery husk like a tiny lantern, much like its nightshade relative the tomatillo, which is why it is also called winter cherry.", "src": ["NIH LiverTox, Ashwagandha chapter", "NIH Office of Dietary Supplements, Ashwagandha fact sheet for health professionals", "NIH NCCIH, Ashwagandha fact sheet", "Bjornsson and colleagues 2020, Liver International, liver injury associated with ashwagandha"]}, "withanolides": {"origin": "Withanolides were named after Withania somnifera, ashwagandha, the plant in which they were first characterized. In 1965 the Israeli chemists David Lavie, Erwin Glotter and Yoel Shvo reported the structure of withaferin A, establishing a new class of steroidal lactones built on a 28-carbon ergostane skeleton with a lactone ring in the side chain. Hundreds of related compounds have since been identified, not only in ashwagandha but in other members of the nightshade family, such as Physalis, the genus of ground cherries and tomatillos. Withaferin A attracted particular attention as a potent, reactive molecule in cancer cell research, while withanolide A and the glycosylated withanosides, which predominate in roots, were studied for nerve growth effects in animal models. As ashwagandha supplements boomed, withanolide percentage became the main commercial standardization metric. That created problems: different analytical methods give different numbers, and leaf extracts can show high withanolide percentages with a profile richer in withaferin A than traditional root preparations. Withanolides have no FDA-recognized use.", "timeline": [["1965", "Lavie, Glotter and Shvo report the structure of withaferin A."], ["1970s to 1990s", "Many related withanolides are identified in ashwagandha and other nightshades."], ["2000s", "Withaferin A becomes a widely studied compound in cancer cell research."], ["2010s", "Withanolide percentage becomes the main standardization claim on ashwagandha labels."]], "cell": "Withaferin A carries an unsaturated ketone and an epoxide that can react with cysteine residues on proteins, which helps explain its broad laboratory activity. It inhibits activation of NF-kappa-B, binds the cytoskeletal protein vimentin, interferes with heat shock protein 90 chaperone function and inhibits the proteasome, effects that are anti-inflammatory and cytotoxic in cell studies. Withanolide A and withanoside IV promote neurite outgrowth in animal models. Whole root extracts show GABA-mimetic activity in animal studies, which fits with sedation in humans, though the specific constituent responsible is not settled. These laboratory actions often require concentrations above those reached after oral supplements.", "body": [["Brain", "GABA-related sedation and nerve growth effects in animal models."], ["Immune system", "Anti-inflammatory effects in laboratory studies."], ["Liver", "Liver metabolism; ashwagandha products are linked to rare cholestatic liver injury."], ["Thyroid", "Ashwagandha products are linked to thyroid hormone increases."]], "journey": "Absorption of withanolides varies by structure. Less polar aglycones such as withanolide A and withaferin A are absorbed to a limited extent, with withanolide A peaking relatively quickly in small studies, while glycosides are poorly absorbed intact and may be hydrolyzed by gut bacteria. Withaferin A has low and variable oral bioavailability in animal studies. Liver metabolism is likely, but the enzymes and the routes of excretion in humans are not well defined.", "sidefx": "No adverse effect profile is defined for withanolides as isolated compounds in humans. Risks are those described for ashwagandha products: drowsiness, stomach upset, rare cholestatic or mixed liver injury, thyroid hormone elevations, additive sedation with other depressants and avoidance in pregnancy. Leaf-rich products deliver more withaferin A, and some industry and regulatory groups have raised concern about leaf use in supplements.", "fun": "Withanolides are not unique to ashwagandha: the husk-wrapped ground cherries and tomatillos of the genus Physalis make a related family of nightshade steroid lactones.", "src": ["NIH LiverTox, Ashwagandha chapter", "NIH Office of Dietary Supplements, Ashwagandha fact sheet", "Lavie, Glotter and Shvo 1965, Journal of the Chemical Society, structure of withaferin A", "PubChem, Withaferin A compound record"]}, "panax-ginseng": {"origin": "Panax ginseng grows wild in the cool mountain forests of northeastern China, Korea and the Russian Far East, and has been used in Chinese medicine for at least two thousand years; it is listed among superior tonic herbs in the classical materia medica attributed to Shennong. The forked root, sometimes shaped like a human figure, contributed to its reputation, and wild roots became so valuable that harvesting was controlled by imperial courts and the plant was driven close to extinction in the wild. Cultivation in Korea and China grew to supply the trade, and Korean red ginseng, made by steaming and drying the root, became a major export. European interest followed reports by Jesuit missionaries; a description by Pierre Jartoux in the early eighteenth century led directly to the discovery of American ginseng in Canada. The Russian botanist Carl Anton Meyer gave the plant the scientific name Panax ginseng in the nineteenth century, choosing a genus name from the Greek for all-healing. Twentieth-century chemistry identified the ginsenosides, and Soviet researchers included ginseng among the original adaptogens. Today ginseng appears in supplements and energy drinks worldwide, without any FDA-recognized indication.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Ginseng is listed as a superior tonic in the classical Chinese materia medica."], ["Early 1700s", "Jesuit Pierre Jartoux describes ginseng to Europeans, prompting a search for it in North America."], ["1800s", "Carl Anton Meyer names the species Panax ginseng."], ["1960s", "Japanese chemists characterize ginsenoside structures."], ["1979", "A JAMA report describes a ginseng abuse syndrome, later criticized for weak methods."]], "cell": "Ginsenosides are triterpene saponins on a dammarane skeleton, divided into protopanaxadiol types such as Rb1 and Rg3 and protopanaxatriol types such as Rg1 and Re. Their actions overlap with steroids in shape, and some bind glucocorticoid and estrogen receptors weakly in laboratory studies. Reported effects include increased endothelial nitric oxide synthase activity, which may underlie blood pressure and erectile effects; increased glucose uptake and improved insulin sensitivity in models; inhibition of platelet aggregation in vitro; modulation of the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis; and effects on acetylcholine and monoamine signaling. Individual ginsenosides sometimes act in opposite directions, which may explain why whole ginseng has inconsistent clinical effects. Gut bacteria convert many ginsenosides into compound K and related metabolites, which may be the main active forms in the body.", "body": [["Pancreas and muscle", "Modest lowering of blood glucose in some studies; additive with diabetes medicines."], ["Brain", "Stimulant-like effects such as insomnia and nervousness; mania reported with MAOIs."], ["Blood vessels", "Nitric oxide effects; blood pressure changes in either direction are reported."], ["Blood", "Antiplatelet effects in laboratory studies; reduced warfarin INR in case reports."], ["Reproductive tissue", "Estrogen-like effects such as breast tenderness and postmenopausal bleeding in case reports."]], "journey": "Intact ginsenosides are poorly absorbed because they are large, sugar-bearing molecules. In the colon, gut bacteria strip off sugar groups step by step, turning protopanaxadiol ginsenosides such as Rb1 into compound K, which is absorbed and reaches peak blood levels several hours after intake. The amount of compound K formed differs greatly between people depending on their gut bacteria. Absorbed metabolites are processed in the liver and excreted mainly in bile, with smaller amounts in urine. Steaming to make red ginseng converts some ginsenosides before ingestion, changing the profile.", "sidefx": "Most commonly reported are insomnia, nervousness, headache and stomach upset. Hypoglycemia can occur with diabetes medicines. Case reports describe mania or hypomania, particularly with antidepressants or MAOIs, estrogen-like effects such as breast tenderness and vaginal bleeding in postmenopausal women. A 1979 report described a ginseng abuse syndrome of hypertension, nervousness and insomnia in heavy users, though the study was uncontrolled. Changes in warfarin response and liver injury when combined with some drugs are also reported.", "fun": "In the eighteenth century wild ginseng in Manchuria was so valuable that the Qing court controlled who could harvest it, and roots shaped like a human body were especially prized.", "src": ["NIH NCCIH, Asian Ginseng fact sheet", "NIH LiverTox, Ginseng chapter", "Siegel 1979, JAMA, Ginseng abuse syndrome", "Kiefer and Pantuso 2003, American Family Physician, Panax ginseng review"]}, "ginsenosides": {"origin": "Chemists long suspected that ginseng's activity lay in its saponins, soap-like glycosides that foam in water, but separating and identifying them was difficult. In the 1960s Japanese researchers led by Shoji Shibata worked out the structures of the main ginseng saponins and named them ginsenosides, labeling them by their positions on chromatography plates, which is why they carry letter-and-number names such as Rb1 and Rg1. Over a hundred ginsenosides have since been described across Panax species. A key later insight came from studies of gut bacteria: most ginsenosides are barely absorbed, and intestinal microbes convert them into smaller metabolites such as compound K, which may be the main active forms. That helped explain why people respond so differently to the same product. Processing also matters; steaming root to make red ginseng creates ginsenosides like Rg3 and Rh2 that are scarce in raw root. Ginsenosides became the standard measure of ginseng product strength, and individual compounds have been studied as drug candidates, though none has an FDA-recognized indication.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Shibata and colleagues in Japan characterize and name the ginsenosides."], ["1980s to 1990s", "Gut bacterial conversion of ginsenosides to compound K is described."], ["1990s", "Ginsenoside content becomes a standard for ginseng extract quality."], ["2000s", "Steaming-derived ginsenosides such as Rg3 attract research interest in red ginseng."]], "cell": "Ginsenosides share a four-ring dammarane steroid-like skeleton with sugar chains attached at different positions. Protopanaxadiol types and protopanaxatriol types often behave differently: for example, Rg1 has been described as more stimulating and Rb1 as more calming in animal studies. Reported cellular actions include activation of endothelial nitric oxide synthase, effects on GLUT4 glucose transport and AMPK, weak agonism at glucocorticoid and estrogen receptors, inhibition of platelet aggregation through effects on thromboxane and cyclic nucleotides, and modulation of calcium and sodium channels. Compound K acts on several of these pathways in laboratory models. The diversity and sometimes opposing nature of these actions makes whole-product effects hard to predict.", "body": [["Gut", "Bacterial conversion to compound K and related metabolites."], ["Blood vessels", "Nitric oxide effects in laboratory studies."], ["Metabolism", "Glucose uptake and insulin sensitivity effects."], ["Blood", "Antiplatelet effects in laboratory studies."], ["Liver", "Metabolism and biliary excretion."]], "journey": "Intact ginsenosides have very low oral bioavailability. Protopanaxadiol types are deglycosylated by colonic bacteria to compound K, which is absorbed and reaches peak levels several hours after intake; protopanaxatriol types are converted to other metabolites such as protopanaxatriol itself. Absorbed compounds undergo liver metabolism, are partly bound to plasma proteins, and are mainly excreted in bile. Some protopanaxadiol ginsenosides persist for a long time in animal plasma. The amount formed varies with each person's gut bacteria.", "sidefx": "No independent side effect profile is defined for ginsenosides apart from ginseng products. Risks are those described for ginseng: insomnia, nervousness, headache, hypoglycemia with diabetes medicines, changes in warfarin response, mania with MAOIs or antidepressants in case reports, and estrogen-like effects. Safety in pregnancy is not established, and a ginsenoside showed embryo effects in animal studies.", "fun": "Ginsenoside names such as Rb1 and Rg1 come from where each compound ran on a chromatography plate, not from any description of what it does.", "src": ["NIH NCCIH, Asian Ginseng fact sheet", "NIH NCCIH, American Ginseng fact sheet", "Christensen 2009, Advances in Food and Nutrition Research, ginsenosides chemistry review", "PubChem, Ginsenoside Rb1 compound record"]}, "american-ginseng": {"origin": "American ginseng grows wild in shady hardwood forests of eastern North America, from southern Canada to the Appalachians. Indigenous peoples of the region used the root medicinally long before European contact. Its discovery by Europeans followed directly from Asia: after the Jesuit missionary Pierre Jartoux described Chinese ginseng and suggested that a similar plant might grow in comparable forests of Canada, the Jesuit Joseph-Francois Lafitau found it near Montreal in the early eighteenth century. A large export trade to China followed, and American ginseng became one of the colonies' notable exports; frontiersmen including Daniel Boone dug and traded it. Overharvesting depleted wild stands, and wild American ginseng is now listed in Appendix II of the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species, which regulates its export. Cultivation, especially in Wisconsin and Ontario, supplies most of the market today, much of it still exported to Asia, where it is valued as a cooling tonic distinct from Asian ginseng. Modern research has focused on blood sugar, colds and warfarin interaction. It has no FDA-recognized indication.", "timeline": [["Early 1700s", "Lafitau identifies American ginseng near Montreal after Jartoux's description of Asian ginseng."], ["1700s to 1800s", "A major ginseng export trade from North America to China develops."], ["1975", "Wild American ginseng is listed under CITES Appendix II."], ["2000", "Small trials report reduced post-meal glucose with American ginseng."], ["2004", "A randomized trial shows American ginseng reduces warfarin effect."]], "cell": "American ginseng contains ginsenosides with a profile weighted toward protopanaxadiol types such as Rb1, and it contains little or none of the ginsenoside Rf found in Asian ginseng, a difference used to tell the species apart. Proposed glucose effects include slowing carbohydrate absorption and improving insulin sensitivity and secretion in models. Root polysaccharides activate macrophages and other immune cells in laboratory studies, which is the basis of the proprietary cold-prevention extract. The warfarin interaction mechanism is uncertain; proposed explanations include induction of drug-metabolizing enzymes or effects on clotting pathways, but the clinical effect was shown in a controlled trial.", "body": [["Pancreas and gut", "Modest reduction of post-meal glucose rises in small trials."], ["Blood", "Reduced warfarin effect in a randomized trial."], ["Immune system", "Polysaccharides activate immune cells in laboratory studies."], ["Brain", "Headache and insomnia are reported side effects."]], "journey": "As with other Panax species, ginsenosides are poorly absorbed intact and are converted by gut bacteria into metabolites such as compound K that are absorbed over several hours. Liver metabolism and biliary excretion follow. Post-meal glucose effects in small studies appeared when the root was given before a meal. Absorption and conversion differ between people depending on gut bacteria, and products differ in ginsenoside content and polysaccharide content.", "sidefx": "Reported side effects include headache, insomnia and stomach upset. The clinically important risks are reduced warfarin effect, which could lower protection against clots, and additive glucose lowering with insulin and other diabetes medicines. Ginseng in general has been associated with estrogen-like effects and with mania when combined with MAOIs in case reports. Safety in pregnancy, breastfeeding and children is not established.", "fun": "Much of the American ginseng grown in Wisconsin is exported to Asia, continuing a trade that began in the 1700s when roots dug in North American forests were shipped to China.", "src": ["NIH NCCIH, American Ginseng fact sheet", "Yuan and colleagues 2004, Annals of Internal Medicine, Brief communication: American ginseng reduces warfarin's effect in healthy patients", "Vuksan and colleagues 2000, Archives of Internal Medicine, American ginseng and postprandial glycemia", "CITES Appendices, Panax quinquefolius"]}, "eleuthero": {"origin": "Eleutherococcus senticosus is a spiny shrub of the forests of the Russian Far East, northeastern China, Korea and Japan. It had a modest place in traditional Chinese medicine, but its modern fame comes from Soviet research. In the 1950s and 1960s the pharmacologist Israel Brekhman and colleagues in Vladivostok, building on the adaptogen concept introduced by Nikolai Lazarev, studied eleuthero as an abundant, cheap alternative to scarce Panax ginseng. Soviet publications reported benefits for workers, athletes and others under physical stress, and eleuthero extracts were widely used in the Soviet Union. These studies were often not randomized or blinded and were published mainly in Russian, so their findings are hard to judge. Eleuthero reached Western markets as Siberian ginseng, a name that implied a link to Panax. In the United States, a 2002 farm law limited the term ginseng on labels to Panax species, and the product is now usually sold as eleuthero. Modern trials have shown inconsistent effects on fatigue and performance, and it has no FDA-recognized indication.", "timeline": [["1950s to 1960s", "Brekhman and colleagues study eleuthero as an adaptogen in the Soviet Union."], ["1970s to 1980s", "Eleuthero extracts are widely used in the Soviet Union and enter Western markets as Siberian ginseng."], ["1996", "A Canadian case report describes an elevated digoxin level in a patient using Siberian ginseng."], ["2002", "A US farm law limits the label term ginseng to Panax species."], ["2000s", "Controlled trials show inconsistent effects on fatigue and endurance."]], "cell": "The term eleutherosides covers a chemically mixed group rather than one family. Eleutheroside B is syringin, a phenylpropanoid glycoside, and eleutheroside E is a lignan glycoside; others are coumarins and sterol glycosides. Laboratory and animal studies report modulation of the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal stress response, antioxidant activity and immune stimulation by root polysaccharides, which activate lymphocytes and macrophages. Unlike Panax ginseng, eleuthero contains no ginsenosides, so ginseng data cannot be transferred to it. The digoxin case remains unresolved: possibilities include cross-reactivity of eleuthero constituents with digoxin immunoassays and contamination with Periploca sepium, whose cardiac glycosides can register as digoxin-like substances.", "body": [["Adrenal axis", "Stress hormone modulation in animal studies."], ["Immune system", "Polysaccharides stimulate immune cells in laboratory studies."], ["Heart and circulation", "Raised blood pressure and palpitations reported in some users; digoxin level case report."], ["Brain", "Insomnia and nervousness reported."]], "journey": "Human pharmacokinetic data are very limited. Glycosides such as syringin and eleutheroside E are likely partly hydrolyzed by gut bacteria before absorption, and absorbed aglycones are metabolized in the liver and excreted in urine and bile. Onset, peak and half-life are not established, and product content differs widely.", "sidefx": "Reported side effects include insomnia, headache, nervousness and stomach upset, with some reports of raised blood pressure. The digoxin case report highlights two separate risks: interference with lab measurements and contamination of products with plants that contain cardiac glycosides. Safety in pregnancy, breastfeeding and children is not established.", "fun": "Eleuthero is a cousin of ginseng in the same plant family, but it was given the name Siberian ginseng largely for marketing, and US law later stepped in to reserve the word ginseng for Panax plants.", "src": ["NIH NCCIH, Eleuthero fact sheet", "McRae 1996, CMAJ, Elevated serum digoxin levels in a patient taking digoxin and Siberian ginseng", "Panossian and Wikman 2010, Pharmaceuticals, adaptogen review", "EMA HMPC, Community herbal monograph on Eleutherococcus senticosus"]}, "schisandra": {"origin": "Schisandra chinensis is a woody climbing vine of northeastern China, Korea and the Russian Far East, bearing clusters of bright red berries. In traditional Chinese medicine the dried fruit, wu wei zi, is said to have all five flavors at once and has long been used as a tonic and for cough, sweating and insomnia. Hunters of the Nanai people of the Russian Far East reportedly used the berries against fatigue, which drew the attention of Soviet researchers in the mid twentieth century, and schisandra joined ginseng, eleuthero and rhodiola among the plants studied as adaptogens. Chinese researchers in the 1970s investigated schisandra for hepatitis because preparations seemed to lower liver enzymes, and work on its lignans contributed to the development of bifendate, a synthetic compound used in China to lower raised liver enzymes. The most clinically important modern finding is a drug interaction: Schisandra extracts inhibit CYP3A4 and P-glycoprotein, and Chinese clinicians have used a schisandra extract preparation to raise tacrolimus levels and reduce the amount of tacrolimus needed. In the United States, schisandra is a dietary supplement without an FDA-recognized indication.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Wu wei zi is recorded in the classical Chinese materia medica."], ["Mid 1900s", "Soviet researchers study schisandra as an adaptogen."], ["1970s", "Chinese research on schisandra lignans and liver enzymes contributes to development of bifendate."], ["2000s", "Human studies show schisandra extracts raise tacrolimus exposure."], ["2010s", "Schisandra extract preparations are used in China to boost tacrolimus levels in transplant patients."]], "cell": "The characteristic constituents are dibenzocyclooctadiene lignans, a ring structure found mainly in the Schisandraceae family. Several, including schisantherin A, schisandrin B and gomisins, inhibit CYP3A4 both competitively and through mechanism-based inactivation, and they inhibit the efflux pump P-glycoprotein in the gut wall and liver. Together these effects increase oral bioavailability and reduce clearance of drugs such as tacrolimus and midazolam. Animal studies also show that longer exposure can induce some liver enzymes through the pregnane X receptor, so the net effect could in principle vary, but human data have consistently shown increased exposure to CYP3A substrates. In the liver, schisandrin B raises glutathione and protects cells against chemical injury in animal models, which underlies the traditional liver-protective reputation.", "body": [["Gut wall", "Inhibition of CYP3A4 and P-gp raises absorption of substrate drugs."], ["Liver", "CYP3A4 inhibition; antioxidant and protective effects in animal models."], ["Kidneys", "At risk from tacrolimus toxicity when levels rise."], ["Stomach", "Heartburn and decreased appetite are reported side effects."], ["Brain", "Mild sedation and effects on sleep reported in animal studies."]], "journey": "Schisandra lignans are lipophilic and are absorbed from the gut, reaching peak plasma levels within a few hours in limited studies. They are metabolized extensively by CYP3A enzymes, which they also inhibit, through demethylation and hydroxylation, and are excreted mainly in bile and feces. Their inhibition of gut wall CYP3A4 and P-gp means the interaction is often strongest for drugs given by mouth. Human pharmacokinetic data come mostly from studies of standardized extract preparations.", "sidefx": "Reported side effects include heartburn, stomach upset and decreased appetite, and occasionally allergic skin rash. The most serious risk is indirect: raised blood levels of narrow-range drugs such as tacrolimus, cyclosporine or sirolimus, with tremor, headache, high blood pressure, kidney injury and other toxicity. Increased sedation with midazolam and other CYP3A4 substrates is possible. It is traditionally avoided in pregnancy.", "fun": "Schisandra's interaction with tacrolimus is strong enough that Chinese transplant clinicians have used a schisandra extract preparation on purpose to raise tacrolimus levels.", "src": ["Xin and colleagues 2007, British Journal of Clinical Pharmacology, Wuzhi tablet and tacrolimus", "Panossian and Wikman 2008, Journal of Ethnopharmacology, Schisandra chinensis pharmacology review", "NIH NCCIH, Herb-drug interactions"]}, "holy-basil": {"origin": "Holy basil, Ocimum tenuiflorum, is native to the Indian subcontinent and Southeast Asia and is one of the most revered plants in Hinduism, where it is associated with the goddess Tulsi and with Vishnu. Many households keep a tulsi plant in a courtyard shrine, and its leaves are used in worship and in daily teas. In Ayurveda it has been used for colds, cough, fever, digestive complaints and as a general tonic, and it is often grouped among adaptogens in modern marketing. The species name was long given as Ocimum sanctum, meaning sacred basil, and botanists now generally use Ocimum tenuiflorum. Several varieties are recognized, often called Rama, Krishna and Vana tulsi, which differ in leaf color and aroma. Western interest grew as tulsi teas became popular in natural food stores, and small clinical studies, mostly in India, have looked at stress, blood glucose and lipids. Systematic reviews describe the results as encouraging but limited by small size and low quality. Holy basil has no FDA-recognized indication.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Tulsi is revered in Hindu tradition and used in Ayurvedic medicine."], ["1700s", "Linnaeus names basil species in the genus Ocimum, including the sacred basil."], ["1990s to 2000s", "Small trials in India study holy basil for blood glucose and stress."], ["2010s", "Tulsi teas become widely available in Western markets."], ["2017", "A systematic review of human trials describes favorable but low-quality evidence."]], "cell": "The aromatic leaf oil is rich in eugenol, a phenylpropanoid that inhibits cyclooxygenase and platelet aggregation in laboratory studies and has local anesthetic properties. Ursolic and oleanolic acids are triterpenes with anti-inflammatory effects through NF-kappa-B inhibition in models. Rosmarinic acid and flavonoids such as orientin and vicenin act as antioxidants. Animal studies report lowered blood glucose, possibly through effects on insulin secretion and glucose uptake, and blunted stress-induced changes in corticosterone. Animal studies also report reduced sperm counts and antifertility effects, which underlie pregnancy and fertility cautions. Most of this work is preclinical.", "body": [["Pancreas and metabolism", "Possible modest lowering of blood glucose in small studies."], ["Blood", "Eugenol inhibits platelets in laboratory studies."], ["Adrenal axis", "Blunted stress hormone responses in animal studies."], ["Reproductive system", "Antifertility effects in animal studies."], ["Airways", "Traditional use for cough and colds; not established in trials."]], "journey": "Human pharmacokinetic data for holy basil are limited. Eugenol is well absorbed from the gut, rapidly conjugated in the liver with glucuronic acid and sulfate, and excreted in urine within hours. Triterpenes such as ursolic acid are poorly absorbed. The amount of each constituent varies greatly between teas, fresh leaves and concentrated extracts.", "sidefx": "Holy basil tea is widely consumed with few reported side effects; stomach upset and nausea are described. The main clinical concerns are low blood sugar when combined with diabetes medicines and a theoretical additive bleeding risk with anticoagulants or antiplatelet drugs. Animal antifertility data lead to caution in pregnancy and in people trying to conceive. Allergic reactions are uncommon.", "fun": "Eugenol, one of holy basil's main aroma compounds, is the same chemical that gives clove oil its smell and its traditional use for toothache.", "src": ["Jamshidi and Cohen 2017, Evidence-Based Complementary and Alternative Medicine, The clinical efficacy and safety of tulsi in humans", "Cohen 2014, Journal of Ayurveda and Integrative Medicine, Tulsi - Ocimum sanctum: A herb for all reasons", "PubChem, Eugenol compound record"]}, "maca": {"origin": "Maca, Lepidium meyenii, is a small turnip-like plant of the mustard family grown on the high puna grasslands of the central Peruvian Andes, especially the Junin plateau, at around 4,000 meters, where few other crops survive. It has been cultivated in the region for a very long time and was traditionally eaten cooked, roasted or boiled, or dried for storage and made into porridges and drinks. Andean tradition associates maca with fertility in people and livestock, a reputation reinforced by Spanish colonial accounts. Maca remained a local staple until the 1990s, when it was marketed internationally as a superfood and natural libido enhancer, and a production boom followed. Peruvian researchers, notably Gustavo Gonzales and colleagues, ran small trials suggesting improved sexual desire and sperm measures without changes in serum testosterone or estradiol. Demand grew so rapidly that it led to concerns about export of seeds and roots to China and about biopiracy. Maca is a food and dietary supplement in the United States and has no FDA-recognized indication.", "timeline": [["Pre-Columbian era", "Maca is cultivated as a high-altitude food crop in the central Peruvian Andes."], ["1840s", "The species is formally described as Lepidium meyenii."], ["1990s", "Maca is marketed internationally as a superfood and libido supplement."], ["2000s", "Small Peruvian trials report effects on sexual desire without changes in sex hormones."], ["2010", "A systematic review describes limited evidence for maca and sexual function."]], "cell": "Maca contains glucosinolates, sulfur-containing compounds typical of the mustard family. When the root is cut or chewed, or by gut bacteria, they are converted to isothiocyanates, such as benzyl isothiocyanate, which have antioxidant and other effects in laboratory studies. Macamides are fatty acid amides unique to maca that inhibit fatty acid amide hydrolase, the enzyme that breaks down endocannabinoids, in laboratory studies; whether this matters in people is unknown. Sterols, macaenes and alkaloids are also present. Because trials generally show no change in serum testosterone, estradiol or LH, any effect on libido is thought to act centrally or through other pathways. Glucosinolate breakdown products can interfere with iodine uptake by the thyroid when eaten in very large amounts.", "body": [["Brain", "Proposed central effects on sexual desire and mood; mechanism unclear."], ["Reproductive system", "Small trials report changes in sperm measures; no consistent change in sex hormone levels."], ["Thyroid", "Glucosinolates are goitrogenic in large amounts (theoretical)."], ["Gut", "Bloating and stomach upset, especially with raw powder; bacterial conversion of glucosinolates."]], "journey": "Maca has not been studied formally for pharmacokinetics in humans. Glucosinolates are hydrolyzed by the plant enzyme myrosinase or by gut bacteria into isothiocyanates, which are absorbed, conjugated with glutathione in the liver and excreted in urine as mercapturic acids. Macamides are lipophilic and are probably absorbed with dietary fat, but their fate is unknown. Gelatinized maca, which is pre-cooked, has less starch and is easier to digest.", "sidefx": "Maca is generally well tolerated as a food. Stomach upset and bloating are the most common complaints, especially with raw powders. No serious adverse effects are well documented for maca itself. The theoretical cautions are for hormone-sensitive conditions and thyroid disease. A practical risk comes from blended sexual enhancement products, some of which FDA has found to contain undeclared prescription drugs.", "fun": "Maca is one of the highest-altitude crops in the world, growing on cold, windy Andean plateaus where frost can occur on most nights of the year.", "src": ["Shin and colleagues 2010, BMC Complementary and Alternative Medicine, Maca for improving sexual function: a systematic review", "Gonzales 2012, Evidence-Based Complementary and Alternative Medicine, Ethnobiology and ethnopharmacology of Lepidium meyenii", "FDA, Tainted sexual enhancement products"]}, "astragalus": {"origin": "Astragalus root, huang qi, is one of the most widely used tonics in traditional Chinese medicine, recorded in the classical materia medica and traditionally used to strengthen qi, especially the protective energy that guards against illness. The dried yellow root slices are often simmered in chicken or other soups in Chinese households. The genus Astragalus is enormous, with thousands of species worldwide, and only a few are used medicinally; others, called locoweeds or milkvetches in North America, accumulate selenium or produce the alkaloid swainsonine and are notorious for poisoning grazing livestock. Modern research on medicinal astragalus focused first on immune effects of its polysaccharides, then on astragalosides, especially astragaloside IV. In the 2000s a company began marketing cycloastragenol, a compound derived from astragaloside IV, as a telomerase activator for healthy aging, based on laboratory work; human benefit has not been shown. In China, astragalus injections and combination formulas are used in hospitals, but in the United States astragalus is a dietary supplement with no FDA-recognized indication.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Huang qi is recorded as a major tonic in the classical Chinese materia medica."], ["1800s to 1900s", "Toxic North American Astragalus species become known as livestock poisons called locoweeds."], ["1980s to 1990s", "Laboratory studies describe immune effects of astragalus polysaccharides."], ["2000s", "Cycloastragenol is marketed as a telomerase activator."]], "cell": "Astragalus polysaccharides, large chains of sugars, interact with receptors on immune cells such as Toll-like receptor 4 in laboratory studies, activating macrophages to release cytokines and increasing natural killer cell and lymphocyte activity. Astragalosides are cycloartane triterpene saponins; astragaloside IV has anti-inflammatory, antioxidant and anti-fibrotic effects in animal models of heart and kidney injury. Isoflavonoids such as formononetin and calycosin act as antioxidants and weak phytoestrogens. Cycloastragenol increased telomerase activity in cultured cells, but no human outcome data support anti-aging claims. Animal studies suggest a mild diuretic effect, which underlies a theoretical lithium caution.", "body": [["Immune system", "Stimulation of macrophages, NK cells and lymphocytes in laboratory studies."], ["Heart", "Protective effects of astragaloside IV in animal models; human evidence limited."], ["Kidneys", "Studied in Chinese trials for kidney disease, often in combinations; possible mild diuretic effect in animals."], ["Gut", "Stomach upset and loose stools reported."]], "journey": "Oral bioavailability of astragaloside IV is low in animal studies, and large polysaccharides are poorly absorbed intact; they may act partly through immune tissue in the gut wall. Isoflavonoids are absorbed and conjugated in the liver. Human pharmacokinetic data for oral astragalus products are limited, so onset, half-life and excretion are not established.", "sidefx": "Oral astragalus is generally well tolerated in studies, with stomach upset and loose stools reported. Allergic reactions are uncommon. The main concerns are theoretical: opposing effects on immunosuppressants in transplant and autoimmune patients, and possible effects on lithium clearance. Safety in pregnancy and breastfeeding has not been established.", "fun": "The same genus that gives Chinese medicine its prized huang qi root also contains locoweeds, plants that can make grazing horses and cattle stagger and act strangely, which is how they got their name.", "src": ["NIH NCCIH, Astragalus fact sheet", "Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center, About Herbs: Astragalus", "Block and Mead 2003, Alternative Medicine Review, immune system effects of echinacea, ginseng and astragalus"]}, "bacopa": {"origin": "Bacopa monnieri is a small creeping herb of wetlands and marshes across tropical and subtropical regions, including India, Southeast Asia, Africa and the southern United States. In Ayurveda it is known as brahmi, a name linked to Brahman, the cosmic consciousness, or to Brahma, the creator god, and it has long been used as a medhya rasayana, a tonic for intellect and memory, sometimes said to have been used by scholars to memorize long texts. The name brahmi is also applied to gotu kola in some regions, a source of lasting confusion. In the 1960s Indian researchers at the Central Drug Research Institute in Lucknow isolated the bacosides and studied their effects on learning in animals. From the 2000s, randomized controlled trials in Australia and elsewhere tested standardized bacopa extracts in healthy adults and older adults, reporting modest improvements in some memory measures after about three months of use. Meta-analyses describe a small effect with limited evidence quality. Bacopa is also sold as an aquarium plant. In the United States it is a dietary supplement without an FDA-recognized indication.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Brahmi is described in Ayurvedic texts as a tonic for intellect and memory."], ["1960s", "Indian researchers isolate bacosides and study their effects on learning in animals."], ["2000s", "Randomized trials of standardized bacopa extracts in adults report modest memory effects."], ["2014", "A meta-analysis suggests small improvements in some cognitive measures."]], "cell": "Bacosides are dammarane-type triterpenoid saponins; bacoside A is a mixture of several closely related compounds. In animal studies they increase activity of antioxidant enzymes in the brain, reduce lipid peroxidation, and may promote repair of nerve cell connections and synapses, and extracts influence acetylcholine levels and choline acetyltransferase activity, serotonin turnover and GABA signaling. Some studies report reduced beta-amyloid deposits in animal models. These diverse effects form the basis for claims about memory, but the specific mechanism in humans is unknown. Animal data also suggest bacopa can raise thyroid hormone levels and increase gut motility, consistent with the GI side effects seen in people.", "body": [["Brain", "Proposed effects on acetylcholine, serotonin and antioxidant defenses; modest memory effects in small trials."], ["Gut", "Increased bowel motility, cramps and nausea are common."], ["Heart", "Slow heart rate is a theoretical cholinergic concern."], ["Thyroid", "Raised thyroid hormone in animal studies."]], "journey": "Human pharmacokinetic data for bacosides are limited. As saponins they are poorly absorbed intact, and gut bacteria likely convert them to aglycones and further metabolites, which may be the active forms. The delay of several weeks before memory effects appear in trials suggests gradual changes rather than an immediate drug effect. Use with food is often described as reducing stomach upset, though this is based mainly on trial reports rather than formal studies.", "sidefx": "In trials, the most common side effects are nausea, abdominal cramps, increased stool frequency, dry mouth and fatigue. Serious adverse effects have not been consistently reported. Theoretical concerns include slow heart rate, bronchospasm and GI obstruction because of cholinergic effects, and changes in thyroid function from animal data. Bacopa grown in contaminated water can accumulate heavy metals. Safety in pregnancy and breastfeeding is not established.", "fun": "Bacopa is sold both as a memory supplement in health food stores and as a decorative plant in aquarium shops.", "src": ["Kongkeaw and colleagues 2014, Journal of Ethnopharmacology, Meta-analysis of randomized controlled trials on cognitive effects of Bacopa monnieri extract", "Stough and colleagues 2001, Psychopharmacology, chronic effects of Bacopa monnieri on cognitive function", "Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center, About Herbs: Bacopa"]}, "gotu-kola": {"origin": "Centella asiatica is a creeping plant of the carrot family that grows in damp, shady places throughout tropical and subtropical Asia and beyond. In Sri Lanka, India and Southeast Asia the leaves are eaten as a vegetable, in salads, sambols and drinks, and the plant has a long place in Ayurveda, traditional Chinese medicine and folk medicine, where it was used for skin diseases, wounds, memory and longevity. In parts of India it is called brahmi, the same name used for bacopa, which causes confusion. European interest began in the nineteenth century when it was tried for leprosy and skin ulcers, and in the twentieth century chemists isolated its triterpene glycosides, asiaticoside and madecassoside, named after Asia and Madagascar, an important source. Standardized triterpene extracts were developed in Europe for wound healing and venous insufficiency, and topical creams remain widespread in skin care. Case reports in the 2000s, including a series of women in Argentina with hepatitis after using gotu kola for weight loss, established a rare liver injury signal now noted in LiverTox. In the United States gotu kola is a dietary supplement with no FDA-recognized indication.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Gotu kola is used in Ayurveda and traditional Chinese medicine and eaten as a leafy vegetable."], ["1800s", "European physicians try Centella for leprosy and skin ulcers."], ["1900s", "Asiaticoside and madecassoside are isolated; standardized extracts are developed for wounds and veins."], ["2005", "A case series from Argentina describes hepatitis linked to gotu kola."]], "cell": "The main active compounds are pentacyclic triterpenes: the glycosides asiaticoside and madecassoside and their aglycones asiatic acid and madecassic acid. In fibroblast cultures they stimulate production of type I collagen and fibronectin through TGF-beta and Smad signaling, which supports wound and scar healing claims. In venous disease, they are proposed to strengthen connective tissue around veins and reduce capillary leakage. Animal studies show anti-anxiety and sedative effects, possibly through GABA-related pathways, and antioxidant effects in brain tissue that underlie memory claims. The mechanism of liver injury is unknown and appears idiosyncratic; reported cases improved after stopping the product, and some recurred on re-exposure.", "body": [["Skin", "Stimulates collagen and wound repair; topical contact allergy possible."], ["Veins", "Possible improvement of venous insufficiency symptoms in small trials."], ["Brain", "Sedative and anti-anxiety effects in animal studies."], ["Liver", "Rare idiosyncratic liver injury with jaundice."]], "journey": "After oral intake, asiaticoside and madecassoside are partly hydrolyzed by gut bacteria and enzymes to asiatic and madecassic acids, which are absorbed and reach peak plasma levels within a few hours in limited studies. They are metabolized in the liver; elimination routes in humans are not well characterized. Topical application acts locally, with little systemic absorption expected.", "sidefx": "Reported side effects include stomach upset, nausea and drowsiness. Topical products can cause allergic contact dermatitis. The most serious reported adverse effect is liver injury, described in LiverTox and case reports as hepatitis with jaundice developing after weeks to months of use, generally improving after stopping, and in rare cases severe. Additive sedation with alcohol or sedatives is a theoretical concern based on animal data. Safety in pregnancy is not established.", "fun": "The compound madecassoside is named after Madagascar, where Centella asiatica was an important source for European extract makers.", "src": ["NIH LiverTox, Gotu Kola chapter", "Jorge and Jorge 2005, Revista Espanola de Enfermedades Digestivas, hepatotoxicity associated with Centella asiatica", "Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center, About Herbs: Gotu Kola", "Gohil, Patel and Gajjar 2010, Indian Journal of Pharmaceutical Sciences, Centella asiatica review"]}, "shatavari": {"origin": "Asparagus racemosus is a spiny climbing plant of the asparagus family found across India, Sri Lanka, the Himalayas, Southeast Asia, Australia and Africa, growing from a cluster of tuberous roots. In Ayurveda it is one of the most important tonics for women, classified as a rasayana, and is used for reproductive health, menstrual problems, menopause and to increase breast milk. The Sanskrit name is often translated as having a hundred husbands or as curer of a hundred diseases, interpretations that reflect its reputation for supporting fertility and general vitality, although such translations vary. Traditional preparations often combine the powdered root with milk or ghee. Modern chemical studies identified steroidal saponins, named shatavarins, along with flavonoids and alkaloids. Animal research in India has explored anti-ulcer, immune, antioxidant and hormonal effects, and a few small human trials have tested it as a galactagogue with conflicting results. Shatavari has become part of global Ayurvedic and women's wellness markets, and in the United States it is a dietary supplement with no FDA-recognized indication.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Shatavari is described in Ayurvedic texts as a tonic for women's health."], ["1900s", "Steroidal saponins are identified in Asparagus racemosus roots."], ["1990s to 2010s", "Small trials and animal studies examine shatavari for lactation and other uses, with mixed results."], ["2010s", "Shatavari becomes a common ingredient in Western lactation and women's health supplements."]], "cell": "The shatavarins are steroidal saponins built on spirostanol and furostanol skeletons, related to diosgenin, a plant steroid used historically as a starting material for synthetic hormones. Proposed actions include weak phytoestrogen-like effects and stimulation of prolactin or mammary tissue, which would underlie the galactagogue reputation, but human hormone data are sparse and inconsistent. Animal studies report a diuretic effect, protection of the stomach lining against ulcers, immune stimulation and modest glucose lowering. Racemofuran and flavonoids have antioxidant effects in laboratory studies. None of these mechanisms has been clearly demonstrated in people.", "body": [["Breast tissue", "Proposed effect on milk production; human evidence conflicting."], ["Reproductive system", "Proposed weak hormone-like effects."], ["Kidneys", "Diuretic effect in animal studies."], ["Stomach", "Anti-ulcer effects in animal studies; stomach upset reported in people."], ["Immune system", "Immune stimulation in animal studies."]], "journey": "Pharmacokinetic studies of shatavari in humans are lacking. Steroidal saponins are generally poorly absorbed intact and are likely hydrolyzed by gut bacteria to their sapogenins, which may be absorbed and metabolized in the liver. Onset, half-life and excretion are not established. Whether constituents pass into breast milk is not known, which matters because breastfeeding is a main marketed use.", "sidefx": "Shatavari is generally reported as well tolerated, with occasional stomach upset. People allergic to asparagus may have allergic reactions, including rash, hives or wheezing. Theoretical concerns include hormone-like effects in hormone-sensitive conditions, a diuretic effect that could alter lithium levels, and glucose lowering with diabetes medicines. Some Ayurvedic products have been found to contain lead, mercury or arsenic, a contamination risk separate from the herb itself.", "fun": "Shatavari is a close relative of the garden asparagus, and both belong to the same genus, Asparagus.", "src": ["NIH LactMed, Shatavari entry", "Alok and colleagues 2013, Asian Pacific Journal of Tropical Disease, Plant profile, phytochemistry and pharmacology of Asparagus racemosus", "Saper and colleagues 2008, JAMA, lead, mercury and arsenic in Ayurvedic medicines sold online"]}, "kava": {"origin": "Kava, Piper methysticum, is a shrub of the pepper family domesticated in the western Pacific, probably in Vanuatu, and carried across Melanesia and Polynesia by voyaging peoples. Its root is ground or chewed and mixed with water to make a drink that is central to ceremonies, hospitality and social life in Fiji, Tonga, Samoa, Vanuatu and Hawaii, among others. European accounts came from Captain James Cook's Pacific voyages in the eighteenth century, and the naturalist Georg Forster, who sailed with Cook, gave the plant its scientific name, which roughly means intoxicating pepper. Kava extracts entered European pharmacy as anxiolytics, and in the 1990s kava became a popular herbal remedy for anxiety in Europe and North America. Around 2000 to 2002, reports of severe liver injury, including liver failure requiring transplant, emerged from Germany, Switzerland and elsewhere. Germany and several other countries restricted kava, and in 2002 FDA issued a consumer advisory on liver injury risk. The German ban was later overturned by a court. Investigations pointed to extraction methods, use of stem peelings and lower-quality cultivars, and individual susceptibility, though the cause remains debated. Kava bars have since spread in the United States. Kava has no FDA-recognized indication.", "timeline": [["1770s", "Georg Forster, sailing with Captain Cook, describes kava and later names it Piper methysticum."], ["1990s", "Kava extracts become popular anxiety remedies in Europe and North America."], ["2002", "FDA issues a consumer advisory on severe liver injury with kava products; several countries restrict sales."], ["2003", "A Cochrane review finds a small benefit of kava extract over placebo for anxiety."], ["2014", "A German court overturns the national kava ban."]], "cell": "The six major kavalactones are lipophilic pyrones. They enhance GABA-A receptor function at a site distinct from the benzodiazepine site, so flumazenil is not expected to reverse their effects. Kavain and methysticin block voltage-gated sodium channels, explaining the numbing of the mouth and possible muscle relaxant and anticonvulsant effects, and some kavalactones block L-type calcium channels. Kavain inhibits norepinephrine reuptake, desmethoxyyangonin inhibits MAO-B, and yangonin binds cannabinoid CB1 receptors in laboratory studies. Kava extracts inhibit several cytochrome P450 enzymes in vitro and reduced CYP2E1 activity in a human study. The liver injury mechanism is unresolved: proposed contributors include the chalcone flavokavain B, which is toxic to liver cells in culture, the alkaloid pipermethystine found mainly in stem peelings and leaves, depletion of glutathione, solvent extraction methods that concentrate different constituents than water preparations, and immune-mediated idiosyncratic reactions in susceptible people, including poor CYP2D6 metabolizers in some hypotheses.", "body": [["Brain", "Relaxation, sedation and, at higher intake, ataxia and slurred speech through GABA-A enhancement and channel blockade."], ["Mouth", "Numbness of tongue and lips from sodium channel blockade."], ["Liver", "Rare severe idiosyncratic hepatocellular injury, including liver failure."], ["Skin", "Kava dermopathy, a dry scaly yellowish rash with heavy chronic use."], ["Muscles and movement", "Muscle relaxation; case reports of dystonia and worsened Parkinson symptoms."]], "journey": "Kavalactones are absorbed from the gut within minutes, which explains the rapid onset of mouth numbness and relaxation; kavain peaks in about one to two hours in limited studies. They are lipophilic and enter the brain readily. Metabolism in the liver involves demethylation, hydroxylation and reduction, partly through CYP2C19 and other enzymes, followed by conjugation. Metabolites are excreted in urine and feces. Half-lives are not well established but appear to be several hours, and effects of a session can last several hours.", "sidefx": "Common effects include drowsiness, mouth numbness, headache, dizziness and stomach upset. Larger amounts produce intoxication with ataxia, slurred speech, dilated pupils, red eyes and impaired judgment and driving. The most serious adverse effect is idiosyncratic hepatocellular liver injury, which can progress to liver failure and has required transplant in reported cases. Heavy chronic use causes kava dermopathy and has been associated with weight loss and malnutrition in heavily using communities. Case reports describe dystonia and reduced levodopa effect.", "fun": "A traditional kava session numbs the mouth so reliably that the tingling lips and tongue are the first sign a drinker notices, a local anesthetic effect from the same compounds that relax the brain.", "src": ["FDA 2002, Consumer advisory on kava-containing dietary supplements and severe liver injury", "NIH LiverTox, Kava Kava chapter", "NIH NCCIH, Kava fact sheet", "Pittler and Ernst 2003, Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews, Kava extract for treating anxiety"]}, "kavalactones": {"origin": "The search for kava's active principles began in the nineteenth century, when European chemists isolated crystalline compounds from the root, including methysticin and yangonin, the latter named after yaqona, the Fijian word for kava. Through the early and mid twentieth century, German researchers characterized the full set of kava pyrones, and the six major kavalactones, kavain, dihydrokavain, methysticin, dihydromethysticin, yangonin and desmethoxyyangonin, were recognized as accounting for most of the root's pharmacological activity. Synthetic kavain was marketed in Germany as an anxiolytic for a time. In the 1990s, kava extracts standardized to a percentage of kavalactones were sold widely in Europe and North America, until liver injury reports led to restrictions and the 2002 FDA consumer advisory. Researchers in the Pacific developed a chemotype system that lists the six kavalactones by relative amount, helping to separate traditional noble cultivars, preferred for drinking, from tudei or two-day cultivars associated with stronger and longer-lasting effects and more side effects. Vanuatu enacted rules to limit export of non-noble kava. Kavalactones have no FDA-recognized indication.", "timeline": [["1800s", "European chemists isolate methysticin and yangonin from kava root."], ["Mid 1900s", "German researchers characterize the six major kavalactones."], ["1990s", "Kava extracts standardized to kavalactones become popular anxiety products."], ["2002", "FDA issues a consumer advisory on liver injury with kava products."], ["2000s", "Chemotype coding is used to distinguish noble from tudei kava cultivars."]], "cell": "Kavalactones are arylethylene alpha-pyrones. Their best-defined action is positive modulation of GABA-A receptors, which in laboratory studies does not depend on the benzodiazepine binding site. Kavain and methysticin block voltage-gated sodium channels, producing local anesthesia and reducing neuronal excitability, and several kavalactones block L-type calcium channels. Kavain inhibits norepinephrine reuptake, desmethoxyyangonin and other kavalactones inhibit MAO-B, and yangonin binds CB1 cannabinoid receptors in laboratory studies. Kavalactones, especially methysticin and dihydromethysticin, inhibit and in some models induce cytochrome P450 enzymes. The specific contribution of kavalactones to liver injury is uncertain, since flavokavains and pipermethystine have also been proposed as hepatotoxic constituents.", "body": [["Brain", "Relaxation and sedation through GABA-A enhancement; ataxia at high intake."], ["Mouth", "Numbness from sodium channel blockade."], ["Liver", "Metabolism; kava products linked to rare severe liver injury."], ["Skin", "Kava dermopathy with heavy chronic use."]], "journey": "Kavalactones are lipophilic and are absorbed quickly from the gut, especially when drunk as an emulsion, with effects starting within minutes and kavain peaking within about one to two hours in limited studies. They cross the blood-brain barrier readily. They are metabolized in the liver by demethylation, hydroxylation and ring opening, partly via CYP2C19, then conjugated and excreted in urine and feces. Half-lives are not well established but appear to be several hours.", "sidefx": "Effects follow those of kava: drowsiness, mouth numbness, dizziness and stomach upset; higher intake causes ataxia, slurred speech and impaired driving, with additive effects from alcohol and sedatives. Kava products have been linked to rare severe liver injury and, with heavy chronic use, to kava dermopathy. Case reports describe dystonia and reduced levodopa effect.", "fun": "Yangonin takes its name from yaqona, the Fijian word for kava, reflecting the drink's deep roots in Pacific culture.", "src": ["NIH LiverTox, Kava Kava chapter", "NIH NCCIH, Kava fact sheet", "Lebot, Merlin and Lindstrom 1992, Kava: The Pacific Drug, Yale University Press", "Singh 1992, Journal of Ethnopharmacology, Kava: an overview"]}, "valerian": {"origin": "Valeriana officinalis is a tall perennial with clusters of pale pink or white flowers that grows across Europe and parts of Asia and has been naturalized in North America. The flowers smell sweet, but the dried root smells strongly of old socks or sweaty feet, a smell caused by isovaleric acid. Greek and Roman physicians including Dioscorides and Galen described the plant, Galen recommending it for insomnia, and it was later used in medieval Europe for nervous complaints and epilepsy. By the nineteenth century valerian was a standard sedative in European and American pharmacy, listed in official pharmacopeias, and during the First and Second World Wars it was used in Britain for stress and shell shock and to calm civilians during air raids, according to historical accounts. Chemists isolated valerenic acid and the unstable valepotriates in the twentieth century. As benzodiazepines replaced herbal sedatives, valerian moved to the supplement market, where it remains one of the best-selling sleep herbs. Clinical trials have given mixed results, and valerian has no FDA-recognized indication.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Greek and Roman physicians, including Galen, describe valerian for sleeplessness."], ["1800s", "Valerian is a standard sedative in European and American pharmacy."], ["1960s", "Valepotriates are characterized in valerian root."], ["1998", "A JAMA case report describes delirium and cardiac complications after abrupt valerian withdrawal."], ["2000s", "Systematic reviews describe inconsistent evidence for valerian and sleep."]], "cell": "Valerenic acid, a sesquiterpene, is the best-characterized active constituent. It enhances GABA-A receptor currents when the receptor includes beta-2 or beta-3 subunits, binding near the site used by the anticonvulsant loreclezole, and mice with a mutation at the beta-3 site lose its anxiolytic effect. Extracts also inhibit GABA breakdown and affect GABA release and uptake in laboratory studies. Valerian extracts bind adenosine A1 receptors, which may contribute to sleepiness, and valerenic acid acts as a partial agonist at serotonin 5-HT5a receptors in laboratory studies. Valepotriates such as valtrate are reactive iridoids that degrade quickly and are poorly absorbed; they showed cytotoxic and mutagenic effects in laboratory tests, raising quality questions. The mechanism of rare liver injury is unknown.", "body": [["Brain", "Mild sedation and reduced sleep latency in some studies through GABA-A modulation."], ["Liver", "Rare idiosyncratic liver injury, often with combination products."], ["Gut", "Stomach upset reported."], ["Heart", "One case report of cardiac complications after abrupt withdrawal of heavy use."]], "journey": "Valerenic acid is absorbed after oral intake and reaches peak plasma levels within about one to two hours in a small study, with a half-life of roughly one to two hours, though levels vary widely between people. Sedative effects may build up over days of use in some trials. Metabolism occurs in the liver, and excretion routes are not well characterized. Valepotriates degrade in the gut and during storage to baldrinals, which may have effects of their own.", "sidefx": "Valerian is generally well tolerated. Reported effects include drowsiness, headache, dizziness, stomach upset, vivid dreams and morning grogginess. The main risk is additive sedation with alcohol, benzodiazepines, opioids and other depressants, with falls and impaired driving. Rare liver injury has been reported, often with multi-ingredient products. A case report described delirium and heart complications after a man abruptly stopped long-term heavy use, suggesting tapering may matter, though such reports are rare.", "fun": "Valerian's smell attracts cats, much like catnip, and the root has also been used to bait traps.", "src": ["NIH Office of Dietary Supplements, Valerian fact sheet", "NIH NCCIH, Valerian fact sheet", "NIH LiverTox, Valerian chapter", "Garges, Varia and Doraiswamy 1998, JAMA, Cardiac complications and delirium associated with valerian root withdrawal"]}, "valerenic-acid": {"origin": "Valerenic acid was identified in the mid twentieth century during chemical studies of Valeriana officinalis, as researchers looked for the constituents behind the root's long reputation as a sedative. At first, much attention went to the valepotriates, iridoid esters isolated in the 1960s, but these proved unstable, poorly absorbed and of uncertain relevance, and attention shifted to the sesquiterpenes, chiefly valerenic acid and its acetoxy and hydroxy derivatives. Because valerenic acid is characteristic of Valeriana officinalis and relatively stable, it became the main marker used to standardize valerian extracts. Its mechanism was clarified in the 2000s: electrophysiology studies in Austria showed that valerenic acid enhances GABA-A receptor currents selectively at receptors containing beta-2 or beta-3 subunits, and in 2009 a study using knock-in mice with a point mutation in the beta-3 subunit showed that the anxiety-reducing effect disappeared, providing strong evidence for its site of action. Human evidence remains limited to whole valerian products, and valerenic acid has no FDA-recognized indication.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Valepotriates are characterized in valerian, initially drawing attention as possible actives."], ["Late 1900s", "Valerenic acid becomes the main standardization marker for valerian extracts."], ["2007", "Electrophysiology studies show subunit-selective GABA-A modulation by valerenic acid."], ["2009", "Knock-in mice with a beta-3 mutation lose valerenic acid's anxiolytic effect."]], "cell": "GABA-A receptors are chloride channels built from five subunits. Valerenic acid binds in the transmembrane region of beta-2 or beta-3 subunits, at or near the site where the anticonvulsant loreclezole and some general anesthetics act, and increases chloride flow when GABA binds. At higher concentrations it can open the channel directly. Receptors with a beta-1 subunit respond weakly. Because this site differs from the benzodiazepine site between alpha and gamma subunits, flumazenil would not be expected to block it. Valerenic acid has also been reported as a partial agonist at 5-HT5a serotonin receptors, which are involved in circadian rhythm regulation, in laboratory studies. It may inhibit GABA breakdown in older laboratory work.", "body": [["Brain", "Enhanced GABA-A signaling with mild sedation and anxiolysis in animals."], ["Liver", "Metabolism (limited data)."], ["Spinal cord", "GABA-A receptors with beta-3 subunits are widely expressed; muscle relaxation is possible."], ["Circadian system", "Laboratory 5-HT5a activity may relate to sleep timing (unproven)."]], "journey": "In a small human study of valerian extract, valerenic acid appeared in plasma within an hour, peaked around one to two hours and had a half-life of about one to two hours, with large differences between people. Its lipophilic structure allows it to cross the blood-brain barrier. Liver metabolism is presumed, and excretion routes are not well characterized. Acetoxyvalerenic acid can be converted to hydroxyvalerenic acid during storage and processing.", "sidefx": "No side effect profile is defined for isolated valerenic acid in humans. Effects follow valerian products: drowsiness, headache, dizziness, morning grogginess and additive sedation with alcohol, opioids and benzodiazepines. Rare liver injury has been reported with valerian products, though not specifically tied to valerenic acid. Safety in pregnancy and breastfeeding is not established.", "fun": "A single amino acid change in one GABA-A subunit is enough to make mice ignore the anxiety-reducing effect of valerenic acid, which is unusually precise evidence for an herbal compound's mechanism.", "src": ["Benke and colleagues 2009, Neuropharmacology, GABA-A receptors as in vivo substrate for the anxiolytic action of valerenic acid", "Khom and colleagues 2007, Neuropharmacology, Valerenic acid potentiates and inhibits GABA-A receptors", "NIH Office of Dietary Supplements, Valerian fact sheet", "Anderson and colleagues 2005, Phytotherapy Research, pharmacokinetics of valerenic acid after valerian administration"]}, "chamomile": {"origin": "Chamomile is one of the oldest documented medicinal plants in Europe and the Mediterranean. Ancient Egyptian, Greek and Roman writers described chamomile flowers for fever, skin complaints and stomach upset, and the name comes from Greek words meaning ground apple, a reference to the apple-like scent of Roman chamomile. German chamomile (Matricaria chamomilla) became the main medicinal species in central Europe, where it entered official pharmacopoeias and is still sold as a traditional herbal medicine for stomach complaints and skin irritation. In the United States chamomile is mainly a food and a dietary supplement, and it is among the most widely consumed herbal teas. Modern research shifted attention from the blue essential oil, rich in chamazulene and bisabolol, to the flavonoid apigenin, which was shown in the 1990s to bind the benzodiazepine site of brain GABA-A receptors. Clinical evidence remains thin: a few small randomized trials, notably from a University of Pennsylvania group, suggested modest benefit in generalized anxiety disorder, while sleep studies have been small and mixed. The safety story is dominated by allergy, because chamomile shares the Asteraceae family with ragweed.", "timeline": [["Antiquity", "Egyptian, Greek and Roman writers describe chamomile for fevers, skin and digestive complaints."], ["1700s to 1800s", "German chamomile becomes a standard entry in European pharmacopoeias."], ["1990s", "Laboratory studies show the flavonoid apigenin binds the benzodiazepine site of GABA-A receptors."], ["2006", "A case report in CMAJ describes bleeding attributed to a chamomile and warfarin interaction."], ["2009 to 2016", "Small randomized trials report modest benefit of chamomile extract in generalized anxiety disorder."]], "cell": "The flavonoid apigenin is the best studied chamomile compound. In receptor binding studies it competes for the benzodiazepine site of the GABA-A receptor, though with far lower affinity than benzodiazepine drugs, and animal studies show mild anxiety-reducing effects without strong sedation or muscle relaxation. Apigenin also inhibits inflammatory signaling in cell models, including cyclooxygenase-2 expression and cytokine release. The essential oil components alpha-bisabolol and chamazulene (formed from matricin during steam distillation, giving the oil its blue color) have anti-inflammatory and antispasmodic effects in animal and laboratory models, which is the proposed basis for use in stomach cramps and skin irritation. Chamomile also contains coumarin compounds such as herniarin and umbelliferone; these are not the same as warfarin-type anticoagulants, and the mechanism of reported warfarin interactions is not established. Allergic reactions arise from sesquiterpene lactones and other proteins shared across the daisy family.", "body": [["Brain", "Weak action at GABA-A receptors is proposed to give mild calming effects; strong sedation is not typical."], ["Stomach and intestines", "Traditional use for cramps and upset stomach; antispasmodic effects shown in animal studies."], ["Skin", "Used in creams for irritation; can also cause allergic contact dermatitis."], ["Immune system", "Cross-reactive allergy with ragweed and other daisy-family plants, rarely anaphylaxis."], ["Blood", "Rare case reports of raised INR and bleeding in people taking warfarin."]], "journey": "Chamomile is usually swallowed as a tea, so the amount of active compounds varies with the plant material, the amount of flowers and the steeping time. Apigenin from chamomile is present partly as glycosides that are split by gut enzymes and bacteria before absorption. Once absorbed, apigenin undergoes extensive glucuronidation and sulfation in the intestinal wall and liver, so most circulating apigenin is conjugated rather than free. Human pharmacokinetic data are limited, and how much apigenin reaches the brain in people is not known. Conjugated flavonoids are eliminated in urine and bile. Essential oil components applied to the skin are absorbed to a small degree.", "sidefx": "Chamomile is generally well tolerated in food amounts. The main adverse effects are allergic: contact dermatitis from creams, hives and, rarely, anaphylaxis after drinking the tea, with higher risk in people allergic to ragweed, chrysanthemums, marigolds or daisies. Using chamomile tea bags or tea as an eye wash has been linked to allergic conjunctivitis. Drowsiness is possible, especially combined with sedative medicines or alcohol. Rare case reports describe raised INR and bleeding in people on warfarin who drank chamomile regularly or used chamomile products, so changes in intake are relevant to anticoagulation monitoring.", "fun": "The deep blue color of chamomile essential oil does not exist in the fresh flower: chamazulene forms from the colorless compound matricin during steam distillation, so the blue oil is partly made by the process used to extract it.", "src": ["NIH NCCIH: Chamomile", "Srivastava JK, Shankar E, Gupta S. Chamomile: a herbal medicine of the past with bright future. Mol Med Rep 2010", "Segal R, Pilote L. Warfarin interaction with Matricaria chamomilla. CMAJ 2006", "European Medicines Agency HMPC monograph: Matricariae flos"]}, "passionflower": {"origin": "Passiflora incarnata, also called maypop, is a vine native to the southeastern United States, where Indigenous peoples ate its fruit and used the plant medicinally. Spanish missionaries in the Americas in the 1500s and 1600s gave the genus its name, seeing in the flower's parts symbols of the Passion of Christ: the corona as the crown of thorns, the stamens as wounds and the styles as nails. The plant entered American medical practice in the 1800s as a remedy for nervousness and sleeplessness, and it was later listed in the National Formulary in the United States. In Europe, passionflower herb is still sold as a traditional herbal medicine for mild nervous tension and sleep, often combined with valerian, hops and lemon balm. Modern clinical research is limited to small trials, including studies in patients anxious before surgery or dental procedures, which suggest mild benefit, and small, lower-quality trials in generalized anxiety disorder and sleep. The active compounds have never been firmly identified, and the flavonoid chrysin, which binds the benzodiazepine site in laboratory studies, is poorly absorbed.", "timeline": [["1500s to 1600s", "Spanish missionaries in the Americas name the passion flower after religious symbolism in its flower."], ["1800s", "Passionflower is used in American medical practice for nervousness and insomnia."], ["1900s", "Passiflora is listed in the US National Formulary as a sedative herb."], ["2000s", "Small randomized trials study passionflower for anxiety before surgery and dental procedures."], ["2013", "A systematic review of clinical trials finds limited but suggestive evidence for anxiety."]], "cell": "Passionflower extracts affect GABA signaling in animal and laboratory experiments. Proposed actions include positive effects at GABA-A receptors, activity at GABA-B receptors and inhibition of GABA uptake, but results differ between extracts and no single active compound has been confirmed. Flavonoids such as vitexin, isovitexin and chrysin are the main candidates; chrysin binds the benzodiazepine site of GABA-A receptors in laboratory studies, though its absorption after oral intake is very poor. Some Passiflora preparations contain trace amounts of harmala-type beta-carboline alkaloids, which inhibit monoamine oxidase in laboratory studies, but amounts in Passiflora incarnata are generally low or undetectable, and a clinically meaningful MAO effect in people has not been shown. Animal models show reduced anxiety-like behavior and prolonged sleep time with some extracts. Overall, the mechanism is best described as a proposed mild enhancement of GABA tone.", "body": [["Brain", "Proposed mild GABA-related calming effect; drowsiness, dizziness and confusion possible."], ["Uterus", "Traditional concern about stimulation of contractions; the reason for caution in pregnancy."], ["Heart", "Rare case reports of rhythm problems with passionflower products; causality is uncertain."], ["Muscles and balance", "Drowsiness can raise fall risk, especially in older adults on other sedatives."]], "journey": "Passionflower is swallowed as tea, tincture, capsule or tablet, usually as an extract of the dried leaves, stems and flowers. Human pharmacokinetic data are very limited. Flavonoid glycosides such as vitexin and isovitexin are expected to be partly broken down by gut bacteria and absorbed slowly, then conjugated by glucuronidation and sulfation in the gut wall and liver. Chrysin is known to have very low oral bioavailability because of extensive first-pass conjugation. Conjugated flavonoids are excreted in urine and bile. Because products differ in plant species, plant part and extraction method, blood levels in practice are unknown and unpredictable.", "sidefx": "In short-term trials passionflower has usually been well tolerated. The most commonly reported effects are drowsiness, dizziness and confusion, which matter most when combined with benzodiazepines, opioids, sleep medicines or alcohol, and in older adults at risk of falls. Isolated case reports describe heart rhythm changes, nausea and other reactions after passionflower products, but contamination and other ingredients make causality uncertain. Because of a traditional belief that the plant can stimulate the uterus, it has long been avoided during pregnancy, and safety data in pregnancy and breastfeeding are lacking.", "fun": "The passion in passionflower has nothing to do with romance: the name comes from missionaries who read the flower as a teaching tool for the Passion of Christ, counting the ten petals and sepals as the faithful apostles.", "src": ["NIH NCCIH: Passionflower", "Miroddi M et al. Passiflora incarnata L.: ethnopharmacology, clinical application, safety and evaluation of clinical trials. J Ethnopharmacol 2013", "European Medicines Agency HMPC monograph: Passiflorae herba", "Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center About Herbs: Passionflower"]}, "lemon-balm": {"origin": "Lemon balm is a Mediterranean member of the mint family that has been cultivated for more than two thousand years. Its genus name Melissa comes from the Greek word for honeybee, and the plant was grown near hives because bees are drawn to its flowers. Greek and Roman physicians, and later medieval Arab and European writers, described lemon balm as a herb that gladdens the heart and lifts the spirits. In the 1600s Carmelite nuns in Paris produced Carmelite water, a distilled alcoholic tonic with lemon balm as a main ingredient, which was sold for centuries as a remedy for nervousness and headache. Lemon balm remained a household garden herb and tea throughout Europe and North America. Modern studies began in the late twentieth century, with laboratory work identifying rosmarinic acid as a GABA transaminase inhibitor and small human trials reporting calmer mood in healthy volunteers. German topical lemon balm creams for cold sores were studied in small trials in the 1990s. Evidence is still limited to small, short studies, many funded by manufacturers or using combinations with valerian.", "timeline": [["Antiquity", "Greek and Roman writers describe lemon balm as a calming and uplifting herb grown for bees."], ["1600s", "Carmelite water, a distilled lemon balm tonic, is made by Carmelite nuns in Paris."], ["1990s", "Small trials study topical lemon balm cream for cold sores."], ["2000s", "Small human studies report calmer mood and changes in cognitive tests after single lemon balm doses."], ["2016", "Reviews summarize lemon balm pharmacology, noting mostly laboratory and small clinical studies."]], "cell": "Lemon balm leaves are rich in rosmarinic acid, a caffeic acid ester also found in rosemary and sage. In laboratory studies rosmarinic acid and related triterpenes inhibit GABA transaminase, the enzyme that breaks down the inhibitory neurotransmitter GABA, which would be expected to raise GABA levels and is the leading explanation for calming effects. Extracts also bind nicotinic and muscarinic acetylcholine receptors in brain tissue studies, which has been linked to reported effects on attention and memory. Topical extracts reduce attachment of herpes simplex virus to cells in culture, the basis for cold sore creams. A separate laboratory finding is that freeze-dried lemon balm extracts, with oxidized phenolic compounds, block binding of thyroid-stimulating hormone and thyroid-stimulating antibodies to thyroid tissue; this was once explored for overactive thyroid but has not been shown to matter clinically in people.", "body": [["Brain", "Proposed increase in GABA tone and cholinergic receptor binding; mild calming and possible drowsiness."], ["Thyroid", "Laboratory studies show blocked TSH binding; clinical relevance unknown, so thyroid disease is a theoretical caution."], ["Skin and lips", "Topical creams studied for cold sores; rare allergic reactions."], ["Stomach and intestines", "Traditional use for indigestion and gas; nausea possible."]], "journey": "Lemon balm is swallowed as tea, extract, capsule or tincture, or applied as a lip cream. After oral intake, rosmarinic acid is absorbed partly in the small intestine, with peak blood levels reported within roughly one to two hours in small human studies, and much of it is broken down by colonic bacteria into caffeic acid, ferulic acid and smaller phenolic acids. Absorbed compounds are conjugated by glucuronidation, sulfation and methylation and are mainly excreted in urine. The essential oil is present only in small amounts in dried leaves and evaporates during storage. Whether meaningful amounts of active compounds reach the brain in people is not established.", "sidefx": "In short-term studies lemon balm has usually been well tolerated. Reported effects include nausea, abdominal discomfort, dizziness, wheezing in rare cases and drowsiness. Drowsiness becomes more relevant when lemon balm is combined with benzodiazepines, sleep medicines, opioids, alcohol or other calming herbs, as is common in sleep blends. Topical creams occasionally cause local irritation or allergic reactions. The thyroid concern rests on laboratory findings rather than documented human cases, so it is usually described as theoretical, but it is one reason heavy use is reported in people with thyroid disease or on thyroid medicines.", "fun": "Melissa, the genus name of lemon balm, is Greek for honeybee, and beekeepers in ancient times reportedly rubbed the leaves inside empty hives to encourage swarms to settle there.", "src": ["European Medicines Agency HMPC monograph: Melissae folium", "Shakeri A, Sahebkar A, Javadi B. Melissa officinalis L.: a review. J Ethnopharmacol 2016", "Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center About Herbs: Lemon balm", "Kennedy DO et al. Modulation of mood and cognitive performance following acute administration of Melissa officinalis. Pharmacol Biochem Behav 2002"]}, "lavender-oil": {"origin": "Lavender, a Mediterranean shrub of the mint family, was used by Romans to scent baths and laundry, and its name is often traced to the Latin lavare, to wash. Distilled lavender oil became a staple of European perfumery and folk medicine, and lavender was grown on a large scale in Provence and in England. The modern word aromatherapy was popularized by the French chemist Rene-Maurice Gattefosse in the 1930s, who promoted lavender oil for burns after an accident in his laboratory, a story often retold but difficult to verify in detail. The oral pharmaceutical story is newer. A German company developed silexan, a standardized lavender oil made by steam distillation of Lavandula angustifolia flowers, and it was approved as an herbal medicine in Germany around 2009 for restlessness with anxious mood. Several randomized trials, largely sponsored by the manufacturer, have reported benefit over placebo in subthreshold anxiety and generalized anxiety disorder. In the United States, the same type of preparation is sold as a dietary supplement with no FDA review. A 2007 report of prepubertal gynecomastia in boys using lavender and tea tree oil products raised endocrine questions that remain debated.", "timeline": [["Antiquity", "Romans use lavender to scent baths and linens."], ["1930s", "Rene-Maurice Gattefosse popularizes the term aromatherapy and promotes lavender oil."], ["2007", "A New England Journal of Medicine report links lavender and tea tree oil products to prepubertal gynecomastia in three boys."], ["Around 2009", "Silexan is approved in Germany as an herbal medicine for restlessness with anxious mood."], ["2010s", "Randomized trials, many sponsored by the manufacturer, report silexan benefit in anxiety disorders."]], "cell": "Lavender oil is a mixture dominated by the monoterpene alcohol linalool and its ester linalyl acetate. In laboratory studies silexan inhibits voltage-gated calcium channels in synaptosomes and hippocampal neurons, reducing calcium entry into nerve terminals and therefore release of excitatory neurotransmitters such as glutamate and norepinephrine. This is a different site from the alpha-2-delta subunit targeted by pregabalin, though the downstream idea of quieting overactive transmission is similar. Silexan does not bind the benzodiazepine site of GABA-A receptors, which is consistent with the lack of sedation, dependence and withdrawal in trials. Animal studies also suggest effects on serotonin 5-HT1A receptor expression and on neurite growth. Inhaled linalool causes sedation in mice, an effect that disappears when the animals cannot smell, which suggests part of aromatherapy acts through the olfactory system. Separately, lavender oil components showed weak estrogen-like and anti-androgen activity in breast cell lines, the proposed explanation for gynecomastia case reports, though the evidence is debated.", "body": [["Brain", "Proposed reduction of excitatory transmission through calcium channel inhibition; reduced anxiety scores in trials."], ["Stomach", "Burping with lavender taste, nausea and indigestion with oral capsules."], ["Skin", "Topical oils can cause allergic or irritant contact dermatitis."], ["Breast tissue", "Case reports of prepubertal breast growth with topical lavender products; causality debated."], ["Lungs", "Aspiration of swallowed essential oil by a child can cause chemical pneumonitis."]], "journey": "Oral lavender oil capsules release linalool and linalyl acetate in the gut. Linalyl acetate is largely hydrolyzed to linalool, which is absorbed quickly, with blood levels rising within about an hour in limited human data. Linalool is oxidized by liver enzymes and conjugated with glucuronic acid, then excreted mainly in urine. Studies of silexan with probe drugs did not show clinically important inhibition or induction of major CYP enzymes. Inhaled oil reaches the blood in small amounts through the lungs, and topical oil is absorbed through the skin to a limited extent, which is the route considered in the gynecomastia reports.", "sidefx": "In clinical trials of oral silexan, the most frequent adverse effects were gastrointestinal: burping, often with a lavender taste, nausea and indigestion. Sedation was not more common than placebo in most trials, and withdrawal symptoms were not seen after stopping. Topical lavender oil can cause contact dermatitis. Case reports describe breast development in prepubertal boys that resolved after lavender-containing products were stopped, and laboratory data show weak hormone activity, but population data have not confirmed a clear link. The most serious risk is accidental ingestion of undiluted essential oil by young children, which can cause vomiting, drowsiness and aspiration pneumonitis.", "fun": "In mouse studies, inhaled linalool made the animals calmer only if they could smell it; mice without a working sense of smell showed no effect, suggesting the nose itself is part of the mechanism of aromatherapy.", "src": ["NIH NCCIH: Lavender", "Schuwald AM et al. Lavender oil potent anxiolytic properties via modulating voltage dependent calcium channels. PLoS One 2013", "Henley DV et al. Prepubertal gynecomastia linked to lavender and tea tree oils. N Engl J Med 2007", "Kasper S et al. Silexan trials in anxiety disorders, Int Clin Psychopharmacol"]}, "hops": {"origin": "Hops are the female flower cones of Humulus lupulus, a climbing plant in the same family as cannabis. Hops were used in some medieval European brewing, and the twelfth-century German abbess Hildegard of Bingen wrote about the plant, noting its preserving effect in beer. Hops gradually replaced other herbal bittering mixtures, and by the 1500s hopped beer was common in much of Europe, prized for flavor and because hop bitter acids inhibit spoilage bacteria. A folk observation that hop pickers tired easily, along with the old practice of sleeping on hop-filled pillows, gave hops a reputation as a sleep aid. In the twentieth century, European herbal medicine paired hops with valerian in sleep products, and German researchers identified 2-methyl-3-buten-2-ol, a volatile compound that forms as the bitter acids break down, as a likely sedative component. In 1999 researchers identified 8-prenylnaringenin in hops as one of the most potent plant estrogens known, which explained old anecdotes about menstrual changes in female hop pickers and led to hop extracts being marketed for menopausal symptoms.", "timeline": [["1100s", "Hildegard of Bingen writes about hops and their preserving effect in beer."], ["1500s", "Hopped beer becomes common across much of Europe."], ["1980s", "2-methyl-3-buten-2-ol is identified as a sedative compound that forms in stored hops."], ["1999", "8-prenylnaringenin is identified in hops as a highly potent phytoestrogen."], ["2000s", "Small trials study valerian and hops combinations for sleep and hop extracts for hot flashes."]], "cell": "Hops contain several groups of active compounds. The bitter alpha acids (such as humulone) and beta acids (such as lupulone) slowly break down during storage into 2-methyl-3-buten-2-ol, a small volatile alcohol that produces sedation in rats, which is the leading explanation for the traditional sleep effect, although amounts in products are uncertain. Hop extracts have shown positive modulation of GABA-A receptors and binding to melatonin receptors in laboratory studies. The prenylflavonoids are the second major story. Xanthohumol, the most abundant, converts to isoxanthohumol in brewing and in the body, and gut bacteria then convert isoxanthohumol to 8-prenylnaringenin. 8-prenylnaringenin is a strong agonist at estrogen receptor alpha in laboratory assays, with potency greater than soy isoflavones, and its production varies widely between people depending on their gut bacteria. Xanthohumol itself has many laboratory actions, including antioxidant and anti-inflammatory effects, but human relevance is unclear.", "body": [["Brain", "Proposed mild sedative effect from hop compounds, studied mostly in combination with valerian."], ["Reproductive system", "8-prenylnaringenin acts as a potent estrogen in laboratory studies; studied for hot flashes."], ["Breast tissue", "Estrogen-like activity raises theoretical concern in hormone-sensitive cancers."], ["Immune system and airways", "Hop workers can develop allergic skin and respiratory reactions."]], "journey": "Hop compounds are swallowed in capsules, teas or tinctures, or inhaled from hop pillows. Prenylflavonoids such as xanthohumol are poorly absorbed and largely conjugated by glucuronidation. Isoxanthohumol reaching the colon is converted by some people's gut bacteria into 8-prenylnaringenin, and the liver can also form it through demethylation, so blood levels differ markedly between individuals. Small human studies show 8-prenylnaringenin is absorbed within a few hours and has a long elimination phase because of enterohepatic recycling, in which conjugates excreted in bile are split by bacteria and reabsorbed. Excretion is through feces and urine. The pharmacokinetics of the sedative 2-methyl-3-buten-2-ol in humans are not well described.", "sidefx": "Hop products are generally well tolerated in short studies. Drowsiness and dizziness are the most expected effects, especially in combination with valerian, prescription sleep medicines, benzodiazepines, opioids or alcohol. Hop workers can develop contact dermatitis and respiratory allergy from handling the cones. Because of the phytoestrogen content, concentrated extracts raise theoretical concerns in hormone-sensitive breast or uterine conditions and in people on tamoxifen or aromatase inhibitors, although clinical harm has not been well documented. Traditional herbal texts also listed depression as a caution for sedative herbs including hops.", "fun": "Hops are dangerous for dogs: some breeds, especially greyhounds, can develop a malignant hyperthermia-like reaction after eating hops, so spent hops from home brewing are a known veterinary poisoning hazard.", "src": ["European Medicines Agency HMPC monograph: Lupuli flos", "Milligan SR et al. Identification of a potent phytoestrogen in hops. J Clin Endocrinol Metab 1999", "Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center About Herbs: Hops", "Zanoli P, Zavatti M. Pharmacognostic and pharmacological profile of Humulus lupulus L. J Ethnopharmacol 2008"]}, "california-poppy": {"origin": "California poppy is a wildflower native to the western United States and northern Mexico, famous for its bright orange petals that close at night and in cloudy weather. Indigenous peoples of California used the plant in various ways, including reported use of the root and leaves for toothache, sleep and calming children, though specific practices varied among groups and are not always well documented. The genus was named in the early 1800s by the poet and botanist Adelbert von Chamisso, who sailed on the Russian Rurik expedition and named the plant after his shipmate, the physician and naturalist Johann Friedrich von Eschscholtz. The flower was adopted as California's state flower in 1903. Eclectic physicians and herbalists in the United States later used it as a gentle sedative and pain remedy, and it entered European herbal practice, especially in France, where it has been used in combination products for nervousness and sleep. Despite belonging to the poppy family, it does not contain morphine or codeine, which are found in the opium poppy, Papaver somniferum. Clinical evidence for the herb alone is very limited.", "timeline": [["Before 1800s", "Indigenous Californians use the plant in traditional medicine and as food."], ["Early 1800s", "Adelbert von Chamisso names the genus Eschscholzia after Johann Friedrich von Eschscholtz of the Rurik expedition."], ["1903", "The California poppy becomes the state flower of California."], ["2004", "A French trial studies a hawthorn, California poppy and magnesium combination for mild anxiety."], ["2015", "Laboratory studies show California poppy alkaloids modulate GABA-A receptors."]], "cell": "California poppy contains benzophenanthridine and protopine-type isoquinoline alkaloids, including californidine, escholtzine, protopine and allocryptopine, rather than the morphinan alkaloids of the opium poppy. In laboratory studies using recombinant receptors, protopine and allocryptopine act as positive modulators of GABA-A receptors, increasing chloride current in response to GABA, which is the most plausible explanation for calming effects. Older laboratory work reported weak interactions with other receptors, including some opioid and serotonin binding, but these effects are small and have not been shown to matter in people. Animal studies show sedative, anxiety-reducing and pain-reducing effects at high extract amounts. Laboratory studies also suggest some alkaloids can inhibit drug-metabolizing enzymes, but clinical interactions have not been documented. The specific compound responsible for any human effect is not established.", "body": [["Brain", "Proposed GABA-A modulation; mild sedation and drowsiness possible."], ["Pain pathways", "Traditional use for mild pain; supported only by animal studies."], ["Uterus", "No data; traditionally avoided in pregnancy."], ["Liver", "Alkaloids are metabolized by the liver; interactions with drug-metabolizing enzymes seen only in laboratory work."]], "journey": "California poppy is swallowed as a tincture, tea or capsule made from the aerial parts. Human pharmacokinetic data are essentially absent. Based on laboratory and animal work, the isoquinoline alkaloids are absorbed from the gut and metabolized in the liver by cytochrome P450 enzymes and conjugation reactions, with excretion through urine and bile. How much of each alkaloid reaches the brain, and how long effects last, have not been measured in people. Alkaloid content also varies with the plant part, growing conditions and the extraction method, so products are not interchangeable.", "sidefx": "Reports of adverse effects with California poppy alone are few, reflecting both mild activity and limited study. Drowsiness, dizziness and confusion are the expected effects, and they matter most when combined with opioids, benzodiazepines, sleep medicines or alcohol, or in older adults at risk of falls. Because no safety data exist in pregnancy or breastfeeding, the herb is traditionally avoided at those times. Isolated reports of reactions with multi-herb products are difficult to attribute. Despite occasional claims, the herb is not expected to cause opioid-type findings such as pinpoint pupils or slow breathing on its own.", "fun": "California poppy petals fold shut at night and on cloudy days, and the Spanish name for the plant, copa de oro or cup of gold, comes from the golden color of the open flowers covering hillsides.", "src": ["Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center About Herbs: California poppy", "Fedurco M et al. Modulatory effects of Eschscholzia californica alkaloids on recombinant GABA-A receptors. Biochem Res Int 2015", "Hanus M et al. Fixed combination of hawthorn, California poppy and magnesium in anxiety. Curr Med Res Opin 2004", "California State Library: State symbols (California poppy)"]}, "skullcap": {"origin": "Two different plants share the name skullcap, from the helmet-like shape of the flower's calyx. American skullcap, Scutellaria lateriflora, is native to North America and was used by Indigenous peoples and later by nineteenth-century American physicians, who promoted it as a nerve tonic and, for a time, as a supposed remedy for rabies, which gave it the folk name mad-dog skullcap. It appeared in the US Pharmacopeia and later the National Formulary before falling out of mainstream use. Chinese skullcap, Scutellaria baicalensis, is a root used for centuries in traditional Chinese medicine as huang qin for fevers, inflammation and infections, and its flavones baicalin, baicalein and wogonin are now widely studied in the laboratory. In the late twentieth century, liver injury cases attributed to skullcap appeared, and investigators found that some products sold as skullcap contained germander, a known liver toxin, which complicates attribution. More recently a prescription medical food, flavocoxid, containing Chinese skullcap flavonoids and catechins, was linked to liver injury and hypersensitivity pneumonitis, and FDA requested its recall in 2017.", "timeline": [["1700s to 1800s", "American skullcap is promoted as a nerve tonic and supposed rabies remedy, earning the name mad-dog skullcap."], ["Centuries", "Chinese skullcap root (huang qin) is used in traditional Chinese medicine."], ["1980s to 1990s", "Liver injury reports with skullcap products appear; adulteration with germander is identified in some cases."], ["2010s", "Laboratory research expands on baicalin, baicalein and wogonin."], ["2017", "FDA requests a recall of the flavocoxid medical food Limbrel after liver injury and hypersensitivity pneumonitis reports."]], "cell": "Skullcap flavones are the main studied compounds. Baicalein, wogonin and related flavones bind the benzodiazepine site of GABA-A receptors in laboratory binding studies, and wogonin produced anxiety-reducing effects in animal studies with less sedation than benzodiazepines, supporting a mild calming mechanism. Baicalin and baicalein inhibit cyclooxygenase and lipoxygenase pathways and reduce inflammatory cytokine production in cell models, which underlies interest in joint and inflammatory conditions. Laboratory studies also show antiviral and antibacterial activity. The mechanism of liver injury is not established; LiverTox treats it as idiosyncratic, and the frequent presence of other herbs, including germander with its toxic furano-diterpenoids, makes it hard to know which ingredient was responsible in many cases. The flavocoxid reports suggest that concentrated flavonoid products may themselves cause hypersensitivity-type injury in rare people.", "body": [["Brain", "Proposed mild calming effect through the GABA-A benzodiazepine site; drowsiness possible."], ["Liver", "Rare idiosyncratic hepatitis reported in case reports, sometimes with adulterated products."], ["Lungs", "Hypersensitivity pneumonitis reported with a flavocoxid product containing skullcap flavonoids."], ["Joints and immune system", "Anti-inflammatory effects in laboratory studies; marketed for arthritis."]], "journey": "Skullcap is swallowed as tea, tincture or capsule. The best studied compound, baicalin, is a glucuronide that is poorly absorbed as such; gut bacteria split it to baicalein, which is absorbed and then reconjugated back to baicalin in the gut wall and liver. This produces two absorption peaks in some studies, an early one and a later one after colonic conversion. Baicalin circulates highly bound to plasma proteins and is excreted in bile, where it can be recycled through the gut, and in urine. American skullcap leaf has a different flavone profile, mainly scutellarin and baicalin, and human pharmacokinetic data for it are minimal.", "sidefx": "Short-term use is often well tolerated, with drowsiness and stomach upset as typical effects. The serious concern is liver injury: LiverTox describes hepatitis with jaundice in case reports, usually after weeks to months of use, often in multi-ingredient products, and sometimes linked to germander adulteration. Rare cases have been severe. Hypersensitivity pneumonitis with cough and shortness of breath has been reported with the flavocoxid product. Additive drowsiness with sedatives and alcohol is possible. Safety in pregnancy and breastfeeding has not been studied.", "fun": "American skullcap was once sold in the 1800s as a cure for rabies, which earned it the name mad-dog skullcap; it had no effect on the disease, which was not preventable until Louis Pasteur's vaccine in 1885.", "src": ["NIH LiverTox: Skullcap", "FDA: Limbrel (flavocoxid) adverse event information and recall request, 2017", "Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center About Herbs: Scutellaria baicalensis", "Hui KM et al. Anxiolytic effect of wogonin, a benzodiazepine receptor ligand isolated from Scutellaria baicalensis. Biochem Pharmacol 2002"]}, "magnolia-bark": {"origin": "Magnolia officinalis is a deciduous tree native to the mountains of China, and its dried bark, called hou po, has been part of traditional Chinese medicine for roughly two thousand years. It appears in classical formulas for digestive complaints, cough and what traditional texts describe as stagnation, and it was also used in Japanese Kampo medicine in formulas for anxiety and sleep problems. Modern chemistry identified two closely related biphenyl compounds in the bark, honokiol and magnolol, which became the focus of research in Japan and China in the late twentieth century. Animal studies showed anxiety-reducing effects without the motor impairment typical of benzodiazepines at certain doses, and laboratory studies later demonstrated that both compounds enhance GABA-A receptors at a site different from the benzodiazepine site. In the United States, magnolia bark extract became popular in stress and so-called cortisol control supplements, often combined with Phellodendron bark, and in some breath mints and chewing gums for its antibacterial effects on oral bacteria. Clinical evidence remains limited to small trials, many using combination products.", "timeline": [["About 2,000 years ago", "Magnolia bark (hou po) is recorded in early Chinese materia medica."], ["1970s to 1980s", "Japanese researchers isolate and study honokiol and magnolol."], ["1990s to 2000s", "Animal studies report anxiety-reducing effects of honokiol."], ["2000s", "Magnolia and Phellodendron combinations are marketed in the US for stress and cortisol."], ["2012", "Laboratory studies characterize honokiol and magnolol as positive allosteric modulators of GABA-A receptors."]], "cell": "Honokiol and magnolol are small, lipophilic biphenyl neolignans that cross cell membranes and the blood-brain barrier easily in animal studies. In recombinant receptor experiments they act as positive allosteric modulators of GABA-A receptors, increasing chloride current in response to GABA. Their effect is not blocked by flumazenil, indicating a binding site distinct from the benzodiazepine site, and they show some preference for receptors containing certain alpha and delta subunits. Magnolol and honokiol also bind cannabinoid receptors, with partial agonist activity at CB2 in laboratory assays. Additional laboratory effects include antioxidant activity, suppression of NF-kB inflammatory signaling, antimicrobial activity against oral bacteria and inhibition of platelet aggregation. Honokiol has been studied extensively as an anticancer compound in cell and animal models, but no human anticancer benefit has been shown.", "body": [["Brain", "GABA-A enhancement in laboratory studies; calming and sleep effects, drowsiness possible."], ["Blood", "Antiplatelet effects in laboratory studies; theoretical bleeding concern with anticoagulants."], ["Mouth", "Antibacterial effects against oral bacteria; used in some breath mints and gums."], ["Stomach and intestines", "Traditional digestive use; heartburn or stomach upset possible."]], "journey": "Magnolia bark is swallowed as capsules, tablets or traditional decoctions. In animal studies honokiol and magnolol are absorbed rapidly, reaching peak blood levels within about an hour, but oral bioavailability is limited by extensive first-pass glucuronidation and sulfation in the intestine and liver. Their lipophilicity allows distribution into the brain. Elimination is mostly through bile and feces, with some enterohepatic recycling, and smaller amounts appear in urine. Human pharmacokinetic data are sparse, and the honokiol and magnolol content of commercial products varies considerably.", "sidefx": "Magnolia bark products are generally well tolerated in short studies. Drowsiness is the expected effect and is more likely when combined with benzodiazepines, sleep medicines, opioids or alcohol, given the shared GABA-A mechanism. Heartburn, stomach upset and, rarely, allergic reactions have been reported. Laboratory antiplatelet effects lead to a theoretical caution with anticoagulants and before surgery, although clinical bleeding has not been well documented. Traditional practice avoids magnolia bark in pregnancy, and no human safety data exist for pregnancy or breastfeeding.", "fun": "Magnolias are among the oldest flowering plant lineages on Earth, older than bees, and their tough flowers evolved to be pollinated mainly by beetles.", "src": ["Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center About Herbs: Magnolia officinalis", "Alexeev M et al. The natural products magnolol and honokiol are positive allosteric modulators of both synaptic and extra-synaptic GABA-A receptors. Neuropharmacology 2012", "Poivre M, Duez P. Biological activity and toxicity of the Chinese herb Magnolia officinalis Rehder and E. Wilson (Houpo) and its constituents. J Zhejiang Univ Sci B 2017"]}, "saffron": {"origin": "Saffron comes from the dried red stigmas of Crocus sativus, a sterile autumn-flowering crocus that cannot set seed and has been propagated by people from corms for thousands of years. Bronze Age frescoes from Akrotiri on the island of Thera show women gathering saffron crocus, and saffron was used in Persian, Greek, Roman, Indian and Chinese cultures as a dye, perfume, spice and medicine. Traditional Persian medicine described it as a remedy for low mood, and this tradition led Iranian researchers in the early 2000s to run a series of small randomized trials of saffron in depression. Several of these reported benefit over placebo and results similar to fluoxetine or imipramine, and meta-analyses have pooled them, but the trials are small, short and come largely from a limited number of research groups, which limits confidence. Saffron has also long been known as dangerous in large amounts, with historical use to induce abortion and reports of bleeding and severe toxicity. Because harvesting is done by hand, saffron is the most expensive spice by weight and has been adulterated for centuries.", "timeline": [["Bronze Age", "Frescoes at Akrotiri on Thera depict women gathering saffron crocus."], ["Antiquity to Middle Ages", "Saffron is traded across Persia, the Mediterranean and Asia as a dye, spice and medicine."], ["1400s", "Saffron adulteration is punished severely in parts of Europe, reflecting its high value."], ["2004 to 2007", "Small Iranian randomized trials report benefit of saffron in mild to moderate depression."], ["2013", "A meta-analysis of small trials finds saffron superior to placebo for depressive symptoms, with limitations."]], "cell": "Saffron's main bioactive compounds are crocin, a water-soluble carotenoid ester that gives the red color; crocetin, the carotenoid backbone released when crocin is hydrolyzed; picrocrocin, a bitter glycoside; and safranal, the volatile aldehyde formed from picrocrocin during drying that gives the aroma. In laboratory and animal studies, crocin and safranal are proposed to inhibit reuptake of serotonin, dopamine and norepinephrine, which is the leading explanation for antidepressant-like effects. Crocetin and crocin also bind NMDA receptors as antagonists in laboratory work, and safranal interacts with GABA-A receptors, which may contribute to anxiety-reducing and anticonvulsant effects in animals. Crocetin is an antioxidant that has been studied for retinal protection. At high exposures, saffron compounds stimulate uterine smooth muscle, and reports describe reduced platelet counts and bleeding, though the mechanism is not well defined.", "body": [["Brain", "Proposed effects on serotonin and other monoamines; small trials report improved mood."], ["Uterus", "Large amounts stimulate contractions and can cause bleeding and miscarriage."], ["Blood", "Reports of reduced platelets and bleeding with large amounts."], ["Eyes", "Crocetin studied in small trials for age-related macular degeneration."], ["Blood vessels", "Possible mild blood pressure lowering in small studies."]], "journey": "After oral intake, crocin is poorly absorbed intact. Enzymes in the intestinal lining and gut bacteria hydrolyze crocin to crocetin, which is absorbed and appears in blood within about one to two hours in limited human studies. Crocetin binds albumin in plasma and is conjugated by glucuronidation. Much of the ingested crocin and crocetin is eliminated in feces, with smaller amounts in urine. Safranal is volatile and is absorbed and metabolized quickly. Whether saffron compounds cross into the brain in meaningful amounts in people is not established, although crocetin crosses the blood-brain barrier in animal studies.", "sidefx": "In clinical trials saffron extracts were usually well tolerated, with dry mouth, nausea, headache, appetite changes, drowsiness or anxiety reported at rates similar to placebo. The serious risks come from large ingestions, which have caused vomiting, dizziness, bloody diarrhea, bleeding from the nose, lips or uterus, yellowing of the skin and eyes, and miscarriage; very large amounts have been reported as potentially fatal. Reduced platelet counts have been described in some reports. Because saffron is proposed to act on monoamine systems, combined use with antidepressants and use in bipolar disorder are theoretical cautions, although documented serotonin syndrome cases are lacking.", "fun": "Each saffron crocus flower has only three stigmas, all picked by hand, so producing a single kilogram of saffron takes well over one hundred thousand flowers, which is why it has long ranked as the most expensive spice in the world.", "src": ["Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center About Herbs: Saffron", "Hausenblas HA et al. Saffron and major depressive disorder: a meta-analysis of randomized clinical trials. J Integr Med 2013", "Moshiri M et al. Clinical applications of saffron and its constituents: a review. Drug Res 2015", "Schmidt M, Betti G, Hensel A. Saffron in phytotherapy: pharmacology and clinical uses. Wien Med Wochenschr 2007"]}, "st-johns-wort": {"origin": "Hypericum perforatum is a yellow-flowered perennial native to Europe and Asia that now grows as a weed across much of the world. It traditionally blooms around June 24, the feast of St. John the Baptist, which gave it its common name, and wort is an old English word for plant. Ancient Greek physicians, including Dioscorides, described Hypericum for wounds and other ailments, and medieval Europeans hung it in homes to ward off evil. Its use for melancholy has a long history, but the modern story began in Germany, where standardized extracts were prescribed widely for depression by the late twentieth century. A 1996 meta-analysis in the BMJ brought international attention, and American sales surged. Two discoveries in 2000 changed its reputation: a study in healthy volunteers showed St. John's wort sharply lowered levels of the HIV drug indinavir, prompting an FDA public health advisory, and reports in The Lancet described heart transplant rejection due to falling cyclosporine levels. The same year, researchers showed that hyperforin activates the pregnane X receptor. A large US trial in 2002 found no benefit over placebo in moderately severe major depression, while a 2008 Cochrane review found benefit in mild to moderate depression.", "timeline": [["Antiquity", "Greek physicians describe Hypericum for wounds and other ailments."], ["1996", "A BMJ meta-analysis reports Hypericum extracts better than placebo in mild to moderate depression."], ["2000", "Studies show St. John's wort lowers indinavir levels and cyclosporine levels with transplant rejection; FDA issues a public health advisory."], ["2000", "Hyperforin is shown to activate the pregnane X receptor, explaining CYP3A4 induction."], ["2002", "A large NIH-funded US trial finds no benefit over placebo in moderately severe major depression."], ["2008", "A Cochrane review concludes extracts are better than placebo and similar to standard antidepressants in major depression, with variable results."]], "cell": "St. John's wort works through two separate actions, one in the brain and one in the liver and gut. In the brain, hyperforin inhibits reuptake of serotonin, norepinephrine, dopamine, GABA and glutamate. Unlike SSRIs, it does not block the transporter binding site directly; laboratory work shows it activates TRPC6 cation channels, raising intracellular sodium and reducing the sodium gradient that drives neurotransmitter transporters. Chronic exposure in animals also changes receptor density in ways similar to other antidepressants. In the liver and intestine, hyperforin binds and activates the pregnane X receptor, a nuclear receptor that increases transcription of CYP3A4, CYP2C9, CYP2C19 and the efflux pump P-glycoprotein encoded by ABCB1. This speeds metabolism and reduces absorption of many drugs. Hypericin, the red pigment, contributes little to the antidepressant or induction effects but absorbs light and generates reactive oxygen species, causing photosensitivity.", "body": [["Brain", "Indirect monoamine reuptake inhibition; antidepressant effect in mild to moderate depression; serotonin syndrome with other serotonergic drugs; mania in bipolar disorder."], ["Liver", "PXR-driven induction of CYP3A4, CYP2C9 and CYP2C19 increases clearance of many drugs."], ["Intestine", "Induced P-glycoprotein pumps drugs such as digoxin and cyclosporine back into the gut lumen."], ["Skin", "Hypericin causes photosensitivity, especially with high exposure or fair skin."], ["Reproductive system", "Lower hormonal contraceptive levels lead to breakthrough bleeding and unintended pregnancy in reports."]], "journey": "St. John's wort extracts are swallowed as tablets, capsules, teas or tinctures. Hyperforin is absorbed with peak levels around three to four hours and a half-life of roughly nine to twelve hours, and it is metabolized by CYP enzymes. Hypericin and pseudohypericin are absorbed slowly, peak several hours after intake and have long half-lives of a day or more, with elimination mainly through bile. The interaction effects follow a slower timeline: enzyme and transporter induction requires new protein synthesis and develops over about one to two weeks of regular use, then fades over a similar period after stopping. Extracts low in hyperforin produce much less induction, which is why product composition matters.", "sidefx": "At usual intakes St. John's wort causes fewer side effects than older antidepressants in trials, with dry mouth, dizziness, stomach upset, fatigue, restlessness and headache most often reported. The dangers come mainly from interactions. Induction has caused heart and kidney transplant rejection, HIV treatment failure, breakthrough bleeding and pregnancies on hormonal contraceptives, reduced anticoagulation with warfarin, and lower digoxin and anticancer drug levels. Combining it with SSRIs, SNRIs, triptans, tramadol or MAOIs has been associated with serotonin syndrome. Hypericin can cause photosensitivity rashes, and rare reports describe nerve pain in sun-exposed skin. Mania has been reported in people with bipolar disorder.", "fun": "Sheep and cattle grazing heavily on St. John's wort can develop severe sunburn-like skin damage, a condition called hypericism, which is how its photosensitizing pigment was first recognized long before its drug interactions were known.", "src": ["NIH NCCIH: St. John's Wort", "Linde K, Berner MM, Kriston L. St John's wort for major depression. Cochrane Database Syst Rev 2008", "Moore LB et al. St. John's wort induces hepatic drug metabolism through activation of the pregnane X receptor. PNAS 2000", "Ruschitzka F et al. Acute heart transplant rejection due to St. John's wort. Lancet 2000", "Hypericum Depression Trial Study Group. Effect of Hypericum perforatum in major depressive disorder. JAMA 2002"]}, "hyperforin": {"origin": "For much of the twentieth century, hypericin, the red pigment, was assumed to be the antidepressant compound of St. John's wort, partly because early laboratory studies suggested it inhibited monoamine oxidase. That idea did not hold up. Hyperforin, a prenylated phloroglucinol first isolated by Soviet researchers in the 1970s and originally studied as an antibacterial compound, became the focus in the late 1990s, when German researchers showed it inhibited reuptake of serotonin, norepinephrine and dopamine in laboratory studies and that extract effects in animal models tracked hyperforin content. In 2000, Moore and colleagues demonstrated that hyperforin is a potent agonist of the pregnane X receptor, the nuclear receptor that controls CYP3A4 expression, explaining the wave of interaction reports that year. In 2007, Leuner and colleagues showed that hyperforin activates TRPC6 channels, offering a mechanism for its unusual, broad reuptake inhibition. These findings led manufacturers and researchers to develop low-hyperforin extracts, which in studies cause much weaker CYP3A4 induction, showing that the interaction risk depends on product chemistry.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Hyperforin is isolated and studied as an antibacterial compound."], ["1998", "Laboratory studies show hyperforin inhibits reuptake of several neurotransmitters."], ["2000", "Hyperforin is identified as a potent PXR agonist responsible for CYP3A4 induction."], ["2000s", "Studies show low-hyperforin extracts cause much weaker induction."], ["2007", "Hyperforin is shown to activate TRPC6 channels."]], "cell": "Hyperforin has two main molecular targets. In neurons, it activates TRPC6, a nonselective cation channel, allowing sodium and calcium to enter the cell. The rise in intracellular sodium reduces the sodium gradient that powers sodium-dependent neurotransmitter transporters, so reuptake of serotonin, norepinephrine, dopamine, GABA and glutamate falls together. This broad, indirect reuptake inhibition differs from SSRIs, which bind a specific transporter site. In hepatocytes and enterocytes, hyperforin binds the ligand-binding pocket of the pregnane X receptor with high affinity. Activated PXR pairs with the retinoid X receptor and binds response elements in genes for CYP3A4, CYP2C9, CYP2C19 and ABCB1, the gene for P-glycoprotein, increasing their expression. More enzyme means faster metabolism, and more P-glycoprotein means more drug pumped back into the gut and out into bile, producing the clinical interactions.", "body": [["Brain", "Broad indirect reuptake inhibition of monoamines, GABA and glutamate; antidepressant-like effects."], ["Liver", "PXR activation induces CYP3A4, CYP2C9 and CYP2C19, speeding drug metabolism."], ["Intestine", "P-glycoprotein induction lowers absorption of drugs such as digoxin and cyclosporine."], ["Serotonin system", "Additive effects with SSRIs, triptans and tramadol can produce serotonin syndrome."]], "journey": "Hyperforin is swallowed as part of St. John's wort extract. It is lipophilic and absorbed from the intestine, reaching peak plasma levels around three to four hours, with a half-life of roughly nine to twelve hours, so levels build with repeated daily use. It is highly protein bound and metabolized by hepatic CYP enzymes, with elimination mainly in bile and feces. Hyperforin is chemically unstable, degrading on exposure to light and oxygen, so content falls during storage. Its clinical effects on drug metabolism lag behind its blood levels, because induction requires new enzyme synthesis over about one to two weeks.", "sidefx": "The adverse effects linked to hyperforin are those of St. John's wort as a whole: dry mouth, dizziness, stomach upset and restlessness, plus the much more important interaction failures. Higher-hyperforin products are associated with stronger induction, and induction has caused transplant rejection with cyclosporine and tacrolimus, HIV treatment failure, contraceptive failure, low INR on warfarin and lower digoxin levels. Hyperforin's reuptake inhibition also contributes to serotonin syndrome risk with SSRIs, SNRIs, triptans and tramadol. Mania has been reported in bipolar disorder with St. John's wort.", "fun": "Hyperforin was first studied as an antibiotic, and it is active against some drug-resistant Staphylococcus aureus strains in the laboratory, decades before anyone suspected it was the antidepressant compound of the plant.", "src": ["Moore LB et al. St. John's wort induces hepatic drug metabolism through activation of the pregnane X receptor. PNAS 2000", "Leuner K et al. Hyperforin, a key constituent of St. John's wort, specifically activates TRPC6 channels. FASEB J 2007", "Chatterjee SS et al. Hyperforin as a possible antidepressant component of Hypericum extracts. Life Sci 1998", "NIH NCCIH: St. John's Wort"]}, "hypericin": {"origin": "Hypericin was isolated from St. John's wort in the early twentieth century and its structure, a large polycyclic naphthodianthrone, was worked out in the following decades. Long before the chemistry, farmers knew that sheep, cattle and horses grazing on St. John's wort could develop severe skin damage in sunlight, a condition called hypericism. For much of the century, hypericin was believed to be the antidepressant compound of the plant, and St. John's wort products were standardized to hypericin content, a practice that continues on many labels. Laboratory work in the 1980s showing antiviral activity against enveloped viruses, including HIV, led to clinical trials of synthetic hypericin in the 1990s; a published phase I trial found no meaningful antiviral effect and frequent phototoxic skin reactions. Attention shifted to hyperforin as the likely antidepressant compound. Hypericin's ability to generate reactive oxygen when activated by light made it a candidate for photodynamic therapy, and synthetic hypericin applied to the skin and activated by visible light has been studied in clinical trials for early-stage cutaneous T-cell lymphoma as an investigational treatment.", "timeline": [["Before 1900s", "Farmers recognize hypericism, a severe photosensitivity in livestock grazing on St. John's wort."], ["Early 1900s", "Hypericin is isolated from St. John's wort."], ["1980s", "Laboratory studies report antiviral activity of hypericin against enveloped viruses."], ["1999", "A published phase I trial in HIV finds phototoxicity and no meaningful antiviral effect."], ["2010s to 2020s", "Synthetic topical hypericin is studied as investigational photodynamic therapy for cutaneous T-cell lymphoma."]], "cell": "Hypericin is a large, flat, highly conjugated molecule that absorbs visible light strongly in the orange-red range. When it absorbs light, it enters an excited triplet state and transfers energy to molecular oxygen, producing singlet oxygen, along with superoxide and other reactive species. Because hypericin is lipophilic, it concentrates in cell membranes and organelles such as the endoplasmic reticulum and Golgi, where the reactive oxygen damages lipids and proteins, leading to cell death by apoptosis or necrosis depending on the light dose. In skin, this produces phototoxic reactions in sun-exposed areas. In photodynamic therapy, the same mechanism is directed at tumor cells by applying hypericin to lesions and illuminating them. Early laboratory claims that hypericin inhibits monoamine oxidase were not confirmed at concentrations reached in people, and hypericin contributes little to PXR activation compared with hyperforin.", "body": [["Skin", "Photosensitizer; sunburn-like rashes and, rarely, severe phototoxic reactions in sun-exposed areas."], ["Peripheral nerves", "Rare reports of burning pain in sun-exposed skin, attributed to phototoxic nerve effects."], ["Eyes", "Laboratory studies raise theoretical concern for light-induced damage to lens proteins; human relevance not established."], ["Liver and drug metabolism", "Contributes little to enzyme induction compared with hyperforin."]], "journey": "After oral intake of St. John's wort, hypericin is absorbed slowly, with a lag time of a couple of hours and peak plasma levels around four to six hours. It binds plasma lipoproteins tightly and distributes into tissues including skin. It undergoes little metabolism and is eliminated slowly, mainly in bile and feces, with a half-life of roughly one to two days; it is not detected in urine. This long half-life means levels accumulate with daily use, and photosensitivity can persist for days after stopping. Pseudohypericin follows a similar but somewhat faster course.", "sidefx": "With usual St. John's wort products, photosensitivity is uncommon, but it becomes more likely with fair skin, heavy sun exposure, other photosensitizing drugs or ultraviolet light therapy. Reactions range from exaggerated sunburn to rash and, rarely, blistering. Rare case reports describe burning nerve pain in sun-exposed skin. In the HIV trials of synthetic hypericin, phototoxic skin reactions were the main dose-limiting effect. Because hypericin is consumed as part of St. John's wort, all the herb's hyperforin-driven drug interactions and serotonin syndrome risk still apply to people taking products labeled by hypericin content.", "fun": "Hypericin glows red under ultraviolet light, and its fluorescence has been explored in medicine to help surgeons and urologists see bladder tumors during procedures, as well as to treat them with light.", "src": ["NIH NCCIH: St. John's Wort", "Gulick RM et al. Phase I studies of hypericin as an antiretroviral agent in HIV-infected adults. Ann Intern Med 1999", "Kubin A et al. Hypericin: the facts about a controversial agent. Curr Pharm Des 2005", "Karioti A, Bilia AR. Hypericins as potential leads for new therapeutics. Int J Mol Sci 2010"]}, "ginkgo": {"origin": "Ginkgo biloba is often called a living fossil: it is the only surviving species of a plant lineage that dates back more than 200 million years, and wild populations survived only in small areas of China. Buddhist monks cultivated ginkgo around temples, and the trees spread to Japan and Korea, then to Europe in the 1700s and North America later that century. In traditional Chinese medicine, the seeds rather than the leaves were the main medicinal part, used for cough and other conditions, and roasted ginkgo nuts remain a seasonal food in East Asia. The leaf extract is a modern product. In the 1960s German researchers developed standardized ginkgo leaf extracts, the best known being EGb 761, which became a widely prescribed medicine in Germany and France for cognitive symptoms and circulation problems. In the United States, ginkgo became one of the top-selling supplements by the late 1990s. The NIH-funded Ginkgo Evaluation of Memory study, published in JAMA in 2008, randomized more than three thousand older adults and found no reduction in dementia. In 2013 the National Toxicology Program reported liver and thyroid tumors in rodents given a ginkgo extract.", "timeline": [["About 200 million years ago", "The ginkgo lineage appears; Ginkgo biloba is its sole surviving species."], ["1700s", "Ginkgo trees are brought from East Asia to Europe."], ["1960s", "Standardized ginkgo leaf extracts are developed in Germany."], ["2008", "The Ginkgo Evaluation of Memory study in JAMA finds no reduction in dementia."], ["2013", "The National Toxicology Program reports liver and thyroid tumors in rodents given ginkgo extract."]], "cell": "Standardized ginkgo leaf extracts contain two main groups of compounds. Flavonol glycosides, mostly quercetin, kaempferol and isorhamnetin derivatives, act as antioxidants in laboratory systems. The terpene trilactones include ginkgolides A, B, C and J and the sesquiterpene bilobalide. Ginkgolide B is a potent antagonist of the platelet-activating factor receptor, reducing PAF-induced platelet aggregation, which is the main proposed basis for bleeding concerns. Ginkgolides and bilobalide also antagonize glycine and GABA-A receptors in laboratory studies, and bilobalide protects neurons and mitochondria in cell models. Extracts improve blood flow and reduce blood viscosity in some studies. Some studies suggest ginkgo induces CYP2C19, lowering omeprazole levels, and case reports describe reduced efavirenz levels. Crude leaves contain ginkgolic acids, allergenic compounds related to poison ivy's urushiol, which standardized extracts remove. Ginkgo seeds contain ginkgotoxin, 4'-O-methylpyridoxine, which interferes with vitamin B6 and GABA synthesis and can cause seizures.", "body": [["Blood and platelets", "Ginkgolide B blocks PAF receptors; case reports link ginkgo to bleeding, especially with anticoagulants."], ["Brain", "Antioxidant and neuroprotective effects in laboratory models; no dementia prevention in a large trial; seizure case reports."], ["Blood vessels", "Possible improved blood flow and reduced viscosity."], ["Liver and thyroid", "Rodent tumors in NTP studies; human relevance unknown."], ["Skin", "Allergic reactions; ginkgolic acids in crude material cause contact dermatitis."]], "journey": "Ginkgo extract is swallowed as tablets or capsules. Terpene lactones are well absorbed, reaching peak levels in about one to two hours; ginkgolides A and B have half-lives of several hours and are excreted largely unchanged in urine, while bilobalide is cleared somewhat faster. Flavonol glycosides are poorly absorbed intact, are hydrolyzed by gut bacteria and enzymes, and are conjugated before excretion in urine and bile. Possible induction of CYP2C19 develops with repeated use. Ginkgotoxin from seeds is absorbed quickly, and symptoms of seed poisoning typically appear within hours of eating them.", "sidefx": "Leaf extracts commonly cause mild headache, dizziness, stomach upset and allergic skin reactions. The main safety concern is bleeding: case reports describe subdural hematoma, bleeding inside the eye and bleeding after surgery in ginkgo users, often with aspirin, warfarin or other drugs, although controlled studies have not consistently shown increased bleeding. Seizures have been reported in people taking ginkgo, and ginkgo seed ingestion, especially by children, can cause vomiting, irritability and seizures. The NTP rodent tumor findings and the IARC classification of ginkgo extract as possibly carcinogenic add uncertainty about long-term use.", "fun": "Several ginkgo trees growing in Hiroshima survived the 1945 atomic bombing near the blast area, sprouted again, and are still alive today; in Japan, trees that survived the bombing are honored as A-bombed survivor trees.", "src": ["NIH NCCIH: Ginkgo", "DeKosky ST et al. Ginkgo biloba for prevention of dementia: a randomized controlled trial. JAMA 2008", "National Toxicology Program Technical Report 578: Toxicology and carcinogenesis studies of Ginkgo biloba extract, 2013", "IARC Monographs Volume 108: Ginkgo biloba extract"]}, "ginkgolides": {"origin": "Ginkgolides were first isolated from the root bark and leaves of Ginkgo biloba by Japanese chemists in the 1930s, but their structures proved extraordinarily difficult to solve. In 1967 Koji Nakanishi and colleagues published the structures of ginkgolides A, B, C and M, revealing a rigid cage of six five-membered rings, including three lactones, a tetrahydrofuran and a tert-butyl group, a chemical feature almost never seen in nature. In the 1980s French researchers led by Pierre Braquet showed that ginkgolide B is a potent antagonist of platelet-activating factor, a lipid mediator involved in platelet aggregation, inflammation and bronchoconstriction, and ginkgolide mixtures were tested in asthma and other conditions without clear clinical success. In 1988 E. J. Corey and colleagues at Harvard reported the total synthesis of ginkgolide B, a celebrated achievement in organic chemistry; Corey received the Nobel Prize in Chemistry in 1990 for his work on synthetic methods. More recently, purified ginkgolide injections have been used in China for ischemic stroke. In the United States, ginkgolides reach people only as part of ginkgo leaf extracts, where they are a key part of standardization.", "timeline": [["1930s", "Japanese chemists first isolate ginkgolides from Ginkgo biloba."], ["1967", "Koji Nakanishi and colleagues determine the cage-like structures of ginkgolides."], ["1980s", "Ginkgolide B is identified as a potent platelet-activating factor receptor antagonist."], ["1988", "E. J. Corey and colleagues report the total synthesis of ginkgolide B."], ["2010s", "Purified ginkgolide injections are used for ischemic stroke in China."]], "cell": "Platelet-activating factor is a phospholipid mediator that binds a G protein-coupled receptor on platelets, neutrophils, endothelial cells and airway smooth muscle, triggering platelet aggregation, vascular permeability and inflammation. Ginkgolide B binds the PAF receptor competitively, preventing PAF from activating it, with ginkgolides A and C being weaker. This antagonism reduces PAF-induced platelet aggregation in laboratory studies, which is the proposed basis for bleeding concerns with ginkgo, though PAF is only one of several pathways that activate platelets. Ginkgolides also block glycine receptor chloride channels in the brain and spinal cord, and ginkgolides and bilobalide antagonize GABA-A receptors in laboratory studies; these inhibitory receptor effects are one hypothesis for rare seizure reports with ginkgo. Bilobalide additionally protects mitochondria in models of brain ischemia.", "body": [["Platelets", "PAF receptor antagonism reduces aggregation in laboratory studies; possible bleeding risk."], ["Lungs and airways", "PAF antagonism studied for asthma without clear clinical benefit."], ["Brain", "Glycine and GABA-A receptor antagonism in laboratory studies; studied after stroke."], ["Kidneys", "Main route of elimination, largely unchanged."]], "journey": "Ginkgolides are swallowed as part of ginkgo leaf extract. They are well absorbed from the gut, with peak plasma levels in about one to two hours. Ginkgolides A and B have half-lives of several hours, with ginkgolide B somewhat longer, and they undergo little metabolism. Most of the absorbed dose is excreted unchanged in urine. Ginkgolide C is less well absorbed. The rigid cage structure and lactone rings make them unusually stable molecules. In China, intravenous ginkgolide preparations bypass absorption altogether.", "sidefx": "Because ginkgolides are consumed within ginkgo extract, their adverse effects overlap with ginkgo's: headache, stomach upset and, most importantly, a possible increase in bleeding, especially with warfarin, DOACs, aspirin, clopidogrel or NSAIDs. Case reports of brain, eye and postoperative bleeding involve ginkgo extracts rather than purified ginkgolides. Rare seizures reported with ginkgo may relate to ginkgolide and bilobalide antagonism of inhibitory receptors, though this is unproven. Safety of concentrated or purified ginkgolides in pregnancy, breastfeeding and children has not been established.", "fun": "The tert-butyl group in ginkgolides, a carbon attached to three methyl groups, is so rare in natural products that ginkgolides were among the first natural compounds found to contain one.", "src": ["NIH NCCIH: Ginkgo", "Nakanishi K. The ginkgolides. Pure Appl Chem 1967", "Corey EJ et al. Total synthesis of ginkgolide B. J Am Chem Soc 1988", "Strømgaard K, Nakanishi K. Chemistry and biology of terpene trilactones from Ginkgo biloba. Angew Chem Int Ed 2004"]}, "huperzine-a": {"origin": "Huperzia serrata, a club moss known in Chinese as qian ceng ta, has been used in traditional Chinese medicine for fever, swelling and other complaints. In the mid-1980s Chinese scientists isolated huperzine A from the plant and found that it is a potent, selective and reversible inhibitor of acetylcholinesterase, the enzyme that breaks down acetylcholine. Because the leading dementia treatments of the time aimed to boost acetylcholine, huperzine A was studied for Alzheimer disease in China, where it was later approved as a prescription drug for dementia and memory impairment. In the United States, huperzine A entered the market as a dietary supplement in the 1990s. The US military studied it as a possible pretreatment against nerve agents, because a reversible inhibitor can shield acetylcholinesterase from irreversible binding by organophosphates in animal studies. A US phase II trial sponsored by the Alzheimer's Disease Cooperative Study, published in 2011, did not find benefit on its main cognitive outcome in mild to moderate Alzheimer disease. Meta-analyses of mostly Chinese trials suggest benefit but rate the evidence as low quality.", "timeline": [["Traditional use", "Huperzia serrata is used in Chinese folk medicine."], ["Mid-1980s", "Chinese scientists isolate huperzine A and identify it as an acetylcholinesterase inhibitor."], ["1990s", "Huperzine A is approved in China for dementia and sold as a supplement in the US."], ["2011", "A US phase II trial in mild to moderate Alzheimer disease misses its main cognitive outcome."], ["2013", "A meta-analysis of mostly Chinese trials suggests benefit but rates trial quality as poor."]], "cell": "Acetylcholinesterase sits in synapses and neuromuscular junctions, rapidly hydrolyzing acetylcholine to end its signal. Huperzine A binds reversibly in the enzyme's active-site gorge with high affinity, blocking access of acetylcholine. It is highly selective for acetylcholinesterase over butyrylcholinesterase, and in animal studies it inhibits brain acetylcholinesterase more potently and for longer than some prescription inhibitors. Raised acetylcholine in the brain is the basis for proposed memory effects, while raised acetylcholine at peripheral muscarinic sites causes sweating, salivation, gut cramping, diarrhea, bronchial secretions and slow heart rate, and at nicotinic sites can cause muscle twitching. In laboratory studies huperzine A also antagonizes NMDA receptors and protects neurons against glutamate and amyloid toxicity. Because it binds reversibly, it can occupy the enzyme and protect it from irreversible organophosphate binding, the basis for military nerve agent research.", "body": [["Brain", "Raised acetylcholine; proposed memory effects; dizziness, insomnia; seizure concern at high exposure."], ["Heart", "Increased vagal tone can slow heart rate and conduction, especially with beta blockers or digoxin."], ["Lungs", "Bronchoconstriction and increased secretions in susceptible people."], ["Stomach and intestines", "Nausea, vomiting, cramping and diarrhea from muscarinic stimulation."], ["Muscles", "Twitching from nicotinic stimulation at high exposure."]], "journey": "Huperzine A is well absorbed after oral intake, reaching peak blood levels within about an hour or so in limited human studies. It is lipophilic enough to cross the blood-brain barrier readily. Its elimination half-life is reported at roughly ten to fourteen hours in limited data, much longer than physostigmine, so effects persist through the day and accumulate with repeated use. Human metabolism appears limited, and most of the dose is excreted unchanged in urine; animal studies suggest some CYP1A2 involvement. Kidney impairment would be expected to prolong exposure, though specific data are lacking.", "sidefx": "Most side effects are predictable cholinergic effects: nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, sweating, increased salivation, blurred vision, dizziness and insomnia. More serious effects include bradycardia and fainting, especially with beta blockers, digoxin or existing conduction disease, and bronchospasm in people with asthma or COPD. Combining huperzine A with donepezil, rivastigmine or galantamine can produce additive cholinergic toxicity. Theoretically, it may prolong the action of succinylcholine during anesthesia. Excess cholinergic stimulation can also lower the seizure threshold. Supportive care is the basis of management, and atropine is the standard treatment for symptomatic muscarinic effects in a medical setting.", "fun": "The same property that makes huperzine A a dementia candidate made it a military research target: by temporarily occupying acetylcholinesterase, it shielded the enzyme from permanent damage by nerve agents in animal studies.", "src": ["Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center About Herbs: Huperzine A", "Rafii MS et al. A phase II trial of huperzine A in mild to moderate Alzheimer disease. Neurology 2011", "Yang G et al. Huperzine A for Alzheimer's disease: a systematic review and meta-analysis. PLoS One 2013", "Wang R, Yan H, Tang XC. Progress in studies of huperzine A, a natural cholinesterase inhibitor from Chinese herbal medicine. Acta Pharmacol Sin 2006"]}, "vinpocetine": {"origin": "Vinpocetine is not found in nature. It is a semisynthetic derivative of vincamine, an alkaloid from the lesser periwinkle, Vinca minor, a groundcover plant common in European gardens. Vincamine itself was studied as a cerebral vasodilator, and in the 1970s Hungarian chemists at the pharmaceutical company Gedeon Richter developed vinpocetine, which was marketed as a prescription drug in Hungary and later in other parts of Europe and Asia for cerebrovascular disorders and cognitive impairment. Clinical trials were mostly small and older, and a Cochrane review concluded there was insufficient evidence to support its use in dementia. In the United States, vinpocetine was never approved as a drug but was sold widely as a dietary supplement for memory, focus and later weight loss. In 2016 the FDA tentatively concluded that it did not meet the legal definition of a dietary ingredient, because it is synthetic and was first studied as a drug. In 2019, after National Toxicology Program studies in animals showed reduced fetal weight and increased pregnancy loss at blood levels comparable to those from supplement use, the FDA advised pregnant women and women who could become pregnant not to take it.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Hungarian chemists at Gedeon Richter develop vinpocetine from the periwinkle alkaloid vincamine."], ["1978", "Vinpocetine is marketed as a prescription drug in Hungary."], ["2003", "A Cochrane review finds insufficient evidence for vinpocetine in dementia."], ["2016", "FDA tentatively concludes vinpocetine does not meet the definition of a dietary ingredient."], ["2019", "FDA warns that vinpocetine may cause miscarriage and fetal harm, based on NTP animal studies."]], "cell": "Vinpocetine inhibits phosphodiesterase type 1, a calcium and calmodulin-dependent enzyme that breaks down cyclic GMP and cyclic AMP. In vascular smooth muscle, higher cyclic nucleotide levels relax the muscle, widening blood vessels and modestly lowering blood pressure, which was the original rationale for use in cerebrovascular disease. In neurons, vinpocetine blocks voltage-gated sodium channels, reducing excitability and glutamate release in laboratory models, which was proposed as neuroprotection. It also inhibits IkB kinase, suppressing NF-kB-driven inflammatory gene expression in cell and animal studies, and it reduces platelet aggregation. The mechanism of fetal harm is not fully established, but NTP developmental studies in rats and rabbits showed reduced fetal weight and increased resorptions and pregnancy loss, at maternal blood levels similar to those reached by people taking supplements, which is why the FDA treated the signal as relevant to humans.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "PDE1 inhibition relaxes smooth muscle; vasodilation, flushing and blood pressure lowering."], ["Blood", "Reduced platelet aggregation; possible additive bleeding with anticoagulants and antiplatelets."], ["Brain", "Sodium channel blockade and vasodilation proposed as neuroprotective; headache and dizziness common."], ["Uterus and fetus", "Reduced fetal weight and pregnancy loss in animal studies; FDA warns against use in pregnancy."], ["Heart", "Palpitations and rhythm changes reported rarely."]], "journey": "Vinpocetine is swallowed in capsules or tablets and absorbed quickly, though oral bioavailability is low and increases when taken with food. Peak blood levels occur within roughly an hour. It is rapidly hydrolyzed, largely in the liver, to apovincaminic acid, an active metabolite that persists somewhat longer than the parent compound. Vinpocetine itself has a short half-life of about one to two hours. It is highly protein bound and crosses the blood-brain barrier. Metabolites are excreted mainly in urine. Crossing of the placenta is assumed from animal developmental findings.", "sidefx": "Common effects include headache, flushing, dizziness, nausea, stomach upset and insomnia. Vinpocetine can lower blood pressure, especially with antihypertensives or alcohol, and may add to bleeding with warfarin, DOACs, aspirin, clopidogrel or NSAIDs. Rare reports describe heart rhythm changes, reduced white blood cell counts and allergic skin reactions. The most important risk is reproductive: animal studies showed fetal harm and pregnancy loss at exposures comparable to human supplement use, leading to the 2019 FDA warning for pregnant women and women who could become pregnant.", "fun": "Vinpocetine comes from the lesser periwinkle, a cousin of the Madagascar periwinkle that gave cancer medicine vincristine and vinblastine; despite the shared vinca name, vinpocetine and the cancer vinca alkaloids act in completely different ways.", "src": ["FDA: Statement on warning pregnant women and women who could become pregnant not to take vinpocetine, 2019", "National Toxicology Program: Vinpocetine developmental toxicity studies", "Szatmari SZ, Whitehouse PJ. Vinpocetine for cognitive impairment and dementia. Cochrane Database Syst Rev 2003", "Jeon KI et al. Vinpocetine inhibits NF-kappaB-dependent inflammation via an IKK-dependent but PDE-independent mechanism. PNAS 2010"]}, "mucuna": {"origin": "Mucuna pruriens is a climbing legume of tropical Africa and Asia. In Ayurvedic medicine it is called kapikacchu, and texts traditionally linked to ancient India describe it among remedies for a condition called kampavata, characterized by tremor and slowness, which some historians have compared to Parkinson disease, although retrospective diagnosis is uncertain. The plant is also a farm crop: velvet bean has been grown as a cover crop and animal feed in many places, including the southern United States in the early twentieth century. Its pods are covered in hairs that cause intense itching, and dried cowhage hairs were used as itching powder. In the 1930s and later, chemists identified levodopa in the seeds, decades before levodopa revolutionized Parkinson treatment in the 1960s. Because seed powders are inexpensive, researchers have studied mucuna as an alternative source of levodopa in low-resource settings. Small trials published in 2004 and 2017 found motor benefits comparable to standard levodopa products in Parkinson disease, but with variable tolerability. In wealthy markets, mucuna is sold mainly in libido, mood and bodybuilding products, where users may not realize it contains a prescription-strength drug.", "timeline": [["Traditional use", "Ayurvedic medicine uses Mucuna pruriens (kapikacchu), including for tremor disorders."], ["Early 1900s", "Velvet bean is grown as a cover crop and feed in the southern United States."], ["1937", "Levodopa is reported in velvet bean seeds."], ["2004", "A small double-blind study compares mucuna seed powder with levodopa products in Parkinson disease."], ["2017", "A randomized crossover study in Neurology reports motor benefit of mucuna powder in Parkinson disease."]], "cell": "Levodopa is an amino acid precursor of dopamine. After absorption, it crosses the blood-brain barrier through the large neutral amino acid transporter, and inside the brain aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase converts it to dopamine, replacing dopamine lost from degenerating nigrostriatal neurons in Parkinson disease. Prescription products combine levodopa with carbidopa, which blocks the decarboxylase outside the brain. Mucuna lacks a decarboxylase inhibitor, so a large share of its levodopa becomes dopamine in the gut, blood vessels and liver, where dopamine stimulates the chemoreceptor trigger zone, causing nausea, and affects blood pressure. Excess dopaminergic stimulation in the brain causes dyskinesia, hallucinations and impulse control problems. With nonselective MAOIs, dopamine and norepinephrine formed from levodopa are not broken down, risking hypertensive crisis. The seed pod hairs contain mucunain, a cysteine protease that activates protease-activated receptors PAR2 and PAR4 on itch-sensing nerves.", "body": [["Brain", "Converted to dopamine; motor benefit in Parkinson disease; dyskinesia, hallucinations, psychosis and impulse control problems possible."], ["Stomach and intestines", "Peripheral dopamine causes nausea and vomiting, more than with carbidopa-levodopa."], ["Blood vessels and heart", "Orthostatic hypotension; hypertensive crisis with MAOIs; palpitations."], ["Pituitary", "Dopamine suppresses prolactin, which can reduce milk supply."], ["Skin", "Pod hairs cause intense itch through mucunain."]], "journey": "Levodopa from mucuna is absorbed in the small intestine by amino acid transporters, so high-protein meals compete with it and reduce absorption and brain entry; iron supplements also reduce absorption. Peak blood levels occur roughly thirty minutes to an hour or more after intake. Without carbidopa, much is decarboxylated to dopamine in the gut and liver, and some is methylated by COMT to 3-O-methyldopa. The plasma half-life of levodopa is short, so effects wear off within a few hours. Dopamine and its metabolites, including homovanillic acid, are excreted in urine. Pyridoxine (vitamin B6) speeds peripheral decarboxylation when no carbidopa is present, reducing the amount that reaches the brain.", "sidefx": "Because mucuna is effectively unregulated levodopa, its side effects mirror levodopa's: nausea and vomiting, which are worse without carbidopa, dizziness or fainting on standing, and headache. With higher or repeated exposure, people may develop dyskinesia, confusion, vivid dreams, hallucinations, psychosis and impulse control problems such as compulsive gambling or shopping. Combination with nonselective MAOIs can cause hypertensive crisis. Raw or poorly processed seeds eaten as food have been linked to acute toxic psychosis. In Parkinson disease, abrupt stopping can worsen symptoms, and very rarely levodopa withdrawal can trigger a severe reaction resembling neuroleptic malignant syndrome.", "fun": "Cowhage pod hairs are so reliably itchy that neuroscientists use them as a research tool: the itch they cause travels through a different nerve pathway from histamine itch, which is why antihistamines do little to relieve it.", "src": ["Cilia R et al. Mucuna pruriens in Parkinson disease: a double-blind, randomized, controlled, crossover study. Neurology 2017", "Katzenschlager R et al. Mucuna pruriens in Parkinson's disease: a double blind clinical and pharmacological study. J Neurol Neurosurg Psychiatry 2004", "Reddy VB et al. Cowhage-evoked itch is mediated by a novel cysteine protease: a ligand of protease-activated receptors. J Neurosci 2008", "Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center About Herbs: Mucuna pruriens"]}, "kanna": {"origin": "Sceletium tortuosum is a low-growing succulent from the arid regions of South Africa. The Khoikhoi and San peoples traditionally chewed the plant, often after crushing and fermenting it, to relieve thirst and hunger, ease stress, improve mood and in social and spiritual settings. Dutch colonists at the Cape recorded the use of a plant they called kanna or channa in the seventeenth century, and the name kougoed, meaning something to chew, is still used for the fermented material. Mid-twentieth-century chemists isolated mesembrine and related alkaloids, and later laboratory studies showed mesembrine to be a potent serotonin reuptake inhibitor and mesembrenone to inhibit both the serotonin transporter and phosphodiesterase 4. In the 2000s a South African company developed a standardized extract, and small studies in healthy volunteers reported effects on cognitive flexibility and reduced amygdala reactivity to fearful faces on brain imaging, a finding consistent with anxiety-reducing effects. The plant has also been sold internationally as a legal high in smoking blends and snuffs. Robust trials in depression or anxiety disorders are still lacking, and Indigenous knowledge rights around the plant have been part of benefit-sharing agreements in South Africa.", "timeline": [["Before 1600s", "Khoikhoi and San peoples chew fermented Sceletium for mood, thirst and hunger."], ["1600s", "Dutch colonists at the Cape record the use of kanna."], ["Mid-1900s", "Chemists isolate and characterize mesembrine and related alkaloids."], ["2011", "Laboratory studies show mesembrine inhibits serotonin reuptake and mesembrenone inhibits PDE4."], ["2013", "A small imaging study reports reduced amygdala reactivity with a standardized extract."]], "cell": "Mesembrine binds the serotonin transporter with high affinity in laboratory studies, blocking reuptake of serotonin from the synapse in a way similar to SSRIs. Mesembrenone also inhibits the serotonin transporter and inhibits phosphodiesterase 4, the enzyme that breaks down cyclic AMP in brain and immune cells. PDE4 inhibition raises cyclic AMP, which in animal studies is linked to antidepressant-like effects and to effects on memory and inflammation; PDE4 inhibition is also the mechanism of the prescription lung drug roflumilast, which commonly causes nausea. Combined serotonin reuptake and PDE4 effects are the proposed basis for mood and stress-reducing actions. In humans, a standardized extract reduced amygdala responses to threat, consistent with reduced anxiety, though clinical relevance is unproven. Because the mechanism overlaps with SSRIs, combination with other serotonergic drugs raises the possibility of serotonin toxicity, although documented cases are scarce.", "body": [["Brain", "Serotonin reuptake inhibition and PDE4 inhibition; possible mood and stress effects; reduced amygdala reactivity in a small study."], ["Stomach", "Nausea; traditional use to suppress hunger and thirst."], ["Heart", "Palpitations reported by some users."], ["Immune system", "PDE4 inhibition has anti-inflammatory effects in laboratory studies."]], "journey": "Kanna is chewed, swallowed as capsules or tinctures, smoked or snorted. Chewing allows some absorption through the lining of the mouth, and users report onset within minutes to an hour, while swallowed extracts act more slowly. Human pharmacokinetic studies are lacking. Animal and laboratory work suggest mesembrine alkaloids are metabolized in the liver by demethylation and other reactions, with metabolites excreted in urine. Traditional fermentation of the plant changes the alkaloid mix in laboratory analyses, which, along with plant variety and extraction, helps explain why products differ widely in strength.", "sidefx": "In small trials of a standardized extract, adverse effects were generally mild, including headache, nausea, abdominal discomfort and either sedation or restlessness. Recreational use at higher amounts has been described with euphoria, mild intoxication and nausea. The main safety concern is pharmacological: mesembrine inhibits serotonin reuptake, so use with MAOIs, SSRIs, SNRIs, tramadol, triptans or linezolid raises the possibility of serotonin syndrome, and mania is a theoretical risk in bipolar disorder. Safety in pregnancy, breastfeeding and children has not been studied.", "fun": "Kanna and kava sound alike and are both sold for stress, but they are unrelated: kava is a Pacific pepper-family root that acts on GABA-A receptors and carries liver warnings, while kanna is a South African ice-plant relative that acts on serotonin reuptake, so the two are not interchangeable in a medication history.", "src": ["Gericke N, Viljoen AM. Sceletium: a review update. J Ethnopharmacol 2008", "Harvey AL et al. Pharmacological actions of Sceletium tortuosum and its principal alkaloids. J Ethnopharmacol 2011", "Terburg D et al. Acute effects of Sceletium tortuosum (Zembrin) on amygdala reactivity. Neuropsychopharmacology 2013", "Chiu S et al. Proof-of-concept randomized controlled study of cognition effects of Sceletium tortuosum extract. Evid Based Complement Alternat Med 2014"]}, "damiana": {"origin": "Turnera diffusa is an aromatic shrub of Mexico, Central America, the Caribbean and parts of South America and the southwestern United States. Indigenous peoples of Mexico used the leaves in teas, and Spanish accounts from the colonial period describe its use as a tonic and aphrodisiac. In the late 1800s damiana was promoted heavily in the United States as a sexual tonic, appearing in patent medicines and pharmaceutical catalogs, and it was listed in American drug compendia for a time before being dropped as evidence failed to appear. In Mexico, damiana is the flavoring of a traditional liqueur. In the late twentieth and early twenty-first centuries, damiana found a new market as a legal smoking herb, and it was one of the plant materials used as a base for early synthetic cannabinoid products sold as K2 or Spice, in which inert-appearing dried leaves were sprayed with potent laboratory-made cannabinoids. Scientific study has been limited mostly to chemistry and animal experiments, including rat studies showing increased sexual behavior in sexually sluggish animals and lower blood glucose in diabetic models. No well-designed human trial has tested damiana alone.", "timeline": [["Before 1500s", "Indigenous peoples in Mexico use damiana leaf as a tea and tonic."], ["Colonial period", "Spanish accounts describe damiana as a tonic and aphrodisiac."], ["Late 1800s", "Damiana is marketed in the US as a sexual tonic in patent medicines."], ["1990s to 2000s", "Animal studies report effects on sexual behavior and blood glucose in rats."], ["2000s", "Damiana appears as a plant base in early synthetic cannabinoid smoking products."]], "cell": "Damiana's active compounds and their targets are not well established. Leaves contain flavonoids, including apigenin derivatives, which in laboratory studies bind weakly to the benzodiazepine site of GABA-A receptors and could contribute to mild calming effects. Extracts have shown nitric oxide-related vascular effects and inhibition of aromatase in some laboratory experiments, which has been proposed as a basis for sexual effects, though this is speculative. Leaves contain arbutin, a glycoside that releases hydroquinone in the body, and small amounts of cyanogenic glycosides, though amounts in usual products are not known to cause toxicity. Animal studies show improved sexual behavior in impotent or sexually exhausted rats and reduced blood glucose in diabetic animals, but the doses and preparations used do not translate directly to human use. Overall, damiana's cellular pharmacology remains a set of laboratory signals without confirmed clinical mechanisms.", "body": [["Brain", "Possible mild calming effect from flavonoids; mood claims are unproven."], ["Reproductive system", "Increased sexual behavior in rats; no proven human effect."], ["Pancreas and metabolism", "Lower blood glucose in some animal studies; theoretical additive effect with diabetes drugs."], ["Lungs", "Smoking any plant material irritates airways; smoking blends may contain synthetic cannabinoids."]], "journey": "Damiana is consumed as tea, capsules, tinctures, liqueur or smoked leaf. Human pharmacokinetic data do not exist. Flavonoids from the leaves would be expected to be partly broken down by gut bacteria, absorbed and conjugated by glucuronidation and sulfation in the gut wall and liver, then excreted in urine and bile, as with other plant flavonoids. Arbutin is absorbed and converted to hydroquinone, which is conjugated and excreted in urine. When smoked, volatile compounds and combustion products are absorbed through the lungs, but no measurements of active compounds have been made.", "sidefx": "Damiana alone has few documented adverse effects; reported complaints include stomach upset, headache, insomnia or drowsiness. Animal data suggest possible blood glucose lowering, so additive effects with insulin or other diabetes drugs are a theoretical concern. The more serious risks come from the products damiana appears in. FDA has repeatedly found undeclared prescription drugs, especially PDE5 inhibitors, in sexual enhancement supplements, which can cause dangerous low blood pressure with nitrates. Herbal smoking blends have contained synthetic cannabinoids, which cause agitation, psychosis, seizures and heart problems. Safety in pregnancy and breastfeeding has not been studied.", "fun": "Botanists have argued about where damiana belongs: it was long placed in its own family, Turneraceae, but DNA studies led many botanists to fold it into the passionflower family, making damiana a cousin of the calming herb passionflower.", "src": ["Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center About Herbs: Damiana", "Szewczyk K, Zidorn C. Ethnobotany, phytochemistry, and bioactivity of the genus Turnera. J Ethnopharmacol 2014", "FDA: Tainted sexual enhancement products"]}, "blue-lotus": {"origin": "Nymphaea caerulea is a water lily native to the Nile and other parts of Africa. Its flowers open in the morning and close at night, and in ancient Egypt it was a symbol of the sun, creation and rebirth. Blue lotus appears often in Egyptian tomb paintings, temple carvings and funerary objects, sometimes shown being held to the nose or near drinking vessels at banquets, which has led some modern writers to argue it was used as an intoxicant. Whether ancient Egyptians used it for psychoactive effects is debated among scholars, since much of the imagery may be symbolic. In recent decades blue lotus has been marketed online and in smoke shops as a legal relaxation product, sold as dried flowers for tea or smoking, extracts, resins and, more recently, vape products and infused drinks. In 2005 Louisiana passed a law prohibiting several plants, including Nymphaea caerulea, for human consumption. Chemical analyses identified aporphine alkaloids, especially nuciferine, and laboratory studies in 2016 characterized nuciferine as having an antipsychotic-like receptor profile. Poison centers and case reports have described altered mental status after use, and a 2017 report analyzed blue lotus resin used in an electronic cigarette.", "timeline": [["Ancient Egypt", "Blue lotus appears widely in Egyptian art as a symbol of the sun and rebirth."], ["2005", "Louisiana prohibits Nymphaea caerulea and several other plants for human consumption."], ["2010s", "Blue lotus is marketed online and in smoke shops for relaxation and euphoria."], ["2016", "Laboratory studies characterize nuciferine as a D2 partial agonist and 5-HT2A antagonist."], ["2017", "A published report analyzes blue lotus resin used in an electronic cigarette, identifying aporphine alkaloids."]], "cell": "The best studied blue lotus alkaloid is nuciferine, an aporphine also found in the sacred lotus, Nelumbo nucifera. In receptor screening studies, nuciferine acts as a partial agonist at dopamine D2 and D5 receptors, an antagonist at serotonin 5-HT2A, 5-HT2B and 5-HT2C receptors, and an agonist at 5-HT1A and other serotonin receptors, a mixed profile resembling some atypical antipsychotics; in mice it produced antipsychotic-like behavioral effects. Aporphine, the parent alkaloid, is also a dopamine receptor ligand. Popular claims that blue lotus contains apomorphine, a potent dopamine agonist used as a Parkinson drug, are not well supported by chemical analyses. Sedation, dreaminess and mild euphoria reported by users are not fully explained, and confusion or agitation in case reports may relate to high concentrations in resins or vapes, mixtures with other substances, or individual sensitivity.", "body": [["Brain", "Dopamine and serotonin receptor actions; relaxation, dreaminess, confusion, agitation or hallucinations; rare seizures reported."], ["Stomach", "Nausea, especially with teas and drinks."], ["Lungs", "Smoking or vaping exposes the airway to combustion products or aerosols."], ["Heart and blood vessels", "Dizziness reported; cardiovascular effects not well studied."]], "journey": "Blue lotus is swallowed as tea, tincture or infused drink, or smoked or vaped as dried flowers or resin. Inhalation produces effects within minutes, while swallowed products act more slowly. Human pharmacokinetic data are absent. In animal studies, nuciferine is absorbed after oral dosing, distributes into the brain and is metabolized in the liver by demethylation and oxidation, with metabolites excreted in urine and bile. Alcohol-based tinctures and wines add their own alcohol to the exposure. Resins and vape liquids can concentrate alkaloids, which may explain stronger effects in case reports.", "sidefx": "Users commonly report drowsiness, relaxation, dizziness and nausea. Case reports and poison center experience describe more serious effects, including confusion, disorientation, agitation, hallucinations and, rarely, seizures, particularly after vaping concentrated resins or combining blue lotus with alcohol or other substances. Because nuciferine acts on dopamine and serotonin receptors, interactions with antipsychotics and dopamine agonists are theoretical concerns. No clinical safety studies exist, and safety in pregnancy, breastfeeding and children is unknown. Product content and strength vary widely, and mislabeling is possible.", "fun": "Blue lotus is not a true lotus at all: it is a water lily, and the sacred lotus of Asia belongs to a completely separate plant lineage, even though both share the alkaloid nuciferine.", "src": ["Poison Control (National Capital Poison Center): Blue lotus: serenity in every petal, but is it safe?", "Poklis JL et al. Blue lotus (Nymphaea caerulea) resin used in a new type of electronic cigarette. J Psychoactive Drugs 2017", "Farrell MS et al. In vitro and in vivo characterization of the alkaloid nuciferine. PLoS One 2016", "Louisiana 2005 state law prohibiting certain plants for human consumption"]}, "hawthorn": {"origin": "Hawthorn is a group of thorny shrubs and small trees in the rose family that grow across Europe, Asia and North America, and the berries, leaves and flowers have a long folk history as a heart remedy in European herbal tradition and as a digestive food in traditional Chinese practice. In the twentieth century German phytomedicine turned the leaf-and-flower preparation into a standardized extract, and the German Commission E gave hawthorn leaf with flower a positive monograph for mild heart insufficiency. Clinical trials followed. Small trials from the 1980s and 1990s reported better exercise tolerance and fewer symptoms in mild heart failure, and a Cochrane review pooling those trials judged the extract a possible add-on. Two larger trials tempered that conclusion: the SPICE trial, which followed patients on standard therapy for two years, did not significantly reduce its combined cardiac endpoint, and the American HERB CHF trial found no improvement in exercise capacity or quality of life, and raised a possible, inconclusive signal of earlier heart failure progression. Hawthorn remains widely sold as a heart supplement in the United States, where it has no approved indication.", "timeline": [["Middle Ages onward", "Hawthorn is used in European folk medicine for heart complaints and as a hedge plant."], ["1980s-1990s", "German Commission E issues a positive monograph for hawthorn leaf with flower in mild heart insufficiency."], ["2008", "A Cochrane review of small trials reports symptom and exercise benefit as an add-on in chronic heart failure."], ["2008", "The SPICE trial reports no significant reduction in its combined cardiac endpoint."], ["2009", "The HERB CHF trial reports no improvement in exercise capacity or quality of life."]], "cell": "Hawthorn extracts are mixtures, and the components most often studied are oligomeric procyanidins (chains of epicatechin units) and flavonoids such as vitexin-rhamnoside and hyperoside. In isolated vessels the procyanidins cause endothelium-dependent relaxation that is blocked by nitric oxide synthase inhibitors, which points to increased endothelial nitric oxide release. In isolated heart preparations, extracts have been reported to increase contractility modestly and to prolong the refractory period; proposed explanations include weak inhibition of the sodium-potassium pump, similar in direction to digoxin but far weaker, and inhibition of phosphodiesterase, raising cyclic AMP. Antioxidant and anti-inflammatory actions have also been reported. None of these effects is tied to a single high-affinity receptor, and how much of each reaches the human heart at supplement exposures is unknown, which is part of why trial results have been modest.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Endothelium-dependent relaxation through nitric oxide in laboratory studies; small and inconsistent blood pressure lowering in people."], ["Heart muscle", "Possible mild increase in contractility and prolonged refractory period in isolated heart preparations."], ["Coronary circulation", "Increased coronary flow reported in animal studies."], ["Whole-patient outcomes", "No significant reduction in death or hospitalization in larger heart failure trials."]], "journey": "Absorption of hawthorn constituents has been studied mostly for individual flavonoids and procyanidin fragments. Flavonoid glycosides are partly absorbed in the small intestine and partly broken down by colonic bacteria; absorbed compounds are conjugated by glucuronidation, sulfation and methylation in the intestinal wall and liver. Larger procyanidin oligomers are poorly absorbed and are largely degraded by gut microbes into smaller phenolic acids that are then absorbed and excreted in urine. Detailed human pharmacokinetics of whole extracts are not well defined, and products vary in plant part and standardization, so blood levels from different products are not comparable.", "sidefx": "Hawthorn is generally well tolerated in trials, with dizziness, nausea, upset stomach, headache and palpitations reported at rates similar to placebo in many studies. The main concern is additive pharmacology: because hawthorn has been reported to dilate blood vessels and possibly affect cardiac contractility, it could in theory add to the effects of digoxin, nitrates, PDE5 inhibitors, antiarrhythmics and blood pressure medicines. A small study found no major change in digoxin pharmacokinetics, but pharmacodynamic interaction has not been well studied. Another concern is indirect: a patient with heart failure who relies on hawthorn instead of guideline therapy may be undertreated.", "fun": "The same genus that fills heart supplement capsules is a street snack in China: tanghulu, the sugar-glazed fruit skewers, are traditionally made with Chinese hawthorn berries, and dried hawthorn flakes are a common candy.", "src": ["NIH NCCIH: Hawthorn fact sheet", "Guo R, Pittler MH, Ernst E. Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews 2008: Hawthorn extract for treating chronic heart failure", "Holubarsch CJ et al. 2008, European Journal of Heart Failure: the SPICE trial", "Zick SM et al. 2009, European Journal of Heart Failure: the HERB CHF trial"]}, "aged-garlic-extract": {"origin": "Garlic has been used as food and medicine for thousands of years, appearing in ancient Egyptian, Greek, Indian and Chinese medical traditions. Its modern chemistry began in the 1940s, when Chester Cavallito and colleagues identified allicin as the pungent antibacterial compound formed when garlic is crushed, and Swiss chemists described alliin, the odorless precursor that the enzyme alliinase converts into allicin. Allicin is unstable and breaks down into many sulfur compounds, which explains why raw garlic, garlic oil, garlic powder and aged extracts have different chemistry. Aged garlic extract was developed in Japan in the twentieth century by soaking sliced garlic in a water and alcohol solution for many months; during aging, the harsh compounds largely disappear and stable water-soluble ones such as S-allylcysteine accumulate, producing a nearly odorless product. A sizeable research program, much of it funded by the manufacturer of the best-known product, has studied blood pressure, cholesterol and artery plaque. Results include modest blood pressure reductions in small trials and mixed findings elsewhere, and the extract has no FDA-recognized indication.", "timeline": [["Ancient times", "Garlic is used as food and medicine in Egypt, Greece, India and China."], ["1944", "Cavallito and Bailey report allicin as garlic's antibacterial compound."], ["Late 1940s", "Alliin and the enzyme alliinase are described as the source of allicin."], ["2002", "A small study shows garlic supplements markedly lower saquinavir levels."], ["2010s", "Small trials and meta-analyses report modest blood pressure reductions with aged garlic extract."]], "cell": "When garlic is cut, alliinase converts alliin to allicin, a reactive thiosulfinate. Aging in aqueous ethanol over many months lets allicin decompose while gamma-glutamyl-S-allylcysteine is slowly hydrolyzed to S-allylcysteine, a stable, water-soluble amino acid derivative. In laboratory studies S-allylcysteine and related compounds scavenge reactive oxygen species and increase cellular glutathione. In vascular cells, garlic sulfur compounds are reported to raise nitric oxide production and to act as hydrogen sulfide donors, both of which relax vascular smooth muscle. In platelets, aged garlic extract has been reported to raise cyclic nucleotide levels and to reduce aggregation and adhesion, though less than raw garlic preparations in some studies. Garlic supplements have also been reported to induce intestinal P-glycoprotein or CYP3A4, a proposed explanation for the drop in saquinavir levels, although the exact mechanism and whether aged extract shares it are not settled.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Nitric oxide and hydrogen sulfide signaling may relax vessels; small trials show modest blood pressure reductions."], ["Platelets", "Reduced aggregation and adhesion in laboratory and small human studies; the basis for bleeding concern."], ["Liver and gut", "Possible effects on drug transporters or metabolism, suggested by lower saquinavir levels with garlic supplements."], ["Arteries", "Small trials of coronary calcium progression report mixed results."], ["Gut", "Mild stomach upset and gas are the most common complaints."]], "journey": "S-allylcysteine is well absorbed from the gut in animal and limited human studies and reaches peak blood levels within about an hour. Unlike many plant compounds it is not heavily metabolized on first pass. The liver and kidney N-acetylate it to N-acetyl-S-allylcysteine, which is excreted in urine, and the kidneys reabsorb a large share of the parent compound, which slows its clearance and lengthens its time in the body. Other compounds in the extract, such as S-allylmercaptocysteine, are less well characterized. Allicin itself is almost absent from aged extracts, so the pharmacokinetics differ sharply from raw garlic, whose allicin-derived compounds are largely metabolized to volatile products such as allyl methyl sulfide that are exhaled, producing garlic breath.", "sidefx": "Aged garlic extract is usually well tolerated, with mild stomach upset and gas being the most common complaints, and it produces much less odor than raw garlic. The key safety issue is bleeding: garlic preparations reduce platelet aggregation, and there are reports of surgical and spontaneous bleeding with garlic products, so caution with warfarin, antiplatelet drugs and NSAIDs and disclosure before surgery are standard. A small controlled study of aged garlic extract in patients on warfarin did not find more bleeding, so the size of the risk with this particular form is uncertain. Garlic supplements lowering saquinavir levels is a reminder that HIV regimens may be affected. Allergic reactions occur rarely.", "fun": "Aged garlic extract barely smells like garlic, because the aging process destroys allicin, the compound responsible for both garlic's sharp bite and much of its odor.", "src": ["NIH NCCIH: Garlic fact sheet", "Piscitelli SC et al. 2002, Clinical Infectious Diseases: effect of garlic supplements on saquinavir pharmacokinetics", "Macan H et al. 2006, Journal of Nutrition: aged garlic extract and warfarin safety", "Cavallito CJ, Bailey JH 1944, Journal of the American Chemical Society: allicin"]}, "guggul": {"origin": "Guggul is the gum resin tapped from Commiphora wightii, a small thorny shrub of dry regions of India and Pakistan and a relative of the myrrh tree. Ayurvedic texts describe guggul for conditions interpreted as obesity and fat disorders, which led Indian researchers in the 1960s to test it for blood lipids. Early Indian studies reported lower cholesterol, an ethyl acetate extract called guggulipid was developed, and it was marketed in India as a lipid-lowering agent. In 2002 a research team reported in Science that the active steroids, guggulsterones, block the farnesoid X receptor, a bile acid sensor, and that the cholesterol-lowering effect in mice depended on this receptor, which gave guggul a modern molecular explanation. The following year, a randomized, placebo-controlled trial in the United States published in JAMA found that guggulipid did not lower LDL cholesterol in adults with high cholesterol; LDL rose slightly and skin rash was more common. Overharvesting for resin has also made the wild plant scarce, and conservation bodies list it as threatened.", "timeline": [["Ancient Ayurveda", "Guggul resin is described for fat disorders, joint pain and other conditions."], ["1960s", "Indian researchers report cholesterol-lowering effects in early studies."], ["1980s", "Guggulipid extract is marketed in India as a lipid-lowering agent."], ["2002", "Guggulsterone is identified as an FXR antagonist in mouse and cell studies."], ["2003", "A US controlled trial in JAMA finds no LDL reduction and more rash."]], "cell": "Guggulsterones are pregnane steroids structurally related to progesterone. In the liver and intestine, the farnesoid X receptor is activated by bile acids and represses CYP7A1, the enzyme that converts cholesterol into bile acids. By antagonizing FXR, guggulsterone is proposed to relieve this repression so more cholesterol is converted into bile acids, which lowered hepatic cholesterol in mice fed a high-cholesterol diet. Guggulsterones are promiscuous, however: in laboratory assays they bind the mineralocorticoid, glucocorticoid, androgen, progesterone and estrogen receptors and activate the pregnane X receptor, which increases expression of CYP3A4 and other drug-handling genes. Animal studies report increased thyroid activity and enhanced iodine uptake. The overall human effect of these overlapping actions is not well understood, which may help explain why controlled human trials did not reproduce the mouse cholesterol results.", "body": [["Liver", "FXR antagonism and possible CYP3A4 induction in laboratory studies; no LDL reduction in a controlled human trial."], ["Skin", "Itchy hypersensitivity rash is the most consistent adverse effect in trials."], ["Thyroid", "Increased thyroid activity reported in animal studies."], ["Gut", "Stomach upset, nausea and loose stools; possible reduced absorption of propranolol and diltiazem."]], "journey": "Human pharmacokinetic data for guggulsterones are limited. As lipophilic steroids they are expected to be absorbed from the gut and metabolized in the liver, and animal studies suggest extensive metabolism and short circulating times. Because guggulsterones activate PXR in cell studies, repeated exposure could in principle increase CYP3A4 and transporter expression and lower levels of drugs that depend on them, but this has not been confirmed in human studies. A small human study found that guggul lowered the absorption of propranolol and diltiazem, a separate and probably gut-level effect. Product composition varies, and crude resins also contain essential oils and gums.", "sidefx": "In the controlled US trial, the most notable adverse effect was a hypersensitivity skin rash, which was more common with guggulipid than with placebo, and some participants stopped because of it. Stomach upset, nausea, belching, loose stools and headache are also reported. Interaction concerns include lower absorption of propranolol and diltiazem, possible induction of CYP3A4-dependent drug metabolism based on laboratory data, possible thyroid effects, and theoretical additive bleeding risk from antiplatelet effects reported in laboratory studies. Rare case reports have described muscle injury and liver injury with guggul-containing products, though causation is difficult to confirm with multi-ingredient products. Some Ayurvedic preparations have been found to contain lead, mercury or arsenic in testing studies, an independent safety concern.", "fun": "Guggul and myrrh come from close relatives in the same genus, so a cholesterol capsule and one of the gifts of the Magi are botanical cousins.", "src": ["Szapary PO et al. 2003, JAMA: Guggulipid for the treatment of hypercholesterolemia", "Urizar NL et al. 2002, Science: A natural product that lowers cholesterol as an antagonist ligand for FXR", "Dalvi SS et al. 1994, Journal of Postgraduate Medicine: effect of gugulipid on bioavailability of diltiazem and propranolol", "Saper RB et al. 2008, JAMA: lead, mercury and arsenic in Ayurvedic medicines sold online"]}, "horse-chestnut": {"origin": "The horse chestnut tree is native to the mountains of the Balkans and was carried into Western Europe in the sixteenth century, where it became a favorite ornamental and street tree. Its glossy brown seeds, known in Britain as conkers, resemble edible sweet chestnuts but belong to an unrelated plant and are bitter and poisonous raw. European folk medicine used the seeds and bark for leg complaints, hemorrhoids and fever. In the twentieth century German and other European manufacturers developed standardized seed extracts based on aescin, a mixture of triterpene saponins, and these became widely prescribed in Europe for chronic venous insufficiency; injectable aescin was also used in some countries for swelling after injury or surgery. A trial published in the Lancet in the 1990s found that horse chestnut seed extract reduced lower leg volume about as much as compression stockings over several months, and a Cochrane review of placebo-controlled trials found reductions in leg pain, swelling and itching. In the United States the extract is a dietary supplement without an approved indication, and compression therapy remains the standard of care.", "timeline": [["16th century", "Horse chestnut trees are introduced from the Balkans into Western Europe as ornamentals."], ["20th century", "Standardized aescin seed extracts are developed in Europe for venous disorders."], ["1996", "A Lancet trial reports leg volume reduction similar to compression stockings."], ["2000s-2012", "Cochrane reviews find short-term benefit for leg pain and swelling in chronic venous insufficiency."]], "cell": "Aescin is a mixture of acylated triterpene glycosides built on protoescigenin and barringtogenol backbones, with beta-aescin the main active form. In laboratory and animal models it reduces the leak of fluid and protein through capillary walls, an effect attributed partly to inhibition of hyaluronidase and elastase, enzymes that degrade the proteoglycan matrix around small vessels, and partly to protection of endothelial cells from hypoxia-triggered activation and leukocyte adhesion. Aescin also increases venous tone in isolated vein preparations, possibly by sensitizing smooth muscle to calcium and through prostaglandin release. Esculin, a coumarin glycoside found in the raw seed, bark and leaves, is a different compound; it is responsible for the toxicity of the raw plant and is removed from properly processed extracts. Although coumarins are often linked with anticoagulation, esculin is not a vitamin K antagonist like warfarin, so bleeding concerns with extracts remain largely theoretical.", "body": [["Leg veins and capillaries", "Reduced capillary leak and increased venous tone; less swelling, heaviness and pain in short-term trials."], ["Gut", "Nausea and stomach upset with extracts; vomiting and diarrhea after eating raw seeds."], ["Nervous system", "Raw seed poisoning can cause twitching, weakness, incoordination and confusion."], ["Kidney", "Rare acute kidney injury reports, mostly with injected aescin."], ["Skin", "Itching and allergic reactions; topical gels sometimes used for bruises."]], "journey": "Oral aescin is poorly absorbed, and extracts are often formulated as delayed-release tablets to reduce stomach irritation. Peak blood levels occur roughly two hours after a dose in the limited human studies available. Aescin is highly bound to plasma proteins, which underlies a theoretical concern about displacing other highly protein-bound drugs. Elimination involves biliary excretion and urinary excretion of a smaller fraction, and gut bacteria break down the glycoside chains. Detailed metabolic pathways in humans are not well characterized, and product-to-product differences in aescin content affect exposure.", "sidefx": "Processed extracts are generally well tolerated in short trials; itching, nausea, upset stomach, headache and dizziness are the most common complaints. Rare reports describe allergic reactions, and kidney and liver injury have been reported mainly with injected aescin, which is not sold in the United States. Theoretical concerns include additive bleeding with anticoagulants and additive glucose lowering with diabetes medicines based on animal data. The more serious risk comes from the raw plant: eating raw seeds, leaves, bark or flowers can cause vomiting, diarrhea, abdominal pain, muscle twitching, weakness, incoordination, dilated pupils and confusion, and severe poisoning with paralysis has been described. Children are the usual victims, often after mistaking conkers for edible chestnuts.", "fun": "In Britain, the seeds are used in conkers, a traditional children's game in which players thread a horse chestnut on a string and take turns trying to smash an opponent's.", "src": ["NIH NCCIH: Horse Chestnut fact sheet", "Pittler MH, Ernst E. Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews: Horse chestnut seed extract for chronic venous insufficiency", "Diehm C et al. 1996, Lancet: comparison of leg compression stocking and oral horse-chestnut seed extract therapy", "Sirtori CR 2001, Pharmacological Research: Aescin pharmacology, pharmacokinetics and therapeutic profile"]}, "butchers-broom": {"origin": "Butcher's broom is a low, spiny evergreen shrub of the Mediterranean region and western Europe in the asparagus family. Its flat, leaf-like structures are actually modified stems, and its common name comes from the tradition that butchers bundled the stiff stems into brooms to scrub chopping blocks. The rhizome and root were used in Mediterranean folk medicine as a diuretic and for swollen legs and hemorrhoids. In the twentieth century chemists identified the steroidal saponins ruscogenin and neoruscogenin as the main active compounds. Animal studies in the 1980s and 1990s linked the extract's venous effects to alpha-adrenergic receptors in vein walls. In Europe, a combination product containing Ruscus extract, hesperidin methyl chalcone and vitamin C became widely used for chronic venous insufficiency, and a meta-analysis of its trials reported improvements in leg pain, heaviness, cramps and swelling. In the United States butcher's broom is sold as a dietary supplement, usually in venous health blends, with no approved indication, and evidence for the single herb on its own is limited.", "timeline": [["Antiquity onward", "Mediterranean folk medicine uses Ruscus root for swelling and urinary complaints."], ["20th century", "Ruscogenin and neoruscogenin are identified as the main steroidal saponins."], ["1980s-1990s", "Animal studies link the venous effects to alpha-adrenergic receptors."], ["2003", "A meta-analysis of a Ruscus, hesperidin methyl chalcone and vitamin C product reports improved venous symptoms."]], "cell": "Ruscogenin and neoruscogenin are spirostanol steroidal sapogenins released from their glycosides in the gut and in processing. In isolated canine and hamster vein preparations, Ruscus extract causes venous contraction that is reduced by alpha-1 and alpha-2 adrenergic blockers and partly by depletion of nerve norepinephrine, suggesting both direct receptor activation and promotion of norepinephrine release from sympathetic endings. Effects on calcium channels and on endothelial cells have also been proposed. Laboratory studies report inhibition of elastase and reduced leukocyte adhesion and capillary leak, which may contribute to reduced edema. Because the alpha-adrenergic action is shown in animals, whether it is strong enough in people to raise blood pressure or oppose alpha blockers is not established; these remain theoretical cautions.", "body": [["Veins", "Venoconstriction through alpha-adrenergic receptors in animal studies; possible reduced leg heaviness and swelling in people."], ["Capillaries", "Reduced permeability and fluid leak in laboratory and animal models."], ["Rectal veins", "Traditional and European use for hemorrhoids; limited trial data."], ["Gut", "Nausea, upset stomach and diarrhea are the main reported side effects."], ["Blood pressure", "Possible rise is a theoretical concern based on adrenergic effects."]], "journey": "Human pharmacokinetic data are sparse. After oral intake, ruscogenin glycosides are partly hydrolyzed in the gut, and the aglycones are absorbed to a limited extent. Small studies using radiolabeled or measured ruscogenins suggest absorption within hours and elimination through urine and bile, but metabolic pathways have not been well characterized in people. Most products combine butcher's broom with flavonoids such as hesperidin and with vitamin C, which have their own absorption profiles and make it difficult to link blood levels to effects.", "sidefx": "Butcher's broom is generally reported as well tolerated in trials of combination products. Nausea, upset stomach and diarrhea are the most frequent complaints, and allergic skin reactions have been reported rarely, including with topical products. Because ruscogenins act on alpha-adrenergic receptors in animal studies, theoretical concerns include increased blood pressure in people with hypertension, reduced effect of alpha blockers used for blood pressure or benign prostatic hyperplasia, and additive adrenergic effects with MAO inhibitors. These interactions have not been documented in controlled human studies. Safety in pregnancy, breastfeeding and children has not been established.", "fun": "Butcher's broom looks as if it grows tiny berries straight out of the middle of its leaves, but those leaves are really flattened stems called cladodes, which is why the flowers and red berries sit on their surface.", "src": ["Boyle P, Diehm C, Robertson C 2003, International Angiology: meta-analysis of Ruscus extract combination in chronic venous insufficiency", "Bouskela E and colleagues, 1990s animal microcirculation studies of Ruscus extract and adrenergic receptors (Journal of Cardiovascular Pharmacology)", "European Medicines Agency HMPC: Community herbal monograph on Ruscus aculeatus, rhizoma", "Natural Medicines-style monograph: Butcher's broom"]}, "dong-quai": {"origin": "Angelica sinensis grows in cool, high-altitude regions of western China, especially Gansu province, and its root has been one of the most used herbs in traditional Chinese medicine for nearly two thousand years. Classical texts classed it as a blood tonic, and it became a core ingredient in formulas for menstrual problems, which is why it is often called female ginseng. Western herbal markets adopted it in the late twentieth century as a natural menopause remedy. Two findings shaped its modern safety profile. In 1997, a randomized, placebo-controlled trial in postmenopausal women in California, published in Fertility and Sterility, found that dong quai alone did not reduce hot flashes and did not thicken the endometrium or change vaginal cells, arguing against a meaningful estrogen-like effect. In 1999, a case report in Pharmacotherapy described a woman stable on warfarin whose INR more than doubled after she began dong quai and returned to range after she stopped it. Together with its coumarin chemistry, this placed dong quai on most lists of herbs that raise bleeding concerns with anticoagulants.", "timeline": [["Han dynasty era", "Dang gui appears in early Chinese materia medica as a blood tonic."], ["Later dynasties", "It becomes a core herb in women's health formulas in traditional Chinese medicine."], ["Late 20th century", "Dong quai is sold in Western markets as a menopause supplement."], ["1997", "A controlled trial finds no hot flash benefit and no estrogen-like effects."], ["1999", "A case report links dong quai to a markedly raised INR with warfarin."]], "cell": "The root contains a mixture of compounds with different actions. Natural coumarins such as osthole and furanocoumarins such as bergapten, psoralen and imperatorin are present; the furanocoumarins can intercalate into DNA and absorb ultraviolet A light, forming the basis of photosensitivity. Ferulic acid inhibits platelet aggregation in laboratory studies, possibly by reducing thromboxane formation. Ligustilide, a phthalide in the volatile oil, relaxes smooth muscle in isolated tissues, including uterine and vascular muscle. Some of these compounds also inhibit cytochrome P450 enzymes in laboratory assays. Reported estrogen receptor binding is weak and inconsistent between studies, and the controlled human trial did not find estrogen-like tissue changes. The warfarin interaction may reflect a combination of antiplatelet effects, coumarin content and possible effects on warfarin metabolism, but the mechanism has not been proven.", "body": [["Blood and platelets", "Antiplatelet effects in laboratory studies; raised INR with warfarin in a case report."], ["Skin", "Furanocoumarins can cause photosensitivity reactions after sun exposure."], ["Uterus", "Smooth muscle effects in laboratory studies; traditionally avoided in pregnancy."], ["Reproductive hormones", "No estrogen-like effects on endometrium or vaginal cells in a controlled trial."], ["Gut", "Stomach upset and bloating are commonly reported."]], "journey": "Human pharmacokinetic data for whole dong quai are limited. Ferulic acid is rapidly absorbed from the gut, conjugated in the liver and excreted in urine within hours. Ligustilide is volatile and unstable, and in animal studies it is extensively metabolized with low oral bioavailability. Coumarins and furanocoumarins are absorbed and metabolized by liver enzymes, and some of them inhibit CYP enzymes in laboratory studies. Because products range from dried root slices simmered in soups to concentrated extracts and multi-herb formulas, exposure to each component varies widely.", "sidefx": "Dong quai is commonly reported to cause mild stomach upset, bloating and headache. The main concerns are bleeding and photosensitivity. A published case linked it to a more than doubled INR in a patient on warfarin, and its antiplatelet and coumarin chemistry raises concern with other anticoagulants, antiplatelets, NSAIDs and bleeding-risk supplements, as well as around surgery. Furanocoumarins can cause sunburn-like rashes and blistering after sun exposure, particularly with other photosensitizing drugs. Uncertain estrogenic activity leads many references to advise caution in hormone-sensitive cancers. It is traditionally avoided in pregnancy because of possible uterine effects, and safety in breastfeeding is not established.", "fun": "The controlled trial that found dong quai did no better than placebo for hot flashes is often noted because traditional practitioners rarely use dong quai alone; it is usually one ingredient in a multi-herb formula, so the trial tested it in a way it is not traditionally used.", "src": ["NIH NCCIH: Dong Quai fact sheet", "Page RL, Lawrence JD 1999, Pharmacotherapy: Potentiation of warfarin by dong quai", "Hirata JD et al. 1997, Fertility and Sterility: Does dong quai have estrogenic effects in postmenopausal women", "NIH Office of Dietary Supplements: background on botanical dietary supplements"]}, "bergamot-polyphenols": {"origin": "The bergamot orange, Citrus bergamia, is a sour, inedible citrus grown commercially almost entirely along a narrow strip of the Ionian coast of Calabria in southern Italy. For centuries it was valued for its peel oil, which became a classic ingredient of eau de cologne and gives Earl Grey tea its flavor. The juice, rich in flavonoids but too bitter to drink, was long treated as a by-product. In the late twentieth and early twenty-first centuries, Italian researchers identified unusual flavanone glycosides in the juice, brutieridin and melitidin, whose side chain resembles the hydroxymethylglutaryl group of statins, and proposed that the juice could lower cholesterol. A polyphenol fraction from juice and albedo was developed and marketed, and small trials, many led by a single Calabrian research group, reported reductions in LDL cholesterol, triglycerides and blood glucose. Independent replication is limited, trial sizes are small, and there are no studies showing fewer heart attacks or strokes. In the United States, citrus bergamot is sold as a dietary supplement and has no approved indication.", "timeline": [["18th century", "Bergamot oil becomes a key ingredient in eau de cologne."], ["19th century", "Bergamot oil is used to flavor Earl Grey tea."], ["2000s", "Italian researchers describe brutieridin and melitidin in bergamot juice."], ["2011", "A small trial reports lipid and glucose lowering with a bergamot polyphenol fraction."], ["2010s-2020s", "Further small trials report lipid effects; no cardiovascular outcome trials."]], "cell": "Bergamot juice contains flavanone glycosides including naringin, neohesperidin and neoeriocitrin, plus brutieridin and melitidin, in which a 3-hydroxy-3-methylglutaryl group is attached to the sugar. That group resembles the part of HMG-CoA that statins mimic, and in laboratory studies these compounds have been reported to inhibit HMG-CoA reductase, the rate-limiting enzyme of cholesterol synthesis. Other proposed actions include activation of AMP-activated protein kinase, which lowers fatty acid and cholesterol synthesis and improves glucose handling, and antioxidant and anti-inflammatory effects. How much of each compound reaches the liver intact is uncertain, because gut bacteria remove sugars from flavanone glycosides. Bergamot peel oil contains bergamottin, a furanocoumarin first identified in this fruit, which inactivates intestinal CYP3A4 like the furanocoumarins in grapefruit; it is uncertain how much is present in polyphenol supplements, so a CYP3A4 interaction is theoretical for these products.", "body": [["Liver", "Proposed reduction in cholesterol synthesis; small trials report lower LDL and triglycerides."], ["Blood vessels", "Antioxidant and endothelial effects proposed in laboratory studies; no outcome data."], ["Glucose metabolism", "Small studies report lower fasting glucose; additive effects with diabetes drugs are possible."], ["Muscle", "Muscle cramps are reported; additive concern with statins is theoretical."], ["Gut", "Heartburn and stomach upset are the most common complaints."]], "journey": "Flavanone glycosides are poorly absorbed intact in the small intestine. In the colon, bacteria remove the sugars, releasing aglycones such as naringenin, hesperetin and eriodictyol, which are absorbed, conjugated by glucuronidation and sulfation in the gut wall and liver, and excreted in urine. Ring-fission products formed by colonic bacteria are absorbed and excreted as smaller phenolic acids. Specific human pharmacokinetic data for brutieridin and melitidin are lacking, so whether they reach the liver in active form is not established. Peak levels of citrus flavanone metabolites typically occur several hours after intake because of the colonic step.", "sidefx": "In the small trials reported, bergamot polyphenol supplements were generally well tolerated, with heartburn, stomach upset and occasional muscle cramps reported. The main clinical concerns are indirect. Some people use these products in place of statins or other proven lipid therapy, which may leave high-risk patients undertreated. Combining them with statins could in theory add to lipid lowering and muscle effects, and products containing furanocoumarins could raise levels of CYP3A4-dependent drugs, although this is not established for polyphenol extracts. Additive glucose lowering is possible with diabetes medicines. Bergamot essential oil is a separate product: applied to skin, its bergapten can cause phototoxic rashes, and it is not meant to be swallowed in concentrated form.", "fun": "Bergamottin, the grapefruit-juice-style CYP3A4 inactivator found in citrus peel oils, takes its name from the bergamot orange, the fruit it was first isolated from.", "src": ["Mollace V et al. 2011, Fitoterapia: Hypolipemic and hypoglycaemic activity of bergamot polyphenols", "Di Donna L et al. 2009, Journal of Natural Products: statin-like principles of bergamot fruit (brutieridin and melitidin)", "NIH NCCIH: Cholesterol and dietary supplements overview", "Bailey DG et al. 2013, CMAJ: grapefruit and furanocoumarin drug interactions"]}, "arjuna": {"origin": "Terminalia arjuna is a large evergreen tree that lines riverbanks across India, and its thick bark has been used in Ayurvedic medicine for many centuries. Classical Ayurvedic texts describe arjuna bark for heart-related complaints, and it is often prepared as a decoction boiled in milk, a preparation called ksheerapaka. In the twentieth century Indian researchers began testing arjuna in modern trials. Small studies in the 1990s and 2000s reported fewer angina episodes, improvements in treadmill exercise tests and modest benefits in heart failure measures, and later studies looked at lipids and blood pressure. These trials were generally small, short and single-center, and some compared arjuna with standard drugs rather than placebo. No large randomized trials have shown fewer heart attacks, strokes or deaths. Arjuna remains popular in India and is sold in the United States as a dietary supplement without an approved indication; like other Ayurvedic products, some preparations have raised concerns about heavy metal contamination in independent testing.", "timeline": [["Classical Ayurveda", "Arjuna bark is described as a remedy for heart complaints."], ["Traditional practice", "Bark decoctions boiled in milk become a standard Ayurvedic heart preparation."], ["1990s", "Small Indian trials report fewer angina episodes with arjuna bark."], ["2000s-2010s", "Further small trials study heart failure, lipids and blood pressure."], ["2008", "A US study finds heavy metals in some Ayurvedic products sold online."]], "cell": "Arjuna bark contains pentacyclic triterpenoids such as arjunolic acid, arjunic acid and arjungenin and their glycosides (arjunetin, arjunglucosides), along with flavonoids such as arjunone and luteolin, abundant tannins and saponins. In laboratory and animal models these compounds scavenge free radicals, raise antioxidant enzyme activity, reduce markers of inflammation and protect heart muscle from injury caused by drugs or reduced blood flow. Extracts have been reported to improve endothelial function, lower lipids through effects on hepatic cholesterol synthesis and bile acid excretion, and slightly increase contractility in some preparations. Some components inhibit cytochrome P450 enzymes in laboratory assays. Which compound drives which effect, and how well these findings carry over to people, are not established.", "body": [["Heart", "Fewer angina episodes and better exercise measures reported in small trials; cardioprotection in animal models."], ["Blood vessels", "Improved endothelial function proposed in small studies."], ["Lipids", "Modest reductions in cholesterol reported in small studies."], ["Gut", "Stomach upset and constipation, possibly from tannins."]], "journey": "Human pharmacokinetic data on arjuna constituents are very limited. Triterpenoid glycosides are expected to be poorly absorbed and partly hydrolyzed by gut bacteria, and flavonoids are conjugated in the gut wall and liver. Tannins are largely not absorbed and act within the gut, where they can bind proteins and possibly other drugs. Traditional milk-based decoctions may change extraction of fat-soluble compounds compared with powders and capsules. Because products are rarely standardized, exposure varies widely between preparations.", "sidefx": "Arjuna is generally reported as well tolerated in small trials, with stomach upset, nausea, headache and constipation the most common complaints. Interaction concerns are largely theoretical: possible additive effects with antiplatelets and anticoagulants, nitrates and antihypertensives, and diabetes medicines, and possible CYP3A4 inhibition based on laboratory data. The bigger risks are indirect. A person with angina or heart failure may rely on arjuna instead of proven therapy, and chest pain may be attributed to a minor complaint. Some Ayurvedic products contain lead, mercury or arsenic, which can cause toxicity unrelated to the herb itself. Safety in pregnancy, breastfeeding and children has not been established.", "fun": "In the Mahabharata, Arjuna is the great archer-hero of the epic, and the tree is said to share his name; Ayurvedic writers described the bark as a protector of the heart.", "src": ["Dwivedi S, Chopra D 2014, Indian Journal of Pharmaceutical Sciences: Revisiting Terminalia arjuna", "Maulik SK, Talwar KK 2012, American Journal of Cardiovascular Drugs: Therapeutic potential of Terminalia arjuna in cardiovascular disorders", "Saper RB et al. 2008, JAMA: lead, mercury and arsenic in Ayurvedic medicines sold online"]}, "hibiscus": {"origin": "Hibiscus sabdariffa, known as roselle, is a tropical plant probably native to Africa and now grown across the tropics. Its fleshy red calyces, the cup of sepals around the flower, are brewed into sour drinks known as agua de jamaica in Mexico, bissap in West Africa, karkade in Egypt and sorrel in the Caribbean. In many of these regions the drink was traditionally used for high blood pressure and as a mild diuretic. Clinical research began in the late twentieth century. Small randomized trials, several conducted in Mexico, compared hibiscus with captopril or lisinopril in adults with mild to moderate hypertension and reported blood pressure reductions, although these trials were short and had design limitations. A placebo-controlled trial in the United States, published in the Journal of Nutrition in 2010, found that hibiscus tea modestly lowered systolic blood pressure in adults with prehypertension or mild hypertension. Systematic reviews describe the evidence as promising but limited by small, short, heterogeneous trials. Hibiscus has no FDA-recognized indication.", "timeline": [["Traditional use", "Roselle calyces are brewed into sour drinks across Africa, the Caribbean, Mexico and Asia."], ["1990s-2000s", "Small trials in Mexico compare hibiscus with captopril and lisinopril."], ["2010", "A US placebo-controlled trial reports modest systolic blood pressure lowering with hibiscus tea."], ["2010s", "Systematic reviews call the evidence promising but limited."]], "cell": "The color and much of the activity of hibiscus calyces come from anthocyanins, mainly delphinidin-3-sambubioside and cyanidin-3-sambubioside, together with organic acids such as hibiscus acid and citric acid and other polyphenols. In laboratory studies, the anthocyanins inhibit angiotensin-converting enzyme by competing at the active site, which would reduce angiotensin II formation. Extracts also relax isolated blood vessels through endothelium-derived nitric oxide and possibly calcium channel effects, and animal studies suggest a mild diuretic and natriuretic effect, perhaps involving aldosterone. Antioxidant and lipid effects have been reported in laboratory and small human studies. These overlapping mild effects may add up to the modest blood pressure changes seen in trials.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Proposed nitric oxide-mediated vasodilation; modest blood pressure lowering in small trials."], ["Kidney", "Possible mild diuretic effect in animal and small human studies."], ["Renin-angiotensin system", "ACE inhibition by anthocyanins in laboratory studies."], ["Gut", "Stomach upset and gas reported occasionally."], ["Liver", "Very large amounts linked to liver changes in animal studies only."]], "journey": "Anthocyanins are absorbed in small amounts from the stomach and small intestine, appear in blood within one to two hours, and are rapidly cleared, with only a small fraction of the intake recovered unchanged in urine. Most of the anthocyanin load reaches the colon, where bacteria break it down into phenolic acids such as protocatechuic acid, which are absorbed, conjugated in the liver and excreted in urine. Hibiscus has been reported in small studies to change the pharmacokinetics of acetaminophen and to lower chloroquine levels, though the mechanisms and clinical relevance are uncertain.", "sidefx": "Hibiscus tea is widely consumed and generally well tolerated. Stomach upset, gas and mild diuresis are reported. The main concern is additive blood pressure lowering in people on antihypertensives or diuretics, which could cause dizziness or orthostatic symptoms. Small studies reported lower chloroquine levels and altered acetaminophen pharmacokinetics. Animal studies of high doses in pregnancy raised concerns about effects on offspring and the onset of puberty, so safety in pregnancy is not established. Very high doses caused liver changes in animal studies, but this has not been shown in people drinking the tea.", "fun": "The same hibiscus calyces used in blood pressure studies are the base of agua de jamaica, a common drink in Mexico, so a trial participant and a taco stand customer may be drinking almost the same thing.", "src": ["McKay DL et al. 2010, Journal of Nutrition: Hibiscus sabdariffa tea lowers blood pressure in prehypertensive and mildly hypertensive adults", "Herrera-Arellano A et al. 2004, Phytomedicine: hibiscus compared with captopril in hypertension", "Natural Medicines-style monograph: Hibiscus", "Mahmoud BM et al. 1994, Journal of Antimicrobial Chemotherapy: hibiscus beverage and chloroquine bioavailability"]}, "olive-leaf": {"origin": "The olive tree has been cultivated around the Mediterranean for thousands of years, and its leaves were used in folk medicine for fevers and other complaints long before modern pharmacology. In the nineteenth century, olive leaf preparations were reported in European medical writing as a fever remedy. In the twentieth century chemists identified oleuropein, a bitter secoiridoid glycoside, as the leaf's main polyphenol; the same compound makes raw olives inedible until it is broken down by curing. Animal studies in the mid-twentieth century reported blood pressure lowering, and laboratory work later described antioxidant and antimicrobial activity. Olive leaf extract became popular as an immune and blood pressure supplement in the 1990s. Human evidence remains limited: a randomized trial in Indonesia, published in Phytomedicine in 2011, reported that olive leaf extract lowered blood pressure in stage 1 hypertension to a similar degree as captopril, and small trials have examined blood sugar and cholesterol with mixed results. Claims about treating infections are based on laboratory data rather than human trials. Olive leaf extract has no FDA-recognized indication.", "timeline": [["Ancient times", "Olive trees are cultivated around the Mediterranean; leaves used in folk medicine."], ["19th century", "Olive leaf preparations are described as a fever remedy in European medical writing."], ["20th century", "Oleuropein is identified as the main bitter polyphenol of olive leaves."], ["1990s", "Olive leaf extract becomes a popular dietary supplement."], ["2011", "A trial reports blood pressure lowering similar to captopril in stage 1 hypertension."]], "cell": "Oleuropein is a secoiridoid glycoside made of hydroxytyrosol linked to elenolic acid and glucose. In the gut and in tissues it is hydrolyzed to hydroxytyrosol and related compounds. In isolated arteries, oleuropein and olive leaf extracts cause relaxation that has been linked to calcium channel blocking and to increased endothelial nitric oxide. Hydroxytyrosol is a potent antioxidant in laboratory assays and reduces oxidation of LDL particles. In animal and cell studies, extracts improve insulin sensitivity and glucose uptake and may slow carbohydrate digestion. Laboratory studies report activity against some bacteria, viruses and fungi, but these effects have not been shown in people. How much of each action contributes to the modest blood pressure changes seen in trials is not known.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Proposed calcium channel and nitric oxide-mediated relaxation; modest blood pressure lowering in small trials."], ["Glucose metabolism", "Small trials report mixed glucose effects; additive lowering with diabetes drugs is possible."], ["Lipids", "Antioxidant protection of LDL in laboratory studies; mixed human results."], ["Immune system", "Antimicrobial effects in laboratory studies only."], ["Airways", "Allergic reactions possible in people with olive pollen allergy."]], "journey": "Oleuropein is partly absorbed intact from the small intestine, but much is hydrolyzed by gut enzymes and bacteria into hydroxytyrosol, which is well absorbed. Hydroxytyrosol is quickly conjugated by glucuronidation and sulfation in the gut wall and liver, peaks in blood within roughly one to two hours, and is excreted in urine within hours. Detailed human pharmacokinetics vary with formulation, and products differ substantially in oleuropein content.", "sidefx": "Olive leaf extract is generally reported as well tolerated in small trials. Stomach upset, headache and dizziness are the most common complaints. Because it may lower blood pressure and blood sugar, additive effects with antihypertensives and diabetes medicines such as insulin and sulfonylureas are possible, leading to dizziness or hypoglycemia. People allergic to olive tree pollen, a common cause of seasonal allergy in Mediterranean regions, may react to olive leaf products. Safety in pregnancy, breastfeeding and children has not been established.", "fun": "Oleuropein is so bitter that freshly picked olives are nearly inedible; brine curing, lye curing and fermentation all exist largely to break it down.", "src": ["Susalit E et al. 2011, Phytomedicine: Olive leaf extract effective in patients with stage 1 hypertension, comparison with captopril", "Natural Medicines-style monograph: Olive leaf", "Omar SH 2010, Scientia Pharmaceutica: Oleuropein in olive and its pharmacological effects"]}, "milk-thistle": {"origin": "Milk thistle is a spiny Mediterranean plant in the daisy family with white-veined leaves; legend links the white veins to drops of the Virgin Mary's milk, which gave rise to names such as Mary's thistle and the species name marianum. Greek and Roman writers including Dioscorides and Pliny described thistle remedies, and medieval European herbalists recommended the seeds for liver and gallbladder complaints. Modern study began in Germany in the 1960s and 1970s, when researchers extracted silymarin from the seeds and characterized its flavonolignans, especially silibinin. Animal experiments showed that silymarin protected the liver against toxins such as carbon tetrachloride and the death cap mushroom toxin, and silymarin products became widely prescribed in Europe for liver disease. Human trials have been disappointing: a Cochrane review found no significant effect on mortality or complications in alcohol-related and viral liver disease, and a multicenter US trial published in 2012 found that silymarin did not lower ALT in chronic hepatitis C. Milk thistle remains one of the best-selling herbal supplements in the United States and has no FDA-recognized indication.", "timeline": [["Ancient times", "Greek and Roman writers describe medicinal use of thistles."], ["Medieval Europe", "Herbalists recommend milk thistle seeds for liver and gallbladder complaints."], ["1960s-1970s", "German researchers isolate silymarin and characterize silibinin."], ["2007", "A Cochrane review finds no significant mortality benefit in alcohol-related or viral liver disease."], ["2012", "A US multicenter trial finds silymarin does not lower ALT in chronic hepatitis C."]], "cell": "Silymarin is a mixture of flavonolignans, mainly silibinin A and B (together called silybin), isosilibinin, silichristin and silidianin, plus the flavonoid taxifolin. In laboratory studies these compounds scavenge free radicals and raise glutathione in liver cells, stabilize cell membranes against lipid peroxidation, and inhibit inflammatory signaling through NF-kappa-B. Silibinin competes with certain toxins for uptake transporters on hepatocytes, notably OATP1B3, which is the basis for its use against amatoxin in some countries. Silymarin also stimulates RNA polymerase I in hepatocytes, increasing ribosomal RNA and protein synthesis, which has been proposed to support liver regeneration, and in animal models it reduces activation of hepatic stellate cells, the cells responsible for fibrosis. These effects are well described in laboratory systems, but human trials have not shown that they translate into reliable clinical benefit at supplement exposures.", "body": [["Liver", "Antioxidant, membrane-stabilizing and anti-fibrotic effects in laboratory studies; no consistent benefit in human trials."], ["Gut", "Nausea, bloating and loose stools; mild laxative effect possibly from increased bile flow."], ["Glucose metabolism", "Small trials suggest possible glucose lowering in type 2 diabetes."], ["Immune system", "Allergic reactions in people sensitive to daisy family plants."]], "journey": "Silymarin is poorly soluble in water and poorly absorbed from the gut, so only a small fraction of an oral dose reaches the blood. Absorbed flavonolignans are rapidly conjugated with glucuronic acid and sulfate in the gut wall and liver, and most circulate as conjugates. They are excreted mainly into bile, where they undergo enterohepatic circulation, with a small fraction excreted in urine. Concentrations in bile are much higher than in plasma, which manufacturers cite as a reason the liver is exposed preferentially. Formulations such as silibinin bound to phosphatidylcholine were developed to improve absorption.", "sidefx": "Milk thistle is generally well tolerated in trials, with nausea, bloating, diarrhea and upset stomach the most common complaints. Allergic reactions, including rare severe reactions, can occur in people allergic to ragweed, chrysanthemums, marigolds or daisies. Small studies suggest it may lower blood glucose, so additive effects with diabetes medicines are possible. A small study found silymarin reduced the conversion of losartan to its active metabolite, consistent with CYP2C9 inhibition, and effects on warfarin are uncertain. The biggest risk is indirect: relying on milk thistle instead of proven care for hepatitis, cirrhosis or suspected mushroom poisoning.", "fun": "Milk thistle seed extract went from an old European liver herb to an emergency antidote candidate because silibinin blocks the same liver cell transporter that the death cap mushroom toxin uses to get inside.", "src": ["NIH NCCIH: Milk Thistle fact sheet", "Rambaldi A, Jacobs BP, Gluud C. Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews 2007: Milk thistle for alcoholic and/or hepatitis B or C liver diseases", "Fried MW et al. 2012, JAMA: silymarin in chronic hepatitis C (SyNCH trial)", "NIH LiverTox: Milk Thistle"]}, "silymarin": {"origin": "Silymarin was isolated from milk thistle seeds by German researchers in the 1960s, and the structure of its main component, silibinin, was worked out around the end of that decade, establishing a new class of natural products called flavonolignans, in which a flavonoid is joined to a lignan-like unit. Animal studies showed that silymarin protected the liver against carbon tetrachloride, acetaminophen and, most strikingly, the toxins of the death cap mushroom, Amanita phalloides. This led to a water-soluble intravenous form, silibinin dihydrogen succinate, marketed in Europe as Legalon SIL and adopted in several European countries as part of treatment for amatoxin poisoning. In the United States, IV silibinin has not been approved; it was made available through an investigational new drug protocol and an open-label trial registered in 2009. Evidence comes from case series and retrospective comparisons rather than randomized trials, which are difficult in rare poisonings, so toxicologists describe it as used in some centers with hedged confidence. Oral silymarin supplements, by contrast, have been studied in chronic liver disease with largely negative results.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Silymarin is isolated from milk thistle seeds in Germany."], ["Late 1960s-1970s", "Silibinin's flavonolignan structure is determined and animal studies show protection against death cap toxins."], ["By the 1980s", "IV silibinin is used for amatoxin poisoning in Europe."], ["2009", "A US open-label investigational trial of IV silibinin for amatoxin poisoning is registered."], ["2012", "A US trial finds oral silymarin does not lower ALT in chronic hepatitis C."]], "cell": "Alpha-amanitin, the main amatoxin, is taken into hepatocytes by the sinusoidal uptake transporter OATP1B3 and to some extent by the sodium taurocholate cotransporting polypeptide. Inside the cell it binds RNA polymerase II and halts messenger RNA synthesis, so protein production stops and the cell dies. Amatoxin also undergoes enterohepatic recycling, re-exposing the liver repeatedly. Silibinin competes for OATP1B3 uptake, reducing the amount of toxin that enters hepatocytes and interrupting the recycling loop. Separately, flavonolignans scavenge free radicals, increase hepatocyte glutathione, inhibit NF-kappa-B-driven inflammation, and stimulate RNA polymerase I, increasing ribosomal RNA and protein synthesis, which may support regeneration. In laboratory studies silibinin also reduces activation of hepatic stellate cells and collagen deposition. Silibinin inhibits some CYP enzymes, particularly CYP2C9, and UGT enzymes in laboratory studies, although clinically meaningful interactions have been shown only in limited cases.", "body": [["Liver", "Blocks amatoxin uptake into hepatocytes; antioxidant and anti-fibrotic effects in laboratory studies."], ["Bile and gut", "High biliary concentrations and enterohepatic recycling; mild laxative effect orally."], ["Kidney", "Amatoxin also injures the kidney; silibinin's main proposed action is hepatic."], ["Glucose metabolism", "Possible glucose lowering in small oral trials."], ["Immune system", "Allergic reactions in people sensitive to daisy family plants."]], "journey": "Oral silymarin is poorly water-soluble and poorly absorbed, with low and variable blood levels. Absorbed silibinin is rapidly conjugated by glucuronidation and sulfation, circulates largely as conjugates bound to plasma proteins, and is excreted mainly in bile, where concentrations far exceed those in plasma; enterohepatic recycling follows. Plasma half-life of free silibinin is short, a few hours. Phosphatidylcholine complexes improve oral absorption. The IV succinate form bypasses absorption limits and produces much higher plasma levels, which is why it, not oral supplements, is the form used in poisoning.", "sidefx": "Oral silymarin is generally well tolerated, with nausea, bloating, upset stomach and loose stools reported. Allergic reactions occur in people sensitive to daisy family plants. IV silibinin has been associated with flushing and a sensation of warmth during infusion. Possible interactions include reduced activation of losartan through CYP2C9 inhibition, uncertain effects on warfarin, and additive glucose lowering with diabetes medicines. The main risk is misplaced confidence: oral supplements do not reach the blood levels of the IV form and are not a treatment for mushroom poisoning, where delays in hospital care can be fatal.", "fun": "Silibinin works against the death cap toxin largely by competing for a liver cell doorway, OATP1B3, so the antidote is less a chemical neutralizer than a bouncer at the hepatocyte door.", "src": ["ClinicalTrials.gov NCT00915681: Intravenous silibinin for hepatic failure induced by amatoxin poisoning", "NIH LiverTox: Milk Thistle", "Letschert K et al. 2006, Toxicological Sciences: OATP1B3 mediates hepatic uptake of alpha-amanitin", "Fried MW et al. 2012, JAMA: silymarin in chronic hepatitis C (SyNCH trial)"]}, "artichoke-leaf": {"origin": "The globe artichoke was developed from the wild cardoon in the Mediterranean region, and its flower bud has been eaten since classical times. Its leaves, too bitter to eat, were used in European folk medicine for digestive and liver complaints. In the first half of the twentieth century European researchers reported that artichoke leaf extracts increased bile flow in animals, and in the 1950s Italian chemists isolated cynarin, a dicaffeoylquinic acid, as a bitter active compound. Later work showed that cynarin is largely formed from another compound during extraction and that several caffeoylquinic acids and flavonoids contribute to the effects. German phytomedicine developed standardized leaf extracts for dyspepsia, and a randomized trial published in 2003 reported improvement in functional dyspepsia symptoms. A Cochrane review of cholesterol trials concluded that evidence was limited and inconsistent. In the United States, artichoke leaf extract is sold as a dietary supplement with no approved indication.", "timeline": [["Classical times", "Globe artichoke is cultivated in the Mediterranean as a food."], ["Early-mid 20th century", "European researchers report increased bile flow with artichoke leaf extracts in animals."], ["1950s", "Italian chemists isolate cynarin from artichoke."], ["2003", "A randomized trial reports improvement in functional dyspepsia."], ["2000s-2010s", "A Cochrane review finds limited and inconsistent cholesterol evidence."]], "cell": "Artichoke leaf contains caffeoylquinic acids, including chlorogenic acid and 1,3- and 1,5-dicaffeoylquinic acids (cynarin is 1,3-dicaffeoylquinic acid, much of it formed from the 1,5 isomer during processing), and flavonoids such as luteolin and its glycosides, plus bitter sesquiterpene lactones such as cynaropicrin. In animal and isolated liver models, extracts increase bile secretion, which may improve fat digestion and reduce fullness. In cultured rat hepatocytes, luteolin and related compounds inhibit cholesterol synthesis, apparently by indirectly reducing HMG-CoA reductase activity rather than binding it like a statin. Antioxidant and hepatoprotective effects are reported in laboratory studies. Bitter sesquiterpene lactones may stimulate digestive secretions through bitter taste receptors and are also responsible for contact allergy in sensitive people.", "body": [["Liver and bile", "Increased bile flow; possible reduced cholesterol synthesis in cell studies."], ["Stomach and intestines", "Reduced fullness, bloating and discomfort in small dyspepsia trials; gas and loose stools as side effects."], ["Gallbladder", "Bile flow stimulation may provoke pain with gallstones."], ["Skin", "Contact allergy possible from sesquiterpene lactones."]], "journey": "Caffeoylquinic acids are partly absorbed in the small intestine, but most reach the colon, where bacteria hydrolyze them to caffeic acid and further to dihydrocaffeic and ferulic acid derivatives. These are absorbed, conjugated by glucuronidation, sulfation and methylation in the gut wall and liver, and excreted in urine. Luteolin glycosides are deglycosylated and conjugated. Peak blood levels of metabolites appear within roughly one to a few hours, with additional colonic metabolites appearing later. Cynarin itself is barely detectable in plasma.", "sidefx": "Artichoke leaf extract is generally well tolerated in trials, with gas, bloating, loose stools and increased hunger reported. Because it stimulates bile flow, it is avoided in bile duct obstruction and used with caution in gallstone disease, where it may provoke biliary pain. Allergic reactions, including contact dermatitis, can occur in people sensitive to daisy family plants, as artichoke contains sesquiterpene lactones. There are no well-documented drug interactions, though additive effects with lipid-lowering drugs are theoretical. Safety in pregnancy, breastfeeding and children has not been established.", "fun": "Cynarin is a classic sweet-taste trick: after eating artichoke, many people find that water tastes sweet, an effect attributed to cynarin and chlorogenic acid briefly blocking sweet taste receptors, which then rebound when rinsed.", "src": ["Holtmann G et al. 2003, Alimentary Pharmacology and Therapeutics: efficacy of artichoke leaf extract in functional dyspepsia", "Wider B et al. Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews: Artichoke leaf extract for treating hypercholesterolaemia", "Gebhardt R 1998, Journal of Pharmacology and Experimental Therapeutics: inhibition of cholesterol biosynthesis by artichoke leaf extracts in hepatocytes"]}, "dandelion": {"origin": "Dandelion is native to Eurasia and now grows on nearly every continent, and it has been eaten as a spring green and used as medicine for centuries. Medieval Arabic and Persian medical writers are reported to have described it, and European herbals of the Renaissance recommended it for liver complaints and as a diuretic. Its French folk name, pissenlit, meaning wet the bed, reflects the long-held belief that it increases urination. Dandelion root was listed in the official pharmacopeias of several countries, including the United States, in the nineteenth and early twentieth centuries as a bitter tonic and mild laxative. Modern research has been limited mostly to animal and laboratory studies; the most cited human study is a small pilot study published in 2009, which found that leaf extract increased urination frequency over the following hours. The European Medicines Agency recognizes dandelion for traditional use in mild digestive complaints and to increase urine flow, based on long-standing use rather than clinical trials. In the United States, dandelion is sold as a food and dietary supplement and has no approved indication.", "timeline": [["Medieval period", "Arabic and Persian medical writers are reported to describe dandelion as a medicine."], ["Renaissance", "European herbals recommend dandelion for liver complaints and as a diuretic."], ["19th-early 20th century", "Dandelion root appears in official pharmacopeias, including in the United States."], ["2009", "A small pilot study reports increased urination after dandelion leaf extract."], ["2009-2010s", "The European Medicines Agency publishes a traditional-use monograph for dandelion."]], "cell": "Dandelion contains bitter sesquiterpene lactones such as taraxinic acid glycosides, triterpenes such as taraxasterol and taraxerol, phenolic acids such as chicoric and chlorogenic acids, and in the root large amounts of inulin, a fructose polymer that acts as prebiotic fiber. The leaves are rich in potassium, which has been proposed to contribute to the diuretic effect by offsetting potassium loss, and in vitamins A, C and K. In animal studies, leaf extracts increase urine output, though the specific compounds responsible are unclear. Bitter compounds stimulate bitter taste receptors and may increase saliva, gastric and bile secretions, which is the traditional basis for its use as a digestive bitter. Laboratory studies report antioxidant, anti-inflammatory and glucose-related effects, but these have not been confirmed in human trials.", "body": [["Kidney", "Possible mild increase in urine output; potassium-rich leaf."], ["Liver and bile", "Bitter compounds may stimulate bile flow; avoided in bile duct obstruction."], ["Gut", "Inulin feeds colonic bacteria; may cause gas, upset stomach or loose stools."], ["Skin", "Contact dermatitis from latex and allergic reactions in daisy family allergy."]], "journey": "Human pharmacokinetic data are lacking. Phenolic acids such as chicoric and chlorogenic acid are partly absorbed and partly metabolized by gut bacteria, then conjugated and excreted in urine. Inulin is not digested in the small intestine and is fermented by colonic bacteria to short-chain fatty acids. Sesquiterpene lactones and triterpenes are expected to be metabolized in the liver, but their fate in people has not been well characterized. In the small human pilot study, increased urination was seen within hours of a dose.", "sidefx": "Dandelion is generally regarded as low risk when eaten as food. Reported side effects of supplements include upset stomach, heartburn, diarrhea and increased urination. Allergic reactions and contact dermatitis can occur, especially in people allergic to ragweed, chrysanthemums, marigolds or daisies. Theoretical concerns include additive effects with diuretics and lithium, high potassium with potassium-sparing drugs or kidney disease, and reduced ciprofloxacin absorption seen in an animal study. Because bitter compounds may stimulate bile flow, dandelion is avoided in bile duct obstruction and used with caution in gallstones.", "fun": "Roasted dandelion root has a long history as a coffee substitute, especially during wartime coffee shortages, and roasted dandelion drinks are still sold today; unlike coffee, they contain no caffeine.", "src": ["Clare BA, Conroy RS, Spelman K 2009, Journal of Alternative and Complementary Medicine: The diuretic effect in human subjects of an extract of Taraxacum officinale folium", "European Medicines Agency HMPC: Community herbal monograph on Taraxacum officinale", "Natural Medicines-style monograph: Dandelion"]}, "peppermint-oil": {"origin": "Peppermint is a natural hybrid of watermint and spearmint that was recognized and cultivated in England in the late seventeenth century, and mint plants had been used for digestive complaints since antiquity. Peppermint oil, distilled from the leaves, became a standard carminative in European and American pharmacy, and peppermint water was a long-standing remedy for colic and indigestion. In the twentieth century researchers showed that peppermint oil relaxes intestinal smooth muscle, and it was used by endoscopists to reduce spasm during procedures. Because swallowing the oil caused heartburn, enteric-coated capsules were developed in the 1970s and 1980s to release the oil in the small intestine. Randomized trials in irritable bowel syndrome followed, and meta-analyses have repeatedly found improvement in global symptoms and abdominal pain compared with placebo. The American College of Gastroenterology 2021 guideline gives a conditional recommendation for peppermint oil in IBS. In the United States, capsules are sold as dietary supplements or medical foods and are not FDA-approved drugs, while menthol is separately an OTC ingredient in topical analgesics and cough drops.", "timeline": [["Late 1600s", "Peppermint is recognized as a distinct mint in England."], ["18th-19th centuries", "Peppermint oil and peppermint water become standard digestive remedies in pharmacy."], ["1970s-1980s", "Enteric-coated peppermint oil capsules are developed and studied in IBS."], ["2000s-2010s", "Meta-analyses find improvement in global IBS symptoms with peppermint oil."], ["2021", "The ACG IBS guideline gives a conditional recommendation for peppermint oil."]], "cell": "Menthol, the main active monoterpene, blocks voltage-gated L-type calcium channels in gastrointestinal smooth muscle, reducing calcium entry and muscle contraction, an action similar in direction to calcium channel blockers but local to the gut. It also activates TRPM8, the cold-sensing ion channel, on sensory nerves, which may produce cooling sensations and reduce visceral pain signaling, and it may affect kappa-opioid and serotonin receptors in laboratory studies. Peppermint oil has antimicrobial effects in laboratory studies and may alter gut bacteria. Relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter explains heartburn when oil is released in the stomach, and local release in the rectum explains perianal burning. In infants, menthol near the nose can trigger reflex breathing changes, and large swallowed amounts of concentrated oil act on the central nervous system, causing sedation and seizures.", "body": [["Intestines", "Smooth muscle relaxation through calcium channel blockade; less cramping and pain in IBS."], ["Esophagus", "Lower esophageal sphincter relaxation; heartburn and reflux."], ["Sensory nerves", "TRPM8 activation causes cooling sensation and may reduce pain signaling."], ["Brain", "CNS depression and seizures after swallowing concentrated essential oil."], ["Airway", "Menthol near infants' faces has been linked to breathing problems; aspiration risk after oil ingestion."]], "journey": "Enteric-coated capsules pass the stomach intact and release oil in the small intestine, delaying menthol appearance in blood by a few hours compared with uncoated oil. Menthol is rapidly absorbed, undergoes extensive first-pass glucuronidation in the liver, and is excreted in urine as menthol glucuronide, with a short half-life. Some oxidation by liver CYP enzymes also occurs. Peppermint oil and menthol inhibit CYP3A4 in laboratory studies, and peppermint oil raised cyclosporine levels in animal studies, though human relevance is uncertain. When stomach acid is reduced, enteric coatings may dissolve early, releasing oil in the stomach.", "sidefx": "Enteric-coated peppermint oil is generally well tolerated in IBS trials. Heartburn, belching with a mint taste, nausea, dry mouth and perianal burning are the most common complaints, and allergic reactions occur rarely. People with reflux or hiatal hernia may have worse symptoms. Acid reducers may increase heartburn by dissolving the coating early. The serious risks are in children: swallowing concentrated essential oil can cause burning of the mouth and throat, vomiting, drowsiness, slow breathing, ataxia and seizures, and coughing after ingestion raises concern for aspiration. Menthol products applied near the face of infants have been linked to breathing problems.", "fun": "The cool feeling from mint is a sensory illusion: menthol opens TRPM8, the same nerve channel that responds to cold temperatures, so the brain reports cold even though nothing has cooled.", "src": ["NIH NCCIH: Peppermint Oil fact sheet", "Lacy BE et al. 2021, American Journal of Gastroenterology: ACG Clinical Guideline: Management of Irritable Bowel Syndrome", "Ford AC et al. 2008, BMJ: effect of fibre, antispasmodics and peppermint oil in IBS, systematic review and meta-analysis", "McKemy DD, Neuhausser WM, Julius D 2002, Nature: identification of a cold receptor (TRPM8)"]}, "slippery-elm": {"origin": "The slippery elm is a medium-sized tree of eastern North America, named for its fragrant, slick inner bark. Many Indigenous peoples of the region used the inner bark for sore throats, coughs, wounds, burns and digestive complaints, and as a food in hard times. European settlers adopted it, and powdered slippery elm bark became a standard remedy in nineteenth-century American medicine for coughs, diarrhea and as a soothing gruel for invalids. It was listed in the United States Pharmacopeia for many years as a demulcent. In the twentieth century, slippery elm lozenges remained popular throat remedies. Research has been sparse: most support comes from its obvious physical behavior, since the inner bark forms a thick gel in water, and from traditional use; small pilot studies have tested multi-ingredient formulas containing slippery elm for irritable bowel symptoms. Dutch elm disease, a fungal infection that spread through North American elms in the twentieth century, has also affected slippery elm populations. In the United States it is sold as a dietary supplement without an approved indication.", "timeline": [["Pre-colonial era", "Indigenous peoples of eastern North America use inner bark for sore throats, wounds and food."], ["19th century", "Powdered slippery elm becomes a standard American demulcent remedy."], ["19th-20th centuries", "Slippery elm bark is listed in the United States Pharmacopeia as a demulcent."], ["20th century", "Dutch elm disease spreads through North American elms."], ["2010", "A small pilot study tests formulas containing slippery elm for IBS symptoms."]], "cell": "The active material of slippery elm is mucilage, a complex mixture of water-soluble polysaccharides built mostly from galacturonic acid, rhamnose and galactose. When the powdered inner bark meets water, these long chains hydrate and entangle into a viscous, slippery gel. On the throat and esophagus, the gel forms a protective film that reduces contact between irritants and nerve endings, the basis for its demulcent action. Some authors propose that contact with the upper gut triggers reflex mucus secretion further along the digestive tract. In the intestine, the polysaccharides act like soluble fiber, holding water and increasing stool bulk, and they are fermented by colonic bacteria to short-chain fatty acids. Tannins in the bark may have mild astringent effects. Laboratory studies report antioxidant activity, but these effects are of uncertain relevance.", "body": [["Throat", "Gel coats irritated tissue, soothing sore throat and cough."], ["Esophagus and stomach", "Coating may ease heartburn and irritation."], ["Intestines", "Acts like soluble fiber, holding water; may slow absorption of other oral drugs."], ["Colon", "Mucilage is fermented by bacteria to short-chain fatty acids."]], "journey": "Slippery elm mucilage is not absorbed into the bloodstream. It acts locally from the mouth to the colon. In the small intestine, it increases the viscosity of gut contents, which can slow the movement of drugs to the absorbing surface or physically trap them, potentially reducing or delaying absorption of medicines taken at the same time. In the colon, bacteria ferment the polysaccharides, and remaining material is excreted in feces. Because it is not absorbed, there are no meaningful blood levels or half-life.", "sidefx": "Slippery elm is generally regarded as low risk. Mild nausea and bloating may occur. Allergic reactions are possible, particularly in people allergic to elm pollen, and contact with the bark has caused skin irritation. The most relevant concern is reduced or delayed absorption of other oral medicines taken at the same time, which matters most for drugs with a narrow range between effective and toxic levels. Whole elm bark has a folk history as an abortifacient when inserted into the cervix, which is distinct from oral inner bark products, but safety in pregnancy has not been established.", "fun": "Slippery elm gruel, made by stirring the powdered bark into hot water or milk, was a common food for the sick in nineteenth-century America, so the same powder sold in capsules today once sat somewhere between medicine and porridge.", "src": ["Natural Medicines-style monograph: Slippery elm", "Hawrelak JA, Myers SP 2010, Journal of Alternative and Complementary Medicine: effects of two natural medicine formulations on irritable bowel syndrome symptoms", "United States Pharmacopeia historical monographs: Ulmus (slippery elm bark)"]}, "marshmallow-root": {"origin": "Marshmallow is a tall perennial of the mallow family that grows in salt marshes and damp meadows of Europe, western Asia and North Africa, which explains its common name. Its genus name, Althaea, comes from a Greek word meaning to heal, and Greek and Roman physicians described the mallows for coughs, sore throats and wounds. Medieval and later European herbalists used the root in syrups and poultices. The connection to candy came in nineteenth-century France, where confectioners whipped the root's mucilage with sugar and egg whites into a soft sweet called pate de guimauve; later manufacturers replaced the root with gelatin, and modern marshmallows contain none of the plant. Marshmallow root stayed in European pharmacy as a cough and throat remedy, and the European Medicines Agency recognizes it as a traditional herbal medicine for dry cough, mouth and throat irritation and mild stomach discomfort, based on long-standing use. Clinical evidence consists of small, mostly uncontrolled studies, often of multi-ingredient syrups. In the United States it is sold as a dietary supplement without an approved indication.", "timeline": [["Ancient Greece and Rome", "Mallow plants are used for coughs, sore throats and wounds."], ["Medieval Europe", "Marshmallow root is used in syrups and poultices by herbalists."], ["19th century", "French confectioners make marshmallow candy from the root's mucilage."], ["20th century", "Gelatin replaces the root in commercial marshmallow candy."], ["2010s", "The European Medicines Agency publishes a traditional-use monograph for marshmallow root."]], "cell": "Marshmallow root contains a high proportion of mucilage, made of acidic polysaccharides rich in galacturonic acid, rhamnose, arabinose and glucuronic acid, along with pectin, starch, flavonoids and phenolic acids. In water the polysaccharides swell into a viscous gel that adheres to mucous membranes, forming a protective layer that shields irritated tissue and may reduce stimulation of cough receptors in the throat. Laboratory studies report that marshmallow polysaccharides adhere to epithelial cells, stimulate their growth and internalization of the polysaccharides, and reduce markers of inflammation, which may support repair of irritated mucosa. In animal studies extracts suppressed cough and lowered blood glucose, but these effects are not established in people.", "body": [["Throat", "Gel coats irritated tissue and may reduce cough reflex sensitivity."], ["Mouth", "Soothes irritation of oral mucosa."], ["Stomach and intestines", "Coating may ease mild discomfort; may slow absorption of other oral drugs."], ["Glucose metabolism", "Possible glucose lowering in animal studies only."]], "journey": "The mucilage of marshmallow root is not absorbed into the bloodstream and acts locally on the mouth, throat and digestive tract. Cold-water infusions are traditionally used because heat extracts more starch along with the mucilage. In the gut, the gel increases the viscosity of intestinal contents, which may slow absorption of other oral medicines. In the colon, bacteria ferment the polysaccharides, and remaining material is excreted in feces. Flavonoids and phenolic acids are present in small amounts and are absorbed and conjugated like other plant phenolics.", "sidefx": "Marshmallow root is generally considered low risk. Mild stomach upset is reported occasionally, and allergic reactions are rare. The main practical concern is reduced or delayed absorption of other oral medicines taken at the same time. Animal studies suggest possible glucose lowering, and many syrups contain sugar, both relevant in diabetes. Safety in pregnancy, breastfeeding and children has not been established. Indirect risk comes from relying on herbal cough remedies when a cough is persistent or accompanied by fever, blood or trouble breathing.", "fun": "The fluffy candy got its name from this marsh plant: the original sweet was made by whipping the root's slimy gel with sugar, long before gelatin took over the recipe.", "src": ["European Medicines Agency HMPC: Community herbal monograph on Althaea officinalis, radix", "Natural Medicines-style monograph: Marshmallow", "Deters A et al. 2010, Journal of Ethnopharmacology: aqueous extracts and polysaccharides from marshmallow roots and epithelial cells"]}, "aloe-vera": {"origin": "Aloe vera is a succulent probably native to the Arabian Peninsula and now grown worldwide. Aloe was used in ancient Egypt, Greece and Rome, and Dioscorides described the bitter dried latex as a purgative and the leaf for wounds. Dried aloe latex, called bitter aloes, was a standard purgative in Western pharmacopeias for centuries. In the twentieth century, attention turned to the clear inner gel, which became a ubiquitous ingredient in burn and skin products, and to oral aloe juices sold for digestion and wellness. Two regulatory events reshaped oral aloe. In 2002, the FDA issued a final rule declaring that aloe and cascara sagrada were not generally recognized as safe and effective as OTC laxative ingredients, because manufacturers had not submitted the safety data FDA requested, including carcinogenicity data. Then a National Toxicology Program two-year study, published as Technical Report 577, found clear evidence of carcinogenic activity in the large intestine of rats given non-decolorized whole leaf aloe extract in drinking water. LiverTox now describes oral aloe as a likely but rare cause of liver injury.", "timeline": [["Ancient times", "Aloe is used in Egypt, Greece and Rome for wounds and as a purgative."], ["Centuries of pharmacy", "Bitter aloes, the dried latex, is a standard purgative in Western pharmacopeias."], ["2002", "FDA removes aloe and cascara from OTC stimulant laxatives for lack of safety data."], ["2005", "Liver injury from oral aloe is first described in the medical literature."], ["2010s", "NTP Technical Report 577 reports intestinal tumors in rats given non-decolorized whole leaf extract."]], "cell": "The aloe leaf has three layers. The inner parenchyma contains the gel, about 99 percent water plus polysaccharides such as acemannan, a partly acetylated mannan, along with glycoproteins and small molecules. In laboratory studies acemannan stimulates macrophages and fibroblasts and may promote wound repair, and the gel has anti-inflammatory and moisturizing effects. Just beneath the rind, pericyclic cells produce the yellow latex, rich in anthraquinone C-glycosides, mainly aloin A and B. Aloin passes unabsorbed to the colon, where bacteria cleave it to aloe-emodin anthrone, the active laxative. This compound stimulates colonic smooth muscle and enteric nerves, inhibits water and sodium absorption, and increases chloride and water secretion, partly through prostaglandins and changes in aquaporin expression. The result is faster transit and watery stool, with potassium loss. Anthraquinones are also suspected in the intestinal tumors seen in rats, which is why decolorized products, filtered to remove most anthraquinones, were developed.", "body": [["Skin", "Gel soothes and moisturizes; possible faster healing of minor burns with mixed evidence; contact irritation in some."], ["Colon", "Latex anthrones stimulate motility and fluid secretion; melanosis coli with long-term use; tumors in rats with whole leaf extract."], ["Kidney and electrolytes", "Potassium and fluid loss from diarrhea; case reports of acute kidney injury."], ["Liver", "Rare hepatocellular injury with oral aloe, likelihood B in LiverTox."], ["Heart", "Low potassium can trigger arrhythmias, especially with digoxin."]], "journey": "Topical gel acts locally with negligible absorption. Oral aloin is poorly absorbed in the small intestine and reaches the colon largely intact, where bacterial enzymes convert it to aloe-emodin anthrone and aloe-emodin. A small fraction of these metabolites is absorbed, conjugated in the liver and excreted in urine, which may turn reddish; most is excreted in feces. The laxative effect typically appears six to twelve hours after ingestion because of the time needed to reach the colon. Polysaccharides from oral gel are largely fermented by colonic bacteria.", "sidefx": "Topical aloe gel is generally well tolerated, though redness, itching and contact dermatitis occur, and it is not suited to deep or severe burns. Oral latex and whole leaf products cause abdominal cramps and diarrhea, and heavier or prolonged use can lead to dehydration, low potassium, muscle weakness and arrhythmias, particularly in people taking digoxin, diuretics or corticosteroids. Long-term use can cause melanosis coli, a brown pigmentation of the colon lining. Oral aloe has been linked to rare hepatocellular liver injury and case reports of acute kidney injury. Oral gel may lower blood glucose in small trials, adding to diabetes medicines. Oral latex is avoided in pregnancy, breastfeeding and young children.", "fun": "Aloe is one plant but two products: the clear gel in a sunburn bottle and the bitter yellow latex once sold as a purgative come from different cell layers of the same leaf, millimeters apart.", "src": ["FDA Final Rule, Federal Register 67 FR 31125, May 9, 2002: status of certain OTC laxative active ingredients", "NIH LiverTox: Aloe Vera", "National Toxicology Program Technical Report 577: toxicology and carcinogenesis studies of a nondecolorized whole leaf extract of Aloe barbadensis Miller", "NIH NCCIH: Aloe Vera fact sheet"]}, "cascara-sagrada": {"origin": "Cascara sagrada, Spanish for sacred bark, comes from a buckthorn tree native to the Pacific Northwest of North America. Indigenous peoples of the region used the bark as a laxative, and it entered American medicine in the late nineteenth century after a Detroit pharmaceutical firm introduced it to physicians in the 1870s; it was added to the United States Pharmacopeia in the 1890s. Cascara quickly became one of the most widely used laxatives in the country, and heavy harvesting stripped many wild trees of their bark. For decades it was a common ingredient in OTC laxatives. Under the FDA's review of OTC drugs, manufacturers were asked to provide safety data, including carcinogenicity data, for stimulant laxative ingredients. When those data were not submitted, the FDA issued a final rule in May 2002 stating that aloe and cascara sagrada were not generally recognized as safe and effective as OTC laxative ingredients, removing them from OTC drug products. Cascara remains available as a dietary supplement, especially in herbal laxative, cleanse and detox teas.", "timeline": [["Pre-colonial era", "Indigenous peoples of the Pacific Northwest use the bark as a laxative."], ["1870s", "Cascara is introduced to American physicians as a laxative."], ["1890s", "Cascara sagrada is added to the United States Pharmacopeia."], ["20th century", "Cascara becomes a common OTC laxative ingredient."], ["2002", "FDA removes cascara and aloe from OTC laxatives for lack of safety data."]], "cell": "The bark contains anthraquinone glycosides, chiefly cascarosides A through D, which are C- and O-glycosides of aloin-type and chrysaloin-type anthrones, plus smaller amounts of emodin and related compounds. The glycosides resist digestion in the small intestine and are activated in the colon, where bacterial glycosidases release anthrones. These anthrones stimulate the myenteric plexus and colonic smooth muscle, increasing propulsive contractions, and they alter epithelial transport by inhibiting sodium and water absorption and increasing chloride secretion, partly through prostaglandin release and reduced aquaporin expression. The net result is faster transit and watery stool. Fresh bark contains free anthrones that irritate the stomach and cause severe vomiting and cramping; aging the bark for at least a year or heating it oxidizes these to gentler compounds. Long-term exposure leads to apoptosis of colonic epithelial cells, whose debris is taken up by macrophages as lipofuscin pigment, visible as melanosis coli.", "body": [["Colon", "Increased motility and fluid secretion; melanosis coli with long-term use."], ["Kidney and electrolytes", "Loss of potassium, sodium and water; dehydration can injure the kidney."], ["Heart", "Low potassium can trigger arrhythmias, especially with digoxin."], ["Liver", "Rare case reports of cholestatic or hepatocellular injury."], ["Urine", "Anthraquinone metabolites may discolor urine yellow-brown or red."]], "journey": "Cascarosides are poorly absorbed in the upper gut and reach the colon largely unchanged. There, bacteria remove sugars to form active anthrones, which act locally. A small fraction of anthraquinone metabolites is absorbed, conjugated in the liver and excreted in urine and bile, which can change urine color depending on pH. Most material leaves in feces. Because activation depends on colonic bacteria, the laxative effect is delayed, typically six to twelve hours, and may be blunted by antibiotics that alter gut flora.", "sidefx": "Abdominal cramping and diarrhea are the expected effects. With frequent or heavy use, cascara can cause dehydration, low potassium and sodium, muscle weakness and arrhythmias, particularly with digoxin, diuretics, corticosteroids or QT-prolonging drugs. Long-term use causes melanosis coli and may lead to dependence on laxatives for bowel movements. Rare case reports describe liver injury, including cholestatic hepatitis. Misuse for weight loss or purging, common in eating disorders, can produce dangerous electrolyte disturbances. It is avoided in bowel obstruction, unexplained abdominal pain, inflammatory bowel disease, pregnancy, breastfeeding and children.", "fun": "Fresh cascara bark is too harsh to use: it must sit for at least a year, or be heat-treated, before it becomes the milder laxative sold in products, an unusual case of an herb that improves by being stored.", "src": ["FDA Final Rule, Federal Register 67 FR 31125, May 9, 2002: status of certain OTC laxative active ingredients", "Nadir A et al. 2000, American Journal of Gastroenterology: cascara sagrada-induced intrahepatic cholestasis causing portal hypertension", "European Medicines Agency HMPC: Community herbal monograph on Rhamnus purshianus D.C., cortex", "Natural Medicines-style monograph: Cascara"]}, "berberine": {"origin": "Berberine is a yellow alkaloid found in the roots, rhizomes and bark of several unrelated plants, including goldenseal of eastern North America, European barberry, Oregon grape and the Chinese herb coptis (huang lian). Plants containing it have been used for centuries in traditional Chinese and Ayurvedic medicine and by Indigenous peoples of North America, especially for diarrhea, infections and as a yellow dye. Berberine was used in China as an over-the-counter remedy for infectious diarrhea, and Chinese researchers noticed lower blood sugar in diabetic patients taking it, prompting clinical study of its metabolic effects. In 2004, a study published in Nature Medicine reported that berberine lowers cholesterol through a mechanism distinct from statins, by stabilizing LDL receptor messenger RNA, and later work showed it activates AMP-activated protein kinase, the same energy-sensing enzyme influenced by metformin. Many small trials, largely from China, followed, and meta-analyses report modest improvements in glucose, A1c and lipids, though trial quality is variable. In the 2020s, social media promotion drove a surge in US sales as a supposed natural alternative to prescription weight-loss drugs, despite much smaller effects.", "timeline": [["Centuries of traditional use", "Coptis, barberry and goldenseal are used for diarrhea, infections and as dyes."], ["20th century", "Berberine is used in China for infectious diarrhea; glucose lowering is noticed."], ["2004", "A Nature Medicine study reports berberine lowers LDL by stabilizing LDL receptor mRNA."], ["2012", "A human study shows repeated berberine inhibits CYP2D6, CYP2C9 and CYP3A4."], ["2020s", "Social media promotion boosts US sales as a natural weight-loss product."]], "cell": "Berberine is a quaternary isoquinoline alkaloid with a permanent positive charge, which limits its passage through membranes. Inside cells it inhibits mitochondrial respiratory complex I, raising the AMP to ATP ratio and activating AMPK. AMPK activation increases glucose uptake in muscle, reduces gluconeogenesis and lipogenesis in the liver, and improves insulin sensitivity. Separately, berberine stabilizes LDL receptor messenger RNA through an ERK-dependent pathway and reduces PCSK9 expression, increasing hepatic LDL uptake, an effect additive to statins in laboratory studies. In the gut, where much of the dose remains, berberine changes the microbiota, and animal studies suggest increased GLP-1 secretion from intestinal L cells, though berberine does not bind the GLP-1 receptor. Berberine inhibits CYP2D6 strongly and CYP3A4 and CYP2C9 more modestly, and it competes with bilirubin for albumin binding, the basis of kernicterus concern in newborns.", "body": [["Liver", "AMPK activation reduces glucose and fat production; LDL receptor upregulation lowers LDL; CYP inhibition raises levels of other drugs."], ["Muscle", "Increased glucose uptake through AMPK."], ["Gut", "Constipation, diarrhea and cramping; changes in gut bacteria; possible increased GLP-1 secretion in animals."], ["Blood and newborns", "Displaces bilirubin from albumin, raising kernicterus risk in infants."], ["Cardiovascular system", "Possible blood pressure lowering (reported)."]], "journey": "Berberine is poorly absorbed, with oral bioavailability well under one percent, because intestinal P-glycoprotein pumps it back into the gut lumen and the gut wall and liver metabolize it extensively on first pass. Plasma concentrations are therefore very low, and gut effects and active metabolites may explain part of its activity. Metabolism involves CYP-mediated demethylation to berberrubine, thalifendine and other metabolites, followed by glucuronidation. Elimination is mainly through bile and feces, with a small urinary fraction. Tissue levels, especially in liver, exceed plasma levels. Repeated use over about two weeks reduced CYP2D6, CYP2C9 and CYP3A4 activity in a human study.", "sidefx": "Gastrointestinal effects are the most common: constipation, diarrhea, cramping, gas and nausea. The key safety concerns are interactions. Berberine can add to diabetes medicines and cause hypoglycemia, especially with insulin and sulfonylureas. By inhibiting CYP3A4, CYP2D6 and CYP2C9, it can raise levels of many drugs; a small study in kidney transplant patients found raised cyclosporine levels. Berberine crosses the placenta and passes into breast milk, and because it displaces bilirubin from albumin, exposure in pregnancy, breastfeeding or the newborn period carries a risk of severe jaundice and kernicterus. Low blood pressure has also been reported.", "fun": "Berberine glows: under ultraviolet light it fluoresces bright yellow, and cell biologists have used it as a stain, so the same molecule sold in blood sugar capsules has also lit up structures under the microscope.", "src": ["NIH NCCIH: Goldenseal fact sheet (berberine content)", "Kong W et al. 2004, Nature Medicine: Berberine is a novel cholesterol-lowering drug working through a unique mechanism distinct from statins", "Guo Y et al. 2012, European Journal of Clinical Pharmacology: Repeated administration of berberine inhibits cytochromes P450 in humans", "Wu X et al. 2005, European Journal of Clinical Pharmacology: effects of berberine on cyclosporine pharmacokinetics in renal transplant recipients", "Yin J, Xing H, Ye J 2008, Metabolism: efficacy of berberine in patients with type 2 diabetes"]}, "goldenseal": {"origin": "Goldenseal is a perennial of the buttercup family native to the deciduous forests of eastern North America. Several Native American peoples, including the Cherokee, used the bright yellow rhizome as a dye and as a wash and remedy for sore eyes, skin problems and digestive complaints, and European settlers adopted it. In the nineteenth century it became a staple of American botanical practice, promoted by Eclectic physicians and sold widely in patent medicines, and demand from that trade, together with loss of forest habitat, sharply reduced wild populations. That pressure continues today because most goldenseal sold is still harvested from the wild, and the plant is listed under CITES Appendix II, which controls international trade. Goldenseal never became an approved medicine. Its modern clinical importance comes from pharmacology research showing that it is one of the most potent herbal inhibitors of drug-metabolizing enzymes tested in people, together with a persistent street myth that it can mask illegal drugs on urine screens, which testing has not supported.", "timeline": [["1800s", "Goldenseal becomes a popular remedy among American Eclectic physicians and in patent medicines, depleting wild stands."], ["1997", "Goldenseal is listed in CITES Appendix II to regulate international trade in the wild root."], ["2000s", "Clinical probe-drug studies show goldenseal inhibits CYP2D6 and CYP3A4 in healthy volunteers."], ["2010", "A National Toxicology Program report describes liver tumors in rodents given goldenseal root powder long term in their diet."], ["2020s", "NIH-funded studies examine goldenseal interactions with drugs such as midazolam and metformin."]], "cell": "The main alkaloids are berberine, a quaternary protoberberine, and hydrastine, a phthalide isoquinoline, with smaller amounts of canadine and related compounds. Hydrastine and berberine inhibit cytochrome P450 enzymes, and methylenedioxyphenyl groups in these alkaloids can be converted into reactive intermediates that bind tightly to the enzyme, a mechanism-based inhibition that helps explain why the effect can outlast the product in the body. In human studies using probe drugs, goldenseal markedly reduced CYP2D6 activity, as measured with debrisoquine, and reduced CYP3A4 activity, as measured with midazolam. Berberine also interacts with transporters and has broad antimicrobial activity in culture, intercalating with DNA and disrupting bacterial cell division proteins, but the concentrations reached in human blood after oral use are far below those used in the lab. Berberine also binds albumin and can displace bilirubin, which is the basis for concern in newborns, whose bilirubin handling is immature.", "body": [["Liver", "Inhibition of CYP3A4 and CYP2D6 slows clearance of many prescription drugs; liver tumors were seen in long-term high-exposure rodent studies."], ["Intestine", "Local antimicrobial and antidiarrheal effects of berberine are studied; gut CYP3A4 inhibition can raise absorption of some drugs."], ["Blood and newborn", "Berberine can displace bilirubin from albumin, raising concern for jaundice and kernicterus in exposed infants."], ["Metabolism", "A small study found lower metformin levels with goldenseal, which may matter for glucose control."], ["Immune system", "Claimed immune boosting for colds is not supported by good human evidence."]], "journey": "Goldenseal is swallowed as root powder, extracts or tinctures. Hydrastine is absorbed reasonably well and reaches peak blood levels within a few hours in limited studies, while berberine is poorly absorbed, with very low oral bioavailability because of intestinal efflux by P-glycoprotein and extensive first-pass metabolism in the gut wall and liver. Both alkaloids are metabolized in the liver by demethylation and conjugation, and metabolites leave mainly in bile and urine. The interaction effects depend less on blood levels of the alkaloids than on enzyme inactivation in the intestine and liver, which recovers only as new enzyme is made, so effects can persist for some days after the product is stopped.", "sidefx": "Most people report few direct side effects, mainly nausea, stomach upset or constipation. The important harms are indirect. By inhibiting CYP3A4 and CYP2D6, goldenseal can raise blood levels of medicines such as some benzodiazepines, statins, calcium channel blockers, antidepressants and transplant drugs, producing toxicity from a medicine that was previously well tolerated. Lower metformin levels in a small study raise a question about glucose control. Exposure during pregnancy, breastfeeding or infancy carries a risk of worsening newborn jaundice. Older reports describe nausea, vomiting and nervous system effects after very large ingestions, but these are poorly documented.", "fun": "The belief that goldenseal can beat a urine drug test appears to trace back to a nineteenth-century novel by the pharmacist John Uri Lloyd, in which the plant is used to fool a test for poison; modern testing has not shown that goldenseal hides drugs.", "src": ["NIH NCCIH: Goldenseal fact sheet", "Gurley BJ et al. In vivo effects of goldenseal on CYP2D6 and CYP3A4, Clinical Pharmacology and Therapeutics", "National Toxicology Program Technical Report on goldenseal root powder", "CITES Appendix II listing for Hydrastis canadensis"]}, "fenugreek": {"origin": "Fenugreek is one of the oldest cultivated plants, a clover-like annual of the legume family grown around the Mediterranean, the Middle East and South Asia. Seeds have been found at ancient archaeological sites in the Near East, and the herb appears in ancient Egyptian, Greek and Roman sources as food, fodder and medicine; the species name foenum-graecum means Greek hay, reflecting its use as animal feed. In Indian cooking the seeds, called methi, flavor curries and pickles, and the leaves are eaten as a vegetable. In traditional Ayurvedic and Middle Eastern practice fenugreek has long been used for digestion, diabetes and to increase breast milk, and those three themes carried directly into the modern supplement market. Western interest grew as researchers looked for food-based approaches to type 2 diabetes and as lactation consultants recommended fenugreek teas. Despite this history, human evidence remains thin, and safety reviews stress its legume allergy potential, uncertain safety in pregnancy and its distinctive odor.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Fenugreek is cultivated in the Near East and used by Egyptian, Greek and Roman cultures for food and medicine."], ["Traditional", "Ayurvedic, Middle Eastern and folk medicine use the seed for digestion, diabetes and breast milk supply."], ["1990s-2000s", "Small clinical trials report modest glucose lowering with fenugreek seed products."], ["2011", "A large E. coli O104:H4 outbreak centered in Germany is traced to fenugreek seeds used for sprouting."], ["2010s", "Reviews conclude evidence for lactation and testosterone claims is weak and of low quality."]], "cell": "Most of the seed's mass is galactomannan, a soluble fiber that swells into a viscous gel in the stomach and small intestine. That gel slows gastric emptying and the contact of starches and sugars with digestive enzymes and absorptive surfaces, blunting the rise in blood glucose after meals in the same way other viscous fibers do. The unusual amino acid 4-hydroxyisoleucine stimulated glucose-dependent insulin release from isolated pancreatic islets in laboratory studies and has been proposed to improve insulin signaling, but how much it contributes in people is unclear. Steroidal saponins such as diosgenin may bind cholesterol and bile acids in the gut. The galactagogue claim has no well-defined mechanism; proposed explanations include effects on prolactin or a general effect of the phytoestrogenic saponins, neither of which is established. The aroma compound sotolon, a lactone, is absorbed and excreted in sweat and urine, which explains the maple syrup smell.", "body": [["Gut", "Soluble fiber gel slows carbohydrate absorption and commonly causes gas, bloating and loose stools."], ["Pancreas and metabolism", "Modest lowering of blood glucose in small trials; additive effects with diabetes medicines are possible."], ["Skin and urine", "Sotolon gives sweat and urine a maple syrup odor, including in breastfed infants of mothers using fenugreek."], ["Immune system", "As a legume, it can trigger allergy, with reported cross-reactivity to peanut and chickpea."], ["Uterus", "Linked to uterine contractions; supplement amounts are considered possibly unsafe in pregnancy."]], "journey": "Fenugreek is eaten as a spice, swallowed as capsules or powder, or steeped as tea. The galactomannan fiber is not absorbed; it travels through the small intestine as a gel, is fermented partly by colon bacteria, and leaves in the stool. Smaller constituents such as 4-hydroxyisoleucine, trigonelline and saponin fragments are absorbed to varying degrees, but human pharmacokinetic data are sparse. Sotolon is absorbed and excreted largely unchanged in sweat and urine, and it can appear in breast milk, which is how a nursing infant can develop the odor. Because the fiber can slow absorption of other oral medicines taken at the same time, spacing is often discussed by pharmacists.", "sidefx": "The most common effects are digestive: gas, bloating and diarrhea. A maple syrup odor of sweat, urine and breast milk is frequent and harmless in itself, but it can cause confusion with maple syrup urine disease in newborns. People with diabetes using insulin or sulfonylureas may experience hypoglycemia. Allergic reactions, from nasal symptoms and wheezing to anaphylaxis, have been reported, especially in people with legume allergy. Case reports describe raised INR in patients on warfarin, so bleeding signs matter. Concern in pregnancy stems from animal data and reports of uterine effects. Rare liver injury has been reported, usually with multi-ingredient products where the role of fenugreek is uncertain.", "fun": "The maple syrup smell is real chemistry: sotolon, the same aroma compound that helps give maple syrup and some aged wines their sweet note, is so potent that imitation maple flavors are often made using fenugreek.", "src": ["NIH NCCIH: Fenugreek fact sheet", "NIH LactMed: Fenugreek", "Robert Koch Institute and EFSA reports on the 2011 E. coli O104:H4 outbreak linked to fenugreek sprouts", "Natural medicine monograph data on fenugreek safety and interactions"]}, "triphala": {"origin": "Triphala, Sanskrit for three fruits, is among the best-known formulas of Ayurveda, the traditional medical system of the Indian subcontinent. It is described in classical Ayurvedic texts as a rasayana, a rejuvenating preparation, and as a mild bowel regulator. The three fruits come from trees native to South Asia: amla or Indian gooseberry from Emblica officinalis (Phyllanthus emblica), haritaki from Terminalia chebula, and bibhitaki from Terminalia bellirica. Each is dried, powdered and blended, traditionally in equal parts, though proportions differ between makers. In India triphala is an inexpensive household remedy, often taken as powder stirred into warm water. It reached Western markets through the growth of Ayurvedic and wellness retail in the late twentieth century. Research since then has focused on its tannin and polyphenol chemistry, with many laboratory studies and few rigorous human trials. A separate issue shaped public health attention to the Ayurvedic market as a whole: studies published in JAMA found lead, mercury or arsenic in a substantial share of Ayurvedic products sold in the US, some deliberately added in traditional metal-containing preparations.", "timeline": [["Classical era", "Triphala is described in classical Ayurvedic texts as a rejuvenating and bowel-regulating formula."], ["Late 1900s", "Ayurvedic products including triphala become widely sold in Western health food stores."], ["2004", "A JAMA study finds heavy metals in about one in five Ayurvedic products bought in the Boston area."], ["2008", "A follow-up JAMA study finds lead, mercury or arsenic in about one in five Ayurvedic products sold online."], ["2010s", "Small trials study triphala for constipation and as a mouthwash for dental plaque."]], "cell": "The fruits in triphala are dominated by hydrolyzable tannins, including chebulinic and chebulagic acids, along with gallic acid, ellagic acid and flavonoids. Tannins precipitate proteins and bind metal ions, which accounts for their astringent taste and their in vitro antimicrobial effects, including against oral bacteria, supporting interest in triphala as a mouthwash. Haritaki in particular has a traditional reputation as a purgative; proposed laxative mechanisms include stimulation of colonic motility and changes in water secretion, but these are not well characterized at the molecular level. Laboratory studies report antioxidant, anti-inflammatory and growth-inhibiting effects in cell lines, but most use concentrations that may not be reached in the body. Gut bacteria convert ellagitannins into urolithins, which are absorbed and may account for some of the systemic activity attributed to the blend.", "body": [["Colon", "Mild laxative effect with softer, more frequent stools; cramping and diarrhea with larger amounts."], ["Mouth", "Tannins reduced plaque and gingival inflammation as a mouthwash in small trials."], ["Gut bacteria", "Polyphenols are transformed by gut microbes into urolithins and other metabolites."], ["Blood and nerves", "Not affected by triphala itself, but contaminated Ayurvedic products can cause lead-related anemia and neuropathy."], ["Kidneys", "Fluid and electrolyte loss with heavy laxative use can strain kidney function."]], "journey": "Triphala is swallowed as powder in water or as tablets and capsules. Large tannin molecules are poorly absorbed and act mainly in the gut, where they bind proteins and are broken down by intestinal bacteria. Smaller phenolic compounds such as gallic acid are absorbed in the small intestine, conjugated in the liver and excreted in urine. Ellagitannins are converted by colonic bacteria into urolithins, which are absorbed, conjugated and excreted over the following day or two. Human pharmacokinetic studies of the full blend are limited, and the effect people notice, a laxative response, depends on local action in the colon rather than blood levels.", "sidefx": "The usual effects are digestive: loose stools, cramping, gas and, with larger amounts or combined laxatives, diarrhea with fluid and electrolyte loss. Allergic reactions are possible. Tannins may bind iron and some medicines in the gut when taken together, though clinical importance is uncertain. The most serious risks come not from the fruits but from contaminated products: lead, mercury and arsenic have been found in Ayurvedic medicines in the US, and lead poisoning presents with anemia, abdominal pain, constipation, fatigue, neuropathy and cognitive changes, with children and pregnant people most vulnerable.", "fun": "Amla, one of triphala's three fruits, is so sour and astringent that eating it makes water taste sweet afterward, a well-known effect in India that reflects how its tannins and acids change taste perception.", "src": ["Saper RB et al. Heavy metal content of Ayurvedic herbal medicine products, JAMA 2004", "Saper RB et al. Lead, mercury, and arsenic in US- and Indian-manufactured Ayurvedic medicines sold via the Internet, JAMA 2008", "Peterson CT et al. Therapeutic uses of triphala in Ayurvedic medicine, Journal of Alternative and Complementary Medicine 2017", "CDC: Lead poisoning associated with Ayurvedic medications"]}, "dgl-licorice": {"origin": "Licorice root, from the Mediterranean and Asian plant Glycyrrhiza glabra, is one of the oldest documented herbal medicines, used in ancient Egyptian, Greek, Chinese and Ayurvedic traditions for coughs, stomach complaints and to sweeten other remedies. Its sweetness comes from glycyrrhizin, a saponin many times sweeter than sugar. In the twentieth century licorice became a real gastrointestinal drug: physicians in Europe noticed that licorice extracts helped peptic ulcers, and the glycyrrhetinic acid derivative carbenoxolone was developed in Britain as an ulcer treatment before H2 blockers. The same work exposed the downside. Patients developed fluid retention, high blood pressure and low potassium, a syndrome that looked like excess aldosterone but with low aldosterone levels. Researchers later traced it to inhibition of the kidney enzyme that inactivates cortisol. Deglycyrrhizinated licorice was developed to keep possible mucosal benefits while removing most of the glycyrrhizin. With the arrival of H2 blockers and then proton pump inhibitors, DGL faded from mainstream medicine and survives today as a chewable supplement for heartburn.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Licorice root is used across Egyptian, Greek, Chinese and Indian medicine for coughs and digestion."], ["1940s-1950s", "European physicians report ulcer healing with licorice extracts, along with edema and hypertension."], ["1960s", "Carbenoxolone, a licorice-derived compound, is used in Britain to treat peptic ulcers."], ["1980s", "Research links licorice-induced hypertension and hypokalemia to inhibition of 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase."], ["2017", "FDA publishes a consumer update warning that eating large amounts of black licorice can cause heart rhythm problems."]], "cell": "In the kidney's collecting duct, mineralocorticoid receptors respond equally well to aldosterone and to cortisol, and cortisol circulates at much higher levels. The enzyme 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 2 normally protects these receptors by converting cortisol to inactive cortisone. Glycyrrhizin is hydrolyzed by gut bacteria to glycyrrhetinic acid, which inhibits this enzyme; cortisol then occupies mineralocorticoid receptors and drives sodium reabsorption and potassium secretion, producing hypertension, edema and hypokalemia with suppressed renin and aldosterone. DGL processing strips most of the glycyrrhizin so this pathway is not engaged in a meaningful way, though residual amounts vary by product. The proposed stomach effects of DGL come from the remaining flavonoids and other constituents, which in laboratory and animal studies increased mucus secretion, prostaglandin levels in the mucosa and mucosal blood flow, and inhibited Helicobacter pylori growth. These effects have not been confirmed by strong human trials.", "body": [["Stomach and esophagus", "Proposed protection of the mucosal lining; human evidence for heartburn and ulcer benefit is limited."], ["Kidneys", "DGL avoids most of the glycyrrhizin effect; whole licorice causes sodium retention and potassium loss."], ["Heart", "Whole licorice can cause hypokalemia severe enough to trigger arrhythmias; DGL is designed to avoid this."], ["Blood vessels", "Whole licorice raises blood pressure through cortisol-driven mineralocorticoid activity."], ["Mouth", "Chewed tablets coat the mouth; DGL has been studied for canker sores in small trials."]], "journey": "DGL is usually chewed and mixed with saliva, on the idea that it acts locally on the esophagus and stomach lining. Most of its activity is thought to be local, and absorption of its flavonoids is limited and poorly studied. For comparison, glycyrrhizin in whole licorice is barely absorbed intact; gut bacteria hydrolyze it to glycyrrhetinic acid, which is absorbed, undergoes enterohepatic recirculation and has a long effective duration, which is why whole licorice effects on blood pressure and potassium can persist for days to weeks after stopping. Because DGL contains little glycyrrhizin, this prolonged systemic exposure is largely avoided.", "sidefx": "DGL is generally well tolerated, with mild stomach upset and an unpleasant taste the most common complaints. The main safety issue is mix-up with whole licorice. Whole licorice and glycyrrhizin-containing products can cause pseudohyperaldosteronism: high blood pressure, edema, headache, muscle weakness, cramps and hypokalemia, which can lead to dangerous arrhythmias and rhabdomyolysis in severe cases. The risk is higher with diuretics, corticosteroids, digoxin and heart or kidney disease. A further risk of any heartburn remedy is delayed diagnosis of serious disease when alarm symptoms are self-treated.", "fun": "The licorice flavor in many American black licorice candies actually comes partly from anise oil, but genuine licorice extract in candy still carries glycyrrhizin, which is why heavy black licorice eaters have ended up in emergency departments with dangerously low potassium.", "src": ["NIH NCCIH: Licorice Root fact sheet", "FDA Consumer Update: Black licorice: trick or treat?", "Stewart PM et al. Mineralocorticoid activity of liquorice: 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase deficiency comes of age, Lancet 1987", "NIH LiverTox and natural medicine monograph data on licorice"]}, "black-seed": {"origin": "Nigella sativa is a small annual flowering plant of the buttercup family, grown from the Mediterranean through the Middle East to South Asia for its pungent black seeds. The seeds go by many names, including black cumin, kalonji, black caraway and charnushka, although the plant is unrelated to true cumin, caraway or onion. They are sprinkled on breads such as naan and are a staple of Middle Eastern, North African and South Asian cooking. Their medicinal reputation is ancient: Nigella seeds have been reported among items found in ancient Egyptian tombs, and Greek physicians including Dioscorides described the seed for headaches and nasal congestion. In Islamic tradition a widely quoted hadith describes the black seed as a remedy for every disease except death, which gives the seed special cultural and religious significance for many families and helps explain its popularity today. Scientific interest grew in the late twentieth century after thymoquinone was identified as a major active constituent, leading to a large body of laboratory research and many small clinical trials, especially from the Middle East and South Asia.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Nigella seeds are used in Egypt and the Near East; seeds are reported among ancient Egyptian tomb finds."], ["1st century", "Dioscorides describes Nigella seed for headaches and nasal catarrh."], ["7th century onward", "A hadith describing black seed as a cure for every disease except death spreads its use across the Islamic world."], ["1960s", "Thymoquinone is identified as a key volatile constituent of the seed oil."], ["2000s-2010s", "Many small clinical trials and meta-analyses examine effects on glucose, blood pressure and lipids."]], "cell": "Thymoquinone is a small quinone that can cycle between oxidized and reduced forms, allowing it to act as an antioxidant at low concentrations and as a pro-oxidant at higher concentrations. In laboratory studies it reduces activation of the inflammatory transcription factor NF-kappa-B, lowers production of inflammatory cytokines, and inhibits cyclooxygenase and 5-lipoxygenase, which reduces prostaglandin and leukotriene formation; the leukotriene effect underlies interest in asthma. Animal studies suggest improved insulin sensitivity and reduced hepatic glucose output, and some studies report effects on smooth muscle and calcium channels that may explain small blood pressure reductions. Thymoquinone also reduced platelet aggregation in laboratory work. Many of these findings come from cell lines and animals at concentrations whose human relevance is unknown. Allergic contact dermatitis likely reflects sensitization to thymoquinone and related compounds in the oil.", "body": [["Metabolism", "Small trials suggest modest lowering of fasting glucose and HbA1c; additive with diabetes medicines."], ["Cardiovascular system", "Small trials report modest reductions in blood pressure and LDL cholesterol."], ["Lungs", "Leukotriene and anti-inflammatory effects underlie small asthma and allergy studies with mixed results."], ["Skin", "Topical oil can cause allergic contact dermatitis, including blistering reactions."], ["Kidneys", "Acute kidney injury has been described in case reports; animal studies at other doses suggest protective effects, so the picture is unclear."]], "journey": "Black seed is eaten as whole or ground seeds, swallowed as oil or softgel capsules, or applied to the skin. Thymoquinone is lipophilic and is absorbed from the gut in animal studies, but human pharmacokinetic data are very limited. In laboratory studies it binds strongly to plasma proteins such as albumin, and it is metabolized in the liver, including reduction to dihydrothymoquinone and conjugation. Excretion routes in humans are not well described. Applied to the skin, components of the oil penetrate enough to cause sensitization, explaining contact allergy. Because products vary widely in thymoquinone content, effects in trials may not match those of store-bought oils.", "sidefx": "Swallowed in food or supplement amounts, black seed commonly causes mild nausea, bloating, burping and an unpleasant taste. People taking insulin, sulfonylureas or blood pressure medicines may see additive lowering. Topical use is the most frequent cause of reported reactions: allergic contact dermatitis that can be severe, with blistering resembling a burn. Acute kidney injury has been described in isolated case reports, and laboratory antiplatelet effects raise a theoretical bleeding concern with anticoagulants or before surgery. Safety of supplement amounts in pregnancy has not been established.", "fun": "Despite its common name, black cumin, Nigella sativa is not related to cumin at all; it belongs to the buttercup family alongside the garden flower love-in-a-mist, Nigella damascena.", "src": ["Natural medicine monograph data on Nigella sativa (black seed)", "Ahmad A et al. A review on therapeutic potential of Nigella sativa, Asian Pacific Journal of Tropical Biomedicine 2013", "Meta-analyses of Nigella sativa effects on glycemic control and blood pressure", "Case reports of allergic contact dermatitis from topical Nigella sativa oil"]}, "boswellia": {"origin": "Frankincense is the aromatic gum resin of Boswellia trees, which grow in dry regions of the Arabian Peninsula, the Horn of Africa and India. It was one of the most valuable trade goods of the ancient world, carried along incense routes to Egypt, the Mediterranean and beyond, burned in temples and listed in the Christian nativity story alongside gold and myrrh. Boswellia serrata, the Indian species called salai guggul, has long been used in Ayurvedic medicine for arthritis, respiratory complaints and wounds. Modern pharmacology began when chemists characterized the boswellic acids, a family of pentacyclic triterpenes, and in the early 1990s German researchers, notably H. P. T. Ammon's group, reported that boswellic acids inhibited 5-lipoxygenase, the enzyme that starts leukotriene synthesis. That finding set boswellia apart from aspirin-like drugs and launched clinical trials in osteoarthritis, ulcerative colitis, asthma and even swelling around brain tumors. Standardized extracts enriched in AKBA were developed to address poor absorption. Results have been promising in small studies but limited by size, design and industry funding.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Frankincense resin is traded across Arabia, Africa and the Mediterranean for incense and medicine."], ["Traditional", "Ayurvedic medicine uses Boswellia serrata resin for arthritis and inflammatory conditions."], ["Early 1990s", "German researchers report that boswellic acids inhibit 5-lipoxygenase."], ["2000s", "Small trials test boswellia extracts in osteoarthritis, ulcerative colitis and asthma."], ["2014", "A Cochrane review of oral herbal therapies for osteoarthritis judges boswellia evidence as promising but limited."]], "cell": "Leukotrienes are inflammatory lipid mediators made from arachidonic acid by the enzyme 5-lipoxygenase. Boswellic acids, especially 3-acetyl-11-keto-beta-boswellic acid (AKBA), inhibit 5-lipoxygenase in laboratory studies, apparently by binding a regulatory site rather than the iron-containing active site used by many synthetic inhibitors. Less leukotriene B4 means less recruitment of white blood cells to inflamed tissue. Boswellic acids also inhibit human leukocyte elastase, reduce NF-kappa-B activation and cytokine production, and in cartilage cell models reduce matrix metalloproteinase activity, which is relevant to joint breakdown. Unlike NSAIDs, they are not primarily cyclooxygenase inhibitors, which is part of why GI bleeding has not emerged as a major problem. Laboratory studies also show inhibition of some CYP enzymes and P-glycoprotein, but whether that matters at human blood levels is unclear.", "body": [["Joints", "Small trials suggest modest reductions in osteoarthritis pain and stiffness."], ["Gut", "Mild nausea, heartburn and diarrhea; small studies in ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease are inconclusive."], ["Lungs", "Leukotriene inhibition underlies small, inconclusive asthma studies."], ["Immune system", "Reduced leukocyte recruitment and inflammatory cytokines in laboratory studies."], ["Skin", "Allergic skin reactions are reported occasionally."]], "journey": "Boswellic acids are large, lipophilic molecules that are poorly and variably absorbed from the gut. In small human studies, blood levels of AKBA were very low, and some other boswellic acids reached higher levels; taking extracts with a high-fat meal increased absorption several-fold in one study. Peak levels are reached after a few hours. Boswellic acids are highly bound to plasma proteins and are metabolized in the liver, with CYP3A4 involved in some oxidation, and elimination appears to be mainly through bile and feces. Because blood levels are low compared with the concentrations used in laboratory studies, researchers debate how much of the in vitro activity occurs in people, and manufacturers have created formulations designed to improve absorption.", "sidefx": "Boswellia is generally well tolerated in trials, with nausea, heartburn, abdominal discomfort and diarrhea the most common complaints. Allergic skin reactions occur occasionally. Rare reports describe liver enzyme elevations, mostly with multi-ingredient products. Laboratory data raise questions about CYP and P-glycoprotein interactions, so caution is applied with narrow-therapeutic-index drugs, though clinical interactions are not well documented. The most important practical risk is using boswellia instead of effective prescribed therapy for inflammatory bowel disease or rheumatoid arthritis. Safety in pregnancy and breastfeeding has not been established.", "fun": "Frankincense smoke contains incensole acetate, a compound that activated a heat-sensing ion channel in mouse brain tissue and produced calming effects in mouse studies, giving a laboratory angle on why the incense has been linked with ritual for thousands of years.", "src": ["Cameron M, Chrubasik S. Oral herbal therapies for treating osteoarthritis, Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews 2014", "Ammon HPT. Boswellic acids and their role in chronic inflammatory diseases, Advances in Experimental Medicine and Biology 2016", "NIH NCCIH research summaries on herbs for osteoarthritis", "Moussaieff A et al. Incensole acetate, an incense component, elicits psychoactivity by activating TRPV3 channels in the brain, FASEB Journal 2008"]}, "devils-claw": {"origin": "Harpagophytum procumbens grows in the red sands of the Kalahari and neighboring savannas of Namibia, Botswana and South Africa. Its name comes from its woody fruit, which bears hooked arms that catch in animal fur and hooves, spreading the seeds; Harpagophytum means grapple plant in Greek. The medicinal part is not the fruit but the swollen secondary storage roots, which are dug, sliced and dried. San and Khoikhoi peoples used the root for fever, pain, digestive problems and as a bitter tonic. In the early twentieth century European colonists in what was then German South West Africa learned of these uses, and the root was introduced to Germany, where it became an established herbal remedy for rheumatic complaints and appetite loss. Germany remains a major market, and harvesting still depends largely on wild collection by rural communities, raising both sustainability and fair-trade concerns. Clinical trials since the 1990s have focused on low back pain and osteoarthritis, making devil's claw one of the more studied herbal pain remedies, though trial quality is mixed.", "timeline": [["Traditional", "San and Khoikhoi peoples of southern Africa use the root for pain, fever and digestive complaints."], ["Early 1900s", "The root is introduced to Europe from German South West Africa (now Namibia)."], ["Mid-1900s", "Germany adopts devil's claw as a herbal remedy for rheumatic pain and poor appetite."], ["1990s-2000s", "Clinical trials test devil's claw extracts in low back pain and osteoarthritis."], ["2014", "A Cochrane review of herbal medicine for low back pain includes Harpagophytum trials with limited-quality evidence."]], "cell": "The root's main active compounds are iridoid glycosides, especially harpagoside, along with harpagide and procumbide. In laboratory studies harpagoside and whole extracts reduce production of tumor necrosis factor-alpha, interleukin-1 beta and interleukin-6 by stimulated immune cells, lower inducible nitric oxide synthase and COX-2 expression, and reduce prostaglandin E2 release, apparently by dampening NF-kappa-B signaling. Unlike NSAIDs, the extract does not appear to be a strong direct inhibitor of the cyclooxygenase enzymes themselves. Some studies suggest harpagoside is partly hydrolyzed to an aglycone that may be the active form, and stomach acid may degrade harpagoside, which has led some manufacturers to use coated tablets. The root is also intensely bitter, and bitter compounds stimulate gastric acid and bile secretion, which supports its traditional use for poor appetite and explains why it is avoided in people with ulcers.", "body": [["Joints and back", "Some trials suggest modest short-term pain relief in low back pain and osteoarthritis."], ["Stomach", "Bitter compounds stimulate acid secretion; diarrhea, nausea and upset stomach are the main side effects."], ["Gallbladder", "Increased bile flow is proposed, so the herb is avoided with gallstones."], ["Blood", "A case report describes purpura with warfarin; INR is watched."], ["Cardiovascular system", "Animal studies at high doses showed effects on heart rate and blood pressure; human relevance is unclear."]], "journey": "Devil's claw is swallowed as powdered root, standardized extracts, tea or tinctures. Harpagoside is absorbed to a limited extent, with low and variable blood levels in small human studies, and part of the dose may be hydrolyzed by stomach acid or gut bacteria before absorption. Metabolism and excretion in humans are not well characterized. Because extracts differ in harpagoside content and other constituents, results from one product may not apply to another, and trials that used higher harpagoside content tended to report more benefit, though this pattern is not firmly established.", "sidefx": "The most common side effects are gastrointestinal: diarrhea, nausea, abdominal discomfort, loss of appetite and altered taste. Headache, dizziness and allergic skin reactions are reported less often. Because it stimulates gastric acid, devil's claw may worsen peptic ulcers. A case report described purpura in a patient taking warfarin, and laboratory studies show inhibition of CYP2C9, CYP2C19 and CYP3A4, so interactions with drugs metabolized by these enzymes are possible but not well documented. Small effects on blood glucose, heart rate and blood pressure have been suggested in animal studies. Safety in pregnancy is not established.", "fun": "The hooked fruit that gives the plant its name spreads its seeds by clamping onto the hooves and fur of passing animals, which is why it is also called the grapple plant; yet the medicine comes from the underground storage roots, not the claw.", "src": ["NIH NCCIH: Devil's Claw fact sheet", "Oltean H et al. Herbal medicine for low back pain, Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews 2014", "European Medicines Agency (HMPC): Community herbal monograph on Harpagophytum procumbens root", "Natural medicine monograph data on Harpagophytum procumbens"]}, "willow-bark": {"origin": "Willow is one of medicine's founding stories. Ancient Egyptian and Sumerian texts mention willow for aches, and Greek and Roman writers including Hippocrates' followers and Dioscorides described willow preparations for pain and inflammation. In 1763 the English clergyman Edward Stone reported to the Royal Society that dried willow bark relieved the fevers of ague, an early documented clinical observation. In the 1820s chemists isolated the active glycoside, named salicin after the Latin salix for willow; Johann Buchner obtained it in Germany in 1828 and Henri Leroux crystallized it in France the following year. Raffaele Piria converted salicin into salicylic acid in 1838, and by the 1870s salicylic acid and sodium salicylate were used for rheumatic fever, though they irritated the stomach badly. At Bayer, Felix Hoffmann produced a stable acetylated form in 1897, and the company marketed acetylsalicylic acid as Aspirin in 1899. Willow bark itself returned to shelves in the late twentieth century as a natural pain reliever, but its salicylate chemistry means it shares many of aspirin's cautions.", "timeline": [["1763", "Edward Stone reports to the Royal Society that willow bark relieves fevers."], ["1828", "Johann Buchner isolates salicin from willow bark."], ["1838", "Raffaele Piria prepares salicylic acid from salicin."], ["1897", "Felix Hoffmann at Bayer synthesizes a stable form of acetylsalicylic acid."], ["1899", "Bayer markets acetylsalicylic acid as Aspirin."], ["1980s", "Studies link aspirin use in children with viral illness to Reye's syndrome, prompting warnings that now extend by caution to other salicylates."]], "cell": "Salicin is a glucoside of salicyl alcohol. After oral intake, gut bacteria and enzymes remove the glucose, and the salicyl alcohol is oxidized, mainly in the liver, to salicylic acid. Salicylic acid is only a weak direct inhibitor of the cyclooxygenase enzymes in test tubes, yet it is a meaningful anti-inflammatory in people; part of its action is suppression of COX-2 gene expression and interference with inflammatory signaling such as NF-kappa-B. The crucial difference from aspirin is the acetyl group. Aspirin transfers that acetyl group to a serine in platelet COX-1, permanently disabling the enzyme for the platelet's lifetime, which is why low-dose aspirin prevents clots. Salicylic acid cannot do this, so willow bark has a much weaker antiplatelet effect, although bleeding concerns remain when combined with anticoagulants. Willow bark also contains flavonoids and polyphenols that may add anti-inflammatory activity, which some researchers think explains effects greater than the salicin content alone would predict.", "body": [["Joints and back", "Modest short-term pain relief in some trials, especially low back pain."], ["Stomach", "Less GI injury than aspirin in short-term studies, but stomach upset and irritation still occur."], ["Lungs", "Can trigger bronchospasm in people with aspirin- or NSAID-exacerbated respiratory disease."], ["Blood", "Weaker antiplatelet effect than aspirin, but additive bleeding risk with anticoagulants is a concern."], ["Kidneys", "Salicylates are cleared by the kidneys; caution in kidney disease."]], "journey": "Salicin is swallowed in capsules, extracts or tea. It is a prodrug: intestinal bacteria and enzymes hydrolyze it to salicyl alcohol, which is absorbed and oxidized in the liver and blood to salicylic acid. Because of this conversion, salicylic acid levels rise more slowly than after aspirin, peaking around two hours later in a small study. Salicylic acid is highly bound to albumin, is conjugated with glycine to salicyluric acid and with glucuronic acid, and is excreted in urine. At high exposures these pathways saturate and elimination slows, which is why salicylate toxicity can escalate, although typical bark products provide less salicylate than a standard aspirin regimen.", "sidefx": "The most common effects are stomach upset, nausea and heartburn. Allergic reactions, including hives and bronchospasm, can occur in people sensitive to aspirin or other salicylates. Bleeding risk is lower than with aspirin but may become relevant with warfarin, antiplatelets, NSAIDs or before surgery. Like other salicylates, willow bark raises concern for Reye's syndrome in children and teenagers with viral illness, a concern extrapolated from aspirin. Large salicylate exposure can cause tinnitus, nausea, vomiting, rapid breathing and acid-base disturbance, though this is rarely reported with bark products alone. Salicylates can reduce methotrexate clearance.", "fun": "Willow trees use salicylic acid as a plant hormone of their own: it helps trigger the plant's defenses against infection, so the molecule that became aspirin started life as part of a plant immune system.", "src": ["NIH NCCIH: Willow Bark fact sheet", "Oltean H et al. Herbal medicine for low back pain, Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews 2014", "Jeffreys D. Aspirin: The Remarkable Story of a Wonder Drug, 2004", "Natural medicine monograph data on Salix species bark"]}, "cats-claw": {"origin": "Cat's claw, una de gato in Spanish, grows as a woody vine climbing into the canopy of the Amazon rainforest and other tropical forests of Central and South America. The name refers to curved thorns along the stem that help the vine hook onto trees. Indigenous peoples of Peru, including the Ashaninka, have long used decoctions of the bark for inflammation, wounds, digestive problems and, according to ethnographic reports, for contraception or to end pregnancy. Two species are traded, Uncaria tomentosa and Uncaria guianensis, with tomentosa the more studied. European researchers began studying the alkaloids in the 1970s and 1980s, and Austrian chemist Klaus Keplinger was influential in describing oxindole alkaloid chemistry and promoting extracts. Cat's claw became a popular immune supplement in the 1990s, marketed at times for HIV and cancer. Human research has stayed limited to small trials in arthritis.", "timeline": [["Traditional", "Indigenous Peruvian peoples use cat's claw bark for inflammation, wounds and digestive complaints."], ["1970s-1980s", "European researchers characterize oxindole alkaloids in Uncaria tomentosa."], ["1990s", "Cat's claw becomes a widely marketed immune supplement in North America and Europe."], ["2000s", "Small trials test cat's claw extracts in osteoarthritis and rheumatoid arthritis."], ["2000s", "A case report describes raised HIV protease inhibitor levels in a patient using cat's claw."]], "cell": "Cat's claw bark contains two groups of oxindole alkaloids. Pentacyclic oxindole alkaloids such as mitraphylline and pteropodine are proposed to modulate immune cell activity, while tetracyclic oxindole alkaloids such as rhynchophylline act on the nervous system and may oppose some immune effects, so products with different alkaloid profiles may behave differently. Non-alkaloid constituents, including quinovic acid glycosides and polyphenols, also contribute. In laboratory studies, bark extracts reduced TNF-alpha production and NF-kappa-B activation in immune cells and protected cells from oxidative stress, which underlies the arthritis interest. Some extracts appeared to enhance DNA repair or white blood cell activity in small studies, the basis of immune claims. Extracts inhibited CYP3A4 in vitro, which fits with a case report of increased levels of HIV protease inhibitors. Rhynchophylline has calcium channel effects in animal studies, a possible explanation for blood pressure lowering.", "body": [["Joints", "Small trials suggest modest pain reduction in osteoarthritis and rheumatoid arthritis."], ["Immune system", "Laboratory and small human studies suggest immune modulation; clinical meaning is unclear and raises concern in transplant and autoimmune disease."], ["Liver", "CYP3A4 inhibition in vitro may raise levels of some drugs."], ["Cardiovascular system", "Possible blood pressure lowering, especially with antihypertensives."], ["Kidneys", "Acute kidney injury has been described in a case report."]], "journey": "Cat's claw is swallowed as bark powder in capsules, as tablets, or boiled into tea. Human pharmacokinetic data are scarce. The oxindole alkaloids are absorbed to some degree and metabolized in the liver, and in vitro data suggest CYP3A4 involvement in both their metabolism and the herb's interaction potential. Pentacyclic and tetracyclic alkaloids can interconvert in solution depending on pH and temperature, which means the alkaloid profile of a tea or extract may differ from the raw bark. Excretion pathways in humans are not well described. Because commercial products vary in species and alkaloid content, findings from one extract may not apply to another.", "sidefx": "Reported side effects are usually mild: nausea, diarrhea, stomach upset and dizziness. Blood pressure lowering may cause lightheadedness, particularly with antihypertensives. Because it may stimulate parts of the immune system, it raises theoretical concern in autoimmune disease and in transplant recipients relying on immunosuppressants. In vitro CYP3A4 inhibition and a case report of raised protease inhibitor levels suggest interactions with CYP3A4 substrates. Acute kidney injury has been described in a case report, and allergic reactions are possible. Traditional use to end pregnancy is why cat's claw is avoided in pregnancy.", "fun": "Cat's claw is reported to climb as high as about 30 meters into the rainforest canopy, using its hooked thorns like grappling hooks to haul itself toward the light.", "src": ["NIH NCCIH: Cat's Claw fact sheet", "Natural medicine monograph data on Uncaria tomentosa", "Lopez Galera RM et al. Interaction between cat's claw and protease inhibitors atazanavir, ritonavir and saquinavir, European Journal of Clinical Pharmacology 2008", "Heitzman ME et al. Ethnobotany, phytochemistry and pharmacology of Uncaria, Phytochemistry 2005"]}, "arnica": {"origin": "Arnica montana grows in mountain meadows of Europe, and its bright yellow, daisy-like flowers have been used in European folk medicine for centuries, applied to bruises, sprains and swellings. Common names such as leopard's bane, wolf's bane and mountain tobacco reflect both its toxicity and the practice of smoking or snuffing the dried leaves in some regions. Swallowed preparations were used historically for many ailments, and the poisonings that followed gave arnica its long-standing reputation as a dangerous internal remedy. In the late eighteenth and nineteenth centuries homeopathy adopted arnica as one of its signature remedies for trauma, in highly diluted forms, and homeopathic arnica remains among the most widely sold homeopathic products, including pellets marketed for bruising after cosmetic surgery. In the United States, FDA has long regarded the herb as unsafe for internal use, while topical herbal products and homeopathic drugs are sold without FDA approval. Clinical trials since the 1990s have produced mixed results for topical arnica and little consistent evidence for homeopathic arnica, while toxicology reports continue to document poisoning after swallowed tinctures and oils.", "timeline": [["Centuries", "European folk medicine uses arnica flowers on bruises and sprains; swallowed preparations cause poisonings."], ["Late 1700s-1800s", "Homeopathy adopts highly diluted arnica as a remedy for trauma and bruising."], ["1970s", "FDA lists arnica among herbs regarded as unsafe for internal use."], ["1980s-1990s", "Helenalin is characterized as the main toxic and anti-inflammatory sesquiterpene lactone."], ["2000s-2010s", "Trials of topical and homeopathic arnica for bruising and osteoarthritis show mixed or negative results."]], "cell": "The key compounds in arnica are sesquiterpene lactones, mainly helenalin and its esters in Central European Arnica montana, and dihydrohelenalin derivatives in some other chemotypes. Helenalin carries reactive alpha-methylene-gamma-butyrolactone and cyclopentenone groups that form covalent bonds with sulfhydryl groups on proteins and glutathione, a chemical process called Michael addition. In laboratory studies this reactivity lets helenalin alkylate the p65 subunit of NF-kappa-B, blocking inflammatory gene expression, which is the scientific basis for anti-inflammatory claims. The same chemistry explains the harms. On skin, helenalin binds skin proteins and creates new antigens, leading to allergic contact dermatitis, with cross-reactivity to other daisy-family sesquiterpene lactones. Swallowed, it irritates the gut lining, causing vomiting and diarrhea, and it has toxic effects on heart muscle and other tissues reported in animal and human poisoning data. Homeopathic dilutions reduce the amount of helenalin to very low or undetectable levels, except in low-dilution products.", "body": [["Skin", "Allergic contact dermatitis with itching, redness and sometimes blisters; more absorption through broken skin."], ["Gut", "Swallowed arnica irritates the mouth, throat, stomach and intestines, causing vomiting, diarrhea and abdominal pain."], ["Heart", "Ingestion has been linked to palpitations, rhythm disturbances and, rarely, cardiovascular collapse."], ["Muscles and nerves", "Weakness and dizziness are reported after significant ingestion."], ["Blood", "Laboratory antiplatelet effects raise a theoretical concern with anticoagulants."]], "journey": "Topical arnica is applied to intact skin, where small amounts of sesquiterpene lactones are absorbed; absorption increases through broken skin, which is why it is not used on wounds. When a non-homeopathic product is swallowed, helenalin acts first as a local irritant on the mouth, esophagus and gut. Absorbed helenalin reacts with proteins and glutathione in blood and tissues, but human pharmacokinetic data are very limited, and elimination pathways are not well described. Homeopathic pellets dissolved under the tongue usually deliver little or no measurable helenalin because of dilution. Alcohol-based tinctures combine arnica with ethanol, an additional risk in children.", "sidefx": "With topical use, the main problem is contact dermatitis, ranging from itching and redness to blistering eczema, particularly with repeated use or in people sensitized to ragweed, chrysanthemums, marigolds or daisies. Swallowing the herb, a tincture, an oil or a low-dilution product can cause burning of the mouth and throat, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, abdominal pain, dizziness, palpitations and heart rhythm disturbances, with muscle weakness and collapse in severe cases; deaths have been reported rarely. Treatment is supportive, with fluids, cardiac monitoring and poison center guidance, and there is no antidote. Highly diluted homeopathic pellets are generally low in toxicity, but the main risk is relying on an unproven product.", "fun": "Arnica is one of the few plants that is both a common skin allergen in dermatology clinics and a best-selling homeopathic remedy, meaning the same plant shows up in patch-test kits and on the bruise-care shelf of the pharmacy.", "src": ["NIH NCCIH: Arnica fact sheet", "Lyss G et al. The anti-inflammatory sesquiterpene lactone helenalin inhibits NF-kappa-B by selectively targeting p65, Journal of Biological Chemistry 1998", "Poison center and toxicology literature on Arnica montana ingestion", "FDA: Homeopathic drug products guidance"]}, "feverfew": {"origin": "Feverfew is a small, bushy perennial with daisy-like flowers, native to southeastern Europe and western Asia and long grown in European cottage gardens. Its common name comes from the Latin febrifugia, meaning something that drives away fever, and Greek and later European herbalists used it for fevers, headaches, menstrual problems and to ease childbirth, which is one reason it is avoided in pregnancy today. Its modern reputation began in Britain in the 1970s, when publicity about people who chewed fresh feverfew leaves for migraine prompted doctors at the City of London Migraine Clinic to investigate. A study of existing users who stopped or continued, followed by placebo-controlled trials in the 1980s, suggested fewer migraine attacks, and some users who stopped had rebound headaches and other symptoms that came to be called post-feverfew syndrome. Later trials gave inconsistent results, partly because products varied greatly in parthenolide content, and reviews, including Cochrane, have judged the evidence as low quality and inconsistent. Feverfew remains a popular migraine supplement, often combined with magnesium and riboflavin.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Greek herbalists, including Dioscorides, describe the plant for inflammation and women's complaints."], ["1970s", "Media reports in Britain about chewing feverfew leaves for migraine spark medical interest."], ["1985", "A British Medical Journal study of feverfew users reports more migraine attacks in those switched to placebo."], ["1988", "A Lancet randomized trial reports fewer migraine attacks with dried feverfew leaf."], ["2015", "A Cochrane review finds low-quality, inconsistent evidence for feverfew in migraine prevention."]], "cell": "Parthenolide, a sesquiterpene lactone, has an alpha-methylene-gamma-lactone group that reacts with sulfhydryl groups on proteins, the same chemistry seen with arnica's helenalin. Through this reactivity it inhibits IkappaB kinase and blocks activation of NF-kappa-B, reducing production of inflammatory cytokines and enzymes such as COX-2 and inducible nitric oxide synthase in laboratory studies. Feverfew extracts also reduce platelet aggregation and the release of serotonin from platelets, which once seemed directly relevant to migraine, since serotonin shifts accompany attacks; they also inhibit arachidonic acid release and prostaglandin formation. Parthenolide has been reported to act on the TRPA1 channel in sensory nerves, desensitizing it, which may reduce release of the migraine-related peptide CGRP in animal models. Which of these effects matters in people is unknown, and some trial products contained little parthenolide, making the clinical picture harder to interpret.", "body": [["Brain and blood vessels", "Possible small reduction in migraine frequency in some trials; effects on vascular tone and sensory nerve signaling are proposed."], ["Blood", "Antiplatelet effects in vitro raise concern for bleeding with anticoagulants and antiplatelets."], ["Mouth", "Chewing fresh leaves can cause mouth ulcers, tongue soreness and loss of taste."], ["Uterus", "Traditionally linked to menstrual stimulation and uterine contractions; considered likely unsafe in pregnancy."], ["Nervous system", "Stopping after long-term use has been linked to rebound headache, anxiety and insomnia."]], "journey": "Feverfew is swallowed as dried leaf in capsules or tablets, as extracts or tea, or chewed fresh. Parthenolide is unstable and degrades during storage and processing, which partly explains large differences between products. Human pharmacokinetic data are limited; in a small study, parthenolide was poorly detectable in blood after oral feverfew, suggesting low absorption or rapid reaction with proteins and glutathione. Metabolism and excretion in people are not well characterized. In migraine trials, benefits, when seen, emerged only after weeks of regular use, consistent with a preventive rather than acute effect.", "sidefx": "Most users report mild effects, including nausea, digestive upset and bloating. Chewing fresh leaves can cause mouth ulcers, inflammation of the lips and tongue and loss of taste. Allergic reactions occur in people sensitive to the daisy family. Because feverfew reduces platelet aggregation in laboratory studies, it may add to bleeding risk with warfarin, direct oral anticoagulants, aspirin, clopidogrel or NSAIDs, and use before surgery is a concern. Stopping after long-term use has been linked to rebound headache, anxiety, poor sleep, and muscle and joint stiffness. Feverfew is considered likely unsafe in pregnancy.", "fun": "Parthenolide has drawn cancer research interest because in laboratory studies it selectively killed leukemia stem cells while sparing normal blood stem cells, leading chemists to develop a more soluble derivative for early testing.", "src": ["NIH NCCIH: Feverfew fact sheet", "Wider B, Pittler MH, Ernst E. Feverfew for preventing migraine, Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews 2015", "Murphy JJ, Heptinstall S, Mitchell JR. Randomised double-blind placebo-controlled trial of feverfew in migraine prevention, Lancet 1988", "Johnson ES et al. Efficacy of feverfew as prophylactic treatment of migraine, British Medical Journal 1985"]}, "butterbur": {"origin": "Butterbur, Petasites hybridus, grows along streams and in damp ground across Europe and parts of Asia and North America, producing rhubarb-like leaves so large that they were reportedly used to wrap butter in warm weather, which may be the source of its English name. The genus name comes from the Greek petasos, a broad-brimmed hat. In medieval Europe the plant was used against plague and fevers, and later for coughs, asthma and spasms. Modern interest centered on Germany, where a standardized root extract processed to remove pyrrolizidine alkaloids was developed and tested in the 1990s and 2000s for migraine prevention and hay fever. Small randomized trials reported fewer migraine attacks and allergy relief comparable to some antihistamines, and in 2012 an American Academy of Neurology and American Headache Society guideline rated butterbur as effective for migraine prevention. At the same time, reports of liver injury accumulated, and regulators in Germany and the United Kingdom acted to restrict butterbur products. The neurology guideline endorsement was subsequently retired because of these liver safety concerns, and butterbur now serves as a teaching example of a promising herb undone by toxicity.", "timeline": [["Middle Ages", "Butterbur is used in Europe against plague, fever and coughs."], ["1990s-2000s", "A German PA-free root extract is tested in small migraine and allergic rhinitis trials."], ["2000s", "German regulators restrict butterbur products after reports of liver injury."], ["2012", "An AAN and AHS guideline rates butterbur as effective for migraine prevention; the UK MHRA warns about liver risk from unlicensed butterbur products."], ["Later 2010s", "The AAN retires its butterbur endorsement because of liver safety concerns."]], "cell": "Butterbur root contains two very different families of chemicals. The sesquiterpene esters petasin and isopetasin are the intended actives: in laboratory and animal studies they inhibit leukotriene synthesis in white blood cells, block L-type calcium channels in smooth muscle, and act on sensory nerve TRPA1 channels, which may reduce release of the migraine-related peptide CGRP. These actions are the scientific case for both allergy and migraine use. The second family, the pyrrolizidine alkaloids such as senecionine, is the problem. These are not toxic as swallowed, but liver CYP3A enzymes oxidize them to dehydropyrrolizidine esters, highly reactive pyrroles that cross-link DNA and proteins. The first cells hit are the endothelial cells lining the hepatic sinusoids; they swell and detach, blocking blood flow out of the liver and producing hepatic sinusoidal obstruction syndrome, with hepatomegaly, ascites and jaundice. The same reactive metabolites are genotoxic and carcinogenic in animals. Some liver injury with PA-free extracts suggests an additional idiosyncratic mechanism not yet understood.", "body": [["Liver", "Pyrrolizidine alkaloids cause sinusoidal obstruction syndrome; hepatitis and acute liver failure have been reported with butterbur products."], ["Brain and nerves", "Possible reduction in migraine frequency in small trials, perhaps via sensory nerve and CGRP effects."], ["Airways and nose", "Leukotriene inhibition underlies small trials in allergic rhinitis and asthma."], ["Gut", "Burping, stomach upset and diarrhea are the most common side effects."], ["Fetus", "Pyrrolizidine alkaloids cross the placenta and are a risk to the fetal liver."]], "journey": "Butterbur is swallowed as capsules or softgels of root extract, or in folk practice as teas and preparations of raw plant. Petasins are absorbed and reach peak levels within a couple of hours in limited studies, then are metabolized in the liver. Pyrrolizidine alkaloids, when present, are well absorbed from the gut and carried by portal blood to the liver, where CYP3A enzymes convert them to reactive pyrroles; some are detoxified by glutathione conjugation or hydrolysis and excreted in urine. Because liver injury reflects cumulative exposure, repeated small doses over weeks can lead to sinusoidal obstruction. Enzyme inducers may increase formation of the toxic metabolites, while glutathione depletion may reduce detoxification.", "sidefx": "In trials, the most frequent complaints were burping, digestive upset, diarrhea, drowsiness and headache. The serious concern is the liver. Raw butterbur and unprocessed teas expose users to pyrrolizidine alkaloids, which can cause hepatic sinusoidal obstruction syndrome with right upper quadrant pain, enlarged liver, ascites and jaundice, sometimes progressing to liver failure. Hepatitis and acute liver failure have also been reported with processed extracts marketed as PA-free. Allergic reactions occur in people sensitive to the daisy family. Pregnancy and breastfeeding are unsafe periods for exposure. Management is stopping exposure and supportive liver care; there is no antidote.", "fun": "Butterbur flowers appear in early spring, often before the leaves, as pinkish spikes rising from bare ground; the huge rhubarb-like leaves that follow later in the season are what gave rise to the story of farmers wrapping butter in them.", "src": ["NIH NCCIH: Butterbur fact sheet", "NIH LiverTox: Pyrrolizidine alkaloids and herbal hepatotoxicity", "Holland S et al. Evidence-based guideline update: NSAIDs and other complementary treatments for episodic migraine prevention in adults, Neurology 2012", "UK MHRA safety information on butterbur and liver toxicity"]}, "black-cohosh": {"origin": "Black cohosh is a tall perennial of eastern North American forests, with long white flower spikes and a dark, knotted root. Native American peoples used the root for gynecological complaints, rheumatism and snakebite, and nineteenth-century American Eclectic physicians adopted it widely; it was a component of Lydia Pinkham's Vegetable Compound, a famous patent medicine for women's complaints first sold in the 1870s. In the twentieth century, German manufacturers developed standardized black cohosh extracts for menopausal symptoms, and these were approved in Germany as traditional herbal medicines. Interest in the United States surged after 2002, when the Women's Health Initiative reported risks of combined hormone therapy and many women sought nonhormonal options. Clinical trials, including NIH-funded studies, have produced inconsistent results, and the original idea that black cohosh works like a plant estrogen has largely been abandoned. Meanwhile, reports of hepatitis and liver failure prompted reviews by regulators and the United States Pharmacopeia, which in 2008 recommended a liver caution statement on product labels. The plant was renamed Actaea racemosa after DNA studies, though the older name Cimicifuga persists on labels.", "timeline": [["1800s", "Eclectic physicians use black cohosh root for women's complaints and rheumatism."], ["1870s", "Black cohosh is an ingredient in Lydia Pinkham's Vegetable Compound."], ["2002", "Women's Health Initiative results drive interest in nonhormonal menopause remedies."], ["2008", "The United States Pharmacopeia recommends a cautionary liver statement on black cohosh product labels."], ["2012", "A Cochrane review finds insufficient evidence that black cohosh relieves menopausal symptoms."]], "cell": "Black cohosh root contains triterpene glycosides such as actein and 23-epi-26-deoxyactein, along with phenolic acids and small amounts of alkaloids including N-methylserotonin. Early studies suggested estrogenic activity, but later work using purified extracts found little binding to estrogen receptors and no consistent estrogen-like effects on the uterus or breast tissue in women. Laboratory studies instead show binding to serotonin receptors, notably 5-HT1A and 5-HT7, and partial activity at mu-opioid receptors; because the hypothalamic thermoregulatory center relies on serotonin and endorphin signaling, these actions have been proposed to explain any effect on hot flashes. How black cohosh injures the liver is not known. Cases often show a hepatitis pattern, some with autoimmune features, suggesting an idiosyncratic immune-mediated reaction; adulteration with other Actaea species has also been raised as a possible contributor in some cases.", "body": [["Brain and thermoregulation", "Proposed serotonin and opioid receptor effects on hot flashes; trial results are inconsistent."], ["Liver", "Rare hepatitis and acute liver failure reports, some requiring transplant; causality is debated."], ["Uterus and breast", "Most studies do not show estrogen-like stimulation."], ["Gut", "Stomach upset and nausea are common mild side effects."], ["Skin", "Rash is occasionally reported."]], "journey": "Black cohosh is swallowed as standardized tablets, capsules or liquid extracts. Human pharmacokinetic data are limited: triterpene glycosides such as 23-epi-26-deoxyactein are absorbed and detected in blood within hours, with an elimination half-life of a few hours in small studies, and they are metabolized in the liver. Excretion pathways are not well characterized. In trials, any effect on hot flashes developed only over weeks of regular use. A human probe study found a small effect on CYP2D6 activity, but most clinically important CYP interactions have not been shown.", "sidefx": "Black cohosh is usually well tolerated, with stomach upset, headache, rash, weight gain and breast tenderness reported occasionally. The important concern is liver injury. Case reports and case series describe hepatitis, sometimes with jaundice and occasionally progressing to acute liver failure needing transplant, appearing weeks to months after starting the product. Because cases are rare and products vary in identity and quality, causality has been debated, but regulators and the USP have judged the signal strong enough to warrant label cautions. Safety in pregnancy and breastfeeding is not established, and it is not to be confused with blue cohosh, which has been linked to newborn heart damage.", "fun": "Black cohosh's old common names include bugbane and black snakeroot; the bugbane name reflects the strong, unpleasant smell of its flowers, which were thought to repel insects.", "src": ["NIH Office of Dietary Supplements: Black Cohosh fact sheet", "NIH LiverTox: Black Cohosh", "Leach MJ, Moore V. Black cohosh (Cimicifuga spp.) for menopausal symptoms, Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews 2012", "Mahady GB et al. United States Pharmacopeia review of the black cohosh case reports of hepatotoxicity, Menopause 2008"]}, "chasteberry": {"origin": "The chaste tree is a shrub with lilac-colored flower spikes native to the Mediterranean and western Asia, and its peppery dried fruit has been used medicinally since antiquity. Ancient Greek writers associated it with chastity; women celebrating the festival of Thesmophoria in honor of Demeter were said to lie on its branches, and medieval monks reportedly used the fruit to dampen sexual desire, earning it the name monk's pepper. Greek and Roman physicians, including Hippocrates' followers and Dioscorides, described it for gynecological complaints and to promote milk flow. In the twentieth century German physicians developed standardized extracts for menstrual disorders, premenstrual syndrome and cyclic breast pain, and chasteberry was accepted in German and later European herbal monographs. Laboratory work in the 1990s identified dopaminergic diterpenes that lower prolactin, providing a plausible mechanism for effects on breast pain and cycle regularity. Clinical trials, many from Germany and Switzerland, have reported benefit for premenstrual symptoms, but reviews highlight small size, short duration and inconsistent methods.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Greek and Roman writers associate the chaste tree with chastity and describe medical uses for women."], ["Middle Ages", "Monks reportedly use the peppery fruit to suppress libido, leading to the name monk's pepper."], ["1950s", "Standardized chasteberry extracts are developed in Germany for menstrual disorders."], ["1990s", "Laboratory studies show dopaminergic diterpenes in Vitex that inhibit prolactin release."], ["2001", "A randomized controlled trial in the British Medical Journal reports improvement in premenstrual symptoms with a Vitex extract."]], "cell": "Prolactin secretion from the anterior pituitary is held in check by dopamine acting on D2 receptors on lactotroph cells. Diterpenes in chasteberry extract, such as rotundifuran and related labdane compounds, bind D2 receptors and reduce prolactin release in pituitary cell studies, behaving like weak dopamine agonists. Some women with premenstrual symptoms and cyclic breast pain are thought to have latent hyperprolactinemia, particularly in response to stress, and lowering prolactin may improve luteal phase progesterone production and reduce breast tenderness, though this link is not firmly established. Extracts also bind mu- and delta-opioid receptors in vitro, which has been proposed to contribute to mood and pain effects. Flavonoids such as apigenin may have weak estrogen receptor activity. Because the active constituents differ between extracts, the balance of these effects may vary from product to product.", "body": [["Pituitary gland", "Dopaminergic action lowers prolactin secretion."], ["Ovaries and menstrual cycle", "Lower prolactin is proposed to support luteal function; menstrual changes and intermenstrual bleeding are reported."], ["Breasts", "Some trials report reduced cyclic breast pain."], ["Brain", "Possible effects on premenstrual mood symptoms; interaction potential with dopamine-active drugs."], ["Skin", "Itching, rash and acne are occasional side effects."]], "journey": "Chasteberry is swallowed as standardized dry extracts in tablets or capsules, or as tinctures. Human pharmacokinetic data are very limited; the diterpenes and iridoid glycosides are presumed to be absorbed from the gut and metabolized in the liver, but blood levels, half-lives and excretion have not been well characterized. In trials, effects on premenstrual symptoms were assessed over several menstrual cycles, consistent with a gradual effect on hormonal regulation rather than an immediate action.", "sidefx": "Side effects in trials are generally mild and uncommon, including nausea, stomach upset, headache, itching, rash and acne. Menstrual changes, such as altered cycle length or intermenstrual bleeding, are reported. Because chasteberry has dopaminergic activity, it may interact with dopamine agonists such as bromocriptine and cabergoline and with dopamine antagonists such as antipsychotics and metoclopramide. Possible interference with hormonal contraceptives and hormone therapy is not well documented. A case report described ovarian hyperstimulation in a woman using chasteberry during fertility treatment. It is generally avoided during pregnancy and breastfeeding, because lowering prolactin could reduce milk supply despite its traditional use as a galactagogue.", "fun": "Chasteberry holds a paradox: ancient texts recommended it to increase breast milk, yet modern pharmacology shows it lowers prolactin, the hormone that drives milk production.", "src": ["NIH NCCIH: Chasteberry fact sheet", "European Medicines Agency (HMPC): Community herbal monograph on Vitex agnus-castus fruit", "Schellenberg R. Treatment for the premenstrual syndrome with agnus castus fruit extract: prospective, randomised, placebo controlled study, British Medical Journal 2001", "Wuttke W et al. Chaste tree (Vitex agnus-castus): pharmacology and clinical indications, Phytomedicine 2003"]}, "red-clover": {"origin": "Red clover is a common meadow and pasture plant of the legume family, native to Europe and western Asia and now widespread worldwide as a forage crop that enriches soil with nitrogen. In folk medicine its flower heads were brewed into teas and syrups for coughs, skin conditions and as so-called blood purifiers, and red clover was an ingredient in nineteenth-century cancer remedies such as the Hoxsey formula, which had no proven benefit. Scientific interest in clover isoflavones began with livestock: in the 1940s sheep in Western Australia grazing on subterranean clover, a related species, developed infertility and reproductive abnormalities known as clover disease, which researchers traced to estrogen-like isoflavones such as formononetin. Decades later, observations that Asian women eating soy-rich diets reported fewer hot flashes encouraged development of isoflavone supplements, and standardized red clover extracts entered the menopause market in the 1990s. Randomized trials, including NIH-supported studies, have generally found small or no reductions in hot flashes compared with placebo, and safety questions center on estrogenic effects in hormone-sensitive tissues.", "timeline": [["Folk era", "Red clover blossom teas and syrups are used for coughs, skin complaints and as blood purifiers."], ["1940s", "Clover disease, infertility in Western Australian sheep grazing subterranean clover, links clover isoflavones to estrogenic effects."], ["1990s", "Standardized red clover isoflavone extracts are marketed for menopausal symptoms."], ["2000s", "Randomized trials show small or no benefit for hot flashes compared with placebo."], ["2010s", "Reviews continue to report mixed evidence and call for long-term safety data."]], "cell": "Red clover isoflavones are diphenolic compounds whose shape resembles estradiol enough to bind estrogen receptors. Formononetin and biochanin A, the dominant forms in red clover, are methylated precursors; after absorption they are demethylated to daidzein and genistein, respectively. These bind estrogen receptor beta several-fold more strongly than estrogen receptor alpha, and their potency is a small fraction of estradiol's. Depending on the tissue, receptor balance and the amount of the body's own estrogen present, they can act as weak agonists or partially block stronger estrogens, behavior that resembles selective estrogen receptor modulators. Some people have gut bacteria that convert daidzein to equol, a metabolite with stronger estrogenic activity, which may help explain variable trial results. Genistein also inhibits tyrosine kinases and topoisomerase in laboratory studies at concentrations higher than typically reached with supplements. Red clover additionally contains coumarin compounds, though they are not the anticoagulant dicoumarol that forms in spoiled sweet clover.", "body": [["Hypothalamus", "Weak estrogen-like effects may slightly influence hot flashes; trial results are mixed."], ["Uterus", "Short-term studies have not shown endometrial thickening, but long-term data are limited."], ["Breast", "Effects in hormone-sensitive breast tissue are uncertain; caution in breast cancer history."], ["Bone", "Small studies suggest possible slowing of bone loss; evidence is weak."], ["Blood", "Coumarin content raises a theoretical concern with anticoagulants."]], "journey": "Red clover isoflavones are swallowed as tablets or capsules, often standardized to total isoflavones, or as teas. In the gut, glycoside forms are hydrolyzed by intestinal enzymes and bacteria to aglycones, which are absorbed. In the gut wall and liver, formononetin and biochanin A are demethylated to daidzein and genistein and then conjugated with glucuronic acid or sulfate. Some daidzein is converted by gut bacteria to equol or other metabolites, depending on each person's microbiome. Isoflavones undergo enterohepatic recirculation and are excreted mainly in urine as conjugates, with half-lives of several hours in limited studies.", "sidefx": "Red clover is generally well tolerated in short-term studies, with headache, nausea, muscle aches and rash reported occasionally. Vaginal spotting has been reported. The main safety questions concern estrogen-like activity: possible effects in hormone-sensitive breast, uterine or ovarian cancer, endometriosis, and interaction with tamoxifen, aromatase inhibitors, hormonal contraceptives and hormone therapy. Coumarin content raises a theoretical bleeding concern with anticoagulants. It is generally avoided in pregnancy and breastfeeding. Long-term safety beyond about a year of use is not well established.", "fun": "The famous blood thinner warfarin traces back to a different clover: cattle in the 1920s bled to death after eating spoiled sweet clover hay, in which mold had converted coumarin into dicoumarol, the compound that led to warfarin.", "src": ["NIH NCCIH: Red Clover fact sheet", "Geller SE et al. Safety and efficacy of black cohosh and red clover for the management of vasomotor symptoms, Menopause 2009", "Bennetts HW et al. A specific breeding problem of sheep on subterranean clover pastures in Western Australia, Australian Veterinary Journal 1946", "Natural medicine monograph data on Trifolium pratense"]}, "evening-primrose-oil": {"origin": "Evening primrose is a North American wildflower whose yellow blossoms open at dusk, an adaptation to night-flying moth pollinators. Native American peoples used the plant as food and for bruises and skin problems, and it spread to Europe in the seventeenth century, where its roots were eaten as a vegetable. The seed oil became a medical product much later. In the twentieth century, researchers established that linoleic acid is an essential fatty acid and that it must be converted by the enzyme delta-6-desaturase into gamma-linolenic acid on the way to prostaglandins. Evening primrose seed oil turned out to be an unusual natural source of GLA. In the 1970s and 1980s, the theory that eczema, breast pain and other conditions reflected a block at the desaturase step, which preformed GLA could bypass, gained support, and the British company Efamol and its founder David Horrobin promoted the oil heavily. In the UK, evening primrose oil was licensed as a prescription medicine for eczema and breast pain, but after independent reviews found insufficient evidence, the regulator withdrew those licenses in 2002. A later Cochrane review confirmed no benefit for eczema.", "timeline": [["1600s", "Evening primrose is introduced from North America to Europe."], ["1970s-1980s", "Evening primrose oil is promoted as a GLA source to bypass delta-6-desaturase in eczema and other conditions."], ["1980s", "Evening primrose oil products are licensed in the UK for atopic eczema and later for breast pain."], ["2002", "The UK Medicines Control Agency withdraws the product licenses for evening primrose oil prescription products for lack of evidence."], ["2013", "A Cochrane review finds that oral evening primrose oil and borage oil do not improve eczema."]], "cell": "Linoleic acid, the main omega-6 fatty acid in the diet, is converted by delta-6-desaturase to gamma-linolenic acid, a step that is slow and rate-limiting. GLA is rapidly elongated to dihomo-gamma-linolenic acid, which is incorporated into cell membrane phospholipids. DGLA can follow two paths: cyclooxygenase converts it into prostaglandin E1, which relaxes blood vessels, reduces platelet aggregation and dampens some inflammatory responses, and 15-lipoxygenase converts it into 15-HETrE, which inhibits leukotriene formation. DGLA can also be desaturated by delta-5-desaturase to arachidonic acid, the precursor of more inflammatory eicosanoids, but in humans this step is limited, so supplemental GLA tends to raise DGLA more than arachidonic acid. The theory behind evening primrose oil was that people with eczema had impaired delta-6-desaturase activity, and that supplying GLA directly would correct a skin barrier and immune imbalance. Clinical trials did not confirm a meaningful benefit, suggesting the theory was incomplete or the effect too small to matter.", "body": [["Skin", "Proposed improvement in skin barrier and eczema was not confirmed in Cochrane-reviewed trials."], ["Breasts", "Trials for cyclic breast pain have been mostly negative or inconclusive."], ["Blood", "DGLA-derived prostaglandin E1 can reduce platelet aggregation; theoretical additive bleeding risk with anticoagulants."], ["Nerves", "Small studies in diabetic neuropathy suggested possible modest benefit."], ["Gut", "Nausea, loose stools and stomach upset are the common side effects."]], "journey": "Evening primrose oil is swallowed in softgels or as liquid oil. Its triglycerides are digested by pancreatic lipase, absorbed in the small intestine, and packaged into chylomicrons that travel through the lymphatic system into the blood. GLA is taken up by tissues, elongated to DGLA, and incorporated into membrane phospholipids of cells including platelets, skin cells and immune cells, where levels rise over weeks of use. Like other fatty acids, GLA and DGLA are ultimately used for energy through beta-oxidation or converted to eicosanoids that are quickly inactivated. There is no classical drug half-life, and membrane changes reverse gradually after use stops.", "sidefx": "Evening primrose oil is usually well tolerated, with nausea, indigestion, loose stools and headache the most common complaints. Because DGLA-derived prostaglandin E1 can reduce platelet aggregation, there is a theoretical additive bleeding risk with warfarin, antiplatelets and other bleeding-risk supplements, and before surgery. Older case reports described seizures in people taking phenothiazines or with schizophrenia; later reviews judged this evidence weak and confounded, but caution remains in many references. Safety in pregnancy is uncertain, with conflicting reports about use to prepare the cervix near term. Allergic reactions are rare.", "fun": "Evening primrose flowers can unfold within minutes at dusk, quickly enough to watch, timing their opening to the arrival of the night-flying moths that pollinate them.", "src": ["NIH NCCIH: Evening Primrose Oil fact sheet", "Bamford JTM, Ray S, Musekiwa A et al. Oral evening primrose oil and borage oil for eczema, Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews 2013", "UK Medicines Control Agency 2002 withdrawal of Epogam and Efamast product licences", "Natural medicine monograph data on Oenothera biennis"]}, "saw-palmetto": {"origin": "Saw palmetto is a low, shrubby palm with saw-toothed leaf stalks that grows across the southeastern United States, especially Florida. Its dark berries were eaten by Native American peoples, including the Seminole, and used as a food and medicine. In the late nineteenth century American physicians and pharmacists promoted saw palmetto berry preparations for urinary and reproductive complaints in men, and the berry appeared in the United States Pharmacopeia and National Formulary in the early twentieth century before falling out of mainstream use. Its revival came from Europe: French and German companies developed lipidosterolic extracts in the 1970s and 1980s, and saw palmetto became widely used for benign prostatic hyperplasia there. Small European trials suggested improvement in urinary symptoms, and in the 1990s saw palmetto became one of the best-selling herbal supplements in the United States. Then came large, rigorous trials. The STEP trial, published in 2006, and the NIH-funded CAMUS trial, published in 2011, found no benefit over placebo even with escalating amounts, and a Cochrane review reached the same conclusion. Saw palmetto remains popular nonetheless.", "timeline": [["Traditional", "Native American peoples of the Southeast eat saw palmetto berries as food and medicine."], ["Late 1800s", "American physicians promote saw palmetto preparations for male urinary complaints."], ["1970s-1980s", "European manufacturers develop lipidosterolic saw palmetto extracts for BPH."], ["2006", "The STEP trial in the New England Journal of Medicine finds saw palmetto no better than placebo for BPH symptoms."], ["2011", "The NIH-funded CAMUS trial in JAMA finds no benefit even with escalating amounts."]], "cell": "Testosterone is converted by 5-alpha-reductase to dihydrotestosterone, a more potent androgen that drives prostate growth. Finasteride and dutasteride block this enzyme and shrink the prostate. Saw palmetto lipidosterolic extract, made mostly of free fatty acids such as lauric, oleic and myristic acid along with phytosterols like beta-sitosterol, inhibits both type 1 and type 2 5-alpha-reductase in laboratory studies and may interfere with DHT binding to androgen receptors. Additional in vitro effects include inhibition of inflammatory mediators, alpha-1 adrenergic antagonism and effects on prostate cell growth. The key point for clinicians is the gap between the lab and the clinic: in most human studies, saw palmetto produced little change in serum DHT, prostate volume or PSA, unlike finasteride, and large trials found no improvement in symptoms. This suggests the concentrations reached in the prostate are too low, or that the in vitro effects do not translate into a clinically meaningful action.", "body": [["Prostate", "Inhibits 5-alpha-reductase in vitro, but large trials showed no improvement in urinary symptoms or flow."], ["Blood", "Case reports of bleeding, including intraoperative bleeding, raise caution with anticoagulants."], ["Gut", "Mild nausea and stomach upset are the most common side effects."], ["Hormones", "Little effect on serum testosterone, DHT or PSA in most human studies."], ["Hair follicles", "Studied for androgenic hair loss with limited evidence."]], "journey": "Saw palmetto is usually swallowed as softgels or capsules of lipidosterolic extract made with hexane, ethanol or supercritical carbon dioxide. Because the active fraction is fat-soluble, teas extract little of it. The fatty acids are absorbed like dietary fats, incorporated into chylomicrons and handled by normal lipid metabolism; phytosterols are poorly absorbed. Human pharmacokinetic data for specific constituents are limited, and blood levels and half-lives are not well characterized. Prostate tissue concentrations are uncertain, which may help explain the gap between laboratory and clinical findings.", "sidefx": "In large trials, side effects occurred at rates similar to placebo, with mild nausea, stomach upset, headache and dizziness most often reported. Case reports describe bleeding, including excessive bleeding during surgery, and possible interaction with anticoagulants and antiplatelets. Rare case reports describe pancreatitis and liver injury, with uncertain causality. Because of theoretical antiandrogen effects, saw palmetto is avoided in pregnancy and breastfeeding and in children. Perhaps the biggest risk is indirect: urinary symptoms attributed to BPH may reflect prostate cancer, infection, stones or bladder problems that need evaluation.", "fun": "The saw in saw palmetto refers to the rows of sharp, tooth-like spines along its leaf stalks, which can cut skin and clothing as easily as a small saw blade.", "src": ["NIH NCCIH: Saw Palmetto fact sheet", "Bent S et al. Saw palmetto for benign prostatic hyperplasia, New England Journal of Medicine 2006", "Barry MJ et al. Effect of increasing doses of saw palmetto extract on lower urinary tract symptoms, JAMA 2011", "Tacklind J et al. Serenoa repens for benign prostatic hyperplasia, Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews 2012"]}, "pygeum": {"origin": "Prunus africana is a tall evergreen tree of the montane forests of Africa, found from Cameroon and Nigeria to Kenya, Uganda, Madagascar and southern Africa. Local healers used the bark for many purposes, including chest pain, fever and urinary problems, and the tree belongs to the same genus as cherries, plums and almonds. European interest began in the mid-twentieth century; a French company developed a standardized lipophilic bark extract in the 1960s, and pygeum became a widely prescribed treatment for benign prostatic hyperplasia in France and other parts of Europe. Demand from the European market drove large-scale bark harvesting, especially in Cameroon and Madagascar, often stripping trees in ways that killed them. Concern about overexploitation led to listing of Prunus africana under CITES Appendix II in 1995, and international trade has since been subject to export controls and periodic suspensions. Clinical evidence comes mainly from small, short European trials from the 1970s to 1990s, summarized in a Cochrane review that found modest benefit but called for larger, rigorous trials, which have not been done.", "timeline": [["Traditional", "African healers use Prunus africana bark for fever, chest pain and urinary complaints."], ["1960s", "A standardized lipophilic pygeum bark extract is developed in France for prostate symptoms."], ["1970s-1990s", "Small European trials report improved urinary symptoms with pygeum extract."], ["1995", "Prunus africana is listed under CITES Appendix II because of overharvesting."], ["2002", "A Cochrane review finds modest benefit in older trials of limited quality."]], "cell": "Pygeum extract is a mixture rather than a single active compound. Phytosterols, especially beta-sitosterol, may reduce prostaglandin synthesis in prostate tissue and compete with cholesterol, a precursor of steroid hormones. Pentacyclic triterpenes such as ursolic and oleanolic acid have anti-inflammatory and antiedema effects in laboratory studies. Ferulic acid esters, including n-docosanol and tetracosanol, were reported in animal studies to lower prostate cholesterol and prolactin levels. In cell and animal studies, extract inhibited proliferation of prostate fibroblasts stimulated by growth factors such as basic fibroblast growth factor, and improved bladder contractility and reduced bladder wall changes caused by outlet obstruction. These findings suggest pygeum may act on both the prostate stroma and the bladder, but which effects occur at clinically reached concentrations is not known.", "body": [["Prostate", "Anti-inflammatory and antiproliferative effects in laboratory studies; modest symptom benefit in older small trials."], ["Bladder", "Animal studies suggest improved bladder function under outlet obstruction."], ["Gut", "Nausea, stomach upset, diarrhea or constipation are the most common side effects."], ["Hormones", "Ferulic esters may lower prolactin in animal studies; human hormonal effects are not established."], ["Skin and immune system", "Rare allergic reactions are reported."]], "journey": "Pygeum is swallowed as capsules or tablets of a lipophilic bark extract. The fat-soluble sterols and triterpenes are absorbed to a limited extent, with phytosterols in particular poorly absorbed from the gut. Human pharmacokinetic data are essentially lacking: blood levels, metabolism, prostate tissue concentrations and excretion have not been well characterized. In trials, effects on urinary symptoms were measured over weeks to a few months of regular use, consistent with a gradual tissue effect rather than a fast-acting one.", "sidefx": "Side effects in trials were mild and similar to placebo, mainly nausea, stomach upset, diarrhea and constipation. Rare allergic reactions are possible. No clinically important drug interactions are well documented. Long-term safety data are limited. The main practical concern is that symptoms attributed to an enlarged prostate, such as frequency, weak stream, retention or blood in the urine, may reflect infection, stones, bladder problems or prostate cancer, and self-treatment can delay diagnosis. Sustainability is another concern: wild bark harvesting has damaged Prunus africana populations.", "fun": "Prunus africana is a close relative of the cherries, plums and almonds of the same genus, yet in its native forests it can grow into a towering tree, far larger than its orchard cousins, which is part of why stripping its bark became such a lucrative trade.", "src": ["Wilt T, Ishani A, Mac Donald R et al. Pygeum africanum for benign prostatic hyperplasia, Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews 2002", "CITES Appendix II listing for Prunus africana", "Natural medicine monograph data on Prunus africana", "Ishani A et al. Pygeum africanum for the treatment of patients with benign prostatic hyperplasia: a systematic review and quantitative meta-analysis, American Journal of Medicine 2000"]}, "stinging-nettle": {"origin": "Stinging nettle grows in nitrogen-rich soil across Europe, Asia, North Africa and North America, and few plants have been used in as many ways. Its fibers were woven into cloth from the Bronze Age onward, and nettle fabric was used for uniforms in Germany during the First World War when cotton was scarce. The young leaves have long been cooked as a spring green, in soups and in dishes like Italian nettle risotto, and the plant served as a source of green dye. Medicinally, Greek and Roman writers described nettle for coughs and as a diuretic, and Roman soldiers are often said to have flogged themselves with nettles to warm their limbs in cold climates, a story that is told often but hard to verify. Deliberate stinging of painful joints, called urtication, is an old folk remedy for arthritis still practiced in some places. In the twentieth century, German researchers developed nettle root extracts for urinary symptoms of BPH, and freeze-dried nettle leaf became a popular allergy supplement after a small American study in the 1990s. Evidence for these uses remains limited.", "timeline": [["Bronze Age", "Nettle fibers are used for textiles in Europe."], ["Ancient", "Greek and Roman writers describe nettle for coughs and as a diuretic."], ["1910s", "Germany uses nettle fiber for fabric during the First World War cotton shortage."], ["1990", "A small randomized study reports freeze-dried nettle leaf for allergic rhinitis, sparking supplement use."], ["2000s", "Small trials test nettle root extract alone and with saw palmetto for BPH, with mixed results."]], "cell": "The sting comes from hollow hairs, called trichomes, with brittle silica tips that break on contact and act like hypodermic needles, injecting a mixture that includes histamine, acetylcholine and serotonin, along with other compounds such as oxalic and tartaric acid that may prolong the pain; the long-standing idea that formic acid is the main irritant is debated. Leaf extracts, by contrast, show anti-inflammatory activity in laboratory studies, inhibiting NF-kappa-B activation, prostaglandin and leukotriene formation, and possibly blocking histamine H1 receptors and mast cell degranulation, which supports the allergy interest. Nettle root works differently. Lignans in the root bind sex hormone-binding globulin and may interfere with its binding to prostate cell membranes, and root extracts inhibit aromatase and 5-alpha-reductase weakly in vitro; the root lectin Urtica dioica agglutinin inhibits growth factor signaling in prostate cells in laboratory work. Leaves are also rich in minerals and vitamin K, which is the basis for warfarin interaction.", "body": [["Skin", "Fresh plant causes stinging, burning and hives; deliberate stinging is a folk arthritis remedy."], ["Nose and airways", "Possible antihistamine and anti-inflammatory effects behind small allergic rhinitis studies."], ["Prostate", "Root extracts studied for BPH symptoms with mixed results."], ["Kidneys", "Traditional diuretic use; possible additive effects with diuretics and lithium."], ["Blood", "Vitamin K in leaf products can reduce warfarin's anticoagulant effect."]], "journey": "Nettle is swallowed as capsules of dried or freeze-dried leaf, root extracts, teas and tinctures, or eaten as cooked greens. Cooking and drying break down the stinging hairs, so prepared products do not sting. Absorption and metabolism of specific constituents such as flavonoids, lignans and the root lectin have not been well characterized in humans. Vitamin K from leaf products is absorbed with dietary fat and handled like vitamin K from other leafy greens. Excretion is not well described, though a mild diuretic effect is traditionally reported. On the skin, the injected irritants act locally within seconds and the reaction usually fades within hours.", "sidefx": "Swallowed nettle products are generally well tolerated, with mild stomach upset, diarrhea and sweating reported. Allergic reactions are possible. Leaf vitamin K can lower INR in people on warfarin. Possible additive effects with diuretics, lithium, antihypertensives and diabetes medicines have been suggested, though clinical data are limited. Nettle is avoided in pregnancy because of traditional concerns about uterine stimulation. Contact with the fresh plant causes stinging, burning, itching and hives, sometimes with numbness, that typically resolve within hours.", "fun": "Nettle stings are delivered by a tiny biological syringe: each hair ends in a silica bulb that snaps off at a precise weak point, leaving a sharp beveled tip that pierces skin and injects its irritant cocktail.", "src": ["NIH NCCIH: Stinging Nettle fact sheet", "European Medicines Agency (HMPC): Community herbal monographs on Urtica dioica leaf and root", "Mittman P. Randomized, double-blind study of freeze-dried Urtica dioica in the treatment of allergic rhinitis, Planta Medica 1990", "Natural medicine monograph data on Urtica dioica"]}, "tribulus": {"origin": "Tribulus terrestris is a low, creeping annual of warm, dry regions worldwide, notorious for its hard, spiny fruits, called goatheads or puncture vines, which can puncture bicycle tires and injure bare feet and animal hooves. The fruit has been used in traditional Chinese medicine, where it is called ci ji li, and in Ayurveda, where it is known as gokshura, for urinary complaints, eye problems, headaches and male vitality. The modern supplement story began in Eastern Europe. In the 1970s and 1980s Bulgarian researchers studied tribulus extracts, including a product made from Bulgarian plants rich in protodioscin, for male infertility and libido, and stories spread that Eastern Bloc athletes used tribulus to boost performance. By the 1990s tribulus had become a staple of Western bodybuilding and testosterone booster products. Human trials since then, in young men, athletes and older men, have consistently found no increase in testosterone, muscle mass or strength. Meanwhile, the plant is known to veterinarians as a cause of photosensitization and nervous system disease in grazing sheep, and case reports describe kidney, liver and other harms in people using tribulus products.", "timeline": [["Traditional", "Chinese medicine and Ayurveda use tribulus fruit for urinary complaints and male vitality."], ["1970s-1980s", "Bulgarian researchers study tribulus extracts for male infertility and libido."], ["1990s", "Tribulus becomes a popular ingredient in Western bodybuilding and testosterone booster supplements."], ["2000s", "Trials in athletes and young men find no increase in testosterone or strength."], ["2014", "A systematic review concludes that tribulus does not increase testosterone in humans."]], "cell": "The compounds most often credited with tribulus activity are steroidal saponins, especially the furostanol saponin protodioscin, along with flavonoids and alkaloids. Supplement makers describe protodioscin as raising luteinizing hormone, which would stimulate testicular testosterone production, or as being converted to dehydroepiandrosterone. In some animal studies, tribulus extracts increased testosterone, luteinizing hormone or sexual behavior, and they relaxed penile smooth muscle through nitric oxide release in laboratory experiments, which may underlie libido and erectile claims. In human trials, however, serum testosterone, luteinizing hormone and body composition did not change. Saponin content varies dramatically with the plant's geographic origin and the part used, which complicates comparisons. In grazing animals, tribulus causes hepatogenous photosensitization, in which saponin-related crystals damage bile ducts and allow phototoxic chlorophyll breakdown products to build up, and a separate locomotor disorder in sheep has been linked to beta-carboline alkaloids.", "body": [["Testes and hormones", "No increase in human testosterone in trials, despite animal data and marketing claims."], ["Kidneys", "Acute kidney injury reported in case reports, sometimes with large amounts or tribulus-containing water."], ["Liver", "Liver injury reported in case reports; animals grazing the plant develop liver damage and photosensitization."], ["Breasts", "Gynecomastia reported in a case report in a young weightlifter."], ["Gut and sleep", "Stomach upset, cramping and sleep disturbance are the common mild complaints."]], "journey": "Tribulus is swallowed as capsules, tablets or powders, often within multi-ingredient sports and libido products. Steroidal saponins are large glycosides that are poorly absorbed intact; gut bacteria may remove sugar groups, producing aglycones that are absorbed to a limited degree. Human pharmacokinetic data are essentially absent, so blood levels, metabolism and excretion are not well characterized. Because effects in trials were measured over weeks of use and found no hormonal change, the question of pharmacokinetics matters less than product composition, which varies with plant origin and may include undeclared ingredients in multi-ingredient boosters.", "sidefx": "Most trial participants reported mild side effects such as stomach upset, cramping and sleep problems. Case reports describe acute kidney injury, liver injury, gynecomastia and neurological problems in people using tribulus products, though causality is uncertain and contamination of multi-ingredient products is a confounder. FDA has repeatedly identified hidden drugs such as anabolic steroids and erectile dysfunction drugs in bodybuilding and sexual enhancement supplements, so a tribulus-containing booster may expose users to more than tribulus. Animal studies raise concern for pregnancy. Possible glucose lowering and diuretic effects are proposed but not well established in humans.", "fun": "Tribulus burrs are so effective at puncturing tires that the plant spread along roads and railways across the American West, where it earned the nickname goathead, and some areas have released seed-eating weevils as a biological control.", "src": ["Qureshi A, Naughton DP, Petroczi A. A systematic review on the herbal extract Tribulus terrestris and the roots of its putative aphrodisiac and performance enhancing effect, Journal of Dietary Supplements 2014", "FDA: Tainted sexual enhancement and bodybuilding products notifications", "Neychev VK, Mitev VI. The aphrodisiac herb Tribulus terrestris does not influence the androgen production in young men, Journal of Ethnopharmacology 2005", "Veterinary toxicology literature on Tribulus terrestris photosensitization in sheep"]}, "tongkat-ali": {"origin": "Eurycoma longifolia is a slender shrub or small tree of the rainforests of Malaysia, Indonesia, Thailand and nearby countries, where it is called tongkat ali, often translated as Ali's walking stick, and pasak bumi in Indonesia. The bitter root has a long folk history in Malay traditional medicine as a tonic after childbirth, for fevers and malaria, and above all as a male vitality remedy. In the late twentieth century Malaysian researchers and government agencies promoted the plant as a national herbal product, and standardized water extracts were developed and studied in Malaysia. Global sales grew through the 2000s as testosterone-booster and male enhancement products moved online. The scientific story is a familiar one for herbal hormone products: many animal and cell studies, a modest number of small human trials, many funded by extract makers, and no approval anywhere as a drug for low testosterone. Two safety stories travel with it. Male enhancement supplements are among the categories in which FDA most often finds hidden prescription drugs, and testing of some Southeast Asian herbal products has found heavy metals.", "timeline": [["Traditional use", "Used in Malay and Indonesian folk medicine as a tonic, for fevers, and for male vitality."], ["1990s to 2000s", "Quassinoids including eurycomanone are characterized and Malaysian research programs promote standardized root extracts."], ["2000s", "Tongkat ali spreads internationally in testosterone-booster and male enhancement supplements sold online."], ["2010s", "Small randomized trials report modest hormone or stress-marker changes; systematic reviews rate the evidence as limited."], ["Ongoing", "FDA health fraud notices continue to list sexual enhancement supplements found to contain hidden drug ingredients."]], "cell": "The root contains quassinoids, highly oxygenated bitter triterpene-derived compounds, of which eurycomanone is the most studied, along with canthinone alkaloids, squalene derivatives and small bioactive peptides sometimes described as a glycoprotein fraction. In rat Leydig cell and animal studies, eurycomanone has been reported to raise testosterone output, with proposed actions including reduced aromatase conversion of testosterone to estradiol and influences on steroid-making enzymes and phosphodiesterase. Some quassinoids are cytotoxic and have antimalarial activity in the laboratory, which matches part of the traditional use. None of these targets has been confirmed as the cause of a clinical effect in people, and effects in a dish do not show what a capsule does in a human body. Human trials have measured total or free testosterone, cortisol, mood scores and sexual function questionnaires with small sample sizes and short durations, so any true effect is likely modest and uncertain.", "body": [["Testes and hormone axis", "Proposed modest effects on testosterone production based on animal work and small trials; no proven clinical benefit."], ["Reproductive and sexual function", "Marketed for libido and erectile function; evidence is limited and adulterated products may contain PDE5 inhibitors."], ["Cardiovascular system", "Main risk comes from hidden drug ingredients, such as a PDE5 inhibitor combined with nitrates causing severe hypotension."], ["Nervous system", "Insomnia, restlessness and irritability have been reported, often with multi-ingredient products."], ["Liver and kidneys", "No clear signal from the herb alone; heavy metal contaminants in some imported products are a separate concern."]], "journey": "Human pharmacokinetic data are sparse. Animal studies of eurycomanone describe poor oral bioavailability, explained by limited membrane permeability rather than rapid destruction in the gut, with the compound distributed and cleared within hours in rodents. How quassinoids are metabolized in the human liver and how they are excreted has not been well defined. Products are made by hot water or alcohol extraction of dried root, and the amount of eurycomanone and other markers varies between brands, which makes trial results hard to compare and blood levels hard to predict. Because many products are blends, any effect a user notices may come from other ingredients such as caffeine or, in adulterated products, a hidden drug.", "sidefx": "In trials of standardized extracts, side effects have been uncommon and mild, such as insomnia, restlessness, irritability and stomach upset. The more serious risks are outside the herb itself. Sexual enhancement supplements are a recurring category in FDA health fraud notices for undeclared sildenafil, tadalafil or their analogs, which can cause headache, flushing and, with nitrates, a dangerous drop in blood pressure. Heavy metals including mercury and lead have been reported in testing of some Southeast Asian herbal products. Because the product is marketed to affect hormones, its use is a concern in hormone-sensitive cancers and alongside prescribed testosterone, although direct harm has not been clearly shown.", "fun": "The name is often translated as Ali's walking stick, and in Indonesia the same root is called pasak bumi, which means nail of the earth, a nod to its long, straight taproot.", "src": ["NIH NCCIH: Using Dietary Supplements Wisely", "FDA: Tainted Sexual Enhancement Products", "Thu et al. 2017, Eurycoma longifolia as a potential adoptogen of male sexual health: a systematic review (Andrologia)", "Rehman, Choe and Yoo 2016, Review on a traditional herbal medicine, Eurycoma longifolia (Molecules)"]}, "horny-goat-weed": {"origin": "Epimedium is a genus of low, leafy plants in the barberry family native mainly to China, with some species in other parts of Asia and the Mediterranean region. In Chinese the herb is yin yang huo, and it has been recorded in classical Chinese materia medica for centuries as a kidney yang tonic used for impotence, weak knees and back, and joint complaints. The English name horny goat weed comes from a legend, retold in many supplement advertisements, that a goat herder noticed his flock becoming more sexually active after grazing on the plant; the story cannot be verified and should be treated as folklore. Modern interest focused on icariin, a flavonoid glycoside isolated from the leaves, after laboratory work showed that it weakly inhibits phosphodiesterase type 5, the enzyme targeted by sildenafil. Since sildenafil reached the market in 1998, horny goat weed has become a staple of male enhancement blends, a product category that FDA repeatedly finds to contain hidden prescription drugs. Epimedium is also used in Chinese multi-herb products for bone health, and some of these have been linked to liver injury reports in China.", "timeline": [["Classical era", "Epimedium (yin yang huo) appears in Chinese materia medica as a tonic for impotence and weak bones."], ["1998", "Sildenafil approval drives interest in natural PDE5-inhibiting compounds, including icariin."], ["2000s", "Laboratory studies describe weak PDE5 inhibition by icariin and synthetic derivatives with stronger activity."], ["2000s to present", "Horny goat weed becomes a common ingredient in male enhancement blends sold online and in convenience stores."], ["Ongoing", "FDA health fraud notices continue to identify sexual enhancement products containing hidden PDE5 inhibitors."]], "cell": "Icariin is a prenylated flavonol glycoside. In enzyme assays it inhibits phosphodiesterase type 5, the enzyme that breaks down cyclic GMP in smooth muscle of the penis and blood vessels, but its potency is far below that of sildenafil, and chemists have made modified icariin derivatives in an attempt to improve it. In rodent studies icariin has been reported to increase nitric oxide synthase expression and cyclic GMP levels in penile tissue, to act on osteoblasts and osteoclasts in ways that favor bone formation, and to show weak estrogen-like activity, partly through its metabolite icaritin. Laboratory studies also describe antiplatelet effects. The gap between these cell findings and human outcomes is large, because icariin is poorly absorbed and quickly transformed, so concentrations used in dishes may never be reached in human tissue. No well-designed human trial has shown that horny goat weed treats erectile dysfunction.", "body": [["Penile and vascular smooth muscle", "Weak PDE5 inhibition in the lab could in theory raise cyclic GMP; no proven effect on erections in people."], ["Heart and circulation", "Rapid heartbeat and low blood pressure have been reported, especially with blends; nitrates plus hidden PDE5 inhibitors are dangerous."], ["Bone", "Animal and small human studies of Epimedium products suggest effects on bone turnover; evidence is limited."], ["Liver", "Some Chinese multi-herb Epimedium products have been linked to liver injury reports, with causality not settled."], ["Nervous system", "Dizziness and, in a case report, hypomania have been described with Epimedium-containing products."]], "journey": "After swallowing, icariin is poorly absorbed as the intact glycoside. Gut bacteria and intestinal enzymes remove sugar groups to form icariside II and then icaritin, which are absorbed better and are then extensively glucuronidated in the gut wall and liver. In animal studies, plasma levels of the parent compound are low and short-lived, and most of the material is eliminated through bile and feces. Formal human pharmacokinetic studies are limited, so onset, peak and duration in people are not defined. Product content varies a great deal, and many capsules are combined with caffeine, yohimbe or other herbs, which complicates any attempt to link an effect to icariin itself.", "sidefx": "Reported side effects include dizziness, dry mouth, thirst, nosebleed and stomach upset. Case reports describe rapid heart rhythm and hypomania in people using Epimedium-containing products, often blends, so the specific cause is uncertain. Low blood pressure is a theoretical concern with antihypertensives. Laboratory antiplatelet effects raise a theoretical bleeding concern with anticoagulants. The most serious practical risk is adulteration, because hidden PDE5 inhibitors can cause severe hypotension with nitrates and hidden stimulants can trigger palpitations. Multi-herb Epimedium bone products in China have been linked to liver injury.", "fun": "Chemists have used icariin as a starting scaffold to make modified molecules that inhibit PDE5 far more strongly than the natural compound, which shows how weak the plant version is by comparison.", "src": ["Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center About Herbs: Epimedium", "FDA: Tainted Sexual Enhancement Products", "Dell'Agli et al. 2008, Potent inhibition of human phosphodiesterase-5 by icariin derivatives (Journal of Natural Products)", "NIH NCCIH: Using Dietary Supplements Wisely"]}, "yohimbe": {"origin": "Pausinystalia johimbe is an evergreen tree of the coastal forests of West and Central Africa, including Cameroon, Gabon and Nigeria, where the bark has a long traditional use as a stimulant and aphrodisiac. European chemists isolated the alkaloid yohimbine from the bark in the 1890s, and it entered European and American pharmacology in the early twentieth century as a supposed aphrodisiac and later as a research tool. Pharmacologists in the late twentieth century found its main action: selective blockade of alpha-2 adrenergic receptors, which made yohimbine a standard laboratory probe for studying the sympathetic nervous system and anxiety. Prescription yohimbine hydrochloride tablets were marketed in the United States for impotence, although its effects in trials were modest. In 1989 FDA issued a rule that OTC drug products sold as aphrodisiacs, yohimbine included, were not generally recognized as safe and effective. Yohimbe bark later reappeared as a dietary supplement in male enhancement, fat-burning and pre-workout products, where testing has found labels that often misstate how much yohimbine is present, and poison centers continue to see sympathomimetic toxicity from these products.", "timeline": [["Traditional use", "Bark used in West and Central Africa as a stimulant and aphrodisiac."], ["1890s", "Yohimbine is isolated from the bark by European chemists."], ["Late 20th century", "Yohimbine is characterized as a selective alpha-2 adrenergic antagonist and used widely as a research tool."], ["1989", "FDA rules that OTC aphrodisiac drug products, including yohimbine, are not generally recognized as safe and effective."], ["2016", "A published analysis finds that many yohimbe supplements have inaccurate yohimbine labeling, some with pharmaceutical-level amounts."]], "cell": "Yohimbine is an indole alkaloid structurally related to reserpine. Its key action is competitive antagonism at alpha-2 adrenergic receptors. Presynaptic alpha-2 receptors on sympathetic nerve terminals and on neurons of the locus coeruleus act as a negative feedback brake: when norepinephrine binds them, further release slows. By blocking that brake, yohimbine increases norepinephrine release in the periphery and the brain, which raises heart rate, blood pressure and arousal and produces anxiety, tremor and sweating. Central effects are strong enough that yohimbine has been used experimentally to provoke panic attacks in people with panic disorder. At higher concentrations it also interacts with some serotonin and dopamine receptors and with alpha-1 receptors. The proposed erectile effect is thought to come from increased central sexual arousal and reduced alpha-2 mediated vasoconstriction in penile tissue. Bark products also contain related alkaloids such as rauwolscine and corynanthine, which differ in receptor selectivity, making the overall effect of a bark product less predictable than that of pure yohimbine.", "body": [["Heart and blood vessels", "Raises heart rate and blood pressure through increased norepinephrine; arrhythmias and cardiac injury have been reported."], ["Brain", "Anxiety, agitation, insomnia and panic; seizures in severe toxicity."], ["Skin and sweat glands", "Sweating and flushing are common sympathetic signs."], ["Genitourinary tract", "Marketed for erectile function; frequent urination and, rarely, priapism have been reported."], ["Kidneys", "Rare kidney injury has been described in case reports."]], "journey": "Yohimbine is absorbed quickly after swallowing, with peak levels usually within about an hour, but its oral bioavailability varies enormously between people, from very low to nearly complete in pharmacokinetic studies. It is metabolized in the liver mainly by CYP2D6 to 11-hydroxy-yohimbine, an active metabolite, with a contribution from CYP3A4, and people who are poor CYP2D6 metabolizers can have much higher and longer-lasting exposure. The half-life of the parent drug is short in most people. Metabolites are excreted mainly in the urine. Because product content also varies, the same label can produce very different blood levels in different people, which partly explains unpredictable toxicity.", "sidefx": "Common effects are anxiety, nervousness, insomnia, tremor, sweating, nausea, headache, flushing and frequent urination. Serious effects are cardiovascular and neurological: severe hypertension, rapid heart rate, arrhythmias, chest pain and, in poison center series and case reports, seizures, myocardial injury, kidney injury and priapism. Risk rises when yohimbe is combined with caffeine, synephrine or other stimulants, as in many pre-workout and fat-burner products, and with MAOIs or tricyclic antidepressants. Bronchospasm and a lupus-like syndrome have also been described in isolated reports. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive, with benzodiazepines commonly used for agitation and seizures.", "fun": "Yohimbine is so reliable at triggering anxiety that psychiatric researchers have used it as a laboratory challenge drug to provoke panic symptoms in people with panic disorder.", "src": ["NIH NCCIH: Yohimbe", "21 CFR 310.528, OTC drug products for use as an aphrodisiac", "Cohen et al. 2016, Drug Testing and Analysis, yohimbine content of dietary supplements", "StatPearls: Yohimbine", "Poison control center literature on yohimbine toxicity"]}, "wild-yam": {"origin": "Dioscorea villosa is a twining vine of eastern North American woodlands, used in nineteenth-century American eclectic medicine as a remedy for colic and called colic root. Its modern reputation comes from a different chapter of history. In the late 1930s and early 1940s the American chemist Russell Marker developed a method, now called the Marker degradation, for turning diosgenin, a steroidal sapogenin found in some yams, into progesterone. Searching for a rich source, he turned to wild Mexican yams such as cabeza de negro, and in 1944 he helped found Syntex in Mexico City to produce progesterone from them. Syntex chemists including Carl Djerassi, Luis Miramontes and George Rosenkranz synthesized norethindrone in 1951, one of the first orally active progestins used in birth control pills. Yam-derived diosgenin and its relatives fed the industrial steroid supply for decades. Supplement marketing later blurred this history into the claim that eating yams or applying wild yam cream provides natural progesterone. The chemistry does not work in the body: human enzymes cannot perform the laboratory steps, so wild yam itself is not a hormone.", "timeline": [["19th century", "Wild yam (colic root) is used in American eclectic medicine for colic and cramps."], ["Late 1930s to early 1940s", "Russell Marker develops the Marker degradation for making progesterone from diosgenin."], ["1944", "Syntex is founded in Mexico City to produce progesterone from Mexican yams."], ["1951", "Syntex chemists synthesize norethindrone, an early oral progestin."], ["2001", "A small placebo-controlled trial finds that wild yam cream does not change menopause symptoms or hormone levels."]], "cell": "Diosgenin is a spirostan steroidal sapogenin, released when the sugar chains of saponins such as dioscin are removed by acid hydrolysis. Its four-ring steroid skeleton resembles cholesterol and the sex hormones, which is why chemists could use it as a starting point. The Marker degradation uses reagents such as acetic anhydride and chromium-based oxidation to open the spiroketal side chain and produce pregnenolone derivatives, then progesterone. Human tissues lack enzymes to carry out these reactions, so diosgenin taken by mouth or rubbed on the skin is not converted into progesterone, estrogen or DHEA, and a small trial confirmed no change in sex hormone levels. In cell and animal studies, diosgenin has shown anti-inflammatory, cholesterol-lowering and weak estrogen-like or anticancer activities, but these findings have not been shown to translate into clinical benefits. The real hormonal activity of some natural progesterone creams comes from added USP progesterone, which is itself produced industrially from plant sterols.", "body": [["Reproductive system", "No proven effect on menopause symptoms from wild yam alone; added progesterone in some creams may cause spotting or breast tenderness."], ["Gastrointestinal tract", "Large oral amounts may cause nausea and stomach upset; traditionally used for colic."], ["Liver and lipids", "Animal studies suggest effects on cholesterol absorption; human evidence is lacking."], ["Skin", "Creams may cause local irritation; diosgenin is not converted to hormones in the skin."]], "journey": "Diosgenin and its parent saponins are poorly absorbed from the gut. Animal studies show low and slow absorption, a long residence in the body for the small absorbed fraction, and elimination mainly in the feces. Absorption through the skin from creams is not well characterized. Crucially, absorbed diosgenin is not transformed into progesterone or other human steroid hormones, because the oxidative steps needed to cut its side chain exist only in the chemistry lab. When a natural progesterone cream does raise progesterone levels, the progesterone was added to the product and is absorbed through the skin like any topical hormone.", "sidefx": "Wild yam is not associated with serious toxicity in typical use. Oral products can cause nausea, vomiting and stomach upset, and creams can irritate the skin. The main risks are indirect: a person may rely on wild yam as hormone therapy or contraception that does not work, or may use a cream with added progesterone without realizing it, which matters for people with hormone-sensitive cancers or abnormal bleeding. Hormone-sensitive conditions are listed as cautions largely because of this product confusion and theoretical laboratory estrogen-like effects.", "fun": "For a time in the mid-twentieth century, wild Mexican yams collected by rural harvesters were a major raw material for the world's steroid hormone industry, including early birth control pills.", "src": ["Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center About Herbs: Wild Yam", "Komesaroff et al. 2001, Climacteric, wild yam cream trial", "American Chemical Society National Historic Chemical Landmark: Russell Marker and the Mexican steroid hormone industry", "NIH NCCIH: Menopausal Symptoms and Complementary Health Approaches"]}, "echinacea": {"origin": "Echinacea species are native to the prairies and open woodlands of central North America, and Plains Indigenous nations used the roots, particularly of Echinacea angustifolia, for wounds, snakebite, toothache, sore throat and infections. In the late nineteenth century a Nebraska lay practitioner, H. C. F. Meyer, promoted the root in a patent remedy, and the Lloyd Brothers pharmacy of Cincinnati, through John Uri Lloyd, turned echinacea into one of the most popular remedies of American eclectic medicine. Its use in the United States faded as antibiotics and evidence-based medicine rose. Interest moved to Germany in the 1930s, where Gerhard Madaus cultivated Echinacea purpurea and developed pressed juice products, and German research made echinacea a mainstream cold remedy in Europe. It returned to the United States as a top-selling supplement in the 1990s. Large trials then complicated the story: a 2005 rhinovirus challenge study of E. angustifolia root found no meaningful effect, a pediatric trial found no benefit and more rashes, and Cochrane reviewers concluded that some products might have weak effects but that results are inconsistent.", "timeline": [["Before 1800s", "Plains Indigenous nations use echinacea roots for wounds, infections and pain."], ["1880s to 1890s", "Echinacea is promoted in patent remedies and adopted by eclectic physicians through the Lloyd Brothers."], ["1930s", "Gerhard Madaus cultivates E. purpurea in Germany and develops pressed juice preparations."], ["2003", "A pediatric trial finds no benefit for colds and more rashes with echinacea."], ["2005", "A rhinovirus challenge trial of E. angustifolia root finds no meaningful effect on infection or symptoms."], ["2014", "A Cochrane review concludes that some products may have weak benefits but evidence is inconsistent."]], "cell": "Echinacea contains several groups of active compounds whose balance depends on the species and plant part. Alkamides, lipophilic fatty acid amides concentrated in the roots and responsible for the tingling sensation on the tongue, bind cannabinoid type 2 receptors on immune cells and alter cytokine release, including tumor necrosis factor alpha, in laboratory studies. Water-soluble polysaccharides and glycoproteins from the aerial parts activate macrophages and other innate immune cells in vitro. Caffeic acid derivatives, including cichoric acid in E. purpurea and echinacoside in E. angustifolia and E. pallida, have antioxidant activity. Laboratory studies also describe direct antiviral effects against enveloped viruses such as influenza. Translating these effects to people is difficult: concentrations in blood are low, products are inconsistent, and immune stimulation in a dish does not prove fewer or shorter colds. Echinacea also affects drug-metabolizing enzymes, reducing intestinal CYP3A activity while increasing hepatic CYP3A activity in a midazolam study.", "body": [["Immune system", "Laboratory immune stimulation; possible small effects on cold outcomes in some trials, none in others."], ["Upper airway", "Marketed for colds and sore throat; tingling of the tongue comes from alkamides."], ["Skin", "Rash and hives can occur, more often in children and atopic people."], ["Lungs", "Asthma attacks have been reported as part of allergic reactions."], ["Liver and gut", "Mixed effects on CYP3A drug metabolism; rare reports of liver enzyme elevation."]], "journey": "Alkamides are the best-studied constituents in humans. They are absorbed through the mouth and gut within roughly half an hour to an hour after a liquid or tablet product and are detectable in plasma at low levels, then cleared within hours, partly by CYP enzymes in the liver. Caffeic acid derivatives such as cichoric acid are poorly absorbed and are often undetectable in blood after oral use. The polysaccharides are large molecules with little absorption, so any effect they have may involve immune tissue in the gut. Elimination routes in people are not well characterized. Because tinctures, pressed juices, dried root capsules and aerial-part extracts deliver different mixtures, pharmacokinetic results from one product cannot be applied to another.", "sidefx": "Most people tolerate echinacea with mild effects such as stomach upset, nausea, unpleasant taste and tongue tingling. Allergic reactions are the main concern and range from rash and hives to asthma attacks and rare anaphylaxis, with higher risk in people with atopy or allergy to daisy-family plants. A pediatric trial found more rashes with echinacea than with placebo. Rare reports describe liver enzyme elevation. Because echinacea is marketed to stimulate the immune system, its use with immunosuppressants and in autoimmune disease is treated cautiously, though clinical harm has not been well documented. Its mixed effects on CYP3A make interactions with narrow-range CYP3A drugs possible.", "fun": "The tingling or numb feeling on the tongue from echinacea root comes from alkamides, and some herbal buyers have traditionally used that tingle as a rough quality test.", "src": ["NIH NCCIH: Echinacea", "Karsch-Volk et al. 2014, Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews, Echinacea for preventing and treating the common cold", "Turner et al. 2005, New England Journal of Medicine, An evaluation of Echinacea angustifolia in experimental rhinovirus infections", "Taylor et al. 2003, JAMA, Efficacy and safety of echinacea in treating upper respiratory tract infections in children", "Gorski et al. 2004, Clinical Pharmacology and Therapeutics, echinacea effects on CYP activity"]}, "elderberry": {"origin": "Elder shrubs grow across Europe, North Africa, western Asia and North America, and the European black elder, Sambucus nigra, has one of the longest folk medicine records of any European plant, with flowers, berries, bark and leaves used for fevers, colds, constipation and skin complaints. Elderflower and elderberry also became foods in cordials, wines, jams and pies, always made from ripe berries that are cooked. Modern supplement interest grew from Israeli research in the 1990s, when the virologist Madeleine Mumcuoglu developed a standardized elderberry extract and small trials suggested shorter influenza symptoms. Elderberry sales then surged, especially during severe flu seasons and the COVID-19 pandemic, far ahead of the evidence. The plant has a real toxic side: raw and unripe berries, seeds, leaves, stems and bark contain cyanogenic glycosides. In 1983 several people in California became ill after drinking juice pressed from raw elderberries together with leaves and stems, an event reported by the CDC in 1984 that remains the classic warning about homemade preparations.", "timeline": [["Ancient to medieval", "European black elder is used in folk medicine and foods across Europe."], ["1983", "An outbreak of nausea, vomiting and weakness in California follows juice from raw elderberries, leaves and stems; CDC reports it in 1984."], ["1990s", "A standardized elderberry extract developed in Israel is tested in small influenza trials."], ["2000s", "Elderberry syrups and lozenges become popular cold and flu supplements."], ["2020", "Elderberry sales surge during the COVID-19 pandemic despite no evidence that it prevents or treats COVID-19."]], "cell": "The dark color of elderberry comes from anthocyanins, mainly cyanidin-3-glucoside and cyanidin-3-sambubioside. In laboratory studies, elderberry extracts and flavonoids bind influenza virus surface proteins and block attachment and entry into cells, and extracts change the release of cytokines such as interleukins and tumor necrosis factor from immune cells. Whether these effects occur at concentrations reached in people is uncertain, because anthocyanins are poorly absorbed. The toxic chemistry is separate. Raw berries, especially unripe ones, and the seeds, leaves, stems and bark contain cyanogenic glycosides such as sambunigrin. When plant tissue is crushed, plant enzymes and gut bacteria can split these glycosides and release hydrogen cyanide, which inhibits cytochrome c oxidase in mitochondria and blocks cellular oxygen use. In typical elderberry illness, symptoms are mainly gastrointestinal, and some plant lectins may also contribute. Cooking ripe berries and removing stems and leaves greatly reduces the risk.", "body": [["Upper airway and immune system", "Marketed for colds and flu; small trials suggest possible modest effects on flu symptoms."], ["Gastrointestinal tract", "Raw plant parts cause nausea, vomiting, cramps and diarrhea."], ["Cells and mitochondria", "Large amounts of cyanide released from raw plant material could impair cellular oxygen use (rare)."], ["Kidneys and bowel", "Elder flower and bark have traditional diuretic and laxative uses."]], "journey": "Elderberry anthocyanins are absorbed in small amounts from the stomach and small intestine, appearing in blood within about an hour at low concentrations, partly as intact glycosides and partly as methylated or glucuronidated forms. Small amounts are excreted in urine. Most of the anthocyanin load reaches the colon, where gut bacteria break it into smaller phenolic acids that are absorbed and excreted over a longer period. Cyanogenic glycosides from raw plant parts are hydrolyzed in the gut, and released cyanide is absorbed quickly and detoxified in the liver by rhodanese to thiocyanate, which is excreted in urine. Commercial syrups and gummies made from cooked berries contain little of these glycosides.", "sidefx": "Commercial elderberry products are generally well tolerated in short-term studies, with occasional stomach upset. The sugar in syrups and gummies is relevant for people with diabetes and for dental health. The main harm comes from raw or improperly prepared plant material: nausea, vomiting, abdominal cramps, diarrhea, dizziness and weakness, sometimes in several people who shared a homemade drink. Severe cyanide poisoning from elderberry is rare. Because elderberry is marketed to stimulate immunity, it is treated cautiously in autoimmune disease and with immunosuppressants, though clinical harm has not been documented. Allergic reactions are possible.", "fun": "The same shrub that gives elderberry syrup also produces the flowers used in elderflower cordials and liqueurs, while its leaves and stems are the parts most likely to make people sick.", "src": ["NIH NCCIH: European Elder", "CDC MMWR 1984, Poisoning from elderberry juice, California", "Zakay-Rones et al. 1995, Journal of Alternative and Complementary Medicine, elderberry extract in influenza", "Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center About Herbs: Elderberry"]}, "andrographis": {"origin": "Andrographis paniculata is an annual herb native to India and Sri Lanka and widely grown across Southeast Asia and southern China. Its intense bitterness earned it the name king of bitters, and in Ayurveda it is known as kalmegh, used for fevers, liver complaints and digestive problems; in traditional Chinese medicine it is chuan xin lian, used for heat-clearing conditions such as sore throat and diarrhea. In Thailand it is used widely as a traditional medicine for colds. Its main bitter compound, andrographolide, was isolated in the early twentieth century and became a frequent subject of laboratory research on inflammation. In the 1990s, combination products with Siberian ginseng were studied in Scandinavia for colds, and andrographis entered Western cold and flu supplements. Safety attention grew in Australia, where the Therapeutic Goods Administration collected many reports of anaphylaxis, often on first use and in people without allergy histories, required label warnings, and in 2026 reported an updated safety review proposing removal of andrographis from low-risk listed medicines; the final outcome may differ from that proposal.", "timeline": [["Traditional use", "Used in Ayurveda (kalmegh), traditional Chinese medicine and Thai medicine for fevers, sore throat and digestive complaints."], ["Early 20th century", "Andrographolide is isolated as the main bitter compound."], ["1990s", "Combination products with Siberian ginseng are tested for colds in Scandinavian trials."], ["2010s", "Australia's TGA reviews anaphylaxis reports and later requires label warnings."], ["2026", "An updated TGA safety review proposes removing andrographis from low-risk listed medicines."]], "cell": "Andrographolide is a labdane diterpene lactone with a reactive unsaturated lactone ring. That reactive structure lets it form covalent bonds with cysteine residues on proteins, including a cysteine on the p50 subunit of NF-kappa-B, a master transcription factor for inflammatory genes. In laboratory studies this reduces production of cytokines such as tumor necrosis factor alpha and interleukin-6, as well as nitric oxide and prostaglandins. Studies also describe effects on immune cell activation and antiviral activity in cells. The same reactivity may help explain the allergic reactions: small reactive molecules can bind to body proteins and create new structures that the immune system recognizes as foreign, although the exact mechanism of andrographis anaphylaxis has not been established. Laboratory antiplatelet and blood pressure-lowering effects underlie theoretical interaction concerns.", "body": [["Upper airway", "Marketed for colds and sore throat; small trials suggest modest symptom improvement."], ["Immune system", "Anti-inflammatory effects in the lab; serious allergic reactions including anaphylaxis in people."], ["Gastrointestinal tract", "Bitter taste, nausea, diarrhea; studied preliminarily in ulcerative colitis."], ["Reproductive system", "Traditional associations with pregnancy loss and animal antifertility data lead to avoidance in pregnancy."], ["Kidneys", "Acute kidney injury reported mainly with injectable products used in other countries."]], "journey": "Andrographolide is absorbed from the gut after swallowing, reaching peak blood levels within roughly one to two hours in small human studies, though its oral bioavailability is limited by poor water solubility and gut efflux. It is metabolized in the intestine and liver mainly by conjugation, including sulfonation and glucuronidation, and some conjugates form through its reactive structure. The parent compound has a short half-life of a few hours. Metabolites are excreted mainly in the urine. Distribution into tissues is not well characterized in humans, and products vary in andrographolide content.", "sidefx": "Common effects are bitter taste, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, headache, fatigue and changes in taste. The most important adverse effect is allergy, from hives and rash to angioedema and anaphylaxis; Australian reports show that many reactions occur on first use and in people without previous allergies, so reactions are hard to predict. Elevated liver enzymes and acute kidney injury have been reported, mainly with injectable andrographolide products used outside the United States. Because of traditional links to pregnancy loss and animal antifertility findings, andrographis is avoided in pregnancy. Theoretical interactions include additive effects with anticoagulants and antihypertensives.", "fun": "Andrographis is so bitter that it is called king of bitters, and in parts of India it has traditionally been used as a bitter tonic precisely because of that taste.", "src": ["Therapeutic Goods Administration (Australia): Andrographis paniculata anaphylaxis updated safety review", "Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center About Herbs: Andrographis", "Hu et al. 2017, PLoS One, Andrographis paniculata for symptomatic relief of acute respiratory tract infections: systematic review", "Xia et al. 2004, Journal of Immunology, andrographolide covalently modifies NF-kappa-B p50"]}, "pelargonium": {"origin": "Pelargonium sidoides grows in the grasslands of South Africa and Lesotho, where traditional healers among Zulu, Xhosa, Basotho and other peoples used its roots for coughs, chest complaints and diarrhea. Its route into Europe is one of herbal medicine's odd stories. Charles Henry Stevens, an Englishman with tuberculosis, traveled to southern Africa around the end of the nineteenth century, was given a root decoction by a local healer, believed himself cured, and returned to England to sell the remedy as a consumption cure. Critics in the British medical establishment attacked the claims, and the remedy later became known by the name umckaloabo, which Stevens popularized. In the 1920s and 1930s a Swiss physician, Adrien Sechehaye, used it to treat tuberculosis patients, but the remedy faded with the arrival of effective antibiotics. Interest returned in Germany, where an ethanol-based root extract was developed and studied in many trials for acute bronchitis and colds and is sold as a licensed herbal medicine. In the United States, pelargonium products are sold as dietary supplements without FDA approval.", "timeline": [["Traditional use", "Root used by healers in South Africa and Lesotho for coughs and chest complaints."], ["Around 1900", "Charles Henry Stevens brings the remedy to England and markets it as a cure for consumption (tuberculosis)."], ["1920s to 1930s", "Swiss physician Adrien Sechehaye uses the remedy in tuberculosis patients."], ["Late 20th century", "A standardized extract is developed in Germany and studied for acute bronchitis."], ["2013", "A Cochrane review finds low-quality evidence of possible benefit in acute bronchitis."]], "cell": "Pelargonium sidoides root contains highly oxygenated 7-hydroxycoumarins such as umckalin, along with gallic acid and its esters, catechins and proanthocyanidin tannins. These coumarins differ structurally from 4-hydroxycoumarins like warfarin and are not expected to block vitamin K recycling, but because the plant contains coumarins, anticoagulant use is commonly listed as a theoretical caution. In laboratory studies, the extract stimulates macrophages to release interferons, tumor necrosis factor and nitric oxide, increases the beat frequency of cilia on airway epithelial cells, and reduces the ability of bacteria such as group A streptococci to stick to human cells, which would make them easier to clear. Antiviral activity has been reported against several respiratory viruses in cell cultures. These mechanisms fit the marketing for bronchitis, but the clinical effect sizes seen in trials are modest and the trials largely tested one product.", "body": [["Airways", "Laboratory effects on cilia and bacterial adhesion; trials suggest modest shortening of acute bronchitis symptoms."], ["Immune system", "Stimulation of innate immune cells and interferon release in laboratory studies."], ["Gastrointestinal tract", "Stomach upset, nausea and diarrhea are the most common side effects."], ["Liver", "Rare reports of liver injury, with causality uncertain."], ["Skin", "Rash and hives as part of allergic reactions."]], "journey": "Pharmacokinetic studies of pelargonium extract in humans are limited. The coumarins and smaller phenolic compounds are presumed to be absorbed from the gut and conjugated in the liver like other plant phenolics, while the larger tannins and proanthocyanidins are poorly absorbed and are broken down by gut bacteria in the colon. How long effects last and how the compounds are eliminated has not been well defined. Because most clinical research used a specific ethanol-based extract, results cannot be assumed to apply to other root preparations, which may have different chemical profiles.", "sidefx": "In trials, side effects have been mostly gastrointestinal, such as stomach upset, nausea and diarrhea, along with rash and other allergic reactions. Reports of liver injury in Germany led to regulatory review, but later causality analyses found the evidence linking most cases to pelargonium weak, so liver injury remains a possible but uncertain risk. Rare reports of nosebleeds or gum bleeding have led to cautions with anticoagulants. Liquid products may contain alcohol, which matters for children, people in recovery and people taking disulfiram or metronidazole.", "fun": "The geraniums sold for windowsills and balconies are mostly Pelargonium species, garden cousins of this medicinal root, while true Geranium plants belong to a different genus in the same family.", "src": ["Timmer et al. 2013, Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews, Pelargonium sidoides extract for acute respiratory tract infections", "European Medicines Agency: herbal monograph on Pelargonium sidoides radix", "Brendler and van Wyk 2008, Journal of Ethnopharmacology, a historical, scientific and commercial perspective on Pelargonium sidoides", "Teschke et al. 2012, Phytomedicine, causality assessment of suspected Pelargonium sidoides hepatotoxicity"]}, "oregano-oil": {"origin": "Oregano, Origanum vulgare, is a perennial herb of the mint family native to the Mediterranean region and western and central Asia. The name comes from Greek words often translated as joy of the mountain. Ancient Greek and Roman writers, including Dioscorides, described oregano and related plants for wounds, stomach complaints and poisonings, and the herb has been a culinary staple across the Mediterranean for millennia. Steam distillation of aromatic plants spread through the medieval Islamic world and later Europe, producing essential oils, and by the nineteenth century thymol, isolated from thyme, was used in medicine and dentistry as an antiseptic and against hookworm. Modern oil of oregano products come from oregano and related species with high carvacrol content. Their popularity grew in North America in the late twentieth century as a natural alternative to antibiotics, promoted for colds, gut infections and yeast. Laboratory studies show genuine antimicrobial activity of carvacrol and thymol, and the food industry has explored them as preservatives, but human clinical trials of oral oregano oil remain very small and preliminary.", "timeline": [["Ancient period", "Greek and Roman writers describe oregano for wounds and digestive complaints."], ["Medieval period", "Steam distillation of aromatic plants spreads, enabling essential oil production."], ["19th century", "Thymol, a related phenol, is used in medicine as an antiseptic and anthelmintic."], ["Late 20th century", "Oil of oregano softgels and drops become popular as natural antimicrobials."], ["2000s to present", "Laboratory and food-science research explores carvacrol as an antimicrobial and preservative."]], "cell": "Carvacrol and thymol are isomeric phenolic monoterpenes. Their lipophilic ring lets them insert into microbial cell membranes, where the phenolic hydroxyl group disturbs membrane structure, increases permeability, dissipates the proton gradient and causes leakage of ions and ATP, eventually killing bacteria and fungi in laboratory cultures. Carvacrol also inhibits biofilm formation in some laboratory models. Beyond microbes, these phenols show antioxidant activity, inhibit inflammatory enzymes and cytokines, activate the TRPA1 and TRPV3 ion channels involved in irritation and warmth sensations, and inhibit platelet aggregation in vitro. The same membrane activity explains why concentrated oil irritates human skin and mucous membranes. In the body, concentrations reached after swallowing a softgel are much lower than those used in culture dishes and are rapidly conjugated, which is one reason laboratory activity has not translated into proven clinical benefit.", "body": [["Gastrointestinal tract", "Stomach upset and heartburn; promoted for gut infections without good human evidence."], ["Mouth and throat", "Concentrated oil causes burning and irritation."], ["Skin", "Undiluted oil can cause irritation, chemical burns and allergic reactions."], ["Blood", "Laboratory antiplatelet effects raise a theoretical bleeding concern with anticoagulants."], ["Lungs", "Swallowed essential oils can be aspirated, especially by children."]], "journey": "Carvacrol and thymol are absorbed from the upper gut after swallowing and are rapidly conjugated in the gut wall and liver to glucuronides and sulfates, which are excreted mainly in the urine within about a day. Very little free compound circulates. Studies of thymol from thyme products in humans found that the free compound was not detectable in plasma and that sulfate conjugates predominated. Through the skin, essential oil components can be absorbed to some degree, especially when skin is damaged. Pharmacokinetic data specific to commercial oregano oil products are limited, and carvacrol content varies widely.", "sidefx": "Common effects are stomach upset, heartburn, burping, unpleasant taste and burning in the mouth or throat. Undiluted oil on the skin can cause irritation, redness and chemical burns, and allergic reactions are possible, especially in people allergic to other mint-family plants. Laboratory antiplatelet effects raise a theoretical bleeding concern with anticoagulants. A broader concern is substitution: relying on oregano oil for a serious bacterial infection can delay effective treatment. As with all essential oils, accidental ingestion by children can cause choking, aspiration and lung injury.", "fun": "Carvacrol is also a main component of thyme and savory oils, and the food industry has studied it as a natural preservative that could replace synthetic antimicrobials in some products.", "src": ["Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center About Herbs: Oregano", "NIH NCCIH: Aromatherapy", "Nostro and Papalia 2012, Recent Patents on Anti-Infective Drug Discovery, antimicrobial activity of carvacrol", "Kohlert et al. 2002, Planta Medica, thymol bioavailability and pharmacokinetics in humans"]}, "eucalyptus-oil": {"origin": "Eucalyptus trees are native to Australia, where Aboriginal peoples used the leaves in smoke, steam and infusions for fevers, wounds and respiratory complaints long before European arrival. Commercial distillation of eucalyptus oil began in the colony of Victoria in the mid-nineteenth century, and Joseph Bosisto, a Melbourne pharmacist, is credited with building the industry with encouragement from the botanist Ferdinand von Mueller in the 1850s. The main component, 1,8-cineole, also called eucalyptol, was named in the 1870s after French chemist Francois Stanislas Cloez studied the oil. Eucalyptus oil entered pharmacopoeias as an inhalant and antiseptic, and it became a standard ingredient of chest rubs, lozenges and mouthwashes. Australian poison centers and pediatric toxicologists later documented a darker side: eucalyptus oil was a common cause of serious childhood poisoning, especially from vaporizer liquids, with children becoming drowsy, vomiting and sometimes seizing after swallowing small amounts. Child-resistant packaging and warnings followed in Australia, and the growing popularity of essential oils has kept these exposures in poison center data worldwide.", "timeline": [["Before European colonization", "Aboriginal Australians use eucalyptus leaves for fevers, wounds and respiratory illness."], ["1850s", "Commercial distillation of eucalyptus oil begins in Victoria, Australia, associated with Joseph Bosisto."], ["1870", "The main component is named eucalyptol following work by Francois Stanislas Cloez."], ["20th century", "Eucalyptus oil becomes a standard ingredient in chest rubs, lozenges and mouthwashes."], ["Late 20th century", "Australian studies document eucalyptus oil as a significant cause of pediatric poisoning."]], "cell": "1,8-cineole is a bicyclic monoterpene ether, small and highly lipophilic, which is why it passes quickly through membranes, the gut wall and the blood-brain barrier. Topically and by inhalation it activates TRPM8, the cold-sensing ion channel on sensory nerve endings that is also activated by menthol, producing a cooling sensation and a feeling of easier nasal airflow even though airflow measurements may not change much. It also acts on other TRP channels. In laboratory studies, 1,8-cineole reduces production of inflammatory mediators such as tumor necrosis factor and interleukins by monocytes, which supported European research in chronic bronchitis and sinusitis. In poisoning, the mechanism of CNS depression and seizures is not fully defined; like other lipophilic terpenes and camphor, cineole is thought to disturb neuronal membrane function and inhibitory signaling. The oil is also an irritant to gut mucosa, and if aspirated it spreads through the airways, damaging surfactant and lung tissue and causing chemical pneumonitis.", "body": [["Brain", "Drowsiness, ataxia, slurred speech, coma and seizures after ingestion."], ["Lungs", "Aspiration pneumonitis after coughing or vomiting; respiratory depression in severe poisoning; strong vapors can trigger bronchospasm in asthma."], ["Gastrointestinal tract", "Burning in the mouth and throat, nausea, vomiting and abdominal pain."], ["Nose and airway nerves", "Cooling sensation and perceived decongestion through TRPM8."], ["Skin", "Irritation and contact dermatitis with undiluted oil."]], "journey": "After swallowing, eucalyptus oil is absorbed rapidly from the stomach and small intestine, and toxic effects can begin within about 30 minutes, often within a few hours at most. Its lipophilicity allows rapid distribution into the brain and fat. 1,8-cineole is metabolized in the liver, mainly by CYP3A4, to hydroxycineole metabolites that are conjugated and excreted in the urine, and some is exhaled, giving the breath a eucalyptus odor that can be a clue. By inhalation, small amounts are absorbed through the airways. Through the skin, absorption is modest with intact skin but greater with prolonged or occlusive use. Most children who receive supportive care recover within about a day.", "sidefx": "With normal topical or inhaled use, effects are usually limited to skin irritation, eye irritation and, in sensitive people, bronchospasm. Ingestion is the main hazard: burning in the mouth, vomiting, abdominal pain, drowsiness, ataxia, small pupils, slurred speech and, in more serious cases, coma, seizures and respiratory depression. Coughing or choking suggests aspiration, which can cause chemical pneumonitis. There is no antidote, and treatment is supportive with airway management, oxygen, benzodiazepines for seizures and poison center guidance. Inducing vomiting is avoided. Applying eucalyptus or other strong essential oils to the face of infants is discouraged because of airway reactions.", "fun": "Eucalyptus oil leaves the breath smelling like eucalyptus after ingestion, a clue that has helped clinicians identify the poison in children who could not say what they swallowed.", "src": ["NIH NCCIH: Eucalyptus", "Tibballs 1995, Medical Journal of Australia, clinical effects and management of eucalyptus oil ingestion in infants and young children", "America's Poison Centers: National Poison Data System annual reports", "StatPearls: Essential Oil Toxicity"]}, "tea-tree-oil": {"origin": "Melaleuca alternifolia is a small tree of the swampy coastal areas of northern New South Wales, Australia. The Bundjalung people traditionally used crushed leaves for coughs, colds and wounds. The name tea tree is usually traced to accounts that early European visitors, including members of James Cook's voyages, brewed a tea from leaves of related plants, though the modern oil comes specifically from M. alternifolia. In the 1920s the Australian chemist Arthur Penfold reported that the oil had antiseptic activity stronger than the carbolic acid used at the time, which launched a commercial industry. Tea tree oil was used in first aid kits and was reportedly supplied to Australian soldiers during the Second World War. Demand fell with the arrival of antibiotics but rose again from the 1970s with natural skin care, and plantation production expanded. Research then defined both its modest topical benefits for acne and fungal skin infections and its hazards: allergic contact dermatitis, especially from oxidized oil, poisoning when swallowed by children, toxicity in pets, and a debated set of case reports linking lavender and tea tree products to breast enlargement in prepubertal boys.", "timeline": [["Traditional use", "Bundjalung people of New South Wales use crushed leaves for wounds and respiratory complaints."], ["1920s", "Arthur Penfold reports strong antiseptic activity of tea tree oil, prompting commercial production."], ["1940s", "Tea tree oil is used in Australian first aid supplies during the Second World War."], ["1970s onward", "Plantation production expands as natural skin care products grow in popularity."], ["2007", "A New England Journal of Medicine report links lavender and tea tree oil products to prepubertal gynecomastia in three boys."]], "cell": "Tea tree oil is a mixture of about one hundred compounds, mainly monoterpenes. The international standard for the oil sets ranges for its components, with terpinen-4-ol as the major one and 1,8-cineole kept low because it irritates skin. Terpinen-4-ol and related terpenes partition into microbial cell membranes, increase permeability, cause leakage of potassium ions and other contents, and inhibit respiration, killing bacteria including Staphylococcus aureus and Cutibacterium acnes and fungi such as Trichophyton and Candida in laboratory tests. Terpinen-4-ol also reduces production of inflammatory mediators by activated immune cells. When the oil is exposed to light, air and heat, terpenes oxidize to peroxides, epoxides and other products such as ascaridole that are stronger skin sensitizers, explaining why aged oil causes more allergic contact dermatitis. In a cell study accompanying the gynecomastia report, lavender and tea tree oils showed weak estrogenic and antiandrogenic activity, but whether typical product use affects hormones in children remains debated. Swallowed terpenes enter the brain readily and cause CNS depression.", "body": [["Skin", "Modest benefit for mild acne and athlete's foot in small trials; irritation and allergic contact dermatitis are common adverse effects."], ["Brain", "Drowsiness, confusion, ataxia and coma after ingestion."], ["Lungs", "Aspiration pneumonitis risk if swallowed oil is coughed or vomited into the airways."], ["Gastrointestinal tract", "Vomiting and diarrhea after ingestion."], ["Endocrine system", "Case reports of prepubertal breast enlargement with lavender and tea tree products; causality debated."]], "journey": "On intact skin, tea tree oil components evaporate quickly and only a small fraction penetrates deeper layers, with absorption greater through broken or occluded skin. After ingestion, the lipophilic terpenes are rapidly absorbed from the gut, distribute into fatty tissues and the brain, and produce symptoms often within about half an hour to a few hours. They are metabolized in the liver by oxidation and conjugation and excreted mainly in the urine, with some exhaled. Human pharmacokinetic data after ingestion come mostly from poisoning case reports, so half-life and peak timing are not well defined. Symptoms after accidental ingestion usually resolve within a day or two with supportive care.", "sidefx": "Topical use commonly causes dryness, itching, stinging and redness. Allergic contact dermatitis is the most important topical adverse effect and is more likely with oxidized oil, undiluted oil and damaged skin. Ingestion causes drowsiness, confusion, ataxia, weakness, vomiting and diarrhea, and in serious cases coma and respiratory depression, mainly in young children; there is no antidote, and vomiting is not induced. Cats and dogs are particularly vulnerable to concentrated oil applied to their fur, developing tremors, weakness and collapse. Case reports of prepubertal gynecomastia that resolved after stopping lavender and tea tree products remain debated.", "fun": "Tea tree oil sold under the international standard must contain at least a set minimum of terpinen-4-ol and no more than a set maximum of 1,8-cineole, so the standard itself is built around one helpful compound and one irritating one.", "src": ["NIH NCCIH: Tea Tree Oil", "Carson, Hammer and Riley 2006, Clinical Microbiology Reviews, Melaleuca alternifolia (tea tree) oil: a review of antimicrobial and other medicinal properties", "Henley et al. 2007, New England Journal of Medicine, prepubertal gynecomastia linked to lavender and tea tree oils", "StatPearls: Essential Oil Toxicity"]}, "propolis": {"origin": "Honeybees collect resins from the buds and bark of trees such as poplars, birches and conifers and mix them with wax and enzymes to make propolis, which they use to seal cracks, reinforce comb and coat the inside of the hive, and to embalm intruders too large to remove. The word comes from Greek, roughly meaning in front of the city, describing propolis built up at hive entrances. Ancient Egyptians, Greeks and Romans used propolis in medicines and embalming, and later European folk medicine used it for wounds. Propolis was reportedly applied to wounds in parts of Europe as late as the early twentieth century. Interest revived in Eastern Europe and later in Brazil and Japan, where Brazilian green propolis, derived from the plant Baccharis dracunculifolia, became a major commercial product. Chemists found that propolis composition depends almost entirely on local plants, so European poplar propolis and Brazilian green propolis differ markedly. Dermatologists meanwhile recognized propolis as an important contact allergen, first among beekeepers and later among users of natural cosmetics and lip products.", "timeline": [["Ancient period", "Egyptians, Greeks and Romans use propolis in medicine and embalming."], ["Medieval to early modern Europe", "Propolis is used in folk wound treatments."], ["20th century", "Dermatologists describe propolis contact allergy in beekeepers."], ["1980s to 1990s", "Brazilian green propolis develops as a major export, especially to Japan."], ["2000s to present", "Small trials study topical propolis for cold sores and mouth rinses for mucositis."]], "cell": "Propolis is a chemically variable mixture of plant resins, waxes, essential oils and pollen. In temperate poplar-type propolis, flavonoid aglycones such as pinocembrin, galangin and chrysin and phenolic acid esters such as caffeic acid phenethyl ester dominate. In laboratory studies these compounds damage bacterial membranes, inhibit bacterial and viral enzymes, block herpes simplex virus attachment, scavenge free radicals and reduce inflammatory signaling; CAPE in particular inhibits NF-kappa-B activation. Brazilian green propolis instead contains prenylated cinnamic acids such as artepillin C. The same caffeic acid esters, especially 3-methyl-2-butenyl caffeate and related compounds, are strong contact allergens that bind skin proteins and trigger a T cell-mediated delayed allergy. They cross-react with balsam of Peru, poplar bud resin and fragrance components, which explains why people allergic to those materials often react to propolis. Laboratory antiplatelet effects underlie theoretical bleeding concerns.", "body": [["Skin", "Allergic contact dermatitis is common, including around the lips with balms and on hands of beekeepers."], ["Mouth and throat", "Marketed for sore throat and mouth sores; lozenges and sprays can cause ulcers and swelling."], ["Immune system", "Anti-inflammatory and antimicrobial effects in laboratory studies; rare severe allergic reactions."], ["Lungs", "Asthma attacks can occur as part of allergic reactions."], ["Blood", "Laboratory antiplatelet effects raise a theoretical bleeding concern."]], "journey": "Human pharmacokinetic data for propolis products are limited. Flavonoids such as pinocembrin and galangin and phenolic acids are absorbed in modest amounts from the gut and are rapidly conjugated in the intestinal wall and liver to glucuronides and sulfates, which are excreted in urine and bile. Esters such as CAPE are hydrolyzed by esterases in blood and tissues, releasing caffeic acid. Unabsorbed compounds reach the colon, where bacteria transform them. Topical products act mainly at the application site, where the allergens contact the skin. Because composition differs so much between regions and products, results from one propolis cannot be assumed for another.", "sidefx": "The leading adverse effect is allergic contact dermatitis, with itching, redness, swelling and blistering at the site of contact; cheilitis around the lips and hand eczema in beekeepers are classic examples. Oral products can cause mouth irritation, ulcers and lip swelling. Rarely, people have developed asthma attacks or anaphylaxis, mostly those with other bee product allergies. Laboratory antiplatelet effects raise a theoretical concern with anticoagulants and before surgery. Alcohol-based tinctures matter for children, people in recovery and people taking disulfiram or metronidazole.", "fun": "When a mouse or other intruder dies inside a hive and is too heavy to remove, bees can coat it in propolis, sealing it off and slowing decay, an observation that echoes the ancient use of propolis in embalming.", "src": ["Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center About Herbs: Propolis", "de Groot 2013, Dermatitis, propolis review of contact allergy and adverse effects", "Bankova and colleagues, review articles on the chemical diversity of propolis by plant source", "NIH NCCIH: Using Dietary Supplements Wisely"]}, "manuka-honey": {"origin": "Honey is one of the oldest wound treatments in recorded medicine, appearing in ancient Egyptian medical papyri and in Greek and Roman texts, and it fell out of mainstream use with the arrival of antibiotics. Manuka honey comes from bees foraging on Leptospermum scoparium, a shrub native to New Zealand and southeastern Australia that Maori communities used medicinally. In the 1980s and 1990s, Peter Molan, a biochemist at the University of Waikato in New Zealand, showed that manuka honey kept strong antibacterial activity even when the hydrogen peroxide activity of ordinary honey was removed, and the term Unique Manuka Factor was coined for this unexplained activity. In 2008 research groups, including Thomas Henle's group in Dresden, identified methylglyoxal as the main compound responsible, and New Zealand researchers later showed that it forms in the honey from dihydroxyacetone in manuka nectar. Rising antibiotic resistance renewed interest in honey for wounds, and sterile medical-grade manuka honey dressings received FDA clearance as medical devices in 2007. Booming demand also led to authenticity problems, prompting New Zealand to set a scientific definition of manuka honey.", "timeline": [["Ancient period", "Honey is used on wounds in Egyptian, Greek and Roman medicine."], ["1980s to 1990s", "Peter Molan describes non-peroxide antibacterial activity in manuka honey."], ["2007", "FDA clears the first medical-grade manuka honey wound dressings as devices."], ["2008", "Methylglyoxal is identified as the main source of manuka honey's non-peroxide activity."], ["2010s", "New Zealand sets a scientific definition for manuka honey to address mislabeling."]], "cell": "Honey kills microbes through several overlapping mechanisms. It is a supersaturated sugar solution with very low water activity, so it pulls water out of bacterial cells by osmosis. It is acidic. Most honeys also contain glucose oxidase, a bee enzyme that slowly produces hydrogen peroxide when honey is diluted by wound fluid. Manuka honey adds a fourth mechanism: methylglyoxal, a reactive dicarbonyl compound formed non-enzymatically from dihydroxyacetone present in manuka nectar as the honey matures. Methylglyoxal reacts with arginine and lysine residues on proteins and with nucleic acids, damaging bacterial structures such as fimbriae and flagella and disrupting cell division. In laboratory studies manuka honey is active against antibiotic-resistant organisms such as MRSA and Pseudomonas and can disrupt biofilms. Honey also promotes moist wound healing and helps remove dead tissue through osmotic effects. Methylglyoxal is also the compound used to grade manuka honey on product labels.", "body": [["Skin and wounds", "Medical-grade dressings provide antibacterial, moist wound environment and debriding effects; stinging is common."], ["Blood glucose", "Oral honey is mostly glucose and fructose and raises blood sugar."], ["Infant gut", "Botulinum spores in honey can colonize the immature infant gut and produce toxin."], ["Throat", "Marketed for sore throat and cough; honey's soothing effect is shared by ordinary honey."], ["Immune system", "Allergic reactions are possible in people allergic to bee products or pollen."]], "journey": "Topically, honey acts where it is applied and is diluted by wound fluid, which releases hydrogen peroxide from glucose oxidase while methylglyoxal acts directly on bacteria. Very little is thought to be absorbed into the bloodstream from dressings. When eaten, honey's sugars are digested and absorbed like other sugars, raising blood glucose. Dietary methylglyoxal is largely broken down in the stomach and small intestine and by the body's glyoxalase system, so eating manuka honey is not expected to deliver antibacterial levels to tissues. In infants, Clostridium botulinum spores in honey can survive the stomach, germinate in the immature gut and produce botulinum toxin, which is absorbed and blocks acetylcholine release at nerve endings.", "sidefx": "On wounds, honey dressings commonly cause stinging or pain on application, and excess moisture can macerate surrounding skin. Allergic reactions are possible, especially in people allergic to bee products or certain pollens. Eaten, honey raises blood glucose, which matters for people with diabetes. The most serious risk is infant botulism, with constipation, poor feeding, a weak cry, drooping eyelids and floppiness developing over days in infants under 12 months who are given honey; it is treated in hospital with supportive care and botulism immune globulin. Food-grade honey is not sterile and is not a substitute for medical-grade dressings.", "fun": "Methylglyoxal is not in manuka nectar itself; the nectar contains dihydroxyacetone, which slowly converts to methylglyoxal as the honey sits, so the antibacterial activity of manuka honey increases with storage for a time.", "src": ["Jull et al. 2015, Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews, Honey as a topical treatment for wounds", "Mavric et al. 2008, Molecular Nutrition and Food Research, identification of methylglyoxal as the dominating antibacterial constituent of manuka honey", "Adams et al. 2009, Carbohydrate Research, dihydroxyacetone in manuka nectar as the precursor of methylglyoxal", "CDC: Infant botulism"]}, "neem": {"origin": "Azadirachta indica, the neem tree, is native to the Indian subcontinent and is now grown widely across tropical Asia and Africa. In India it is called the village pharmacy: twigs are used as chew sticks for cleaning teeth, leaves are used for skin conditions and fevers, the bark for malaria, and the seed oil, also called margosa oil, for skin complaints, lice and as a lamp oil. Ayurvedic and Unani texts describe it extensively. Western science took notice in the twentieth century when entomologists observed that locusts avoided neem during swarms, which led to the discovery of azadirachtin, a complex limonoid that disrupts insect feeding and molting. Azadirachtin became the basis of registered biopesticides, and neem was the subject of a well-known patent dispute in the 1990s and 2000s in which a European patent on a neem fungicide was challenged as traditional knowledge. Medical research also documented harm. In 1981 Malaysian physicians reported that infants given margosa oil as a traditional remedy developed a Reye-like toxic encephalopathy, and later reports from India described similar childhood poisonings, some fatal. Animal research on neem as a contraceptive confirmed antifertility effects, which is why neem is avoided in pregnancy.", "timeline": [["Ancient period", "Neem leaves, bark, twigs and seed oil are used in Ayurvedic and Unani medicine."], ["Mid-20th century", "Insect avoidance of neem leads to research on its pesticidal compounds."], ["1968", "Azadirachtin is isolated from neem seeds as an insect antifeedant."], ["1981", "Malaysian physicians report margosa (neem) oil poisoning as a cause of Reye syndrome in infants."], ["1990s to 2000s", "A European patent on a neem fungicide is challenged and revoked as traditional knowledge."]], "cell": "Neem contains more than one hundred limonoids, highly oxygenated triterpenoids. Azadirachtin, the best known, mimics and disrupts the insect molting hormone ecdysone and acts on insect taste receptors, causing insects to stop feeding and fail to molt; this is the basis of neem pesticides and has little relevance to mammals at pesticide exposure levels. Leaf limonoids such as nimbin and nimbolide show antimicrobial, anti-inflammatory and anticancer activities in laboratory studies, and leaf extracts lower blood glucose in diabetic animals. The mechanism of neem oil toxicity in children is not fully defined. The clinical and pathological picture of vomiting, encephalopathy, metabolic acidosis and fatty change in the liver resembles Reye syndrome, which is associated with mitochondrial injury, and animal studies of neem oil have reported mitochondrial dysfunction and impaired fatty acid oxidation. Infants may be particularly vulnerable because of immature detoxification pathways. Antifertility effects in animals include reduced sperm function and pregnancy loss, and neem oil was studied as a vaginal contraceptive.", "body": [["Brain", "Drowsiness, seizures, coma and toxic encephalopathy in children after oral neem oil."], ["Liver", "Fatty change resembling Reye syndrome in poisoned children; rare liver injury in adults."], ["Metabolism", "Metabolic acidosis in poisoned children; possible glucose lowering with diabetes medicines."], ["Reproductive system", "Antifertility and pregnancy loss effects in animal studies; traditional abortifacient use."], ["Skin and mouth", "Used topically and in dental products; irritation and allergic reactions are possible."]], "journey": "Human pharmacokinetic data for neem constituents are very limited. Neem oil is a fatty oil containing lipophilic limonoids and other compounds, which are presumed to be absorbed from the gut along with dietary fat. In poisoning reports, infants developed vomiting and drowsiness within hours of ingestion, with seizures and coma following in severe cases, suggesting rapid absorption of the toxic components. How neem compounds are metabolized and eliminated in humans has not been well characterized. Topical products deliver little systemic exposure on intact skin. Azadirachtin in pesticides breaks down quickly in sunlight and the environment.", "sidefx": "Adults using neem products orally may have stomach upset and unpleasant taste, and rare cases of liver injury have been reported. Topical products can irritate skin or cause allergic dermatitis. The most serious harm is in infants and young children given neem oil by mouth: vomiting, drowsiness, rapid breathing from metabolic acidosis, seizures, coma and a Reye-like syndrome with liver fatty change, which has been fatal in some cases. There is no antidote, and care is supportive. Neem is avoided in pregnancy and by people trying to conceive because of antifertility and pregnancy loss effects in animals. It may add to the glucose-lowering effects of diabetes medicines.", "fun": "Neem twigs have been used as natural toothbrushes for centuries in India, and they are still sold as chew sticks in some markets today.", "src": ["Sinniah and Baskaran 1981, Lancet, Margosa oil poisoning as a cause of Reye's syndrome", "Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center About Herbs: Neem", "National Research Council 1992, Neem: A Tree for Solving Global Problems", "US EPA: azadirachtin and neem oil biopesticide information"]}, "bitter-melon": {"origin": "Momordica charantia is a tropical vine in the gourd family, probably domesticated in South or Southeast Asia, and its warty, intensely bitter fruit is eaten as a vegetable across India, China, Southeast Asia, Africa, the Caribbean and Latin America. In Ayurveda it is karela, used for diabetes, skin disease and worms; in traditional Chinese medicine it is ku gua, used for heat-related conditions; and in the Caribbean it is known as cerasee, used as a bitter tea. Its use for sugar diabetes is one of the most widespread traditional diabetes remedies in the world, and immigrant communities often continue the practice. Laboratory research from the mid-twentieth century identified several glucose-lowering candidates, including a steroidal glycoside mixture named charantin and an insulin-like peptide called polypeptide-p. Animal studies were consistently positive. Human trials, however, have been small, short and inconsistent, and Cochrane reviewers concluded that there is not enough evidence to support bitter melon for type 2 diabetes. Safety research added concerns about hypoglycemia with diabetes medicines, hemolysis in G6PD deficiency from seed compounds, and pregnancy loss in animal studies.", "timeline": [["Ancient period", "Bitter melon is cultivated and used as food and medicine in South and Southeast Asia."], ["Mid-20th century", "Charantin and other glucose-lowering compounds are reported from the fruit."], ["1980s", "An insulin-like polypeptide (polypeptide-p) from the plant is studied."], ["2000s", "AMPK activation by cucurbitane triterpenoids is described in laboratory studies."], ["2012", "A Cochrane review finds insufficient evidence for bitter melon in type 2 diabetes."]], "cell": "Bitter melon contains several classes of compounds that may lower blood glucose. Cucurbitane-type triterpenoids, the compounds responsible for much of the bitter taste, activate AMP-activated protein kinase in muscle and fat cells and increase movement of GLUT4 glucose transporters to the cell surface, similar in direction to an effect of metformin. The charantin mixture lowers glucose in animals. Polypeptide-p is a plant peptide with insulin-like activity that was studied by injection in early work; how much survives digestion is doubtful. Extracts also inhibit intestinal glucose absorption and liver glucose production in animals. The seeds contain vicine, a pyrimidine glycoside also found in fava beans, which is converted to divicine and generates oxidative stress that red blood cells lacking G6PD cannot handle, leading to hemolysis. Seeds also contain ribosome-inactivating proteins called momorcharins, which induced pregnancy loss in animal studies and underlie the traditional abortifacient reputation.", "body": [["Pancreas, muscle and liver", "Glucose-lowering effects through insulin-like and AMPK pathways in laboratory and animal studies."], ["Blood", "Hemolysis risk in G6PD deficiency from seed vicine."], ["Reproductive system", "Pregnancy loss and antifertility effects in animal studies."], ["Gastrointestinal tract", "Abdominal pain, diarrhea and bitter taste."], ["Brain", "Confusion, sweating and shakiness are signs of hypoglycemia with combined use of diabetes medicines."]], "journey": "Pharmacokinetic data for bitter melon compounds in humans are lacking. The triterpenoids are presumed to be partly absorbed from the gut and metabolized in the liver, while peptides such as polypeptide-p are likely largely digested. Vicine from seeds is hydrolyzed in the gut, partly by bacteria, to divicine, which is absorbed and acts on red blood cells. The time course of glucose lowering in people has not been well defined, and products differ widely in what part of the plant they contain and how concentrated they are, so effects are hard to predict from one product to another.", "sidefx": "Common effects are stomach pain, diarrhea, nausea and a strongly bitter taste. The most important risk is hypoglycemia when bitter melon is combined with insulin or oral diabetes medicines, presenting with shakiness, sweating, confusion and, if severe, seizures or loss of consciousness; hypoglycemic episodes in children have also been reported. In people with G6PD deficiency, seeds can trigger hemolytic anemia with fatigue, pallor and dark urine. Because of animal pregnancy loss and antifertility data, bitter melon is avoided in pregnancy and by people trying to conceive.", "fun": "Vicine, the compound in bitter melon seeds that can harm people with G6PD deficiency, is the same compound that causes favism, the hemolytic reaction to eating fava beans.", "src": ["Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center About Herbs: Bitter Melon", "Ooi et al. 2012, Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews, Momordica charantia for type 2 diabetes mellitus", "Tan et al. 2008, Chemistry and Biology, cucurbitane triterpenoids from Momordica charantia activate AMPK", "NIH NCCIH: Diabetes and Dietary Supplements"]}, "gymnema": {"origin": "Gymnema sylvestre is a woody climbing plant of the forests of central and southern India, tropical Africa and Australia, belonging to the milkweed subfamily of the dogbane family. In Ayurveda it has been used for centuries for madhumeha, the honey urine disease that corresponds to diabetes, and its Hindi name gurmar means destroyer of sugar. The name reflects a striking effect that anyone can notice: after chewing the leaves, sugar tastes like sand for a while. The effect was described in the scientific literature in the late nineteenth century, and chemists later characterized the active compounds, a family of triterpene saponins named gymnemic acids. In the twentieth century, Indian researchers studied gymnema extracts in diabetic animals and in small groups of people with diabetes, reporting lower glucose and suggesting effects on pancreatic beta cells. These studies made gymnema a staple of blood sugar supplements. The trials, however, were small, often uncontrolled and decades old, and modern well-designed studies are scarce. LiverTox describes a case report of liver injury attributed to gymnema.", "timeline": [["Ancient period", "Gymnema is used in Ayurveda for diabetes under the name gurmar."], ["Late 19th century", "The sweet-blocking effect is described in the scientific literature and gymnemic acid is named as its active principle."], ["20th century", "Gymnemic acids are characterized as a family of triterpene saponins."], ["1990", "Small Indian studies report glucose lowering with a gymnema extract in people with diabetes."], ["2010s", "Gymnemic acids are shown to act on the human sweet taste receptor T1R2/T1R3."]], "cell": "Gymnemic acids are triterpene glycosides. On the tongue, they bind the transmembrane domain of the T1R3 subunit of the heterodimeric sweet taste receptor T1R2/T1R3, preventing sugars and many artificial sweeteners from activating it, so sweet taste is suppressed until the compound washes off and receptors recover. Gurmarin, a small peptide from the same plant, blocks sweet taste in rodents but has little effect in humans. Because similar sweet receptors and glucose transporters exist in the intestine, gymnemic acids have been proposed to reduce glucose absorption in the gut. Animal studies also report increased insulin secretion, increased permeability of beta cells and, in some reports, regeneration of beta cells in diabetic rats, but these findings are not confirmed in humans. The glucose-lowering actions, if real, explain the interaction with insulin and sulfonylureas.", "body": [["Tongue", "Temporary loss of sweet taste through sweet receptor blockade."], ["Gut", "Proposed reduction of glucose absorption."], ["Pancreas", "Animal studies suggest increased insulin secretion; human data limited."], ["Blood glucose", "Possible modest lowering that can add to diabetes medicines."], ["Liver", "Rare case report of drug-induced liver injury."]], "journey": "The sweet-blocking effect occurs locally on the tongue within moments of contact and fades as saliva clears the compounds, generally within an hour or so. Systemic pharmacokinetics of gymnemic acids in humans have not been well defined; as large saponins, they are likely poorly absorbed, and much of their action may take place in the gut lumen. Metabolism and excretion in people are not characterized. Product potency depends on gymnemic acid content, which varies between extracts, and many products are combined with other glucose-lowering ingredients, making effects difficult to attribute to gymnema alone.", "sidefx": "Gymnema is usually well tolerated in short trials, with occasional nausea and stomach upset, plus the temporary loss of sweet taste. The main concern is hypoglycemia when combined with insulin, sulfonylureas or other glucose-lowering drugs or herbs, with shakiness, sweating, confusion and, if severe, seizures or loss of consciousness. A case report described drug-induced liver injury attributed to gymnema, and LiverTox treats the hepatotoxicity evidence as limited. Allergic reactions are possible. Safety in pregnancy and breastfeeding is unknown.", "fun": "After chewing gymnema leaves, a spoonful of sugar can taste like plain sand, while salty, sour and bitter tastes are left almost unchanged.", "src": ["LiverTox (NIH): Gymnema sylvestre", "Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center About Herbs: Gymnema", "Sanematsu et al. 2014, Journal of Biological Chemistry, gymnemic acids interact with the transmembrane domain of human T1R3", "NIH NCCIH: Diabetes and Dietary Supplements"]}, "cinnamon-cassia": {"origin": "Cinnamon is the inner bark of several trees in the genus Cinnamomum. Ceylon or true cinnamon, Cinnamomum verum, comes mainly from Sri Lanka, while cassia, from C. cassia and related species, comes from China, Indonesia and Vietnam and is the type most often sold as ground cinnamon in the United States. Cinnamon was among the most prized spices of the ancient world, mentioned in Egyptian, biblical and Greek sources, and the search for it shaped Portuguese and Dutch colonial ventures in Sri Lanka. In Chinese medicine, cassia bark, rou gui, is a warming herb. Modern interest in cinnamon for diabetes began with laboratory findings in the late 1990s and early 2000s that cinnamon extracts enhanced insulin action in cells, followed by a 2003 trial in Pakistan reporting lower glucose and lipids in people with type 2 diabetes. Later trials and meta-analyses were mixed. A safety issue emerged from food chemistry: cassia contains far more coumarin than Ceylon cinnamon, and coumarin can injure the liver in sensitive people. In 2006, German authorities warned about coumarin in cassia-flavored foods, and European food safety assessments later set tolerable coumarin intakes, while the US had already prohibited coumarin as a direct food additive in the 1950s.", "timeline": [["Ancient period", "Cinnamon is traded as a luxury spice in Egypt, the Middle East and Mediterranean."], ["1950s", "The US prohibits coumarin as a direct food additive because of liver toxicity in animal studies."], ["2003", "A trial in Pakistan reports that cinnamon lowered glucose and lipids in type 2 diabetes."], ["2006", "German food authorities warn about coumarin levels in cassia cinnamon foods."], ["2010s", "Meta-analyses of cinnamon for diabetes report small or inconsistent effects."]], "cell": "Cinnamon's proposed glucose-lowering activity has been attributed to type A procyanidin polymers and to cinnamaldehyde. In cell studies, these compounds increase insulin receptor phosphorylation, activate downstream signaling, increase GLUT4 translocation and glucose uptake, and may inhibit enzymes that break down carbohydrates; cinnamon may also slow stomach emptying after a meal. Coumarin is a benzopyrone found in cassia, tonka beans and sweet clover. In most humans it is hydroxylated by CYP2A6 to 7-hydroxycoumarin, which is conjugated and excreted. A minor pathway forms coumarin 3,4-epoxide, a reactive compound that can be converted to o-hydroxyphenylacetaldehyde, which is toxic to liver cells. People with genetic or acquired reductions in CYP2A6 activity shift more coumarin through this toxic route, which explains why liver injury affects only a sensitive subset. Coumarin itself has no meaningful anticoagulant activity; dicoumarol, which forms when sweet clover spoils, and warfarin are different 4-hydroxycoumarins. Cinnamaldehyde is a contact allergen and causes oral mucosal reactions with flavored gum and candy.", "body": [["Liver", "Coumarin-related hepatitis at high intakes in sensitive people, reported to be reversible after stopping."], ["Blood glucose", "Possible small fasting glucose reductions; may add to diabetes medicines."], ["Mouth and skin", "Cinnamaldehyde can cause mouth sores and contact allergy."], ["Lungs", "Inhaled dry powder can cause choking, coughing and aspiration pneumonitis."], ["Gastrointestinal tract", "Possible slowing of gastric emptying; stomach upset."]], "journey": "Coumarin is absorbed rapidly and almost completely from the gut, but extensive first-pass metabolism by CYP2A6 in the liver converts most of it to 7-hydroxycoumarin, which is glucuronidated and excreted in the urine within hours, so the half-life of coumarin itself is short in most people. In individuals with low CYP2A6 activity, more coumarin is metabolized through the epoxide pathway. Cinnamaldehyde is absorbed and rapidly oxidized to cinnamic acid, then to benzoic acid and hippuric acid, which are excreted in urine. Procyanidins are poorly absorbed and largely metabolized by colon bacteria. Coumarin content varies widely among cassia products, and water extracts contain less than whole bark powder.", "sidefx": "Cinnamon supplements are generally well tolerated in trials, with occasional stomach upset and heartburn. Cinnamaldehyde can cause mouth sores, burning and swelling, typically from cinnamon-flavored gum or candy, and allergic contact dermatitis. The main safety concern with large cassia intakes is coumarin-related liver injury in sensitive people, which in reported cases resolved after stopping. Hypoglycemia is possible with diabetes medicines. Swallowing or inhaling dry ground cinnamon, as in the internet cinnamon challenge, can cause choking, coughing, aspiration pneumonitis and, in people with asthma, bronchospasm.", "fun": "Warfarin's chemical family traces back to sweet clover disease in cattle, caused when coumarin in moldy clover was converted to the anticoagulant dicoumarol; plain coumarin in cassia cinnamon does not thin the blood.", "src": ["NIH NCCIH: Cinnamon", "LiverTox (NIH): Cinnamon", "Khan et al. 2003, Diabetes Care, cinnamon improves glucose and lipids of people with type 2 diabetes", "German Federal Institute for Risk Assessment (BfR) 2006 and 2012, coumarin in cinnamon", "Grant-Alfieri et al. 2013, Pediatrics, Ingesting and aspirating dry cinnamon by children and adolescents: the cinnamon challenge"]}, "garcinia-cambogia": {"origin": "Garcinia gummi-gutta, long sold under the name Garcinia cambogia, is a tree of southern India and Southeast Asia whose small, ribbed, pumpkin-shaped fruit is dried and used as a souring agent in curries, especially in Kerala, much as tamarind is used elsewhere. In the 1960s and 1970s, researchers identified (-)-hydroxycitric acid as the main acid in the rind and showed at the drug company Hoffmann-La Roche that it inhibited ATP citrate lyase and reduced fat synthesis and food intake in rats. These findings were marketed as a natural fat blocker in the 1990s. A carefully designed randomized trial published in JAMA in 1998 found that garcinia produced no more weight loss than placebo. Sales nevertheless boomed again after television promotion in 2012. Safety concerns accumulated in parallel: in 2009 FDA warned consumers to stop using certain Hydroxycut products after reports of serious liver injury, including a death; those products contained garcinia among other ingredients. Later reports, including cases with garcinia as a single ingredient, led LiverTox to list it as a likely cause of clinically apparent liver injury, and case reports also described mania and serotonin toxicity.", "timeline": [["Traditional use", "Dried garcinia rind is used as a souring agent in South Indian cooking."], ["1960s to 1970s", "Hydroxycitric acid is identified and shown to inhibit ATP citrate lyase in animal studies."], ["1998", "A randomized trial in JAMA finds no more weight loss with garcinia than with placebo."], ["2009", "FDA warns consumers to stop using certain Hydroxycut products after serious liver injury reports."], ["2010s", "Case reports and series link garcinia-containing products to liver injury, mania and serotonin toxicity."]], "cell": "Hydroxycitric acid is a structural analog of citrate. In the cytoplasm, ATP citrate lyase splits citrate exported from mitochondria into acetyl-CoA and oxaloacetate, providing the acetyl-CoA building blocks for fatty acid and cholesterol synthesis. HCA competitively inhibits this enzyme, and in rats this reduced fat synthesis and food intake when carbohydrate intake was high. In humans, eating patterns, adaptation through other metabolic pathways and limited absorption may explain why this mechanism does not produce meaningful weight loss. HCA has also been proposed to increase serotonin availability in brain tissue, which is used to explain appetite claims and may relate to case reports of serotonin toxicity and mania, especially with serotonergic antidepressants. The mechanism of liver injury is unknown. Proposed explanations include an idiosyncratic or immune-mediated reaction, effects on liver lipid metabolism and oxidative stress, and contributions from other ingredients in multi-component products, which makes causality complex in many reports.", "body": [["Liver", "Hepatocellular liver injury, sometimes severe, occasionally requiring transplant."], ["Brain", "Proposed serotonin effects; case reports of mania and serotonin toxicity with antidepressants."], ["Adipose tissue and metabolism", "Inhibits fat synthesis in animals; little or no meaningful weight loss in humans."], ["Gastrointestinal tract", "Nausea, diarrhea and abdominal discomfort."], ["Muscle and pancreas", "Isolated reports of rhabdomyolysis and pancreatitis."]], "journey": "Human pharmacokinetic data for hydroxycitric acid are limited. Small studies show that HCA appears in plasma after oral intake, with peak levels within a few hours, and that a portion is excreted in the urine. Absorption may depend on the salt form, such as calcium, potassium or magnesium salts, which are used in different products. HCA can also form a lactone, which may affect measured levels. How HCA is metabolized and whether it accumulates with repeated use are not well defined. In weight-loss blends, garcinia is often combined with caffeine and green tea extract, which have their own absorption patterns and add their own risks.", "sidefx": "Mild effects include headache, nausea, diarrhea, dizziness and dry mouth. The most important serious effect is liver injury, usually a hepatocellular pattern appearing within weeks to months, presenting with fatigue, nausea, abdominal pain, dark urine and jaundice; some cases have progressed to acute liver failure and transplant. Case reports describe mania, sometimes with psychosis, and serotonin toxicity with agitation, tremor, sweating and fever in people also taking serotonergic antidepressants. Isolated reports describe rhabdomyolysis and pancreatitis. Many weight-loss products combine garcinia with stimulants and other hepatotoxic ingredients, which compounds these risks.", "fun": "In Kerala, the same dried garcinia rind sold in weight-loss pills is a traditional ingredient in fish curry, where it is valued simply for its sour taste.", "src": ["LiverTox (NIH): Garcinia cambogia", "Heymsfield et al. 1998, JAMA, Garcinia cambogia (hydroxycitric acid) as a potential antiobesity agent: a randomized controlled trial", "FDA 2009 consumer warning on Hydroxycut products", "Lopez et al. 2014, Journal of Medical Toxicology, serotonin toxicity associated with Garcinia cambogia and an SSRI"]}, "green-coffee-bean": {"origin": "Coffee beans are the seeds of Coffea arabica and Coffea canephora, native to Ethiopia and central Africa, and roasted coffee has been a global beverage for centuries. Roasting transforms the bean, breaking down much of its chlorogenic acid content and creating the flavor compounds of brewed coffee. Researchers studying chlorogenic acids found that these polyphenols affected glucose handling in laboratory and animal studies, and observational studies linked coffee drinking to lower risk of type 2 diabetes, which suggested that unroasted green coffee might be useful. Green coffee extract supplements appeared in the 2000s. In 2012 a small trial conducted in India and published by US authors claimed dramatic weight loss, and a nationally televised promotion turned green coffee extract into a bestselling supplement almost overnight. The Federal Trade Commission later charged that the study data were seriously flawed, and in 2014 the supplier that sponsored the study, Applied Food Sciences, settled the charges, after which the authors retracted the paper. Systematic reviews describe the remaining evidence as small and of low quality.", "timeline": [["Centuries ago", "Coffee spreads from Ethiopia and Yemen to become a global roasted beverage."], ["2000s", "Green coffee extracts rich in chlorogenic acids are marketed as supplements."], ["2012", "A small weight-loss trial is heavily promoted on national television, driving a sales surge."], ["2014", "The FTC settles charges with the supplier behind the study, and the authors retract the paper."], ["2010s", "Systematic reviews describe green coffee weight-loss evidence as low quality."]], "cell": "Chlorogenic acids are esters formed between caffeic acid and quinic acid, with 5-caffeoylquinic acid the most abundant. In laboratory and animal studies they inhibit glucose-6-phosphatase, the liver enzyme that releases glucose into the blood, reduce intestinal glucose uptake, activate AMP-activated protein kinase and influence fat metabolism. They are also antioxidants. These actions provided a biological rationale for weight and glucose claims, but human trials are small and inconsistent. Caffeine, present in green coffee extracts in varying amounts, blocks adenosine A1 and A2A receptors in the brain and heart. Because adenosine normally promotes sleepiness and slows heart rate, blocking it raises alertness, heart rate and blood pressure and promotes the release of catecholamines, which accounts for most of the side effects people notice from these products. Caffeine also modestly increases energy expenditure and fat oxidation.", "body": [["Brain", "Alertness, nervousness and insomnia from caffeine."], ["Heart and blood vessels", "Increased heart rate and palpitations from caffeine; small trials suggest modest blood pressure effects of chlorogenic acids."], ["Liver and gut", "Proposed slowing of glucose absorption and release; not proven in people."], ["Adipose tissue", "Weight-loss claims are not supported by reliable trials."], ["Bones", "High caffeine intake modestly increases calcium loss in urine."]], "journey": "A portion of chlorogenic acid is absorbed intact or after hydrolysis in the small intestine, but most reaches the colon, where gut bacteria split it into caffeic and quinic acids and further into smaller phenolic acids that are absorbed, conjugated in the liver and excreted in the urine over many hours. Caffeine is absorbed quickly and almost completely, peaking within about an hour, and is metabolized mainly by CYP1A2 in the liver to paraxanthine, theobromine and theophylline before excretion in urine. Caffeine clearance slows in pregnancy, liver disease and with CYP1A2 inhibitors such as fluvoxamine and ciprofloxacin, while smoking speeds it.", "sidefx": "Side effects are mainly due to caffeine: headache, nervousness, jitteriness, insomnia, stomach upset, frequent urination, palpitations and increased heart rate. People with arrhythmias, hypertension, anxiety disorders or who are pregnant are more sensitive. Combining green coffee extract with other caffeine sources or stimulants in weight-loss stacks increases the risk of palpitations and, rarely, arrhythmias. Very high caffeine intakes can cause vomiting, tremor, arrhythmias and seizures. Chlorogenic acids themselves are generally well tolerated in short trials.", "fun": "Roasting can destroy a large share of a coffee bean's chlorogenic acid, so a dark roast and an unroasted green bean from the same plant have very different chemistry.", "src": ["NIH Office of Dietary Supplements: Dietary Supplements for Weight Loss", "Federal Trade Commission 2014 press release: Applied Food Sciences settlement over green coffee claims", "Onakpoya, Terry and Ernst 2011, Gastroenterology Research and Practice, green coffee extract as a weight loss supplement: systematic review", "Retraction notice 2014, Diabetes, Metabolic Syndrome and Obesity: Targets and Therapy"]}, "white-kidney-bean": {"origin": "The common bean, Phaseolus vulgaris, was domesticated in Mesoamerica and the Andes thousands of years ago and includes kidney, navy, pinto and black beans. In the 1940s, researchers found that beans contain a protein that inhibits alpha-amylase, the enzyme that breaks down starch, later called phaseolamin. In the early 1980s, starch blocker tablets made from bean extracts became a sensation in the United States, marketed with the promise that people could eat starch without absorbing its calories. In 1982 FDA ruled that these products were drugs that required approval, and they were taken off the market. The same year, a study published in the New England Journal of Medicine by researchers in Dallas measured starch absorption directly and found that the starch blocker tablets of the time did not reduce the absorption of starch calories, likely because the inhibitor was not potent or stable enough. Manufacturers later developed more concentrated and processed extracts and returned them to the market as dietary supplements in the late 1990s and 2000s. Small trials have shown mixed results.", "timeline": [["Thousands of years ago", "The common bean is domesticated in Mesoamerica and the Andes."], ["1940s", "An alpha-amylase inhibitor is identified in beans."], ["1982", "FDA classifies starch blocker tablets as drugs requiring approval, and they leave the market."], ["1982", "A New England Journal of Medicine study finds that starch blockers do not reduce starch calorie absorption."], ["Late 1990s to 2000s", "Processed white kidney bean extracts return as dietary supplements."]], "cell": "Phaseolamin, also called alpha-amylase inhibitor 1, is a glycoprotein that binds tightly to mammalian pancreatic and salivary alpha-amylase, blocking the active site that breaks starch into smaller sugars. When enough inhibitor is present and stable in the gut, less starch is digested in the small intestine and the rise in blood glucose after a starchy meal is smaller. The undigested starch passes into the colon, where bacteria ferment it into short-chain fatty acids and gases, which explains the gas, bloating and loose stools. The inhibitor is sensitive to stomach acid and digestive enzymes, and its potency depends heavily on processing, which helps explain why early products failed. Kidney beans also contain phytohaemagglutinin, a lectin that binds sugars on the surface of intestinal cells, damages the gut lining and triggers vomiting and diarrhea; it is destroyed by soaking and thorough boiling but not by low-temperature cooking.", "body": [["Small intestine", "Inhibits starch digestion by blocking pancreatic alpha-amylase."], ["Colon", "Undigested starch is fermented, producing gas, bloating and loose stools."], ["Blood glucose", "Possible modest reduction in post-meal glucose rise."], ["Gut lining", "Lectin from raw or undercooked beans causes vomiting and diarrhea."], ["Immune system", "Allergic reactions possible in legume allergy."]], "journey": "Phaseolamin acts within the gut lumen during a meal and is not meaningfully absorbed into the blood as an active protein. Part of it is denatured by stomach acid and digested by proteases, which reduces its activity, and the amount that remains active determines how much starch digestion is blocked. Undigested starch then travels to the colon, where bacteria ferment it over hours. The protein and its fragments are eliminated in the feces or digested to amino acids. Because the effect depends on taking the extract with starchy food, its timing relative to meals matters for any effect.", "sidefx": "The most common effects are gas, bloating, abdominal discomfort and diarrhea from fermentation of undigested starch in the colon. In people using insulin or oral diabetes medicines, reduced post-meal glucose could add to their effects. People with legume allergies may react. Combining with acarbose or miglitol, prescription drugs that also block carbohydrate digestion, could increase gastrointestinal effects. Separately from the supplement, eating raw or undercooked kidney beans causes lectin food poisoning with abrupt nausea, vomiting and diarrhea within hours, usually resolving without long-term harm.", "fun": "Red kidney beans cooked in a slow cooker at low temperature can be more toxic than raw beans, because gentle heating may not destroy the lectin phytohaemagglutinin, which is why food safety guidance calls for thorough boiling.", "src": ["NIH Office of Dietary Supplements: Dietary Supplements for Weight Loss", "Bo-Linn et al. 1982, New England Journal of Medicine, Starch blockers: their effect on calorie absorption from a high-starch meal", "FDA Bad Bug Book: Phytohaemagglutinin", "Barrett and Udani 2011, Nutrition Journal, a proprietary alpha-amylase inhibitor from white bean: review of clinical studies"]}, "banaba": {"origin": "Lagerstroemia speciosa, known in the Philippines as banaba and elsewhere as giant crape myrtle or pride of India, is an ornamental flowering tree of tropical South and Southeast Asia. Its leaves have long been boiled into a tea in Filipino folk practice for diabetes and urinary complaints. Interest from scientists in Japan and the Philippines in the late twentieth century focused on the leaf's ability to lower blood glucose in diabetic animals, and corosolic acid, a pentacyclic triterpenoid also found in loquat leaves and other plants, emerged as the marker compound. Extracts standardized to a stated percentage of corosolic acid were then marketed in Japan and the United States as natural blood sugar support. Later work suggested that ellagitannins in the leaf, such as lagerstroemin, also have insulin-like activity in cells, so the whole extract may matter more than any single compound. Banaba fits a familiar pattern for blood sugar supplements: an ethnobotanical tradition, positive results in diabetic mice, a small number of short human trials with modest effects, and commercial standardization to one marker compound. It has never been evaluated in large outcome trials, and it remains a dietary supplement rather than a drug.", "timeline": [["Traditional", "Banaba leaf tea is used in Philippine folk medicine for diabetes and urinary complaints."], ["1990s", "Japanese and Philippine researchers report glucose lowering by banaba extracts in diabetic animal models."], ["2000s", "Extracts standardized to corosolic acid are marketed as blood sugar supplements, and small human trials are published."], ["2012", "A review by Stohs and colleagues summarizes the limited human evidence and the absence of serious adverse event signals in trials."]], "cell": "Corosolic acid is a pentacyclic triterpenoid acid closely related to ursolic acid. In muscle and fat cell models it is reported to promote translocation of the GLUT4 glucose transporter to the cell membrane, which increases glucose uptake in a way that resembles insulin action. Proposed upstream effects include enhanced phosphorylation of the insulin receptor and its substrates, possibly through inhibition of protein tyrosine phosphatases that normally switch insulin signaling off. Additional laboratory findings include inhibition of intestinal alpha-glucosidase, which slows carbohydrate digestion, and reduced hepatic gluconeogenesis. Ellagitannins in the leaf, particularly lagerstroemin, have been shown to activate glucose transport in fat cells independently. None of these mechanisms has been confirmed in humans at the concentrations reached after oral use, and the relative contribution of corosolic acid versus the tannins remains uncertain.", "body": [["Skeletal muscle", "Proposed increase in glucose uptake through GLUT4 translocation, based on cell and animal data."], ["Liver", "Animal data suggest reduced glucose output from gluconeogenesis."], ["Small intestine", "Possible alpha-glucosidase inhibition slowing carbohydrate absorption."], ["Pancreas and endocrine system", "No direct insulin release is established; effects add to drugs that do raise insulin, which is where hypoglycemia risk arises."], ["Kidney", "An isolated case report of kidney injury with corosolic acid plus an NSAID, causality uncertain."]], "journey": "Human pharmacokinetic data for corosolic acid are sparse. As a lipophilic triterpenoid acid, it is expected to be poorly and variably absorbed from the gut, and the ellagitannins are largely broken down by colonic bacteria into urolithins before absorption. Metabolism and elimination pathways in humans have not been clearly defined, and no reliable half-life is established. Because extracts differ in their corosolic acid percentage and in how much tannin and other leaf material they retain, exposure varies between products, which makes it hard to compare trials.", "sidefx": "In the small human trials, adverse effects were uncommon and mild, mostly stomach upset. The practical concern is additive glucose lowering: a person with diabetes who uses banaba alongside insulin or a sulfonylurea may have more frequent or more severe hypoglycemia, with sweating, tremor, hunger, confusion and, if severe, seizures or loss of consciousness. Blood sugar blends commonly combine banaba with chromium, cinnamon, bitter melon, gymnema or berberine, which makes it harder to know which ingredient contributed. An isolated case report described acute kidney injury and lactic acidosis in a person with pre-existing kidney disease using corosolic acid together with an NSAID; this does not establish a cause, but it is the reason kidney disease is listed as a caution. Long-term safety has not been studied.", "fun": "Corosolic acid is not unique to banaba; loquat leaves, apple peel and several other plants contain it, and it is a close chemical cousin of ursolic acid, a common waxy compound on fruit skins.", "src": ["Stohs SJ, Miller H, Kaats GR 2012, J Ethnopharmacol, review of Lagerstroemia speciosa and corosolic acid", "Natural Medicines-style monograph: Banaba", "Miura T et al. 2012, Evidence-Based Complementary and Alternative Medicine, corosolic acid review"]}, "glucomannan": {"origin": "Konjac (Amorphophallus konjac) is a perennial plant in the arum family, related to the giant corpse flower, grown for its large underground corm. In Japan, China and Southeast Asia, konjac flour has been made into a firm jelly called konnyaku and into translucent shirataki noodles for centuries, valued for having almost no digestible calories. The fiber responsible, glucomannan, is a long chain of glucose and mannose units with a few acetyl groups that give it remarkable water-holding capacity. In the late twentieth century it was marketed in Western countries as a weight-loss supplement on the logic that a gel filling the stomach should reduce appetite. Problems appeared quickly with tablet forms, which could swell in the esophagus before dissolving; Australia restricted glucomannan tablets in the 1980s after reports of esophageal obstruction. A second problem came from konjac mini-cup jelly candies, popular snacks in Asia, which caused choking deaths in children in several countries; the FDA issued warnings and import actions in the early 2000s. More recently FDA accepted glucomannan as a dietary fiber for Nutrition Facts labeling based on its cholesterol-lowering evidence, and European regulators accepted a weight-loss claim at specified intakes, though independent reviews describe the weight effect as small.", "timeline": [["Centuries ago", "Konjac flour is made into konnyaku jelly and noodles in Japan and China."], ["1980s", "Glucomannan is marketed in the West as a diet aid; Australia restricts tablet forms after reports of esophageal obstruction."], ["2001", "FDA warns about konjac mini-cup jelly candies after child choking deaths and moves to detain imports."], ["2010", "European regulators accept a weight-loss health claim for glucomannan at specified intakes as part of a calorie-restricted diet."], ["2010s to 2020s", "FDA accepts glucomannan as a dietary fiber for food labeling based on cholesterol-lowering data."]], "cell": "Glucomannan acts physically rather than pharmacologically. Its long polysaccharide chains hydrate and entangle, forming a highly viscous gel that can hold many times its own weight in water. In the stomach this increases volume and slows gastric emptying; in the small intestine the viscous layer slows mixing and diffusion, so glucose and fat from a meal are absorbed more slowly, blunting the post-meal glucose rise. The gel also traps bile acids and reduces their reabsorption in the ileum, so the liver converts more cholesterol to new bile acids and upregulates LDL receptors, which explains the modest LDL reduction. In the colon, bacteria ferment glucomannan to short-chain fatty acids such as acetate, propionate and butyrate, which feed colonocytes and may alter the microbiota. Stool weight and water content increase, which accounts for the constipation benefit. The same swelling explains the main hazard: if a dry tablet or a dense gel lodges in the esophagus, it expands and cannot easily pass.", "body": [["Esophagus", "Main site of serious harm: dry tablets or dense gels can swell and obstruct."], ["Stomach", "Increased volume and slower emptying produce fullness."], ["Small intestine", "Viscous gel slows glucose and fat absorption and traps bile acids."], ["Liver and blood lipids", "Increased bile acid synthesis lowers LDL cholesterol modestly."], ["Colon", "Fermented to short-chain fatty acids; adds stool bulk and water, with gas and bloating."]], "journey": "Glucomannan is not absorbed into the bloodstream. It passes through the stomach and small intestine as a hydrated gel, mixing with food and bile. In the colon, resident bacteria ferment much of it to short-chain fatty acids and gases; the short-chain fatty acids are absorbed by the colonic lining and used locally or by the liver. The unfermented remainder leaves in the stool. Because there is no systemic exposure, it has no meaningful half-life or liver metabolism, and its drug interactions come from physical binding or delayed absorption in the gut.", "sidefx": "Gas, bloating, abdominal discomfort and loose stools are the common effects, as with other fermentable fibers. The serious adverse effects are mechanical: esophageal obstruction from tablets or capsules that swell before reaching the stomach, intestinal obstruction in people with narrowing of the bowel or inadequate fluid intake, and choking on konjac jelly candies, which are firm, slippery and do not dissolve quickly in the mouth. Children, older adults and people with swallowing disorders are at highest risk. In people using insulin or sulfonylureas, blunted glucose absorption can contribute to low blood sugar. Oral medicines taken at the same time may be absorbed less or more slowly. Allergic reactions are rare.", "fun": "One unit of glucomannan can soak up so much water that a spoonful of powder stirred into a glass can turn the whole drink into a thick gel within minutes, which is exactly why it fills a stomach and exactly why it should not swell in a throat.", "src": ["NIH NCCIH herb and supplement information: glucomannan", "FDA consumer warnings and import alert on konjac mini-cup jelly candies", "Sood N et al. 2008, Am J Clin Nutr, meta-analysis of glucomannan effects on lipids and glucose", "EFSA 2010 Scientific Opinion on glucomannan health claims"]}, "bitter-orange": {"origin": "Citrus aurantium, the bitter or Seville orange, was carried from Asia into the Mediterranean in the Middle Ages and became the orange of marmalade, orange-flower water, neroli oil and the liqueur Cointreau. Its dried peel has a long place in traditional Chinese medicine as a digestive remedy. Synephrine itself was synthesized in the early twentieth century and briefly used as a drug, then found to occur naturally in citrus. The modern supplement story begins with ephedra: as safety concerns mounted around ephedra-based fat burners in the late 1990s and early 2000s, manufacturers switched to bitter orange extract, marketing synephrine as a safer, ephedra-free stimulant. After the FDA ban on ephedrine alkaloid supplements took effect in 2004, bitter orange became a standard fat-burner ingredient, usually paired with caffeine. Case reports soon described strokes, heart attacks, arrhythmias and ischemic colitis in otherwise healthy users of these products. Defenders argue that p-synephrine is a much weaker adrenergic agent than ephedrine or its isomer m-synephrine (phenylephrine), and that the harms come from stacking with caffeine and other stimulants. Regulators and NCCIH have taken a cautious line, noting insufficient evidence of benefit and real safety concerns. Synephrine appears on some athletic banned-substance lists, which matters for student athletes.", "timeline": [["Middle Ages", "Bitter orange spreads into Mediterranean cultivation and later becomes the marmalade orange."], ["1920s to 1930s", "Synephrine is synthesized and studied as a sympathomimetic drug, and later found in citrus."], ["Late 1990s to 2003", "Ephedra-free fat burners adopt bitter orange as an alternative stimulant."], ["2004", "FDA's ban on ephedrine alkaloid supplements takes effect, accelerating the shift to bitter orange."], ["2000s to 2010s", "Case reports link bitter orange products, usually with caffeine, to stroke, MI, arrhythmia and ischemic colitis."]], "cell": "Synephrine is a phenylethylamine with a para-hydroxyl group and an N-methyl side chain; the meta isomer is phenylephrine, a well-known alpha-1 agonist, while the para isomer that dominates bitter orange is reported to have much weaker alpha-1 and beta-1/beta-2 activity and some beta-3 activity, which is the basis for claims about fat breakdown. Binding studies show low affinity at most adrenergic receptors, but the receptor effects are real enough to raise heart rate and blood pressure in some volunteers, especially when caffeine blocks adenosine receptors and inhibits phosphodiesterase at the same time, amplifying cyclic AMP signaling downstream of beta receptors. Bitter orange extracts can also contain octopamine, tyramine, hordenine and N-methyltyramine, which release or mimic norepinephrine. Synephrine and these amines are substrates of monoamine oxidase, so MAO inhibitors can greatly raise their effect. Separately, the fruit and juice contain furanocoumarins, chiefly bergamottin and 6',7'-dihydroxybergamottin, which irreversibly inactivate CYP3A4 in the intestinal wall, the same mechanism as grapefruit juice; Seville orange juice raised felodipine levels in a human study.", "body": [["Heart", "Tachycardia and increased contractility; case reports of arrhythmia and myocardial infarction."], ["Blood vessels", "Vasoconstriction and blood pressure rise; case reports of stroke and ischemic colitis."], ["Brain", "Jitteriness, anxiety, insomnia, headache, especially with caffeine."], ["Adipose tissue", "Proposed beta-3 mediated lipolysis; weight effects in humans are small or unclear."], ["Gut wall", "Juice furanocoumarins inactivate intestinal CYP3A4, raising levels of some oral drugs."]], "journey": "p-Synephrine is absorbed from the gut and reaches peak levels within about one to two hours. It undergoes extensive first-pass metabolism by monoamine oxidase in the gut wall and liver to p-hydroxymandelic acid and related products, which are excreted in the urine. Reported half-life is a few hours, so effects of a single dose fade over the day, though repeated stimulant dosing keeps levels elevated. Because MAO is the main clearance route, MAO inhibitors can sharply increase exposure. The furanocoumarins act locally on intestinal enzymes, and their inhibitory effect can persist for a day or more because the enzyme must be newly synthesized.", "sidefx": "Common effects are jitteriness, headache, insomnia, palpitations and a measurable rise in heart rate and blood pressure in some people. Serious events reported with bitter orange products, usually multi-ingredient and caffeine-containing, include hemorrhagic and ischemic stroke, angina and myocardial infarction, ventricular arrhythmias including in people with long QT, ischemic colitis, and rare sudden death. Because these reports involve combination products, the contribution of synephrine alone is uncertain, but the pattern resembles that seen with ephedra. Risk appears higher with heart disease, hypertension, MAO inhibitor use, decongestants, and in combination with caffeine or yohimbine. Topical bitter orange oil can cause photosensitivity because of furanocoumarins.", "fun": "Phenylephrine, the decongestant and pressor used in hospitals, is m-synephrine, so the bitter orange ingredient and a common IV vasopressor differ only in where a single hydroxyl group sits on the ring.", "src": ["NIH NCCIH: Bitter Orange fact sheet", "Stohs SJ, Preuss HG, Shara M 2011, Phytotherapy Research, review of p-synephrine safety", "Malhotra S et al. 2001, Clin Pharmacol Ther, Seville orange juice and felodipine interaction", "Natural Medicines-style monograph: Bitter Orange"]}, "raspberry-ketone": {"origin": "Raspberry ketone, chemically 4-(4-hydroxyphenyl)butan-2-one, is the main compound responsible for the characteristic smell of red raspberries. It has long been used by the flavor and fragrance industry and is accepted as a food flavoring. Because raspberries contain only trace amounts, the commercial compound is produced synthetically or by biotechnology, which makes natural raspberry ketone one of the most expensive flavor ingredients. Its reinvention as a weight-loss supplement grew from Japanese rodent research in the 2000s showing that high exposures prevented diet-induced weight gain in mice and increased fat breakdown in isolated fat cells. The authors noted structural similarity to synephrine and capsaicin, compounds already associated with metabolism claims. Around 2012, enthusiastic television promotion created a surge of demand, and raspberry ketone capsules and blends flooded the market. The human evidence never followed: there is no published human trial of raspberry ketone alone showing weight loss, and the one commonly cited human study tested a multi-ingredient product that also contained caffeine and other active compounds. In 2014 United States Senate hearings on deceptive weight-loss marketing drew attention to this kind of promotion, and the Federal Trade Commission has pursued deceptive weight-loss claims generally. Raspberry ketone illustrates the gap between a mouse study and a consumer product.", "timeline": [["Mid twentieth century", "Raspberry ketone is identified as the key raspberry aroma compound and used in flavoring."], ["2005", "A Japanese rodent study reports that raspberry ketone prevents diet-induced weight gain in mice."], ["2010", "Fat-cell studies report increased lipolysis and adiponectin release."], ["2012", "Television promotion drives a surge in raspberry ketone supplement sales."], ["2014", "Senate hearings on weight-loss marketing scrutinize unsupported claims for products like this."]], "cell": "Raspberry ketone is a small phenolic ketone. In isolated rodent fat cells, it increased norepinephrine-stimulated lipolysis, apparently by promoting translocation of hormone-sensitive lipase from the cytosol to lipid droplets, the step that lets the enzyme break stored triglycerides into free fatty acids and glycerol. In 3T3-L1 adipocytes, a common fat-cell model, it increased secretion of adiponectin, an adipokine associated with insulin sensitivity, and reduced lipid accumulation. These effects were seen at concentrations far higher than would be expected in human blood after oral use, and the rodent feeding studies used amounts that are very large relative to body weight. The structural resemblance to synephrine has led to speculation about adrenergic activity, but this has not been demonstrated in humans. Anecdotal reports of jitteriness and palpitations usually involve products that also contain caffeine or other stimulants.", "body": [["Adipose tissue", "Increased lipolysis and adiponectin in rodent and cell studies only."], ["Liver", "Rodent studies reported less fat accumulation in the liver at high exposures."], ["Heart", "Palpitations reported with stimulant-containing blends rather than the compound alone."], ["Metabolism and blood sugar", "Animal data hint at glucose effects; no human confirmation."]], "journey": "In rodents, raspberry ketone is absorbed rapidly from the gut and extensively metabolized. Major pathways include reduction of the ketone to the corresponding alcohol, rhododendrol, along with oxidation and conjugation with glucuronic acid and sulfate. Metabolites are excreted mainly in the urine within a day. Human pharmacokinetic studies are lacking, so no human half-life, peak time or tissue distribution can be stated. Because rhododendrol has caused skin depigmentation when used topically in cosmetics, some researchers have raised theoretical questions about the metabolite, though no such effect has been shown from oral supplements.", "sidefx": "Few adverse effects attributable to raspberry ketone alone have been documented, largely because it has been so little studied in humans. Users of weight-loss blends report jitteriness, rapid heartbeat, anxiety and insomnia, which most likely come from caffeine, green tea extract or other stimulants in the same capsule. Some modeling and safety assessments have questioned whether supplement-level intake, far above flavoring use, has been adequately evaluated for heart and reproductive safety. People with heart disease, hypertension, pregnancy or diabetes have no safety data to rely on. The main practical harm is spending money and attention on a product without demonstrated benefit while stimulant co-ingredients carry the real cardiovascular risk.", "fun": "Natural raspberry ketone is so scarce that it can cost thousands of dollars per kilogram, while the synthetic version in supplements is cheap, which is why nearly all capsules contain the lab-made compound.", "src": ["Morimoto C et al. 2005, Life Sciences, anti-obese action of raspberry ketone in mice", "Park KS 2010, Planta Medica, raspberry ketone and adiponectin in 3T3-L1 cells", "Natural Medicines-style monograph: Raspberry Ketone"]}, "forskolin": {"origin": "Coleus forskohlii, now often classified as Plectranthus barbatus, is a perennial in the mint family native to the Indian subcontinent, where its root has a place in Ayurvedic practice for heart, lung and digestive complaints. In the early 1970s, a collaboration between the Central Drug Research Institute in Lucknow and the Hoechst research center in Bombay screened Indian medicinal plants for cardiovascular activity and found that a coleus root extract lowered blood pressure in animals. The active compound, a labdane diterpene, was isolated and named forskolin (also coleonol). In 1981, Kenneth Seamon and John Daly at the US National Institutes of Health showed that forskolin directly activates adenylyl cyclase, the enzyme that makes cyclic AMP, in a way that does not require a hormone receptor. That discovery made forskolin one of the most widely used tools in cell biology, because it lets researchers raise cAMP in almost any cell on demand. A water-soluble derivative, colforsin daropate, was later developed and approved in Japan for acute heart failure. In the supplement market, forskolin took a different path: small body-composition trials in the 2000s and celebrity promotion in the 2010s repositioned coleus extract as a fat burner, a use the evidence does not strongly support.", "timeline": [["Traditional", "Coleus root is used in Ayurvedic practice for heart, lung and digestive complaints."], ["1970s", "Indian and Hoechst researchers isolate forskolin while screening plants for blood pressure activity."], ["1981", "Seamon and Daly show that forskolin directly activates adenylyl cyclase."], ["1990s", "A forskolin derivative, colforsin daropate, is developed for heart failure in Japan."], ["2000s to 2010s", "Small trials and heavy marketing reposition forskolin as a weight-loss supplement."]], "cell": "Adenylyl cyclases are membrane enzymes that convert ATP into cyclic AMP, the second messenger used by beta-adrenergic, glucagon, histamine H2 and many other receptors. Normally, a stimulatory G protein (Gs) must be activated by a receptor before it switches the cyclase on. Forskolin binds in a pocket between the two catalytic domains of the enzyme and stabilizes the active configuration directly, so it raises cAMP in nearly any cell expressing the forskolin-sensitive isoforms (types 1 to 8; type 9 is relatively insensitive). Elevated cAMP activates protein kinase A and exchange proteins activated by cAMP. In vascular smooth muscle this relaxes the vessel; in the heart it increases calcium handling, contractility and rate; in airway smooth muscle it relaxes bronchi; in platelets it inhibits aggregation; in fat cells it activates hormone-sensitive lipase; and in the eye it reduces aqueous humor production, which lowered intraocular pressure in small studies. In kidney epithelia, cAMP drives the fluid secretion and proliferation of cysts in polycystic kidney disease, which is why the supplement raises a theoretical concern there.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Vasodilation that can lower blood pressure, flushing."], ["Heart", "Increased rate and contractility through cAMP and calcium handling."], ["Lungs", "Bronchodilation in small studies."], ["Blood and platelets", "Inhibits platelet aggregation in laboratory studies."], ["Adipose tissue", "Increased lipolysis in cells; inconsistent human body-composition results."], ["Eye", "Reduced aqueous humor production in small studies."]], "journey": "Forskolin is a lipophilic diterpene with low water solubility, and oral bioavailability in humans has not been well defined. Animal studies indicate absorption from the gut followed by hepatic metabolism, including deacetylation and hydroxylation, with elimination in bile and urine. Supplement extracts are standardized to a stated percentage of forskolin, but the amount reaching the bloodstream from typical oral products is uncertain, which complicates interpreting both benefit and harm. Eye drops and inhaled forms were studied outside the United States to achieve local effects without large systemic exposure.", "sidefx": "The most consistent complaints are loose stools and diarrhea, followed by flushing and headache. Because forskolin raises cAMP in blood vessels and the heart, symptomatic low blood pressure and a fast heart rate are possible, especially with antihypertensives, nitrates or other vasodilators. Its antiplatelet effect in the laboratory raises a theoretical concern for additive bleeding with warfarin, direct oral anticoagulants or antiplatelet drugs, though clinical bleeding reports are scarce. People with polycystic kidney disease are flagged because laboratory work shows cAMP accelerates cyst growth. Product quality is variable, and weight-loss blends often contain stimulants that add their own effects.", "fun": "Forskolin is so reliable at raising cyclic AMP that it is a staple reagent in biology labs, used to prove that a cell's response depends on cAMP; the supplement aisle sells a version of the same molecule as a diet pill.", "src": ["Seamon KB, Padgett W, Daly JW 1981, Proc Natl Acad Sci USA, forskolin activation of adenylate cyclase", "Natural Medicines-style monograph: Coleus / Forskolin", "Bhat SV et al. 1977, Tetrahedron Letters, structure of forskolin", "Godard MP et al. 2005, Obesity Research, forskolin body composition trial"]}, "hoodia": {"origin": "Hoodia gordonii is a spiny, leafless succulent of the Kalahari and Namib desert regions of South Africa, Namibia and Botswana. It looks like a cactus but belongs to the milkweed and dogbane family, Apocynaceae, and its large flesh-colored flowers smell of rotting meat to attract flies. The San peoples of southern Africa traditionally ate pieces of the stem to dull hunger and thirst on long hunting trips. In the 1960s the South African Council for Scientific and Industrial Research (CSIR) began screening traditional food plants, and its chemists later isolated a steroidal glycoside they called P57 and patented it as an appetite suppressant. The rights were licensed to the British company Phytopharm, which partnered with Pfizer and later Unilever; both large companies eventually walked away. The commercialization became a landmark biopiracy case because the San had not been consulted, and in 2003 the CSIR and the South African San Council signed a benefit-sharing agreement that gave the San a share of future royalties. Meanwhile, a flood of unlicensed hoodia diet pills appeared worldwide, wild plants were overharvested, and hoodia species were placed under CITES Appendix II to control international trade. The story ended with a whimper when Unilever's controlled trial found no effect on how much people ate.", "timeline": [["1960s", "The South African CSIR begins studying traditional San food plants, including hoodia."], ["1997", "Phytopharm licenses the CSIR patent on P57 as an appetite suppressant."], ["2003", "The CSIR and the South African San Council sign a benefit-sharing agreement over hoodia royalties."], ["2004", "Hoodia species are listed in CITES Appendix II, restricting international trade in wild plants."], ["2008", "Unilever ends its hoodia development program."], ["2011", "A randomized placebo-controlled trial reports no effect of purified hoodia extract on food intake or body weight."]], "cell": "P57 is an oxypregnane steroidal glycoside, a steroid backbone carrying a chain of unusual deoxy sugars, structurally related to the cardiac glycosides found in other members of the same plant family but without their strong sodium-potassium ATPase blocking action. The leading hypothesis came from rat experiments in which P57 injected into the brain ventricles raised ATP content in hypothalamic neurons and reduced food intake over the following day. The idea was that hypothalamic sensing of a high-energy cell state is one of the signals for fullness, so a compound that raises neuronal ATP could mimic having eaten. This has never been shown in humans, and there is a basic access problem: P57 is a large glycoside, and it is not established that meaningful amounts are absorbed from the gut or cross the blood-brain barrier after oral intake. Studies in animals also raised the possibility that some reduced eating reflected nausea or aversion rather than true satiety. In the human trial, nausea, vomiting and unusual skin sensations were more common with the extract, and blood pressure and heart rate rose slightly, which suggests some systemic activity without the hoped-for appetite effect.", "body": [["Brain (hypothalamus)", "Proposed site of appetite suppression based on rat injection studies; no confirmed effect in humans."], ["Heart and blood vessels", "Small rises in blood pressure and heart rate were reported with purified extract in a controlled trial."], ["Stomach and gut", "Nausea and vomiting were more common with the extract than with placebo."], ["Liver", "Small increases in bilirubin and alkaline phosphatase were reported in the controlled trial; clinical meaning unclear."], ["Skin", "Unusual skin sensations were reported more often with the extract than with placebo."]], "journey": "Human pharmacokinetics of hoodia constituents are essentially unknown. P57 and related pregnane glycosides are large, sugar-bearing molecules, and compounds of this type are generally poorly absorbed intact from the gut; some may be partly broken down by gut bacteria or digestive hydrolysis into smaller aglycones before absorption. Laboratory studies have looked at how these glycosides cross intestinal cell layers and interact with liver enzymes, but no reliable human data describe peak levels, half-life, metabolism or excretion. Because commercial products vary so much in their real hoodia content, even the amount ingested by a person using a product labeled hoodia is often uncertain.", "sidefx": "In the controlled trial, nausea, vomiting and unusual skin sensations such as tingling were more frequent than with placebo, and small but measurable rises in blood pressure, heart rate, bilirubin and alkaline phosphatase were recorded. Serious harm specific to hoodia has not been clearly documented, but the real-world risk often comes from what else is in the bottle: weight-loss blends sold under the hoodia name have included caffeine, other plant stimulants and, in some cases, undeclared drug ingredients. Skipped meals can also matter for people on glucose-lowering drugs.", "fun": "Hoodia flowers smell like rotting meat, a trick that lures flies to pollinate them in the desert, which made the plant an unlikely face for a glossy diet-pill industry.", "src": ["Blom WA et al. 2011, American Journal of Clinical Nutrition, effect of 15-day consumption of Hoodia gordonii purified extract", "MacLean DB and Luo LG 2004, Brain Research, increased ATP content and food intake suppression by P57", "Vermaak I, Hamman JH and Viljoen AM 2011, Planta Medica, Hoodia gordonii review", "CITES Appendix II listing, Hoodia species", "CSIR and South African San Council benefit-sharing agreement, 2003"]}, "cbg": {"origin": "Cannabigerol was one of the first cannabinoids characterized in the modern era. In 1964 Yechiel Gaoni and Raphael Mechoulam at the Weizmann Institute in Israel described it in the same period they established the structure of delta-9-THC, and it was named for its geranyl side chain. For decades it was a minor footnote, because ordinary cannabis plants contain only small amounts: the plant makes cannabigerolic acid (CBGA) and then rapidly converts it into the acid forms of THC, CBD and cannabichromene. Its importance became clear when the biosynthetic pathway was mapped and CBGA was recognized as the common precursor, earning it the nickname mother cannabinoid. After the 2018 Farm Bill removed hemp from the federal Controlled Substances Act, breeders developed hemp strains in which the downstream synthases are weak or absent, so CBGA accumulates, and CBG products followed CBD onto store shelves as the next non-intoxicating cannabinoid. FDA has not approved any CBG product, and federal hemp law was rewritten in late 2025 with changes taking effect in November 2026, so the legal landscape for these products is in flux.", "timeline": [["1964", "Gaoni and Mechoulam report the isolation and structure of cannabigerol."], ["2010", "Laboratory work identifies CBG as a potent alpha-2 adrenoceptor agonist and 5-HT1A antagonist."], ["2018", "The US Farm Bill removes hemp with low delta-9-THC content from the Controlled Substances Act, opening a market for hemp cannabinoids."], ["2020s", "High-CBG hemp strains and CBG oils and gummies become widely sold; small human studies begin."], ["2025", "Congress redefines hemp in federal law, with changes taking effect in November 2026."]], "cell": "CBG differs from THC and CBD in which receptors it prefers. In rodent brain membrane studies it acted as a potent agonist at alpha-2 adrenergic receptors, the same receptor class that clonidine and dexmedetomidine activate, and as a moderate antagonist at serotonin 5-HT1A receptors. At CB1 and CB2 cannabinoid receptors it binds only weakly and behaves as a partial agonist, which is consistent with its lack of intoxicating effects. It also modulates sensory transient receptor potential channels, activating TRPV1 and TRPA1 and blocking the cold receptor TRPM8, and it has been reported to inhibit reuptake of the endocannabinoid anandamide. In the plant, CBGA is produced inside glandular trichomes when the enzyme geranylpyrophosphate olivetolate geranyltransferase joins olivetolic acid to geranyl pyrophosphate; THCA synthase, CBDA synthase and CBCA synthase then cyclize the geranyl chain in different ways. Heating then removes the carboxyl group from each acid to give the neutral cannabinoids, so CBGA becomes CBG on drying, smoking or cooking. Rodent experiments link CBG's alpha-2 action to lower blood pressure, a finding that has not been established in humans.", "body": [["Brain and nervous system", "Not intoxicating; alpha-2 and serotonin receptor actions suggest possible calming or sleepy effects, unproven in people."], ["Heart and blood vessels", "Rodent studies show blood pressure lowering, consistent with alpha-2 agonism; human data lacking."], ["Gut", "Studied in animal models of colitis; stomach upset and dry mouth are reported with cannabinoid products."], ["Liver", "Likely site of metabolism; laboratory studies suggest inhibition of several CYP enzymes."], ["Eyes", "Early animal work explored effects on eye pressure; no proven use in glaucoma."]], "journey": "Human pharmacokinetic data for CBG are very limited, so much is inferred from related cannabinoids. Like CBD and THC, CBG is highly fat-soluble and poorly water-soluble. Oral products are expected to have slow, variable absorption and substantial first-pass metabolism in the liver, with food and fat content changing how much is absorbed; inhaled forms reach the blood within minutes. Once absorbed, cannabinoids are extensively protein bound and distribute into fat, which prolongs elimination. Metabolism is expected to involve liver CYP enzymes producing hydroxylated metabolites, which are then conjugated and eliminated in feces and urine. Laboratory studies indicate that CBG can inhibit several CYP enzymes, so it may raise levels of other drugs, but the size of this effect in people is unknown.", "sidefx": "Reported side effects in small human studies and user surveys are mostly mild: sleepiness, dry mouth, dizziness and stomach upset. Because it activates alpha-2 receptors in animal studies, low blood pressure and added sedation with drugs such as clonidine, opioids, benzodiazepines or alcohol are plausible concerns. Many real-world problems trace back to product quality. Hemp products are frequently mislabeled, and some contain THC or semi-synthetic cannabinoids, which can cause intoxication, anxiety, fast heart rate, impaired driving and positive drug tests. Children who eat candy-like gummies may become very sleepy.", "fun": "Most cannabis plants carry only a trace of CBG because they convert it so efficiently, so commercial CBG comes largely from hemp strains bred to have weak or missing THCA and CBDA synthases, essentially plants stuck at the first step of their own assembly line.", "src": ["Gaoni Y and Mechoulam R 1964, Journal of the American Chemical Society, isolation and structure of cannabinoids including cannabigerol", "Cascio MG et al. 2010, British Journal of Pharmacology, evidence that cannabigerol is a highly potent alpha-2 adrenoceptor agonist", "Nachnani R, Raup-Konsavage WM and Vrana KE 2021, Journal of Pharmacology and Experimental Therapeutics, the pharmacological case for cannabigerol", "FDA: Regulation of Cannabis and Cannabis-Derived Products", "Congressional Research Service 2025, Change to Federal Definition of Hemp and Implications for Federal Enforcement"]}, "cbn": {"origin": "Cannabinol has the distinction of being the first cannabinoid ever isolated. In the 1890s the British chemists Thomas Wood, W. T. Newton Spivey and Thomas Easterfield extracted a crystalline compound from charas, a cannabis resin from India, and believed it was the plant's active principle. Their material came from resin that had aged in storage, and it was decades before chemists understood why: CBN is not made directly by the plant but forms as THC slowly oxidizes. Robert Cahn in England worked out much of its structure in the early 1930s, and Roger Adams in the United States and Alexander Todd in Britain completed structural work and synthesis around 1940. Once THC itself was isolated and characterized in 1964, CBN was recognized as a weaker relative whose amount reflects how old and how poorly stored cannabis is. The modern CBN market grew after the 2018 Farm Bill, when hemp companies began selling it as a sleep aid, building on the popular belief that old cannabis is especially sleepy. Much commercial CBN is now produced by chemically converting hemp-derived CBD or THC, a practice that federal hemp law changes taking effect in November 2026 address by excluding synthesized cannabinoids from the hemp definition.", "timeline": [["1890s", "Wood, Spivey and Easterfield isolate cannabinol from aged Indian cannabis resin, the first cannabinoid isolated."], ["1930s", "Robert Cahn works out much of the chemical structure of cannabinol."], ["1940", "Roger Adams and Alexander Todd complete structural and synthetic work on cannabinol."], ["1964", "Isolation of delta-9-THC clarifies that CBN is a breakdown product of THC."], ["2020s", "Hemp-derived CBN sleep gummies become widespread; small human sleep trials report mixed results."]], "cell": "CBN is structurally THC with a fully aromatic ring: oxidation removes hydrogens from THC's cyclohexene ring, flattening it into a benzene ring. This small change sharply reduces potency at the CB1 receptor, the brain receptor responsible for cannabis intoxication. In binding and functional studies CBN behaves as a weak partial agonist at CB1, roughly an order of magnitude less potent than THC, with relatively greater affinity for CB2 receptors on immune cells. It also activates TRPA1 and affects other TRP channels in laboratory studies. Because CB1 activation by CBN is weak, its intoxicating effect at ordinary exposures is small. The sleepy reputation of old cannabis may reflect its CBN content together with the loss of more stimulating aromatic terpenes, but controlled evidence is thin. Early human experiments in the 1970s found little effect of CBN alone, while combinations with THC produced somewhat stronger sedation than THC alone in at least one study. Commercial CBN made from CBD by chemical conversion can carry reaction by-products and other cannabinoids if poorly purified.", "body": [["Brain and nervous system", "Weak CB1 activation; possible mild sleepiness, more noticeable with THC, alcohol or sedatives."], ["Immune system", "CB2 activity is studied in laboratory models of inflammation; no proven clinical effect."], ["Liver", "Main site of metabolism by CYP enzymes, with possible effects on other drugs."], ["Lungs", "Vaped products carry the general risks of inhaled cannabinoid oils and additives."], ["Whole body in children", "Accidental ingestion of combined sleep gummies can cause marked sedation."]], "journey": "CBN is fat-soluble and poorly soluble in water. Inhaled CBN reaches the blood within minutes, while oral products are absorbed slowly and variably, with first-pass metabolism in the liver reducing how much reaches the circulation and food fat increasing absorption. In the blood it is highly protein bound and distributes into fatty tissues, which gives a long and variable terminal elimination. Laboratory studies implicate CYP2C9 and CYP3A4 in oxidizing CBN to hydroxylated metabolites, including 11-hydroxy-CBN, which are then conjugated with glucuronic acid. Metabolites are eliminated in feces and urine. Some immunoassay drug screens target THC metabolites, and whether a positive result reflects THC in the product or cross-reactivity depends on the test, so confirmatory testing is what distinguishes them.", "sidefx": "Reported effects are mostly sleepiness, dizziness, dry mouth and next-morning grogginess. The larger concerns come from combinations and product quality. Many CBN sleep products also contain THC, CBD or melatonin, so impairment, slowed reaction time and unsafe driving are possible, and labels frequently do not match lab-tested content. Added sedation with opioids, benzodiazepines, sleep medicines, sedating antihistamines and alcohol raises the risk of falls and, at extremes, slowed breathing. Children who eat candy-like gummies can become deeply sleepy, mostly from THC content.", "fun": "Because THC slowly turns into CBN with time, light and air, the ratio of CBN to THC in a cannabis sample has been used by forensic chemists as a rough clue to how old or how poorly stored the sample is.", "src": ["Wood TB, Spivey WTN and Easterfield TH 1899, Journal of the Chemical Society, cannabinol", "Russo EB and Marcu J 2017, Advances in Pharmacology, Cannabis pharmacology: the usual suspects and a few promising leads", "NIH NCCIH: Cannabis (Marijuana) and Cannabinoids", "FDA: Regulation of Cannabis and Cannabis-Derived Products", "Congressional Research Service 2025, Change to Federal Definition of Hemp and Implications for Federal Enforcement"]}, "delta-8-thc": {"origin": "Delta-8 THC was described by chemists in the mid-twentieth century as a minor, more chemically stable isomer of delta-9 THC, and it occurs naturally in cannabis only in trace amounts. Raphael Mechoulam's group in Israel, which elucidated the structure of delta-9 THC in 1964, also studied delta-8, and a small 1990s Israeli study tested it against chemotherapy nausea in children; it was otherwise a laboratory curiosity. Its commercial life began after the 2018 Farm Bill removed hemp with low delta-9 THC content from the Controlled Substances Act. The hemp industry suddenly held a surplus of CBD, and manufacturers found that acid treatment converts CBD into delta-8 THC, an intoxicating compound that sellers argued counted as legal hemp because the law named only delta-9. Gummies, vapes and candies followed in gas stations and smoke shops, often in packaging that copied familiar snacks. Poison centers began reporting rising exposures, many unintentional ingestions in young children; the CDC issued a health alert in 2021; and FDA published consumer warnings and, with the FTC, sent warning letters about copycat edibles. In 2025 Congress narrowed the federal hemp definition to exclude synthesized cannabinoids, with the change taking effect in November 2026, although state laws and enforcement remain a patchwork.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Mechoulam's group defines the structure of delta-9 THC and studies the delta-8 isomer."], ["2018", "The Farm Bill removes low-delta-9 hemp from federal control, creating the market loophole for delta-8."], ["2021", "The CDC issues a health alert on delta-8 adverse events, and FDA publishes consumer warnings."], ["2023", "FDA and the FTC send warning letters to companies selling delta-8 edibles that mimic snack brands."], ["2025", "A federal law narrows the hemp definition to exclude synthesized cannabinoids, effective November 2026."]], "cell": "Delta-8 THC is a tricyclic terpenophenolic cannabinoid identical to delta-9 THC except that the double bond in the cyclohexene ring sits between carbons 8 and 9 rather than 9 and 10. Like delta-9, it binds the CB1 receptor, a Gi/o-coupled receptor concentrated on presynaptic terminals in the cortex, hippocampus, basal ganglia and cerebellum. Activation inhibits adenylyl cyclase, opens potassium channels and closes voltage-gated calcium channels, which reduces release of glutamate and GABA. This retrograde brake on synaptic transmission explains impaired short-term memory, slowed reaction time, poor coordination, altered time perception, euphoria and increased appetite. Binding studies and animal work suggest somewhat lower potency than delta-9, but the difference is modest and the products are frequently stronger than labeled. CB2 receptors on immune cells are also partially activated. Peripheral effects include tachycardia from sympathetic activation and vasodilation that causes red eyes and orthostatic dizziness. The chemistry of conversion matters: acid-catalyzed cyclization of CBD produces a mixture that can include delta-9 THC, delta-10 THC, other isomers and incompletely characterized by-products, so the pharmacology of a given product is not simply that of pure delta-8.", "body": [["Brain", "CB1 activation causes euphoria, sedation, memory and coordination impairment; large pediatric ingestions can cause coma."], ["Heart and vessels", "Tachycardia and orthostatic hypotension from sympathetic activation and vasodilation."], ["Lungs", "Vaped products carry the inhalation risks of cannabis vapes, including additive-related lung injury."], ["Gut", "Increased appetite and dry mouth; chronic heavy use is linked to cannabinoid hyperemesis syndrome."], ["Liver", "Converts delta-8 to an active 11-hydroxy metabolite that prolongs and strengthens edible effects."]], "journey": "Inhaled delta-8 reaches the lungs and brain within minutes and wears off over a few hours. Edibles are absorbed slowly and unpredictably through the gut, and much of the dose passes through the liver first, where CYP2C9 and CYP3A4 convert it to 11-hydroxy-delta-8-THC, an active metabolite that crosses into the brain readily. This first-pass step is why edibles feel different, start late and last long, and why people sometimes take a second serving before the first has worked. The drug is highly lipophilic and highly protein bound, distributes into fat, and redistributes slowly, giving a long terminal half-life in regular users. Further oxidation produces the inactive carboxylic acid metabolite, which is glucuronidated and excreted in feces and urine; standard THC immunoassays cross-react with these metabolites, so delta-8 users typically test positive.", "sidefx": "Common effects mirror marijuana: sleepiness, dizziness, dry mouth, red eyes, hunger, rapid heart rate, anxiety and slowed reactions. Higher exposures produce vomiting, confusion, panic, hallucinations and low blood pressure. Children are the most vulnerable group because of their small size and because candy-like products contain several adult servings; reported pediatric cases include deep sedation, poor muscle tone, slowed breathing and, occasionally, seizures or intensive care admission. Regular heavy use can be followed by cannabinoid hyperemesis syndrome and by withdrawal irritability and insomnia. Unknown by-products of the conversion process add uncertainty that does not exist for regulated drugs.", "fun": "Almost no delta-8 on store shelves came out of a plant: it was made in a reactor from CBD, which means a compound sold as natural hemp is, chemically, a semi-synthetic product, which is the exact reason Congress later moved to exclude synthesized cannabinoids from the hemp definition.", "src": ["FDA: 5 Things to Know about Delta-8 Tetrahydrocannabinol", "CDC Health Alert Network (2021): delta-8 THC adverse events", "America's Poison Centers: delta-8 THC exposure data"]}, "thcv": {"origin": "Tetrahydrocannabivarin was identified around 1970 as a propyl-chain homolog of THC in cannabis resin, part of the wave of cannabinoid chemistry that followed the structural work of Raphael Mechoulam's group in the 1960s. Cannabinoids are built by the plant from a fatty acid starter unit; most use a five-carbon pentyl chain, which gives THC and CBD, while varin cannabinoids such as THCV and CBDV use a three-carbon propyl chain. Some landrace strains from southern Africa and Asia were found to carry more varins than typical drug cannabis. Pharmacologists, notably Roger Pertwee's group in Aberdeen, showed in the 2000s that THCV could block CB1 receptors at low concentrations, which made it interesting at a time when the CB1 inverse agonist rimonabant was being sold in Europe for obesity. Rimonabant was suspended in Europe in 2008 because of depression and suicidality, and never reached the US market. THCV attracted research as a possibly gentler way to dampen CB1 signaling, leading to small human studies including a pilot randomized trial in type 2 diabetes. Since the 2018 hemp boom, THCV has been marketed as diet weed or an energizing cannabinoid, often blended with THC and sometimes made synthetically from CBD.", "timeline": [["Around 1970", "Chemists identify THCV as the propyl homolog of THC in cannabis resin."], ["2000s", "Pharmacology studies show CB1 antagonist activity at low concentrations."], ["2008", "The CB1 blocker rimonabant is suspended in Europe for psychiatric adverse effects, shaping interest in gentler CB1 modulators."], ["2016", "A small randomized pilot trial reports glucose effects of THCV in type 2 diabetes."], ["2020s", "THCV gummies and vapes are marketed for appetite control in the hemp market."]], "cell": "THCV shares the tricyclic dibenzopyran core of THC, but its side chain is propyl rather than pentyl. Because the pentyl chain contributes strongly to CB1 binding and activation, shortening it changes behavior: in many receptor assays THCV binds CB1 but fails to activate it, blocking the effect of other agonists, so it behaves as a neutral antagonist, without suppressing the receptor's baseline activity as rimonabant does. At higher concentrations in animals, THCV can produce THC-like agonist effects. At CB2 receptors on immune cells THCV acts as a partial agonist, and preclinical work also describes activity at TRPV1 and other TRP channels and at 5-HT1A receptors. The appetite story rests on the observation that CB1 activation increases food intake and reward, so blocking CB1 might do the reverse; in rodents THCV reduced food intake and improved insulin sensitivity. Whether ordinary human exposure reaches the receptor occupancy needed for these effects is unknown, and the product content, which often includes THC, frequently overrides the pharmacology of THCV itself.", "body": [["Brain", "CB1 blockade at low levels may reduce appetite and reward signaling; higher levels or THC blends cause intoxication."], ["Pancreas and metabolism", "Small human and animal studies suggest effects on glucose control and insulin sensitivity."], ["Immune cells", "CB2 partial agonism with anti-inflammatory effects in laboratory models."], ["Liver", "Likely site of CYP oxidation, as for other cannabinoids (limited data)."]], "journey": "Human pharmacokinetic data are sparse. By analogy to THC, inhaled THCV reaches the circulation within minutes, while oral products are absorbed slowly and undergo first-pass hepatic metabolism, likely by cytochrome P450 enzymes forming hydroxylated and carboxylic acid metabolites. As a lipophilic cannabinoid it is expected to be highly protein bound, to distribute into fat, and to be eliminated slowly in feces and urine. Whether THCV itself cross-reacts with THC immunoassays is less clear, but because so many products contain delta-9 or delta-8 THC, positive screens are common in users.", "sidefx": "In small studies THCV was generally tolerated, with mild effects such as dry mouth, nausea, dizziness and reduced appetite reported. The larger practical risk is product content: THCV gummies and vapes are frequently blended with delta-9 or delta-8 THC, so users and children who find them can experience sedation, intoxication, tachycardia and anxiety. Synthetic production and unregulated manufacturing add uncertainty about contaminants. Because rimonabant, a stronger CB1 blocker, caused depression and suicidality, any psychiatric effect of long-term CB1 antagonism remains an open question for THCV.", "fun": "The plant chooses between THC and THCV at the very first step: the same enzymes build both, and the only difference is whether the starter fatty acid has three carbons or five, so a two-carbon difference at the start turns a receptor activator into a receptor blocker.", "src": ["Pertwee 2008, British Journal of Pharmacology, review of THC, CBD and THCV pharmacology", "Jadoon et al. 2016, Diabetes Care, THCV and CBD in type 2 diabetes", "NIH NCCIH: Cannabis (Marijuana) and Cannabinoids", "Christensen et al. 2007, Lancet, meta-analysis of rimonabant psychiatric adverse effects"]}, "cannabidiol": {"origin": "Cannabidiol was isolated in 1940, when Roger Adams's group in Illinois extracted it from wild Minnesota hemp and, around the same time, Alexander Todd's group in Britain worked on cannabis constituents; its correct structure was determined by Raphael Mechoulam and Yuval Shvo in 1963. For decades CBD was seen as the inactive partner of THC. Interest in seizures came from animal studies and small human studies in the 1970s and 1980s, then exploded in 2013 when media coverage of Charlotte Figi, a Colorado child with Dravet syndrome who reportedly improved on a high-CBD cannabis oil, drove families to seek CBD. GW Pharmaceuticals in the UK developed a purified, standardized plant-derived oral solution and ran randomized controlled trials in Dravet and Lennox-Gastaut syndromes. In June 2018 FDA approved Epidiolex, the first FDA-approved drug purified from the cannabis plant; the indication was expanded to tuberous sclerosis complex in 2020, and the DEA removed Epidiolex from federal control the same year. Meanwhile the 2018 Farm Bill legalized hemp, and a large consumer CBD market appeared. FDA has maintained that because CBD was first studied as a drug, it cannot lawfully be sold as a dietary supplement or food additive, and in 2023 the agency said a new regulatory pathway would be needed.", "timeline": [["1940", "Roger Adams's group isolates cannabidiol from hemp."], ["1963", "Mechoulam and Shvo determine the structure of CBD."], ["2013", "Media coverage of a child with Dravet syndrome drives demand for high-CBD cannabis oil."], ["2018", "FDA approves Epidiolex for Lennox-Gastaut and Dravet syndromes; the Farm Bill legalizes hemp."], ["2020", "Epidiolex is approved for tuberous sclerosis complex and removed from federal control."], ["2023", "FDA states existing food and supplement frameworks are not appropriate for CBD and calls for a new pathway."]], "cell": "Unlike THC, CBD barely activates the CB1 receptor, which is why it does not cause a high. Instead it touches many targets at micromolar concentrations. It is a negative allosteric modulator of CB1, changing how other agonists bind. It antagonizes GPR55, an orphan G protein-coupled receptor that increases presynaptic calcium and glutamate release, so blocking it can quiet excitatory transmission. It activates and then desensitizes TRPV1 channels, reducing calcium influx. It inhibits the equilibrative nucleoside transporter ENT1, raising extracellular adenosine, an endogenous anticonvulsant and sedative that acts on A1 receptors. It enhances 5-HT1A receptor signaling, which has been linked to anxiety effects in animals. Which of these matter for seizure control is not settled, and the Epidiolex label states the mechanism is unknown. CBD also inhibits several drug-metabolizing enzymes, notably CYP2C19, plus CYP2C9, CYP3A4 and UGT enzymes in vitro, and inhibits P-glycoprotein at the gut, which underlie its interaction profile. Liver enzyme elevations appear dose-related and are amplified by valproate, although the cellular mechanism of that synergy is still being studied.", "body": [["Brain", "Reduces neuronal hyperexcitability and seizure frequency in specific syndromes; causes somnolence, especially with clobazam."], ["Liver", "Dose-related transaminase elevations, more frequent with valproate; inhibits CYP2C19 and other enzymes."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea and decreased appetite are common; high-fat meals greatly increase absorption."], ["Skin and immune system", "Rash and hypersensitivity reactions are labeled; the oral solution contains sesame seed oil."], ["Blood", "INR increases with warfarin have been reported, consistent with altered warfarin metabolism."]], "journey": "Oral CBD has low and variable bioavailability because it is poorly water soluble and undergoes extensive first-pass metabolism. Epidiolex is dissolved in sesame oil, and taking it with a high-fat meal increases peak concentration and total exposure several-fold, which is why the label calls for consistent timing relative to food. Peak levels occur roughly 2.5 to 5 hours after a dose. CBD is more than 94 percent protein bound and distributes widely into fat and tissue. It is metabolized in the liver and gut by CYP2C19 and CYP3A4, and by UGT1A7, UGT1A9 and UGT2B7. The main active metabolite is 7-hydroxy-CBD, which is further oxidized to 7-carboxy-CBD, the most abundant circulating metabolite and largely inactive. Elimination is mostly fecal, with an effective half-life of about 56 to 61 hours after twice-daily dosing, so steady state takes several days.", "sidefx": "In the Epidiolex trials the most common adverse effects were somnolence, decreased appetite, diarrhea, transaminase elevations, fatigue, malaise, asthenia, rash, insomnia and infections. Transaminase elevations were dose-related and markedly more frequent in patients also on valproate; most resolved with dose changes, but monitoring is required. Somnolence is amplified by clobazam because CBD raises the active metabolite N-desmethylclobazam. Hypersensitivity reactions, suicidal ideation (a class warning for all antiseizure drugs) and withdrawal seizures are labeled. Hemp products add risks of mislabeled content, THC contamination causing intoxication or positive drug tests, and unpredictable interactions with warfarin, tacrolimus and other drugs.", "fun": "Because FDA approved CBD as a drug ingredient before it was marketed in supplements, a long-standing rule in food and drug law, the drug exclusion clause, is the main reason a chemical sold on gas station counters is still not legally a dietary supplement.", "src": ["Epidiolex FDA prescribing information", "Devinsky et al. 2017, New England Journal of Medicine: CBD in Dravet syndrome trial", "Thiele et al. 2018, Lancet: CBD in Lennox-Gastaut syndrome trial", "Bonn-Miller et al. 2017, JAMA: labeling accuracy of cannabidiol extracts sold online", "NIH LiverTox: Cannabidiol"]}, "hemp-seed-oil": {"origin": "Hemp has been grown for fiber and seed for thousands of years in China and elsewhere, and hemp seed was eaten as a grain and pressed for oil long before its cousin varieties were bred for resin. In the United States, hemp cultivation was effectively ended by the Marihuana Tax Act of 1937 and later by the Controlled Substances Act of 1970, although sterilized imported seed and oil remained available. In the 1990s and early 2000s, hemp foods became popular, and studies showed that some products caused positive THC urine tests; the cause was resin from the flowers contaminating the outside of the seed, not THC within the seed, and improved cleaning reduced the problem. The DEA tried in 2001 to ban hemp foods containing any THC, but a federal appeals court blocked the rule in 2004 in a case brought by the Hemp Industries Association. After the 2018 Farm Bill legalized hemp, FDA responded with no questions to three GRAS notices covering hulled hemp seed, hemp seed protein powder and hemp seed oil, confirming these as foods. The result is a product that is nutritionally ordinary, but whose name is easily confused with CBD and THC products.", "timeline": [["Ancient times", "Hemp seed is eaten and pressed for oil in China and other regions."], ["1937", "The Marihuana Tax Act ends practical US hemp farming."], ["2004", "A federal appeals court blocks a DEA rule that would have banned hemp seed foods."], ["2018", "The Farm Bill legalizes hemp, and FDA raises no questions about GRAS notices for hemp seed oil, hulled seed and protein."]], "cell": "Hemp seed oil is a triglyceride oil dominated by polyunsaturated fatty acids. Linoleic acid, an omega-6 fatty acid, and alpha-linolenic acid, an omega-3, occur in roughly a 3 to 1 ratio, which nutrition writers often highlight. It also contains gamma-linolenic acid and stearidonic acid, which are one desaturation step further along their pathways than linoleic and alpha-linolenic acid, plus tocopherols and phytosterols. After digestion these fatty acids are incorporated into the phospholipids of cell membranes, where they influence membrane fluidity and serve as substrates for cyclooxygenase and lipoxygenase enzymes. Linoleic acid can be converted to arachidonic acid, the parent of many inflammatory eicosanoids, while gamma-linolenic acid can be converted to dihomo-gamma-linolenic acid, which produces less inflammatory prostaglandins. Alpha-linolenic acid is converted only inefficiently to EPA and very little to DHA, so plant omega-3 oils are not equivalent to fish oil. In the skin, linoleic acid is an important component of ceramides in the barrier layer, which is the rationale for studies in eczema. None of these effects involve cannabinoid receptors, because the seed contains little or no THC or CBD.", "body": [["Skin", "Linoleic acid supports the skin barrier; small trials suggest reduced dryness and itching in eczema."], ["Heart and blood lipids", "Replacing saturated fat with polyunsaturated oils can modestly improve LDL; hemp-specific trials show small or no effects."], ["Gut", "Digested like other dietary fats; large amounts can loosen stools."], ["Brain", "No cannabinoid effects; no intoxication from properly cleaned seed oil."]], "journey": "Hemp seed oil is digested like other dietary fats: pancreatic lipase splits the triglycerides into fatty acids and monoglycerides, which bile salts carry into intestinal cells. There they are reassembled into triglycerides and packaged into chylomicrons that enter the lymph and then the blood. Tissues take up the fatty acids for energy or for building membranes and signaling lipids; the liver repackages some into lipoproteins. Fatty acids are ultimately oxidized for energy and leave the body as carbon dioxide and water. Changes in membrane fatty acid composition develop over weeks of consistent intake.", "sidefx": "As a food, hemp seed oil is well tolerated. Large amounts can cause loose stools or stomach upset, as with any oil. Allergy to hemp seed is uncommon but reported, sometimes in people sensitized to cannabis. The main practical problems are confusion and contamination: products labeled hemp oil may be CBD extracts that carry their own effects and interactions, and older or poorly cleaned seed products occasionally contained enough THC on the seed surface to cause a positive urine screen. Because the oil is highly unsaturated, it oxidizes quickly with heat and light, producing off flavors.", "fun": "The THC that once turned up in hemp seed products was not inside the seed at all: it was resin from the plant's flowers stuck to the shell, which is why better washing and hulling solved most of the drug-test problem.", "src": ["FDA GRAS notice responses for hemp seed-derived ingredients, 2018", "Callaway 2004, Euphytica: hempseed as a nutritional resource", "Callaway et al. 2005, Journal of Dermatological Treatment: hempseed oil in atopic dermatitis", "Leson et al. 2001, Journal of Analytical Toxicology: THC in hemp foods and urine testing"]}, "beta-caryophyllene": {"origin": "Caryophyllene takes its name from the clove, Syzygium aromaticum, whose older botanical name was Caryophyllus aromaticus, and it was one of the first sesquiterpenes studied by nineteenth-century essential oil chemists. Its strained structure, with a four-membered cyclobutane ring fused to a nine-membered ring, took decades to define, and it became a classic target for synthetic chemists; E. J. Corey's group reported a total synthesis in 1964. For most of the twentieth century it was simply a flavor and fragrance ingredient, permitted as a flavoring in foods and found in black pepper, cloves, hops, rosemary, oregano and cannabis. In 2008 Jürg Gertsch and colleagues in Switzerland reported in PNAS that beta-caryophyllene binds and activates the CB2 cannabinoid receptor selectively and reduces inflammation in mice, and they called it a dietary cannabinoid. That finding made it the first widely eaten food compound shown to act directly on a cannabinoid receptor. Since then it has appeared in terpene supplements and in CBD products marketed around the so-called entourage effect, although human trials remain scarce.", "timeline": [["19th century", "Chemists isolate caryophyllene from clove oil."], ["1964", "Corey's group reports a total synthesis of caryophyllene."], ["2008", "Gertsch and colleagues describe BCP as a selective CB2 agonist and dietary cannabinoid."], ["2010s", "Animal studies explore BCP in pain, colitis and inflammation; terpene supplements appear."]], "cell": "The CB2 receptor is a Gi/o-coupled receptor expressed mainly on immune cells, including macrophages, microglia and B cells, with low expression in most neurons. Beta-caryophyllene fits a binding pocket in CB2 and activates it, inhibiting adenylyl cyclase and activating MAP kinase pathways. In macrophages this reduces production of inflammatory mediators such as tumor necrosis factor alpha and interleukin-1 beta, and in animal models it reduced inflammatory pain and colitis, effects that disappeared in CB2 knockout mice, which is the strongest evidence that the receptor mediates them. Because BCP has little affinity for CB1, the receptor responsible for THC intoxication, it does not produce psychoactive effects. Some studies also describe activation of PPAR-alpha and PPAR-gamma, nuclear receptors that regulate lipid metabolism and inflammation. Caryophyllene oxide, formed when the double bonds of BCP react with oxygen in air, lacks meaningful CB2 activity; it is the form found in aged oils and is partly responsible for the sensitizing potential of oxidized essential oils. Many cell findings use concentrations far above what dietary intake produces, so translation to human effects is uncertain.", "body": [["Immune system", "CB2 activation on macrophages and microglia reduces inflammatory cytokines in laboratory models."], ["Gut", "Reduced colitis in animal studies; spice-level intake is common."], ["Nervous system", "Reduced inflammatory and neuropathic pain in animals; no CB1-mediated intoxication."], ["Skin", "Oxidized terpene oils containing caryophyllene oxide can cause allergic contact dermatitis."]], "journey": "Human pharmacokinetic data are limited. BCP is lipophilic and volatile, with modest oral absorption that formulation studies have tried to improve. In the liver it is oxidized, including to caryophyllene oxide and hydroxylated and acid metabolites, which are conjugated and eliminated in urine and feces. Animal studies show distribution into tissues, including the brain. Food-level intake from black pepper and herbs is small but routine, and concentrated supplements deliver more, with no established clinical significance.", "sidefx": "As a food component BCP is considered low risk. Concentrated products have little human safety data; stomach upset is the most plausible effect. The best-documented adverse effect relates to oxidation: aged essential oils containing caryophyllene oxide and other oxidized terpenes can cause allergic contact dermatitis. Essential oils as a group are a poisoning hazard for small children, who can develop vomiting, drowsiness and aspiration pneumonitis after swallowing them. Because many BCP products are blended with CBD or THC, sedation or intoxication in a user usually comes from the other ingredients.", "fun": "Anyone who has eaten black pepper has already taken a cannabinoid receptor agonist: beta-caryophyllene was the first common food compound shown to activate a cannabinoid receptor directly, and it does so without any high.", "src": ["Gertsch et al. 2008, PNAS, Beta-caryophyllene is a dietary cannabinoid", "Corey, Mitra and Uda 1964, Journal of the American Chemical Society, total synthesis of caryophyllene", "Sköld et al. 2006, Food and Chemical Toxicology, air oxidation and skin sensitization of beta-caryophyllene", "Fidyt et al. 2016, Cancer Medicine, review of beta-caryophyllene and caryophyllene oxide"]}, "lions-mane": {"origin": "Hericium erinaceus grows on dead or dying hardwood trees in North America, Europe and Asia, forming cascades of white spines that suggest a lion's mane or a pom-pom, and it has been eaten as a delicacy in Chinese and Japanese cooking for centuries, where it is called hou tou gu or yamabushitake. Its modern reputation as a brain mushroom comes from work by Hirokazu Kawagishi and colleagues in Japan, who in the early 1990s isolated aromatic compounds called hericenones from the fruiting body and reported that they stimulated nerve growth factor synthesis in cultured cells, and in 1994 described erinacines, diterpenoids from the mycelium, with stronger effects in animal studies. Nerve growth factor, discovered by Rita Levi-Montalcini, who shared the 1986 Nobel Prize for it, supports survival of cholinergic neurons involved in memory, which made a food that might raise it attractive in the search for dementia treatments. A small double-blind trial published in 2009 in older Japanese adults with mild cognitive impairment reported improved cognitive scores that faded after the supplement was stopped. Since the 2010s lion's mane has become a staple of nootropic stacks and mushroom coffee, far ahead of the human evidence.", "timeline": [["Centuries ago", "Lion's mane is eaten as a delicacy in China and Japan."], ["1986", "Rita Levi-Montalcini and Stanley Cohen share the Nobel Prize for discovering growth factors including NGF."], ["Early 1990s", "Kawagishi's group isolates hericenones from the fruiting body."], ["1994", "Erinacines from the mycelium are reported to stimulate NGF synthesis."], ["2009", "A small Japanese trial in mild cognitive impairment reports cognitive score improvements."]], "cell": "Nerve growth factor is a neurotrophin that binds TrkA receptors on cholinergic basal forebrain neurons and sensory neurons, activating PI3K-Akt and MAP kinase pathways that promote survival, neurite growth and synaptic maintenance. NGF itself is a protein that does not cross the blood-brain barrier, so small molecules that make brain cells produce more of it are attractive. In cultured astrocytes, hericenones were reported to increase NGF secretion, and erinacines, particularly erinacine A, increased NGF levels in the brains of rats and supported neurite outgrowth, possibly via JNK signaling. Erinacine A also reduced amyloid plaque burden and neuronal loss in mouse models of Alzheimer disease and protected dopaminergic neurons in Parkinson disease models. Lion's mane polysaccharides, mainly beta-glucans, modulate immune cells and the gut microbiome in animals. Important caveats apply: some later cell studies did not reproduce NGF induction by hericenones, the amounts used in animals often exceed plausible human intake, and the compounds' brain penetration in humans has not been measured.", "body": [["Brain", "NGF-related neuron support and reduced Alzheimer-type pathology in animal models; human cognitive effects uncertain."], ["Peripheral nerves", "Faster nerve regeneration after injury in rodent studies."], ["Gut", "Polysaccharides alter gut microbes and protect the stomach lining in animal studies."], ["Immune system", "Beta-glucans activate immune cells in laboratory studies."]], "journey": "Human pharmacokinetic data do not exist for hericenones or erinacines. In rats, erinacine A is absorbed after oral dosing, has limited bioavailability, and has been detected in the brain, supporting the idea that it can cross the blood-brain barrier. Hericenones are relatively lipophilic aromatic compounds whose metabolism is not characterized. Polysaccharides are not absorbed intact; they interact with gut immune tissue and are fermented by colonic bacteria. Because commercial products vary between fruiting body, mycelium and mycelium grown on grain, the content of each compound differs greatly between products.", "sidefx": "Lion's mane is eaten as a food and appears well tolerated in small trials, with mild stomach upset the most commonly mentioned effect. Rare allergic reactions are reported, including skin rash and respiratory symptoms, and people allergic to other mushrooms may be at higher risk. Laboratory studies describing antiplatelet and glucose-lowering effects have led to theoretical cautions with anticoagulants and diabetes medications, but no clinical interactions are established. Product quality is a practical concern: mycelium grown on grain may be largely starch, and mushroom coffee blends add caffeine.", "fun": "The compounds most linked to lion's mane's brain reputation come from two different parts of the organism: hericenones are found in the spiny fruiting body people cook, while erinacines, the ones with the stronger animal data, are found mainly in the hidden mycelium.", "src": ["Kawagishi et al. 1994, Tetrahedron Letters, erinacines A to C", "Mori et al. 2009, Phytotherapy Research, randomized trial in mild cognitive impairment", "Li et al. 2018, Behavioural Neurology, review of Hericium erinaceus neurohealth properties", "Memorial Sloan Kettering, About Herbs: Hericium erinaceus"]}, "hericenones": {"origin": "Hericenones and erinacines were discovered by Hirokazu Kawagishi and colleagues at Shizuoka University in Japan, who were screening edible mushrooms for compounds that could stimulate nerve growth factor, the neurotrophin discovered by Rita Levi-Montalcini. In the early 1990s they reported hericenones, aromatic compounds built on a resorcinol ring with a geranyl side chain, from the fruiting body of Hericium erinaceus, and showed that several stimulated NGF synthesis in cultured mouse astrocytes. In 1994 the same group described erinacines A, B and C from cultured mycelium, which belonged to an entirely different structural class, the cyathane diterpenoids, and reported more potent NGF-stimulating activity. Over the following decades, Taiwanese and Japanese groups studied erinacine A in rodents, reporting increased brain NGF, protection of dopaminergic neurons, reduced amyloid plaques and improved behavior in Alzheimer models, and detection of the compound in rat brain after oral dosing. Meanwhile, a 2014 study testing purified hericenones in a human astrocytoma cell line did not find increased NGF gene expression, a reminder that early findings need replication. Erinacine A-enriched mycelium has since been tried in small human pilot studies, with preliminary results.", "timeline": [["Early 1990s", "Kawagishi's group isolates hericenones from lion's mane fruiting bodies."], ["1994", "Erinacines A to C are described from lion's mane mycelium."], ["2000s to 2010s", "Rodent studies report increased brain NGF and neuroprotection with erinacine A."], ["2014", "A cell study fails to reproduce NGF gene induction by several hericenones."], ["2020s", "Small human pilot studies test erinacine A-enriched mycelium."]], "cell": "The proposed shared endpoint is increased NGF, which binds TrkA receptors and activates Ras-MAP kinase and PI3K-Akt cascades to promote neuron survival and neurite outgrowth. Hericenones are lipophilic aromatic molecules whose exact target is unknown; in astrocyte cultures some raised NGF secretion, while others enhanced neurite outgrowth in PC12 cells only when NGF was also present, suggesting they may potentiate rather than replace NGF signaling. Erinacines are tricyclic cyathane diterpenoids, and erinacine A appears to increase NGF and other neurotrophic signals in the brain, with studies implicating JNK pathway activation, reduced inflammatory microglial activation, lower oxidative stress and reduced endoplasmic reticulum stress in neuronal models. In Alzheimer mouse models, erinacine A reduced amyloid plaque burden, perhaps by increasing insulin-degrading enzyme, and in Parkinson models it protected dopaminergic neurons from toxin injury. These effects were demonstrated with purified compounds or enriched mycelium at amounts not directly comparable to typical human supplement use, and receptor-level mechanisms remain undefined.", "body": [["Brain", "Erinacine A raised NGF, reduced amyloid plaques and protected dopamine neurons in rodents."], ["Astrocytes", "Hericenones stimulated NGF secretion in some cell studies, not reproduced in others."], ["Peripheral nerves", "Lion's mane extracts sped nerve regeneration in rodent injury models."], ["Liver", "Likely site of metabolism (not characterized in humans)."]], "journey": "Human pharmacokinetics are unknown. In rats, orally given erinacine A is absorbed with limited bioavailability, distributes to tissues, and has been detected in brain tissue, supporting blood-brain barrier penetration. Hericenone absorption and metabolism have not been characterized. Content differs sharply by product: fruiting body products carry hericenones and little or no erinacine, while mycelium products contain erinacines, often diluted by the grain on which the mycelium grows, and only some products are standardized and tested.", "sidefx": "No adverse effects specific to purified hericenones or erinacines have been characterized in humans. Toxicology studies of erinacine A-enriched mycelium in rats did not find significant organ toxicity at the amounts tested. The adverse effects relevant in practice are those of lion's mane products: mild stomach upset and rare allergic reactions. Theoretical cautions with anticoagulants and diabetes medications come from laboratory and animal data on whole lion's mane, not on these compounds specifically.", "fun": "Lion's mane makes its two famous compound families in different places: the hericenones in the shaggy fruiting body that cooks buy, and the erinacines in the mycelium hidden in wood, so a chef eating lion's mane gets almost none of the compounds behind the strongest animal brain data.", "src": ["Kawagishi et al. 1991, Tetrahedron Letters, hericenones C, D and E", "Kawagishi et al. 1994, Tetrahedron Letters, erinacines A to C", "Phan et al. 2014, Food and Function, hericenones and NGF gene expression in astrocytoma cells", "Li et al. 2018, Behavioural Neurology, review of Hericium erinaceus neurohealth properties"]}, "reishi": {"origin": "Lingzhi, the Chinese name for Ganoderma, appears in the Shennong Bencao Jing, the classic Chinese materia medica compiled around the Han dynasty, where it was ranked among the superior herbs thought to promote longevity. It became a recurring symbol of immortality in Chinese art, carved into jade and painted beside sages and deer, and in Japan it was called reishi. Wild reishi was rare and costly, and the shiny, lacquered shelf mushrooms were prized as gifts. Reliable cultivation on logs and sawdust was developed in the twentieth century, and large-scale production in Japan and China from the 1970s turned reishi into a mass-market supplement. Japanese and Chinese chemists isolated dozens of triterpenes, including ganoderic acids, and immune-active polysaccharides in the 1980s, which helped frame reishi as an immunomodulator and anticancer adjunct. Western interest grew through integrative oncology and the wellness market. Systematic reviews by the Cochrane Collaboration later concluded that evidence was insufficient to support reishi for cancer treatment and showed no significant benefit for cardiovascular risk factors. Molecular studies also revealed that many products sold as Ganoderma lucidum are actually other Ganoderma species, and the East Asian lingzhi is now often classified as Ganoderma lingzhi.", "timeline": [["About 200 CE", "The Shennong Bencao Jing ranks lingzhi among the superior herbs."], ["1970s", "Commercial cultivation expands in Japan and China."], ["1980s", "Ganoderic acids and immune-active polysaccharides are isolated."], ["2000s", "Case reports describe liver injury with reishi powder products."], ["2015-2016", "Cochrane reviews find insufficient evidence for cancer and cardiovascular use."]], "cell": "Reishi contains two main active groups. Its polysaccharides, chiefly beta-1,3-glucans with beta-1,6 branches, are large molecules recognized by pattern recognition receptors on innate immune cells, such as Dectin-1 and complement receptor 3 on macrophages and dendritic cells, and toll-like receptors 2 and 4. Binding triggers signaling through Syk kinase and NF-kappa B, increasing cytokine release, phagocytosis and antigen presentation and activating natural killer and T cells in laboratory studies. Its triterpenes, a family of highly oxidized lanostane compounds including ganoderic and lucidenic acids, account for the bitterness and have been reported in vitro to inhibit HMG-CoA reductase, angiotensin-converting enzyme, 5-alpha-reductase and histamine release, and to be cytotoxic to tumor cells at high concentrations. Adenosine and other constituents were reported to inhibit platelet aggregation, which underlies the bleeding caution. The mechanism of the rare liver injury cases is unknown and may involve idiosyncratic reactions, product contamination or concentrated powder formulations.", "body": [["Immune system", "Beta-glucans activate macrophages, dendritic cells and NK cells in laboratory studies; immune marker changes in small human studies."], ["Blood and platelets", "Reduced platelet aggregation in small studies, a theoretical bleeding risk."], ["Heart and vessels", "Possible small blood pressure lowering; Cochrane found no significant effect on cardiovascular risk factors."], ["Liver", "Rare hepatitis case reports with powdered products, including a fatal case."], ["Pancreas and metabolism", "Glucose lowering in animal studies; human effects inconsistent."]], "journey": "Reishi polysaccharides are too large to be absorbed intact in meaningful amounts; they act on immune tissue in the gut wall, including Peyer's patches, and are fermented by colonic bacteria into short-chain fatty acids. Triterpenes are absorbed: in a small human study, ganoderic acids appeared in plasma within about an hour after an extract and were cleared quickly, with short half-lives. Their metabolism is thought to involve hepatic oxidation and conjugation, and elimination routes in humans are not well defined. Food may reduce triterpene absorption. Because products differ between fruiting body, spores and mycelium, and between water and alcohol extracts, exposure to each constituent varies greatly.", "sidefx": "Reported side effects in trials and clinical summaries include dryness of the mouth, throat and nose, stomach upset, dizziness, itching and rash. More serious concerns come from case reports: hepatotoxicity, including a fatal fulminant hepatitis after reishi powder use, and chronic diarrhea attributed to spore powder. Platelet effects make bleeding a theoretical risk with anticoagulants and around surgery, and possible blood pressure and glucose lowering may add to antihypertensives and diabetes drugs. Immune stimulation raises theoretical concern in transplant recipients. Product misidentification and contamination add uncertainty.", "fun": "DNA testing has shown that much of what is sold worldwide as Ganoderma lucidum is a different species: the traditional Chinese lingzhi is now often classified as Ganoderma lingzhi, while true G. lucidum is mainly European, so the name on the bottle and the mushroom inside frequently do not match.", "src": ["Wachtel-Galor et al. 2011, Herbal Medicine: Biomolecular and Clinical Aspects (NCBI Bookshelf), Ganoderma lucidum chapter", "Jin et al. 2016, Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews, Ganoderma lucidum for cancer", "Klupp et al. 2015, Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews, Ganoderma lucidum for cardiovascular risk factors", "Wanmuang et al. 2007, Journal of the Medical Association of Thailand, fatal fulminant hepatitis associated with Ganoderma lucidum powder", "Memorial Sloan Kettering, About Herbs: Reishi Mushroom"]}, "ganoderic-acids": {"origin": "Reishi's bitterness was noted in traditional Chinese texts, which grouped lingzhi varieties by color and taste, but its chemical source was identified only in the twentieth century. In the early 1980s Japanese chemists isolated the first ganoderic acids, named A and B, from Ganoderma lucidum fruiting bodies and showed that they were highly oxygenated lanostane triterpenoids, built from the same 30-carbon lanosterol skeleton that animals use to make cholesterol. Over the following decades, Japanese, Chinese and Taiwanese groups isolated more than a hundred related triterpenes, including ganoderic acids C through Z variants, lucidenic acids and ganoderiols, from fruiting bodies, spores and cultured mycelium. Screening work in the 1980s and 1990s reported inhibition of angiotensin-converting enzyme, cholesterol synthesis and histamine release, and later work focused on cancer cell lines, where some ganoderic acids induced apoptosis and inhibited invasion. Biotechnology groups have worked on increasing triterpene production in submerged mycelium culture. Human research remains limited to small pharmacokinetic studies showing that some ganoderic acids are absorbed and cleared quickly.", "timeline": [["Early 1980s", "Japanese chemists isolate ganoderic acids A and B from reishi."], ["1980s", "Studies report ACE and cholesterol synthesis inhibition by reishi triterpenes in vitro."], ["1990s-2000s", "More than a hundred related triterpenes are identified."], ["2012", "A small human study measures plasma ganoderic acids after a reishi extract."]], "cell": "Ganoderic acids share a tetracyclic lanostane core with multiple ketone, hydroxyl and carboxylic acid groups. Their structural resemblance to lanosterol and cholesterol intermediates may explain reported inhibition of HMG-CoA reductase and of lanosterol demethylation in cholesterol synthesis in vitro. Some ganoderic acids inhibited angiotensin-converting enzyme in cell-free assays, and others inhibited 5-alpha-reductase, the enzyme that converts testosterone to dihydrotestosterone, prompting interest in prostate enlargement. In tumor cell lines, ganoderic acids such as A, T and DM reduced NF-kappa B and AP-1 activity, lowered matrix metalloproteinase expression, arrested the cell cycle and triggered mitochondrial apoptosis. In animal models of liver injury, triterpene fractions lowered liver enzymes and oxidative stress markers. These findings come mostly from purified compounds at micromolar concentrations, higher than levels measured in human plasma after oral extracts. Because there are many structurally similar ganoderic acids with different activities, results for one cannot be generalized to all, and standardized products report total triterpenes rather than specific compounds.", "body": [["Liver", "Hepatoprotective effects in animal injury models; whole reishi products rarely linked to hepatitis in humans."], ["Cholesterol metabolism", "HMG-CoA reductase inhibition in vitro, not shown clinically."], ["Blood vessels", "ACE inhibition in vitro; human blood pressure effects not established for purified compounds."], ["Prostate", "5-alpha-reductase inhibition in laboratory studies."], ["Tumor cells", "Apoptosis and reduced invasion in cell lines at high concentrations."]], "journey": "In a small study in healthy volunteers, ganoderic acids A and F were absorbed rapidly after an oral reishi extract, reaching peak plasma levels within about half an hour to an hour, and were eliminated quickly with short half-lives; food reduced and delayed absorption. Animal studies show extensive hepatic metabolism by reduction, oxidation and conjugation, producing many metabolites, with elimination mainly in bile and feces. Alcohol extracts and spore oil concentrate triterpenes, while water extracts and teas mainly carry polysaccharides.", "sidefx": "No adverse effect profile has been defined for purified ganoderic acids in humans. Reishi products cause bitter taste, stomach upset, dry mouth and occasional rash, and rare liver injury case reports exist for powdered reishi, although the responsible constituent is unknown. Theoretical additive effects with statins and antihypertensives follow from enzyme inhibition in vitro but have not been demonstrated clinically.", "fun": "Ganoderic acids are built from lanosterol, the same molecule human cells use as the starting point for cholesterol, so the bitter chemicals of a mushroom long associated with longevity are, structurally, cousins of the cholesterol pathway they were later found to inhibit in the test tube.", "src": ["Kubota et al. 1982, Helvetica Chimica Acta, structures of ganoderic acids A and B", "Teekachunhatean et al. 2012, ISRN Pharmacology, pharmacokinetics of ganoderic acids A and F", "Wachtel-Galor et al. 2011, Herbal Medicine: Biomolecular and Clinical Aspects, Ganoderma lucidum chapter", "Memorial Sloan Kettering, About Herbs: Reishi Mushroom"]}, "turkey-tail": {"origin": "Trametes versicolor is one of the most common fungi in the world, growing in overlapping shelves on dead hardwood, with banded colors that inspired the name turkey tail. It was used in traditional Chinese medicine, where it is called yun zhi, cloud mushroom. Its modern medical history began in Japan in the 1960s, when a chemical engineer at the Kureha Chemical company reportedly became interested after hearing of a neighbor with cancer who used the mushroom; the company screened strains and developed a protein-bound polysaccharide extract from the CM-101 strain, called PSK and sold as Krestin. Japan approved PSK in 1977 as an adjuvant for cancer treatment, and it became one of the best-selling cancer drugs in Japan for years. In China, researchers led by Yang Qingyao developed a related extract, polysaccharopeptide or PSP, in the 1980s. In the United States turkey tail is sold only as a dietary supplement. The National Institutes of Health funded a phase 1 trial at Bastyr University and the University of Minnesota in women with breast cancer after radiation, published in 2012, which reported dose-related immune recovery, and a small veterinary study in dogs with hemangiosarcoma drew public attention the same year.", "timeline": [["Traditional use", "Turkey tail is used in Chinese medicine as yun zhi."], ["1960s", "Kureha begins developing a turkey tail extract in Japan."], ["1977", "Japan approves PSK (Krestin) as a cancer adjuvant."], ["1980s", "PSP is developed in China."], ["2012", "An NIH-funded phase 1 trial reports immune effects in women with breast cancer."]], "cell": "The active components are polysaccharide-peptide complexes, mostly beta-glucans linked covalently to protein, and free beta-glucans in the fungal cell wall. On innate immune cells, these molecules engage pattern recognition receptors that evolved to detect fungi. PSK has been shown to be a toll-like receptor 2 agonist, triggering MyD88 and NF-kappa B signaling in dendritic cells, which then mature, present antigen more effectively and release interleukin-12, steering T cells toward a Th1 antitumor response. Beta-glucans also bind Dectin-1 and complement receptor 3, priming neutrophils and macrophages. In laboratory and animal studies, turkey tail extracts increased natural killer cell activity and partially restored lymphocyte counts after chemotherapy or radiation. Some studies also reported direct effects on tumor cells, such as reduced proliferation and invasion. In the gut, turkey tail polysaccharides act as prebiotics, shifting bacterial populations in small human studies. Whether whole mushroom powders deliver comparable amounts of active polysaccharide-peptide to standardized PSK is uncertain.", "body": [["Immune system", "Dendritic cell maturation and NK cell activation via TLR2 and Dectin-1; immune recovery markers in small human studies."], ["Gut", "Prebiotic shifts in gut bacteria in small studies; bloating and stool changes possible."], ["Bone marrow", "Lymphocyte recovery after radiation reported in a small trial."], ["Tumor tissue", "Antiproliferative effects in laboratory studies; clinical benefit established only for PSK in Japanese trials."]], "journey": "Turkey tail polysaccharides are large molecules that are mostly not absorbed intact. They interact with immune cells in the gut wall, especially in Peyer's patches, where dendritic cells and M cells sample them, and the resulting immune signals spread systemically. Animal studies with labeled PSK suggest some fragments are absorbed and distributed, but human pharmacokinetics are not established. Unabsorbed polysaccharide is fermented by colonic bacteria into short-chain fatty acids or excreted in feces.", "sidefx": "In trials and clinical summaries turkey tail and its extracts have been generally well tolerated. Digestive symptoms such as bloating, gas, diarrhea or constipation are the most commonly reported effects, and allergic reactions are possible. No well-documented pharmacokinetic drug interactions exist, but the effect of immune stimulation on chemotherapy, radiation, checkpoint inhibitors and immunosuppressive therapy has not been defined, which is why supplement use is shared with the treating team. Quality varies, and mycelium grown on grain may be diluted by starch.", "fun": "PSK was developed in Japan reportedly after a company engineer heard about a neighbor with cancer who credited the mushroom, and it went on to become one of the best-selling cancer drugs in Japan for years, an unusual path from folk report to approved drug.", "src": ["NCI PDQ: Medicinal Mushrooms (Health Professional Version)", "Torkelson et al. 2012, ISRN Oncology, phase 1 trial of Trametes versicolor in breast cancer", "Lu et al. 2011, Cancer Research, PSK as a TLR2 agonist", "Memorial Sloan Kettering, About Herbs: Coriolus versicolor"]}, "psk-krestin": {"origin": "PSK was developed by the Kureha Chemical Industry company in Japan in the 1960s and 1970s. According to the company's history, an engineer became interested in turkey tail after hearing that a neighbor with stomach cancer had used it, and researchers screened many strains before selecting CM-101, grown as mycelium in culture and extracted with hot water to yield a protein-bound polysaccharide. Japan's Ministry of Health approved it in 1977 under the brand name Krestin as an adjuvant for cancer treatment, and it became widely prescribed, at times among the top-selling cancer drugs in Japan. Through the 1980s and 1990s Japanese groups ran randomized trials adding PSK to surgery and chemotherapy in gastric, colorectal, esophageal and lung cancers, and later meta-analyses of the gastric and colorectal trials reported improved survival. The mechanism was poorly defined for decades until laboratory work published in 2011 identified PSK as a selective toll-like receptor 2 agonist, giving a molecular explanation for its immune effects. PSK has never been approved by FDA; it is discussed in the National Cancer Institute's PDQ summary on medicinal mushrooms, and turkey tail supplements sold in the US are not the same product.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Kureha begins screening turkey tail strains for an anticancer extract."], ["1977", "Japan approves PSK (Krestin) as an adjuvant cancer therapy."], ["1980s-1990s", "Randomized Japanese trials add PSK to surgery and chemotherapy."], ["2000s", "Meta-analyses report survival benefit in gastric and colorectal cancer."], ["2011", "PSK is identified as a selective TLR2 agonist."]], "cell": "PSK is a mixture of beta-glucan chains, mostly beta-1,4 main chains with beta-1,3 and beta-1,6 branches, covalently bound to protein, with an average molecular weight in the tens of kilodaltons. Its best-defined target is toll-like receptor 2: PSK activated TLR2-expressing reporter cells and dendritic cells, and its effects were lost in TLR2-deficient mice. TLR2 signaling through MyD88 activates NF-kappa B, leading dendritic cells to mature, upregulate costimulatory molecules and release interleukin-12, which drives natural killer cells and Th1 T cells to produce interferon-gamma. In tumor-bearing mice, PSK slowed tumor growth in a T cell- and NK cell-dependent manner. Other reported actions include reversal of tumor-induced immunosuppression mediated by transforming growth factor-beta, reduced tumor cell invasion through effects on matrix metalloproteinases, and antioxidant effects through superoxide dismutase-like activity. PSK does not directly kill most tumor cells at relevant concentrations; its value in trials appears to come from supporting antitumor immunity during and after conventional therapy.", "body": [["Immune system", "TLR2 activation of dendritic cells, IL-12 release and NK and T cell activation."], ["Gut immune tissue", "Main site of first contact for an oral polysaccharide."], ["Tumor microenvironment", "Reduced immunosuppression and invasion in laboratory models."], ["Bone marrow", "Blood count changes in trials are hard to separate from chemotherapy effects."]], "journey": "PSK is given by mouth in Japan. As a large protein-bound polysaccharide it is poorly absorbed intact; animal studies with radiolabeled PSK found that part of the dose is broken down in the gut, with some low-molecular-weight fragments absorbed and distributed to tissues, and remaining material excreted in feces, with some label exhaled or excreted in urine. Its immune effects are thought to arise largely from contact with immune cells in the gut wall, which then signal systemically. Human pharmacokinetic parameters are not well established.", "sidefx": "In Japanese trials PSK was generally well tolerated. Reported adverse effects include nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, loss of appetite, and occasional rash or allergic reactions; changes in blood counts and liver enzymes were reported but are hard to separate from concurrent chemotherapy. No well-documented pharmacokinetic drug interactions exist. The main practical issues in the US are product identity, since supplements labeled PSK-like may not match pharmaceutical PSK, and the unknown effects of immune stimulation with modern chemotherapy and checkpoint inhibitors.", "fun": "PSK spent more than thirty years as a widely prescribed cancer medicine in Japan before anyone identified its receptor; the TLR2 explanation came in 2011, long after the drug had become a fixture of Japanese oncology.", "src": ["NCI PDQ: Medicinal Mushrooms (Health Professional Version)", "Lu et al. 2011, Cancer Research, PSK is a selective TLR2 agonist", "Oba et al. 2007, Cancer Immunology, Immunotherapy, meta-analysis of PSK in gastric cancer", "Sakamoto et al. 2006, Cancer Immunology, Immunotherapy, meta-analysis of PSK in colorectal cancer"]}, "shiitake": {"origin": "Shiitake has been cultivated in East Asia for close to a thousand years. Chinese tradition credits a Song dynasty farmer, Wu Sang Kwuang, with discovering that cutting logs caused the mushroom to sprout, and log cultivation spread to Japan, where the name shiitake comes from shii, a type of oak, and take, mushroom. In the twentieth century, inoculation with pure spawn plugs, developed in Japan in the 1940s, and later cultivation on sawdust blocks made shiitake one of the most widely produced mushrooms in the world, and China now grows the large majority. Medical interest followed the discovery in 1969 by Goro Chihara and colleagues in Japan of lentinan, a beta-glucan from shiitake that inhibited sarcoma 180 tumors in mice; lentinan was later approved in Japan as an injectable cancer adjunct. Japanese researchers also isolated eritadenine, an adenine-derived compound that lowered cholesterol in rats. Meanwhile, dermatologists described shiitake dermatitis, first reported in Japan in 1977, a whip-like rash after raw or undercooked shiitake, which has since been reported worldwide as raw shiitake became trendy in Western cooking.", "timeline": [["About 1000 years ago", "Log cultivation of shiitake begins in China and spreads to Japan."], ["1940s", "Pure spawn plug inoculation is developed in Japan."], ["1969", "Chihara's group reports lentinan's antitumor activity in mice."], ["1977", "Shiitake dermatitis is first described in Japan."], ["2015", "A small trial reports immune marker changes in adults eating cooked shiitake daily."]], "cell": "Shiitake's best-studied constituent is lentinan, a beta-1,3-glucan backbone with beta-1,6 branches that forms a triple helix. Innate immune cells recognize such glucans through Dectin-1, which signals through Syk kinase and CARD9 to activate NF-kappa B, and through complement receptor 3, which primes neutrophils and natural killer cells. The result in laboratory studies is increased cytokine production, macrophage activation and T cell responses. In a small human trial, eating cooked shiitake daily for several weeks was associated with increased proliferation of gamma-delta T cells and NK T cells and changes in inflammatory markers. Eritadenine inhibits S-adenosylhomocysteine hydrolase, altering phosphatidylethanolamine methylation in the liver, which changes lipoprotein secretion and lowered plasma cholesterol in rats. Shiitake dermatitis is thought to be a toxic or immunologic reaction to lentinan, which is destroyed by heat; the rash appears in a scratch-pattern distribution, suggesting that scratching spreads the reaction along the skin through a Koebner-like process. Mushroom worker's lung is a separate hypersensitivity pneumonitis caused by inhaling large numbers of spores in cultivation facilities.", "body": [["Immune system", "Beta-glucans activate macrophages, dendritic cells and NK cells; small human study shows immune marker changes."], ["Skin", "Flagellate dermatitis after raw or undercooked shiitake."], ["Liver and lipids", "Eritadenine lowers cholesterol in rodents."], ["Lungs", "Hypersensitivity pneumonitis from spore exposure in mushroom workers."], ["Gut", "Fiber and beta-glucans fermented by gut bacteria."]], "journey": "As food, shiitake is digested like other vegetables. Beta-glucans are not digested by human enzymes; they reach immune tissue in the small intestine, where M cells in Peyer's patches sample particles and dendritic cells and macrophages respond, and they are fermented by colonic bacteria into short-chain fatty acids. Small fragments may enter the circulation, but human pharmacokinetics are not established. Eritadenine is absorbed in animals; its human fate is unknown. Cooking denatures heat-labile components responsible for dermatitis and improves digestibility.", "sidefx": "Cooked shiitake is well tolerated by most people. The signature adverse effect is shiitake dermatitis: one to two days after eating raw or lightly cooked shiitake, intensely itchy red linear streaks appear on the trunk and limbs, resolving over days to weeks. Extract powders have been associated in case reports with eosinophilia, rash and digestive symptoms, and workers in shiitake cultivation can develop hypersensitivity pneumonitis. Allergic reactions to eating shiitake are uncommon.", "fun": "Shiitake dermatitis looks so much like whip marks that it is called flagellate dermatitis, and it occurs only when the mushroom is eaten raw or undercooked, because the suspected trigger, lentinan, breaks down with heat.", "src": ["Chihara et al. 1969, Nature, antitumor polysaccharide from Lentinus edodes", "Nakamura 1977, first report of shiitake dermatitis (Japanese dermatology literature)", "Dai et al. 2015, Journal of the American College of Nutrition, daily shiitake consumption and immunity", "Memorial Sloan Kettering, About Herbs: Shiitake Mushroom"]}, "lentinan": {"origin": "Lentinan was discovered in Japan by Goro Chihara and colleagues at the National Cancer Center Research Institute, who reported in Nature in 1969 that a polysaccharide from shiitake strongly inhibited sarcoma 180 tumors implanted in mice, even though it did not kill tumor cells directly in culture. That finding helped define the idea of a biological response modifier, a substance that fights cancer by changing the host's immune response. Structural studies showed lentinan to be a large beta-1,3-glucan with beta-1,6 branches arranged in a triple helix. Ajinomoto and other Japanese companies developed it as an injectable product, and it was approved in Japan in the 1980s as an adjunct to chemotherapy for inoperable or recurrent gastric cancer. Japanese randomized trials, and later a meta-analysis of individual patient data published in 2009, reported improved survival when lentinan was added to chemotherapy. Lentinan was never approved in the US or Europe. Later work linked lentinan to shiitake dermatitis and developed superfine dispersed oral lentinan to improve its uptake by gut immune tissue.", "timeline": [["1969", "Chihara's group reports lentinan's antitumor effect in mice."], ["1970s", "Lentinan's triple-helix beta-glucan structure is characterized."], ["1980s", "Japan approves injectable lentinan as a gastric cancer adjunct."], ["2009", "An individual patient data meta-analysis reports survival benefit with lentinan plus chemotherapy in advanced gastric cancer."]], "cell": "Lentinan's beta-1,3-glucan backbone with beta-1,6 branches resembles the cell wall of fungal pathogens, so innate immune receptors recognize it as a danger signal. Dectin-1, a C-type lectin receptor on macrophages, dendritic cells and neutrophils, binds the glucan and signals through Syk kinase and CARD9 to activate NF-kappa B, triggering cytokine release, phagocytosis and reactive oxygen species production. Complement receptor 3 binds glucan fragments and primes neutrophils and NK cells to kill complement-coated tumor cells. Downstream, dendritic cells produce interleukin-12, pushing helper T cells toward a Th1 profile and enhancing cytotoxic T lymphocyte responses; macrophages release tumor necrosis factor and interleukin-1. The triple-helix conformation appears important for activity, as denatured single-strand forms are weaker in some assays. Lentinan has little direct cytotoxic effect, so its antitumor activity in mice disappears in animals lacking T cells, supporting a host-mediated mechanism. The same immune activation explains fever and hypersensitivity reactions during infusion.", "body": [["Immune system", "Dectin-1 and CR3 activation of macrophages, dendritic cells, neutrophils and NK cells; Th1 shift."], ["Tumor", "Antitumor effect in animals depends on host T cells rather than direct killing."], ["Skin", "Thought to cause shiitake dermatitis after raw mushroom intake."], ["Whole body", "Fever and hypersensitivity reactions possible with IV infusion."]], "journey": "Given intravenously, lentinan circulates as a large polysaccharide that is taken up by macrophages in the liver, spleen and other tissues, where it is slowly degraded; human pharmacokinetic parameters are not well defined. Oral lentinan is minimally absorbed intact. It interacts with immune cells in the intestinal wall, and superfine dispersed preparations were developed to improve uptake by Peyer's patches. Unabsorbed material is fermented by colonic bacteria or excreted in feces.", "sidefx": "Reported adverse effects of IV lentinan in Japan include fever, rash, chest tightness, nausea and hypersensitivity reactions, with rare anaphylactic shock noted in Japanese prescribing information. Oral supplements are generally well tolerated, with occasional digestive upset. Lentinan is the leading suspected cause of shiitake dermatitis, the linear itchy rash after raw or undercooked shiitake. The effect of immune stimulation on modern chemotherapy, immunotherapy and immunosuppressants is not defined.", "fun": "Lentinan helped launch the idea of the biological response modifier: in Chihara's 1969 experiments it shrank mouse tumors while leaving tumor cells in a dish untouched, showing that a mushroom sugar could work entirely through the host's immune system.", "src": ["Chihara et al. 1969, Nature, antitumor polysaccharide from Lentinus edodes", "Oba et al. 2009, Cancer Immunology, Immunotherapy, lentinan meta-analysis in advanced gastric cancer", "NCI PDQ: Medicinal Mushrooms (Health Professional Version)", "Brown and Gordon 2001, Nature, Dectin-1 as a beta-glucan receptor"]}, "maitake": {"origin": "Grifola frondosa grows in large, overlapping clusters at the base of oaks and other hardwoods in Japan, China, Europe and eastern North America, where foragers call it hen of the woods. Its Japanese name, maitake, means dancing mushroom, often explained by the story that people danced with joy on finding it, since wild maitake was valuable enough to be traded for silver. Reliable indoor cultivation was developed in Japan in the 1980s, making it widely available. In the same period Hiroaki Nanba at Kobe Pharmaceutical University isolated beta-glucan fractions from maitake, the D-fraction and later the MD-fraction, and reported antitumor activity in mice, which led to commercial standardized extracts. Researchers also described an SX-fraction with glucose-lowering effects in diabetic animals. In the United States, Memorial Sloan Kettering investigators ran a phase 1 and 2 trial of a maitake extract in postmenopausal breast cancer survivors, published in 2009, finding that it was tolerated and produced both stimulatory and inhibitory immune changes, and a later phase 2 trial in myelodysplastic syndrome reported increased neutrophil and monocyte function. A 2010 case report described an INR increase in a patient on warfarin after starting maitake.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Maitake cultivation is developed in Japan, and Nanba isolates the D-fraction."], ["1990s", "MD-fraction and SX-fraction products are developed."], ["2009", "A Memorial Sloan Kettering trial reports mixed immune effects in breast cancer survivors."], ["2010", "A case report describes an INR increase with maitake and warfarin."], ["2015", "A phase 2 trial in myelodysplastic syndrome reports enhanced neutrophil and monocyte function."]], "cell": "Maitake's D-fraction is a protein-bound beta-glucan with a beta-1,6 backbone and beta-1,3 branches, and the MD-fraction is a further purified version. Like other fungal beta-glucans, they are recognized by Dectin-1 and complement receptor 3 on macrophages, dendritic cells and neutrophils, triggering Syk and NF-kappa B signaling, cytokine release, phagocytosis and activation of natural killer and T cells. In mice, D-fraction enhanced antitumor responses and promoted Th1 immunity. In the MSK breast cancer trial, immune measures moved in both directions depending on the measure, so maitake is better described as an immunomodulator than a simple stimulant. The SX-fraction, a glycoprotein, improved insulin sensitivity and lowered fasting glucose in diabetic rodents, possibly by enhancing insulin receptor signaling, and alpha-glucosidase inhibition has also been reported in vitro. The mechanism of the warfarin interaction in the case report is unknown; it may involve altered warfarin metabolism or other factors, and it has not been confirmed in controlled studies.", "body": [["Immune system", "Dectin-1 and CR3 activation; mixed stimulatory and inhibitory immune changes in a small human trial."], ["Pancreas and metabolism", "Improved insulin sensitivity and lower glucose in animal studies."], ["Blood", "INR increase with warfarin reported in a case report."], ["Bone marrow", "Enhanced neutrophil and monocyte function in a small myelodysplastic syndrome trial."], ["Heart and vessels", "Blood pressure lowering in animal studies."]], "journey": "Maitake polysaccharides are not digested by human enzymes and are largely not absorbed intact. They act on immune cells lining the gut, particularly in Peyer's patches, which then signal systemically, and are fermented by colonic bacteria into short-chain fatty acids. Small fragments may reach the circulation, but human pharmacokinetic data are lacking. Glycoprotein fractions may be partly digested. Unabsorbed material is excreted in feces.", "sidefx": "In small trials maitake extracts were generally well tolerated, with digestive upset the most common complaint. Allergic reactions are possible. The main concerns are interactions: possible additive glucose lowering with insulin and oral diabetes drugs, possible additive blood pressure lowering, and an INR increase reported in a patient on warfarin. Immune stimulation raises theoretical concern with immunosuppressive therapy.", "fun": "Maitake's Japanese name means dancing mushroom, and one traditional explanation is that foragers danced when they found it, because a good cluster could be worth its weight in silver in feudal Japan.", "src": ["Deng et al. 2009, Journal of Cancer Research and Clinical Oncology, maitake extract in breast cancer patients", "Wesa et al. 2015, Cancer Immunology, Immunotherapy, maitake extract in myelodysplastic syndrome", "Hanselin et al. 2010, Annals of Pharmacotherapy, INR elevation with maitake and warfarin", "Memorial Sloan Kettering, About Herbs: Maitake"]}, "chaga": {"origin": "Chaga grows as a hard, black, cracked mass on birch trees across the cold forests of Russia, Scandinavia, Canada and the northern United States. The mass is not a fruiting body but a sterile conk, a dense knot of mycelium and wood, whose black crust is rich in melanin and whose rusty interior gives a bitter tea. Siberian and Baltic folk medicine used chaga tea as a tonic and for stomach complaints, and Russian physicians studied it in the mid-twentieth century; an extract preparation was used in the Soviet Union for gastric conditions. Aleksandr Solzhenitsyn introduced chaga to a broad readership in his 1968 novel Cancer Ward, in which characters discuss birch fungus as a folk cancer remedy. In recent decades chaga became a wellness trend in North America and Europe, sold as chunks, powders, capsules and coffee blends, and wild harvesting has raised concerns about overharvesting. Laboratory studies reported antioxidant, anti-inflammatory and anticancer effects of its triterpenes and polysaccharides. Since 2014, nephrologists in Japan, Korea and elsewhere have published case reports of oxalate nephropathy after months of daily chaga use, including patients who progressed to end-stage kidney disease and required dialysis.", "timeline": [["Traditional use", "Chaga tea is used in Siberian and Northern European folk medicine."], ["Mid-1900s", "Russian physicians study chaga extracts for stomach disorders."], ["1968", "Solzhenitsyn's novel Cancer Ward describes birch fungus as a folk cancer remedy."], ["2010s", "Chaga becomes a wellness trend in North America and Europe."], ["2014 onward", "Case reports describe oxalate nephropathy and end-stage kidney disease after prolonged chaga use."]], "cell": "Chaga's laboratory pharmacology comes from several groups of compounds. Betulin and betulinic acid, pentacyclic triterpenes taken up from birch bark, induced apoptosis in tumor cell lines through mitochondrial pathways. Inotodiol and other lanostane triterpenes showed anti-inflammatory and cytotoxic effects. Melanin pigments and polyphenols are potent free radical scavengers in vitro, and beta-glucans activate innate immune cells through Dectin-1 and related receptors. Extracts inhibited platelet aggregation and lowered blood glucose in animal studies. The clinically documented harm, however, comes from oxalate. Chaga contains oxalate at levels reported to exceed those of well-known high-oxalate foods. Soluble oxalate is absorbed in the colon, especially when dietary calcium is low or when fat malabsorption after bariatric or bowel surgery leaves calcium bound to fat instead of oxalate. Humans cannot metabolize oxalate, so the kidneys filter and secrete it. When urinary oxalate concentration is high, calcium oxalate crystals form in tubular lumens and epithelial cells, triggering inflammation through the NLRP3 inflammasome, tubular injury, interstitial fibrosis and, with continued exposure, permanent loss of kidney function.", "body": [["Kidneys", "Calcium oxalate crystals deposit in tubules, causing acute or chronic kidney injury and sometimes end-stage disease."], ["Gut", "Soluble oxalate is absorbed, more so after bariatric or bowel surgery or with low calcium intake."], ["Blood and platelets", "Antiplatelet effects in laboratory studies, a theoretical bleeding concern."], ["Pancreas and metabolism", "Glucose lowering in animal studies."], ["Immune system", "Beta-glucans and triterpenes modulate immune cells in vitro."]], "journey": "Chaga is usually consumed as a hot-water tea or extract, which pulls oxalate, polyphenols and polysaccharides into solution. Soluble oxalate is absorbed along the intestine, mainly in the colon; calcium in the gut binds oxalate and limits absorption, which is why low-calcium diets and fat malabsorption increase uptake. Absorbed oxalate is not metabolized and is eliminated almost entirely by the kidneys through filtration and tubular secretion. Reduced kidney function slows oxalate clearance and raises plasma levels, making crystal deposition more likely, a vicious cycle in people with existing kidney disease. Triterpenes such as betulinic acid are poorly water soluble and poorly absorbed; their metabolism in humans is not well characterized. Polysaccharides act in the gut and are fermented by colonic bacteria.", "sidefx": "Short-term chaga use usually causes few symptoms beyond possible stomach upset. The serious adverse effect is oxalate nephropathy, described in case reports after months of daily use: fatigue, loss of appetite, nausea, swelling and declining urine output with rising creatinine, kidney biopsy showing calcium oxalate crystals, and in some cases progression to end-stage kidney disease requiring dialysis. Many reported patients had risk factors such as chronic kidney disease, diabetes or prior gut surgery. Laboratory antiplatelet and animal glucose-lowering effects are theoretical concerns with anticoagulants and diabetes drugs.", "fun": "Chaga's black crust is so rich in melanin, the same type of pigment found in human skin, that the fungus looks like a lump of burnt charcoal on the tree, and many people walk past it believing a birch was struck by fire.", "src": ["Kikuchi et al. 2014, Clinical Nephrology, chaga mushroom-induced oxalate nephropathy", "Lee et al. 2020, Journal of Korean Medical Science, end-stage renal disease after long-term ingestion of chaga mushroom", "Memorial Sloan Kettering, About Herbs: Chaga Mushroom"]}, "cordyceps": {"origin": "Ophiocordyceps sinensis, known in Tibetan as yartsa gunbu, summer grass winter worm, infects ghost moth caterpillars in the soil of the Tibetan Plateau and Himalaya; the fungus fills and kills the larva, then sends a thin stalk up through the ground in spring. Tibetan medical texts from the fifteenth century describe it as a tonic, and it entered Chinese medicine as dong chong xia cao, valued for the lungs, kidneys and vitality. Harvesting it became a major source of income for Tibetan and Nepali herders, and prices rose so high that it became among the most expensive biological products traded in China, inviting adulteration, including lead or other metals inserted to add weight. Cordyceps drew international attention in 1993 when Chinese women distance runners set world records and their coach credited a diet that reportedly included caterpillar fungus, although doping was widely suspected. Because wild collection cannot meet demand, Chinese researchers developed fermented mycelium strains, such as the CS-4 strain later identified as Paecilomyces hepiali, while Cordyceps militaris, a related orange club fungus that is easy to cultivate, became the basis of many Western supplements.", "timeline": [["15th century", "Tibetan medical texts describe yartsa gunbu as a tonic."], ["1950", "Cordycepin is isolated from Cordyceps militaris."], ["1993", "Chinese distance runners set world records; their coach credits a diet including cordyceps."], ["1990s", "Lead poisoning from adulterated cordyceps is reported."], ["2000s", "Fermented mycelium (CS-4) and cultivated C. militaris dominate the supplement market."]], "cell": "Cordyceps products contain several bioactive groups whose relative amounts depend on species and growth method. Nucleosides such as adenosine act on adenosine receptors, which affect blood vessel tone, heart rate and platelet function, and cordycepin, abundant in C. militaris, is an adenosine analog that interferes with RNA processing and activates AMP-activated protein kinase, an energy-sensing enzyme that increases glucose uptake and fatty acid oxidation. Polysaccharides activate macrophages and other innate immune cells, while mannitol and ergosterol derivatives contribute other laboratory effects. Animal studies reported improved endurance, better oxygen use, increased ATP turnover in liver tissue, lower blood glucose and increased steroid production by mouse Leydig cells, which underlies sexual function claims. Laboratory studies also found inhibition of platelet aggregation. Human exercise trials have been small, short and often used blends, with some reporting modest improvements in oxygen uptake or ventilatory threshold in older or untrained adults and others showing no benefit in trained athletes.", "body": [["Muscles and metabolism", "AMPK activation and altered energy metabolism in animals; small, mixed human exercise effects."], ["Blood and platelets", "Antiplatelet effects in laboratory studies."], ["Immune system", "Polysaccharides activate innate immune cells."], ["Kidneys", "Studied in Chinese trials for kidney disease, with variable quality."], ["Reproductive system", "Increased steroid production in animal cells; human effects unproven."]], "journey": "Human pharmacokinetics of cordyceps products are poorly characterized. Adenosine and cordycepin are absorbed but are rapidly deaminated by adenosine deaminase in blood and tissues, giving very short exposure to the parent compounds. Polysaccharides are mostly not absorbed intact and act on gut immune tissue before fermentation by colonic bacteria. Sterols and other lipophilic compounds are likely metabolized in the liver. Because wild O. sinensis, CS-4 mycelium and C. militaris differ chemically, findings from one cannot be applied directly to another.", "sidefx": "In trials cordyceps products were usually well tolerated, with digestive symptoms such as nausea, diarrhea or constipation and dry mouth reported. Allergic reactions are possible. Laboratory antiplatelet and animal glucose-lowering effects create theoretical interactions with anticoagulants and diabetes drugs. The most serious documented harms involve product quality: lead poisoning from adulterated traditional cordyceps and arsenic found in testing of wild caterpillar fungus. Energy and pre-workout blends may contain caffeine or other stimulants responsible for palpitations or insomnia.", "fun": "Wild cordyceps is so valuable that sellers have been caught pushing tiny lead wires or metal pins into the caterpillar bodies to increase their weight, which is how a supposed longevity tonic became a documented cause of lead poisoning.", "src": ["Memorial Sloan Kettering, About Herbs: Cordyceps", "Wu et al. 1996, Science of the Total Environment, lead poisoning from contaminated cordyceps", "Hirsch et al. 2017, Journal of Dietary Supplements, Cordyceps militaris and high-intensity exercise", "Winkler 2008, Economic Botany, yartsa gunbu and the Tibetan rural economy"]}, "cordycepin": {"origin": "Cordycepin was isolated in 1950 by K. G. Cunningham and colleagues in Britain from cultures of Cordyceps militaris, the orange club fungus that parasitizes insect pupae, and reported in Nature as a metabolic product with antibacterial activity. Its structure was soon identified as 3'-deoxyadenosine, making it one of the first nucleoside antibiotics described. Through the 1960s and 1970s it became a standard laboratory tool for molecular biologists, because it blocks polyadenylation of messenger RNA and could be used to study RNA processing. Interest in cancer grew because leukemia cells with high levels of terminal deoxynucleotidyl transferase appeared especially sensitive, but cordycepin was destroyed within minutes by adenosine deaminase, so investigators paired it with the adenosine deaminase inhibitor pentostatin, an FDA-approved leukemia drug, in an early clinical trial. In 2017, genomic work showed that C. militaris itself makes pentostatin from the same gene cluster that produces cordycepin, protecting its own product, an elegant natural version of the clinical strategy. More recently, a phosphoramidate ProTide derivative, NUC-7738, was designed to bypass adenosine deaminase and the transporters cordycepin needs, and entered early-phase cancer trials.", "timeline": [["1950", "Cunningham and colleagues isolate cordycepin from Cordyceps militaris."], ["1960s-1970s", "Cordycepin becomes a laboratory tool for studying mRNA polyadenylation."], ["2000s", "An early clinical trial combines cordycepin with pentostatin in leukemia."], ["2017", "Researchers show C. militaris produces pentostatin alongside cordycepin from one gene cluster."], ["2021", "A ProTide derivative, NUC-7738, is reported in early-phase cancer studies."]], "cell": "Cordycepin differs from adenosine only by lacking the hydroxyl group on the 3' carbon of the ribose sugar. Cells take it up through nucleoside transporters such as ENT1, then adenosine kinase phosphorylates it to monophosphate and, in further steps, to cordycepin triphosphate. RNA polymerases and poly(A) polymerase accept the triphosphate in place of ATP, but once it is incorporated the missing 3'-hydroxyl leaves no attachment point for the next nucleotide, so the chain stops. Polyadenylation of messenger RNA is particularly sensitive, which disrupts mRNA stability and translation of many transcripts, including inflammatory genes. Cordycepin monophosphate also mimics AMP and activates AMP-activated protein kinase, which inhibits mTOR signaling, reduces lipid synthesis and promotes glucose uptake, effects linked to anti-proliferative and metabolic findings in cells. At adenosine receptors it has additional effects on blood vessels and platelets. In blood and tissues, adenosine deaminase converts cordycepin to inactive 3'-deoxyinosine, so its laboratory potency is rarely achieved in the body without an adenosine deaminase inhibitor or a protected derivative.", "body": [["Cancer cells", "RNA chain termination and AMPK activation slow proliferation in laboratory studies."], ["Immune cells", "Reduced inflammatory gene expression through disrupted polyadenylation in laboratory models."], ["Blood", "Adenosine-like antiplatelet effects in laboratory studies."], ["Bone marrow", "Expected target of toxicity at high systemic exposure, as with other nucleoside analogs."], ["Liver and metabolism", "AMPK activation reduces lipid synthesis in animal models."]], "journey": "After oral intake, cordycepin is absorbed but is rapidly deaminated by adenosine deaminase in the gut, blood and tissues to 3'-deoxyinosine, giving the parent drug a half-life measured in minutes in animal studies. Inside target cells it must be transported by nucleoside transporters and phosphorylated by adenosine kinase to become active, and cancer cells can resist it by lowering these. Pentostatin blocks adenosine deaminase and dramatically extends exposure, which is why the two were combined in trials. Metabolites are eliminated mainly in urine. Supplement content of cordycepin varies widely, and oral bioavailability of active drug is low.", "sidefx": "No defined adverse effect profile exists for cordycepin in supplement form. As a nucleoside analog, it is cytotoxic at high concentrations, and systemic exposure high enough to affect cancer cells would be expected to suppress the bone marrow, as seen with related drugs. Combination with adenosine deaminase inhibitors greatly raises exposure. Laboratory antiplatelet effects create a theoretical bleeding concern with anticoagulants. Pregnancy safety is unknown, and nucleoside analogs as a class raise concern.", "fun": "Cordyceps militaris solved the cordycepin breakdown problem before medicine did: the same gene cluster that makes cordycepin also makes pentostatin, an adenosine deaminase inhibitor, so the fungus protects its own drug with a molecule that FDA later approved for hairy cell leukemia.", "src": ["Cunningham et al. 1950, Nature, cordycepin from Cordyceps militaris", "Xia et al. 2017, Cell Chemical Biology, fungal cordycepin biosynthesis coupled with pentostatin production", "Schwenzer et al. 2021, Clinical Cancer Research, NUC-7738 ProTide of 3'-deoxyadenosine", "Tuli et al. 2013, 3 Biotech, review of cordycepin pharmacology"]}, "agaricus-blazei": {"origin": "The almond mushroom grows naturally in parts of Brazil and North America. In the 1960s a Japanese-Brazilian farmer, Takatoshi Furumoto, collected it near Piedade in São Paulo state and sent samples to Japan, where interest grew after reports that people in the region had low rates of disease, a claim that was never established. Japanese researchers studied its polysaccharides from the 1980s and reported antitumor activity in mice, and the mushroom, marketed as Himematsutake or Agaricus blazei Murrill, became one of the most popular supplements among Japanese cancer patients. Mycologists later pointed out that the name was misapplied: the cultivated mushroom is generally classified as Agaricus subrufescens, a species described in North America in the nineteenth century, with Agaricus brasiliensis proposed as another name. Safety signals emerged in the 2000s. In 2006 Japanese oncologists reported severe hepatic damage in cancer patients using Agaricus extracts, and the same year Japan's Ministry of Health, Labour and Welfare asked a manufacturer to suspend sales of one product after a rat study suggested it promoted tumor development, while noting the finding applied to that product. Small clinical trials have since studied immune markers, quality of life and insulin resistance.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Furumoto collects the mushroom in São Paulo state and sends it to Japan."], ["1980s", "Japanese studies report antitumor effects of its polysaccharides in mice."], ["2006", "Japanese case reports describe severe liver damage in cancer patients using Agaricus extracts."], ["2006", "Japan's health ministry asks a manufacturer to suspend one Agaricus product after an animal study."], ["2000s-2010s", "Taxonomists clarify that the cultivated species is generally Agaricus subrufescens."]], "cell": "Agaricus contains beta-glucans that differ from those of shiitake or reishi, with a larger proportion of beta-1,6 linkages, along with protein-polysaccharide complexes. Innate immune cells recognize these through Dectin-1, complement receptor 3 and toll-like receptors 2 and 4, activating NF-kappa B and producing cytokines such as interleukin-12 and tumor necrosis factor, which enhance natural killer and T cell activity in laboratory studies. In small human trials, Agaricus extracts were associated with increased NK cell activity in gynecologic cancer patients receiving chemotherapy and with improved insulin resistance in people with type 2 diabetes. Agaritine, a hydrazine derivative found in Agaricus species including the common button mushroom, can be metabolized into reactive diazonium ions that are mutagenic in laboratory tests and caused tumors in some mouse studies, though its relevance at dietary levels is debated. The mechanism of the reported liver injury cases is unknown; the cases involved concentrated extracts in cancer patients and may reflect idiosyncratic reactions or product-specific factors.", "body": [["Immune system", "Beta-glucans activate macrophages, dendritic cells and NK cells; NK activity changes in small trials."], ["Liver", "Rare severe hepatic damage reported in Japanese cancer patients."], ["Pancreas and metabolism", "Improved insulin resistance in a small trial."], ["Gut", "Polysaccharides interact with gut immune tissue; digestive upset possible."]], "journey": "Agaricus polysaccharides are not digested by human enzymes and are largely not absorbed intact; they act on immune cells in the gut wall and are fermented by colonic bacteria. Small molecules such as agaritine are absorbed and, in animal studies, metabolized to reactive intermediates, while ergosterol is converted to vitamin D2 when the mushroom is exposed to ultraviolet light. Human pharmacokinetic data for Agaricus products are limited, and product content varies with species identity, cultivation and extraction method.", "sidefx": "In small trials Agaricus extracts were generally well tolerated, with nausea, diarrhea and stomach upset the most commonly reported effects. The serious concern is liver injury: Japanese oncologists reported severe hepatic damage in cancer patients using Agaricus extracts, with improvement after stopping in some cases. Allergic reactions are possible. Possible additive glucose lowering with diabetes medications, the theoretical relevance of agaritine, and the product-specific animal tumor-promotion finding that led to a sales suspension in Japan add to the caution.", "fun": "The scientific name on most Agaricus blazei products is wrong: the species William Murrill named blazei in Florida in 1945 is a different mushroom, and the Brazilian almond mushroom is generally identified as Agaricus subrufescens, a species described in New York in the 1890s and once grown commercially in the eastern United States.", "src": ["Mukai et al. 2006, Japanese Journal of Clinical Oncology, severe hepatic damage with Agaricus blazei extract", "Japan Ministry of Health, Labour and Welfare 2006 notice on an Agaricus product", "Kerrigan 2005, Mycologia, Agaricus subrufescens and the misapplied name A. blazei", "Memorial Sloan Kettering, About Herbs: Agaricus"]}, "tremella": {"origin": "Tremella fuciformis is a jelly fungus that grows on dead branches in tropical and subtropical forests, where it is actually a parasite of other wood-decaying fungi, mainly species of Annulohypoxylon, rather than of the wood itself. In China it has long been prized as a delicacy and tonic, valued in traditional medicine for nourishing the lungs and moistening dryness, and simmered with rock sugar, jujubes and goji berries into sweet soups. Wild snow fungus was scarce and expensive until cultivation on logs developed in China, with large-scale production growing in the twentieth century once growers learned to co-culture tremella with its host fungus on sawdust. Fujian Province became a major production center, and dried snow fungus became cheap and widely available. Chinese researchers studied its polysaccharides from the 1980s, reporting immune, antioxidant, glucose and lipid effects in animals, and a small Korean human study published in 2018 examined a tremella extract for cognitive function. The cosmetics industry adopted tremella polysaccharide as a plant-based humectant comparable to hyaluronic acid. Food safety reports in China have warned about bongkrekic acid poisoning from spoiled foods, and the toxin-producing bacterium has been detected in some tremella samples.", "timeline": [["Traditional use", "Snow fungus is used in Chinese cuisine and medicine as a tonic for dryness."], ["20th century", "Co-culture with host fungi enables large-scale cultivation in China."], ["1980s", "Chinese researchers study tremella polysaccharides in animals."], ["2018", "A small Korean trial examines a tremella extract and cognition."], ["2010s-2020s", "Tremella polysaccharide becomes a popular skin care humectant."]], "cell": "The main bioactive constituents are acidic heteropolysaccharides, chiefly glucuronoxylomannan, made of a mannose backbone with side chains of xylose and glucuronic acid. The many hydroxyl and carboxyl groups bind water through hydrogen bonding, so the polysaccharide swells and forms viscous gels; on skin this creates a film that slows water loss, the basis of its cosmetic use. In laboratory and animal studies, tremella polysaccharides activated macrophages through toll-like receptor signaling, increased antioxidant enzymes such as superoxide dismutase, reduced inflammatory cytokines, and improved glucose and lipid measures in diabetic or high-fat-fed rodents. Some studies reported protection of neurons from oxidative injury, which led to the human cognition study. The contrasting danger in spoiled fungus comes from a different organism: Burkholderia gladioli pathovar cocovenenans produces bongkrekic acid, which binds the mitochondrial adenine nucleotide translocase and locks it, so ADP cannot enter mitochondria and ATP cannot leave. Cells rapidly run out of usable energy, causing liver, kidney, heart and brain failure, and there is no antidote.", "body": [["Skin", "Polysaccharide film reduces water loss in cosmetic use."], ["Immune system", "Macrophage activation and cytokine changes in laboratory studies."], ["Pancreas and metabolism", "Glucose and lipid improvements in animal studies."], ["Brain", "Neuroprotection in laboratory studies; a small human cognition study."], ["Mitochondria (contaminated fungus)", "Bongkrekic acid blocks the adenine nucleotide translocase, causing multiorgan failure."]], "journey": "Tremella polysaccharides are not digested by human enzymes and are largely not absorbed. They increase the viscosity of intestinal contents, interact with gut immune tissue, and are fermented by colonic bacteria into short-chain fatty acids, which may contribute to metabolic effects reported in animals. Unfermented material is excreted in feces. Topical polysaccharide stays on the skin surface and in the outer stratum corneum. By contrast, bongkrekic acid from contaminated food is absorbed rapidly and causes symptoms within hours.", "sidefx": "Properly prepared tremella is a well-tolerated food. Supplements may cause stomach upset or bloating, and allergic reactions are possible. Theoretical additive effects with diabetes medications and anticoagulants come from animal data only. The serious hazard is contamination: fresh or long-soaked fungus kept at warm temperatures can support Burkholderia gladioli growth, and bongkrekic acid poisoning causes vomiting, abdominal pain, sweating, weakness, confusion and rapid progression to shock and multiorgan failure, with high fatality.", "fun": "Snow fungus is not really a wood rotter at all: it lives as a parasite on another fungus inside the wood, which is why growers could only cultivate it reliably after they learned to grow both fungi together.", "src": ["Ban et al. 2018, Journal of Medicinal Food, Tremella fuciformis extract and cognition", "China CDC Weekly 2025, bongkrekic acid food poisoning review", "Anwar et al. 2017, Journal of Medical Toxicology, bongkrekic acid review"]}, "mushroom-beta-glucans": {"origin": "Beta-glucans entered immunology through yeast. In the 1940s Louis Pillemer and colleagues studied zymosan, a crude preparation of yeast cell walls, as an activator of the complement system, and later work identified the active component as beta-1,3-glucan. Through the 1960s and 1970s, Japanese researchers isolated antitumor glucans from mushrooms, including lentinan from shiitake, schizophyllan from split gill fungus and PSK from turkey tail, and Japan approved several as cancer adjuncts. Nicholas DiLuzio at Tulane studied particulate yeast glucans as stimulators of macrophages in the same era. The receptor question was answered in 2001, when Gordon Brown and Siamon Gordon at Oxford identified Dectin-1 as the main beta-glucan receptor on macrophages, opening the modern field of C-type lectin signaling in antifungal immunity. In parallel, nutrition research showed that the soluble beta-glucan fiber in oats lowers LDL cholesterol, and in 1997 FDA authorized a food health claim for oat soluble fiber and coronary heart disease risk, later extended to barley. Today, yeast and mushroom beta-glucans are sold as immune supplements, while a laboratory test for beta-D-glucan in blood is used in hospitals to help detect invasive fungal infections.", "timeline": [["1940s", "Pillemer's group studies zymosan, a yeast cell wall preparation, as a complement activator."], ["1969", "Lentinan from shiitake is reported to have antitumor activity in mice."], ["1997", "FDA authorizes a food health claim for oat soluble fiber, including beta-glucan, and heart disease risk."], ["2001", "Brown and Gordon identify Dectin-1 as the beta-glucan receptor."], ["2004", "A blood beta-D-glucan assay is cleared by FDA to aid diagnosis of invasive fungal infection."]], "cell": "Fungal and yeast beta-glucans are glucose polymers with a beta-1,3-linked backbone and beta-1,6 branches, structures absent from human cells, so the innate immune system treats them as a signature of fungi. Dectin-1, a C-type lectin receptor on macrophages, dendritic cells and neutrophils, binds beta-1,3-glucan; particulate glucans cluster the receptor into a phagocytic synapse that triggers strong signaling, while small soluble glucans bind without full activation. Signaling runs through an ITAM-like motif to Syk kinase and the CARD9-BCL10-MALT1 complex, activating NF-kappa B and the NLRP3 inflammasome, producing cytokines such as interleukin-1 beta, interleukin-6, interleukin-12 and interleukin-23, reactive oxygen species and phagocytosis. Complement receptor 3 binds glucans at a lectin site and primes neutrophils and NK cells to kill targets coated with complement fragment iC3b, a mechanism explored in cancer immunotherapy combinations. Beta-glucans can also induce trained immunity, a lasting epigenetic reprogramming of monocytes that enhances later responses. Cereal beta-glucans from oats and barley have mixed beta-1,3/1,4 linkages without branching; they act mainly as viscous soluble fiber, binding bile acids and reducing cholesterol absorption.", "body": [["Gut immune tissue", "Peyer's patch M cells and dendritic cells sample particulate glucans and start immune signaling."], ["Innate immune cells", "Dectin-1 and CR3 activation of macrophages, neutrophils, dendritic cells and NK cells."], ["Liver and cholesterol", "Cereal beta-glucan fiber binds bile acids and lowers LDL."], ["Skin", "Topical glucans studied for wound healing and skin soothing."], ["Gut microbiome", "Fermented to short-chain fatty acids by colonic bacteria."]], "journey": "Beta-glucans are not broken down by human digestive enzymes. Particulate yeast and mushroom glucans are taken up by M cells in Peyer's patches and engulfed by gut macrophages, which in animal studies carried glucan fragments to lymph nodes, spleen and bone marrow, slowly degrading them and releasing smaller fragments that prime neutrophils through CR3. Cereal beta-glucans dissolve to form viscous solutions in the gut, slowing absorption of glucose and bile acids. All types are fermented by colonic bacteria into short-chain fatty acids, and unfermented material leaves in feces. Human pharmacokinetic data are limited.", "sidefx": "Oral beta-glucans are generally well tolerated, with bloating, gas and stomach upset the most common effects, especially with cereal fiber. Allergic reactions to the source mushroom or yeast are possible. Because they stimulate innate immunity, theoretical concerns exist in transplant recipients, people on immunosuppressants and those with autoimmune disease, although clinical harm has not been well documented. Intravenous glucans used in some countries can cause fever and hypersensitivity reactions, but those are different products from oral supplements.", "fun": "Hospitals use beta-glucan as a clue to fungal infection: a blood test for beta-D-glucan detects fragments of fungal cell walls in serum using a reagent made from horseshoe crab blood cells, the same type of reagent used to detect bacterial endotoxin.", "src": ["Brown and Gordon 2001, Nature, Dectin-1 is a beta-glucan receptor", "FDA 21 CFR 101.81, soluble fiber health claim", "NCI PDQ: Medicinal Mushrooms (Health Professional Version)", "Goodridge, Wolf and Underhill 2009, Immunological Reviews, beta-glucan recognition by the innate immune system"]}, "red-yeast-rice": {"origin": "Red yeast rice, called hong qu in China and beni-koji in Japan, is cooked rice fermented with the mold Monascus purpureus. It has been used for centuries in East Asia to color and flavor foods such as Peking duck, red rice wine and fermented tofu, and Chinese materia medica described it for digestion and circulation. Its modern pharmacology began with the statin story. In the 1970s Akira Endo in Japan searched fungi for inhibitors of cholesterol synthesis and found mevastatin (compactin) in Penicillium; he later isolated monacolin K from a Monascus species, and at almost the same time Merck scientists isolated the identical molecule, mevinolin, from Aspergillus terreus. That molecule became lovastatin, approved by the FDA in 1987 as the first commercial statin. In the 1990s a red yeast rice product marketed as a dietary supplement prompted the FDA to argue it was really an unapproved drug because it contained lovastatin, a position upheld in court. Since then products have drifted between almost no monacolin and near-prescription amounts. The 2024 beni-koji event in Japan, in which supplements from one manufacturer were linked to kidney injury and deaths and investigators pointed to the contaminant puberulic acid, showed that the risks go beyond the statin itself.", "timeline": [["Centuries ago", "Red yeast rice is used in East Asia as a food colorant, in rice wine and in traditional medicine."], ["1970s", "Akira Endo identifies fungal inhibitors of HMG-CoA reductase and later monacolin K from Monascus."], ["1987", "The FDA approves lovastatin, the same molecule as monacolin K, as a prescription statin."], ["Late 1990s", "The FDA treats a lovastatin-containing red yeast rice supplement as an unapproved drug, and the courts uphold its position."], ["2024", "Beni-koji supplements in Japan are linked to kidney injury and deaths; authorities point to the contaminant puberulic acid."]], "cell": "Monacolin K circulates as an inactive lactone until it is hydrolyzed to its open beta-hydroxy acid. The acid resembles HMG-CoA and binds the catalytic site of HMG-CoA reductase, blocking conversion of HMG-CoA to mevalonate, the committed step of cholesterol synthesis. Hepatocytes sense the fall in intracellular sterol through SREBP-2, which moves to the nucleus and increases transcription of the LDL receptor; more LDL receptors on the liver surface pull LDL particles out of plasma. The same block lowers downstream isoprenoids such as farnesyl and geranylgeranyl pyrophosphate and coenzyme Q10, which is part of the explanation offered for statin muscle toxicity. Red yeast rice also contains other monacolins, sterols and pigments whose contribution is unclear. Citrinin, produced by some Monascus strains during fermentation, is a separate problem: it is toxic to kidney tubular cells in animal studies, disturbing mitochondrial function and generating oxidative stress.", "body": [["Liver", "Main site of action: HMG-CoA reductase inhibition, LDL receptor up-regulation and occasional enzyme elevations or hepatitis."], ["Skeletal muscle", "Statin-type myalgia and, rarely, rhabdomyolysis, especially with CYP3A4 inhibitors or other statins."], ["Kidney", "Exposed to citrinin in contaminated products and to myoglobin in rhabdomyolysis; the 2024 Japanese event caused tubular kidney injury."], ["Cardiovascular system", "LDL lowering is the intended effect; long-term cardiovascular outcome data for supplements are limited."], ["Gastrointestinal tract", "Heartburn, bloating and stomach upset are common minor complaints."]], "journey": "Monacolin K is absorbed from the gut and undergoes heavy first-pass metabolism in the intestinal wall and liver by CYP3A4, so only a small fraction of the parent compound reaches the circulation; this is exactly why CYP3A4 inhibitors such as clarithromycin, itraconazole and grapefruit juice can raise exposure several-fold. Esterases hydrolyze the lactone to the active acid. Both forms are highly protein bound. The liver takes up the acid and concentrates it, which suits a drug whose target is liver cholesterol synthesis. Elimination is mainly through bile into the feces with a smaller urinary fraction, based on lovastatin data. Research on whole red yeast rice suggests the food matrix and other monacolins may change absorption, but studies are small. Citrinin kinetics in people are poorly characterized.", "sidefx": "Most reported effects mirror statins: muscle aches, weakness, stomach upset and headache. The serious statin effects are myopathy and rhabdomyolysis, with dark urine and risk of acute kidney injury, and liver enzyme elevation with occasional hepatitis; LiverTox describes case reports of liver injury attributed to red yeast rice. Risk rises when it is combined with a prescription statin, gemfibrozil, cyclosporine or a CYP3A4 inhibitor. Because labels rarely state monacolin content, a person who stopped a statin for muscle pain may unknowingly restart a similar drug. Contamination is the second layer: citrinin is a nephrotoxic mycotoxin, and the 2024 Japanese event showed that a fermentation contaminant could cause tubular kidney damage at a population scale. Pregnancy is a concern because statin-like compounds are generally avoided.", "fun": "The molecule a red mold makes in fermented rice and the one Merck isolated from Aspergillus and sold as Mevacor are the same molecule, which is why a food coloring ended up at the center of a legal fight over whether a supplement was secretly a prescription drug.", "src": ["NIH NCCIH: Red Yeast Rice", "LiverTox (NIH): Red Yeast Rice", "Endo A. A historical perspective on the discovery of statins. Proc Jpn Acad Ser B 2010", "Japan Ministry of Health, Labour and Welfare reports on beni-koji supplements, 2024"]}, "monacolin-k": {"origin": "Monacolin K sits at the meeting point of traditional fermentation and modern cardiology. In the early 1970s Akira Endo, working at the Japanese company Sankyo, reasoned that fungi might make compounds that block cholesterol synthesis in competing organisms, and after screening thousands of cultures he found mevastatin (compactin) in a Penicillium mold. Later in the decade Endo isolated a closely related compound from a Monascus species and named it monacolin K, while Alfred Alberts and colleagues at Merck isolated mevinolin from Aspergillus terreus. The two turned out to be the same molecule, and under the name lovastatin it became the first statin approved by the FDA, in 1987. Monacolin K therefore has two identities: a prescription drug with decades of outcome data, and a variable constituent of red yeast rice supplements. That second identity is what led the FDA to argue in the late 1990s that a red yeast rice product with added or standardized monacolin K was an unapproved drug, a position the courts accepted. Market surveys since then have found products ranging from trace amounts to doses close to the prescription drug, so the patient cannot know which pharmacology they are getting.", "timeline": [["Early 1970s", "Akira Endo screens fungi for cholesterol synthesis inhibitors and discovers mevastatin."], ["Late 1970s", "Monacolin K is isolated from Monascus and mevinolin from Aspergillus terreus; they prove identical."], ["1987", "The FDA approves lovastatin as the first commercial statin."], ["Late 1990s", "The FDA treats a red yeast rice product standardized for monacolin K as an unapproved drug."], ["2000s to 2020s", "Product surveys find monacolin K content ranging from trace to near-prescription amounts."]], "cell": "Monacolin K is a fungal polyketide with a hexahydronaphthalene core joined to a lactone ring. In the body the lactone opens to a beta-hydroxy acid that mimics the HMG-CoA intermediate and binds HMG-CoA reductase with an affinity far higher than the natural substrate, blocking production of mevalonate. Mevalonate is the starting point for cholesterol and for isoprenoids that anchor small GTPases such as Ras and Rho to membranes, as well as for coenzyme Q10 and dolichol. In hepatocytes, falling sterol levels release SREBP-2 from the endoplasmic reticulum; after cleavage in the Golgi it enters the nucleus and switches on transcription of LDL receptor and HMG-CoA reductase genes. Increased LDL receptors clear LDL particles from plasma. In skeletal muscle, reduced isoprenoid and coenzyme Q10 production and effects on mitochondrial function are proposed mechanisms for myopathy; genetic variation in the hepatic uptake transporter OATP1B1 (SLCO1B1) affects statin muscle risk, best shown for simvastatin.", "body": [["Liver", "Target organ: reduced cholesterol synthesis and more LDL receptors; occasional enzyme elevation."], ["Blood vessels", "Lower LDL exposure over time reduces atherosclerotic risk with prescription lovastatin."], ["Skeletal muscle", "Myalgia and, rarely, rhabdomyolysis, especially with CYP3A4 inhibitors."], ["Kidney", "Injured secondarily by myoglobin in rhabdomyolysis."], ["Gastrointestinal tract", "Mild stomach upset and bowel changes are common."]], "journey": "After swallowing, monacolin K is absorbed incompletely and meets CYP3A4 in the intestinal wall and liver, which removes most of it before it reaches the general circulation; systemic bioavailability of lovastatin is low. This large first-pass effect is the reason CYP3A4 inhibitors and grapefruit juice raise exposure so sharply. Esterases and the liver convert the lactone to the active acid. Plasma peaks occur within a few hours, and the active form has a short half-life, yet LDL lowering persists because the effect depends on changed gene expression in hepatocytes rather than on constant plasma levels. Both forms bind plasma proteins extensively. Metabolites leave mainly through bile into the feces, with only a small share in urine. In red yeast rice, the food matrix and accompanying monacolins may alter absorption, but data are limited.", "sidefx": "Muscle complaints are the signature side effect: aches and cramps are common, true myopathy with CK elevation is less common, and rhabdomyolysis with myoglobinuria and kidney injury is rare but serious. Risk rises with strong CYP3A4 inhibitors, other statins, gemfibrozil, cyclosporine, high exposure, older age, hypothyroidism and kidney disease. Liver enzyme elevations occur, and clinically apparent hepatitis is rare. Statins as a class carry a small signal for new-onset diabetes. In supplements, the hidden-statin problem adds a practical risk: a person who quit a statin because of muscle pain may restart the same molecule unknowingly, and duplication with a prescription statin is easy to miss. Pregnancy is a concern because statins are generally avoided.", "fun": "Endo screened thousands of fungal broths to find the first statin, and one of the molecules from that search turned out to be the same compound that a red mold had been quietly producing in fermented rice for centuries.", "src": ["Endo A. A historical perspective on the discovery of statins. Proc Jpn Acad Ser B 2010", "FDA prescribing information: Mevacor (lovastatin)", "NIH NCCIH: Red Yeast Rice", "StatPearls: Lovastatin"]}, "citrinin": {"origin": "Citrinin was first isolated in the 1930s by British chemists working on the metabolites of Penicillium citrinum, the mold that gives it its name, and in the 1940s it was briefly examined as an antibiotic because it kills some bacteria. Its kidney toxicity in animals ended that interest. Over the following decades citrinin was found in moldy wheat, oats, rye, barley and rice, often together with ochratoxin A, another kidney toxin made by related Penicillium and Aspergillus species. Yellow rice disease in postwar Japan, in which stored rice became moldy and discolored, drew attention to Penicillium toxins in rice, including citrinin. Citrinin returned to the spotlight when red yeast rice became a popular cholesterol supplement, because many Monascus strains produce it during fermentation. Testing of commercial products found citrinin in a share of them, and European regulators set maximum permitted levels for red yeast rice supplements. Interest grew again after the 2024 Japanese beni-koji event, although investigators in that case pointed to a different contaminant, puberulic acid, rather than citrinin, which illustrates how fermented supplements can carry more than one hidden toxin.", "timeline": [["1930s", "Citrinin is isolated from Penicillium citrinum."], ["1940s", "Citrinin is studied briefly as an antibacterial and abandoned because of kidney toxicity in animals."], ["Postwar decades", "Moldy stored rice and grains in Japan and elsewhere draw attention to Penicillium toxins including citrinin."], ["2000s to 2010s", "Testing finds citrinin in some red yeast rice supplements; EFSA reviews its risks."], ["2020s", "European limits for citrinin in red yeast rice supplements are tightened."]], "cell": "Citrinin is a small polyketide quinone methide that fluoresces yellow under ultraviolet light. In laboratory studies it enters renal proximal tubule cells through organic anion transporters, the same family that handles many drugs and toxins, and concentrates in the kidney. Inside cells it disrupts mitochondrial function: it impairs the electron transport chain, opens the mitochondrial permeability transition pore, lowers ATP and increases reactive oxygen species. The result is oxidative damage to lipids and proteins and activation of caspase-dependent apoptosis. Some studies report effects on cell cycle progression and chromosome damage, but the genotoxicity evidence is mixed, and the International Agency for Research on Cancer has not classified citrinin as a human carcinogen. When ochratoxin A is present at the same time, cell and animal experiments suggest the two may add to each other's kidney toxicity, which matters because they often appear together in contaminated grain.", "body": [["Kidney", "Main target: proximal tubule injury in animal studies."], ["Liver", "Partly metabolizes citrinin to dihydrocitrinone; liver effects reported at high animal exposures."], ["Reproductive and developmental", "Embryotoxic effects reported in animal studies."], ["Gastrointestinal tract", "Absorbs dietary citrinin from contaminated grain or supplements."]], "journey": "Human data on citrinin kinetics are limited but growing through biomonitoring. After ingestion of contaminated food or supplements, citrinin is absorbed from the gut and binds plasma proteins. Part of it is converted, probably in the liver, to dihydrocitrinone, which appears less toxic in cell studies. Both the parent compound and dihydrocitrinone are found in human urine, and urinary measurement is now used in research to estimate population exposure. In animal studies citrinin concentrates in the kidney, consistent with uptake by tubular organic anion transporters, and is excreted mainly in urine. How long it stays in the human body has not been well defined, and the relationship between urinary levels and kidney risk in people is still unknown.", "sidefx": "At the low levels found in most foods, citrinin does not cause recognized symptoms. The concern is chronic or high exposure, where animal data show tubular kidney injury, with swelling of tubular cells, reduced concentrating ability and, at higher exposures, kidney failure. There are no well-characterized human poisoning syndromes, and attributing kidney damage in a supplement user to citrinin would require testing the product. Because citrinin and ochratoxin A often appear together, combined effects on the kidney are plausible. In practical terms, citrinin is one reason clinicians ask about red yeast rice when kidney function changes without explanation and why kidney disease raises extra concern about untested fermented supplements.", "fun": "Citrinin glows yellow under ultraviolet light, a property that early chemists used to spot it in moldy grain and that laboratories still use in some detection methods.", "src": ["EFSA Panel on Contaminants in the Food Chain: Scientific Opinion on the risks for public and animal health related to the presence of citrinin in food and feed, 2012", "EFSA 2020 statement on the maximum level of citrinin in food supplements based on rice fermented with Monascus purpureus", "NIH NCCIH: Red Yeast Rice"]}, "brewers-yeast": {"origin": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae is the yeast of bread, beer and wine, and people have used it for thousands of years, long before anyone knew it was alive. In the 1850s and 1860s Louis Pasteur showed that fermentation is carried out by living yeast cells, settling a fierce debate with chemists such as Justus von Liebig. Spent yeast from breweries was a byproduct looking for a use, and in the early twentieth century it became a nutrition supplement once the vitamin concept emerged. Brewer's yeast was an important source of the B vitamin complex in research on beriberi and pellagra, and in the 1950s Walter Mertz and Klaus Schwarz described a glucose tolerance factor in brewer's yeast that improved glucose handling in rats; chromium was proposed as its active component. This work turned brewer's yeast into a folk diabetes remedy and helped make chromium a popular supplement, even though later human trials of chromium gave inconsistent results. Brewer's yeast is also a longstanding folk galactagogue in lactation cookies and teas, a use without good trial evidence. Today it is sold as debittered powders, flakes and tablets, and it is a food-type supplement with modest risks for most people.", "timeline": [["Ancient times", "Saccharomyces yeast is used for bread and beer across many cultures."], ["1850s to 1860s", "Louis Pasteur shows that living yeast cells carry out fermentation."], ["Early 1900s", "Brewer's yeast becomes a recognized source of B vitamins in nutrition research."], ["1950s", "Mertz and Schwarz describe a glucose tolerance factor in brewer's yeast, later linked to chromium."], ["2000s to 2010s", "Trials and reviews of chromium supplements for diabetes report inconsistent effects."]], "cell": "Brewer's yeast sold as a supplement is heat-killed, so it acts as a nutrient package rather than a living organism. Its B vitamins serve as coenzymes: thiamin as thiamin pyrophosphate for pyruvate dehydrogenase, riboflavin as FAD, niacin as NAD and NADP, and B6 as pyridoxal phosphate for amino acid metabolism. Chromium is the component most linked to blood sugar claims. Proposed models suggest chromium binds a small peptide called chromodulin, which may amplify insulin receptor tyrosine kinase activity after insulin binds, increasing glucose uptake; this model is debated, and chromium is no longer universally regarded as an essential trace element. Yeast cell walls contain beta-glucans, which interact with immune receptors such as dectin-1 in laboratory studies. Yeast is rich in nucleic acids, whose purines are broken down to uric acid, explaining the gout caution. Yeast also produces anti-Saccharomyces cerevisiae antibodies in some people with Crohn's disease, a laboratory marker that has prompted some sources to caution against yeast products in that condition.", "body": [["Pancreas and insulin-sensitive tissues", "Chromium is proposed to support insulin signaling; human trial results are inconsistent."], ["Nervous system", "B vitamins support energy metabolism in nerve cells; deficiency, not supplementation, causes disease."], ["Joints and kidney", "Purines raise uric acid, which can provoke gout in susceptible people."], ["Gastrointestinal tract", "Gas and bloating are the most common effects."], ["Immune system", "Yeast allergy and anti-yeast antibodies in some people with Crohn's disease."]], "journey": "Brewer's yeast is digested like food. Stomach acid and pancreatic enzymes break down the dead yeast cells, releasing proteins, vitamins and minerals for absorption in the small intestine. Water-soluble B vitamins are absorbed by specific transporters and excess amounts leave in urine. Chromium absorption is low, and absorbed chromium travels bound to transferrin and is excreted mainly in urine. Purines from yeast nucleic acids are converted by xanthine oxidase to uric acid, which is cleared by the kidneys. Beta-glucans are largely not absorbed and interact with the gut immune system before leaving in the stool. Brewer's yeast does not reliably supply vitamin B12 because yeast does not make it; only fortified products do.", "sidefx": "Most people tolerate brewer's yeast, with gas, bloating and stomach upset as the common complaints, especially when starting. Allergic reactions occur in people sensitive to yeast. Purine content can provoke gout flares. Chromium may modestly add to glucose-lowering medicines, which matters for people on insulin or sulfonylureas. Some yeast products appear on tyramine caution lists for people on monoamine oxidase inhibitors, where tyramine can trigger a dangerous rise in blood pressure; the risk depends on the product. Inactive brewer's yeast does not cause yeast infections, but this should not be confused with live probiotic Saccharomyces boulardii, which has caused bloodstream infections in critically ill or immunocompromised patients and those with central lines.", "fun": "Brewer's yeast and baker's yeast are the same species, Saccharomyces cerevisiae, which was also the first eukaryote to have its whole genome sequenced, in 1996.", "src": ["NIH Office of Dietary Supplements: Chromium Fact Sheet for Health Professionals", "NIH Office of Dietary Supplements: Vitamin B12 Fact Sheet for Health Professionals", "Goffeau A et al. Life with 6000 genes. Science 1996", "LactMed (NIH): Brewer's Yeast"]}, "nutritional-yeast": {"origin": "Nutritional yeast grew out of the same Saccharomyces cerevisiae that brewers and bakers had used for millennia, but instead of being a leftover from beer it is grown on purpose for food. During the First World War, German scientists developed ways to grow yeast on molasses and other cheap sugars as a protein source when food was scarce, and food yeasts were explored again during the Second World War and in famine relief. In the second half of the twentieth century, vegetarian and vegan communities adopted heat-deactivated yeast flakes as a savory seasoning because of their cheesy, nutty flavor, which comes partly from naturally occurring glutamate and nucleotides. Manufacturers began fortifying flakes with vitamin B12, which yeast does not make on its own, and this turned nutritional yeast into one of the more practical B12 sources for people who avoid animal foods. Today it is sold in fortified and unfortified versions, often side by side, and the difference matters clinically. The informal nickname nooch comes from vegan cooking culture. Nutritional yeast is best understood as a food rather than a medicine, with a few cautions around gout, allergy and assumptions about B12.", "timeline": [["1910s", "Methods to grow yeast on molasses for protein are developed in Germany during wartime shortages."], ["1940s", "Food yeasts are revisited as protein supplements during the Second World War."], ["Late 20th century", "Vegetarian and vegan cooks adopt deactivated yeast flakes as a cheesy seasoning."], ["Late 20th century onward", "Fortified nutritional yeast becomes a recognized vegan source of vitamin B12."]], "cell": "Because it is heat-deactivated, nutritional yeast does not colonize the gut or ferment anything; it delivers the contents of yeast cells as food. Its B vitamins become coenzymes in energy metabolism: thiamin pyrophosphate in carbohydrate metabolism, FAD from riboflavin in oxidation reactions, NAD from niacin, and pyridoxal phosphate from B6 in amino acid metabolism. When fortified, vitamin B12 is usually added as cyanocobalamin, which the body converts to methylcobalamin, the cofactor for methionine synthase that regenerates methionine and folate, and adenosylcobalamin, the cofactor for methylmalonyl-CoA mutase. Lack of these reactions produces megaloblastic anemia and nerve damage, which is why fortification status matters. Yeast cell walls contribute beta-glucans, which in laboratory studies bind immune receptors such as dectin-1 on macrophages and may modestly affect cholesterol absorption. Yeast nucleic acids supply purines that are broken down to uric acid.", "body": [["Blood and bone marrow", "Fortified B12 and folate support normal red cell production; lack of B12 causes macrocytic anemia."], ["Nervous system", "B12 is needed for myelin; deficiency causes numbness and gait problems."], ["Kidney and urine", "Excess riboflavin turns urine bright yellow; uric acid from purines is excreted here."], ["Joints", "Purines can contribute to gout flares in susceptible people."], ["Gastrointestinal tract", "Fiber and beta-glucans may cause gas or bloating."]], "journey": "Nutritional yeast is digested like other foods. Proteins are broken into amino acids, and B vitamins are released and absorbed in the small intestine by carrier-mediated transport. Added vitamin B12 follows its normal path: it is released from food protein in the stomach, binds haptocorrin, then intrinsic factor in the duodenum, and is absorbed in the terminal ileum. People with pernicious anemia or ileal disease absorb little B12 this way regardless of diet. Excess water-soluble vitamins, especially riboflavin, appear in the urine within hours, giving it a bright color. Purines are converted by xanthine oxidase to uric acid and excreted by the kidneys. Beta-glucans pass mostly unabsorbed into the colon, where gut bacteria ferment some of them.", "sidefx": "Nutritional yeast is generally well tolerated as a food. The common effects are gas or bloating from its fiber and bright yellow urine from riboflavin, which is harmless. Heavily fortified products can supply enough niacin to cause facial flushing in some people. Its purines can trigger gout flares in susceptible people. Yeast allergy is uncommon but possible. Some sources caution people with Crohn's disease about yeast products because of anti-yeast antibodies, although evidence of harm from eating yeast is limited. The most clinically important problem is indirect: a person who believes an unfortified product covers their vitamin B12 needs can develop deficiency over months to years, with anemia, fatigue, sore tongue, numbness and, if prolonged, lasting nerve damage.", "fun": "Nutritional yeast and the yeast in bread and beer are the same species; the difference is that nutritional yeast is grown for itself and then killed by heat so it cannot leaven anything.", "src": ["NIH Office of Dietary Supplements: Vitamin B12 Fact Sheet for Health Professionals", "NIH Office of Dietary Supplements: Riboflavin Fact Sheet for Health Professionals", "NIH Office of Dietary Supplements: Niacin Fact Sheet for Health Professionals"]}, "kombucha": {"origin": "Kombucha's exact origin is uncertain. Popular histories place it in northeastern China or Russia, and fermented tea with a floating culture was well known in Russia and Eastern Europe by the early twentieth century, where it was called tea fungus or tea kvass. The name kombucha itself is confusing, because in Japanese kombu-cha means a seaweed tea, a different drink. Scientific descriptions of the culture appeared in the early twentieth century, when microbiologists identified the cellulose-forming acetic acid bacteria and yeasts that make up the mat. Home brewing spread through North America and Western Europe in the 1990s, passed from household to household by sharing the culture. In 1995 the CDC reported two Iowa women who developed severe metabolic acidosis, one of whom died, after drinking kombucha brewed from the same culture; a direct cause was not proven, but the report shaped later caution. Commercial bottled kombucha grew into a large market in the 2000s, and in 2010 several products were temporarily pulled from shelves after testing found alcohol above the 0.5 percent threshold that separates non-alcoholic drinks from regulated alcoholic beverages.", "timeline": [["Early 1900s", "Fermented tea with a floating culture is known in Russia and Eastern Europe as tea fungus."], ["1990s", "Home brewing spreads in North America and Western Europe by sharing cultures."], ["1995", "CDC MMWR reports severe metabolic acidosis in two Iowa women possibly linked to kombucha."], ["2010", "Some commercial kombucha is pulled from shelves over alcohol content above 0.5 percent."], ["2010s to 2020s", "Commercial kombucha becomes a mainstream beverage; human trials remain few."]], "cell": "Kombucha is a symbiotic system. Yeasts break sucrose into glucose and fructose and ferment them to ethanol and carbon dioxide. Acetic acid bacteria, largely Komagataeibacter species, oxidize ethanol to acetic acid and glucose to gluconic acid, and they spin cellulose fibers that form the rubbery floating mat. Glucuronic acid has been promoted as a detoxifying ingredient, but many analyses find little of it, and the detox claim is not supported. The acids drop the pH, which slows many pathogens; when a brew is not acidic enough, contaminating molds and bacteria can grow. Tea contributes caffeine and polyphenols such as catechins, some of which are transformed by the microbes. In laboratory studies kombucha extracts show antioxidant and antimicrobial effects, but these do not establish benefit in people. The live microbes in unpasteurized kombucha vary widely between batches and are not standardized probiotics.", "body": [["Gastrointestinal tract", "Acids and carbonation can cause bloating or heartburn; live microbes pass through the gut."], ["Liver", "Rare case reports of liver injury in heavy drinkers; also metabolizes the small ethanol content."], ["Acid-base balance", "Case reports of severe metabolic acidosis, mostly with home brews."], ["Teeth", "Acidic drinks contribute to enamel erosion."], ["Nervous system", "Caffeine from tea and small amounts of alcohol can have mild effects."]], "journey": "Kombucha is absorbed like other beverages. Ethanol is absorbed quickly in the stomach and small intestine and metabolized in the liver by alcohol dehydrogenase and aldehyde dehydrogenase, which is why disulfiram and metronidazole, which interfere with this pathway, are relevant. Acetic acid and gluconic acid are absorbed and enter normal metabolism, ending as carbon dioxide and water. Caffeine is absorbed within an hour and metabolized mainly by CYP1A2. Tea polyphenols are poorly absorbed, and gut bacteria break many of them down in the colon. Live microbes from unpasteurized products pass through the gut; most are not thought to colonize long-term. Lead leached from ceramic vessels, when present, is absorbed and stored in bone and blood.", "sidefx": "Most commercial kombucha causes nothing more than bloating, heartburn or stomach upset, and its acidity can erode tooth enamel with frequent sipping. The serious reports are rare and mostly involve heavy intake or home brews: severe metabolic or lactic acidosis, liver injury in case reports, allergic reactions, and lead poisoning when brewed in lead-glazed ceramic containers, because the acidic liquid leaches lead. Contaminated home brews can carry molds or pathogens, which is the reason for caution in pregnancy and immune suppression. Alcohol content can rise after bottling if fermentation continues, which matters for people avoiding alcohol and for those on disulfiram or metronidazole. Caffeine from tea adds mild stimulant effects.", "fun": "The rubbery disk floating on top of kombucha is almost pure bacterial cellulose, and researchers have studied it as a material for wound dressings and even vegan leather.", "src": ["CDC. Unexplained severe illness possibly associated with consumption of kombucha tea, Iowa, 1995. MMWR 1995", "LiverTox (NIH): Kombucha Tea", "Kapp JM, Sumner W. Kombucha: a systematic review of the empirical evidence of human health benefit. Ann Epidemiol 2019"]}, "koji-kojic-acid": {"origin": "Kojic acid is named after koji, the mold-covered steamed rice that has been used in Japan for centuries to start the fermentation of sake, soy sauce and miso. The koji mold is Aspergillus oryzae, a domesticated fungus. In 1907 the Japanese scientist Kendo Saito reported a new crystalline compound from the mold, and Teijiro Yabuta later named it kojic acid and worked out its structure as a small hydroxypyrone. For most of the twentieth century it was a chemical curiosity and an industrial fermentation product, used in some countries as a food anti-browning agent and studied for antibiotic activity. Its cosmetic career began when Japanese researchers found it inhibited tyrosinase, the enzyme that makes melanin, and it was approved in Japan in the late 1980s as a skin-whitening ingredient in quasi-drug products. In 2003 the Japanese health ministry suspended its use in quasi-drugs after rodent studies raised tumor concerns, then allowed it back after further review concluded that topical use posed little risk to people. In the United States it is sold as a cosmetic ingredient, and the Cosmetic Ingredient Review panel and European regulators accept it within concentration limits.", "timeline": [["1907", "Kendo Saito reports a new compound from Aspergillus oryzae grown on steamed rice."], ["1910s to 1920s", "Teijiro Yabuta names kojic acid and later establishes its structure."], ["Late 1980s", "Japan approves kojic acid as a skin-whitening ingredient in quasi-drug products."], ["2003", "Japan suspends kojic acid in quasi-drugs over rodent tumor findings, later lifting the suspension after review."], ["2010", "The Cosmetic Ingredient Review publishes its safety assessment of kojic acid in cosmetics."]], "cell": "Melanin is made in melanocytes inside organelles called melanosomes, and the first two steps, turning tyrosine into DOPA and DOPA into dopaquinone, are catalyzed by tyrosinase, a copper-containing enzyme with two copper ions at its active site. Kojic acid has a hydroxyl group next to a ring ketone, an arrangement that grips metal ions, and it binds the copper pair so the enzyme cannot use oxygen to oxidize its substrates. It acts as a competitive or mixed inhibitor depending on the substrate. Less dopaquinone means less eumelanin and pheomelanin, so newly formed skin cells carry less pigment and existing dark spots fade gradually as the epidermis turns over. Kojic acid does not destroy melanocytes, which is why pigment returns when use stops. The same metal binding gives it antioxidant and antimicrobial properties. In rodents fed large amounts, kojic acid reduced iodine uptake by the thyroid, lowering thyroid hormone, raising TSH and causing thyroid enlargement, and some laboratory genotoxicity assays were positive; these findings drove the regulatory reviews, although skin absorption in humans is low.", "body": [["Skin (melanocytes)", "Inhibits tyrosinase and reduces new melanin, producing gradual lightening of dark spots."], ["Skin (immune system)", "Can act as a sensitizer and cause allergic contact dermatitis, plus irritation with redness and stinging."], ["Thyroid", "High oral exposure in rodents reduced iodine uptake and enlarged the thyroid; not shown with human topical use."], ["Liver", "Rodent high-dose studies raised liver tumor questions that led to regulatory review; topical human relevance considered low."], ["Gut (food)", "Trace amounts may occur in some foods made with koji molds."]], "journey": "Kojic acid is a small, water-soluble molecule, and when applied to the skin in a cosmetic only a small fraction passes through the outer barrier into the circulation; damaged or inflamed skin and occlusion increase this. The cosmetic effect depends on reaching melanocytes in the basal layer of the epidermis. The small absorbed amount is expected to be conjugated, mainly by glucuronidation and sulfation, and eliminated in urine, though human data are sparse. Kojic acid is unstable in formulations, turning brown with light, heat and air, which is why it is often supplied as more stable esters such as kojic dipalmitate that must be hydrolyzed in the skin to act.", "sidefx": "The main problems are on the skin. Stinging, redness, dryness and peeling are common, especially when kojic acid is combined with glycolic acid, retinoids or peels. It is also a recognized sensitizer, so repeated use can produce allergic contact dermatitis with itching, swelling and small blisters, sometimes appearing after months of uneventful use. Irritation itself can trigger post-inflammatory darkening in people with darker skin, undoing the intended effect. Thyroid and tumor findings come from high-exposure rodent studies and have not been shown with human cosmetic use. A practical concern in skin lightening is the product category: imported creams may contain mercury, potent steroids or hydroquinone without labeling.", "fun": "Koji mold, Aspergillus oryzae, is a domesticated relative of Aspergillus flavus, the source of the potent liver toxin aflatoxin; centuries of selection by brewers produced strains that keep making kojic acid but do not make aflatoxin.", "src": ["Burnett CL et al. 2010, International Journal of Toxicology, Final report of the safety assessment of kojic acid as used in cosmetics", "European Commission Scientific Committee on Consumer Safety: Opinion on kojic acid", "Bentley R 2006, Natural Product Reports, From miso, sake and shoyu to cosmetics: a century of science for kojic acid", "Japan Ministry of Health, Labour and Welfare 2003 notice on kojic acid in quasi-drugs", "FDA: Skin Products with Mercury"]}, "creatine": {"origin": "Creatine was discovered in the 1830s by the French chemist Michel Eugene Chevreul, who isolated it from meat broth and named it after the Greek word kreas, meaning flesh. Later in the nineteenth century chemists found creatinine in urine and recognized that it came from creatine, and that meat-eating raised creatine excretion. In 1927 two research groups, Philip and Grace Eggleton in Britain and Cyrus Fiske and Yellapragada Subbarow in the United States, discovered phosphocreatine in muscle, and the creatine kinase reaction was later described by Karl Lohmann, revealing creatine as an energy buffer for contraction. Modern supplementation began in 1992, when Roger Harris, Karin Soderlund and Eric Hultman in Sweden showed that oral creatine raised muscle creatine stores in humans. Athletes adopted it rapidly through the 1990s, and it became one of the most researched dietary supplements, with consistent evidence for repeated high-intensity exercise. Medicine found its own use when inherited creatine deficiency syndromes were described, beginning with GAMT deficiency in 1994, in which creatine under specialist care can partly restore brain creatine. Hopes that creatine would slow neurodegenerative diseases faded after large trials in Parkinson disease and Huntington disease were stopped for futility.", "timeline": [["1830s", "Michel Eugene Chevreul isolates creatine from meat and names it from the Greek word for flesh."], ["1927", "Phosphocreatine is discovered in muscle by the Eggletons and by Fiske and Subbarow."], ["1992", "Harris, Soderlund and Hultman show oral creatine raises muscle creatine stores in humans."], ["1994", "GAMT deficiency, the first inherited creatine synthesis disorder, is described."], ["2010s", "Large trials in Parkinson disease and Huntington disease are stopped for futility."], ["2017", "The International Society of Sports Nutrition publishes a position stand concluding creatine is effective and generally safe in healthy people."]], "cell": "Muscle contraction runs on ATP, but a muscle cell holds only enough ATP for a few seconds of maximal effort. Phosphocreatine is the first backup. Creatine kinase, an enzyme present as cytosolic and mitochondrial forms, moves a high-energy phosphate from phosphocreatine to ADP, rebuilding ATP almost instantly and buffering the ATP to ADP ratio during sprints and heavy lifts. In mitochondria the reaction runs the other way, loading creatine with phosphate from newly made ATP, so the creatine and phosphocreatine pair also acts as a shuttle that carries energy from mitochondria to the myofibrils. Creatine enters cells through the SLC6A8 transporter, which couples its uptake to sodium and chloride, and supplementation raises intracellular total creatine until the cells reach a ceiling; people with low starting stores, such as vegetarians, gain the most. More phosphocreatine lets muscles sustain repeated short bursts, which allows more training work and, over time, greater gains in lean mass. Because creatine is osmotically active, cells draw in water as stores rise, explaining early weight gain. The body makes creatine in two steps: AGAT, mainly in the kidney, joins arginine and glycine to form guanidinoacetate, and GAMT, mainly in the liver, methylates it using S-adenosylmethionine.", "body": [["Skeletal muscle", "Holds most of the body's creatine; supplementation raises phosphocreatine and supports repeated high-intensity efforts and lean mass gains with training."], ["Kidneys", "Site of the first synthesis step and of creatinine excretion; supplements raise serum creatinine without necessarily reflecting injury."], ["Liver", "Completes creatine synthesis by methylating guanidinoacetate; creatine is not metabolized by CYP enzymes."], ["Brain", "Uses creatine for energy buffering; inherited creatine disorders cause intellectual disability and seizures."], ["Gut", "Large single amounts can cause bloating, cramping and diarrhea."]], "journey": "Creatine monohydrate is well absorbed from the gut, with plasma levels peaking within about one to two hours. It does not bind plasma proteins to any meaningful degree. From the blood it is taken up by muscle, heart and brain through the SLC6A8 transporter, a process enhanced by insulin and therefore by eating carbohydrate and protein at the same time. Inside cells it is trapped as creatine and phosphocreatine. Excess creatine that tissues do not take up is filtered by the kidneys and excreted unchanged in urine. Each day a small, steady fraction of the body's creatine and phosphocreatine pool cyclizes non-enzymatically into creatinine, which leaves the cell, is filtered by the kidneys and is excreted in urine. Because supplementation enlarges the pool, more creatinine is made, which is why serum creatinine rises and estimated GFR may fall without a true change in kidney filtration. After stopping, muscle stores drift back to baseline over several weeks.", "sidefx": "In healthy adults, creatine has a good safety record across many trials. The most common effects are early weight gain from water retention and stomach upset, bloating or diarrhea, especially with large single servings. Concerns about cramps, dehydration and heat illness have not been supported by controlled studies, although athletes using stimulant-containing blends face separate risks. The most important clinical issue is laboratory: serum creatinine rises and can be mistaken for kidney disease. Case reports of kidney injury exist, mostly in people with existing kidney disease or other nephrotoxic exposures, so kidney disease and nephrotoxic drugs are cautions. A few case reports describe mania in people with bipolar disorder. Product contamination is a general sports-supplement risk.", "fun": "Creatine is named after the Greek word for flesh because Chevreul first found it in meat broth, and the creatinine on every basic metabolic panel is simply creatine that has closed into a ring on its own, with no enzyme needed.", "src": ["NIH Office of Dietary Supplements: Dietary Supplements for Exercise and Athletic Performance", "Harris RC, Soderlund K and Hultman E 1992, Clinical Science, elevation of creatine in resting and exercised muscle of normal subjects by creatine supplementation", "Kreider RB et al. 2017, Journal of the International Society of Sports Nutrition, position stand: safety and efficacy of creatine supplementation", "Wyss M and Kaddurah-Daouk R 2000, Physiological Reviews, creatine and creatinine metabolism", "Antonio J et al. 2021, Journal of the International Society of Sports Nutrition, common questions and misconceptions about creatine supplementation"]}, "caffeine": {"origin": "Caffeine is the most widely consumed psychoactive substance in the world, reaching people through coffee, tea, cacao, yerba mate, guarana and kola long before chemistry could name it. The German chemist Friedlieb Ferdinand Runge isolated it from coffee beans in 1819, by most accounts after Johann Wolfgang von Goethe handed him beans and suggested he analyze them, and Emil Fischer later worked out its purine structure and synthesized it in the 1890s as part of the purine chemistry that contributed to his 1902 Nobel Prize. Medicine adopted caffeine as a respiratory stimulant, and caffeine citrate is still a standard FDA-approved treatment for apnea of prematurity. The modern safety story is about concentration. Energy drinks spread worldwide from the late 1980s, pre-workout powders followed, and bulk powdered caffeine sold online put an entire day's worth of strong coffee into a spoonful. Deaths of young people linked to powdered caffeine in 2014 led to FDA warning letters and to 2018 guidance stating that highly concentrated and pure caffeine sold in bulk to consumers is unlawful as a dietary supplement.", "timeline": [["1819", "Friedlieb Ferdinand Runge isolates caffeine from coffee beans."], ["1890s", "Emil Fischer establishes caffeine's purine structure and synthesizes it."], ["1999", "FDA approves caffeine citrate for apnea of prematurity."], ["2004", "WADA removes caffeine from the prohibited list and places it on its monitoring program."], ["2018", "FDA issues guidance that highly concentrated and pure caffeine sold in bulk is unlawful as a dietary supplement."]], "cell": "At everyday exposures caffeine works by blocking adenosine receptors. Adenosine accumulates during waking activity and acts on A1 receptors, which are inhibitory and coupled to Gi proteins, to dampen release of glutamate, dopamine and acetylcholine, and on A2A receptors in the striatum, which sit beside dopamine D2 receptors and oppose their signaling. Caffeine fits these receptors because it shares the purine skeleton of adenosine but cannot activate them, so the brakes come off: arousal rises, perceived effort falls and dopamine signaling is indirectly enhanced. In blood vessels the effect is mixed, with mild constriction in the brain that explains why caffeine is added to some headache products. At the much higher concentrations reached in overdose, other actions take over. Caffeine inhibits phosphodiesterase enzymes, raising cyclic AMP; it promotes catecholamine release, producing a beta-adrenergic state; and it sensitizes ryanodine receptors that release calcium inside muscle cells. Beta-2 stimulation activates the sodium-potassium pump and shifts potassium into cells, causing hypokalemia, while lactate rises from beta-adrenergic metabolic effects. Together these explain the tachyarrhythmias, tremor, vomiting and seizures of serious poisoning.", "body": [["Brain", "Alertness and reduced fatigue at ordinary amounts; anxiety, agitation and seizures in overdose."], ["Heart", "Mild rise in heart rate and blood pressure normally; supraventricular and ventricular tachyarrhythmias in poisoning."], ["Gut", "Stimulates gastric acid and bowel movement; vomiting is an early and nearly universal sign of overdose."], ["Kidneys", "Mild diuresis; potassium loss into cells rather than urine drives hypokalemia in toxicity."], ["Skeletal muscle", "Improved endurance in many sport studies; tremor and rhabdomyolysis in severe poisoning."], ["Lungs", "Stimulates breathing, the basis of caffeine citrate for apnea of prematurity."]], "journey": "Caffeine is absorbed almost completely from the gut, with effects felt within about a quarter hour to 45 minutes and peak blood levels typically within one to two hours; powders and liquids absorb quickly, and very large pill ingestions can absorb erratically. It is water and fat soluble, distributes through total body water, crosses the blood-brain barrier and placenta easily and enters breast milk. The liver handles nearly all of it through CYP1A2, which removes methyl groups to form paraxanthine, the main metabolite in humans, plus theobromine and theophylline, all of which are themselves active. Clearance varies widely: smoking induces CYP1A2 and shortens the half-life, while pregnancy, oral contraceptives, liver disease and inhibitors such as fluvoxamine and ciprofloxacin prolong it. Newborns lack mature CYP1A2 and eliminate caffeine mostly unchanged in urine over days, which is exactly why a dose lasts so long in premature infants. Metabolites leave in the urine.", "sidefx": "Ordinary side effects are jitteriness, insomnia, anxiety, palpitations, heartburn and frequent urination, with withdrawal headache, fatigue and poor concentration after regular use stops. Poisoning is a different illness. Early signs are vomiting, tremor, restlessness, tachycardia and fast breathing; serious cases develop supraventricular or ventricular arrhythmias, hypotension, seizures, hypokalemia, hyperglycemia, metabolic acidosis and rhabdomyolysis, and cardiac arrest has occurred. Teens and young adults using pills, energy shots, pre-workouts or bulk powders are the typical patients, and stacking caffeine with other stimulants raises the risk.", "fun": "Caffeine is a plant pesticide that paralyzes and kills some feeding insects, yet citrus and coffee flowers lace their nectar with small amounts; a 2013 study found that honeybees fed caffeinated nectar remembered the flower scent better, so the plant uses the drug to make pollinators come back.", "src": ["StatPearls: Caffeine Toxicity", "FDA 2018 guidance: Highly Concentrated Caffeine in Dietary Supplements", "FDA OTC monograph, 21 CFR Part 340 (stimulant drug products)", "Wright and colleagues 2013, Science, caffeine in floral nectar enhances pollinator memory", "NIH NCCIH: Energy Drinks"]}, "beta-alanine": {"origin": "Beta-alanine is a natural amino acid that the body does not use to build proteins; its job is to combine with histidine to form carnosine, a dipeptide packed into skeletal muscle and especially rich in fast-twitch fibers. Carnosine was discovered in meat extract in 1900 by the Russian chemists Vladimir Gulevich and S. Amiradzhibi, and for most of the century it was a physiology curiosity found in the muscles of sprinting animals such as racehorses, greyhounds and whales that dive on held breath. The sports supplement era began when researchers led by Roger Harris, who had earlier helped popularize creatine, showed in the mid-2000s that dietary beta-alanine, rather than carnosine itself, raises muscle carnosine because beta-alanine supply is the limiting step. That finding turned an obscure amino acid into a staple of pre-workout formulas, where its tingling side effect has become part of the marketing as a sign the product is working. The International Society of Sports Nutrition published a position stand in 2015 concluding that it gives modest benefits for high-intensity efforts.", "timeline": [["1900", "Gulevich and Amiradzhibi discover carnosine in meat extract."], ["1930s-1950s", "Carnosine's role as a muscle buffer begins to be described."], ["2006", "Harris and colleagues report that oral beta-alanine raises human muscle carnosine."], ["2015", "The ISSN publishes a position stand on beta-alanine for exercise."], ["2010s", "Beta-alanine becomes a standard ingredient in pre-workout products."]], "cell": "Inside muscle fibers, carnosine synthase joins beta-alanine to L-histidine using ATP. Histidine is plentiful, so the amount of beta-alanine available sets how much carnosine is made. Carnosine's imidazole ring has a pKa close to the pH inside working muscle, which lets it pick up hydrogen ions released when glycolysis runs hard and hydrolysis of ATP outpaces oxidative recovery. By soaking up acid, carnosine may delay the fall in intracellular pH that impairs calcium handling and cross-bridge cycling and contributes to fatigue in efforts lasting roughly one to several minutes. Proposed secondary roles include increasing calcium sensitivity of contractile proteins, chelating metals and quenching reactive aldehydes, though their importance in humans is uncertain. Beta-alanine also binds MrgprD, a receptor on small sensory nerve fibers in the skin; activating these neurons produces the familiar prickling paresthesia, which rises with the size of a single dose. Because beta-alanine travels on the taurine transporter, high intakes may reduce taurine uptake in some tissues, an effect seen in animal studies but not clearly harmful in human trials.", "body": [["Skeletal muscle", "Carnosine content rises over weeks, improving buffering in fast-twitch fibers used for sprints and intervals."], ["Skin and sensory nerves", "Activation of MrgprD on sensory neurons produces transient tingling of face, neck and hands."], ["Heart", "Carnosine is present in cardiac muscle; no meaningful clinical effect is established."], ["Brain", "Carnosine and related compounds occur in brain tissue; cognitive claims rest on very limited evidence."], ["Kidneys", "Excrete a small fraction unchanged; no known kidney harm in healthy users."]], "journey": "After swallowing, beta-alanine is absorbed in the small intestine through amino acid transporters and reaches peak plasma levels within about an hour. Plasma levels then fall quickly as the amino acid is taken into muscle by the taurine transporter and a beta-alanine-specific uptake process, or is broken down by transamination to malonate semialdehyde, which feeds into acetyl-CoA metabolism. Only a small fraction appears in urine. Muscle carnosine builds gradually over several weeks of regular intake and then declines slowly over weeks to months after supplementation stops, so performance effects depend on cumulative loading, not on a single dose before training. Sustained-release tablets flatten the plasma peak, which reduces tingling and modestly reduces urinary loss.", "sidefx": "The signature effect is paresthesia, a prickling or itching sensation of the face, ears, neck and hands that begins within minutes and fades within about an hour or two. It is harmless but can be alarming to someone who does not expect it and can be mistaken for an allergic reaction. Rashes and itching have been reported rarely. Large studies have not identified meaningful organ toxicity in healthy adults over periods of weeks to months, though long-term safety, pregnancy safety and use in teenagers are not well studied. In practice, most adverse events linked to pre-workout drinks come from the caffeine and other stimulants combined with beta-alanine.", "fun": "Some of the highest muscle carnosine levels in nature are found in animals built for bursts or breath-holding, such as racehorses, greyhounds and diving whales, which is part of what first suggested carnosine acts as a muscle acid buffer.", "src": ["Trexler and colleagues 2015, Journal of the International Society of Sports Nutrition, position stand on beta-alanine", "Harris and colleagues 2006, Amino Acids, beta-alanine and muscle carnosine", "NIH Office of Dietary Supplements: Dietary Supplements for Exercise and Athletic Performance", "Boldyrev, Aldini and Derave 2013, Physiological Reviews, physiology of carnosine"]}, "hmb": {"origin": "HMB sits on a minor branch of leucine metabolism: most leucine is processed in mitochondria, but a small share of its breakdown product alpha-ketoisocaproate is diverted in the cytosol to HMB. Interest in it as a supplement came from animal science. Steven Nissen and colleagues at Iowa State University studied leucine metabolites in livestock and reported in the 1990s that HMB seemed to improve lean growth, then ran human resistance-training studies in the mid-1990s that suggested less muscle damage and greater strength gains. HMB entered the sports supplement market soon after, and later several companies added it to adult nutrition shakes and wound-healing powders aimed at older and malnourished patients. The early dramatic results did not hold up well in trained athletes, and a few later studies reporting very large effects drew criticism, so modern reviews describe the evidence as mixed: modest benefit in untrained people and older adults, small or none in experienced lifters. The International Society of Sports Nutrition published a position stand on HMB in 2013.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Iowa State researchers study HMB in livestock and then in human resistance training."], ["Mid-1990s", "HMB appears as a sports supplement."], ["2000s", "HMB is added to some oral nutrition formulas and wound-healing powders."], ["2013", "The ISSN publishes a position stand on HMB."], ["2010s", "Meta-analyses describe small, inconsistent effects, smaller in trained athletes."]], "cell": "Leucine is transaminated in muscle to alpha-ketoisocaproate. Most of that is oxidized in mitochondria by the branched-chain alpha-keto acid dehydrogenase complex, but in the cytosol, mainly in the liver, KIC dioxygenase converts a small share to HMB. HMB is proposed to act in three ways. First, it may activate mTORC1, the nutrient-sensing kinase that switches on translation initiation and protein synthesis, partly through phosphorylation of p70S6 kinase and 4E-BP1. Second, it may blunt muscle protein breakdown by reducing activity of the ubiquitin-proteasome pathway, which tags damaged or unneeded proteins for destruction and is especially active in muscle wasting, immobilization and inflammation; animal and cell studies show reduced expression of breakdown enzymes. Third, HMB can be converted to HMB-CoA and then to HMG-CoA, a precursor for cholesterol synthesis, which has been proposed to help repair muscle cell membranes after damaging exercise. Many of these findings come from cell culture and animal models, and the human effects are much smaller than the mechanistic story implies.", "body": [["Skeletal muscle", "Proposed to reduce breakdown and support synthesis; small gains in lean mass in some trials."], ["Liver", "Main site of HMB formation from leucine-derived KIC."], ["Kidneys", "Excrete a substantial portion of HMB unchanged in urine."], ["Blood lipids", "Early studies suggested small cholesterol and blood pressure changes; not consistently replicated."]], "journey": "HMB is absorbed from the gut, with the free acid reaching peak plasma levels faster and higher than the calcium salt, which peaks in roughly one to two hours. It circulates unbound to any specialized carrier and is taken up by muscle and other tissues. Part of the dose is converted to HMB-CoA and enters the mevalonate pathway or is otherwise oxidized, while a meaningful fraction leaves unchanged in the urine. The plasma half-life is a few hours, and any effect on body composition depends on regular intake over weeks rather than a single dose. The body's own production from leucine is small compared with supplement amounts.", "sidefx": "Short-term studies in young and older adults report few side effects beyond occasional stomach upset, bloating or constipation, and blood tests of liver and kidney function have generally stayed normal over weeks to months. Long-term safety, safety in pregnancy and use in teenagers are not well studied. Because HMB is often combined with creatine, caffeine or other compounds, symptoms after a product usually come from another ingredient.", "fun": "HMB research began in farm animals: investigators looking for ways to improve lean growth in livestock noticed effects of leucine metabolites before anyone tested HMB in gym-goers.", "src": ["Wilson and colleagues 2013, Journal of the International Society of Sports Nutrition, ISSN position stand on HMB", "Nissen and colleagues 1996, Journal of Applied Physiology, HMB and resistance training", "NIH Office of Dietary Supplements: Dietary Supplements for Exercise and Athletic Performance", "Rowlands and Thomson 2009, Journal of Strength and Conditioning Research, HMB meta-analysis"]}, "acetyl-l-carnitine": {"origin": "Carnitine was discovered in meat extract in 1905 by the Russian chemists Vladimir Gulevich and Robert Krimberg, and its name comes from the Latin carnis, meaning flesh. For decades nobody knew what it did. In the late 1940s and 1950s the entomologist Gottfried Fraenkel found that mealworm larvae died without a dietary factor he called vitamin BT, which turned out to be carnitine, and Irving Fritz and others then showed that carnitine is needed for the oxidation of long-chain fatty acids by mitochondria. In humans the liver and kidneys make enough carnitine from lysine and methionine, so it is not a true vitamin except in certain inherited disorders, dialysis and drug-induced deficiency, where prescription levocarnitine is used. The acetyl ester attracted attention in Italy, where pharmaceutical research in the 1980s and 1990s explored acetyl-L-carnitine for age-related cognitive decline and neuropathy, and it became a prescription product in some countries. In the United States it is sold as a supplement. Its clinical record is uneven: dementia reviews were disappointing, depression and diabetic neuropathy data are modest, and a large chemotherapy trial produced a harmful signal.", "timeline": [["1905", "Gulevich and Krimberg discover carnitine in meat extract."], ["1950s", "Fraenkel identifies carnitine as an essential insect growth factor, and its role in fatty acid oxidation is established."], ["1980s-1990s", "Acetyl-L-carnitine is studied in Italy for cognitive decline and neuropathy."], ["2013", "SWOG S0715 reports more taxane neuropathy with acetyl-L-carnitine than placebo."], ["2013", "Researchers link dietary carnitine to TMAO production by gut bacteria."]], "cell": "Long-chain fatty acids cannot cross the inner mitochondrial membrane on their own. Carnitine palmitoyltransferase 1 on the outer membrane swaps the CoA of a fatty acyl-CoA for carnitine, the acylcarnitine is carried across by the carnitine-acylcarnitine translocase, and carnitine palmitoyltransferase 2 inside regenerates acyl-CoA for beta-oxidation. Carnitine also acts as a buffer, accepting excess acyl groups and acetyl groups so that free CoA stays available; acetylcarnitine is the result of that buffering when acetyl-CoA accumulates. Supplemental acetyl-L-carnitine is broken down into carnitine and acetyl groups, and the acetyl group can contribute to acetyl-CoA pools and, in neurons, to acetylcholine synthesis. Animal studies suggest effects on nerve growth factor signaling, mitochondrial membrane function and regeneration of injured peripheral nerves, which explain the interest in neuropathy. In the taxane trial, however, the human result ran opposite to the animal hypothesis. Carnitine has also been reported to inhibit entry of thyroid hormone into cell nuclei, which is why it has been studied in hyperthyroidism and why it is a caution with thyroid replacement.", "body": [["Muscle and heart", "Rely on carnitine-dependent fatty acid oxidation for much of their energy."], ["Brain", "Acetyl form crosses the blood-brain barrier; cognitive and mood effects are modest or unproven."], ["Peripheral nerves", "Studied for diabetic neuropathy with modest results; linked to more taxane neuropathy in a trial."], ["Kidneys", "Make carnitine and reabsorb it efficiently; losses occur in dialysis."], ["Gut", "Bacteria convert unabsorbed carnitine to trimethylamine, which the liver oxidizes to TMAO."]], "journey": "Oral acetyl-L-carnitine is absorbed in the small intestine by active transport and passive diffusion, and absorption becomes less efficient as the amount rises, so much of a supplement dose is not absorbed. Some is hydrolyzed to carnitine in the gut wall and blood. Unabsorbed carnitine reaches the colon, where bacteria convert it to trimethylamine, which is absorbed and oxidized in the liver to trimethylamine N-oxide; trimethylamine is also responsible for the fishy body odor some users notice. Absorbed carnitine and acetylcarnitine distribute into muscle, heart and brain through the OCTN2 transporter. The kidneys filter carnitine and normally reabsorb almost all of it, excreting the excess when blood levels rise, so supplements mainly increase urinary loss once stores are full.", "sidefx": "Most users notice nothing or have mild nausea, stomach upset, diarrhea or restlessness. A fishy body odor can occur from trimethylamine. Seizures have been reported in people with and without a seizure history, and agitation has been described. The most important finding is from the SWOG S0715 trial, in which women receiving taxane chemotherapy for breast cancer who took acetyl-L-carnitine developed more neuropathy than those on placebo. Case reports describe raised INR with carnitine and coumarin anticoagulants, and carnitine may blunt the effects of thyroid hormone.", "fun": "Carnitine was first recognized as essential not in people but in mealworms: Fraenkel's beetle larvae could not grow without it, which is why it was briefly called vitamin BT, with the T standing for Tenebrio, the mealworm's genus.", "src": ["NIH Office of Dietary Supplements: Carnitine fact sheet", "Hershman and colleagues 2013, Journal of Clinical Oncology, SWOG S0715", "Hudson and Tabet, Cochrane review: Acetyl-L-carnitine for dementia", "Koeth and colleagues 2013, Nature Medicine, carnitine and TMAO", "ASCO guideline: Prevention and management of chemotherapy-induced peripheral neuropathy"]}, "l-arginine": {"origin": "Arginine was isolated in 1886 by the Swiss chemist Ernst Schulze and his colleague Ernst Steiger from lupine seedlings, and its name comes from the silvery appearance of its salts. Its fame came a century later. In the 1980s Robert Furchgott showed that blood vessels relax only when the endothelium is present, and the endothelium-derived relaxing factor was identified as nitric oxide; Salvador Moncada's group showed in 1988 that vascular cells make nitric oxide from L-arginine. Furchgott, Louis Ignarro and Ferid Murad shared the 1998 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine for nitric oxide signaling, and the supplement industry quickly marketed arginine as a way to boost nitric oxide for heart health, erections and the gym pump. The clinical reality has been modest. Blood pressure falls slightly in pooled trials, but the VINTAGE MI trial, published in 2006, gave arginine after acute myocardial infarction and was stopped early after more deaths occurred in the arginine group with no improvement in vascular stiffness or heart function. Medicine still uses arginine in narrower ways: intravenous arginine hydrochloride as a growth hormone stimulation test, arginine in urea cycle disorders, and specialist use in mitochondrial disease.", "timeline": [["1886", "Schulze and Steiger isolate arginine from lupine seedlings."], ["1980", "Furchgott describes endothelium-dependent relaxation of blood vessels."], ["1988", "Moncada and colleagues show that vascular cells make nitric oxide from L-arginine."], ["1998", "Furchgott, Ignarro and Murad receive the Nobel Prize for nitric oxide signaling."], ["2006", "VINTAGE MI reports no benefit and excess deaths with arginine after heart attack."]], "cell": "In endothelial cells, endothelial nitric oxide synthase oxidizes the guanidino nitrogen of L-arginine, using oxygen, NADPH and the cofactor tetrahydrobiopterin, to produce nitric oxide and citrulline. Nitric oxide diffuses into the underlying smooth muscle and activates soluble guanylate cyclase, which raises cyclic GMP, activates protein kinase G and lowers intracellular calcium, relaxing the vessel. This is the same pathway that nitroglycerin feeds directly and that PDE5 inhibitors prolong by blocking cGMP breakdown, which explains additive blood pressure lowering. The puzzle called the arginine paradox is that cells already contain far more arginine than nitric oxide synthase needs, so extra arginine should not matter, yet sometimes it does; explanations include separate arginine pools near the enzyme and competition from the endogenous inhibitor asymmetric dimethylarginine. Arginase competes for the same substrate, converting arginine to ornithine and urea for the urea cycle and polyamine synthesis. Arginine also donates its amidino group to glycine to start creatine synthesis and depolarizes pancreatic beta cells and pituitary cells, triggering insulin and growth hormone release, the basis of the IV stimulation test.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Precursor for endothelial nitric oxide; small blood pressure reductions in pooled trials."], ["Heart", "No benefit after acute myocardial infarction in a randomized trial, with a harm signal."], ["Liver and gut", "Arginase breaks down much of an oral dose; part of the urea cycle."], ["Pancreas and pituitary", "Stimulates insulin, glucagon and growth hormone release."], ["Penis", "Nitric oxide drives erection through cGMP, the target of PDE5 inhibitors; supplement effects are modest."], ["Kidneys", "Major site of arginine synthesis from citrulline; filter and reabsorb arginine."]], "journey": "Oral arginine is absorbed in the small intestine by cationic amino acid transporters, but much of it is metabolized by arginase in the gut wall and liver before reaching the circulation, so oral bioavailability is limited and variable. This first-pass loss is why many products now use citrulline, which escapes the liver and is converted to arginine in the kidneys, raising plasma arginine more effectively. Blood levels peak within about one to two hours and fall over a few hours. Arginine is distributed into cells through the same transporters and is used for protein synthesis, nitric oxide, creatine, polyamines and the urea cycle. The kidneys filter it and reabsorb nearly all of it, and its nitrogen eventually leaves the body as urea.", "sidefx": "Common effects are nausea, bloating, abdominal cramps and diarrhea, which rise with larger single amounts. Lightheadedness and low blood pressure can occur, particularly when arginine is combined with PDE5 inhibitors, nitrates or antihypertensives. Intravenous arginine hydrochloride can cause hyperkalemia and metabolic acidosis and causes tissue damage if it leaks from the vein, and pediatric dosing errors with the IV product have been fatal. Theoretical concerns include triggering herpes outbreaks, because herpes viruses need arginine to replicate in laboratory studies, and worsening airway inflammation in asthma. The most important clinical finding is the VINTAGE MI result after heart attack.", "fun": "Nitric oxide, the molecule arginine is marketed to boost, was named Molecule of the Year by Science in 1992, a striking honor for a small gas once known mainly as an air pollutant.", "src": ["Schulman and colleagues 2006, JAMA, VINTAGE MI trial", "Palmer, Ashton and Moncada 1988, Nature, vascular endothelial cells synthesize nitric oxide from L-arginine", "Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 1998, nitric oxide as a signaling molecule", "Arginine hydrochloride injection FDA label"]}, "bcaa": {"origin": "The three branched-chain amino acids were identified one at a time. Leucine was first isolated from cheese by the French chemist Joseph Proust in 1819 and named by Henri Braconnot in 1820 from the Greek for white, after its white crystals. Valine was isolated by Emil Fischer in 1901, and isoleucine by Felix Ehrlich in the early 1900s. Medicine met them through disease: in 1954 John Menkes and colleagues described infants with a fatal neurologic illness whose urine smelled like maple syrup, later traced to failure of the enzyme complex that breaks down branched-chain keto acids. In the 1970s and 1980s, BCAAs were explored in liver failure because patients with cirrhosis have low BCAA and high aromatic amino acid levels, a pattern thought to contribute to hepatic encephalopathy, and the central fatigue hypothesis proposed that BCAAs could limit brain tryptophan uptake during exercise. Discovery of leucine as a direct signal for mTOR-driven protein synthesis made BCAAs a sports staple, although careful studies show that BCAAs alone cannot replace complete protein. A trial in amyotrophic lateral sclerosis in the early 1990s was stopped with a possible excess of deaths.", "timeline": [["1819", "Proust isolates leucine from cheese."], ["1901", "Emil Fischer isolates valine."], ["1904", "Felix Ehrlich isolates isoleucine."], ["1954", "Menkes describes maple syrup urine disease."], ["1993", "An Italian BCAA trial in ALS is stopped early with a possible excess of deaths."], ["2017", "A Cochrane review finds BCAAs may improve hepatic encephalopathy symptoms."]], "cell": "Most amino acids are processed first by the liver, but the liver has little branched-chain aminotransferase, so BCAAs pass through to skeletal muscle, which takes up and transaminates them. The aminotransferase moves their amino group to alpha-ketoglutarate, making glutamate and branched-chain keto acids, and the branched-chain alpha-keto acid dehydrogenase complex then commits the keto acids to oxidation; leucine ends as acetyl-CoA and acetoacetate, valine as succinyl-CoA and isoleucine as both. Leucine also works as a signal. Inside cells, the protein Sestrin2 binds leucine and releases its hold on GATOR2, which allows mTORC1 to activate at the lysosome and switch on translation through S6 kinase and 4E-BP1. That switch explains the brief rise in muscle protein synthesis after BCAAs, but building protein still requires all the essential amino acids, so the response fades when the other amino acids run short. At the blood-brain barrier, BCAAs share the LAT1 transporter with tryptophan, tyrosine, phenylalanine and levodopa, which is the basis for both the central fatigue hypothesis and the levodopa interaction.", "body": [["Skeletal muscle", "Main site of BCAA metabolism; leucine activates mTORC1 to stimulate protein synthesis."], ["Liver", "Little BCAA transamination; BCAAs studied for hepatic encephalopathy in cirrhosis."], ["Brain", "Compete with aromatic amino acids and levodopa for LAT1 transport."], ["Pancreas", "Leucine can stimulate insulin release."], ["Kidneys", "Excrete nitrogen as urea; protein limits apply in kidney disease."]], "journey": "BCAAs from supplements are free amino acids, so they are absorbed rapidly in the small intestine through neutral amino acid transporters and appear in blood within about half an hour, peaking within an hour. Because the liver removes little of them, blood levels rise more than with most amino acids. Muscle takes them up and transaminates them; the keto acids are partly released and oxidized in liver and other tissues. Their carbon skeletons feed the citric acid cycle, ketone production and glucose production, and their nitrogen is carried as glutamine and alanine to the liver for urea synthesis and urinary excretion. Blood levels return toward baseline within a few hours.", "sidefx": "Healthy people usually tolerate BCAAs well, with occasional nausea, bloating or diarrhea. The bigger issues are specific settings: maple syrup urine disease, where BCAAs and their keto acids accumulate and cause neurologic injury; ALS, where an early trial raised concern about harm; and Parkinson disease, where BCAAs can compete with levodopa for transport. Observational studies associate high circulating BCAA levels with insulin resistance and type 2 diabetes, though this may reflect impaired breakdown rather than intake, and supplement effects are uncertain.", "fun": "Maple syrup urine disease gets its name from the sweet, burnt-sugar smell of affected babies' urine and earwax, caused by a breakdown product of isoleucine called sotolone, the same aroma compound found in fenugreek and maple syrup.", "src": ["Menkes, Hurst and Craig 1954, Pediatrics, a new syndrome: progressive familial infantile cerebral dysfunction associated with an unusual urinary substance", "Cochrane review: Branched-chain amino acids for people with hepatic encephalopathy", "Wolfe 2017, Journal of the International Society of Sports Nutrition", "Wolfson and colleagues 2016, Science, Sestrin2 is a leucine sensor for mTORC1", "NIH Office of Dietary Supplements: Dietary Supplements for Exercise and Athletic Performance"]}, "taurine": {"origin": "Taurine was first isolated in 1827 by the German scientists Friedrich Tiedemann and Leopold Gmelin from ox bile, and its name comes from Bos taurus, the ox, not from bull testicles as energy drink rumors claim. For more than a century it was seen as a simple end product of sulfur metabolism. Its importance became clear through cats. In the 1970s Kenneth Hayes and colleagues showed that cats fed taurine-poor diets developed retinal degeneration, and in 1987 Paul Pion and colleagues reported that a common heart muscle disease in cats, dilated cardiomyopathy, was caused by taurine deficiency and reversed with supplementation, which changed commercial pet food worldwide. Human infant formulas, which unlike breast milk contained little taurine, began adding it in the 1980s. Energy drinks made taurine a household word from the late 1980s onward, and the drug is approved in Japan for heart failure. In 2023 a widely discussed study reported that taurine levels decline with age and that supplementation extended lifespan in mice and worms, which revived aging claims that have not been tested in human outcome trials.", "timeline": [["1827", "Tiedemann and Gmelin isolate taurine from ox bile."], ["1975", "Retinal degeneration from taurine deficiency is described in cats."], ["1987", "Pion and colleagues link feline dilated cardiomyopathy to taurine deficiency."], ["1980s", "Taurine is added to infant formula."], ["2023", "A study reports taurine supplementation extends lifespan in mice and worms."]], "cell": "Taurine is one of the most abundant free amino compounds in heart, muscle, retina and brain, yet it is not built into proteins because it carries a sulfonic acid group rather than a carboxyl group. Cells make it from cysteine by oxidizing the sulfur to cysteine sulfinic acid, decarboxylating to hypotaurine and oxidizing again; humans have limited capacity, so diet contributes substantially. Cells concentrate taurine through the sodium and chloride dependent taurine transporter and use it as an osmolyte: releasing taurine helps a swollen cell shrink, and accumulating it protects a shrinking cell, which is important in kidney medulla and brain. In the liver, taurine is joined to bile acids to make conjugated bile salts that emulsify dietary fat. In heart and skeletal muscle it modulates calcium entry and storage, and in the brain it acts as a weak agonist at GABA-A and glycine receptors, an inhibitory rather than stimulating effect. Inside mitochondria, taurine modifies specific transfer RNAs, a step needed for accurate translation of mitochondrial proteins; loss of this modification is linked to the mitochondrial diseases MELAS and MERRF.", "body": [["Liver and gut", "Forms taurine-conjugated bile salts that help digest fat."], ["Heart", "Modulates calcium handling; deficiency causes cardiomyopathy in cats; approved for heart failure in Japan."], ["Brain and retina", "Inhibitory neuromodulator; essential for photoreceptor health in cats."], ["Kidneys", "Regulate the body's taurine pool by adjusting reabsorption; osmolyte in medulla."], ["Skeletal muscle", "High concentrations; small exercise effects in trials."]], "journey": "Taurine from food or supplements is absorbed in the small intestine by the taurine transporter and other beta amino acid carriers, with blood levels peaking about one to two hours after a single oral amount in small studies and a plasma half-life of roughly an hour or so. It is taken up into tissues, where most of the body's taurine is stored at concentrations far above blood. The kidneys control total body taurine by adjusting how much they reabsorb, excreting more when intake is high. A portion is conjugated to bile acids in the liver and secreted in bile; much of that is reabsorbed in the enterohepatic circulation, and gut bacteria break down the rest.", "sidefx": "At the amounts found in food and energy drinks, taurine has a reassuring safety record and no consistent side effects; mild stomach upset is possible with large supplement amounts. Most adverse events attributed to energy drinks, such as palpitations, arrhythmias, insomnia and anxiety, are explained by caffeine. Long-term safety of high-amount supplements, use in pregnancy and use in children beyond infant formula are not well studied.", "fun": "Cats are obligate taurine eaters: they cannot make enough, and before pet foods were reformulated in the late 1980s, many house cats developed heart failure and blindness from taurine deficiency.", "src": ["Pion, Kittleson, Rogers and Morris 1987, Science, myocardial failure in cats associated with low plasma taurine", "Singh and colleagues 2023, Science, taurine deficiency as a driver of aging", "EFSA 2009 Scientific Opinion on taurine and glucuronolactone in energy drinks", "Schaffer and Kim 2018, Biomolecules and Therapeutics, effects and mechanisms of taurine"]}, "d-ribose": {"origin": "Ribose is one of the oldest molecules in biology, part of RNA and of the energy carriers ATP, NADH and FAD. It was first made synthetically in Emil Fischer's laboratory in the late nineteenth century during his systematic work on sugars, before anyone knew its biological role, and the name comes from arabinose, another sugar, by rearranging letters. In 1909 Phoebus Levene and Walter Jacobs at the Rockefeller Institute identified D-ribose as the sugar in yeast nucleic acid, which is now known as RNA. In the second half of the twentieth century, cardiac researchers studying ischemia noticed that heart muscle rebuilds its ATP pool slowly after oxygen deprivation, partly because making new nucleotides depends on ribose supply. Experimental studies in animals suggested that giving ribose sped this recovery, which led to small human trials in angina and heart failure in the 1990s and 2000s and to marketing of D-ribose powder for energy, fibromyalgia and chronic fatigue. Rare patients with myoadenylate deaminase deficiency, who have exercise-related muscle cramps, were also studied. The human evidence never grew beyond small trials.", "timeline": [["1891", "Ribose is synthesized in Emil Fischer's laboratory during his sugar chemistry work."], ["1909", "Levene and Jacobs identify D-ribose in yeast nucleic acid."], ["1970s-1980s", "Animal studies suggest ribose speeds ATP recovery in ischemic heart muscle."], ["1990s", "Small human studies test ribose in angina and myoadenylate deaminase deficiency."], ["2006", "A small open-label pilot study reports benefits in fibromyalgia and chronic fatigue."]], "cell": "Every nucleotide needs a ribose. Cells normally make ribose-5-phosphate from glucose through the oxidative pentose phosphate pathway, whose first step, glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase, is slow in heart and skeletal muscle. Ribose-5-phosphate is converted by PRPP synthetase to phosphoribosyl pyrophosphate, the activated sugar used both to build purines from scratch and to salvage free bases such as hypoxanthine and adenine back into nucleotides. During ischemia or very intense exercise, ATP breaks down to ADP and AMP and then to adenosine, inosine and hypoxanthine, which can leak out of the cell; the total adenine nucleotide pool shrinks and takes days to rebuild. Supplemental D-ribose enters cells, is phosphorylated by ribokinase to ribose-5-phosphate and skips the slow G6PD step, so PRPP rises and salvage and synthesis speed up in experimental models. Whether this translates to better symptoms in people is unproven. Ribose also lowers blood glucose for reasons that are not fully understood, possibly greater insulin release or increased glucose use. In laboratory studies ribose is a much more reactive glycating sugar than glucose, forming advanced glycation end products, a theoretical concern for long-term high intake.", "body": [["Heart", "Studied for faster ATP recovery after ischemia; small human trials in angina and heart failure."], ["Skeletal muscle", "Proposed faster nucleotide recovery after intense exercise; little performance benefit in healthy people."], ["Pancreas and blood glucose", "Can lower blood glucose, possibly through insulin release."], ["Gut", "Large amounts cause diarrhea and stomach upset."], ["Kidneys", "A portion of a large dose spills into urine."]], "journey": "D-ribose is absorbed rapidly and largely completely from the small intestine, with blood levels peaking within about half an hour to an hour. It enters cells, where ribokinase traps it as ribose-5-phosphate, and it is then used for nucleotide synthesis or converted through the non-oxidative pentose phosphate pathway into glycolytic intermediates and eventually burned for energy. Plasma levels fall quickly, and with larger amounts a portion of the sugar exceeds kidney reabsorption and is lost in the urine. The body's own production from glucose continues regardless of supplements.", "sidefx": "Mild diarrhea, nausea, stomach discomfort and headache are the most commonly reported effects and rise with larger single amounts. Ribose can lower blood glucose, causing shakiness, sweating, hunger and confusion, a particular concern for people using insulin or sulfonylureas. Long-term safety, safety in pregnancy and the clinical meaning of ribose's high glycating reactivity in laboratory studies are unknown.", "fun": "The name ribose was coined by rearranging the letters of arabinose, the related sugar from gum arabic that chemists already knew, so one of life's most important molecules is named with a word game.", "src": ["Levene and Jacobs 1909, Berichte der deutschen chemischen Gesellschaft, on the sugar of yeast nucleic acid", "Natural Medicines-style monograph facts: D-ribose", "Teitelbaum, Johnson and St Cyr 2006, Journal of Alternative and Complementary Medicine", "Pauly and Pepine 2003, Journal of Cardiovascular Pharmacology and Therapeutics, D-ribose as a supplement for cardiac energy metabolism"]}, "whey-protein": {"origin": "Whey is the liquid left when milk is curdled for cheese, and people have drunk it for thousands of years; it shows up in the old nursery rhyme of Little Miss Muffet eating her curds and whey. Physicians of earlier centuries prescribed whey cures, and spa towns in Europe served it to visitors. For most of the industrial era, however, whey was a waste problem for cheese makers. It was fed to pigs, spread on fields or released into waterways, where its sugar and protein load could deplete oxygen and harm fish. Membrane filtration technology developed in the second half of the twentieth century allowed manufacturers to separate and dry whey protein into concentrates and isolates, turning a pollutant into a valuable product. Bodybuilding and sports nutrition made whey powder a mass-market item from the 1980s and 1990s, and research on muscle protein synthesis explained why: whey is a complete protein that digests quickly and is rich in leucine. Hydrolyzed whey is also used in some infant formulas and medical nutrition products. Independent product testing has periodically found heavy metals in some protein powders, and lawsuits have challenged products that boosted protein numbers with cheap added amino acids.", "timeline": [["Ancient times", "Whey is consumed as a by-product of cheese making and used in folk remedies."], ["18th-19th centuries", "Whey cures are offered at European spas."], ["1970s", "Membrane filtration allows commercial whey protein concentrates."], ["1980s-1990s", "Whey powders become central to sports and bodybuilding nutrition."], ["2010s", "Meta-analyses show protein supplements add small lean mass gains with resistance training."]], "cell": "Milk protein is roughly four-fifths casein and one-fifth whey. Casein clots in the acid stomach and releases amino acids slowly, while whey proteins stay soluble, empty quickly and are digested by pepsin and pancreatic proteases into amino acids and small peptides that enter the blood within minutes to an hour. This fast, high spike in essential amino acids, especially leucine, matters at the cellular level. In muscle, leucine is sensed by Sestrin2 and activates mTORC1 at the lysosome, which phosphorylates S6 kinase and 4E-BP1 and switches on translation of new muscle proteins. Unlike isolated BCAAs, whey also supplies every other essential amino acid, so the synthetic response can be sustained. Resistance exercise sensitizes muscle to amino acids for many hours, which is why protein intake around training is studied. Whey peptides additionally stimulate insulin and the gut incretin hormones GLP-1 and GIP, and bioactive components such as lactoferrin, immunoglobulins and alpha-lactalbumin have been studied for immune and sleep effects with limited human evidence.", "body": [["Skeletal muscle", "Leucine-rich amino acid supply stimulates muscle protein synthesis, especially after resistance exercise."], ["Gut", "Fast gastric emptying and digestion; lactose in concentrates can cause gas and diarrhea in lactose intolerance."], ["Pancreas", "Whey peptides raise insulin and incretin hormones, modestly lowering after-meal glucose."], ["Immune system", "Milk proteins trigger IgE-mediated allergy in cow's milk protein allergy."], ["Kidneys", "Excrete nitrogen as urea; protein limits apply in chronic kidney disease."]], "journey": "Whey protein is broken down in the stomach and small intestine into amino acids, dipeptides and tripeptides, which are absorbed by intestinal transporters. Blood amino acids rise within about half an hour and peak around an hour after a whey drink, faster and higher than after casein or whole-food protein. The liver takes up many amino acids, while the branched-chain amino acids pass largely to muscle. Amino acids are built into new proteins or, when supply exceeds need, are deaminated; the carbon skeletons are burned for energy or turned into glucose or fat, and the nitrogen is converted to urea in the liver and excreted by the kidneys.", "sidefx": "Most people tolerate whey well. Bloating, gas, cramps and diarrhea are common in people with lactose intolerance, especially with concentrates. In cow's milk protein allergy, whey can cause hives, vomiting, wheezing and anaphylaxis. Observational reports and case series link whey supplements to acne flares in some young adults. Independent testing has found lead, cadmium or arsenic in some protein powders, and multi-ingredient products may contain stimulants or other additives.", "fun": "Before filtration technology made it valuable, whey was such a disposal headache that cheese plants were sometimes cited for polluting rivers with it; the same liquid is now one of the most profitable parts of the dairy industry.", "src": ["Morton and colleagues 2018, British Journal of Sports Medicine, protein supplementation and muscle mass meta-analysis", "Jager and colleagues 2017, Journal of the International Society of Sports Nutrition, position stand on protein and exercise", "Boirie and colleagues 1997, PNAS, slow and fast dietary proteins", "NIH Office of Dietary Supplements: Dietary Supplements for Exercise and Athletic Performance"]}, "ecdysterone": {"origin": "Ecdysteroids are the hormones that make insects and crustaceans molt, a process called ecdysis. In 1954 the German biochemists Adolf Butenandt and Peter Karlson isolated ecdysone from silkworm pupae, a task that reportedly required hundreds of kilograms of pupae to yield milligrams of hormone. In the 1960s Japanese researchers discovered that some plants contain large amounts of ecdysteroids, apparently as a defense that disrupts the development of insects feeding on them; 20-hydroxyecdysone, also called ecdysterone, turned out to be common in ferns, spinach, quinoa and plants such as Rhaponticum (Leuzea) and Cyanotis. Soviet researchers studied Leuzea extracts and ecdysterone as tonics and performance aids, and the compounds reached Western bodybuilding magazines by the 1990s. Interest revived in the 2010s when German researchers reported estrogen receptor beta activity and a small WADA-funded study reported strength gains, prompting WADA to add ecdysterone to its monitoring program. Turkesterone, from Ajuga turkestanica, became a social media sensation around 2021 despite almost no human trials, and analyses of commercial products have found content that frequently differs from the label.", "timeline": [["1954", "Butenandt and Karlson isolate ecdysone from silkworm pupae."], ["1960s", "Ecdysteroids are discovered in plants, including 20-hydroxyecdysone."], ["1980s", "Soviet researchers study Leuzea and ecdysterone as tonics."], ["2019", "A WADA-funded study reports strength gains with ecdysterone and recommends anti-doping attention."], ["2020s", "Ecdysterone is on the WADA monitoring program; turkesterone trends on social media."]], "cell": "In insects, 20-hydroxyecdysone enters cells and binds the ecdysone receptor, a nuclear receptor that partners with ultraspiracle protein to switch on gene cascades for molting and metamorphosis. Mammals have no ecdysone receptor, and ecdysteroids do not bind the androgen receptor, so they are not anabolic steroids in the usual sense. Their proposed actions in mammals are indirect. Cell studies suggest that ecdysterone activates estrogen receptor beta, which in muscle cells can promote hypertrophy, and that ecdysteroids stimulate the PI3K and Akt signaling pathway that drives protein synthesis and glucose uptake. Rodent studies report increased muscle fiber size and grip strength and reduced blood glucose, but the doses, routes and models vary, and the human data consist of a handful of small trials with mixed results. Turkesterone differs from ecdysterone by an extra hydroxyl group and is claimed to be more potent based on older animal studies, a claim not tested in good human trials.", "body": [["Skeletal muscle", "Anabolic effects in animal and cell studies; uncertain in humans."], ["Liver", "Partly metabolizes ecdysteroids; liver injury in users should raise suspicion of hidden steroids."], ["Blood glucose", "Glucose lowering in animal studies; human relevance unknown."], ["Kidneys", "Excrete ecdysterone and metabolites in urine, which allows doping detection."]], "journey": "Ecdysterone is poorly absorbed from the gut, with low oral bioavailability in animal studies, and human pharmacokinetic data are limited. Peak blood levels appear within a few hours after oral intake in small human studies. Gut bacteria and the liver modify the molecule by removing hydroxyl groups and cleaving the side chain, producing metabolites such as 14-deoxy derivatives. The parent compound and metabolites are excreted in urine and feces, and anti-doping laboratories have developed urine methods to detect them. Turkesterone pharmacokinetics in humans are essentially undescribed.", "sidefx": "Small human studies have not reported significant adverse effects over weeks, but they were too small and short to detect uncommon problems. Long-term safety, safety in pregnancy and safety in adolescents are unknown. The greatest practical risk with natural anabolic products is what else may be in the capsule: muscle-building supplements are among the categories most often found adulterated with anabolic steroids, prohormones or SARMs, which can cause liver injury, acne, hypertension, mood changes and suppression of the body's own testosterone.", "fun": "Some plants appear to make ecdysteroids as chemical warfare: by packing their leaves with insect molting hormone, they may disrupt the development of caterpillars and other insects that eat them.", "src": ["Isenmann and colleagues 2019, Archives of Toxicology", "Parr and colleagues 2014, Molecular Nutrition and Food Research", "Butenandt and Karlson 1954, Zeitschrift fur Naturforschung, isolation of ecdysone", "Dinan and Lafont 2006, Journal of Endocrinology, effects of ecdysteroids on mammals", "WADA Monitoring Program"]}, "sodium-bicarbonate-sport": {"origin": "Sodium bicarbonate has been a household chemical and stomach remedy since the nineteenth century, when commercial baking soda spread through kitchens and pharmacies, and it remains an OTC antacid and a prescription intravenous drug for acid-base emergencies. Its sport history began with exercise physiologists in the early twentieth century, who noticed that blood acidity rose during hard work; studies in the early 1930s, including work by researchers linked to the Harvard Fatigue Laboratory, tested whether alkaline salts could delay fatigue. Interest grew in the 1980s and 1990s as athletes in rowing, swimming and middle-distance running experimented with soda loading. Horse racing provides a cautionary parallel: trainers tube-fed horses mixtures of bicarbonate called milkshakes before races, and racing authorities responded by testing for total carbon dioxide in blood. In human sport, bicarbonate is legal and is listed among the few supplements with good evidence for short, intense exercise; the International Society of Sports Nutrition published a position stand on it in 2021. The main barrier is the gut: the large amounts used often cause vomiting and diarrhea, which has driven capsules, split protocols and gel-based delivery products.", "timeline": [["1840s-1860s", "Commercial baking soda becomes widely available as a kitchen and stomach remedy."], ["1930s", "Early studies test alkaline salts and exercise fatigue."], ["1980s", "Athletes and researchers revive bicarbonate loading for middle-distance events."], ["1990s-2000s", "Horse racing authorities test total carbon dioxide to detect bicarbonate milkshakes."], ["2021", "The ISSN publishes a position stand on sodium bicarbonate and exercise performance."]], "cell": "During intense exercise, glycolysis produces hydrogen ions faster than mitochondria can handle them, and intramuscular pH falls. Acidity interferes with phosphofructokinase, calcium release and binding, and cross-bridge cycling, contributing to fatigue in efforts lasting about one to several minutes. Muscle exports hydrogen ions and lactate together through monocarboxylate transporters, and the rate of export depends partly on the gradient between the cell and the blood. Swallowed sodium bicarbonate is absorbed and raises plasma bicarbonate and pH, so the extracellular fluid can accept more acid, steepening the gradient and speeding hydrogen ion and lactate efflux. Bicarbonate does not easily enter muscle cells, so the effect is outside the cell, in contrast to beta-alanine, which raises carnosine inside the cell. The same alkalinizing effect explains the harms. Alkalosis shifts potassium into cells, causing hypokalemia, and increases binding of calcium to albumin, lowering ionized calcium and causing perioral tingling, muscle twitching and carpopedal spasm. Breathing may slow as the body tries to retain carbon dioxide. In the stomach, bicarbonate reacts with acid to release carbon dioxide gas, which causes bloating and, in rare cases with an already distended stomach, rupture.", "body": [["Stomach", "Reacts with acid to form carbon dioxide; bloating, belching, rare rupture when overly full."], ["Intestines", "Osmotic load causes cramps and diarrhea."], ["Blood", "Higher bicarbonate and pH; potassium shifts into cells and ionized calcium falls."], ["Skeletal muscle", "Faster acid efflux may delay fatigue in high-intensity efforts."], ["Kidneys", "Excrete excess bicarbonate and sodium; impaired kidneys raise alkalosis risk."], ["Heart and circulation", "Sodium load causes fluid retention; severe hypokalemia and alkalosis can trigger arrhythmias."]], "journey": "In the stomach, part of an oral dose immediately neutralizes hydrochloric acid, producing carbon dioxide, water and sodium chloride. The rest passes into the small intestine and is absorbed, raising plasma bicarbonate within about an hour and typically peaking roughly one to three hours after ingestion, which is why athletes time intake before competition. The extra bicarbonate joins the body's carbonic acid buffer system; some is converted to carbon dioxide that is exhaled. The kidneys correct the load by filtering more bicarbonate and reducing its reabsorption, along with excreting the extra sodium, so blood levels return toward normal over hours in people with healthy kidneys. Vomiting, dehydration, diuretic use or kidney disease interfere with this correction and allow alkalosis to persist or worsen.", "sidefx": "Gastrointestinal distress is the rule rather than the exception: bloating, belching, nausea, vomiting, stomach cramps and diarrhea, sometimes severe enough to wreck the performance it was meant to help. The sodium load causes fluid retention and transient weight gain and may raise blood pressure in susceptible people. Excess bicarbonate, especially with vomiting or impaired kidney function, produces metabolic alkalosis with hypokalemia and low ionized calcium, leading to tingling, cramps, tetany, weakness, confusion and in severe cases seizures and arrhythmias. Combining bicarbonate with calcium supplements can contribute to milk-alkali syndrome. Gastric rupture has been reported after large baking soda ingestions on a very full stomach.", "fun": "The fizz of a grade-school baking soda volcano is the same reaction that happens in the stomach: bicarbonate meeting acid and releasing carbon dioxide, which is why bicarbonate loading often comes with a lot of belching.", "src": ["Grgic and colleagues 2021, Journal of the International Society of Sports Nutrition, position stand on sodium bicarbonate", "FDA OTC monograph, 21 CFR Part 331 (antacid drug products)", "StatPearls: Metabolic Alkalosis", "Carr, Hopkins and Gore 2011, Sports Medicine, meta-analysis of sodium bicarbonate and performance"]}, "l-theanine": {"origin": "Theanine was identified in 1949 by the Japanese chemist Yajiro Sakato, who found it in gyokuro, a shade-grown green tea prized for its savory, brothy flavor. Tea plants make theanine in their roots from glutamic acid and ethylamine and send it to the leaves; sunlight converts theanine into catechins, which is why growers shade tea bushes before harvest to keep theanine high and bitterness low. Theanine is found almost nowhere else in nature apart from a few relatives of tea and a mushroom species. In Japan, theanine was approved as a food additive in the 1960s, and industrial production by enzymatic synthesis made it cheap enough for supplements; it has also been the subject of GRAS notifications in the United States for use in foods. Research in the 1990s and 2000s reported increased alpha brain wave activity and a sense of relaxed alertness, and studies combining theanine with caffeine found improved attention in some tests, which helped create the caffeine plus theanine focus product category. The evidence remains small-scale, but the safety record is reassuring.", "timeline": [["1949", "Yajiro Sakato identifies theanine in gyokuro green tea."], ["1960s", "Japan permits theanine as a food additive."], ["1990s", "EEG studies report increased alpha wave activity after theanine."], ["2000s", "Studies of caffeine plus theanine report improved attention in some tasks."], ["2010s", "Caffeine plus theanine focus products become a common supplement category."]], "cell": "Theanine is glutamate joined to ethylamine through an amide bond, so it looks to the brain like a modified glutamate or glutamine. After crossing the blood-brain barrier on the large neutral amino acid transporter, it binds weakly to ionotropic glutamate receptors, with low affinity for AMPA, kainate and NMDA types, and may act as a mild antagonist at some of them. It also appears to inhibit the transport of glutamine into neurons, which could reduce the supply for glutamate synthesis. Animal studies report increases in brain GABA, dopamine and serotonin after theanine, though doses in these experiments are often high. In human EEG studies, theanine increases alpha band activity, a pattern associated with wakeful relaxation rather than sleep. When paired with caffeine, which blocks adenosine receptors and increases arousal, theanine may blunt some subjective jitteriness and a small part of the blood pressure rise, though findings vary between studies. Its effects are subtle, which is consistent with weak receptor binding.", "body": [["Brain", "Weak glutamate receptor effects and increased alpha EEG activity; small effects on stress and attention."], ["Cardiovascular system", "Possible mild blood pressure lowering; may blunt caffeine-related rises in some studies."], ["Kidneys", "Main site of hydrolysis to glutamate and ethylamine; metabolites excreted in urine."], ["Gut", "Absorbed through amino acid transporters in the small intestine."]], "journey": "L-theanine is absorbed from the small intestine through the same sodium-dependent transporters used by glutamine, with blood levels peaking about an hour after ingestion. It enters the brain through the large neutral amino acid transporter. The kidneys hydrolyze much of it to glutamic acid and ethylamine using glutaminase, and theanine and its products are excreted in urine. The plasma half-life is roughly an hour in small human studies, and levels return close to baseline within several hours. A cup of brewed tea delivers a small fraction of the amount found in typical supplements.", "sidefx": "Short trials report few side effects. Headache, mild drowsiness, dizziness and stomach upset have been described occasionally. Theoretical concerns include mild blood pressure lowering and additive calming with sedatives. Laboratory studies have reported interactions with some chemotherapy agents, of unknown clinical significance. Safety in pregnancy, breastfeeding and long-term use is not well studied. In caffeine plus theanine products, the caffeine is responsible for most stimulant side effects.", "fun": "Tea farmers shade their bushes before harvesting top grades like gyokuro and the leaves used for matcha because sunlight converts theanine into bitter catechins, so the deep savory taste of those teas is theanine kept in the dark.", "src": ["Sakato 1949, Journal of the Agricultural Chemical Society of Japan, on theanine in tea", "Natural Medicines-style monograph facts: L-theanine", "Williams and colleagues 2020, Plant Foods for Human Nutrition, systematic review of L-theanine and stress", "Owen and colleagues 2008, Nutritional Neuroscience, caffeine and L-theanine on cognition and mood"]}, "five-htp": {"origin": "5-Hydroxytryptophan sits one step before serotonin in the body's synthesis pathway, and its biological role became clear as serotonin itself was characterized in the late 1940s and 1950s, first as a vasoconstrictor in blood serum and then as a brain neurotransmitter. Researchers soon learned that serotonin cannot cross the blood-brain barrier but 5-HTP can, which made it a tool for raising brain serotonin in experiments and an early candidate antidepressant in the 1960s and 1970s, particularly in Europe and Japan, where it was sometimes combined with a decarboxylase inhibitor. Commercial 5-HTP is extracted from the seeds of Griffonia simplicifolia, a climbing shrub of West and Central Africa. When L-tryptophan was pulled from the US market after the 1989 eosinophilia-myalgia syndrome outbreak, 5-HTP filled the gap as a mood and sleep supplement. In 1998, Mayo Clinic researchers reported a contaminant they called Peak X in several commercial 5-HTP products, and a small number of eosinophilia-myalgia-like illnesses had been reported with 5-HTP, though whether the contaminant caused illness remains uncertain. Today the main safety concern is serotonin syndrome when 5-HTP is combined with antidepressants and other serotonergic drugs.", "timeline": [["1948", "Serotonin is isolated from blood serum and named."], ["1960s-1970s", "5-HTP is tested as an antidepressant in small trials, mainly in Europe and Japan."], ["1989", "After the L-tryptophan eosinophilia-myalgia outbreak, 5-HTP gains popularity as a supplement."], ["1998", "Peak X contaminant is reported in some commercial 5-HTP products."], ["2002", "A Cochrane review finds evidence for tryptophan and 5-HTP in depression insufficient."]], "cell": "Serotonin synthesis begins with tryptophan hydroxylase, which adds a hydroxyl group to tryptophan using tetrahydrobiopterin and oxygen; this step is slow and tightly regulated. 5-HTP is the product, and supplying it directly bypasses that control point. Aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase, a vitamin B6 dependent enzyme present in the gut, liver, kidney and brain, then removes a carboxyl group to make serotonin. Because AADC is everywhere, much of an oral dose becomes serotonin outside the brain, where it acts on gut receptors and causes nausea and diarrhea, and is cleared by MAO-A to 5-hydroxyindoleacetic acid. The fraction that reaches the brain crosses the blood-brain barrier on the large neutral amino acid transporter and is converted to serotonin in neurons, increasing the pool available for release. Carbidopa blocks peripheral AADC and increases the share reaching the brain, a strategy used in some countries. When another drug blocks serotonin reuptake or breakdown, additional precursor can push synaptic serotonin high enough to overstimulate 5-HT2A and 5-HT1A receptors, producing serotonin syndrome. In the pineal gland, serotonin is also converted to melatonin, the basis for sleep claims.", "body": [["Brain", "Increases serotonin synthesis; mood and sleep effects uncertain; serotonin syndrome with other serotonergic drugs."], ["Gut", "Peripheral serotonin causes nausea, vomiting, cramps and diarrhea."], ["Pineal gland", "Serotonin is converted to melatonin."], ["Muscle and nerves", "Clonus, tremor and hyperreflexia in serotonin toxicity."], ["Kidneys", "Excrete 5-HIAA, which can be falsely raised in carcinoid testing."]], "journey": "Oral 5-HTP is well absorbed from the small intestine, and blood levels peak about one to two hours after a dose. A large share is decarboxylated to serotonin in the gut wall, liver and blood vessels, where it is either taken up by platelets or broken down by MAO-A. The portion that reaches the brain crosses on the large neutral amino acid transporter and is converted to serotonin by AADC inside neurons. Serotonin is eventually metabolized to 5-HIAA, which is excreted in urine, so urinary 5-HIAA rises after 5-HTP use. The half-life is a few hours, with considerable variation between studies, and carbidopa prolongs and increases plasma 5-HTP.", "sidefx": "Gastrointestinal effects are the most common: nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, cramps and loss of appetite, all driven by peripheral serotonin. Drowsiness, vivid dreams and headache occur. Serotonin syndrome is the serious risk, particularly with SSRIs, SNRIs, MAOIs, tramadol, triptans, linezolid or dextromethorphan, and presents with agitation, sweating, tremor, inducible clonus, diarrhea and fever. Mania has been reported in people with bipolar disorder. Scleroderma-like skin changes were reported with 5-HTP and carbidopa. A few eosinophilia-myalgia-like illnesses have been reported, with product contamination suspected.", "fun": "The Griffonia simplicifolia seeds used for 5-HTP come from a wild African vine; much of the supply is reported to be wild-harvested in West African countries such as Ghana rather than grown on plantations.", "src": ["StatPearls: Serotonin Syndrome", "Cochrane review: Tryptophan and 5-hydroxytryptophan for depression (Shaw, Turner and Del Mar 2002)", "Williamson and colleagues 1998, Nature Medicine", "Birdsall 1998, Alternative Medicine Review, 5-hydroxytryptophan: a clinically effective serotonin precursor"]}, "l-tyrosine": {"origin": "Tyrosine was discovered in 1846 by the German chemist Justus von Liebig, who isolated it from casein, the main protein in cheese, and its name comes from the Greek tyros, meaning cheese. Over the next century it became clear that tyrosine is the starting point for some of the body's most important signaling molecules: the catecholamines dopamine, norepinephrine and epinephrine, the thyroid hormones thyroxine and triiodothyronine, and the pigment melanin. The disease phenylketonuria, described by Asbjorn Folling in Norway in 1934, showed tyrosine's dependence on phenylalanine metabolism: when phenylalanine hydroxylase fails, phenylalanine builds up and tyrosine becomes an essential nutrient, so modern PKU medical foods supply it. Interest in tyrosine as a cognitive aid came largely from military research in the 1980s and later, which tested whether tyrosine could protect soldiers' mental performance in cold, at altitude or during sleep deprivation, on the reasoning that stressed neurons might run short of catecholamine precursor. Results were promising but small, and trials in depression were disappointing. Tyrosine is now a common ingredient in focus and thyroid support supplements.", "timeline": [["1846", "Justus von Liebig isolates tyrosine from casein."], ["1934", "Asbjorn Folling describes phenylketonuria."], ["1960s", "Tyrosine hydroxylase is identified as the rate-limiting enzyme for catecholamine synthesis."], ["1980s-1990s", "Military studies test tyrosine for cognition under cold, altitude and other stress."], ["2015", "A systematic review concludes tyrosine may help cognition mainly under acute demanding conditions."]], "cell": "Most tyrosine in the body either comes from dietary protein or is made in the liver from phenylalanine by phenylalanine hydroxylase. In catecholamine neurons and the adrenal medulla, tyrosine hydroxylase adds a hydroxyl group to make L-DOPA, using tetrahydrobiopterin and iron; aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase then makes dopamine, dopamine beta-hydroxylase makes norepinephrine, and in the adrenal medulla PNMT makes epinephrine. Tyrosine hydroxylase is normally close to saturated with substrate and is controlled by feedback from catecholamines, which is why extra tyrosine changes little in resting conditions. When neurons fire heavily, the enzyme is activated by phosphorylation and tyrosine supply may become limiting, which is the theory behind its use under stress. In the thyroid, tyrosine residues within thyroglobulin are iodinated by thyroid peroxidase and coupled to form T4 and T3, but dietary tyrosine does not limit thyroid hormone production in healthy people. In melanocytes, tyrosinase converts tyrosine to melanin. Tyrosine enters the brain on LAT1, competing with phenylalanine, tryptophan, branched-chain amino acids and levodopa.", "body": [["Brain", "Precursor for dopamine and norepinephrine; possible protection of cognition under acute stress."], ["Adrenal medulla", "Precursor for epinephrine and norepinephrine."], ["Thyroid", "Tyrosine residues in thyroglobulin are iodinated to form thyroid hormones."], ["Skin", "Converted by tyrosinase to melanin."], ["Liver", "Made from phenylalanine; broken down to fumarate and acetoacetate."]], "journey": "L-tyrosine is absorbed from the small intestine through neutral amino acid transporters and reaches peak blood levels about one to two hours after a dose. The liver takes up a share and breaks it down through tyrosine aminotransferase and homogentisate pathways to fumarate, which enters the citric acid cycle, and acetoacetate, a ketone body. Some reaches the brain via LAT1, where uptake depends on competing amino acids in the blood. A small fraction becomes catecholamines, thyroid hormone or melanin. Nitrogen leaves the body as urea in urine. N-acetyl-L-tyrosine dissolves better in water but much of it is excreted unchanged, so it raises blood tyrosine less efficiently.", "sidefx": "Tyrosine is generally well tolerated in short studies, with occasional nausea, heartburn, headache or fatigue. Theoretical concerns include raised blood pressure in people taking MAOIs, interference with levodopa transport, and effects on thyroid conditions. In practice, palpitations, anxiety or tremor after focus or thyroid support blends more often reflect caffeine, other stimulants, iodine or undeclared thyroid hormone in the product. Long-term safety and safety in pregnancy beyond normal food intake are not well studied.", "fun": "The crunchy white crystals in well-aged cheeses such as Parmesan and aged Gouda are often tyrosine, the same amino acid Liebig first pulled out of cheese protein and named after it.", "src": ["Jongkees and colleagues 2015, Journal of Psychiatric Research, effect of tyrosine supplementation on clinical and healthy populations", "Natural Medicines-style monograph facts: L-tyrosine", "NIH MedlinePlus Genetics: Phenylketonuria", "Fernstrom and Fernstrom 2007, Journal of Nutrition, tyrosine, phenylalanine and catecholamine synthesis"]}, "l-tryptophan": {"origin": "Tryptophan was isolated in 1901 by Frederick Gowland Hopkins and Sydney Cole at Cambridge, who extracted it from the milk protein casein, and Hopkins, whose feeding experiments showed that diets need more than protein, fat and carbohydrate, later shared the 1929 Nobel Prize for the discovery of growth-stimulating vitamins. In the twentieth century tryptophan was connected to pellagra, because the body uses it to make niacin, and to serotonin and melatonin. By the 1980s it was a popular US supplement for sleep and mood. In 1989 physicians in New Mexico recognized a cluster of patients with severe muscle pain and very high eosinophil counts who had used L-tryptophan, and the CDC soon documented a nationwide outbreak of what was named eosinophilia-myalgia syndrome, with more than a thousand cases and dozens of deaths. Investigations traced it to tryptophan from a single Japanese manufacturer that had changed its fermentation and purification process, and contaminants such as a compound called EBT were identified, although the exact cause has never been fully settled. FDA recalled tryptophan supplements and restricted imports for years; the supplement returned to the US market in the 2000s.", "timeline": [["1901", "Hopkins and Cole isolate tryptophan from casein."], ["1930s-1940s", "Tryptophan is linked to niacin production and pellagra prevention."], ["1989", "Eosinophilia-myalgia syndrome outbreak is linked to L-tryptophan; FDA recalls products."], ["1990", "Investigations trace the outbreak to one manufacturer's product."], ["2000s", "L-tryptophan returns to the US supplement market."]], "cell": "Tryptophan is an essential amino acid, used first for protein synthesis. Beyond that, two main pathways compete for it. The kynurenine pathway, started by tryptophan 2,3-dioxygenase in the liver and indoleamine 2,3-dioxygenase in immune cells and other tissues, handles the great majority and produces kynurenine, kynurenic acid, the NMDA receptor agonist quinolinic acid and ultimately NAD and niacin. Inflammation induces IDO and diverts tryptophan away from serotonin, a link studied in depression. The serotonin pathway begins with tryptophan hydroxylase, which in the brain is not saturated with substrate, so raising brain tryptophan does increase serotonin synthesis, unlike the situation with tyrosine and catecholamines. AADC converts 5-HTP to serotonin, and in the pineal gland serotonin is acetylated and methylated to melatonin. Brain entry occurs on LAT1 in competition with other large neutral amino acids, which is why a carbohydrate meal, by driving those competitors into muscle through insulin, can raise brain tryptophan more than a protein meal does. Combined with drugs that block serotonin reuptake or breakdown, extra tryptophan can produce serotonin toxicity.", "body": [["Brain", "Precursor for serotonin; modest sleep effects; serotonin syndrome risk with other serotonergic drugs."], ["Pineal gland", "Serotonin is converted to melatonin."], ["Liver", "Kynurenine pathway converts most tryptophan, including to niacin."], ["Muscle and connective tissue", "Eosinophilia-myalgia syndrome caused severe myalgia, fasciitis and neuropathy in the 1989 outbreak."], ["Immune system", "IDO activation during inflammation diverts tryptophan to kynurenine."]], "journey": "L-tryptophan is absorbed in the small intestine through neutral amino acid transporters, with blood levels peaking roughly one to two hours after an oral dose. In blood, most tryptophan is loosely bound to albumin, the only amino acid carried this way, and the free fraction is available for brain uptake; fatty acids and some drugs can displace it. The liver uses most of it through the kynurenine pathway, producing niacin and other metabolites. A smaller fraction crosses the blood-brain barrier on LAT1, where it competes with other large neutral amino acids, and is converted to serotonin and melatonin. Metabolites, including 5-HIAA and kynurenine derivatives, are excreted in urine.", "sidefx": "Common side effects are nausea, belching, dry mouth, dizziness, headache and drowsiness. Additive sedation with sleep medicines, benzodiazepines and alcohol can impair driving and raise fall risk. With MAOIs, SSRIs and other serotonergic drugs, tryptophan can contribute to serotonin syndrome with agitation, sweating, tremor, clonus and fever. The 1989 eosinophilia-myalgia syndrome caused disabling muscle pain, skin thickening, neuropathy and lung problems, and some patients never fully recovered; the cause was traced to contaminated product rather than tryptophan itself, but the episode shaped supplement regulation.", "fun": "The belief that turkey makes people sleepy because of tryptophan is mostly a myth: turkey has no more tryptophan than many other meats, and the competing amino acids in a protein-rich meal limit how much reaches the brain; the large meal and holiday setting are better explanations.", "src": ["CDC MMWR 1989, Eosinophilia-myalgia syndrome associated with ingestion of L-tryptophan", "Hertzman and colleagues 1990, New England Journal of Medicine, association of EMS with L-tryptophan", "StatPearls: Eosinophilia-Myalgia Syndrome", "Cochrane review: Tryptophan and 5-hydroxytryptophan for depression"]}, "gaba-supplement": {"origin": "GABA was found in the brain in 1950 by several groups working at about the same time, including Eugene Roberts with Sam Frankel and, separately, Jorge Awapara and Sidney Udenfriend. At first it was considered just a metabolic curiosity, but work in the 1950s and 1960s, including studies on crayfish nerve and on mammalian cortex, established it as the main inhibitory neurotransmitter of the brain. That discovery underlies whole classes of medicines: benzodiazepines, barbiturates, many anesthetics and alcohol act at GABA-A receptors, baclofen acts at GABA-B receptors, and vigabatrin and tiagabine raise GABA levels. GABA itself never became a drug, because it was thought to cross the blood-brain barrier poorly. Japanese food scientists developed GABA-enriched foods made by fermentation with lactic acid bacteria, and fermented GABA was studied in small trials for blood pressure and stress. Supplements followed in the US and elsewhere. A related compound, phenibut, adds a phenyl ring that lets it reach the brain; it was developed in the Soviet Union and is sold online, where it has caused dependence, withdrawal and poisonings, and FDA has said it is not a lawful dietary ingredient.", "timeline": [["1950", "Roberts and Frankel, and Awapara and Udenfriend, report GABA in the brain."], ["1950s-1960s", "GABA is established as the main inhibitory neurotransmitter."], ["1960s", "Phenibut, a GABA derivative, is developed in the Soviet Union."], ["1990s-2000s", "Fermented GABA products are developed and studied in Japan."], ["2015", "A review concludes evidence on GABA crossing the blood-brain barrier is conflicting and limited."]], "cell": "GABA is made in neurons from glutamate by glutamic acid decarboxylase, which needs vitamin B6. Released into the synapse, it binds GABA-A receptors, which are chloride channels; chloride entry hyperpolarizes the neuron and makes it harder to fire. Benzodiazepines, barbiturates, alcohol and neurosteroids bind other sites on the same channel and strengthen this effect. GABA also activates GABA-B receptors, which act through G proteins to open potassium channels and close calcium channels, reducing transmitter release. GABA is cleared by transporters and broken down by GABA transaminase to succinic semialdehyde and then succinate, a route called the GABA shunt. Swallowed GABA faces the blood-brain barrier, which has GABA transporters that tend to move GABA out of the brain, and most studies find little passage, although some animal data suggest limited entry. Any effects may therefore come from peripheral sites: GABA receptors on enteric nerves and the vagus nerve, which signal to the brain, and actions on sympathetic nerves and blood vessels that could explain small blood pressure reductions seen with fermented GABA. Phenibut and gabapentin differ in how they reach and act on the nervous system.", "body": [["Brain", "Main inhibitory neurotransmitter; oral supplements likely reach the brain poorly."], ["Gut and vagus nerve", "Proposed site of action for oral GABA through enteric and vagal signaling."], ["Blood vessels", "Possible small blood pressure reductions with fermented GABA products."], ["Liver and tissues", "GABA transaminase breaks GABA down to succinate for energy."]], "journey": "Oral GABA is absorbed from the small intestine, and blood levels rise within about half an hour to an hour in small human studies, then fall over a few hours. It is distributed in the circulation, where it can reach peripheral nerves and blood vessels. Entry into the brain appears limited. GABA is metabolized by GABA transaminase in the liver and other tissues to succinic semialdehyde and then succinate, which enters the citric acid cycle and is used for energy. Very little appears unchanged in the urine. Fermented foods such as kimchi, tempeh and some specially processed teas contain small amounts of GABA.", "sidefx": "Small trials report few side effects: occasional drowsiness, stomach upset and headache, and transient tingling or flushing with large amounts. Mild blood pressure lowering may add to the effects of antihypertensives, and additive drowsiness with sedatives or alcohol is a theoretical concern. Long-term safety and safety in pregnancy are unknown. The more important safety issue is confusion with phenibut, a GABA derivative sold online that causes sedation, dependence and a severe withdrawal syndrome with agitation, insomnia and psychosis.", "fun": "GABA was found in plants and bacteria decades before anyone knew it was a brain chemical; plants make it quickly when they are injured or stressed, and it may help some plants defend against insects that feed on them.", "src": ["Roberts and Frankel 1950, Journal of Biological Chemistry, gamma-aminobutyric acid in brain", "Boonstra and colleagues 2015, Frontiers in Psychology", "Hepsomali and colleagues 2020, Frontiers in Neuroscience", "StatPearls: Physiology, GABA"]}, "sam-e": {"origin": "S-adenosylmethionine was discovered in 1952 by the Italian-born biochemist Giulio Cantoni, working in the United States, who showed that methionine must first be activated by ATP before it can donate a methyl group; the activated form is SAMe. It soon proved to be the main methyl donor in all living cells, second only to ATP in the number of reactions it takes part in. In the 1970s Italian researchers developed stable salt forms that allowed SAMe to be given as a drug, and it became a prescription medicine in Italy and several other European countries for depression, liver disease with cholestasis and osteoarthritis, given by mouth or by injection. Early trials, some using intravenous SAMe, reported antidepressant effects comparable to tricyclic antidepressants. SAMe was introduced to the US supplement market in 1999 and became popular for mood and joints. In 2002 the Agency for Healthcare Research and Quality reviewed the evidence and found it promising but limited, and later Cochrane reviews judged the depression and osteoarthritis evidence to be low quality. Product quality has been a recurring problem because SAMe is chemically unstable.", "timeline": [["1952", "Giulio Cantoni discovers S-adenosylmethionine."], ["1970s", "Stable SAMe salts allow its use as a drug in Europe."], ["1999", "SAMe enters the US supplement market."], ["2002", "AHRQ publishes an evidence report on SAMe for depression, osteoarthritis and liver disease."], ["2016", "A Cochrane review rates evidence for SAMe in depression as low quality."]], "cell": "Methionine adenosyltransferase joins methionine to the adenosine of ATP, creating SAMe, whose positively charged sulfur makes the attached methyl group easy to transfer. Hundreds of methyltransferases use it: COMT, which inactivates catecholamines; enzymes that make phosphatidylcholine for membranes; enzymes that methylate DNA and histones to control gene expression; and enzymes that make creatine, epinephrine and melatonin. Each transfer turns SAMe into S-adenosylhomocysteine, a potent inhibitor of methyltransferases that is hydrolyzed to homocysteine. Homocysteine is either remethylated back to methionine using folate and vitamin B12 or sent down the transsulfuration pathway, using vitamin B6, to cysteine and then glutathione, the liver's main antioxidant, which underpins interest in SAMe for liver disease. After decarboxylation, SAMe also donates an aminopropyl group for the polyamines spermidine and spermine. How SAMe might relieve depression is not settled; proposed mechanisms include effects on serotonin, norepinephrine and dopamine turnover, membrane phospholipid methylation and folate-dependent pathways. Its serotonergic effects are supported by reports of serotonin syndrome and mania.", "body": [["Brain", "Proposed effects on monoamine turnover and membranes; mood effects and mania risk."], ["Liver", "Main site of SAMe synthesis; supplies glutathione through transsulfuration; studied in cholestasis."], ["Joints", "Studied for osteoarthritis pain with small, low-quality trials."], ["Gut", "Nausea, diarrhea and flatulence are common."], ["Blood", "Converted to homocysteine; folate and B12 status affect the cycle."]], "journey": "SAMe is unstable in stomach acid and moisture, so it is sold as enteric-coated tablets in blister packs. Oral bioavailability is low, and blood levels peak several hours after an enteric-coated dose, later when taken with food. In the body it enters the same pool as endogenously made SAMe, mostly in the liver, and is used in methylation reactions, producing S-adenosylhomocysteine and homocysteine, which are then remethylated or sent to transsulfuration. Its plasma half-life is short, roughly an hour and a half in limited studies. Metabolites are excreted in urine and feces. Intravenous and intramuscular forms used in Europe bypass the absorption problem.", "sidefx": "The most common effects are gastrointestinal: nausea, diarrhea, flatulence and stomach discomfort, along with dry mouth, headache, insomnia, anxiety and restlessness. Mania and hypomania have been reported in people with bipolar disorder. Serotonin syndrome has been reported when SAMe was combined with clomipramine, and similar concerns apply to other serotonergic drugs. Because SAMe is converted to homocysteine, theoretical concerns have been raised about homocysteine levels, though studies have not shown a consistent rise. Product content may be lower than labeled.", "fun": "SAMe's positively charged sulfur makes it such a good methyl donor that it is involved in more enzyme reactions than any other cofactor except ATP, the molecule it is built from.", "src": ["Cantoni 1953, Journal of Biological Chemistry, S-adenosylmethionine: a new intermediate formed enzymatically from L-methionine and ATP", "AHRQ 2002 Evidence Report/Technology Assessment No. 64: S-adenosyl-L-methionine", "Galizia and colleagues 2016, Cochrane review: S-adenosyl methionine for depression in adults", "NIH NCCIH: SAMe"]}, "phosphatidylserine": {"origin": "Phosphatidylserine was identified in 1941 and 1942 by Jordi Folch at the Rockefeller Institute, who separated it from the brain cephalin fraction, a crude mix of phospholipids. It turned out to be a minor but important membrane lipid, concentrated in the brain and kept almost entirely on the inner face of cell membranes. In the 1980s and early 1990s, Italian and American researchers tested phosphatidylserine extracted from cow brain in older adults with memory complaints and early dementia, and some trials reported small improvements in memory tests. The outbreak of bovine spongiform encephalopathy in the 1990s made cow brain products unacceptable, and manufacturers switched to phosphatidylserine made from soy lecithin, later also from sunflower, through enzymatic conversion of other phospholipids. Plant-derived PS has a different fatty acid profile, and trials with it have been fewer and less convincing. In 2003 FDA allowed qualified health claims linking phosphatidylserine to reduced risk of cognitive dysfunction and dementia in the elderly, but only with wording stating that the scientific evidence is very limited and preliminary. Meanwhile cell biologists discovered that PS exposure is the eat-me signal for apoptotic cells.", "timeline": [["1941-1942", "Jordi Folch identifies phosphatidylserine in brain cephalin."], ["1980s-early 1990s", "Trials of bovine brain PS in older adults report small memory benefits."], ["1990s", "Prion concerns lead manufacturers to switch to soy-derived PS."], ["1992", "PS exposure is described as a signal for removal of apoptotic cells."], ["2003", "FDA permits qualified health claims for PS with very limited evidence language."]], "cell": "Cell membranes are asymmetric. ATP-driven flippases keep phosphatidylserine on the inner, cytoplasmic leaflet, where its negative charge helps recruit and activate proteins such as protein kinase C, Raf-1 and synaptotagmin-related machinery in neurons, and supports the activity of Na+/K+-ATPase. When a cell undergoes apoptosis, caspases inactivate flippases and activate scramblases, PS appears on the outer surface, and macrophage receptors recognize it as an eat-me signal, allowing quiet removal of dying cells without inflammation. Activated platelets also expose PS, creating the negatively charged surface on which the tenase and prothrombinase complexes assemble, which is why PS is central to clotting. In the brain, PS is enriched in docosahexaenoic acid, and neuronal levels of PS depend partly on DHA supply. How swallowed PS could help memory is unclear: it is largely broken down in the gut to lysophosphatidylserine and fatty acids, and its components are reassembled into phospholipids. Small studies suggesting reduced cortisol responses to exercise or stress have no clear mechanism.", "body": [["Brain", "Enriched in neuronal membranes; memory effects of supplements are uncertain."], ["Blood and platelets", "PS exposure on activated platelets supports clotting factor assembly."], ["Immune system", "Macrophages recognize PS on apoptotic cells for clearance."], ["Adrenal axis", "Small studies suggest blunted cortisol response to exercise; unconfirmed."]], "journey": "Oral phosphatidylserine is digested in the small intestine by pancreatic phospholipases, which remove a fatty acid to form lysophosphatidylserine; this is absorbed by intestinal cells, where it can be reacylated or converted to other phospholipids. Absorbed phospholipids are packaged into chylomicrons and lipoproteins and distributed to tissues, and some PS may be converted to phosphatidylethanolamine by decarboxylation. How much intact supplemental PS reaches the brain is not well defined, and the body makes its own PS by base exchange from phosphatidylcholine and phosphatidylethanolamine. Components are eventually broken down and recycled like other dietary fats.", "sidefx": "Studies report few side effects. Stomach upset, gas and insomnia have been described, especially with larger amounts. Soy-derived products could be a concern for people with soy allergy, though the protein content is usually low. Theoretical interactions with cholinesterase inhibitors and anticholinergic drugs, and with anticoagulants given PS's role in clotting surfaces, have not been shown to matter clinically. Long-term safety and safety in pregnancy are unknown.", "fun": "Phosphatidylserine is a molecular eat-me flag: billions of cells die in the body every day, and macrophages find and swallow them quietly mostly because PS has flipped from the inside of their membranes to the outside.", "src": ["Folch 1942, Journal of Biological Chemistry, brain cephalin, a mixture of phosphatides", "FDA 2003 qualified health claim letter: phosphatidylserine and cognitive dysfunction and dementia", "Crook and colleagues 1991, Neurology", "Fadok and colleagues 1992, Journal of Immunology, exposure of phosphatidylserine on apoptotic lymphocytes"]}, "citicoline": {"origin": "CDP-choline was discovered in 1956 by Eugene Kennedy and Samuel Weiss, who showed that cells make phosphatidylcholine, the most abundant membrane phospholipid, through an activated intermediate built from cytidine triphosphate and phosphocholine. The route is now called the Kennedy pathway. Japanese researchers developed citicoline as a drug in the late 1960s and 1970s, and it became a prescription medicine in Japan and later in many European, Asian and Latin American countries for head injury, stroke and cognitive impairment, given intravenously or orally. Early, smaller trials and a pooled analysis suggested benefit after acute ischemic stroke, so an international trial called ICTUS tested citicoline against placebo; it was stopped for futility, and the 2012 report found no improvement in recovery. The same year, the US COBRIT trial in traumatic brain injury also found no benefit. A Cochrane review of older adults with chronic cerebral disorders found some short-term evidence for memory and behavior, though the trials were limited. In the US, citicoline is sold as a dietary supplement and appears in focus and nootropic products, often with caffeine.", "timeline": [["1956", "Kennedy and Weiss describe CDP-choline as an intermediate in phosphatidylcholine synthesis."], ["1970s", "Citicoline becomes a prescription drug in Japan and later Europe."], ["2002", "A pooled analysis of earlier trials suggests possible benefit in acute stroke."], ["2012", "The ICTUS stroke trial finds no benefit of citicoline."], ["2012", "The COBRIT trial finds no benefit in traumatic brain injury."]], "cell": "In the Kennedy pathway, choline is phosphorylated to phosphocholine, then CTP:phosphocholine cytidylyltransferase, the rate-limiting enzyme, joins it to CTP to form CDP-choline, and cholinephosphotransferase transfers the phosphocholine to diacylglycerol to make phosphatidylcholine. Supplying citicoline does not deliver the intact molecule to the brain; it is hydrolyzed in the gut and liver to cytidine and choline, and in humans cytidine is largely converted to uridine. Both components cross the blood-brain barrier, where uridine is converted back to UTP and CTP, and choline is rephosphorylated, so brain cells can reassemble CDP-choline. The proposed benefits after injury come from supporting membrane repair and from animal studies showing reduced activation of phospholipase A2, which releases arachidonic acid and free fatty acids that fuel inflammation and oxidative injury after ischemia. Choline is also the precursor for acetylcholine, and animal studies report increased dopamine and norepinephrine levels, which underlie interest in cognition and the possible interaction with levodopa. Translating these effects into clinical benefit has failed in large trials.", "body": [["Brain", "Supplies choline and uridine for membrane phospholipids and acetylcholine; large stroke and TBI trials were neutral."], ["Eye", "Small glaucoma studies report possible effects on visual function."], ["Liver and gut", "Hydrolyze citicoline into choline and cytidine."], ["Lungs", "Much of the labeled carbon from citicoline leaves the body as exhaled carbon dioxide."]], "journey": "Oral citicoline is well absorbed, but it is broken down during absorption and first pass through the liver into choline and cytidine, and cytidine is converted to uridine in humans. Choline levels in blood rise within a few hours and uridine levels rise somewhat later, then both decline over many hours in a biphasic pattern. The components distribute widely, cross the blood-brain barrier and are reincorporated into phospholipids, while the rest are metabolized. Much of the dose is eventually eliminated as carbon dioxide in exhaled air, with a smaller share in urine.", "sidefx": "Citicoline is generally well tolerated in trials, including the large stroke and brain injury studies, where side effects resembled placebo. Reported effects include headache, insomnia, restlessness, stomach upset, nausea and diarrhea, with rare reports of low blood pressure and changes in heart rate. It may increase levodopa effects. Safety in pregnancy and long-term use as a supplement are not well studied. In focus products, caffeine and other stimulants explain most stimulant-type side effects.", "fun": "Eugene Kennedy, who discovered CDP-choline, also mapped where fatty acids are burned in the cell, showing with Albert Lehninger that fatty acid oxidation happens in mitochondria.", "src": ["Kennedy and Weiss 1956, Journal of Biological Chemistry, the function of cytidine coenzymes in the biosynthesis of phospholipides", "Davalos and colleagues 2012, Lancet, ICTUS", "Zafonte and colleagues 2012, JAMA, COBRIT", "Fioravanti and Yanagi, Cochrane review: CDP-choline for cognitive and behavioural disturbances"]}, "alpha-gpc": {"origin": "Choline was first named by the German chemist Adolph Strecker in 1862 after he obtained it from bile, and the name comes from the Greek chole, meaning bile. Its importance grew as acetylcholine was identified as the first neurotransmitter in the early twentieth century, and the US Institute of Medicine recognized choline as an essential nutrient in 1998. Glycerophosphocholine, a breakdown product of phosphatidylcholine, occurs naturally in cells and in small amounts in foods such as milk and organ meats. In the 1980s and 1990s, Italian pharmaceutical researchers developed a purified form, choline alfoscerate, as a drug for cognitive decline and vascular dementia, and it became a prescription medicine in Italy and several other countries, given by mouth or injection. Trials in dementia, including studies combining it with the cholinesterase inhibitor donepezil, reported modest cognitive effects, though most were small or from a limited number of research groups. In the US, alpha-GPC is sold as a supplement and became popular in nootropic stacks and pre-workout products after small studies reported increased growth hormone and power output. In 2021 a large Korean observational study linked prescribed choline alfoscerate use to higher ten-year stroke risk, a signal that has not been confirmed.", "timeline": [["1862", "Adolph Strecker names choline after obtaining it from bile."], ["1914-1921", "Acetylcholine is identified, and Otto Loewi shows chemical nerve transmission."], ["1980s-1990s", "Choline alfoscerate is developed as a cognitive drug in Italy."], ["1998", "The US Institute of Medicine recognizes choline as an essential nutrient."], ["2021", "A Korean observational study links choline alfoscerate use to higher stroke risk."]], "cell": "Alpha-GPC is choline attached through a phosphate to glycerol, without the fatty acid tails of phosphatidylcholine, which makes it water soluble and able to supply a large share of choline by weight. Glycerophosphocholine phosphodiesterase splits it into choline and glycerol-3-phosphate. In cholinergic neurons, choline acetyltransferase combines choline with acetyl-CoA to make acetylcholine, and choline uptake into nerve terminals by the high-affinity choline transporter is the rate-limiting step. Animal studies report increased acetylcholine release in the hippocampus after alpha-GPC, the basis for cognitive claims, and the glycerophosphate and choline are also used to rebuild phosphatidylcholine in membranes. Cholinesterase inhibitors such as donepezil slow acetylcholine breakdown, so the combination could add cholinergic effects, while anticholinergic drugs block muscarinic receptors and oppose them. In the gut, bacteria convert unabsorbed choline to trimethylamine, which the liver turns into trimethylamine N-oxide; TMAO has been associated with atherosclerosis and thrombosis in research, one proposed explanation for the observational stroke signal.", "body": [["Brain", "Supplies choline for acetylcholine and membrane phospholipids; modest cognitive effects in dementia trials."], ["Skeletal muscle and nerves", "Acetylcholine drives neuromuscular transmission; small sport studies report modest power effects."], ["Pituitary", "Small studies report increased growth hormone release."], ["Gut and liver", "Bacteria make trimethylamine from choline; liver converts it to TMAO."], ["Blood vessels", "Observational stroke signal; TMAO is studied for links to vascular disease."]], "journey": "Oral alpha-GPC is absorbed from the small intestine and largely hydrolyzed to choline and glycerophosphate during or after absorption. Blood choline levels rise within about an hour and peak within a few hours. Choline crosses the blood-brain barrier through a carrier and is used for acetylcholine and phospholipid synthesis or oxidized in the liver and kidneys to betaine, a methyl donor. Unabsorbed choline reaches gut bacteria and is converted to trimethylamine and then TMAO, which is excreted in urine; excess trimethylamine can cause a fishy body odor. Part of the dose is fully oxidized and leaves as carbon dioxide.", "sidefx": "Reported side effects include heartburn, nausea, headache, dizziness, insomnia, skin rash and confusion, generally mild. Very large amounts of choline can cause sweating, diarrhea, low blood pressure and a fishy body odor. The most important open question is the observational association with stroke in a large Korean cohort; confounding is possible, but the finding argues against assuming that alpha-GPC is harmless in people with vascular risk. In nootropic and pre-workout products, caffeine and other stimulants often explain palpitations and insomnia.", "fun": "The name choline is from the Greek word for bile, chole, because it was first obtained from bile; the same root gives us cholesterol, cholera and the gallbladder term cholecystitis.", "src": ["NIH Office of Dietary Supplements: Choline fact sheet", "Lee and colleagues 2021, JAMA Network Open", "Amenta and colleagues, ASCOMALVA trial reports", "Parker and colleagues 2015, Journal of the International Society of Sports Nutrition, alpha-GPC and power output"]}, "magnesium-l-threonate": {"origin": "Magnesium L-threonate is a newer magnesium salt that came out of neuroscience research rather than nutrition. Researchers working with Guosong Liu, then at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology, were interested in why magnesium seemed to matter for synaptic plasticity, since magnesium ions sit inside the NMDA receptor channel and help decide when it opens. Ordinary magnesium salts did not raise brain magnesium much in animals, so the group screened other salts and reported in 2010 in the journal Neuron that the threonate salt raised cerebrospinal fluid magnesium in rats and was associated with more synapses and better learning and memory scores. The salt was then commercialized under a trademarked ingredient name and entered the supplement market as a brain-targeted magnesium. Human research since then has been limited to small, short trials, some funded by the ingredient developer, so the leap from rat synapses to human memory remains unproven. It is sold as a dietary supplement, not reviewed by FDA for effectiveness, and it carries the same core safety issues as any magnesium product.", "timeline": [["2010", "A rodent study published in Neuron reports that magnesium L-threonate raises brain magnesium and synapse density."], ["2010s", "The salt is commercialized as a branded supplement ingredient for memory and focus."], ["2016", "A small industry-associated human trial in older adults with memory complaints reports modest results."], ["Present", "Marketed widely in brain and sleep blends; large independent human trials are lacking."]], "cell": "Magnesium is the second most abundant cation inside cells and is needed for hundreds of enzymes, especially any reaction using ATP, because the true substrate is usually an ATP-magnesium complex. At the synapse, extracellular magnesium sits in the pore of the NMDA receptor at resting membrane potential and blocks calcium entry until the membrane depolarizes. This voltage-dependent block lets the NMDA receptor act as a coincidence detector, which is central to long-term potentiation. The rodent work behind this supplement proposed that modestly raising brain magnesium shifts synapses toward a state that favors plasticity, increasing the density of functional synapses in the hippocampus and prefrontal cortex. Threonate, a four-carbon sugar acid that is a normal breakdown product of ascorbic acid, has been proposed to help move magnesium into cells, but this has not been shown clearly in people. At very high blood levels, magnesium blocks calcium-dependent acetylcholine release at the neuromuscular junction and slows conduction in the heart, which explains the weakness, lost reflexes and heart block of severe hypermagnesemia.", "body": [["Brain", "Proposed synaptic effects through NMDA receptor modulation; evidence mostly from rodents."], ["Gut", "Loose stools or stomach upset, the most common complaint with magnesium products."], ["Kidneys", "Main route of magnesium removal; poor kidney function is the main reason magnesium accumulates."], ["Heart", "Very high magnesium slows conduction, causing bradycardia, hypotension and heart block."], ["Muscles and nerves", "High magnesium reduces acetylcholine release, causing weakness and loss of deep tendon reflexes."]], "journey": "Magnesium is absorbed in the small intestine through both passive paracellular movement and active channels in the gut lining, and the fraction absorbed falls as intake rises. Different salts dissolve differently, and organic salts are often described as more soluble than magnesium oxide, though differences in real-world absorption are modest. Once absorbed, magnesium is distributed mostly to bone and inside cells, with only a small fraction in the blood, which is why a normal serum level does not always reflect total body stores. Serum magnesium is about one third protein bound. Magnesium is not metabolized. The kidneys filter it and reabsorb most of it, mainly in the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle, and adjust excretion to match intake. That adjustment is what keeps supplemental magnesium safe for most people, and it is lost in advanced kidney disease. Threonate is handled as a normal small organic acid.", "sidefx": "Most people notice nothing, or mild drowsiness, headache or loose stools. Because the elemental magnesium per capsule is modest, diarrhea is often less prominent than with magnesium citrate or oxide. The important adverse effect is hypermagnesemia, which almost always requires impaired kidney function or large combined intakes, such as a supplement plus a magnesium laxative or antacid. The progression runs from nausea, flushing and drowsiness to loss of deep tendon reflexes, then hypotension, bradycardia, respiratory depression and finally heart block or cardiac arrest. Interactions are mostly about absorption: magnesium binds tetracyclines, fluoroquinolones, bisphosphonates and some HIV integrase inhibitors in the gut, reducing their effect when taken together.", "fun": "The same magnesium ion that this supplement aims to nudge upward in the brain is the plug in the NMDA receptor pore, and the anesthetic ketamine works by blocking that very same channel from inside.", "src": ["NIH Office of Dietary Supplements: Magnesium Fact Sheet for Health Professionals", "Slutsky I et al. 2010, Neuron, Enhancement of learning and memory by elevating brain magnesium", "StatPearls: Hypermagnesemia", "StatPearls: Magnesium"]}, "uridine": {"origin": "Uridine is one of the four nucleosides of RNA and has been part of biochemistry since nucleic acid chemistry was worked out in the early twentieth century. Its life as a supplement came from research at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology led by Richard Wurtman, who had long studied how nutrients that are precursors for brain chemicals might change brain function. In the 2000s his group showed in gerbils and rats that giving uridine together with the omega-3 fat DHA and choline increased markers of synaptic membranes and dendritic spines, because all three feed the pathway that builds phosphatidylcholine, the main fat of nerve cell membranes. That work led to a medical nutrition drink containing UMP, DHA, choline and other nutrients that was tested in early Alzheimer disease, with mixed results that did not change practice. Separately, uridine became important in oncology: because uridine competes with toxic metabolites of fluorouracil, a prodrug, uridine triacetate, was developed and approved by FDA as an emergency antidote for fluorouracil and capecitabine overdose and for the rare inherited disorder hereditary orotic aciduria. Supplement UMP sold in nootropic stacks borrows from the first story but is not the same as the prescription drug from the second.", "timeline": [["Early 1900s", "Uridine is identified as a component of nucleic acids during early RNA chemistry."], ["2000s", "Wurtman and colleagues report increased synaptic markers in rodents given uridine, DHA and choline together."], ["2010s", "A medical nutrition product containing UMP, DHA and choline is tested in early Alzheimer disease, with mixed results."], ["2015", "FDA approves uridine triacetate for hereditary orotic aciduria and as an emergency treatment for fluorouracil or capecitabine overdose or early severe toxicity."]], "cell": "Cells can make pyrimidines from scratch through the de novo pathway, which ends in orotate and then UMP, or recycle them through salvage, in which uridine is phosphorylated by uridine-cytidine kinase. Supplemental uridine enters the salvage route. UMP is phosphorylated to UDP and UTP; UTP is aminated to CTP; and CTP combines with phosphocholine to form CDP-choline, the rate-limiting intermediate in the Kennedy pathway for phosphatidylcholine. The theory behind brain supplements is that brain levels of CTP limit membrane synthesis, so more uridine plus more choline and DHA would let neurons build more synaptic membrane. Uridine nucleotides also act outside cells: UTP and UDP activate P2Y2, P2Y4 and P2Y6 purinergic receptors, which affect neurite growth in culture. In oncology the relevant mechanism is competition: fluorouracil is converted to fluorinated uridine nucleotides that are wrongly built into RNA and inhibit thymidylate synthase, and a flood of normal uridine dilutes the toxic nucleotides, protecting normal tissue, which is why uridine triacetate works as an antidote and why uridine supplements could in principle blunt chemotherapy.", "body": [["Brain", "Proposed support of synaptic membrane synthesis; shown mainly in animals."], ["Gut", "Mild upset or diarrhea reported; UMP is dephosphorylated to uridine before absorption."], ["Liver", "Major site of uridine production and breakdown that helps set plasma uridine levels."], ["Bone marrow and gut lining", "Protected by uridine during fluoropyrimidine toxicity, the basis of the prescription antidote."], ["Kidneys", "Excrete uridine breakdown products."]], "journey": "Nucleotides like UMP are poorly absorbed intact; intestinal phosphatases remove the phosphate, and uridine is absorbed through nucleoside transporters in the gut lining. Plasma uridine rises within an hour or two, but the liver clears much of it on first pass, and uridine phosphorylase breaks it down to uracil, which is further degraded through dihydrouracil to beta-alanine, ammonia and carbon dioxide. Uridine crosses the blood-brain barrier through nucleoside transporters, which is the basis of brain-targeting claims. Plasma levels return toward baseline within hours, so the effect of a single intake is short. Breakdown products leave mainly in urine and as exhaled carbon dioxide. The prescription prodrug uridine triacetate was designed to overcome the poor bioavailability of plain uridine, delivering much higher plasma uridine than the supplement form.", "sidefx": "Reported side effects of supplement uridine and UMP are few and mild, mostly stomach upset, loose stools and headache. Safety data in pregnancy, breastfeeding, children and chronic disease are lacking. The interaction worth knowing is with fluoropyrimidine chemotherapy, fluorouracil and capecitabine, where uridine counteracts the drug; how much supplemental amounts matter is uncertain, but the mechanism is the same one used therapeutically by uridine triacetate. People using nootropic stacks often combine uridine with racetams, choline sources and stimulants, so effects of the whole mix are unpredictable.", "fun": "The same molecule that supplement buyers take hoping to sharpen memory is, in a prodrug form, the emergency antidote that oncology teams reach for when a fluorouracil infusion pump runs too fast.", "src": ["FDA prescribing information: Vistogard (uridine triacetate)", "FDA prescribing information: Xuriden (uridine triacetate)", "Wurtman RJ et al. 2006, Brain Research, synaptic proteins and phosphatides with uridine, DHA and choline", "Scheltens P et al. 2012, Journal of Alzheimer's Disease, nutrient combination trial in mild Alzheimer disease"]}, "piracetam": {"origin": "Piracetam was synthesized in the 1960s at the Belgian company UCB, which was looking for compounds related to the brain chemical GABA. It did not act like a sedative, but the Romanian-born pharmacologist Corneliu Giurgea, working at UCB, found that it seemed to protect learning and memory in animals under stress such as low oxygen. In the early 1970s he coined the word nootropic, from Greek roots meaning mind and turning, to describe a new class of drugs that would enhance cognition without the usual sedative or stimulant effects, and piracetam was its founding member. Piracetam was marketed in many European and other countries for conditions ranging from cognitive decline to vertigo, and it remains authorized in some places, notably for cortical myoclonus. Larger and better-designed trials later failed to show clear benefit in dementia or acute stroke. In the United States it was never approved, but internet sales grew with the nootropic and 'biohacking' movement. In 2019 FDA and FTC sent warning letters to companies selling it as a supplement, and a study that year found unlisted piracetam in several cognitive supplements, in amounts that varied widely and did not match labels.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Piracetam is synthesized at UCB in Belgium as a cyclic derivative related to GABA."], ["1972", "Corneliu Giurgea introduces the term nootropic with piracetam as the model drug."], ["1970s to 1990s", "Piracetam is marketed in many countries outside the US for cognitive and other indications."], ["2000s", "Cochrane reviews find no convincing benefit in dementia or acute ischemic stroke."], ["2019", "FDA warning letters target companies selling piracetam as a supplement; a published study finds unlisted piracetam in cognitive supplements."]], "cell": "Piracetam is a small, water-soluble cyclic derivative of GABA, but it does not bind GABA receptors in a meaningful way. Proposed mechanisms are unusually vague for a drug used for decades. One idea is that it interacts with the polar heads of membrane phospholipids and restores membrane fluidity, which could in turn help membrane proteins such as receptors and ion channels work normally, a theory that fits its apparently larger effects in aged or injured tissue. Another line of work showed that piracetam and related racetams act as weak positive modulators of AMPA-type glutamate receptors, a property more strongly developed in aniracetam, and some studies suggested effects on cholinergic transmission. The best-documented peripheral effects are hematologic: piracetam reduces platelet aggregation, lowers plasma fibrinogen and von Willebrand factor in some studies, and improves red cell deformability, which is why labeling abroad warns about bleeding and why it was tried in sickle cell disease and vascular conditions. In cortical myoclonus the mechanism remains unknown. Piracetam should not be confused with levetiracetam, which shares part of the name and the pyrrolidone ring but works by binding synaptic vesicle protein SV2A.", "body": [["Brain", "Proposed cognitive effects not convincingly shown in trials; can cause nervousness, agitation or insomnia; used abroad for cortical myoclonus."], ["Blood", "Reduces platelet aggregation and fibrinogen, adding to bleeding risk with anticoagulants."], ["Kidneys", "Clear the drug almost entirely unchanged; impairment causes accumulation."], ["Gut", "Nausea, abdominal discomfort and diarrhea are reported."], ["Body weight", "Weight gain is listed among side effects in labeling abroad."]], "journey": "Piracetam is rapidly and almost completely absorbed after oral intake, with peak plasma levels in under an hour in a fasting adult. It distributes widely, is not bound to plasma proteins, and crosses the blood-brain barrier and the placenta. It is not significantly metabolized by the liver, which means few metabolic drug interactions. Elimination is by glomerular filtration, with nearly all of the drug appearing unchanged in urine, and the half-life of about five hours lengthens substantially as kidney function declines, which is why labeling abroad ties dosing to creatinine clearance and contraindicates it in end-stage kidney disease. Because it is small, water-soluble and unbound, it is removed by hemodialysis. None of this can be relied upon for online products, whose actual content may differ from the label or include other drugs.", "sidefx": "Reported side effects include nervousness, agitation, irritability, insomnia, drowsiness, headache, weight gain and GI upset. Bleeding is the main serious concern, linked to the antiplatelet effect and of greatest relevance in patients on warfarin, antiplatelet drugs, or with bleeding disorders, recent hemorrhage or upcoming surgery. Accumulation in kidney disease raises the chance of side effects. In patients treated for myoclonus, abrupt withdrawal has been associated with seizures and return of myoclonus. In the United States, the largest risk may be the product itself: online powders and unlisted ingredients in supplements mean unknown amounts and possible combination with other drugs or stimulants.", "fun": "The whole category of nootropics, now a crowded supplement aisle, was named by one pharmacologist to describe a single molecule, piracetam, more than fifty years ago.", "src": ["Cohen PA, Zakharevich I, Gerona R 2019, JAMA Internal Medicine, presence of piracetam in cognitive enhancement dietary supplements", "FDA warning letters (2019) to companies marketing piracetam as a dietary supplement", "Cochrane review: Piracetam for dementia or cognitive impairment", "Winblad B 2005, CNS Drug Reviews, piracetam review"]}, "fish-oil": {"origin": "Interest in fish oil as medicine started with cod liver oil, used for centuries in northern Europe and widely given to children in the nineteenth and early twentieth centuries to prevent rickets, before anyone knew the active ingredient was vitamin D. The omega-3 story began in the 1970s, when Danish researchers Hans Olaf Bang and Jorn Dyerberg studied Inuit communities in Greenland and noted diets rich in marine fat, low reported rates of heart attacks, and a tendency to bleed easily; they linked this to eicosapentaenoic acid in marine foods. Later work questioned how low heart disease rates really were, but the hypothesis drove decades of research. Fish oil lowered triglycerides reliably, and prescription omega-3 products were approved by FDA for very high triglycerides. Large prevention trials of supplement-type omega-3 in the 2000s and 2010s were mostly neutral for heart events, while a 2018 trial of high-purity EPA as icosapent ethyl reduced cardiovascular events in high-risk patients, a result still debated because of the mineral oil placebo. Meanwhile, fish oil became one of the best-selling supplements in the United States.", "timeline": [["1800s", "Cod liver oil is widely used in Europe for rickets and general health."], ["1970s", "Bang and Dyerberg study Greenland Inuit diets and link marine omega-3 fats to blood lipid and clotting differences."], ["2004", "FDA approves the first prescription omega-3-acid ethyl ester product for very high triglycerides."], ["2018", "Large trials report neutral results for supplement-type omega-3 in prevention, while a trial of icosapent ethyl reports reduced cardiovascular events in high-risk patients."], ["2019", "FDA approves an expanded cardiovascular risk reduction indication for icosapent ethyl."]], "cell": "EPA and DHA are long-chain omega-3 fatty acids with five and six double bonds. Once eaten they are incorporated into the phospholipids of cell membranes, partly displacing the omega-6 fatty acid arachidonic acid. When cells are activated and phospholipase A2 releases membrane fatty acids, more EPA and less arachidonic acid enter the cyclooxygenase and lipoxygenase pathways. EPA produces thromboxane A3, a much weaker platelet activator than thromboxane A2, and leukotriene B5, a weaker inflammatory signal than leukotriene B4. EPA and DHA are also precursors of specialized pro-resolving mediators, the resolvins, protectins and maresins, which help switch off inflammation. In the liver, omega-3 fatty acids reduce triglyceride production by suppressing the lipogenic transcription factor SREBP-1c, activating PPAR-alpha to increase fatty acid oxidation, and reducing the supply of fatty acids for VLDL assembly. In heart cells, incorporation into membranes affects sodium and calcium channel behavior, which once suggested an antiarrhythmic effect; human trials instead found more atrial fibrillation at higher intakes, a reminder that mechanisms in cells do not always predict outcomes.", "body": [["Liver", "Reduces VLDL triglyceride production; the most reliable clinical effect is lower triglycerides."], ["Blood", "Mild reduction in platelet aggregation, adding to bleeding risk with anticoagulants."], ["Heart", "Atrial fibrillation risk rises at higher intakes in trials; event prevention results are mixed."], ["Brain and eye", "DHA is a major structural fat of the retina and brain membranes."], ["Blood vessels", "Small blood pressure lowering in pooled studies."], ["Gut", "Fishy burps, heartburn and loose stools are common."]], "journey": "Fish oil fats are digested like other dietary fats. Pancreatic lipase releases fatty acids from triglyceride forms, while ethyl ester forms need a separate lipase step and are absorbed less well without a fatty meal. The fatty acids are packaged into chylomicrons in the intestinal lining, travel through lymph into the blood, and are delivered to the liver and tissues. Plasma levels peak several hours after a meal, but the meaningful measure is incorporation into membranes, such as the red cell omega-3 index, which rises over weeks and takes weeks to months to fall after stopping. EPA and DHA are used for energy through beta-oxidation, converted to eicosanoids and pro-resolving mediators, or stored in membranes and adipose tissue. They end up mainly as carbon dioxide and water, with no meaningful kidney excretion of the parent fats.", "sidefx": "The common side effects are a fishy aftertaste, burping, heartburn, nausea and loose stools. The safety issues that matter clinically are bleeding and atrial fibrillation. Bleeding risk alone is small, but it adds to anticoagulants, antiplatelets, NSAIDs and other supplements such as ginkgo and garlic. In trials of high intakes, especially high-purity EPA, atrial fibrillation and flutter occurred more often, and prescription labels warn about it. In people with very high triglycerides, DHA-containing products can raise LDL cholesterol. Cod liver oil contains vitamins A and D, so large intakes can contribute to vitamin A toxicity. Fish allergy is a consideration, although highly refined oils contain little protein.", "fun": "The Greenland Inuit observations that launched the fish oil industry included a detail that is now on prescription labels: easy bleeding, noted decades before anyone ran a trial.", "src": ["NIH Office of Dietary Supplements: Omega-3 Fatty Acids Fact Sheet for Health Professionals", "NIH NCCIH: Omega-3 Supplements: In Depth", "Bhatt DL et al. 2019, New England Journal of Medicine, REDUCE-IT trial", "Manson JE et al. 2019, New England Journal of Medicine, VITAL trial", "FDA prescribing information: Vascepa (icosapent ethyl)"]}, "krill-oil": {"origin": "Antarctic krill, Euphausia superba, are small swimming crustaceans that form some of the largest animal swarms on Earth and sit near the base of the Southern Ocean food web, feeding whales, seals, penguins and fish. Commercial krill fishing began in the second half of the twentieth century, first mainly for animal feed and aquaculture. As the omega-3 supplement market grew, companies developed extraction methods for krill oil and promoted it as a premium alternative to fish oil, highlighting that much of its EPA and DHA is attached to phospholipids, similar to how these fats sit in cell membranes, and that it contains astaxanthin, the pigment that gives krill, salmon and flamingos their pink-red color. Krill oil ingredients went through the FDA GRAS notification process for use in foods, and krill oil became a common supplement in the 2000s and 2010s. Krill fishing is managed internationally under the Commission for the Conservation of Antarctic Marine Living Resources, and environmental groups continue to debate its effects on the ecosystem. Clinical research is smaller and more often industry-funded than research on fish oil, and there are no large outcome trials.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Large-scale Antarctic krill fishing develops, mainly for feed and food products."], ["1982", "The Convention on the Conservation of Antarctic Marine Living Resources enters into force, covering krill harvest management."], ["2000s", "Krill oil is commercialized as a dietary supplement and promoted for phospholipid-bound omega-3."], ["2010s", "Small trials compare krill oil with fish oil for absorption and triglyceride lowering, with mixed results."]], "cell": "Krill oil delivers the same active fatty acids as fish oil, EPA and DHA, but a large share is esterified to phosphatidylcholine rather than to glycerol as triglycerides. Once absorbed, EPA and DHA are built into membrane phospholipids throughout the body and partly displace arachidonic acid. When phospholipase A2 releases fatty acids during cell activation, EPA competes with arachidonic acid at cyclooxygenase and lipoxygenase, producing thromboxane A3 and leukotriene B5, which are weaker platelet and inflammatory signals than their arachidonic acid counterparts. EPA and DHA also give rise to resolvins and protectins, which help actively end inflammation. In the liver, omega-3 fatty acids reduce VLDL triglyceride output by suppressing SREBP-1c and activating PPAR-alpha. The phosphatidylcholine carrier also supplies choline. Astaxanthin is a xanthophyll carotenoid that spans cell membranes and quenches singlet oxygen and lipid peroxyl radicals in laboratory studies; it also helps protect the polyunsaturated oil itself from oxidation. Whether the phospholipid form changes clinical effects, rather than just the absorption curve, has not been settled.", "body": [["Liver", "Reduced VLDL triglyceride production, the main measurable lipid effect."], ["Blood", "Mild antiplatelet effect, adding to bleeding risk with anticoagulants."], ["Immune system", "Possible allergic reactions in people with crustacean shellfish allergy."], ["Gut", "Burping, heartburn and loose stools, sometimes described as milder than with fish oil."], ["Cell membranes", "EPA and DHA incorporate into membranes over weeks."]], "journey": "Phospholipid-bound omega-3 fatty acids are digested by pancreatic phospholipase A2, which releases one fatty acid and leaves a lysophospholipid; both are absorbed by intestinal cells, re-esterified, and packaged into chylomicrons. Some evidence suggests phospholipid forms are partly incorporated into HDL particles and delivered differently to tissues, but whether this gives better tissue levels than equal amounts of fish oil is uncertain, and because krill oil capsules usually contain less EPA and DHA, comparisons depend heavily on how studies matched intake. Plasma levels peak hours after a meal, while membrane and red cell omega-3 levels rise over weeks. EPA and DHA are eventually oxidized for energy or converted to lipid mediators and leave the body mostly as carbon dioxide and water. Astaxanthin is fat-soluble, absorbed with fat into chylomicrons and carried in lipoproteins.", "sidefx": "Side effects resemble fish oil: burping, fishy taste, heartburn, nausea and loose stools. The clinically relevant concerns are bleeding with anticoagulants, antiplatelets or other bleed-risk supplements, and allergic reactions in people with crustacean shellfish allergy, since refined oils may still contain small amounts of protein and product labels often carry shellfish allergen statements. As with any omega-3 source, higher intakes have been linked to atrial fibrillation in trials of other products. Long-term safety data specific to krill oil are limited, and product quality, oxidation and astaxanthin content vary.", "fun": "The pink color of wild salmon and flamingos comes from eating krill and other crustaceans rich in astaxanthin, the same pigment that turns krill oil capsules deep red.", "src": ["NIH Office of Dietary Supplements: Omega-3 Fatty Acids Fact Sheet for Health Professionals", "NIH NCCIH: Omega-3 Supplements: In Depth", "Ulven SM, Holven KB 2015, Vascular Health and Risk Management, krill oil versus fish oil review", "Commission for the Conservation of Antarctic Marine Living Resources: krill fisheries"]}, "algal-oil": {"origin": "The omega-3 fats in fish do not start in fish. Marine microalgae and other single-celled organisms make most of the long-chain omega-3 DHA in the ocean, and the fats move up the food chain into zooplankton and fish. In the 1980s, the US company Martek Biosciences, which grew out of NASA-related research on algae as food for long space missions, developed fermentation methods to grow DHA-rich microalgae such as Crypthecodinium cohnii in large tanks. As research highlighted the role of DHA in infant retina and brain development, Martek's algal DHA, paired with a fungal source of arachidonic acid, became a widely used ingredient in infant formula after FDA raised no objection to a GRAS notice around 2001. Other producers later used Schizochytrium strains, some of which also make EPA. Algal oil then expanded into prenatal vitamins, fortified foods and vegan omega-3 supplements, offering a fish-free source with low exposure to ocean contaminants such as mercury. The DHA itself is chemically the same as in fish oil, so most of what is known about omega-3 effects applies, although outcome trials specifically with algal oil are fewer.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Martek Biosciences develops fermentation of DHA-producing microalgae."], ["2001", "FDA raises no objection to a GRAS notice for algal DHA and fungal arachidonic acid in infant formula."], ["2000s", "Algal DHA spreads into infant formula, prenatal vitamins and fortified foods."], ["2010s", "Schizochytrium-based oils containing both DHA and EPA become common vegan omega-3 supplements."]], "cell": "DHA, docosahexaenoic acid, is a 22-carbon omega-3 fatty acid with six double bonds, and it is the most concentrated polyunsaturated fat in the retina and the gray matter of the brain. In photoreceptor outer segment membranes, DHA-rich phospholipids create a highly flexible environment that supports the shape changes of rhodopsin during light detection. In neurons, DHA in membranes affects receptor and ion channel function and is converted to neuroprotectin D1 and other docosanoids. DHA is preferentially taken up into the brain as lysophosphatidylcholine through the transporter MFSD2A at the blood-brain barrier, a pathway discovered relatively recently. Systemically, DHA and EPA displace arachidonic acid from membranes, reduce production of thromboxane A2 and other pro-inflammatory eicosanoids, and give rise to resolvins and protectins. In the liver they suppress SREBP-1c and activate PPAR-alpha, lowering VLDL triglyceride secretion. Some DHA is retroconverted to EPA in the body. Because the molecule is identical whether it comes from algae or fish, these mechanisms are shared; differences between products come from the EPA to DHA ratio and the amount per serving.", "body": [["Eye", "DHA is the main structural fat in photoreceptor membranes of the retina."], ["Brain", "DHA is concentrated in gray matter and synaptic membranes."], ["Liver", "Reduced VLDL triglyceride production with adequate intakes."], ["Blood", "Possible mild antiplatelet effect, theoretical additive risk with anticoagulants."], ["Placenta and fetus", "DHA is transferred to the fetus, especially in the third trimester."]], "journey": "Algal oils are usually triglycerides, digested by pancreatic lipase and absorbed with other dietary fat. DHA is re-esterified in intestinal cells and packaged into chylomicrons, then distributed through lymph and blood to the liver and tissues. Absorption is better with a meal containing fat. Blood DHA rises over days, and red cell and tissue levels rise over weeks. The brain takes up DHA mainly as lysophosphatidylcholine through MFSD2A, and the placenta actively transfers DHA to the fetus. DHA is used for energy through peroxisomal and mitochondrial beta-oxidation, converted to lipid mediators, or stored in membranes. It leaves the body mainly as carbon dioxide and water.", "sidefx": "Algal oil is generally well tolerated. Burping, mild nausea or loose stools can occur, usually with less fishy aftertaste than fish oil. Because DHA and EPA have mild antiplatelet effects, a theoretical additive bleeding risk exists with anticoagulants and antiplatelet drugs, as with any omega-3 source, though the signal is less studied for algal products. Atrial fibrillation signals seen with high omega-3 intakes in trials of other products are a general consideration. Algal oils avoid fish and shellfish allergens and generally have low levels of mercury and other ocean contaminants, which is one reason they are used in infant and prenatal products.", "fun": "Some of the research that led to algal DHA in baby formula grew out of work on growing algae as a food source for long space missions.", "src": ["NIH Office of Dietary Supplements: Omega-3 Fatty Acids Fact Sheet for Health Professionals", "Middleton P et al. 2018, Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews, omega-3 fatty acid addition during pregnancy", "Nguyen LN et al. 2014, Nature, MFSD2A is a transporter for DHA", "FDA GRAS notices for algal DHA oils"]}, "flaxseed": {"origin": "Flax, Linum usitatissimum, is one of the oldest cultivated plants. Its fibers were spun into linen in the ancient Near East and Egypt thousands of years ago, and the species name means most useful. Linseed oil, pressed from the seeds, was used for food in some regions and widely for paints, varnishes and linoleum, whose name comes from the Latin for flax and oil. Flaxseed was also a traditional laxative because its seed coat forms a slippery gel in water. Modern interest grew in the late twentieth century when nutrition researchers identified flax as the richest common plant source of alpha-linolenic acid, the plant omega-3, and of lignans, especially secoisolariciresinol diglucoside. That combination led to research on cholesterol, blood pressure, hot flashes and hormone-related cancers. Ground flaxseed has shown modest benefits for cholesterol and constipation in small trials, while hot flash trials have been disappointing and cancer data remain mostly observational or preliminary. The oil, which lacks most of the fiber and lignans, behaves differently from the ground seed, a distinction that is often blurred in marketing.", "timeline": [["Ancient times", "Flax is cultivated for linen fiber and seed in the Near East and Egypt."], ["1860s", "Linoleum, made with linseed oil, is invented as a floor covering."], ["1990s", "Research focuses on flaxseed ALA and lignans for heart and hormone-related conditions."], ["2000s to 2010s", "Small trials report modest cholesterol and blood pressure effects with ground flaxseed; hot flash trials are largely negative."]], "cell": "Flaxseed acts through three separate components. The seed coat mucilage is a soluble fiber that absorbs water to form a gel, increasing stool bulk and softness and slowing gastric emptying. In the gut lumen this gel binds bile acids, so the liver uses more cholesterol to make new bile acids and increases LDL receptor activity, which modestly lowers LDL cholesterol. The oil fraction is rich in alpha-linolenic acid, an 18-carbon omega-3 fatty acid. Humans can elongate and desaturate ALA to EPA through delta-6 and delta-5 desaturases, but the conversion is limited and competes with omega-6 linoleic acid for the same enzymes, and conversion onward to DHA is very small. Most ALA is burned for energy. The lignan secoisolariciresinol diglucoside is converted by colonic bacteria to the enterolignans enterodiol and enterolactone, which bind estrogen receptors weakly and can act as weak estrogens or anti-estrogens depending on background hormone levels; they may also affect aromatase. Raw and unripe seeds contain cyanogenic glycosides such as linustatin, which can release small amounts of cyanide, largely reduced by heat processing.", "body": [["Gut", "Fiber increases stool bulk; gas and bloating are common; obstruction is possible without enough fluid."], ["Liver", "Increased bile acid synthesis after bile acid binding contributes to modest LDL lowering."], ["Blood vessels", "Small blood pressure reductions in pooled studies."], ["Hormone-sensitive tissues", "Lignans act as weak phytoestrogens; clinical significance unclear."], ["Blood", "ALA has weak antiplatelet effects in theory; clinical bleeding risk not well shown."]], "journey": "Whole flaxseeds have a hard coat and often pass through the gut intact, which is why ground seed is typically used. Once ground, the fiber stays in the gut, absorbs water and is partly fermented by colonic bacteria, producing short-chain fatty acids and gas. ALA in the oil is digested and absorbed like other dietary fats, packaged into chylomicrons and either oxidized for energy or partly converted in the liver to longer omega-3s. Lignans are freed from the seed matrix and converted by gut bacteria to enterodiol and enterolactone, which are absorbed, conjugated in the liver, circulate through enterohepatic cycling, and are excreted in urine, where they can be measured as markers of intake. Unabsorbed fiber leaves in stool. Because the fiber gel surrounds other gut contents, it can slow absorption of medicines taken at the same time.", "sidefx": "Gas, bloating, abdominal discomfort and loose stools are common when ground flaxseed is added quickly. Taken with too little fluid, or in people with swallowing problems or narrowing of the esophagus or bowel, the fiber can cause obstruction. Allergic reactions, including rare anaphylaxis, have been reported. The fiber can delay absorption of oral drugs. Effects on glucose and blood pressure are small. Flax oil causes fewer GI effects but turns rancid easily. Raw or unripe seeds contain cyanogenic glycosides; meaningful cyanide toxicity from typical food use is not expected, and heat processing reduces these compounds. Theoretical concerns about lignans in hormone-sensitive conditions and pregnancy remain unsettled.", "fun": "The word linoleum literally means flax oil, because the floor covering was originally made by oxidizing linseed oil until it set into a solid sheet.", "src": ["NIH NCCIH: Flaxseed and Flaxseed Oil", "NIH Office of Dietary Supplements: Omega-3 Fatty Acids Fact Sheet for Health Professionals", "Parikh M et al. 2019, Nutrients, dietary flaxseed as a strategy for improving human health", "Natural Medicines-style monograph: Flaxseed"]}, "nattokinase": {"origin": "Natto is a traditional Japanese food made by fermenting cooked soybeans with Bacillus subtilis var. natto, producing a sticky, stringy, strong-smelling dish that has been eaten for centuries. In the 1980s the Japanese researcher Hiroyuki Sumi and colleagues reported that natto contained a potent fibrin-dissolving enzyme, which they named nattokinase, after observing that natto placed on an artificial fibrin clot dissolved it. The enzyme turned out to be a serine protease related to subtilisin, a well-known bacterial protease used in laundry detergents. Nattokinase was then extracted and sold as a supplement, first in Japan and later worldwide, marketed as a natural way to prevent blood clots, improve circulation and lower blood pressure. Human research has been small and mixed, and a key unresolved question is how much active enzyme survives digestion and reaches the blood. Meanwhile, clinicians noted case reports of bleeding in people combining nattokinase with aspirin. There is also an irony for warfarin users: natto the food is one of the richest sources of vitamin K2 and can reduce warfarin's effect, while the purified enzyme supplement may increase bleeding.", "timeline": [["Centuries ago", "Natto, fermented soybeans, becomes a traditional food in Japan."], ["1987", "Sumi and colleagues report a fibrinolytic enzyme in natto and name it nattokinase."], ["1990s to 2000s", "Nattokinase supplements spread from Japan to global markets."], ["2000s", "Case reports describe bleeding, including intracerebral hemorrhage, in nattokinase users."], ["Recent years", "Small trials of atherosclerosis progression and blood pressure report no clear or only modest benefits."]], "cell": "Nattokinase is a single-chain serine protease of about 275 amino acids in the subtilisin family, with the classic catalytic triad of aspartate, histidine and serine. In laboratory systems it cleaves cross-linked fibrin directly, unlike tissue plasminogen activator, which works indirectly by converting plasminogen to plasmin. It has also been reported to cleave and inactivate plasminogen activator inhibitor-1, which would increase the body's own fibrinolysis, to enhance release of tissue plasminogen activator, and to reduce platelet aggregation in some experiments. Some studies suggest it can degrade amyloid fibrils in vitro. The big barrier is the gut: as a protein, most nattokinase should be digested by stomach acid and pancreatic proteases, and while animal studies and some human marker studies suggest some activity reaches the circulation, the size and clinical meaning of this are uncertain. Its reported effects on blood pressure have been attributed to fibrinolytic peptides or inhibition of angiotensin-converting enzyme by digestion products, but these mechanisms are not well established.", "body": [["Blood", "Reported increased fibrinolysis and reduced clotting tendency; additive bleeding risk with anticoagulants and antiplatelets."], ["Brain", "Intracerebral hemorrhage described in case reports, some involving combined aspirin use."], ["Blood vessels", "Small blood pressure reductions reported in some trials."], ["Gut", "Most of the enzyme is digested; mild upset possible."], ["Immune system", "Soy-derived products may matter in soy allergy."]], "journey": "Nattokinase is a protein, so the expected fate after oral intake is digestion into peptides and amino acids by stomach acid, pepsin and pancreatic proteases. Supplement makers often use enteric-coated capsules to protect it from acid. Animal studies with labeled enzyme and some human studies measuring fibrinolytic markers suggest that a small fraction of intact or active enzyme may cross the gut lining, possibly by transcytosis, with changes in some markers lasting several hours. Distribution, metabolism and elimination of any absorbed enzyme are not well characterized, though like other circulating proteins it would be cleared by proteolysis. Products are labeled in fibrinolytic units, a measure of activity in a laboratory assay that does not reliably predict activity in the body, and the methods are not standardized across brands.", "sidefx": "Most users report no side effects or mild GI upset. The important risk is bleeding. Easy bruising, nosebleeds, gum bleeding and blood in urine or stool can occur, and case reports describe intracerebral hemorrhage, particularly when nattokinase was combined with aspirin. Risk is likely highest with anticoagulants, antiplatelets, thrombolytics, bleeding disorders, recent surgery or a history of hemorrhagic stroke. Another danger is indirect: people who believe the supplement prevents clots may stop prescribed anticoagulants, raising the risk of stroke or pulmonary embolism. Products derived from soy may be relevant in soy allergy, and the food natto is high in vitamin K2, which opposes warfarin.", "fun": "Nattokinase belongs to the same enzyme family as subtilisin, a bacterial protease that has been added to laundry detergents for decades to digest protein stains.", "src": ["Sumi H et al. 1987, Experientia, a novel fibrinolytic enzyme in natto", "Weng Y et al. 2017, International Journal of Molecular Sciences, nattokinase: an oral antithrombotic agent", "Natural Medicines-style monograph: Nattokinase"]}, "plant-sterols": {"origin": "Plant sterols were recognized as cholesterol relatives early in the twentieth century, and in the 1950s researchers found that feeding beta-sitosterol lowered blood cholesterol in animals and people. A beta-sitosterol product was even marketed as a cholesterol drug in the United States for some years, but it required large amounts of poorly soluble powder and fell out of use when more potent drugs arrived. The modern era began in Finland, a country with very high rates of heart disease at the time. Researchers developed a way to esterify sitostanol, made from wood pulp byproducts, with fatty acids so it could dissolve in margarine, and a stanol ester spread was launched there in 1995 after a trial showed LDL lowering. Sterol ester spreads from other companies followed. In 2000 FDA authorized a health claim linking plant sterol and stanol esters to reduced risk of coronary heart disease on qualifying foods. Meanwhile, the discovery of the rare disorder sitosterolemia, in which mutations in the ABCG5 or ABCG8 transporters cause plant sterols to accumulate and drive early atherosclerosis, raised the question of whether higher plant sterol levels might be harmful in general, a debate that is still not fully settled.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Beta-sitosterol is shown to lower cholesterol and is briefly marketed as a cholesterol drug."], ["1974", "Sitosterolemia is described as an inherited disorder of plant sterol accumulation."], ["1995", "A stanol ester margarine is launched in Finland after trials show LDL lowering."], ["2000", "FDA authorizes a health claim for plant sterol and stanol esters and coronary heart disease risk."], ["2000", "Mutations in ABCG5 and ABCG8 are identified as the cause of sitosterolemia."]], "cell": "Cholesterol from food and bile must be dissolved into bile acid micelles before it can reach the brush border of the small intestine. Plant sterols and stanols are more hydrophobic than cholesterol and have a higher affinity for these micelles, so they push cholesterol out, leaving more of it to pass into stool. The cholesterol that does reach the enterocyte enters through the transporter NPC1L1, the target of ezetimibe. Plant sterols can also enter through NPC1L1, but the enterocyte sends most of them straight back to the gut lumen through the heterodimer ABCG5/ABCG8, and the liver pumps any that reach it into bile through the same transporter. With less cholesterol arriving in chylomicron remnants, the liver senses lower cholesterol, activates SREBP-2, and increases LDL receptors and cholesterol synthesis. The net result is a modest fall in LDL cholesterol, partly offset by increased synthesis, which is why effects add to statins, which block synthesis. Stanols, the saturated versions, are absorbed even less than sterols.", "body": [["Small intestine", "Site of action: displaces cholesterol from micelles and reduces its absorption."], ["Liver", "Responds with more LDL receptors and higher cholesterol synthesis."], ["Blood lipids", "Modest LDL lowering; little effect on HDL or triglycerides."], ["Blood vessels", "Accumulated plant sterols in sitosterolemia drive early atherosclerosis."], ["Carotenoid status", "Lower blood beta-carotene and some carotenoids due to reduced absorption."]], "journey": "Phytosterols and stanols are taken in foods, usually as fatty acid esters that are hydrolyzed by pancreatic cholesterol esterase in the gut, freeing the sterols to join bile acid micelles. Only a small percentage of plant sterols, and even less of stanols, is absorbed, compared with about half of cholesterol. What is absorbed is mostly pumped back into the gut by ABCG5/ABCG8 in the enterocytes, and the small amount reaching the blood travels in lipoproteins and is secreted by the liver into bile. Nearly all of the intake therefore leaves in feces, along with the extra cholesterol that was displaced. The LDL-lowering effect develops over two to three weeks and fades after stopping. Because the action is local in the gut, the effect is best when sterols are eaten with meals that contain cholesterol and bile flow.", "sidefx": "Plant sterols are generally well tolerated, with occasional constipation, diarrhea, gas or nausea. They modestly lower blood levels of beta-carotene and some other fat-soluble carotenoids, which is usually offset by eating fruits and vegetables. The main contraindication is sitosterolemia, a rare inherited disorder in which plant sterols are absorbed and retained, causing tendon xanthomas, premature atherosclerosis and sometimes hemolysis and low platelets. Whether modestly higher plant sterol levels in the general population carry cardiovascular risk is debated, and there are no large outcome trials showing that fortified foods prevent heart attacks or strokes.", "fun": "Some of the first plant stanol margarines were made from sitostanol derived from tall oil, a byproduct of turning pine trees into paper pulp.", "src": ["FDA 21 CFR 101.83, health claims: plant sterol/stanol esters and risk of coronary heart disease", "Gylling H et al. 2014, Atherosclerosis, European Atherosclerosis Society consensus on plant sterols and stanols", "Berge KE et al. 2000, Science, ABCG5 and ABCG8 mutations in sitosterolemia", "StatPearls: Sitosterolemia"]}, "policosanol": {"origin": "Policosanol was developed in Cuba in the late 1980s and 1990s by researchers at the National Center for Scientific Research in Havana, who extracted a mixture of long-chain alcohols from the wax that coats sugar cane, a major Cuban crop. The mixture, rich in octacosanol, was registered as a cholesterol-lowering drug in Cuba and several other countries, and the same group published many trials reporting LDL reductions comparable to low doses of statins, along with antiplatelet effects and benefits in leg pain from peripheral artery disease. Those striking results made policosanol a popular supplement in the United States, where it is sold as a dietary supplement rather than a drug. Concern grew because nearly all positive trials came from one group. In 2006 a German randomized controlled trial published in JAMA, led by Heiner Berthold, tested Cuban sugar cane policosanol in people with high cholesterol and found no meaningful LDL lowering at any dose, and other independent studies had similar results. Policosanol is now often cited in evidence-based medicine teaching as an example of results that did not replicate, though it remains on supplement shelves.", "timeline": [["Late 1980s", "Cuban researchers develop policosanol from sugar cane wax."], ["1990s", "Many trials from the originating group report LDL lowering and antiplatelet effects."], ["2000s", "Policosanol becomes a popular US supplement marketed as a natural cholesterol reducer."], ["2006", "A German randomized trial in JAMA finds no significant LDL lowering with sugar cane policosanol."]], "cell": "Policosanol is a mixture of very long straight-chain primary alcohols, mostly octacosanol with 28 carbons, plus triacontanol, hexacosanol and others. These are waxy, poorly soluble molecules. Proposed mechanisms come mostly from laboratory and animal work. One proposal is that policosanol or its metabolites activate AMP-activated protein kinase, which phosphorylates and inhibits HMG-CoA reductase, reducing cholesterol synthesis indirectly rather than by direct enzyme blockade as statins do; this would increase LDL receptor activity. Another proposal is that the long-chain alcohols are oxidized to very-long-chain fatty acids, such as octacosanoic acid, that affect lipid metabolism. Antiplatelet effects were reported as reduced thromboxane B2 and decreased aggregation in response to several agonists. The difficulty is that independent human trials did not see the lipid changes these mechanisms predict, so either the mechanisms are not active at the concentrations reached in people, or the original findings were not reproducible. Composition also differs by source, so results from sugar cane wax cannot be assumed for beeswax or rice bran products.", "body": [["Liver", "Proposed indirect reduction of cholesterol synthesis; not confirmed clinically."], ["Blood", "Reported reduced platelet aggregation; possible additive bleeding risk."], ["Blood lipids", "No significant LDL change in independent trials."], ["Gut", "Indigestion is among the reported mild effects."], ["Skin", "Skin redness reported occasionally."]], "journey": "Policosanol alcohols are absorbed poorly and variably from the gut, probably with dietary fat through micelles and chylomicrons. Animal studies with radiolabeled octacosanol suggest that what is absorbed reaches the liver and is oxidized to the corresponding very-long-chain fatty acid, which then undergoes peroxisomal beta-oxidation like other fatty acids. A large part of the intake is not absorbed and passes out in feces. Human pharmacokinetic data are limited, and half-life and blood levels are not well defined. The poor and inconsistent absorption may be one reason human results are inconsistent.", "sidefx": "In trials, policosanol was well tolerated, with occasional indigestion, headache, insomnia, dizziness, skin redness and, rarely, weight loss or increased urination. The main theoretical safety concern is additive bleeding with aspirin, clopidogrel, warfarin or other antithrombotic drugs, based on reported antiplatelet effects. A larger practical risk is indirect: relying on an unproven product instead of prescribed lipid-lowering therapy, or using combination blends that also contain red yeast rice, which carries its own statin-like risks of muscle injury and drug interactions.", "fun": "Octacosanol, the main component of policosanol, also forms part of the waxy coating on wheat germ and was marketed decades earlier as an endurance supplement for athletes.", "src": ["Berthold HK et al. 2006, JAMA, effect of policosanol on LDL cholesterol: a randomized controlled trial", "Natural Medicines-style monograph: Policosanol", "Marinangeli CPF et al. 2010, Critical Reviews in Food Science and Nutrition, policosanols as nutraceuticals", "Chen JT et al. 2005, Pharmacotherapy, meta-analysis of natural therapies for hyperlipidemia"]}, "mct-oil": {"origin": "Medium-chain triglycerides were developed in the 1950s as a medical nutrition product. Researchers looking for a fat that patients with poor fat absorption could use found that triglycerides made of 8- and 10-carbon fatty acids, separated from coconut oil by fractionation, were absorbed even when bile or pancreatic enzymes were lacking or when lymph flow was disrupted. MCT became a standard part of nutrition care for conditions such as pancreatic insufficiency, short bowel syndrome, cholestatic liver disease in children, intestinal lymphangiectasia and chylothorax. In 1971 the pediatric neurologist Peter Huttenlocher and colleagues described an MCT-based version of the ketogenic diet for childhood epilepsy, which allowed more carbohydrate than the classic diet because MCT produces ketones so efficiently. MCT also became important for some long-chain fatty acid oxidation disorders, while being contraindicated in medium-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency. In the 2010s, MCT oil moved from hospital formularies into coffee shops, popularized by keto and 'bulletproof coffee' trends and claims about weight loss and mental clarity. The research for these consumer uses is far thinner than for the medical ones.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Medium-chain triglycerides are developed as a fat source for patients with fat malabsorption."], ["1971", "Huttenlocher and colleagues describe the MCT ketogenic diet for childhood epilepsy."], ["1980s to 1990s", "MCAD deficiency is recognized as a common fatty acid oxidation disorder and is added to newborn screening in later years."], ["2010s", "MCT oil becomes a mainstream consumer product through ketogenic diet and coffee trends."]], "cell": "Medium-chain fatty acids have 6 to 12 carbons, with caprylic acid (C8) and capric acid (C10) dominating commercial MCT oil. Their shorter length makes them more water-soluble than long-chain fats. In the gut, lipases hydrolyze MCT quickly, and the free medium-chain fatty acids diffuse into intestinal cells without needing bile acid micelles. Instead of being re-esterified and packed into chylomicrons, they pass directly into portal blood bound to albumin and go straight to the liver. In liver cells, medium-chain fatty acids cross into mitochondria without carnitine palmitoyltransferase-1, the regulated gate for long-chain fats, and are activated to acyl-CoA inside the mitochondrial matrix. They undergo rapid beta-oxidation, flooding the liver with acetyl-CoA and driving ketogenesis even when carbohydrate is present. Medium-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase handles the 6- to 12-carbon steps, which is why its deficiency makes MCT dangerous. In seizure models, decanoic acid directly inhibits AMPA-type glutamate receptors, suggesting an anticonvulsant action beyond ketones.", "body": [["Gut", "Rapid hydrolysis; cramps, diarrhea and bloating are common, especially when started quickly."], ["Liver", "Rapid beta-oxidation and ketone production; prolonged high intake may contribute to fatty liver."], ["Brain", "Uses ketone bodies as an alternative fuel; anticonvulsant effects in the MCT ketogenic diet."], ["Blood lipids", "High saturated fat content; effects on LDL vary."], ["Metabolism", "Increases ketone levels; risk in MCAD deficiency and poorly controlled diabetes."]], "journey": "MCT oil is digested quickly by lingual, gastric and pancreatic lipases. The released medium-chain fatty acids are absorbed by intestinal cells and pass directly into portal blood, rather than into lymph like long-chain fats. They reach the liver within minutes to hours, where most are taken up on first pass, enter mitochondria without carnitine, and are oxidized to acetyl-CoA. Excess acetyl-CoA is converted to the ketone bodies beta-hydroxybutyrate and acetoacetate, which circulate to brain, heart and muscle as fuel. Ketone levels rise within one to two hours and fall over several hours. A small amount of acetone, formed from acetoacetate, is exhaled, and some ketones are lost in urine. Most of the fat ends up as carbon dioxide and water. Little MCT is stored in adipose tissue, which is the basis of weight-loss claims.", "sidefx": "The main side effects are abdominal cramps, nausea, bloating and diarrhea, which often occur when intake is increased quickly or taken on an empty stomach. MCT oil is high in calories and saturated fat. In people with poorly controlled diabetes, added ketone production may matter, especially with SGLT2 inhibitors. In medium-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency, MCT cannot be oxidized normally and can provoke hypoglycemia, lethargy and metabolic crisis, so it is contraindicated. Prolonged high intake has been linked to fat accumulation in the liver in some reports. MCT oil does not supply essential fatty acids, so long-term use as a main fat source requires dietitian planning.", "fun": "MCT was used in hospital nutrition decades before it showed up in coffee: the MCT ketogenic diet for epilepsy dates back to 1971.", "src": ["Huttenlocher PR, Wilbourn AJ, Signore JM 1971, Neurology, medium-chain triglycerides as a therapy for intractable childhood epilepsy", "StatPearls: Medium-Chain Acyl-CoA Dehydrogenase Deficiency", "Chang P et al. 2016, Brain, seizure control by decanoic acid through direct AMPA receptor inhibition", "Bach AC, Babayan VK 1982, American Journal of Clinical Nutrition, medium-chain triglycerides: an update"]}, "exogenous-ketones": {"origin": "Ketone bodies were first noticed in the nineteenth century in the urine of people with uncontrolled diabetes, and for decades they were seen mainly as markers of metabolic disaster. That view changed in the 1960s when George Cahill and colleagues showed that during prolonged fasting the brain switches to using ketones for much of its fuel, making them a normal survival fuel. The idea of giving ketones directly, rather than forcing the body to make them through fasting or a ketogenic diet, was pursued by Richard Veech at the US National Institutes of Health and Kieran Clarke at the University of Oxford. They developed a ketone monoester, (R)-3-hydroxybutyl (R)-3-hydroxybutyrate, that could raise blood ketones to levels normally reached only after days of fasting, and published studies in athletes in the 2010s. Ketone salts, made by pairing beta-hydroxybutyrate with sodium, potassium, calcium or magnesium, reached the consumer market around the same time and became popular in keto diet circles. Research into ketones for heart failure, brain injury and epilepsy is ongoing, while consumer performance and weight-loss claims have outpaced the evidence.", "timeline": [["1800s", "Ketone bodies are identified in the urine of people with uncontrolled diabetes."], ["1960s", "Cahill and colleagues show the brain uses ketones as a major fuel during prolonged fasting."], ["2000s", "Veech and Clarke develop a ketone monoester that raises blood ketones without fasting."], ["2010s", "Ketone salt and ester products enter the consumer market; trials in athletes report mixed results."]], "cell": "Beta-hydroxybutyrate is a four-carbon molecule made in liver mitochondria from acetyl-CoA when fat breakdown outpaces the Krebs cycle, as in fasting. Tissues such as heart, brain, skeletal muscle and kidney take up BHB through monocarboxylate transporters, convert it to acetoacetate by BHB dehydrogenase, activate acetoacetate to acetoacetyl-CoA with the enzyme SCOT, and split it into acetyl-CoA for the Krebs cycle. The liver itself lacks SCOT and cannot use ketones, which is why it exports them. Only the D-form of BHB is the natural substrate for BHB dehydrogenase; salts usually contain a racemic mix, and the L-form is handled more slowly by other pathways. Raised ketones feed back to reduce lipolysis in fat tissue, partly through HCAR2 receptors, and can lower blood glucose by reducing liver glucose output and possibly by stimulating insulin release. Beyond fuel, BHB inhibits class I histone deacetylases, changing gene expression in laboratory studies, and suppresses the NLRP3 inflammasome, effects that drive interest in aging and inflammation research but are not established in people.", "body": [["Brain", "Uses ketones as an alternative fuel; effects on cognition in healthy people unclear."], ["Heart", "Takes up ketones readily; research interest in heart failure."], ["Muscle", "Uses ketones; performance effects in trials are mixed."], ["Gut", "Nausea, cramps and diarrhea are common, especially with esters and large salt servings."], ["Kidneys", "Handle sodium and potassium loads from salts; excrete some ketones."], ["Blood", "Small acute falls in glucose and free fatty acids; acid-base effects in vulnerable people."]], "journey": "BHB salts dissolve in the gut and BHB is absorbed through monocarboxylate transporters, along with the mineral cation. Ketone esters are hydrolyzed by esterases in the gut and liver into BHB and 1,3-butanediol, and the liver converts butanediol into more BHB, which is why esters produce higher and longer ketone levels. Blood BHB peaks within roughly 30 to 60 minutes and returns toward baseline over a few hours. BHB distributes in body water, crosses the blood-brain barrier through monocarboxylate transporters, and is oxidized in extrahepatic tissues. Acetoacetate can break down spontaneously to acetone, which is exhaled and gives a fruity breath odor. Some BHB and acetoacetate spill into urine when levels are high, and the accompanying minerals are excreted by the kidneys.", "sidefx": "GI symptoms, including nausea, abdominal discomfort, bloating and diarrhea, are the most common effects, and ketone esters are known for an unpleasant taste. BHB salts deliver substantial amounts of sodium, potassium, calcium or magnesium, which matters for people with kidney disease, heart failure or high blood pressure. In healthy people the body buffers the acid load and regulates ketone production, so acidosis is not expected; in people with type 1 diabetes, insulin deficiency or SGLT2 inhibitor use, added ketones raise concern and can make ketone test results harder to interpret. Products containing 1,3-butanediol may produce alcohol-like effects in large amounts. Long-term safety data are limited.", "fun": "The liver makes ketones for the rest of the body but cannot burn them itself, because it lacks the enzyme SCOT needed to use acetoacetate.", "src": ["Cahill GF Jr 2006, Annual Review of Nutrition, fuel metabolism in starvation", "Cox PJ et al. 2016, Cell Metabolism, nutritional ketosis alters fuel preference and thereby endurance performance in athletes", "Stubbs BJ et al. 2017, Frontiers in Physiology, on the metabolism of exogenous ketones in humans", "Newman JC, Verdin E 2017, Annual Review of Nutrition, beta-hydroxybutyrate: a signaling metabolite"]}, "psyllium": {"origin": "Plantago ovata, the source of psyllium, is a small plant grown mainly in northwestern India, which supplies most of the world's psyllium. The seed husks have long been used in traditional Ayurvedic and Unani medicine, under the name isabgol or ispaghula, for constipation and diarrhea. Western medicine adopted psyllium as a gentle bulk laxative in the early twentieth century, and the US company G. D. Searle introduced Metamucil in 1934, one of the best-known bulk laxative brands. Psyllium became part of the FDA OTC laxative review, which classified bulk-forming laxatives as generally recognized as safe and effective when labeled properly. In 1998 FDA authorized a health claim that soluble fiber from psyllium husk, as part of a diet low in saturated fat and cholesterol, may reduce the risk of heart disease, based on consistent LDL-lowering trials. Over time, case reports of esophageal and bowel obstruction from fiber products taken without enough fluid led FDA to require a specific choking warning for OTC products containing water-soluble gums such as psyllium. Reports of asthma and anaphylaxis in health care workers and manufacturing staff who inhaled psyllium dust also made it a classic example of occupational allergy.", "timeline": [["Ancient times", "Psyllium husk is used in Ayurvedic and Unani medicine for bowel problems."], ["1934", "Metamucil, a psyllium bulk laxative, is introduced in the United States."], ["1980s", "Bulk-forming laxatives including psyllium are reviewed under the FDA OTC drug monograph process."], ["1990s", "FDA requires choking warnings on OTC products containing water-soluble gums such as psyllium."], ["1998", "FDA authorizes a health claim for soluble fiber from psyllium husk and coronary heart disease risk."]], "cell": "Psyllium husk is mainly a highly branched arabinoxylan, a polysaccharide with a xylose backbone and arabinose side chains. Because of its structure, colonic bacteria ferment it only slowly and partially, so unlike many soluble fibers it keeps its water-holding gel through the whole colon. This is why it softens hard stool in constipation and also adds form to loose stool in diarrhea, acting as a stool normalizer. Increased bulk stretches the colonic wall and promotes propulsive movement. In the small intestine, the viscous gel slows mixing and absorption of nutrients, which blunts the rise in glucose after a meal, and it traps bile acids, carrying them out in stool. To replace lost bile acids, the liver converts more cholesterol through the enzyme cholesterol 7-alpha-hydroxylase, lowers its internal cholesterol and increases LDL receptors, which lowers serum LDL modestly. The same gel-forming property that makes psyllium useful creates its main danger: if it starts swelling before it reaches the stomach, it can form a plug in the throat or esophagus.", "body": [["Colon", "Water-holding gel softens and bulks stool and supports regular movements."], ["Esophagus", "Can swell and obstruct if taken with too little liquid or by people with swallowing problems."], ["Liver", "Increased bile acid synthesis from cholesterol after bile acid binding."], ["Blood lipids", "Modest LDL lowering; basis of the FDA health claim."], ["Blood glucose", "Modest reduction in post-meal glucose rise."], ["Airways", "Inhaled powder can sensitize workers, causing asthma or anaphylaxis."]], "journey": "Psyllium is not absorbed. After it is mixed with liquid and swallowed, it begins to hydrate and swell, forming a gel that passes through the stomach and small intestine, slowing gastric emptying and binding bile acids and some other substances. In the colon, a small portion is fermented by bacteria into short-chain fatty acids and gas, which explains bloating, but most of the gel survives and leaves in stool, carrying water with it. The laxative effect usually appears within one to three days. Because it is not absorbed, there is no metabolism, half-life or kidney excretion in the usual sense, and it is considered compatible with pregnancy and breastfeeding. Its effect on other drugs comes from the gel trapping or slowing medications in the gut, which is why labels commonly advise spacing.", "sidefx": "Bloating, gas, cramping and a feeling of fullness are common, especially when intake rises quickly, and usually ease over time. The serious adverse effects are mechanical and allergic. Taken dry, with too little liquid, or by people with swallowing difficulty, esophageal narrowing or bowel strictures, psyllium can cause choking, esophageal obstruction, or bowel obstruction and impaction. Allergic reactions range from rhinitis and asthma to anaphylaxis, most often in people previously sensitized by inhaling the powder, such as nurses who prepare it or factory workers. Psyllium can reduce or delay absorption of other oral drugs, including lithium, carbamazepine and digoxin.", "fun": "Psyllium works on both constipation and diarrhea because its gel resists being broken down by gut bacteria, so it keeps holding water all the way to the end of the colon.", "src": ["StatPearls: Psyllium", "FDA 21 CFR 101.81, health claims: soluble fiber from certain foods and risk of coronary heart disease", "FDA 21 CFR 201.319, water-soluble gums, hydrophilic gums and hydrophilic mucilloids: required warning", "McRorie JW 2015, Nutrition Today, evidence-based approach to fiber supplements"]}, "collagen-peptides": {"origin": "Collagen is the most abundant protein in the human body and in other animals, forming the strong fibers of skin, tendon, bone and cartilage. People have eaten collagen for a very long time in the form of gelatin and bone broth, made by boiling skin and bones so that the triple helix unwinds. Industrial gelatin production developed in the nineteenth century for food, photography and pharmaceutical capsules. Hydrolyzed collagen, in which gelatin is broken further into short peptides by enzymes so it dissolves even in cold liquids, was developed later and marketed from the late twentieth century, first in Japan and Europe and then globally, as a supplement for skin, hair, nails and joints. Japanese researchers reported that certain collagen dipeptides, especially prolyl-hydroxyproline, survive digestion and appear in blood, offering a possible explanation for effects beyond simple protein. Collagen also has a darker chapter: in the 1970s, very low calorie 'liquid protein' diets based on hydrolyzed collagen became popular in the United States and were linked to sudden cardiac deaths, prompting investigations by CDC and FDA and warnings about such diets. Today collagen is one of the fastest-growing supplement categories, with research quality lagging behind marketing.", "timeline": [["1800s", "Industrial gelatin production expands for food, photography and capsules."], ["1970s", "Collagen-based liquid protein diets are linked to sudden cardiac deaths in the US."], ["1990s to 2000s", "Hydrolyzed collagen supplements are marketed for skin and joints."], ["2005", "Japanese researchers report collagen dipeptides such as prolyl-hydroxyproline in human blood after ingestion."], ["2010s to 2020s", "Collagen becomes a major supplement category; trials remain small and often industry-funded."]], "cell": "Collagen molecules are triple helices of three chains with a repeating glycine-X-Y pattern, where X and Y are often proline and hydroxyproline. Hydroxyproline is made after translation by prolyl hydroxylase, an enzyme that needs vitamin C, which is why scurvy weakens connective tissue. Hydrolyzed collagen is produced by heating and enzymatic digestion into peptides of a few thousand daltons or smaller. In the gut, most of these are digested to free amino acids, but proline- and hydroxyproline-containing dipeptides and tripeptides resist further breakdown and can be absorbed intact by the peptide transporter PEPT1. In laboratory studies, prolyl-hydroxyproline stimulates fibroblast proliferation and hyaluronic acid production and affects chondrocytes, suggesting a signaling role, and animal studies show labeled collagen peptides reaching skin and cartilage. Whether these signals produce meaningful change in human skin or joints is the open question. Nutritionally, collagen is an incomplete protein because it lacks tryptophan and is low in several essential amino acids, so it supports protein synthesis less effectively than complete proteins such as whey.", "body": [["Skin", "Small trials report modest changes in hydration and elasticity; evidence quality limited."], ["Joints", "Some trials report modest reductions in joint pain; mixed overall."], ["Muscle", "Supplies amino acids but is an incomplete protein."], ["Gut", "Fullness, bloating or mild upset."], ["Immune system", "Allergic reactions possible, especially marine collagen in fish allergy."]], "journey": "After being swallowed, collagen peptides are broken down by stomach and pancreatic proteases and brush border peptidases. Most enter intestinal cells as amino acids, glycine, proline and others, through amino acid transporters, but a fraction of hydroxyproline-containing dipeptides and tripeptides is absorbed intact through PEPT1. Blood levels of these peptides peak within about one to two hours and fall within several hours. Absorbed amino acids join the body's amino acid pool and are used for protein synthesis anywhere in the body, not specifically for collagen, or are oxidized for energy, with the nitrogen converted to urea in the liver and excreted by the kidneys. Hydroxyproline that is not reused is broken down in the liver and kidney.", "sidefx": "Collagen peptides are generally well tolerated. Reported effects include fullness, bloating, heartburn, mild GI upset and an unpleasant taste. Allergic reactions are possible, particularly to marine collagen in fish-allergic people and to other source animals. Some independent testing has reported heavy metals in collagen products, varying by brand. Combination 'beauty' products often contain high amounts of biotin, which FDA has warned can interfere with certain laboratory tests, including troponin, with potential for missed diagnoses. Used as a sole protein source, collagen is inadequate because it lacks tryptophan.", "fun": "Gelatin dessert, glue made from animal hides, and the collagen powder stirred into coffee are all versions of the same protein at different stages of breakdown.", "src": ["Iwai K et al. 2005, Journal of Agricultural and Food Chemistry, collagen-derived peptides in human blood after ingestion", "de Miranda RB et al. 2021, International Journal of Dermatology, effects of hydrolyzed collagen supplementation on skin aging", "FDA Safety Communication: biotin (vitamin B7) interference with lab tests", "Isner JM et al. 1979, Circulation, sudden death associated with liquid protein diets"]}, "colostrum": {"origin": "Colostrum is the thick, yellowish first milk produced by mammals in the days after giving birth. In calves, which are born without antibodies in their blood, colostrum is essential: the newborn gut can absorb intact antibodies for about the first day of life, and calves that miss it are at high risk of infection, which is why colostrum management is a core part of dairy farming. Human babies receive most of their IgG across the placenta, so human colostrum mainly provides IgA that acts in the gut. Folk use of colostrum dishes exists in several farming cultures. In the twentieth century researchers explored whether antibodies from cows could protect people's guts, leading to hyperimmune bovine colostrum, made by immunizing pregnant cows against specific pathogens such as enterotoxigenic E. coli, Cryptosporidium or rotavirus. Some of these products showed promise in small trials, notably for preventing travelers' diarrhea. In parallel, ordinary bovine colostrum became a general 'immune' and sports supplement in the 1990s and 2000s, promoted for recovery and gut barrier support. Because colostrum contains insulin-like growth factor-1, anti-doping authorities have advised athletes against it, although it is not formally prohibited.", "timeline": [["Early 1900s", "Veterinary research establishes that calves depend on colostrum antibodies for early immunity."], ["1980s to 1990s", "Hyperimmune bovine colostrum products are developed against specific gut pathogens."], ["1990s to 2000s", "Bovine colostrum is marketed as an immune and sports supplement."], ["2000s", "Anti-doping authorities advise athletes against colostrum because of growth factor content."]], "cell": "Bovine colostrum is far more concentrated than mature milk in immunoglobulins, especially IgG1, along with IgA and IgM, lactoferrin, lysozyme, lactoperoxidase, oligosaccharides, cytokines and growth factors including IGF-1, IGF-2, TGF-beta and epidermal growth factor. In adults, the stomach and pancreatic enzymes digest much of this protein, but a meaningful fraction of IgG survives to the small intestine and even the colon, where it can bind bacteria, viruses and toxins and block their attachment to the gut lining, a form of passive local immunity. Hyperimmune products concentrate antibodies against a chosen target, such as colonization factors of enterotoxigenic E. coli. Lactoferrin binds iron, depriving bacteria of an essential nutrient, and its digestion releases antimicrobial peptides such as lactoferricin. Growth factors have been shown in animal and cell models to support repair of the intestinal lining and to reduce NSAID-induced gut permeability in small human studies. Whether growth factors are absorbed in amounts that matter systemically is uncertain; some studies reported small rises in IGF-1, which is the basis of anti-doping caution.", "body": [["Gut lumen", "IgG and lactoferrin may bind pathogens and toxins locally."], ["Intestinal lining", "Proposed support of barrier and repair through growth factors; evidence limited."], ["Immune system", "Allergic reactions in milk-allergic people."], ["Gut", "Bloating, gas or diarrhea, especially with lactose intolerance."], ["Endocrine system", "IGF-1 content raises questions in sport; systemic effects uncertain."]], "journey": "Colostrum proteins are swallowed and exposed to stomach acid and pepsin, then pancreatic proteases. Immunoglobulins are relatively resistant to digestion, especially when buffered by other milk proteins, so a portion of IgG passes intact through the small intestine, and some is recovered in stool. In adults, intact antibodies are not meaningfully absorbed into the blood, unlike in newborn calves whose gut is temporarily permeable. Most colostrum protein is digested into peptides and amino acids that are absorbed and used like any dietary protein, with nitrogen eventually excreted by the kidneys as urea. Lactose is digested by lactase or, in lactose-intolerant people, fermented by colon bacteria, causing gas and diarrhea.", "sidefx": "Colostrum is generally well tolerated in people who tolerate dairy. Reported side effects include nausea, bloating, gas and loose stools, often related to lactose. The main safety concern is allergy in people with cow's milk protein allergy, ranging from hives to rare anaphylaxis. Products differ widely in content and processing; unpasteurized or poorly processed products could carry microbial risks for immunocompromised people. Athletes face a regulatory issue rather than a medical one, since anti-doping authorities caution against colostrum because of growth factors and because supplements can be contaminated.", "fun": "A newborn calf can absorb whole antibodies from colostrum through its gut for only about its first day of life, after which the gut lining 'closes' and the same antibodies are mostly digested.", "src": ["World Anti-Doping Agency: Q and A on colostrum", "Natural Medicines-style monograph: Bovine Colostrum", "Otto W et al. 2011, Scandinavian Journal of Gastroenterology, hyperimmune bovine colostrum for prevention of travelers' diarrhea", "Playford RJ et al. 2001, Clinical Science, colostrum and NSAID-induced gut permeability"]}, "digestive-enzymes": {"origin": "The idea of supplementing digestion with enzymes goes back to the nineteenth century, when pepsin from animal stomachs and pancreatin from pig pancreas were sold as remedies for indigestion, and papain from papaya was used as a meat tenderizer and digestive aid. The modern divide between prescription and supplement enzymes came from the treatment of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency, especially in cystic fibrosis. Pancreatic enzyme products were sold for decades without FDA approval, and their enzyme content varied widely. In the 1990s, very high doses of high-strength pancreatic enzymes in children with cystic fibrosis were linked to fibrosing colonopathy, a scarring of the colon. FDA later required all pancreatic enzyme drugs for pancreatic insufficiency to go through formal approval, with a 2010 deadline, creating the prescription pancrelipase products used today. Meanwhile, microbial enzymes produced by fermentation became widely available over the counter: lactase for lactose intolerance and alpha-galactosidase, introduced as a consumer product in 1990 to reduce gas from beans. Broad-spectrum enzyme blends for general digestion grew into a large supplement category, though evidence for them is much weaker than for the targeted enzymes.", "timeline": [["1800s", "Pepsin, pancreatin and papain are sold as digestive aids."], ["1970s to 1980s", "Lactase products for lactose intolerance become available to consumers."], ["1990", "An alpha-galactosidase product for gas from beans is introduced to consumers."], ["1990s", "Fibrosing colonopathy is linked to high-dose pancreatic enzymes in children with cystic fibrosis."], ["2010", "FDA deadline for pancreatic enzyme drugs for pancreatic insufficiency to be approved prescription products."]], "cell": "Each enzyme in these products targets a specific chemical bond. Lactase, a beta-galactosidase usually produced by fungi such as Aspergillus or yeasts such as Kluyveromyces, splits lactose into glucose and galactose. In people whose intestinal lactase declines after childhood, undigested lactose reaches the colon, where bacteria ferment it into gas and short-chain fatty acids and draw water in, causing bloating and diarrhea; supplemental lactase works in the stomach and small intestine to reduce this load. Alpha-galactosidase, typically from Aspergillus niger, cleaves the alpha-1,6 bonds in raffinose, stachyose and verbascose, sugars in beans and cruciferous vegetables that humans cannot digest, releasing galactose and sucrose that are absorbed instead of fermented. Pancreatin, from pig pancreas, provides lipase, protease and amylase but is largely inactivated by stomach acid unless enteric-coated, which is one reason prescription pancrelipase uses acid-resistant microspheres. Bromelain from pineapple stem and papain from papaya are cysteine proteases; bromelain has been reported to have antiplatelet and fibrinolytic activity in laboratory studies, which underlies theoretical bleeding concerns.", "body": [["Small intestine", "Site where supplemental enzymes break down target foods before colonic fermentation."], ["Colon", "Less gas and bloating when fermentable sugars are reduced."], ["Blood glucose", "Released sugars are absorbed, which may matter in diabetes."], ["Immune system", "Allergic reactions to pork, pineapple, papaya or mold-derived enzymes."], ["Blood", "Bromelain and papain have theoretical antiplatelet effects."]], "journey": "Digestive enzymes are proteins and act in the gut lumen rather than in the body. Once swallowed with food, they mix with the meal in the stomach, where acid and pepsin begin to inactivate them; fungal lactase and alpha-galactosidase tolerate acid somewhat better than pancreatic enzymes. Activity continues into the duodenum and small intestine, where the released sugars, peptides or fatty acids are absorbed as from normal digestion. The enzymes themselves are eventually digested by the body's own proteases into amino acids and absorbed as protein. A small amount of bromelain appears to be absorbed intact, which is relevant to its proposed systemic effects. Unabsorbed material leaves in stool. Because they act only during the meal, timing with the first bites of food matters.", "sidefx": "Most people have no problems or mild nausea, cramping, bloating or diarrhea. Allergic reactions can occur to the enzyme source, including pork (pancreatin), pineapple (bromelain), papaya (papain, with cross-reactivity in latex-fruit syndrome) and molds (fungal enzymes). Galactosemia is a contraindication for products that release galactose. Sugar-releasing enzymes may slightly increase glucose absorption in diabetes. Bromelain and papain carry theoretical bleeding concerns with anticoagulants. Prescription-level pancreatic enzymes at high doses have caused fibrosing colonopathy, a reason that pancreatic insufficiency is managed with approved products and medical oversight. A broader risk is delayed diagnosis: persistent bloating, diarrhea, weight loss or greasy stools can signal celiac disease, pancreatic disease or other conditions.", "fun": "The bean-gas enzyme alpha-galactosidase is made by Aspergillus niger, the same mold species used industrially to produce citric acid for soft drinks.", "src": ["NIDDK: Lactose Intolerance", "FDA prescribing information: Creon (pancrelipase)", "FDA 2010 guidance and announcements on exocrine pancreatic insufficiency drug products", "Ganiats TG et al. 1994, Journal of Family Practice, does Beano prevent gas? a double-blind crossover study"]}, "apple-cider-vinegar": {"origin": "Vinegar is one of the oldest fermented products, made by letting alcohol-forming yeasts turn fruit sugars into alcohol and then letting acetic acid bacteria turn the alcohol into acetic acid. It has been used for food preservation, cleaning and medicine across many ancient cultures, and Hippocrates is often said to have recommended vinegar for wounds and coughs. Apple cider vinegar has a long folk history in North America. Its modern fame came in the late 1950s, when a Vermont physician, D. C. Jarvis, published a popular book on folk medicine promoting apple cider vinegar and honey for many ailments, and later with the rise of internet wellness culture and products 'with the mother', the cloudy layer of bacteria and cellulose left in unfiltered vinegar. Scientific interest focused on acetic acid and blood sugar: small studies, many from Carol Johnston's group in Arizona and researchers in Sweden and Japan, found that vinegar taken with a starchy meal modestly lowered the post-meal glucose rise, probably by slowing stomach emptying. Gummies and tablets made ACV a supplement category in the 2010s, while clinicians reported injuries to the esophagus and teeth and a case of hypokalemia with extreme long-term intake.", "timeline": [["Ancient times", "Vinegar is used for food preservation and medicine in many cultures."], ["1958", "A popular folk medicine book promotes apple cider vinegar and honey in the United States."], ["1998", "A case report links very high long-term cider vinegar intake to hypokalemia and osteoporosis."], ["2000s", "Small studies report that vinegar with meals modestly blunts post-meal glucose and slows gastric emptying."], ["2010s", "ACV gummies and tablets become popular supplements."]], "cell": "Apple cider vinegar is mostly water with a few percent acetic acid, plus small amounts of other organic acids, minerals and apple polyphenols. Acetic acid is the main active ingredient. In the stomach, acid in the meal slows gastric emptying, likely through duodenal acid-sensing feedback, so carbohydrate reaches the small intestine more slowly and glucose rises less sharply. Laboratory studies suggest acetic acid also partly inhibits disaccharidases such as sucrase and maltase at the brush border. Once absorbed, acetate is taken up by cells and converted to acetyl-CoA, and in animal studies acetate activates AMP-activated protein kinase in the liver, which reduces fat synthesis and increases fat oxidation, a proposed basis for weight claims that has not been shown convincingly in people. Acetate also acts on free fatty acid receptors FFAR2 and FFAR3, which are involved in gut hormone and appetite signaling. The harmful effects come from acidity: concentrated acid denatures proteins in mucosa and dissolves enamel hydroxyapatite, which is why undiluted vinegar, held in the mouth or lodged in the esophagus as a tablet, can cause injury.", "body": [["Stomach", "Slower gastric emptying, the main proposed basis of glucose effects; a concern in gastroparesis."], ["Esophagus", "Irritation or injury from undiluted vinegar or lodged tablets."], ["Teeth", "Acid erosion of enamel with frequent exposure."], ["Blood glucose", "Modest blunting of post-meal rise in small studies."], ["Kidneys and electrolytes", "Hypokalemia reported with very large long-term intake."]], "journey": "Acetic acid is absorbed quickly from the stomach and small intestine, with blood acetate rising within minutes and peaking within about half an hour. Acetate travels to the liver and other tissues, where it is converted by acetyl-CoA synthetase into acetyl-CoA. From there it is oxidized in the Krebs cycle to carbon dioxide and water or used for fat and cholesterol synthesis. The acid load is buffered by bicarbonate, and the body handles typical food amounts without difficulty. Because acetate is cleared within hours, any metabolic effect from a single serving is short-lived. Minerals and polyphenols in the vinegar are absorbed like those in other foods.", "sidefx": "Common effects are throat irritation, heartburn, nausea and, with frequent exposure, tooth enamel erosion and sensitivity. More serious problems include esophageal injury from undiluted vinegar or tablets that stick in the esophagus, and chemical burns when vinegar is applied to skin or warts. Slowed gastric emptying can worsen gastroparesis and complicate insulin timing in type 1 diabetes. A published case described hypokalemia, high renin and low bone density in a woman who drank large amounts of cider vinegar for years, raising concern for people on diuretics or digoxin. Gummies often add sugar, and acid content of supplements varies.", "fun": "The cloudy 'mother' in unfiltered vinegar is a living mat of acetic acid bacteria and the cellulose they produce, the same kind of biofilm used to brew kombucha.", "src": ["Johnston CS, Gaas CA 2006, Medscape General Medicine, vinegar: medicinal uses and antiglycemic effect", "Hlebowicz J et al. 2007, BMC Gastroenterology, effect of apple cider vinegar on delayed gastric emptying in type 1 diabetes", "Lhotta K et al. 1998, Nephron, hypokalemia, hyperreninemia and osteoporosis with cider vinegar", "Hill LL et al. 2005, Journal of the American Dietetic Association, esophageal injury by apple cider vinegar tablets"]}, "d-mannose": {"origin": "Mannose is a natural sugar found in small amounts in some fruits such as cranberries and in the sugar chains on many human proteins. Its link to urinary infections came from microbiology. In the 1970s and 1980s researchers studying how E. coli causes bladder infections found that the bacteria carry hair-like type 1 fimbriae tipped with an adhesin, later identified as FimH, that binds mannose-containing structures on bladder cells, and that adding mannose blocked attachment in laboratory and animal models. Mannose and mannose-like molecules became candidates for preventing UTIs without antibiotics, and D-mannose supplements spread in the 2000s, often marketed alongside cranberry, whose proanthocyanidins block a different adhesin. A 2014 trial from Croatia reported fewer recurrences with D-mannose, and other small studies followed, but a 2022 Cochrane review judged the evidence low certainty. In 2024 a large randomized trial in UK primary care, enrolling women with recurrent UTIs, found D-mannose did not reduce clinically suspected infections compared with placebo. Researchers continue to develop more potent synthetic FimH blockers called mannosides. Separately, D-mannose is a real treatment for the rare inherited disorder MPI-CDG, a congenital disorder of glycosylation.", "timeline": [["1970s to 1980s", "Mannose is shown to block E. coli type 1 fimbriae attachment in laboratory and animal studies."], ["1990s", "The FimH adhesin is identified as the mannose-binding tip of type 1 fimbriae."], ["2014", "A trial in women with recurrent UTI reports fewer recurrences with D-mannose."], ["2022", "A Cochrane review rates evidence for D-mannose as low certainty."], ["2024", "A large primary care randomized trial finds no reduction in UTIs with D-mannose."]], "cell": "Uropathogenic E. coli, the main cause of bladder infections, use type 1 fimbriae to hold on to the bladder lining against the flow of urine. At the tip of each fimbria sits FimH, a lectin with a pocket that binds terminal mannose residues on uroplakin Ia, a glycoprotein covering the bladder's surface umbrella cells. Attachment lets bacteria invade umbrella cells and form intracellular bacterial communities, which helps explain recurrences. Free D-mannose in urine can occupy the FimH pocket, competitively blocking binding so that bacteria are more likely to be washed out. The interaction is relatively weak, which is one reason high urine concentrations are needed and why more potent synthetic mannosides are under development. D-mannose does not kill bacteria and does not affect E. coli strains that rely on other adhesins, such as P fimbriae, which are associated with kidney infections. In human cells, mannose is phosphorylated by hexokinase to mannose-6-phosphate and then converted by phosphomannose isomerase to fructose-6-phosphate or used to build glycoproteins; in MPI-CDG, a deficiency of this enzyme, dietary mannose bypasses the block.", "body": [["Bladder", "Proposed blocking of E. coli attachment; clinical benefit uncertain."], ["Kidneys", "Excrete most D-mannose unchanged into urine."], ["Gut", "Loose stools and bloating, especially with larger amounts."], ["Blood glucose", "Small portion metabolized; minor effect on glucose expected."], ["Liver", "Converts a small portion via phosphomannose isomerase to fructose-6-phosphate."]], "journey": "D-mannose is absorbed from the small intestine, partly through glucose transporters and partly by diffusion, and blood levels rise within about an hour. Unlike glucose, very little is taken up and metabolized by tissues, so most of it is filtered by the kidneys and not reabsorbed, appearing unchanged in urine within an hour or two and staying for several hours. This is what makes urinary anti-adhesion plausible. The fraction that is metabolized is phosphorylated and converted to fructose-6-phosphate in glycolysis or used in glycosylation. Unabsorbed mannose that reaches the colon is fermented by bacteria, contributing to loose stools.", "sidefx": "D-mannose is generally well tolerated. The most common effects are loose stools, bloating and diarrhea. Because it is a sugar, a small effect on blood glucose is possible in people with diabetes, though most is excreted unchanged. Safety in pregnancy, breastfeeding, children and kidney disease is not established. The most important risk is indirect: relying on D-mannose during an active infection can delay treatment of a bladder infection that progresses to kidney infection or urosepsis, especially in older adults, who may present with confusion rather than urinary symptoms.", "fun": "D-mannose is harmless to most people but toxic to honeybees, which cannot process it fast enough and become trapped in an energy-wasting cycle, a phenomenon called mannose toxicity in bees.", "src": ["Cooper TE et al. 2022, Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews, D-mannose for preventing and treating urinary tract infections", "Hayward G et al. 2024, JAMA Internal Medicine, D-mannose for prevention of recurrent urinary tract infection among women", "Kranjcec B, Papes D, Altarac S 2014, World Journal of Urology, D-mannose powder for prophylaxis of recurrent UTIs", "Mulvey MA 2002, Cellular Microbiology, adhesion and entry of uropathogenic E. coli"]}, "glutathione": {"origin": "Glutathione was first described in the late nineteenth century as a sulfur-containing substance in yeast, and in the 1920s Frederick Gowland Hopkins at Cambridge studied it extensively and named it, though its exact structure as a tripeptide of glutamate, cysteine and glycine was confirmed later, around 1929 to 1935. It turned out to be the most abundant small thiol in animal cells and central to defense against oxidative stress and to the detoxification of drugs and chemicals. Its clinical importance became obvious with acetaminophen poisoning: in the 1970s researchers showed that liver injury occurs when glutathione is depleted and the toxic metabolite NAPQI is no longer neutralized, which led to acetylcysteine, a cysteine source for glutathione synthesis, as the antidote. Glutathione itself was later sold as an oral supplement and, in some countries, used in injectable form. In Asia and later elsewhere, IV glutathione became popular for skin lightening, and regulators including the Philippines FDA warned against it. In the United States, compounded injectable glutathione is used in wellness and IV hydration clinics. In 2019 FDA warned compounders after adverse events, including reactions linked to high endotoxin levels, were associated with glutathione from one supplier, and cautioned against using supplement-grade ingredient for sterile injectables.", "timeline": [["1888", "A sulfur-containing substance later recognized as glutathione is described in yeast."], ["1920s", "Frederick Gowland Hopkins studies and names glutathione; its tripeptide structure is confirmed by the mid-1930s."], ["1970s", "Acetaminophen liver injury is linked to glutathione depletion, leading to acetylcysteine as antidote."], ["2000s to 2010s", "IV glutathione becomes popular for skin lightening; regulators in several countries issue warnings."], ["2019", "FDA warns compounders about adverse events with injectable glutathione from a specific bulk supplier."]], "cell": "Glutathione is gamma-glutamyl-cysteinyl-glycine, a tripeptide with an unusual gamma peptide bond that protects it from ordinary peptidases inside cells. It is made in two ATP-dependent steps: glutamate-cysteine ligase joins glutamate and cysteine, a step limited by cysteine supply, and glutathione synthetase adds glycine. Cells keep most glutathione in its reduced form, GSH, and the ratio of GSH to oxidized glutathione (GSSG) is a key marker of cellular redox state. Glutathione peroxidases use GSH to reduce hydrogen peroxide and lipid hydroperoxides, generating GSSG, which glutathione reductase recycles using NADPH from the pentose phosphate pathway. Glutathione S-transferases attach glutathione to electrophilic toxins and drug metabolites, such as NAPQI from acetaminophen, forming conjugates that are processed into mercapturic acids for urinary excretion. Glutathione also regulates proteins by glutathionylation of cysteine residues. In melanocytes, thiols such as glutathione divert melanin synthesis toward lighter pheomelanin and may inhibit tyrosinase, the proposed basis for skin-lightening use, which is not well supported by quality trials.", "body": [["Liver", "Highest glutathione content; conjugates and detoxifies drug metabolites such as NAPQI."], ["Lungs", "Glutathione in airway lining fluid; inhaled forms can trigger bronchospasm in asthma."], ["Skin", "Proposed lightening through effects on melanin synthesis; evidence limited."], ["Red blood cells", "Glutathione protects hemoglobin from oxidation; deficiency of the recycling pathway (G6PD) causes hemolysis."], ["Immune system", "Infusion and allergic reactions possible with injectable products."]], "journey": "Oral glutathione is mostly broken down in the gut by gamma-glutamyl transpeptidase and peptidases into glutamate, cysteine and glycine, which are absorbed and used by cells to make their own glutathione, so blood levels of intact glutathione rise little after a single oral dose. Some studies of long-term oral or liposomal products reported increases in body glutathione stores, but results vary. IV glutathione produces high blood levels immediately, but plasma glutathione is cleared within minutes, mostly by gamma-glutamyl transpeptidase on kidney and other cell surfaces, which breaks it down into amino acids that cells take up and resynthesize. Cells cannot import intact glutathione efficiently, so their internal levels depend on synthesis. Glutathione conjugates formed in the liver are processed into mercapturic acids and excreted in urine or bile.", "sidefx": "Oral glutathione is generally well tolerated, with occasional bloating, cramps or loose stools; long-term use has been associated with lower zinc levels in some reports. Injectable glutathione carries the risks of any IV product from compounding or unregulated sources: infusion reactions, allergic reactions, fever and chills from endotoxin contamination, infection and IV site problems. FDA warnings in 2019 described such adverse events. Skin-lightening clinics in some countries have been associated with serious reactions, and regulators there have warned against the practice. Nebulized glutathione has caused bronchospasm, especially in people with asthma. There is theoretical concern that antioxidants could blunt chemotherapy that relies on oxidative damage.", "fun": "Acetaminophen overdose is treated not with glutathione itself but with acetylcysteine, because cells cannot import intact glutathione well and instead need cysteine to build their own.", "src": ["FDA 2019: FDA warns compounders not to use glutathione from Letco Medical to compound sterile drugs", "FDA: FDA highlights concerns with using dietary ingredient glutathione to compound sterile injectables", "Witschi A et al. 1992, European Journal of Clinical Pharmacology, systemic availability of oral glutathione", "Richie JP Jr et al. 2015, European Journal of Nutrition, randomized trial of oral glutathione supplementation on body stores", "Sonthalia S et al. 2016, Journal of Cutaneous and Aesthetic Surgery, glutathione as a skin whitening agent: facts, myths and evidence"]}, "pqq": {"origin": "PQQ was discovered through bacterial biochemistry. In the 1960s, researchers studying how certain bacteria grow on methanol found that their methanol dehydrogenase contained an unusual non-protein cofactor. Around 1979 to 1980, Stephen Salisbury and colleagues in the United Kingdom, with independent work by J. A. Duine's group in the Netherlands, determined its structure as pyrroloquinoline quinone, a new class of enzyme cofactor that joined NAD and flavins as electron carriers. Enzymes using it became known as quinoproteins. Researchers then detected trace amounts of PQQ in many foods and in human milk, and animal studies by Robert Rucker's group at the University of California, Davis reported that mice fed diets lacking PQQ grew poorly and had reproductive and immune problems, suggesting a nutritional role. In 2003 a paper in Nature proposed that PQQ was a vitamin, based on a putative mammalian enzyme, but other researchers disputed the enzyme assignment, and the vitamin claim is not accepted. Work in the 2010s showing that PQQ increased mitochondrial biogenesis in mice helped launch PQQ as a commercial supplement, and EFSA evaluated PQQ disodium salt as a novel food in 2017.", "timeline": [["1964", "An unusual cofactor is reported in bacterial methanol dehydrogenase."], ["1979", "The structure of pyrroloquinoline quinone is determined."], ["1990s", "Rodent studies suggest PQQ-deficient diets impair growth and reproduction."], ["2003", "A Nature paper proposes PQQ as a vitamin; the claim is later disputed."], ["2010", "PQQ is reported to stimulate mitochondrial biogenesis in mouse and cell studies."], ["2017", "EFSA evaluates PQQ disodium salt as a novel food."]], "cell": "PQQ is a tricyclic ortho-quinone that can cycle between oxidized and reduced forms many thousands of times, making it a very efficient redox catalyst in laboratory systems. In bacteria, it is tightly bound to quinoprotein dehydrogenases, accepting electrons from substrates such as methanol or glucose and passing them into the electron transport chain. In mammals, no enzyme that requires PQQ has been confirmed. Research has focused instead on signaling: in mouse liver cells and in mice, PQQ increased phosphorylation of CREB and expression of PGC-1alpha, the master regulator of mitochondrial biogenesis, leading to more mitochondria and higher respiratory capacity. PQQ also scavenges reactive oxygen species and can modulate the redox modulatory site of NMDA receptors, which has been linked to neuroprotection in animal models of stroke. It interacts with other redox molecules and can generate superoxide under some conditions. How much of this applies at the blood levels reached in people taking supplements is unknown, and human studies have relied on indirect markers.", "body": [["Mitochondria", "Increased biogenesis in animal and cell studies; not shown clearly in humans."], ["Brain", "Proposed neuroprotective and cognitive effects; small human trials with modest or inconsistent results."], ["Kidneys", "Main route of elimination; high doses caused kidney injury in early rat studies."], ["Nervous system", "Headache, drowsiness or insomnia reported."], ["Immune and metabolic systems", "Small changes in inflammatory markers reported in small studies."]], "journey": "Human pharmacokinetic data for PQQ are limited and come mostly from small studies. After oral intake, a portion of PQQ is absorbed in the small intestine, with blood levels rising and peaking within a few hours. Much of the absorbed amount appears to be excreted by the kidneys over the following day. Because PQQ is reactive, it may form adducts with amino acids and other molecules in food and in the body, complicating measurement. Distribution to tissues in humans is not well characterized; animal studies show uptake into skin and kidney, among others. No clear human metabolic pathway has been described.", "sidefx": "Reported side effects in small human studies include headache, drowsiness, fatigue, insomnia and nausea, but overall data are limited. Early rat studies at very high intakes reported kidney injury, which is why kidney disease is a commonly cited caution even though this has not been shown in humans at supplement levels. Safety in pregnancy, breastfeeding and children has not been studied. Most commercial PQQ products are stacks with CoQ10 and other ingredients, so effects and interactions often come from the combination; CoQ10, for example, may reduce warfarin effect.", "fun": "PQQ was discovered in bacteria that live on methanol, the toxic wood alcohol, where it helps them turn that poison into energy.", "src": ["Salisbury SA et al. 1979, Nature, a novel coenzyme from bacterial primary alcohol dehydrogenases", "Kasahara T, Kato T 2003, Nature, a new redox-cofactor vitamin for mammals; and later correspondence disputing the claim", "Chowanadisai W et al. 2010, Journal of Biological Chemistry, PQQ stimulates mitochondrial biogenesis through CREB phosphorylation and PGC-1alpha expression", "EFSA 2017, Scientific Opinion on the safety of pyrroloquinoline quinone sodium salt as a novel food"]}, "astaxanthin": {"origin": "Astaxanthin is the pigment behind the color of salmon flesh, shrimp shells, lobster and krill, none of which make it themselves; they accumulate it through the food chain from microalgae and zooplankton. Its structure was worked out in the late 1930s by Richard Kuhn's group in Germany, who studied it in lobster, and the name comes from the lobster genus Astacus. For most of the twentieth century astaxanthin mattered mainly to fish farming: farmed salmon fed plain feed have pale grey flesh, so synthetic astaxanthin and the related canthaxanthin became standard feed additives, and in the United States astaxanthin is listed as a color additive for salmonid fish feed. The supplement industry turned instead to the freshwater green alga Haematococcus pluvialis, which piles up astaxanthin inside thick-walled red resting cells when it is stressed by bright light or lack of nutrients. Since the 1990s algal astaxanthin has been sold as a powerful antioxidant, with marketing that often claims it is many times stronger than vitamin C or E. Those comparisons come from test-tube chemistry and do not translate into proven health benefits. Human trials have been small and short, mostly measuring skin features, eye fatigue or oxidative stress markers, with mixed results.", "timeline": [["1938", "Richard Kuhn's group characterizes astaxanthin from lobster, naming it after the genus Astacus."], ["1990s", "Synthetic astaxanthin becomes a standard color additive in farmed salmon feed, listed for this use in US regulations."], ["1990s", "Haematococcus algae-derived astaxanthin enters the dietary supplement market."], ["2010s", "Small randomized trials examine skin, eye fatigue and exercise outcomes, with mixed results."], ["2020", "The European Food Safety Authority reviews astaxanthin safety in food supplements and sets an acceptable daily intake."]], "cell": "Astaxanthin is a C40 xanthophyll with a long conjugated polyene chain capped at each end by a ring carrying a hydroxyl and a keto group. That shape matters: the polar ends anchor at the inner and outer surfaces of the membrane while the conjugated chain runs through the hydrophobic core, so the molecule can intercept singlet oxygen and lipid peroxyl radicals throughout the bilayer. Beta-carotene, which lacks polar end groups, sits inside the membrane and can disorder it, while astaxanthin appears to stabilize it. In cell and animal models, astaxanthin reduces lipid peroxidation, lowers activation of the inflammatory transcription factor NF-kB, and protects mitochondria from oxidative damage. Unlike beta-carotene, its rings are already oxidized, so it is not cleaved into retinal and is not regarded as a useful vitamin A source in humans. A practical caution applies to the antioxidant story: strong radical-quenching power in a test tube does not mean the compound reaches tissues in amounts that change disease outcomes, and that link has not been shown.", "body": [["Skin", "Small trials report changes in skin moisture and wrinkle measures; findings are inconsistent and mostly industry-funded."], ["Eyes", "Studied for eye fatigue from screen work in small trials with mixed results."], ["Blood and lipoproteins", "Carried in VLDL, LDL and HDL; some trials report lower markers of LDL oxidation."], ["Skeletal muscle", "Proposed to reduce exercise-induced oxidative stress; performance benefits are not established."], ["Gut", "Can tint stool orange-red; GI upset is the most common complaint."]], "journey": "After swallowing, astaxanthin is released from the food or oil matrix and, being fat-soluble, is packaged into bile-acid micelles in the small intestine. Absorption is modest and much better with a fat-containing meal. Enterocytes load it into chylomicrons, which enter the lymph and then the bloodstream; peak plasma levels occur several hours after a dose. As chylomicrons are processed, astaxanthin passes to the liver and is redistributed in VLDL, LDL and HDL, which carry it to tissues. Small human studies report an elimination half-life of roughly 16 to 21 hours. The liver metabolizes it to smaller oxidized fragments, and elimination is mainly through bile into the feces, with little appearing in the urine. Different geometric isomers and esterified forms in algal products are absorbed differently, which is one reason study results vary.", "sidefx": "Short human studies have not identified a consistent pattern of serious harm. The most common complaints are stomach upset and more frequent bowel movements, and stool can turn orange or red because of the pigment. Very high intakes over time have been linked to skin color changes in animals and occasionally in people, similar to the yellow-orange skin seen with heavy carrot intake. Allergic reactions can occur, and krill- or crustacean-derived products are a concern for people with shellfish allergy. Laboratory studies have reported weak effects on drug-metabolizing enzymes, but no clinically important drug interactions are established. Combination products with fish or krill oil carry the cautions of those oils. Safety in pregnancy, breastfeeding and childhood has not been established.", "fun": "Live lobsters are dark blue-green because astaxanthin is bound tightly inside a shell protein called crustacyanin, which shifts its color; cooking unfolds the protein and releases free astaxanthin, which is why lobsters and shrimp turn bright red in the pot.", "src": ["EFSA Panel on Nutrition, Novel Foods and Food Allergens 2020, Safety of astaxanthin for its use as a novel food in food supplements", "21 CFR 73.35, Astaxanthin color additive", "Ambati RR et al. 2014, Marine Drugs, Astaxanthin: sources, extraction, stability, biological activities and commercial applications", "NIH Office of Dietary Supplements, Dietary Supplement Label Database (astaxanthin products)"]}, "spermidine": {"origin": "Polyamines have one of the oldest discovery stories in biochemistry. In 1678 Antonie van Leeuwenhoek described crystals forming in human semen under his microscope; centuries later these were identified as spermine phosphate, which is where the names spermine and spermidine come from. Spermidine and spermine were chemically characterized in the 1920s, and through the twentieth century polyamines were studied mainly as growth factors: every dividing cell needs them, and tumors make them in large amounts. That observation led to eflornithine (DFMO), which blocks the first enzyme of polyamine synthesis and became an approved drug for African sleeping sickness, facial hair growth and, more recently, high-risk neuroblastoma maintenance. Spermidine's reputation flipped in 2009, when Frank Madeo, Tobias Eisenberg and colleagues reported that adding spermidine extended lifespan in yeast, worms and flies by inducing autophagy. Later mouse work reported heart protection and longer life. The supplement market followed with wheat germ extracts sold for healthy aging, yet the main randomized human trial, which tested spermidine for a year in older adults with subjective memory decline, did not find a memory benefit.", "timeline": [["1678", "Antonie van Leeuwenhoek describes crystals in semen, later identified as spermine phosphate."], ["1920s", "Spermidine and spermine are chemically characterized."], ["1978", "DFMO (eflornithine) is described as an irreversible inhibitor of ornithine decarboxylase, the first step in polyamine synthesis."], ["2009", "Eisenberg, Madeo and colleagues report that spermidine induces autophagy and extends lifespan in yeast, worms and flies."], ["2016", "Mouse studies report heart protection and longer lifespan with dietary spermidine."], ["2022", "The SmartAge randomized trial in older adults finds no memory benefit from a year of spermidine supplementation."]], "cell": "Spermidine is a flexible chain carrying three positively charged amine groups at body pH. Cells build it from ornithine: ornithine decarboxylase makes putrescine, and spermidine synthase adds an aminopropyl group donated by decarboxylated S-adenosylmethionine. Its most specific job is to donate part of its structure to eukaryotic translation initiation factor 5A, creating the unusual amino acid hypusine; hypusinated eIF5A helps ribosomes move through difficult stretches of protein sequence, and cells cannot proliferate without it. Spermidine also binds DNA, RNA and phospholipids, stabilizing them. The autophagy story centers on acetylation: spermidine inhibits the acetyltransferase EP300, which lowers acetylation of autophagy proteins and lets autophagy proceed, and hypusinated eIF5A also supports production of the transcription factor TFEB, which drives lysosome and autophagy genes. Autophagy clears damaged proteins and mitochondria, and in model organisms this is linked to longer life. Polyamine levels in tissues fall with age, which is the basis of the replacement idea, though whether oral supplements meaningfully restore tissue levels in people is unclear.", "body": [["Heart", "Mouse studies report less cardiac stiffening and better function with dietary spermidine; human evidence is observational."], ["Brain", "Fly and mouse studies suggest memory effects; a year-long human trial found no memory benefit."], ["Gut", "Absorbed from food and made by gut bacteria; wheat germ products may carry gluten."], ["Hair follicles", "Small studies explore hair growth claims; evidence is weak."], ["Tumors", "Rapidly dividing cells depend on polyamines, which is the basis for polyamine-blocking cancer drugs and a theoretical concern with supplements."]], "journey": "Spermidine from food and supplements is absorbed in the small intestine through polyamine transport systems, and gut bacteria add to the body's supply. Human studies disagree about how much oral supplements raise blood spermidine, which may be because cells tightly regulate their polyamine pool: when levels rise, cells make antizyme, a protein that shuts down polyamine synthesis and uptake. Inside cells, excess spermidine is acetylated by spermidine/spermine N1-acetyltransferase, the rate-limiting step in polyamine breakdown, and the acetylated product is either exported or oxidized by polyamine oxidase back to putrescine. Acetylated polyamines are excreted in the urine. Half-life, peak timing and protein binding of supplemental spermidine are not well defined in humans.", "sidefx": "Clinical trials of wheat germ-derived spermidine have reported good tolerance, with mild GI symptoms the most common complaint. The main practical issue is the source: many products are wheat germ extracts, and gluten content varies, which matters for people with celiac disease or wheat allergy. Because rapidly dividing cells, including cancer cells, rely on polyamines, there is a theoretical concern about raising polyamine intake in active cancer; human evidence either way is limited. Safety in pregnancy, breastfeeding and childhood has not been established. No important drug interactions are documented, though supplementation runs opposite to the purpose of drugs like eflornithine.", "fun": "Spermidine is one of the few nutrients whose most specific job is to become part of a single protein: it is used to build hypusine, an amino acid found almost nowhere except in translation factor eIF5A.", "src": ["Madeo F, Eisenberg T, Pietrocola F, Kroemer G 2018, Science, Spermidine in health and disease", "Eisenberg T et al. 2009, Nature Cell Biology, Induction of autophagy by spermidine promotes longevity", "Eisenberg T et al. 2016, Nature Medicine, Cardioprotection and lifespan extension by the natural polyamine spermidine", "Schwarz C et al. 2022, JAMA Network Open, Effects of spermidine supplementation on cognition and biomarkers in older adults with subjective cognitive decline"]}, "msm": {"origin": "Methylsulfonylmethane is a small molecule, two methyl groups attached to a sulfonyl group, also called dimethyl sulfone. It occurs naturally in trace amounts in some foods, in milk and coffee, and in the atmosphere as part of the marine sulfur cycle, where ocean plankton release dimethyl sulfide that oxidizes in the air. Its supplement story grows out of dimethyl sulfoxide, DMSO. In the 1960s, Stanley Jacob, a surgeon at the University of Oregon, and chemist Robert Herschler promoted DMSO as a pain-relieving and anti-inflammatory solvent that passes easily through skin. DMSO generated great excitement, then a regulatory backlash after eye lens changes appeared in animal studies, and today its only FDA-approved human use is a bladder instillation for interstitial cystitis. MSM is the main oxidized metabolite of DMSO, but without DMSO's garlic-like breath odor, and in the 1980s Jacob and Herschler patented and promoted it as a nutritional sulfur source. The idea that people are short of dietary sulfur and that MSM fills the gap for cartilage has never been well supported, since most dietary sulfur comes from the amino acids methionine and cysteine. MSM later received an FDA GRAS notice for certain food uses and became a staple of joint formulas.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Stanley Jacob and Robert Herschler promote DMSO as a skin-penetrating anti-inflammatory solvent."], ["1978", "FDA approves DMSO as a bladder instillation for interstitial cystitis."], ["1980s", "MSM, the oxidized metabolite of DMSO, is patented and marketed as a nutritional supplement."], ["2006", "A small pilot randomized trial reports modest knee osteoarthritis pain improvement with MSM."], ["2000s", "MSM becomes a common ingredient in multi-ingredient joint supplements."]], "cell": "MSM is chemically quite inert: the sulfur atom is already fully oxidized, so it does not readily donate sulfur to the reactions that build cartilage sulfates, which rely mainly on sulfate derived from methionine and cysteine. Laboratory studies instead describe effects on inflammatory signaling. In cultured cells, MSM reduces activation of NF-kB, the transcription factor that switches on inflammatory genes, and lowers production of cytokines such as interleukin-6 and tumor necrosis factor alpha, as well as nitric oxide and prostaglandin-producing enzymes. Some studies report increased cellular glutathione and lower markers of oxidative stress after exercise. MSM is a small, polar, water-soluble molecule that distributes broadly through body water and crosses the blood-brain barrier. How any of these laboratory effects might relate to joint pain in people is not established, and the human trials are too small and short to answer that question.", "body": [["Joints", "Small trials report modest improvement in osteoarthritis pain scores; no proven effect on cartilage structure."], ["Skeletal muscle", "Small exercise studies report changes in soreness and oxidative stress markers, with mixed results."], ["Brain", "Crosses the blood-brain barrier; detectable on MR spectroscopy in supplement users, with no known harm."], ["Gut", "Nausea, bloating and diarrhea are the most common complaints."], ["Kidneys", "Main route of elimination, largely as unchanged MSM."]], "journey": "MSM is absorbed readily from the gut, and because it is small and water-soluble it spreads through total body water, including the brain. Some MSM in the body is not from supplements at all: it is made by oxidation of DMSO and by gut bacteria acting on dietary sulfur compounds, and it is present at low levels in blood and urine of people who do not use supplements. It undergoes little metabolism and is excreted mainly unchanged in the urine. Detailed human pharmacokinetic data such as half-life and peak timing are limited, though small studies suggest it lingers in the body for days after regular use ends.", "sidefx": "In trials, MSM has been well tolerated. Reported side effects are mild and include nausea, bloating, diarrhea, headache, fatigue and trouble sleeping. Allergic reactions are possible. No serious toxicity syndrome or established drug interaction has been identified for MSM alone. The more important safety point is what it is mixed with: joint formulas often combine MSM with glucosamine and chondroitin, which have been linked to raised INR in people taking warfarin, and sometimes with turmeric or other herbs that carry their own cautions. Safety in pregnancy, breastfeeding and childhood has not been established.", "fun": "MSM is small enough to show up as its own peak on brain magnetic resonance spectroscopy; radiologists have reported finding it in the brains of healthy people who were using MSM supplements, a surprise that sent them looking through the patients' supplement lists.", "src": ["Butawan M, Benjamin RL, Bloomer RJ 2017, Nutrients, Methylsulfonylmethane: applications and safety of a novel dietary supplement", "Kim LS et al. 2006, Osteoarthritis and Cartilage, Efficacy of methylsulfonylmethane in osteoarthritis pain of the knee", "Lin A et al. 2001, Magnetic Resonance in Medicine, Methylsulfonylmethane detected in human brain by proton MR spectroscopy", "Rimso-50 (dimethyl sulfoxide) FDA label"]}, "dim": {"origin": "The story of DIM begins with the long-standing observation that people who eat more cruciferous vegetables, such as broccoli, cabbage, kale and Brussels sprouts, have somewhat lower rates of certain cancers in population studies. These vegetables store sulfur-containing glucosinolates, which are kept apart from the enzyme myrosinase until the plant is chopped or chewed. When glucobrassicin meets myrosinase, it releases indole-3-carbinol, and in the acidic stomach indole-3-carbinol condenses into a family of products of which 3,3'-diindolylmethane is the main one. Laboratory work from the 1970s onward showed that cruciferous indoles block chemically induced tumors in rodents and switch on liver enzymes that process carcinogens and estrogens. In the 1990s, studies focusing on estrogen metabolism found that indole-3-carbinol increased the ratio of 2-hydroxyestrone to 16-alpha-hydroxyestrone, a pattern proposed to be less growth-promoting for breast tissue, and indole-3-carbinol and DIM entered the supplement market as estrogen balance products. Small trials explored cervical dysplasia, recurrent respiratory papillomatosis and breast cancer biomarkers with mixed results. A randomized trial in women taking tamoxifen added a practical concern by finding lower levels of tamoxifen's active metabolites with DIM.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Rodent studies show cruciferous indoles block chemically induced tumors and induce liver enzymes."], ["1990s", "Indole-3-carbinol is shown to shift estrogen metabolism toward 2-hydroxylation in humans."], ["2000s", "Small trials of indole-3-carbinol and DIM examine cervical dysplasia and respiratory papillomatosis with mixed results."], ["2000s", "DIM becomes a popular hormone-balance supplement, often in specially formulated absorbable forms."], ["2017", "A randomized trial in women taking tamoxifen reports lower tamoxifen active metabolite levels with DIM."]], "cell": "DIM's best-characterized action is on the aryl hydrocarbon receptor, a cytoplasmic transcription factor that senses planar aromatic molecules. Once activated, AhR moves to the nucleus and switches on genes including CYP1A1, CYP1A2 and CYP1B1. CYP1A enzymes hydroxylate estradiol and estrone at the 2-position, while other pathways produce 16-alpha-hydroxyestrone, a metabolite that still activates the estrogen receptor. Shifting the balance toward 2-hydroxylation is the biochemical basis of hormone-balance marketing, although it has not been shown to prevent disease. Induction of CYP1A2 also explains potential drug interactions, since that enzyme metabolizes theophylline, clozapine, caffeine and tizanidine. Laboratory studies further report that DIM acts as an androgen receptor antagonist, affects growth signaling pathways such as Akt and NF-kB, and promotes death of cancer cells in culture at concentrations higher than those usually reached in people. The mechanism of the tamoxifen interaction is not settled; tamoxifen depends on conversion by CYP2D6 and CYP3A4 to active metabolites such as endoxifen, and DIM appears to lower those levels.", "body": [["Liver", "Induction of CYP1A1 and CYP1A2 changes estrogen and drug metabolism."], ["Breast", "Proposed to favor less estrogenic metabolites; lowered tamoxifen active metabolites in a small trial, which is a clinical concern."], ["Reproductive tract", "Studied for cervical dysplasia with mixed results; menstrual changes are reported."], ["Prostate", "Antiandrogen effects in laboratory studies; no proven clinical benefit."], ["Kidneys and urine", "Metabolites are excreted, and users sometimes notice darker urine."]], "journey": "Pure crystalline DIM is poorly absorbed because it is highly fat-soluble and does not dissolve well, so many products use formulations designed to improve uptake. In small human studies, blood levels peak a few hours after a dose. Indole-3-carbinol supplements largely turn into DIM and other condensation products in the stomach, so very little indole-3-carbinol itself reaches the blood. DIM is metabolized in the liver to hydroxylated forms that are conjugated with glucuronide and sulfate, and metabolites are excreted in urine and feces. Half-life and protein binding have not been well defined in people.", "sidefx": "In small trials, DIM has caused headache, nausea, diarrhea and darker urine. Rash and menstrual changes have been reported. Higher intakes have been linked to more GI upset and dizziness. The most important safety issues come from hormonal and enzyme effects: a reduction in active tamoxifen metabolites in women with breast cancer, theoretical interference with hormonal contraception and hormone replacement, and possible lowering of drugs cleared by CYP1A2. Because DIM alters estrogen metabolism, supplement use in pregnancy and breastfeeding is generally avoided in references, and safety has not been established. Eating cruciferous vegetables provides far smaller amounts and is not linked to these concerns.", "fun": "Broccoli keeps its indole precursors and the enzyme that releases them in separate compartments, a defense system sometimes called the mustard oil bomb; chewing breaks the compartments open, and the stomach then finishes the chemistry that turns indole-3-carbinol into DIM.", "src": ["Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center About Herbs: Diindolylmethane and Indole-3-carbinol", "Thomson CA et al. 2017, Breast Cancer Research and Treatment, Diindolylmethane and tamoxifen metabolites", "Linus Pauling Institute Micronutrient Information Center: Indole-3-Carbinol", "Higdon JV et al. 2007, Pharmacological Research, Cruciferous vegetables and human cancer risk"]}, "calcium-d-glucarate": {"origin": "Glucaric acid, historically called saccharic acid, has been known to chemists since the nineteenth century as the product of oxidizing glucose with nitric acid. It turned out to be a normal human metabolite too: the body forms small amounts from glucuronic acid, and doctors once measured urinary D-glucaric acid as a rough marker of liver enzyme induction, since drugs such as phenobarbital raise its excretion. Glucaric acid also occurs naturally in many fruits and vegetables, including oranges, apples, grapefruit and cruciferous vegetables. Its supplement history comes from cancer research in the 1980s and 1990s, when laboratory groups in the United States studied glucaric acid salts in rodents exposed to chemical carcinogens and reported fewer mammary and other tumors. The proposed explanation centered on beta-glucuronidase, an enzyme that undoes the body's glucuronide conjugation, and the idea that blocking it would keep estrogens and carcinogens on their way out in the stool. Calcium D-glucarate, a stable salt, was marketed as a detox supplement and later became a fixture of estrogen-balance stacks alongside DIM. Human evidence has remained limited to small biomarker studies, and no clinical outcome trials show benefit.", "timeline": [["1800s", "Glucaric (saccharic) acid is produced by nitric acid oxidation of glucose and studied by sugar chemists."], ["1960s-1970s", "Urinary D-glucaric acid is used in research as a marker of liver enzyme induction by drugs."], ["1980s-1990s", "Rodent studies report reduced chemically induced tumors with glucarate salts."], ["2000s", "Calcium D-glucarate is sold as a detox and estrogen-balance supplement, often with DIM."], ["2004", "A US Department of Energy report lists glucaric acid among top value-added chemicals from biomass."]], "cell": "The body attaches glucuronic acid to many fat-soluble compounds in the liver, a process called glucuronidation, which makes them water-soluble so they can be excreted in bile and urine. Estrogens, bilirubin, many drugs and some carcinogens are handled this way. Once a glucuronide reaches the intestine in bile, bacteria that make beta-glucuronidase can cut the glucuronic acid off again, freeing the original compound to be reabsorbed, a loop called enterohepatic recirculation. D-glucaric acid from calcium D-glucarate is converted in stomach acid to D-glucaro-1,4-lactone, a direct inhibitor of beta-glucuronidase. In theory, less enzyme activity means more of the conjugated compounds stay conjugated and leave in the stool, lowering body exposure to estrogens and toxins. Animal studies support this chain of events, but whether supplement amounts change hormone levels or disease risk in people is not established. The same mechanism, if it works, could also slightly reduce recirculation of drugs such as estrogen components of hormonal contraceptives, which is why the interaction is described as theoretical.", "body": [["Intestine", "Inhibition of bacterial beta-glucuronidase is the proposed main site of action."], ["Liver", "Glucuronidation in the liver produces the conjugates that the supplement aims to keep conjugated."], ["Breast", "Rodent studies report fewer chemically induced mammary tumors; no human outcome data."], ["Blood lipids", "Small animal studies suggest cholesterol lowering; human evidence is lacking."], ["Kidneys", "Glucaric acid is excreted in urine."]], "journey": "After swallowing, calcium D-glucarate dissociates into calcium and D-glucaric acid. In the acidic stomach, glucaric acid partly converts into D-glucaro-1,4-lactone and other lactones, which exist in balance with the open acid form. Some of these forms act within the gut lumen, where much beta-glucuronidase activity is located, and some are absorbed and reach tissues and blood. Glucaric acid is not significantly metabolized further and is excreted in the urine. The body also produces its own glucaric acid as an end product of the glucuronic acid pathway. Detailed human pharmacokinetic values have not been established.", "sidefx": "Calcium D-glucarate appears to be well tolerated, and serious adverse effects have not been described; mild stomach upset is the main complaint. The calcium it contains counts toward total calcium intake and may bind some oral drugs in the gut, such as tetracyclines, fluoroquinolones and levothyroxine, if used at the same time. A theoretical concern exists that reduced recirculation could lower the effect of drugs or hormones that depend on it, including estrogens in hormonal contraceptives, but this has not been shown in people. Because it is often combined with DIM or herbs, the overall product may carry larger risks than glucarate itself. Safety in pregnancy, breastfeeding and childhood has not been established.", "fun": "Glucaric acid has a second career outside medicine: it was named in a 2004 US Department of Energy report as one of the top value-added chemicals that could be made from plant biomass, and it is being developed as a biodegradable ingredient for detergents and nylon-like polymers.", "src": ["Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center About Herbs: Calcium Glucarate", "Walaszek Z 1990, Cancer Letters, Potential use of D-glucaric acid derivatives in cancer prevention", "US Department of Energy 2004, Top Value Added Chemicals from Biomass, Volume I", "Linus Pauling Institute Micronutrient Information Center: Glucarate"]}, "chlorella": {"origin": "Chlorella was described in 1890 by the Dutch microbiologist Martinus Beijerinck, who named it for its green color and small size. Its fame came during and after the Second World War, when scientists worried about feeding a growing world population looked at fast-growing microalgae as a protein crop. Chlorella doubles quickly under light, is roughly half protein by dry weight, and can be grown in tanks on land, so research groups in the United States, Germany and Japan built pilot cultivation systems in the late 1940s and 1950s, and the Carnegie Institution of Washington published a major report on algal culture in 1953. Algae never became an everyday staple because production was expensive and the tough cell wall made the cells hard to digest, but Japan developed a commercial chlorella health food industry in the 1960s, and Taiwan followed. Manufacturers learned to crack the cell wall mechanically to improve digestibility. In the 1970s in Japan, some chlorella products high in pheophorbide, a chlorophyll breakdown product formed during poor processing, caused photosensitive skin reactions, which led to limits on pheophorbide content. Chlorella later became a Western superfood and detox product, with claims about heavy metal removal that remain unproven.", "timeline": [["1890", "Martinus Beijerinck describes Chlorella vulgaris."], ["1953", "The Carnegie Institution of Washington publishes a major report on mass culture of algae as a food source."], ["1960s", "Commercial chlorella health food production grows in Japan and Taiwan."], ["1970s", "Photosensitive dermatitis linked to pheophorbide-rich chlorella products is reported in Japan, leading to limits on pheophorbide content."], ["2000s", "Chlorella is marketed in Western countries as a superfood and detox supplement."]], "cell": "Chlorella is a whole organism rather than a single compound, so its effects come from a mixture. Each cell contains chloroplasts packed with chlorophyll and carotenoids, protein-rich cytoplasm, and a rigid cell wall built partly from sporopollenin-like and polysaccharide materials. The vitamin K1 in chloroplasts is the most clinically important component: vitamin K is the cofactor for gamma-glutamyl carboxylase, which activates clotting factors II, VII, IX and X in the liver, and warfarin works by blocking the recycling of vitamin K. Extra vitamin K therefore lets the liver make active clotting factors despite warfarin, lowering the INR. The cell wall binds metals such as cadmium and lead in laboratory studies, which is the basis of detox marketing, but binding in a test tube does not show removal of metals from human tissues. Polysaccharide and glycoprotein fractions activate macrophages and other immune cells in animal studies. Pheophorbide, formed when chlorophyll breaks down, is a photosensitizer: it absorbs light in the skin and generates reactive oxygen species that injure skin cells.", "body": [["Blood clotting", "Vitamin K content can lower the INR in people on warfarin."], ["Skin", "Pheophorbide-rich products cause photosensitive dermatitis on sun-exposed skin."], ["Gut", "Nausea, gas, cramping and green stool are common; cracked cell wall products are easier to digest."], ["Immune system", "Immune stimulation in animal studies; theoretical concern with immunosuppressive therapy."], ["Airways", "Allergy and asthma have been reported, including in people handling chlorella powder."]], "journey": "Whole chlorella cells are partly digested in the gut. Breaking the cell wall during manufacturing makes the protein, pigments and vitamins inside more available. Absorbed components behave like the same nutrients from food: vitamin K1 is fat-soluble and absorbed with dietary fat into chylomicrons, then taken up by the liver; B vitamins, iron and amino acids follow their usual pathways. Undigested cell wall material passes out in the stool, often coloring it green. Because the product is a mixture, there is no single half-life. For warfarin users, the practical point is that a steady intake has a steady effect on INR, while starting, stopping or changing products causes shifts.", "sidefx": "Most side effects are digestive: nausea, diarrhea, gas, cramping and green stool, especially in the first weeks. Allergic reactions occur, including asthma and anaphylaxis in sensitized people. Photosensitive dermatitis has been linked to products with high pheophorbide content. The most important interaction is with warfarin, where vitamin K can reduce anticoagulation and raise clot risk; stopping chlorella abruptly can do the opposite and raise the INR. Because algae are grown in open or closed systems, products can be contaminated with heavy metals or other organisms depending on quality control. Some products contain iodine, which matters in thyroid disease. Immune stimulation is a theoretical concern for people on immunosuppressants. Safety in pregnancy and breastfeeding is not established.", "fun": "Chlorella was once seriously proposed as food for long space missions and as a way to end world hunger; a few cubic meters of well-lit culture can produce a remarkable amount of protein, but it turned out that people did not much like eating it.", "src": ["Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center About Herbs: Chlorella", "Burlew JS (editor) 1953, Algal Culture from Laboratory to Pilot Plant, Carnegie Institution of Washington Publication 600", "Merchant RE, Andre CA 2001, Alternative Therapies in Health and Medicine, A review of recent clinical trials of the nutritional supplement Chlorella pyrenoidosa", "NIH Office of Dietary Supplements: Vitamin K fact sheet (warfarin interaction)"]}, "spirulina": {"origin": "Spirulina has a long history as food. Spanish accounts from the conquest of Mexico in the sixteenth century describe the Aztecs skimming a blue-green material from Lake Texcoco, drying it into cakes called tecuitlatl and eating it. Around Lake Chad in central Africa, the Kanembu people have long harvested a similar cyanobacterium from alkaline ponds and dried it into cakes called dihe, sold in local markets; European scientists drew attention to this tradition in the 1960s. Spirulina grows best in warm, highly alkaline water where few competitors survive, which makes open-pond farming possible. Commercial production began in Mexico in the 1970s, and the product spread as a health food and protein supplement. Space agencies studied it as a possible food for long missions because it grows quickly and is rich in protein. In 2013, FDA approved spirulina extract, whose blue pigment phycocyanin replaces synthetic dyes, as a color additive for candy and gum, later expanding its uses. Despite its long food history, the clinical evidence for health claims is limited, and the main safety concern is quality: harvests can be contaminated with other cyanobacteria that make liver toxins called microcystins.", "timeline": [["1500s", "Spanish chroniclers describe Aztec harvesting of tecuitlatl from Lake Texcoco."], ["1960s", "Scientists describe the traditional dihe harvest by Kanembu communities around Lake Chad."], ["1970s", "Commercial spirulina production begins in Mexico and spreads as a health food."], ["2000s", "Studies of blue-green algae supplements raise concern about microcystin contamination."], ["2013", "FDA approves spirulina extract as a color additive in candy and chewing gum."]], "cell": "Spirulina is a cyanobacterium, a photosynthetic bacterium, not a true alga, and it grows as spiral filaments. Its cells contain chlorophyll a and phycobilisomes, light-harvesting structures built from phycocyanin, the protein-bound blue pigment that gives spirulina extract its color. In laboratory studies, phycocyanin scavenges free radicals, reduces lipid peroxidation and inhibits cyclooxygenase-2, and spirulina extracts activate macrophages, natural killer cells and cytokine production in animal models, which is the basis of immune claims and of theoretical concern in autoimmune disease or with immunosuppressants. Its protein is roughly 60 percent of dry weight and contains all essential amino acids, including phenylalanine. The B12-like compounds in spirulina are mostly pseudovitamin B12, which has a different lower ligand, binds human intrinsic factor poorly and does not support human B12-dependent enzymes; it may even interfere with B12 status measurements. Contamination is a separate issue: Microcystis and related cyanobacteria produce microcystins, cyclic peptides that enter liver cells through OATP transporters and inhibit protein phosphatases 1 and 2A, causing cytoskeleton collapse and liver cell death.", "body": [["Liver", "Microcystin-contaminated algae products can cause liver injury; pure spirulina is not considered hepatotoxic."], ["Immune system", "Immune stimulation in animal studies; case reports of autoimmune flares."], ["Blood lipids and glucose", "Small trials report modest or inconsistent improvements."], ["Gut", "Nausea, bloating, diarrhea and dark green stool are common."], ["Nervous system", "Not a reliable B12 source; relying on it can allow B12 deficiency with nerve damage over time."]], "journey": "Spirulina lacks the tough cellulose cell wall of many algae, so it is digested fairly easily. Its protein is broken down into amino acids and absorbed like other dietary protein, including phenylalanine, which is why PKU is a concern. Fat-soluble carotenoids and gamma-linolenic acid are absorbed with dietary fat. Phycocyanin is a large protein that is mostly digested to peptides and to its pigment component, phycocyanobilin, which may be absorbed. Pseudovitamin B12 binds intrinsic factor poorly and is largely not absorbed in a useful form. There is no single half-life because spirulina is a mixture of nutrients that follow their normal metabolic pathways and are eliminated in urine and feces.", "sidefx": "Most side effects are mild: nausea, diarrhea, bloating, headache and dark stools. Allergic reactions occur. Case reports describe autoimmune flares, including skin and muscle autoimmune disease, after starting spirulina, which supports caution in autoimmune conditions. Rare case reports describe liver injury and rhabdomyolysis, though cause is often uncertain. The best-documented hazard is contamination: blue-green algae products harvested from natural waters can contain microcystins, and products can also carry heavy metals or bacteria. Children are more vulnerable because of their size. People with PKU need to avoid it because of phenylalanine. Relying on spirulina for B12 can lead to deficiency in vegans.", "fun": "Spirulina is so efficient at turning sunlight into protein that it was studied by space agencies as a life-support crop for long missions, and its blue pigment now colors many candies and frosted treats that are advertised as free of artificial dyes.", "src": ["NIH Office of Dietary Supplements: Vitamin B12 fact sheet", "21 CFR 73.530, Spirulina extract color additive", "Gilroy DJ et al. 2000, Environmental Health Perspectives, Assessing potential health risks from microcystin toxins in blue-green algae dietary supplements", "Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center About Herbs: Spirulina"]}, "sea-moss": {"origin": "Chondrus crispus, the original Irish moss, is a small, frilly red seaweed of rocky North Atlantic shores. Coastal communities in Ireland and Scotland have long boiled it with milk to make a set pudding, and it was used as a food during the Great Famine of the 1840s. The seaweed also traveled into Caribbean tradition, where sea moss drinks blended with milk, spices and sweeteners are sold as a tonic, particularly in Jamaica, Trinidad and other islands; much Caribbean sea moss comes from warm-water species such as Gracilaria and Eucheuma rather than Chondrus. The gelling material in these seaweeds is carrageenan, named after the Irish town of Carragheen. Industrial extraction of carrageenan expanded in the twentieth century, and it became a widely used thickener in dairy products, plant milks and processed foods, regulated as a food additive. Sea moss itself had a dramatic revival around 2020, driven by social media and celebrity endorsements, with claims that it contains almost all the minerals the human body needs. Those claims are marketing, there are almost no clinical trials of sea moss, and the variable iodine content is a real thyroid consideration.", "timeline": [["1840s", "Irish moss is eaten as a famine food during the Great Famine in Ireland."], ["20th century", "Industrial carrageenan extraction from red seaweeds expands for use as a food thickener."], ["1948", "Jan Wolff and Israel Chaikoff describe suppression of thyroid hormone synthesis by excess iodine."], ["2020", "Sea moss gel becomes a social media wellness trend."]], "cell": "The main structural material of sea moss is carrageenan, a family of large sulfated galactose polymers that form gels in the presence of potassium or calcium and milk proteins. In the gut, food-grade carrageenan is largely not digested and behaves as soluble fiber, adding bulk and water to stool. Its sulfate groups give it heparin-like anticoagulant activity in laboratory studies, which is the basis of a theoretical bleeding concern, though this has not been shown with normal food intake. The most clinically relevant component is iodine. The thyroid actively traps iodide using the sodium-iodide symporter and attaches it to thyroglobulin through thyroid peroxidase to make T4 and T3. A sudden large iodine load normally shuts down organification for a time, the Wolff-Chaikoff effect; most people escape this within days, but those with autoimmune thyroiditis, prior thyroid surgery or fetuses and newborns may not, and develop hypothyroidism. In people with nodular goiter or previous iodine deficiency, extra iodine can instead fuel unregulated hormone production, the Jod-Basedow effect, causing hyperthyroidism. Seaweeds can also concentrate arsenic, lead, cadmium and mercury from seawater.", "body": [["Thyroid", "Iodine content can cause hypothyroidism or hyperthyroidism, especially in people with thyroid disease."], ["Gut", "Carrageenan acts as soluble fiber; bloating and loose stools can occur."], ["Heart", "Hyperthyroidism from iodine excess can cause palpitations and tachycardia."], ["Blood", "Sulfated polysaccharides are anticoagulant in laboratory studies; clinical relevance is unclear."], ["Kidneys", "Mineral and possible heavy metal content may matter in kidney disease."]], "journey": "Sea moss is eaten as a food-like gel or powder. Its carrageenan is not broken down by human enzymes and passes mostly intact to the colon, where it adds bulk and is eliminated in the stool. Iodine is released as iodide and absorbed almost completely in the stomach and small intestine. Iodide distributes in the extracellular fluid and is taken up by the thyroid, salivary glands, stomach lining and breastfeeding breast tissue, which is why iodine passes into breast milk. The kidneys excrete most iodine that the thyroid does not trap, so urine iodine reflects recent intake. Other minerals are absorbed in amounts that depend on the product and the person's needs. There is no single half-life for a whole seaweed product.", "sidefx": "Most people notice nothing or mild digestive effects such as bloating and loose stools. The more important risks come from what seaweed carries. Iodine content varies widely between species, harvest sites and preparations, and labels rarely list it, so regular use can push the thyroid toward hyperthyroidism, with palpitations, tremor, heat intolerance and weight loss, or hypothyroidism, with fatigue, cold intolerance and weight gain. People with thyroid disease, those on levothyroxine, antithyroid drugs, amiodarone or lithium, and pregnant or breastfeeding people are most vulnerable. Seaweeds can contain heavy metals. Homemade gels can grow bacteria and mold if not refrigerated. Allergic reactions are possible.", "fun": "Carrageenan from red seaweeds such as sea moss is in many everyday foods, from chocolate milk to plant-based ice cream, where it keeps cocoa from settling and gives a creamy feel; most people eat it regularly without knowing it came from the same seaweed now sold as a superfood.", "src": ["NIH Office of Dietary Supplements: Iodine fact sheet", "21 CFR 172.620, Carrageenan", "Wolff J, Chaikoff IL 1948, Journal of Biological Chemistry, Plasma inorganic iodide as a homeostatic regulator of thyroid function", "Leung AM, Braverman LE 2014, Nature Reviews Endocrinology, Consequences of excess iodine"]}, "kelp-iodine": {"origin": "Iodine was discovered in 1811 by the French chemist Bernard Courtois, who was extracting saltpeter for gunpowder from seaweed ash and noticed violet vapor rising when he added sulfuric acid; the name comes from the Greek word for violet. Seaweed ash, called kelp in Scotland, was already an industry, burned for soda and later for iodine. Within a decade, the Geneva physician Jean-Francois Coindet was treating goiter with iodine, building on the old practice of using burnt sponge and seaweed for swollen necks. Large studies in Ohio schoolchildren around 1920 showed that iodine prevented goiter, and iodized salt was introduced in the United States in 1924, dramatically reducing goiter in the so-called goiter belt. In 1948 Jan Wolff and Israel Chaikoff described how excess iodine temporarily shuts down thyroid hormone production, explaining why iodine can cause hypothyroidism, while iodine-induced hyperthyroidism had been recognized since the nineteenth century as the Jod-Basedow phenomenon. Potassium iodide later gained a different role as an FDA-approved thyroid blocker after radiation releases. Kelp and iodine supplements are marketed for thyroid support, but in iodine-sufficient people extra iodine mostly brings risk.", "timeline": [["1811", "Bernard Courtois discovers iodine while processing seaweed ash."], ["1820", "Jean-Francois Coindet reports treating goiter with iodine in Geneva."], ["1924", "Iodized salt is introduced in the United States."], ["1948", "Wolff and Chaikoff describe suppression of thyroid hormone synthesis by excess iodine."], ["2007", "A case report links a kelp supplement to arsenic toxicity."]], "cell": "Thyroid follicular cells pull iodide from the blood with the sodium-iodide symporter on their basolateral surface, concentrating it far above plasma levels. At the apical surface, pendrin moves iodide into the follicle, where thyroid peroxidase oxidizes it and attaches it to tyrosine residues on thyroglobulin, then couples iodotyrosines to form T4 and T3. When a large iodine load arrives, high intracellular iodide inhibits organification, probably through formation of iodolipids and iodolactones, the Wolff-Chaikoff effect. Normal glands escape within days by reducing symporter expression so less iodide enters. If escape fails, as in autoimmune thyroiditis, after partial thyroidectomy or radioiodine, or in the immature fetal thyroid, hypothyroidism and goiter follow. In a gland with autonomous nodules, or one adapted to deficiency, the extra substrate is turned into hormone without normal TSH control, causing hyperthyroidism, the Jod-Basedow effect. Brown seaweeds store iodine at very high concentrations, partly as an antioxidant defense, and they also accumulate arsenic, some of it in toxic inorganic form depending on species.", "body": [["Thyroid", "Excess iodine can cause hypothyroidism, hyperthyroidism or goiter depending on underlying thyroid status."], ["Heart", "Hyperthyroidism causes tachycardia and atrial fibrillation; hypothyroidism causes bradycardia."], ["Mouth and salivary glands", "Iodism causes metallic taste, burning mouth and salivary gland swelling."], ["Skin", "Acne-like iodide eruptions; arsenic exposure can cause skin changes."], ["Fetus", "Excess maternal iodine can cause fetal goiter and hypothyroidism."]], "journey": "Iodine from kelp and supplements is converted to iodide in the gut and absorbed almost completely in the stomach and small intestine. It distributes in extracellular fluid. The thyroid takes up a share that depends on its needs and on how much iodine is already in the body; salivary glands, stomach lining and the breastfeeding breast also concentrate iodide, so it appears in saliva and breast milk. Iodine incorporated into thyroid hormones is stored in thyroglobulin and released slowly, and when hormones are broken down, deiodinases free iodide for reuse. Most iodide that is not trapped is excreted by the kidneys within a day or so, so urine iodine tracks recent intake. Thyroid effects, however, can last weeks after exposure stops.", "sidefx": "The main harms are thyroid dysfunction and contamination. Hypothyroidism from excess iodine causes fatigue, cold intolerance, weight gain, constipation and slowed heart rate; hyperthyroidism causes palpitations, tremor, heat intolerance, weight loss and sometimes atrial fibrillation, and rarely thyroid storm. Iodism produces metallic taste, burning mouth, sore teeth and gums, salivary gland swelling, headache and acne-like rash. Hypersensitivity reactions to iodides, including vasculitis, are described with medicinal iodides. Kelp may contain arsenic, and a published case linked a kelp supplement to arsenic-related symptoms including hair loss, fatigue and memory problems. Interactions with amiodarone, lithium, antithyroid drugs, levothyroxine, iodinated contrast and radioactive iodine work-ups are clinically important. Pregnancy and breastfeeding require care because excess iodine can harm the fetal and infant thyroid.", "fun": "Iodine was found by accident in wartime: Bernard Courtois was making saltpeter from seaweed ash for Napoleon's gunpowder when a cloud of violet vapor rose from his vat and settled into dark crystals, an element no one had seen before.", "src": ["NIH Office of Dietary Supplements: Iodine fact sheet", "Leung AM, Braverman LE 2014, Nature Reviews Endocrinology, Consequences of excess iodine", "Amster E, Tiwary A, Schenker MB 2007, Environmental Health Perspectives, Case report: potential arsenic toxicosis secondary to herbal kelp supplement", "FDA: Potassium Iodide as a Thyroid Blocking Agent in Radiation Emergencies (guidance and Q&A)"]}, "bee-pollen": {"origin": "Bees gather pollen as their protein source. Foraging workers moisten pollen with nectar and salivary secretions, pack it into pellets on their hind legs, and bring it back to the hive, where much of it is stored as fermented bee bread to feed larvae. Beekeepers collect pellets by placing a pollen trap at the hive entrance, a grid that knocks some pellets off as bees pass through. Pollen has been part of folk medicine in several cultures, and in the twentieth century it was promoted as a complete food, partly through claims that athletes in Europe used it for stamina. Controlled studies did not find meaningful performance benefits. Bee pollen became popular in the United States as an energy and allergy supplement, often with the idea that local pollen would desensitize people to seasonal allergies, which has not been shown and can be dangerous, since swallowing a concentrated allergen can trigger severe reactions. In the 2010s, FDA warned consumers about imported weight-loss capsules marketed as bee pollen, such as Zi Xiu Tang Bee Pollen, after testing found undeclared sibutramine, a weight-loss drug withdrawn from the US market in 2010 because of cardiovascular risk, and phenolphthalein, a laxative ingredient removed from the market as a suspected carcinogen.", "timeline": [["20th century", "Bee pollen is promoted in Europe and North America as a complete food and stamina aid."], ["2010", "Sibutramine is withdrawn from the US market because of increased heart attack and stroke risk."], ["2010", "A case report describes a probable warfarin and bee pollen interaction with a raised INR."], ["2014", "FDA warns consumers about Zi Xiu Tang Bee Pollen weight-loss capsules containing sibutramine and phenolphthalein."], ["2010s", "European food safety agencies report pyrrolizidine alkaloids in some pollen products."]], "cell": "Bee pollen is not a single substance but a sample of the local flowers. Each pellet contains thousands of pollen grains whose outer walls are built of sporopollenin, an extremely tough polymer, around a cytoplasm rich in proteins, amino acids, sugars, lipids, B vitamins, carotenoids, flavonoids such as quercetin and kaempferol, and phenolic acids. Bees add nectar sugars and salivary enzymes. The pollen proteins are the same allergens that cause hay fever. In a sensitized person, these proteins cross-link IgE antibodies bound to mast cells and basophils, which degranulate and release histamine, tryptase, leukotrienes and prostaglandins, causing hives, angioedema, bronchospasm and, in severe reactions, vasodilation and hypotension. Swallowing concentrated pollen delivers far more allergen than breathing it in. Antioxidant and anti-inflammatory effects of pollen extracts are reported in laboratory and animal work, but they have not translated into proven clinical benefits. The mechanism of the reported warfarin interaction is unknown. Some plant pollens carry pyrrolizidine alkaloids, which are liver toxins, and contamination by pesticides or mold toxins depends on the environment.", "body": [["Immune system", "IgE-mediated allergy to pollen proteins can cause anaphylaxis."], ["Airways", "Bronchospasm and asthma attacks in sensitized people."], ["Skin", "Hives, itching and angioedema."], ["Blood clotting", "A case report describes a raised INR in a warfarin user."], ["Heart", "Adulterated weight-loss products with sibutramine can raise heart rate and blood pressure."]], "journey": "Swallowed pollen grains are only partly digested because sporopollenin walls resist human enzymes; nutrients leak out through pores in the walls or after the grains crack, and many grains pass through intact in the stool. Proteins, sugars and fats that are released are absorbed like other food nutrients, and flavonoids are absorbed in small amounts and conjugated in the gut wall and liver before excretion in urine and bile. For allergy, absorption is not even required: pollen proteins contacting the mouth, throat and gut lining can sensitize or trigger mast cells, and allergic reactions can begin within minutes. Hidden drugs in adulterated products follow their own pharmacokinetics; sibutramine, for example, is metabolized by CYP3A4 to active metabolites.", "sidefx": "The most serious risk is allergy. Reactions range from itching of the mouth and throat to hives, angioedema, asthma attacks and anaphylaxis, sometimes after the first dose, and people with pollen allergies or asthma are most at risk. GI upset is common. A case report described a raised INR in a patient on warfarin. Adulterated weight-loss products labeled bee pollen exposed users to sibutramine, which can cause palpitations, high blood pressure, stroke and seizures, and phenolphthalein, which causes diarrhea and is a suspected carcinogen. Contamination with pyrrolizidine alkaloids, pesticides or mold toxins is possible. Safety in pregnancy, breastfeeding and childhood has not been established.", "fun": "Some bee pollen weight-loss capsules investigated by FDA contained no meaningful bee pollen effect at all; their weight-loss action came from a prescription drug that had been pulled from the US market for heart risks, hidden under a natural-sounding name.", "src": ["FDA consumer updates and warnings on bee pollen weight-loss products with undeclared drugs (2014)", "Hurren KM, Lewis CL 2010, case report: Probable interaction between warfarin and bee pollen", "Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center About Herbs: Bee Pollen", "Komosinska-Vassev K et al. 2015, Evidence-Based Complementary and Alternative Medicine, Bee pollen: chemical composition and therapeutic application"]}, "royal-jelly": {"origin": "Royal jelly is the food that turns an ordinary female bee larva into a queen. All honeybee larvae receive glandular secretions from young nurse bees for the first few days, but larvae destined to be queens are fed royal jelly in abundance throughout development, and they grow into larger, fertile, long-lived queens rather than workers. That striking transformation made royal jelly an obvious candidate for rejuvenation claims. Commercial production developed in the twentieth century, using artificial queen cups that beekeepers harvest after a few days, and China became the largest producer. Royal jelly was popular in Europe and Japan as a tonic by the mid-twentieth century and spread worldwide as a supplement. Its reputation took a serious hit in Australia in the 1990s, when cases of severe asthma, anaphylaxis and deaths after royal jelly use were reported, mostly in people with asthma or allergies, and Australian regulators required warning statements on product labels. Research interest continued: in 2011, a study proposed that a single royal jelly protein, royalactin, drives queen development, though later work disputed that conclusion, and the full explanation of how diet determines caste is still being studied.", "timeline": [["20th century", "Commercial royal jelly harvesting develops using artificial queen cells."], ["1990s", "Severe asthma, anaphylaxis and deaths linked to royal jelly are reported in Australia."], ["1990s", "Australia requires warning statements about allergic reactions on royal jelly products."], ["2006", "A case report describes hematuria and a raised INR in a warfarin user taking royal jelly."], ["2011", "A study proposes the protein royalactin drives queen development; later research disputes this."]], "cell": "Royal jelly is mostly water, with proteins, sugars and lipids. Its proteins are dominated by the major royal jelly proteins, a family of glycoproteins related to insect yellow proteins, along with antimicrobial peptides such as royalisin. Its lipid fraction is unusual, made largely of short hydroxy fatty acids, especially 10-hydroxy-2-decenoic acid, which is found almost nowhere else and is used as a quality marker. Clinically, the proteins matter most because they are allergens. In sensitized people, MRJPs and other proteins cross-link IgE on mast cells and basophils, releasing histamine, leukotrienes and other mediators that cause bronchospasm, hives, angioedema and hypotension. Some royal jelly allergens may cross-react with other bee products and pollens, which may explain why atopic people are at highest risk even on first use. Laboratory studies report weak binding of royal jelly components to estrogen receptors, effects on cholesterol synthesis and immune cell activity, but these have not translated into proven clinical benefits. The mechanism of the reported warfarin interaction is unknown.", "body": [["Airways", "Bronchospasm and severe asthma in sensitized people, including fatal cases."], ["Immune system", "IgE-mediated anaphylaxis, hives and angioedema."], ["Colon", "Hemorrhagic colitis has been described in a case report."], ["Blood clotting", "A case report describes hematuria with a raised INR in a warfarin user."], ["Reproductive and hormonal system", "Weak estrogenic activity in laboratory studies; small menopause symptom trials with mixed results."]], "journey": "Royal jelly is swallowed as a food-like mixture. Its proteins are digested to peptides and amino acids, though allergenic proteins can trigger reactions on contact with the mouth, throat and gut lining before digestion is complete, and reactions can begin within minutes. Sugars and lipids are absorbed like other food components, and 10-HDA is absorbed and metabolized by fatty acid pathways. Freeze-drying and processing change some components, and royal jelly degrades if not kept cold, which may affect its protein content. There is no single half-life. Topical products deliver components to the skin, where contact dermatitis can occur.", "sidefx": "The defining risk is allergy: royal jelly has caused acute asthma, anaphylaxis and deaths, predominantly in people with asthma, allergic rhinitis or eczema, sometimes after the first dose. Hives, angioedema and throat tightness are reported. Contact dermatitis occurs with creams. A case report describes hemorrhagic colitis after royal jelly use. Another case report linked royal jelly to hematuria and a raised INR in a patient on warfarin. Weak estrogenic activity in laboratory studies raises theoretical concern in hormone-sensitive conditions. GI upset is common. Safety in pregnancy, breastfeeding and childhood has not been established.", "fun": "Worker bees and queens can come from genetically identical larvae; what mostly separates a short-lived sterile worker from a queen who lays eggs for years is how much and how long she is fed this jelly, an example of diet switching on a different developmental program.", "src": ["Thien FC et al. 1996, Clinical and Experimental Allergy, Asthma and anaphylaxis induced by royal jelly", "Lee NJ, Fermo JD 2006, Pharmacotherapy, Warfarin and royal jelly interaction", "Kamakura M 2011, Nature, Royalactin induces queen differentiation in honeybees", "Buttstedt A, Ihling CH, Pietzsch M, Moritz RF 2016, Nature, Royalactin is not a royal making of a queen", "Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center About Herbs: Royal Jelly"]}, "glucosamine": {"origin": "Glucosamine was first prepared in 1876 by the German surgeon Georg Ledderhose, who obtained it by hydrolyzing chitin, the tough polymer of crustacean shells and insect exoskeletons, and its structure was settled in the twentieth century. It turned out to be a central building block in the body as well, part of hyaluronan, keratan sulfate, heparan sulfate and many glycoproteins. The idea that swallowing it might feed cartilage repair led to European studies in the 1980s of crystalline glucosamine sulfate, which became a prescription product in several European countries. In the United States, glucosamine became one of the best-selling supplements in the late 1990s after popular books promoted it as an arthritis cure. To test the claims, the NIH funded the Glucosamine/chondroitin Arthritis Intervention Trial, GAIT, published in 2006, which randomized patients with knee osteoarthritis to glucosamine hydrochloride, chondroitin sulfate, both, celecoxib or placebo; overall, the supplements were not significantly better than placebo for pain. Debate continues about whether the sulfate form behaves differently, and guidelines from major rheumatology groups generally do not recommend glucosamine. Meanwhile, reports of raised INR in people on warfarin established its most important safety concern.", "timeline": [["1876", "Georg Ledderhose prepares glucosamine from chitin."], ["1980s", "European trials study crystalline glucosamine sulfate for osteoarthritis."], ["Late 1990s", "Glucosamine becomes one of the top-selling US supplements."], ["2006", "The NIH GAIT trial finds glucosamine and chondroitin no better than placebo overall for knee osteoarthritis pain."], ["2000s", "Case reports and pharmacovigilance data link glucosamine to raised INR in warfarin users."]], "cell": "Glucosamine is glucose with an amino group in place of one hydroxyl. Cells make it as glucosamine-6-phosphate from fructose-6-phosphate and glutamine in the hexosamine biosynthesis pathway, and then convert it to UDP-N-acetylglucosamine, the activated sugar used to build glycosaminoglycans and to add O-GlcNAc tags to proteins. In cartilage, chondrocytes use these sugars to assemble aggrecan and hyaluronan, which hold water and give cartilage its cushioning. In laboratory studies, high concentrations of glucosamine reduce interleukin-1 beta-driven inflammatory signaling and expression of cartilage-degrading enzymes in chondrocytes. The difficulty is concentration: oral glucosamine reaches the blood and joint fluid at levels far below those used in many lab studies, and the body already makes plenty from glucose. Because the hexosamine pathway is involved in insulin signaling, glucosamine was once suspected of worsening insulin resistance, but oral studies in people generally show no clinically important change in glucose. The mechanism behind raised INR with warfarin remains unknown.", "body": [["Joints", "Proposed to support cartilage; large trials show little or no pain benefit for most people."], ["Blood clotting", "Linked to raised INR and bleeding in warfarin users."], ["Gut", "Nausea, heartburn, bloating, diarrhea or constipation."], ["Pancreas and glucose metabolism", "Theoretical insulin resistance; clinical studies mostly show no significant glucose effect."], ["Airways", "Case reports describe asthma exacerbation."]], "journey": "Oral glucosamine is absorbed from the small intestine, though a large share is metabolized in the gut wall and liver on first pass, so oral bioavailability is limited. In small human studies, blood levels peak a few hours after a dose, and glucosamine is detectable in joint fluid at low concentrations. Much of it enters normal hexosamine metabolism, where it is incorporated into glycosaminoglycans and glycoproteins, used for energy, or broken down. Elimination occurs through urine and feces, and some carbon from glucosamine is exhaled as carbon dioxide after metabolism. The reported elimination half-life is roughly 15 hours in small studies. Sulfate and hydrochloride salts dissociate in the gut, so differences between products may reflect formulation and dose rather than different glucosamine molecules.", "sidefx": "Glucosamine is generally well tolerated; GI effects such as nausea, heartburn, bloating, diarrhea and constipation are the most common. The key safety issue is the warfarin interaction: case reports and pharmacovigilance data describe raised INR and bleeding when glucosamine, with or without chondroitin, is used with warfarin. Allergic reactions occur; shellfish-derived products raise concern in shellfish allergy, though true reactions attributed to glucosamine appear rare because the allergens are proteins rather than the sugar. Case reports describe asthma exacerbation and rare liver injury. Effects on blood glucose appear small in most studies. Safety in pregnancy, breastfeeding and childhood has not been established.", "fun": "Glucosamine for supplements is often made from the shells of shrimp, crab and lobster, the same chitin that Georg Ledderhose broke down in 1876, which means one of the best-selling joint supplements is, chemically, recycled seafood packaging.", "src": ["Clegg DO et al. 2006, New England Journal of Medicine, Glucosamine, chondroitin sulfate, and the two in combination for painful knee osteoarthritis", "NIH NCCIH: Glucosamine and Chondroitin for Osteoarthritis", "Knudsen JF, Sokol GH 2008, Pharmacotherapy, Potential glucosamine-warfarin interaction resulting in increased international normalized ratio", "NIH LiverTox: Glucosamine"]}, "chondroitin": {"origin": "Chondroitin takes its name from the Greek word for cartilage, chondros. It was isolated from cartilage in the late nineteenth century, and its repeating sugar and sulfate structure was worked out in the twentieth century as glycosaminoglycan chemistry developed. Biologically it is a key component of aggrecan, the large proteoglycan that lets cartilage absorb load by holding water. In Europe, pharmaceutical-grade chondroitin sulfate was developed as a symptomatic slow-acting drug for osteoarthritis and became a prescription product in several countries. In the United States it is sold as a supplement, most often combined with glucosamine, and became widely used in the late 1990s. The NIH-funded GAIT trial, published in 2006, found that chondroitin, glucosamine or both were not significantly better than placebo for knee osteoarthritis pain overall, though a small subgroup analysis raised questions that were never confirmed. Product quality has been a recurring issue, since chondroitin is extracted from bovine, porcine, chicken or shark cartilage and is expensive to purify, and testing has found products with less chondroitin than labeled. Chondroitin also played an unexpected role in one of the most serious drug contamination events in recent US history, the 2008 heparin crisis.", "timeline": [["Late 1800s", "Chondroitin sulfuric acid is isolated from cartilage by German chemists."], ["1990s", "Pharmaceutical-grade chondroitin is marketed for osteoarthritis in Europe; supplement use grows in the US."], ["2006", "The NIH GAIT trial finds chondroitin no better than placebo overall for knee osteoarthritis pain."], ["2008", "Oversulfated chondroitin sulfate is identified as the contaminant in heparin linked to severe allergic-type reactions and deaths."], ["2000s", "Reports link glucosamine-chondroitin products to raised INR in warfarin users."]], "cell": "Chondroitin sulfate is a linear polysaccharide of alternating glucuronic acid and N-acetylgalactosamine, with sulfate groups at the 4- or 6-position of the galactosamine. In cartilage, chains of it are attached to the core protein of aggrecan, and the dense negative charge of the sulfates and carboxyl groups attracts sodium ions and water, swelling the proteoglycan network against the collagen framework; that swelling pressure is what lets cartilage resist compression. In laboratory studies, chondroitin reduces interleukin-1 beta-induced NF-kB activation in chondrocytes and synovial cells and lowers expression of matrix metalloproteinases and aggrecanases that break down cartilage. Clinically, the problem is delivery: the large intact molecule is poorly absorbed, and what reaches the blood is largely smaller fragments, so it is uncertain how much of the laboratory effect occurs in joints. Its sulfated polyanionic structure resembles heparin, which underlies a theoretical anticoagulant concern. The reported rise in INR with warfarin has mostly involved combinations with glucosamine, and the mechanism is unknown.", "body": [["Joints", "Proposed to support cartilage; large US trials show little or no pain benefit overall."], ["Blood clotting", "Raised INR and bleeding reported in warfarin users, mainly with glucosamine combinations."], ["Gut", "Mild nausea, bloating and changes in bowel habit; bacteria break down much of the oral dose."], ["Skin and hair", "Case reports of swelling and hair loss."], ["Airways", "Asthma exacerbation reported with combination products."]], "journey": "Chondroitin sulfate is a large, highly charged molecule, so very little crosses the intestinal wall intact. Gut bacteria and enzymes break it into smaller oligosaccharides, disaccharides and free sugars and sulfate, some of which are absorbed. Studies measuring blood levels after oral doses show modest rises in low-molecular-weight chondroitin-related material over several hours, and some radiolabeled material reaches joint tissues in animal studies. Absorbed fragments are further degraded by tissue enzymes and excreted mainly in urine, while unabsorbed material is excreted in feces. Bioavailability differs with molecular weight, sulfation pattern and source, which helps explain inconsistent results between products. Detailed human half-life and protein binding data are limited.", "sidefx": "Chondroitin is generally well tolerated. Mild GI effects such as nausea, stomach upset, bloating, diarrhea and constipation are the most common. Allergic reactions are possible, and animal sources raise concern for people with relevant allergies. Case reports describe swelling, hair loss and asthma exacerbation. The most important safety issue is bleeding risk in warfarin users, reported mainly with glucosamine-chondroitin combinations, and theoretically with other anticoagulants or antiplatelets because of its heparin-like structure. Product content can differ from the label. Safety in pregnancy, breastfeeding and childhood has not been established.", "fun": "In 2007 and 2008, heparin made from pig intestines in China was found to be adulterated with oversulfated chondroitin sulfate, a cheap chemically modified version of the joint supplement that mimicked heparin in standard tests; it triggered severe allergic-type reactions and deaths, and FDA changed how heparin is tested as a result.", "src": ["Clegg DO et al. 2006, New England Journal of Medicine, GAIT trial", "NIH NCCIH: Glucosamine and Chondroitin for Osteoarthritis", "Kishimoto TK et al. 2008, New England Journal of Medicine, Contaminated heparin associated with adverse clinical events and activation of the contact system", "Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center About Herbs: Chondroitin"]}, "oral-hyaluronic-acid": {"origin": "Hyaluronic acid was isolated in 1934 by Karl Meyer and John Palmer at Columbia University from the vitreous humor of cow eyes. They named it from hyalos, the Greek word for glass, because of the vitreous, and uronic acid, one of its sugar components. Over the following decades, hyaluronan was found throughout the body, especially in joint fluid, skin, the eye and umbilical cord, and its structure as a repeating disaccharide was worked out in the 1950s. Endre Balazs, a Hungarian-born scientist, developed highly purified non-inflammatory hyaluronan from rooster combs, which led to its use in eye surgery as a viscoelastic gel in the late 1970s and early 1980s and later to injections for knee osteoarthritis, which FDA regulates as medical devices, and to dermal fillers. Oral hyaluronic acid followed a different path, emerging in Japan as a beauty food ingredient and then spreading worldwide in skin and joint supplements. Most hyaluronan for supplements is now made by bacterial fermentation, though rooster comb material is still used. The evidence for oral products comes from small, mainly industry-funded trials, and their story is separate from the much larger body of research on injected and topical hyaluronan.", "timeline": [["1934", "Karl Meyer and John Palmer isolate hyaluronic acid from bovine vitreous humor."], ["1950s", "The repeating disaccharide structure of hyaluronan is established."], ["1980s", "Purified hyaluronan gels are used as viscoelastic aids in eye surgery."], ["1997", "The first intra-articular hyaluronan products for knee osteoarthritis are approved as devices in the United States."], ["2000s-2010s", "Oral hyaluronic acid spreads as a beauty and joint supplement, with small trials of skin moisture and knee pain."]], "cell": "Hyaluronan is unusual among glycosaminoglycans: it is not sulfated, not attached to a core protein, and it is made directly at the plasma membrane by hyaluronan synthase enzymes that extrude the growing chain into the extracellular space. Chains can reach millions of daltons, and the polymer forms a highly hydrated, viscous network that lubricates joints and fills space in skin and the vitreous. Cells sense hyaluronan through receptors including CD44, RHAMM and toll-like receptors. Size matters: large, intact hyaluronan is generally anti-inflammatory and anti-angiogenic, while small fragments produced during tissue injury can signal danger and promote inflammation and blood vessel growth. Hyaluronan and CD44 are also involved in cell migration, which is why some tumors are rich in hyaluronan and why theoretical cancer concerns are raised. For oral supplements, the key question is delivery. Animal studies with labeled hyaluronan suggest that fragments are absorbed after bacterial breakdown and some label reaches skin and joints, and another proposed route is signaling through toll-like receptor 4 in the gut lining, but how much this contributes in humans is not established.", "body": [["Joints", "Natural lubricant of synovial fluid; oral supplements studied for knee pain with modest, small-trial results."], ["Skin", "Major water-holding component of the dermis; small trials report improved skin moisture with oral HA."], ["Eye", "Fills the vitreous; injected HA gels are used in eye surgery."], ["Gut", "Bacteria degrade most oral HA; GI side effects are uncommon."], ["Liver", "Clears circulating hyaluronan from the blood rapidly."]], "journey": "Swallowed hyaluronic acid is a large molecule that resists stomach acid but is broken down in the lower gut, largely by bacterial enzymes, into oligosaccharides and the sugars glucuronic acid and N-acetylglucosamine. Some fragments are absorbed. Animal studies with labeled HA show label appearing in blood and in tissues including skin and joints over hours to days, though much of it may be in the form of small fragments or recycled sugars rather than intact HA. Hyaluronan that circulates in blood, whether made by the body or absorbed, is cleared quickly by receptors on liver sinusoidal endothelial cells and broken down by hyaluronidases; in tissues, turnover takes days. Little appears in urine. Supplement products differ in molecular weight, which may affect breakdown and absorption.", "sidefx": "Oral hyaluronic acid has been well tolerated in trials, with uncommon mild GI upset. Allergic reactions are possible, and rooster comb-derived products raise concern for people with poultry or egg protein allergies. No clinically important drug interactions are known. Because hyaluronan is involved in tumor biology, a theoretical concern about cancer has been raised, though there is no evidence that oral supplements cause or worsen cancer. Combination beauty and joint products carry the risks of their other ingredients. Safety in pregnancy, breastfeeding and childhood has not been established. The injected and filler forms have their own distinct adverse effects, such as joint flares and vascular occlusion, which do not apply to oral use.", "fun": "Some streptococci wrap themselves in a hyaluronan capsule chemically identical to human hyaluronan, which helps them hide from the immune system; harmless strains of the same bacterial group are now grown in fermenters to make much of the hyaluronic acid sold in supplements and cosmetics.", "src": ["Meyer K, Palmer JW 1934, Journal of Biological Chemistry, The polysaccharide of the vitreous humor", "Kawada C et al. 2014, Nutrition Journal, Ingested hyaluronan moisturizes dry skin", "Balogh L et al. 2008, Journal of Agricultural and Food Chemistry, Absorption, uptake and tissue affinity of high-molecular-weight hyaluronan after oral administration in rats and dogs", "Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center About Herbs: Hyaluronic Acid"]}, "boron": {"origin": "Boron was isolated as an element in 1808, independently by Humphry Davy in England and by Joseph Gay-Lussac and Louis Thenard in France. Its compounds had been used for centuries: borax, mined in Tibet and later in California's Death Valley, was used in glazes, metalwork and cleaning, and boric acid became a popular antiseptic in the nineteenth century, used for wounds, eye washes, diaper powders and even food preservation. That popularity produced a long record of poisoning, including outbreaks in newborn nurseries where boric acid solutions or powders were mistaken for formula or applied to broken skin, which led to its removal from most medical uses. In 1923, the British botanist Katherine Warington showed that boron is essential for plant growth, and boron fertilizers became standard in agriculture. Whether it is essential for people has never been established. In the 1980s, studies by Forrest Nielsen and colleagues at the US Department of Agriculture reported that boron intake changed calcium metabolism and raised estrogen and testosterone in postmenopausal women, which launched boron supplements for bones and hormones. Later studies have been small and inconsistent. Boric acid has returned in a different form, as vaginal suppositories for recurrent yeast infections, which are dangerous if swallowed.", "timeline": [["1808", "Boron is isolated by Davy in England and by Gay-Lussac and Thenard in France."], ["1800s", "Boric acid becomes a widely used antiseptic and food preservative."], ["1923", "Katherine Warington shows boron is essential for plant growth."], ["1987", "USDA studies report effects of boron on mineral metabolism and sex hormones in postmenopausal women."], ["1988", "A large case series of boric acid ingestions describes the typical toxicity pattern."]], "cell": "In the body, boron exists mostly as boric acid, a weak Lewis acid that forms reversible complexes with molecules carrying adjacent hydroxyl groups, called cis-diols. These include sugars, ribose-containing molecules such as NAD+, S-adenosylmethionine and riboflavin, and some steroid precursors. This chemistry is the basis of proposed effects on energy metabolism, vitamin D activation, and estrogen and testosterone levels, but no boron-dependent enzyme or protein has been identified in humans, so it is not classed as an essential nutrient. Small human studies report changes in urinary calcium and magnesium and in serum sex hormones, but findings are not consistent. At toxic doses, boric acid acts as a general cellular poison: it irritates and injures the GI lining, damages the skin, which produces a diffuse red rash that peels, and affects the central nervous system and kidneys, where it concentrates. Animal studies show testicular toxicity and developmental effects at high exposures, the basis of reproductive concerns.", "body": [["Bone", "Small studies suggest effects on calcium and magnesium metabolism; fracture prevention is not established."], ["Endocrine system", "Small studies report changes in estrogen and testosterone levels with inconsistent results."], ["Skin", "Boric acid poisoning causes a bright red 'boiled lobster' rash that later peels."], ["Kidneys", "Main route of elimination; injured in severe poisoning."], ["Central nervous system", "Lethargy, irritability and seizures in severe poisoning, especially in infants."]], "journey": "Boron from food and supplements is absorbed almost completely from the gut as boric acid. It distributes throughout body water, with some concentration in bone. It is not metabolized, because breaking boron-oxygen bonds requires far more energy than the body supplies. The kidneys excrete it largely unchanged, and limited human data suggest an elimination half-life of about a day. Kidney failure slows elimination, and because boric acid is a small, water-soluble molecule with low protein binding, hemodialysis removes it effectively in severe poisoning. Boric acid can also be absorbed through broken skin and mucous membranes, which explains historic poisonings from wound and diaper powders.", "sidefx": "Supplement amounts are generally well tolerated, though nausea and stomach upset occur. Possible hormonal effects make boron a concern in hormone-sensitive conditions. Toxicity follows high exposure, most often from boric acid: nausea, persistent vomiting, sometimes with blue-green coloring, diarrhea, abdominal pain, headache, a bright red rash that later peels, lethargy and seizures, with kidney injury and shock in severe cases. Infants and children are most vulnerable. Chronic excessive intake can cause poor appetite, rash, hair loss and GI symptoms. Pregnancy is a concern because boric acid is a reproductive toxicant in animals. There is no antidote; treatment is supportive, and hemodialysis is used for severe poisoning.", "fun": "Boron is in your kitchen as well as your supplement shelf: borosilicate glass, the heat-resistant glass of laboratory beakers and some bakeware, gets its toughness from boron oxide, and boric acid is still sold as an ant and cockroach poison.", "src": ["NIH Office of Dietary Supplements: Boron fact sheet", "Nielsen FH et al. 1987, FASEB Journal, Effect of dietary boron on mineral, estrogen, and testosterone metabolism in postmenopausal women", "Litovitz TL et al. 1988, American Journal of Emergency Medicine, Clinical manifestations of toxicity in a series of 784 boric acid ingestions", "Institute of Medicine 2001, Dietary Reference Intakes for vitamin A, vitamin K, arsenic, boron and other trace elements"]}, "shilajit": {"origin": "Shilajit, from Sanskrit words often translated as conqueror of mountains and destroyer of weakness, is a dark, tar-like substance that oozes from cracks in rock in high mountain ranges, especially the Himalayas, and also in the Altai, Caucasus and other ranges, where it is called mumie. It forms over long periods from plant material and microbes decomposing in rock crevices, and it softens and seeps out in warm months. Classical Ayurvedic texts, including the Charaka Samhita, describe shilajatu as a rasayana, a rejuvenating substance, and it has been used across South and Central Asia for strength, vitality and healing. Traditional preparation involves dissolving, filtering and drying the raw material to remove impurities. In recent years, shilajit became a social media wellness trend, sold as resin, capsules and gummies for testosterone and energy. The safety concern is long-standing for Ayurvedic products as a group: in 2004, the CDC described lead poisoning cases linked to Ayurvedic medicines, and in 2008, a study led by Robert Saper found detectable lead, mercury or arsenic in about one fifth of Ayurvedic products purchased online, both US- and India-made. Small trials of shilajit for testosterone and sperm have been reported, but evidence is limited and often industry-supported.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Classical Ayurvedic texts describe shilajatu as a rejuvenating rasayana."], ["2004", "CDC reports lead poisoning cases associated with Ayurvedic medications."], ["2008", "A JAMA study finds lead, mercury or arsenic in about one fifth of Ayurvedic products sold online."], ["2010s", "Small trials examine shilajit for testosterone, sperm measures and fatigue."], ["2020s", "Shilajit resin and gummies become a social media wellness trend."]], "cell": "Shilajit is dominated by humic substances, especially fulvic acid, a family of small, oxygen-rich, acidic organic molecules formed when plant material decomposes. Fulvic acids bind metal ions, which may help carry minerals but also means they can carry toxic metals. Dibenzo-alpha-pyrones, which are derived from plant and microbial metabolism, are proposed to support mitochondrial energy production through coenzyme Q10-related pathways in laboratory and animal studies. Animal and cell work also reports antioxidant and anti-inflammatory effects. Small human studies report modest rises in testosterone and changes in sperm count and motility, but mechanisms are unclear and results have not been confirmed in large independent trials. The serious toxicology comes from contaminants. Lead binds sulfhydryl groups and inhibits enzymes such as delta-aminolevulinic acid dehydratase and ferrochelatase in heme synthesis, causing anemia, and it disrupts calcium signaling in nerves, causing neuropathy and encephalopathy. Arsenic inhibits pyruvate dehydrogenase and other enzymes, and mercury damages kidneys and the nervous system.", "body": [["Blood", "Lead contamination inhibits heme synthesis, causing anemia."], ["Nervous system", "Heavy metals can cause neuropathy, confusion and, in severe lead poisoning, encephalopathy and seizures."], ["Gut", "Lead causes abdominal pain and constipation; arsenic causes vomiting and diarrhea."], ["Kidneys", "Lead and mercury can injure the kidneys."], ["Reproductive system", "Small trials report testosterone and sperm changes; heavy metals harm fertility and fetal development."]], "journey": "Shilajit is usually dissolved in water or milk or swallowed as capsules. Its fulvic acids and small organic molecules are partly absorbed and partly pass through in stool; detailed human pharmacokinetic studies do not exist for this variable mixture. Contaminating metals follow their own paths. Lead is absorbed from the gut, more efficiently in children and in iron or calcium deficiency, travels mostly within red blood cells, and is stored in bone, where it can persist for years and be released later, including during pregnancy. It is excreted slowly in urine and feces. Arsenic is absorbed well and excreted mainly in urine over days, and mercury compounds vary in absorption and are excreted slowly. Because metals accumulate, symptoms may appear only after weeks or months of use.", "sidefx": "In small trials, shilajit has been reported to cause mild GI upset, dizziness and allergic rash. The serious risk comes from contamination. Lead poisoning causes abdominal pain, constipation, fatigue, irritability, headache, anemia, peripheral neuropathy and, in severe cases, encephalopathy and seizures. Arsenic causes GI symptoms, skin changes and neuropathy, and mercury causes tremor, mood changes and kidney injury. Raw shilajit may also contain fungal toxins and microbes. Children and pregnant people are most vulnerable to heavy metals. Possible hormonal effects raise concern in hormone-sensitive conditions. Confirmed significant heavy metal poisoning is treated with chelation under specialist guidance.", "fun": "Shilajit is literally oozing out of rock: on hot summer days in the mountains, it softens and seeps from cliff cracks, and collectors in some regions climb steep rock faces to scrape it off, a source that is also why its mineral and metal content varies so much from batch to batch.", "src": ["Saper RB et al. 2008, JAMA, Lead, mercury, and arsenic in US- and Indian-manufactured Ayurvedic medicines sold via the Internet", "CDC 2004, MMWR, Lead poisoning associated with Ayurvedic medications - five states, 2000-2003", "Pandit S et al. 2016, Andrologia, Clinical evaluation of purified shilajit on testosterone levels in healthy volunteers", "Agarwal SP et al. 2007, Phytotherapy Research, Shilajit: a review"]}, "deer-antler-velvet": {"origin": "Deer antlers are the fastest-growing bone tissue known in mammals. Each year, male deer and elk shed their antlers and grow new ones, which are covered in a soft, hairy, blood-rich skin called velvet while they grow; once growth is complete, the bone hardens and the velvet is rubbed off. Traditional Chinese medicine has used dried antler velvet, lu rong, for roughly two thousand years as a tonic for vitality, bones and kidney yang, and Korea remains one of the largest consumers. Modern production centers on deer farms, especially in New Zealand, where velvet is cut from farmed red deer and elk under regulated procedures. In Western markets, antler velvet was rebranded as an athletic supplement, marketed for its content of insulin-like growth factor 1, which drives growth and repair. This attracted the attention of anti-doping authorities because IGF-1 itself is prohibited by the World Anti-Doping Agency. In 2013, high-profile reports of professional athletes using deer antler spray brought the products into headlines. Small controlled trials in athletes have not shown consistent benefits on strength, performance or hormone levels, and a systematic review concluded that evidence for health benefits was lacking.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Antler velvet (lu rong) is used as a tonic in traditional Chinese medicine."], ["1980s-1990s", "Deer farming for velvet expands, especially in New Zealand."], ["2000s", "Antler velvet is marketed in the West as an athletic supplement with IGF-1 claims."], ["2005", "FDA approves mecasermin, recombinant IGF-1, for severe primary IGF-1 deficiency."], ["2012", "A systematic review finds little evidence for health benefits of antler velvet supplements."], ["2013", "Reports of professional athletes using deer antler spray draw public and anti-doping attention."]], "cell": "Growing antler is a remarkable tissue: a cap of rapidly dividing mesenchymal cells at the tip differentiates into cartilage, which is then replaced by bone, while a dense network of blood vessels and nerves grows alongside. This process is driven partly by IGF-1 and other growth factors, which is why velvet contains measurable amounts. IGF-1 binds the IGF-1 receptor, a tyrosine kinase that activates the PI3K-Akt and MAPK pathways, promoting protein synthesis, cell growth and glucose uptake; in the body, most IGF-1 is made by the liver under the control of growth hormone. The problem for supplements is twofold. First, the amount of IGF-1 in a capsule or spray is tiny compared with what the body already makes. Second, IGF-1 is a 70-amino-acid peptide, so swallowed IGF-1 is digested by stomach and pancreatic proteases like other proteins. Sublingual sprays are claimed to bypass digestion, but meaningful absorption has not been shown. The rest of velvet is collagen, glycosaminoglycans such as chondroitin, minerals and lipids, which are nutrients rather than drugs.", "body": [["Skeletal muscle", "Marketed for growth and recovery; small trials show no consistent strength benefit."], ["Joints", "Contains collagen and chondroitin; joint claims are weakly supported."], ["Endocrine system", "IGF-1 claims; small trials found no meaningful change in circulating IGF-1 or testosterone."], ["Gut", "Proteins, including IGF-1, are digested; GI upset is uncommon."], ["Metabolism", "Absorbed IGF-1 would lower glucose, a theoretical concern in diabetes."]], "journey": "Antler velvet is swallowed as dried powder, capsules or extracts, or sprayed under the tongue. In the stomach and small intestine, its proteins, including IGF-1 and other growth factors, are broken down by pepsin and pancreatic proteases into peptides and amino acids, which are absorbed like dietary protein. Collagen fragments, glycosaminoglycan fragments and minerals are absorbed partly and in the usual ways. Studies have not shown that oral or sublingual products raise blood IGF-1 meaningfully. Absorbed components are processed by normal metabolism and excreted in urine, while unabsorbed material leaves in the feces. No meaningful half-life applies to the mixture.", "sidefx": "Antler velvet appears to be well tolerated in small trials, with occasional GI upset. Allergic reactions are possible. If IGF-1 were absorbed in meaningful amounts, theoretical effects would include low blood sugar, swelling and promotion of tissue growth, which is why hormone-sensitive cancers and diabetes are listed as theoretical concerns. The practical risk for athletes is anti-doping, since IGF-1 is prohibited and products advertise it. Product content varies and is rarely verified. Chronic wasting disease, a prion disease spreading in some deer and elk populations, raises a theoretical sourcing concern for cervid products, although no human cases linked to antler velvet have been established. Safety in pregnancy and breastfeeding is not established.", "fun": "A large elk can grow antlers weighing many kilograms in a single season, adding length at a pace measured in centimeters per day at peak growth, which makes growing antler one of the fastest-growing tissues in the animal kingdom and a model studied by researchers interested in bone regeneration.", "src": ["Gilbey A, Perezgonzalez JD 2012, Journal of Sports Sciences, Health benefits of deer and elk velvet antler supplements: a systematic review", "World Anti-Doping Agency Prohibited List, S2 Peptide hormones, growth factors and related substances", "Increlex (mecasermin) FDA label", "Price J, Allen S 2004, Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B, Exploring the mechanisms regulating regeneration of deer antlers"]}, "lithium-orotate": {"origin": "Lithium has a long and uneven medical history. It was discovered in 1817 by Johan August Arfwedson in Sweden, and in the nineteenth century lithium salts were promoted for gout and so-called uric acid conditions, and lithium waters became fashionable. In the late 1940s, lithium chloride was sold in the United States as a salt substitute for people on low-sodium diets, which led to serious poisonings and deaths in heart patients and its withdrawal in 1949. The same year, the Australian psychiatrist John Cade reported that lithium calmed patients with mania, and Mogens Schou in Denmark later confirmed its benefit in rigorous studies. The FDA approved lithium carbonate for mania in 1970, and it remains a cornerstone treatment for bipolar disorder, valued also for reducing suicide. Lithium orotate came from a different tradition. In the 1970s the German physician Hans Nieper promoted mineral orotates, arguing that orotic acid carried minerals into cells more effectively, and some older animal work suggested different brain levels with the orotate salt, a claim that has not been established. Lithium orotate is now sold online as a low-strength mood and brain health supplement, outside the monitoring that prescription lithium requires.", "timeline": [["1817", "Johan August Arfwedson discovers lithium."], ["1949", "John Cade reports lithium's effect in mania; lithium chloride salt substitutes are withdrawn after poisonings in the US."], ["1970", "FDA approves lithium carbonate for mania."], ["1970s", "Hans Nieper promotes mineral orotates, including lithium orotate."], ["2007", "A case report describes lithium toxicity from an internet-purchased lithium orotate supplement."]], "cell": "Whatever salt it comes in, lithium acts as the lithium ion once dissolved. Lithium inhibits inositol monophosphatase and inositol polyphosphate 1-phosphatase, depleting inositol and dampening phosphoinositide signaling in overactive neurons, the inositol depletion hypothesis. It also inhibits glycogen synthase kinase-3 beta, directly and by promoting its inhibitory phosphorylation, which affects circadian rhythms, neuroplasticity and cell survival pathways; laboratory studies show increased BDNF and neuroprotective effects. Because lithium is a small monovalent cation similar to sodium, the kidney handles it like sodium: about 80 percent of filtered lithium is reabsorbed, mainly in the proximal tubule. Anything that makes the kidney hold onto sodium, such as dehydration, low salt intake, thiazide diuretics, NSAIDs, ACE inhibitors and ARBs, also makes it hold onto lithium. Lithium also interferes with ADH action in the collecting duct, causing nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, inhibits thyroid hormone release, causing hypothyroidism and goiter, and can raise calcium through effects on the parathyroid glands.", "body": [["Brain", "Mood stabilizing effects at therapeutic levels; tremor, confusion, ataxia, seizures and coma with toxicity."], ["Kidneys", "Main route of elimination; nephrogenic diabetes insipidus and chronic kidney changes with long-term use."], ["Thyroid", "Inhibits thyroid hormone release, causing hypothyroidism and goiter."], ["Heart", "Sinus node dysfunction and conduction changes, especially in toxicity."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and diarrhea are early signs of toxicity and also worsen it through fluid loss."]], "journey": "Lithium orotate dissolves in the gut, releasing lithium ions and orotic acid. Lithium is absorbed almost completely from the small intestine, with immediate-release forms peaking in the blood within a few hours. It is not bound to plasma proteins and is not metabolized. It distributes through total body water, entering the brain more slowly than plasma, which is why brain effects can lag behind blood levels and persist after levels fall. Elimination is almost entirely by the kidneys, with a half-life of roughly a day in healthy adults and longer in older adults and people with kidney impairment. Because it is a small, unbound molecule, hemodialysis removes lithium effectively. Activated charcoal does not bind it. Orotic acid is an intermediate in pyrimidine synthesis and is handled by normal metabolism.", "sidefx": "At low intake, people may notice nausea, diarrhea, fine tremor, thirst or frequent urination. Toxicity causes worsening GI symptoms, coarse tremor, muscle twitching, slurred speech, unsteady gait and confusion, progressing to seizures, coma and cardiac conduction problems; chronic toxicity can occur at moderate levels and may leave lasting neurological damage. Longer exposure can cause hypothyroidism, nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, kidney changes and high calcium. Serotonin syndrome is reported with serotonergic drugs, and neurotoxicity is reported with antipsychotics. In pregnancy, lithium exposure is linked to cardiac malformations including Ebstein anomaly, though the risk is smaller than once believed, and to neonatal toxicity. Using a supplement without blood level monitoring removes the safety net that prescription lithium relies on.", "fun": "Lithium was the original secret ingredient of a famous soft drink: 7 Up was launched in 1929 under a long name that included lithiated lemon-lime, and it contained lithium citrate until the late 1940s, when US regulators moved lithium out of foods and drinks.", "src": ["Pauze DK, Brooks DE 2007, Journal of Medical Toxicology, Lithium toxicity from an Internet dietary supplement", "Lithium carbonate FDA label (boxed warning)", "StatPearls: Lithium Toxicity", "Shorter E 2009, Bipolar Disorders, The history of lithium therapy"]}, "colloidal-silver": {"origin": "Silver has been used against infection for centuries, from silver vessels for storing water to silver nitrate for wounds. In 1881 the German obstetrician Carl Crede introduced silver nitrate eye drops for newborns to prevent gonorrheal eye infection, a practice that saved the sight of many infants. Around 1902, the Philadelphia physician Albert Barnes and chemist Hermann Hille developed Argyrol, a mild silver protein solution used for eye, nose and throat infections, and its profits funded the Barnes art collection. Silver preparations were widely used until sulfa drugs and penicillin arrived in the 1930s and 1940s, when silver largely disappeared from internal medicine. In the 1990s, colloidal silver returned as an alternative remedy sold as a natural antibiotic. In 1999, FDA issued a final rule declaring that OTC drug products containing colloidal silver or silver salts are not generally recognized as safe and effective, so they cannot be sold as drugs for any disease; some are still sold as supplements without disease claims. During the COVID-19 pandemic, FDA and FTC sent warning letters to sellers promoting silver to treat or prevent the virus. Topical silver remains useful in medicine, in silver sulfadiazine cream and silver-containing wound dressings.", "timeline": [["1881", "Carl Crede introduces silver nitrate eye prophylaxis for newborns."], ["1902", "Argyrol, a silver protein antiseptic, is introduced and widely used."], ["1930s-1940s", "Sulfa drugs and penicillin replace most medical silver use."], ["1999", "FDA rules that OTC colloidal silver drug products are not generally recognized as safe and effective."], ["2020", "FDA and FTC warn sellers promoting colloidal silver for COVID-19."]], "cell": "Silver's antimicrobial action depends on silver ions, released from metal or particle surfaces in moist conditions. These ions bind thiol groups in bacterial enzymes and membrane proteins, disrupt the respiratory chain, generate reactive oxygen species and bind DNA, killing many microbes on contact. That is useful on a wound or a surface, but in the body silver ions are quickly bound by chloride, proteins and other molecules, so effective concentrations do not reach infections elsewhere. Swallowed silver is absorbed in small amounts and circulates bound to proteins such as albumin. It deposits in the basement membranes of skin, around sweat glands and hair follicles, in mucosa, nails, the eye, the liver, spleen and kidneys. In the skin, light reduces silver compounds to metallic silver and silver sulfide and selenide granules, which scatter light and appear slate-gray to blue-gray, most strongly on sun-exposed areas; silver also stimulates melanin production. This deposition is the basis of argyria. In the gut, silver can form complexes with some drugs, reducing their absorption.", "body": [["Skin", "Argyria: permanent blue-gray discoloration, most visible on sun-exposed areas."], ["Mouth and nails", "Gums and nail beds turn gray-blue."], ["Eyes", "Silver deposits in the conjunctiva and cornea (argyrosis)."], ["Liver and kidneys", "Silver deposits in these organs; injury is rare and mostly in case reports."], ["Nervous system", "Rare case reports of seizures and neurological effects with heavy use."]], "journey": "After swallowing, most silver passes through the gut unabsorbed. The small fraction absorbed travels bound to plasma proteins and distributes widely, accumulating in skin, mucosa, liver, spleen, kidneys and other tissues, where it is stored as insoluble granules. Silver is not metabolized in the usual sense; it is reduced and complexed with sulfur and selenium. Elimination is mainly by the liver into bile and out in the feces, with small amounts in urine. Because tissue stores are released very slowly, silver remains in the body for years, and argyria develops gradually over months to years of exposure and persists after use stops. Chelating agents have not been shown to reverse it.", "sidefx": "The hallmark adverse effect is argyria, a permanent blue-gray or slate-gray discoloration of the skin, gums, nail beds and eyes that can be cosmetically and socially distressing. Because it resembles cyanosis, it can complicate assessment, though people with argyria are usually comfortable and well oxygenated. Metallic taste and GI upset occur. Rare case reports describe seizures, kidney injury and neurological problems with heavy use. Colloidal silver reduces absorption of quinolone and tetracycline antibiotics, levothyroxine and penicillamine. A major indirect harm is delay of effective treatment when people use silver instead of antibiotics or other therapy. Pregnancy, breastfeeding and children are avoided because of accumulation.", "fun": "A Montana political candidate who drank homemade colloidal silver in the late 1990s, fearing antibiotic shortages, developed permanent blue-gray argyria and ran for the US Senate with blue skin, becoming one of the best-known cases of the condition.", "src": ["FDA 1999, Over-the-counter drug products containing colloidal silver ingredients or silver salts, Final Rule (21 CFR 310.548)", "NIH NCCIH: Colloidal Silver", "Lansdown AB 2006, Current Problems in Dermatology, Silver in health care: antimicrobial effects and safety in use", "StatPearls: Argyria"]}, "nad-iv": {"origin": "NAD+ was discovered in 1906 by the British biochemists Arthur Harden and William Young, who found that yeast juice needed a heat-stable factor, which they called cozymase, to ferment sugar. Over the next decades, Hans von Euler-Chelpin worked out its chemical nature, sharing the 1929 Nobel Prize in Chemistry with Harden, and Otto Warburg showed in the 1930s that it carries hydrogen in metabolic reactions. Its link to nutrition came through pellagra, a disease of skin, gut and brain common in the American South in the early twentieth century; Joseph Goldberger showed it was caused by poor diet, and Conrad Elvehjem identified nicotinic acid, niacin, as the cure in 1937, because niacin is the building block of NAD+. Interest in NAD+ as an anti-aging target grew in the 2000s after researchers showed that sirtuins, enzymes linked to longevity in yeast and other organisms, require NAD+, and after nicotinamide riboside was identified as an NAD+ precursor in 2004. Mouse studies showing that NAD+ levels fall with age and that precursors improve metabolic and mitochondrial measures fueled a supplement market for NR and NMN, while wellness clinics began offering IV NAD+ drips for energy, aging and addiction. Human evidence for clinical benefit remains limited.", "timeline": [["1906", "Arthur Harden and William Young discover cozymase, later identified as NAD+."], ["1937", "Conrad Elvehjem identifies nicotinic acid as the cure for pellagra."], ["2000", "Sirtuin deacetylases are shown to depend on NAD+."], ["2004", "Nicotinamide riboside is identified as an NAD+ precursor in yeast and humans."], ["2010s-2020s", "NR and NMN supplements and IV NAD+ wellness infusions spread, with small human trials."]], "cell": "NAD+ sits at the center of energy metabolism. In glycolysis and the TCA cycle, NAD+ accepts a hydride to become NADH, and NADH then delivers electrons to complex I of the mitochondrial electron transport chain, driving ATP production. Because these reactions only cycle NAD+ between oxidized and reduced forms, they do not use it up. Other enzymes do consume it. Sirtuins remove acetyl groups from proteins, using NAD+ and releasing nicotinamide, which links nutrient status to gene expression and mitochondrial function. PARPs, activated by DNA damage, build long ADP-ribose chains from NAD+ to recruit repair proteins, and heavy DNA damage can deplete NAD+. CD38, an enzyme on immune and other cells that increases with age and inflammation, hydrolyzes NAD+ and is thought to drive much of the age-related decline seen in animals. Cells rebuild NAD+ mainly through a salvage pathway in which NAMPT converts nicotinamide to NMN, which is converted to NAD+. NR enters this pathway by being phosphorylated to NMN. NAD+ itself is a large charged molecule that does not cross cell membranes well, so IV NAD+ is largely broken down outside cells, and its components are taken up and rebuilt inside.", "body": [["Mitochondria", "NADH feeds the electron transport chain for ATP production."], ["Nucleus", "PARPs consume NAD+ during DNA repair; sirtuins use it to regulate gene expression."], ["Immune system", "CD38 on immune cells degrades NAD+ and rises with inflammation and age."], ["Heart and chest", "IV infusion commonly causes chest tightness and pressure, typically eased by slowing the rate."], ["Gut", "Nausea and abdominal cramping during infusions; mild GI upset with oral precursors."]], "journey": "Given intravenously, NAD+ enters the blood and is rapidly degraded by enzymes such as CD38 and nucleotide pyrophosphatases into components including ADP-ribose, NMN, nicotinamide and adenosine. A small pilot study of a long infusion found that plasma NAD+ rose only after hours of infusion, and urine metabolites appeared, suggesting rapid breakdown and excretion. Oral NR and NMN are absorbed from the gut, partly converted to nicotinamide in the gut wall and liver, and used by cells through the salvage pathway; NMN may also be taken up through a specific transporter in some tissues. Blood NAD+ levels rise over hours with oral precursors. Excess nicotinamide is methylated in the liver to methylnicotinamide and oxidized further, and these metabolites are excreted in the urine.", "sidefx": "Infusion discomfort is the most commonly reported problem with IV NAD+: chest tightness or pressure, nausea, abdominal cramping, flushing, headache, lightheadedness and anxiety, which typically ease when the infusion is slowed or stopped. Because chest pain has many causes, it still needs assessment. Any IV therapy carries risks of infiltration, phlebitis, infection and allergic reactions, and compounded products vary in quality. Oral NR and NMN are generally well tolerated in short trials, with mild GI upset. Theoretical concerns include supporting cancer cell metabolism and possible effects of high methylation demand from nicotinamide breakdown. Long-term safety, and safety in pregnancy and breastfeeding, have not been established.", "fun": "NAD+ was found in a jar of yeast juice: Arthur Harden and William Young noticed that boiled yeast extract, which could not ferment sugar by itself, restarted fermentation when added back to filtered extract, revealing a heat-stable helper they called cozymase that turned out to be NAD+.", "src": ["Rajman L, Chwalek K, Sinclair DA 2018, Cell Metabolism, Therapeutic potential of NAD-boosting molecules: the in vivo evidence", "Grant R et al. 2019, Frontiers in Aging Neuroscience, A pilot study of the human NAD+ metabolome during intravenous NAD+ infusion", "Bieganowski P, Brenner C 2004, Cell, Discoveries of nicotinamide riboside as a nutrient and conserved NRK genes", "NIH Office of Dietary Supplements: Niacin fact sheet"]}});
